export const BRAIN = {"built":"2026-11-27","commit":"a1265f6","pages":[{"path":"index.md","title":"index.md","meta":"","sections":[{"h":"Index","a":"index","text":"One line per wiki page. Read this first, then open only the pages you need.\nFor a date or a lecture title, open `wiki/threads/schedule.md`. For a due date, open `wiki/threads/due-dates.md`."},{"h":"Lectures","a":"lectures","text":"- [Code track: Building UI pages](wiki/lectures/code-track-building-ui.md): The first code-track pages: Building UI notes, the 17 to 13 vote, golden pairs, verifier, Gradio setup, practice set, office hour of 1 October.\n- [Hackathon (17 to 20 Oct 2026)](wiki/lectures/hackathon.md): Hackathon Sat 17 to Tue 20 Oct 2026, 9 PM IST, results Thu 22 Oct: three problems (BillShield, Eval Co-pilot, Concept Check), nine rules, six check-ins.\n- [Lecture 1: Orientation and State of AI 2026](wiki/lectures/lecture-01.md): Lecture 1 (Sat 19 Sep 2026, both tracks): the Verifier's Law, the 2% editor project, the verify game, the three boxes, golden pairs task.\n- [Lecture 2: What is an interface; UI as the first interface](wiki/lectures/lecture-02.md): Lecture 2 (Fri 25 Sep 2026, both tracks): the smiley game, interface as common language plus protocol, UI, API, HTML, HTTP, and next steps.\n- [Lecture 3, no-code track: PRD to UI design to frontend app](wiki/lectures/lecture-03-no-code.md): Lecture 3, no-code track (Sat 26 Sep 2026): one salon's haircut preview from four questions to a flow, a PRD and design.md, screens in Stitch, an Android app from Google AI Studio.\n- [Lecture 3: Building UI, design to deploy](wiki/lectures/lecture-03.md): Lecture 3 (Sat 26 Sep 2026, code track): 10 golden pairs, a verifier, the 17 to 13 vote a follow-up overturned, Gradio in four lines.\n- [Lecture 4: API as the second interface](wiki/lectures/lecture-04.md): Lecture 4 (Sat 03 Oct 2026, both tracks): Round Three, the six parts of a request and reply, CRUD, the Request Builder and practice set.\n- [Lecture 5, no-code track: Building APIs and backend](wiki/lectures/lecture-05-no-code.md): Lecture 5, no-code track (Fri 9 Oct 2026): the Magic Mirror app moves from Google AI Studio into Antigravity; a map of the files, pseudocode, keys in .env, one click traced with Groq, Replicate left to wire.\n- [Lecture 5: Building APIs and backend](wiki/lectures/lecture-05.md): Lecture 5 (Fri 9 Oct 2026, code track): a raw requests.post to Groq replaces the echo chat; status codes, endpoint, free tier limits, practice set.\n- [Lecture 6: Databases and domain modelling](wiki/lectures/lecture-06.md): Lecture 6 (Sat 10 Oct, both tracks): Aarav's app forgets, memory.txt, storage on your server, the HLD, domain modelling, keys and pointers, Modelling Lab, practice set due Thu 15 Oct.\n- [Lecture 7, no-code track: Connect backend to database](wiki/lectures/lecture-07-no-code.md): Lecture 7, no-code track (Fri 16 Oct 2026): the MERN stack as a restaurant, a salon booking that vanishes on refresh, four tables, a to-do app connected to Supabase from Antigravity in eight steps.\n- [Lecture 7: Connect backend to database](wiki/lectures/lecture-07.md): Lecture 7 (Fri 16 Oct 2026, code track): the Groq key leaves the browser; one function behind FastAPI and Pydantic, deployed to Vercel; practice set due Sat 17 Oct.\n- [Lecture 8, no-code track: End to end MVP build and deploy](wiki/lectures/lecture-08-no-code.md): Lecture 8, no-code track (Sat 17 Oct 2026): the salon app on Supabase with RLS and CRUD, then live with Git, GitHub and Vercel; two tests, environment variables, appointment 2041.\n- [Lecture 8: End to end MVP](wiki/lectures/lecture-08.md): Lecture 8 (Sat 17 Oct 2026, code track): storage moves from memory.txt to Postgres tables on Supabase; race condition, four dials, RLS.\n- [Lecture 9: LLMs from First Principles](wiki/lectures/lecture-09.md): Lecture 9 (Fri 30 Oct 2026, both tracks): verifiable means automatable, the track query that hallucinated (1/10 bare, 5 with tracks), Verifier's Rule, Practice set 1.\n- [Lecture 10: Tool calling and MCP](wiki/lectures/lecture-10.md): Lecture 10 (Sat 31 Oct 2026, both tracks): the track question scored 1, 5, 7 out of 10; golden pair; function calling as the reverse call.\n- [Lecture 11: Tool calling and MCP](wiki/lectures/lecture-11.md): Lecture 11 (Fri 13 Nov 2026, both tracks): Priya's sheet, tool schema, errors, execution record, MCP, host/client/server; Tool Bench card scores 3, 4 or 5, 5 of 5.\n- [Lecture 12, no-code track: Building with tool calling and MCP](wiki/lectures/lecture-12-no-code.md): Lecture 12, no-code track (Sat 14 Nov 2026): Priya's AI employee for Northwind Goods, eight tools, the card as a contract, the app holds the brakes; the Google Sheets MCP server in Antigravity, order 1051.\n- [Lecture 12: Building with tool calling and MCP](wiki/lectures/lecture-12.md): Lecture 12 (Sat 14 Nov 2026, code track notes): tool_call.py and mcp_server.py with Groq and FastMCP; Practice set 3 due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026.\n- [Lecture 13: Retrieval-augmented generation](wiki/lectures/lecture-13.md): Lecture 13 (Fri 20 Nov 2026, both tracks): RAG as retrieve, augment, generate; 2,834-token sheet, 128,000-token window, chunks, labels, index, Retrieval Bench.\n- [Lecture 14, no-code track: Building RAG apps](wiki/lectures/lecture-14-no-code.md): Lecture 14, no-code track (Sat 21 Nov 2026): the Retrieval Bench's seven stations, an HR helper with six documents, chunks with overlap, metadata and filters; a car manual app with Antigravity, Supabase pgvector and Groq.\n- [Lecture 14: Building RAG apps](wiki/lectures/lecture-14.md): Lecture 14 (Sat 21 Nov 2026, code track): token budget, golden set of ten, shared words 5, rare words 6, meaning 8 of 10; RAG Lab levels 5 to 9.\n- [Office hour, Thu 1 Oct 2026: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click](wiki/lectures/office-hour-01-oct.md): Office hour (Thu 1 Oct 2026, after Lecture 3, both tracks): Stitch questions, splitting a job before picking an LLM, and what an interface is for once you can say what you want (operate, consult, delegate).\n- [Office hour, Thu 15 Oct 2026: Write the Logic Before the Code](wiki/lectures/office-hour-15-oct.md): Office hour (Thu 15 Oct 2026, after Lecture 6, both tracks): pseudocode as sequence, selection and repetition; three levels of asking an LLM; Q&A on keys, tables, security and when to start building.\n- [Office hour, Tue 22 Sep 2026: Tools change. The foundation stays.](wiki/lectures/office-hour-22-sep.md): Office hour (Tue 22 Sep 2026, after Lecture 1, both tracks): how office hours run, an LLM against a world model, the no-code track order, and how domain knowledge gets in as a second brain.\n- [Office hour, Tue 24 Nov 2026: Your RAG app, part by part.](wiki/lectures/office-hour-24-nov.md): Office hour (Tue 24 Nov 2026, after Lecture 14, both tracks): chunk size, when RAG is the wrong tool, what Groq, gte-small, Supabase and pgvector each do, why build the app, and why an off-topic question still got sources (top-k, guardrail).\n- [Idea to App workshop (Week 0)](wiki/lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md): Week 0 workshop (Fri 18 Sep 2026, both tracks): North Star's four elements, the mom test, Aarav's observation, falsifiable hypothesis, cohort zero, input-process-output."},{"h":"Ideas","a":"ideas","text":"- [Abstraction](wiki/concepts/abstraction.md)\n- [API](wiki/concepts/api.md)\n- [Atomic task](wiki/concepts/atomic-task.md)\n- [Backend](wiki/concepts/backend.md)\n- [BM25](wiki/concepts/bm25.md)\n- [Business logic](wiki/concepts/business-logic.md)\n- [Causal chain](wiki/concepts/causal-chain.md)\n- [Chunk](wiki/concepts/chunk.md)\n- [Common language](wiki/concepts/common-language.md)\n- [Context](wiki/concepts/context.md)\n- [CRUD](wiki/concepts/crud.md)\n- [Deploy](wiki/concepts/deploy.md)\n- [Domain knowledge](wiki/concepts/domain-knowledge.md)\n- [Domain model](wiki/concepts/domain-model.md)\n- [Done Equals](wiki/concepts/done-equals.md)\n- [Embedding](wiki/concepts/embedding.md)\n- [Endpoint](wiki/concepts/endpoint.md)\n- [Environment variable](wiki/concepts/environment-variable.md)\n- [ER diagram](wiki/concepts/er-diagram.md)\n- [Execution record](wiki/concepts/execution-record.md)\n- [Falsifiable](wiki/concepts/falsifiable.md)\n- [FastAPI](wiki/concepts/fastapi.md)\n- [FastMCP](wiki/concepts/fastmcp.md)\n- [Function calling (tool calling)](wiki/concepts/function-calling.md)\n- [Golden dataset](wiki/concepts/golden-dataset.md)\n- [Golden pair](wiki/concepts/golden-pair.md)\n- [Golden set](wiki/concepts/golden-set.md)\n- [Hallucination](wiki/concepts/hallucination.md)\n- [High-level design (HLD)](wiki/concepts/high-level-design.md)\n- [Host, client, server](wiki/concepts/host-client-server.md)\n- [Hypothesis](wiki/concepts/hypothesis.md)\n- [Index](wiki/concepts/index.md)\n- [Input, process, output](wiki/concepts/input-process-output.md)\n- [Interface](wiki/concepts/interface.md)\n- [JSON](wiki/concepts/json.md)\n- [Judgment-based verification](wiki/concepts/judgment-based-verification.md)\n- [Key (primary key)](wiki/concepts/key.md)\n- [Label](wiki/concepts/label.md)\n- [LLM assistant](wiki/concepts/llm-assistant.md)\n- [localhost](wiki/concepts/localhost.md)\n- [Manual MVP](wiki/concepts/manual-mvp.md)\n- [MCP (Model Context Protocol)](wiki/concepts/mcp.md)\n- [North Star](wiki/concepts/north-star.md)\n- [Observation](wiki/concepts/observation.md)\n- [Pointer (foreign key)](wiki/concepts/pointer.md)\n- [PostgreSQL](wiki/concepts/postgresql.md)\n- [Protocol](wiki/concepts/protocol.md)\n- [Pydantic](wiki/concepts/pydantic.md)\n- [Race condition](wiki/concepts/race-condition.md)\n- [Rate limit](wiki/concepts/rate-limit.md)\n- [Repository, commit and push](wiki/concepts/repository.md)\n- [Request and response](wiki/concepts/request.md)\n- [Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)](wiki/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md)\n- [RLS (row level security)](wiki/concepts/rls.md)\n- [SDK](wiki/concepts/sdk.md)\n- [Spec](wiki/concepts/spec.md)\n- [Status code](wiki/concepts/status-code.md)\n- [Storage](wiki/concepts/storage.md)\n- [Temperature](wiki/concepts/temperature.md)\n- [The loop](wiki/concepts/the-loop.md)\n- [The mom test](wiki/concepts/the-mom-test.md)\n- [Token budget](wiki/concepts/token-budget.md)\n- [Tool card](wiki/concepts/tool-card.md)\n- [Tool](wiki/concepts/tool.md)\n- [User interface (GUI, UI)](wiki/concepts/user-interface.md)\n- [V0 / MVP](wiki/concepts/v0-mvp.md)\n- [Vector database](wiki/concepts/vector-database.md)\n- [Verification by rule](wiki/concepts/verification-by-rule.md)\n- [Verifier](wiki/concepts/verifier.md)\n- [The Verifier's Law](wiki/concepts/verifiers-law.md)\n- [The Verifier's Rule](wiki/concepts/verifiers-rule.md)"},{"h":"Across lectures","a":"across-lectures","text":"- [Aarav, the course's example user](wiki/threads/aaravs-example.md): Who Aarav is (26, Pune, business analyst) and his worked example from observation to hypothesis to a 21-day manual MVP plan, Week 0.\n- [Due dates and where to hand in](wiki/threads/due-dates.md): Every practice set, assignment and task with its due date exactly as written and where it goes: Assignment 1, Lectures 3 to 8, hackathon, Practice sets 1 to 3, RAG Lab.\n- [From memory.txt to tables](wiki/threads/from-memory-txt-to-tables.md): How Aarav's app learned to remember: memory.txt (L6), the file dies on Vercel (L7, L8), Supabase tables (L8), then context, tools, search (L10 to L14).\n- [Schedule: every lecture, date and title](wiki/threads/schedule.md): The date, title and track of every lecture from 1 to 19, with holidays, the hackathon and the catch-up week. From the curriculum sheet, which wins on dates and titles.\n- [The scoreboards](wiki/threads/scoreboards.md): Scores that moved as things were added around the model: track question 1, 5, 7 of 10; tool card 3, 4 or 5, 5; searches 5, 6, 8.\n- [The check comes first](wiki/threads/the-check-comes-first.md): Writing the check before the run: Verifier's Law, golden dataset (L1), Verifier's Rule (L9), golden pair (L10, L11), eval (PS2), golden set (L14).\n- [The track question](wiki/threads/the-track-question.md): The one question the model was asked in Lectures 9 to 12, which track to choose: exact wordings, invented tracks, scores 1, 5, 7, fix.\n- [Where sources disagree: tools, names and numbers](wiki/threads/where-sources-disagree-tools-and-numbers.md): Course pages that disagree on deploy tools, Lecture 5's model, when tables reach Supabase, file counts, function names, bench size, coach time and Aarav's projects.\n- [Where sources disagree: status, titles and dates](wiki/threads/where-sources-disagree.md): Course pages that disagree on lecture status, lecture titles and numbers, hackathon end and the Practice set 2 date, with both versions and which wins."},{"h":"How to","a":"how-to","text":"- [Call an LLM with requests.post](wiki/how-to/call-an-llm-with-requests-post.md)\n- [Create a Supabase project and tables](wiki/how-to/create-a-supabase-project-and-tables.md)\n- [Decide what your app stores](wiki/how-to/decide-what-your-app-stores.md)\n- [Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel](wiki/how-to/deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel.md)\n- [Deploy a Gradio app to Hugging Face Spaces](wiki/how-to/deploy-a-gradio-app-to-hugging-face-spaces.md)\n- [Do Assignment 1, First Proof](wiki/how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md)\n- [Fix a failed LLM API request](wiki/how-to/fix-a-failed-llm-api-request.md)\n- [Fix a failed request](wiki/how-to/fix-a-failed-request.md)\n- [Fix common Python and Gradio errors](wiki/how-to/fix-common-python-and-gradio-errors.md)\n- [Give an MCP address to a second app](wiki/how-to/give-an-mcp-address-to-a-second-app.md)\n- [Keep your Groq key safe](wiki/how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md)\n- [Put your project on GitHub](wiki/how-to/put-your-project-on-github.md)\n- [Read the network tab](wiki/how-to/read-the-network-tab.md)\n- [Run a Gradio app locally](wiki/how-to/run-a-gradio-app-locally.md)\n- [Set up Python and Gradio as a first-time coder](wiki/how-to/set-up-python-and-gradio-first-time.md)\n- [Use the AI Delegation Framework](wiki/how-to/use-the-delegation-framework.md)\n- [Use the Lectures 9 to 12 checklist](wiki/how-to/use-the-lectures-9-to-12-checklist.md)\n- [Write a tool call and an MCP server in Python (Practice set 3)](wiki/how-to/write-a-tool-call-and-an-mcp-server-in-python.md)\n- [Write a tool card against tests](wiki/how-to/write-a-tool-card-against-tests.md)\n- [Write the eval (Practice set 2)](wiki/how-to/write-the-eval.md)"},{"h":"From sand to language models","a":"from-sand-to-language-models","text":"- [Chapter 1: How does sand do maths?](wiki/course/01-sand-to-screen.md)\n- [Chapter 2: How does a machine remember?](wiki/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers.md)\n- [Chapter 3: Can you trust a machine?](wiki/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine.md)\n- [Chapter 4: What is learning?](wiki/course/04-what-is-learning.md)\n- [Chapter 5: What are attention and transformers?](wiki/course/05-attention-and-transformers.md)\n- [Chapter 6: What hardware runs a language model?](wiki/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms.md)\n- [Chapter 7: How does a guesser become an assistant?](wiki/course/07-guesser-to-assistant.md)\n- [Chapter 8: What do language models learn from now?](wiki/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now.md)\n- [About the course: From Sand to Language Models](wiki/course/about-the-course.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/abstraction.md","title":"Abstraction","meta":"title: Abstraction\nsummary: Hiding detail so you work at a higher level; Lecture 5's passenger and driver picture and why SDKs are a cushion.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Abstraction","a":"abstraction","text":"Abstraction is \"Hiding detail so you work at a higher level. Comfort that costs understanding.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- In Lecture 5, the SDK code that worked showed neither the URL nor POST: both were hidden behind abstraction. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- The picture: a passenger enjoys the comfort of a car; a driver understands its controls and knows what to do when things go wrong. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- SDKs, libraries, no-code/vibe-coding tools are like comfortable passenger seats. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- \"Comfort without understanding comes at the cost of control.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- \"Use abstractions for comfort. Build understanding for control.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- The controls to understand: APIs, endpoints, authentication, requests, and responses. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- Ripping off the cushion means making the same call with `requests.post`, every part visible. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- \"But hidden machinery doesn't disappear.\" When something breaks, what you understand determines what you can fix. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n\nSee also: [SDK](sdk.md), [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/api.md","title":"API","meta":"title: API\nsummary: API (Application Programming Interface), from Lecture 2: the interface between two machines; an HTTP API when the medium is the internet.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"API","a":"api","text":"An API, Application Programming Interface, is the machine-to-machine interface. When the medium is the internet it is an HTTP API. [Lecture 2 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface)"},{"h":"How Lecture 2 derived it","a":"how-lecture-2-derived-it","text":"- Typing chatgpt.com turns the name into an IP address that leads to another machine, owned by OpenAI, listening for requests. [Lecture 2 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface)\n- Siddhant's MacBook Pro and OpenAI's machine, which might run Linux on NVIDIA hardware, do not understand each other. The room named the bridge: the API. [Lecture 2 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface)\n- For the web, HTML is the common language and HTTP is the protocol. [Lecture 2 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)"},{"h":"Kinds","a":"kinds","text":"- API is the broad term; the kind depends on the environment. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- Over the internet: HTTP API. Saving files to storage: a file API such as FTP. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- Any two machines communicating need an API. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- The UI is the first interface (human to machine); the API is the second (machine to machine). [Lecture 2 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md), [Interface](interface.md), [Protocol](protocol.md), [User interface](user-interface.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/atomic-task.md","title":"Atomic task","meta":"title: Atomic task\nsummary: Lecture 9: a unit of work specific enough that you can be held accountable for it; Aarav's Monday; Practice set 1 lists 25 to 60.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Atomic task","a":"atomic-task","text":"A unit of work specific enough that you can be held accountable for it. Lecture 9. [Lecture 9 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"How Lecture 9 got there","a":"how-lecture-9-got-there","text":"- Aarav's Monday: email, then Jira, then Slack. The top Jira item: a presentation for upper management on cutting spend and improving the P&L. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)\n- \"Help my company grow\" is not one. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)\n- His to-do split into three: collect last quarter's data, research pricing trends, generate the presentation. All three are verifiable. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)\n- A task becomes verifiable the moment its outcome is clear. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Practice set 1: one row per atomic task from last week, with the time spent. Expect 25 to 60 rows. [Practice set 1, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#02-fill-the-sheet-0-of-5-ready)\n- An atomic task that does not move your North Star gets one line and nothing more. [Practice set 1, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#01-start-with-your-north-star)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Listing tasks from an LLM. It gives you the average job, not yours. [Practice set 1, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#03-rules)\n- A vague task: \"Help the team\" gets you four weeks of rewriting this sheet. [Practice set 1, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#06-what-happens-next)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/backend.md","title":"Backend","meta":"title: Backend\nsummary: The machine between your UI and everything else; it holds keys and stores data. Why the key cannot live in the page. Lecture 4, built in Lecture 5.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Backend","a":"backend","text":"The backend is the machine between your UI and everything else. It holds keys and stores data. [Lecture 4 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"How Lecture 4 got there","a":"how-lecture-4-got-there","text":"- In step 9 of the Request Builder you see your Groq key in plain text in DevTools. If Aarav's product worked like this, every visitor could read his key and spend his money. [Request Builder, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#09-find-your-key-and-where-it-should-live)\n- In step 5, a Read of diagnoses you had just created came back 200 and an empty list, because that machine has no database. [Request Builder, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#then-read-what-you-created)"},{"h":"The three boxes","a":"the-three-boxes","text":"- UI, then backend, then Groq: the UI calls only the backend, and the backend holds the Groq key and stores the data, because a page can do neither. [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#04-deliverable-5-design-your-api)\n- The backend keeps the Groq key in an environment variable, so the visitor's browser never sees it. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#02-where-a-key-lives)\n- Backends are not subject to CORS, which is one more reason the call to Groq moves to a backend. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Building that backend is the Lecture 5 practical, for both tracks: code track in Python, no-code track in Antigravity. [Lecture 4 notes, Dates](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#dates)\n- Code track: the step 8 Python file becomes the body of your FastAPI route in Lecture 5. [Request Builder, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#08-put-a-model-behind-it)\n- Where the data should live is Lecture 6. [Request Builder, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#then-read-what-you-created)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- In Antigravity, if a step in the agent's plan puts the Groq key in the page, reject that step. [Lecture 4 kit, 5](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-kit.md#no-code-track-the-four-boxes)\n- In your API design, no key sits in the UI box. [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#04-deliverable-5-design-your-api)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](../how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/bm25.md","title":"BM25","meta":"title: BM25\nsummary: Lecture 14: keyword search where rare shared words count more; one line of Python, log(N / seen_in(w)), found 6 of 10.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"BM25","a":"bm25","text":"BM25 (best match 25) is \"Keyword search where rare shared words count more than common ones.\" Lecture 14. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"How Lecture 14 got there","a":"how-lecture-14-got-there","text":"- Counting shared words found 5 of 10 and missed B, \"Why is the model making things up?\", which has no rare word. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words), [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- The word lecture is in 47 of the 183 passages; the word law is in 2. A match on law tells you far more. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- So each shared word adds log(N / seen_in(w)) instead of 1, where N is 183. \"law\" weighs about 4.5, \"lecture\" about 1.4. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- The log keeps the weights small as the pile grows. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- Result: 6 of 10. J moved from place 41 to the top 3; B stayed at place 27. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- When the question has the exact term: a name, a code, an error message. It needs no extra model, and you can explain every score. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- It found F and J, which search by meaning lost. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)"},{"h":"Where it fails","a":"where-it-fails","text":"- When people use other words: \"making things up\" was at place 27. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n\nSee also: [Embedding](embedding.md), [Golden set](golden-set.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/business-logic.md","title":"Business logic","meta":"title: Business logic\nsummary: Business logic, from Lecture 7: the one function that takes the input and makes the output, such as Aarav's diagnose(workflow). Deploy it first.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Business logic","a":"business-logic","text":"Business logic is \"The one function that takes the input and makes the output. Everything else is around it.\" [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"How Lecture 7 derived it","a":"how-lecture-7-derived-it","text":"- Strip Gradio to what it hides: a text area, a button, a box for the answer, one fetch. [Lecture 7 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#05-step-01--the-leak)\n- Remove every cushion and one function is left: `diagnose(workflow)` takes text in, returns a plan. Everything else was decoration. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- It is what you deploy first, not the UI. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)\n- Strip your own app the same way. In English first: the input, the output, the process in between. Then replace the body of the function. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)\n- The file is a template: Groq today, a CRM or mail API tomorrow. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)\n- Rule from the practice set: one function first. If it does not run from the terminal, nothing above it will. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#05-rules)\n- Writing your function is yours, not your LLM's. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#05-rules)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md), [FastAPI](fastapi.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/causal-chain.md","title":"Causal chain","meta":"title: Causal chain\nsummary: The causes you believe produced an observation, labelled as assumptions. Idea to App workshop, Week 0; Aarav's chain has seven links.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Causal chain","a":"causal-chain","text":"\"The causes you believe produced the observation, labelled as assumptions.\" [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How the workshop derived it","a":"how-the-workshop-derived-it","text":"- It is the fourth of the coach's four moves on an observation: story, subject, evidence, causal chain. [Workshop notes, 07](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain)\n- Aarav's, stacked: AI changes fast, so urgency without direction; the feed rewards novelty, so overload; no mental model to filter, so every idea feels possible. [Workshop notes, 07](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Picking the intervention point. Aarav's chain has seven links and only link 3 is addressed. [Aarav example](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#4--causal-chain)\n- \"Saying which links you will not touch is part of the answer.\" [Aarav example](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#4--causal-chain)\n- Assignment 1's discovery document must include one. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n\nSee also: [Falsifiable](falsifiable.md), [Aarav's example](../threads/aaravs-example.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/chunk.md","title":"Chunk","meta":"title: Chunk\nsummary: Chunk: a piece of the data small enough to fit the context window. Lecture 13: 10 chunks of 100,000 tokens, and the cost of sending all.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chunk","a":"chunk","text":"A chunk is \"A piece of the data small enough to fit.\" Cutting the data this way is chunking. [Lecture 13 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)"},{"h":"How Lecture 13 derived it","a":"how-lecture-13-derived-it","text":"- Pasting everything crossed GPT-OSS 120B's context window of about 128,000 tokens, and the API answered 400. [Lecture 13 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- The rule: keep the same LLM, and call it as many times as you like. [Lecture 13 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- So cut the data into pieces that fit: a million tokens in chunks of 100,000 is 10 chunks. [Lecture 13 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)\n- Send each chunk with the question, one call at a time, until a chunk holds the answer. [Lecture 13 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)"},{"h":"The catch","a":"the-catch","text":"- One question now costs 10 calls; with 1,000 chunks, 1,000 calls. Slow, and you pay for every one. [Lecture 13 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)\n- The fix is to label the chunks and send only the ones that matter. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n\nSee also: [Label](label.md), [Retrieval-augmented generation](retrieval-augmented-generation.md), [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/common-language.md","title":"Common language","meta":"title: Common language\nsummary: Common language, from Lecture 2: a vocabulary both systems understand; step one of an interface, not the interface. Binary and HTML are examples.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Common language","a":"common-language","text":"A common language is a vocabulary both systems understand. It is step one of an interface, not the interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 2 derived it","a":"how-lecture-2-derived-it","text":"- In round one of the smiley game, no one asked the machine which language it understands. [Lecture 2 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#03-round-one-a-hundred-programmers-one-machine-no-smiley)\n- Round two supplied a command list, the common language, and still failed. [Lecture 2 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#04-round-two-a-common-language-still-no-smiley)\n- A common language alone was not enough: rules were missing too. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)"},{"h":"Examples from the lecture","a":"examples-from-the-lecture","text":"- Phone call: both speak German. Letter: say Hindi. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- Meme: shared context. You laugh hardest if you have seen the scene. [Lecture 2 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#08-the-meme-as-an-interface)\n- ChatGPT: English. [Lecture 2 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface)\n- Machines: binary, the one language every machine shares. [Lecture 2 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)\n- The web: HTML, the common language for structuring pages. [Lecture 2 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Using words the machine never had. \"Pick a pen\" is English, not a command. [Lecture 2 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#04-round-two-a-common-language-still-no-smiley)\n- Using a command without its details. \"Go to 0\" was underspecified: the command took X and Y. [Lecture 2 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#04-round-two-a-common-language-still-no-smiley)\n- Calling the common language the interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#15-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md), [Interface](interface.md), [Protocol](protocol.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/context.md","title":"Context","meta":"title: Context\nsummary: The data that reaches the model inside its context window. Lecture 10: more context raised the score from 1 to 5 to 7, never 10.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Context","a":"context","text":"Context is \"The data that reaches the model inside its context window.\" Lecture 10 named it; the word carries through every lecture from there. [Lecture 10 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no)"},{"h":"How Lecture 10 got there","a":"how-lecture-10-got-there","text":"- The same question, same model, with only the context changed: none scored 1, a short block on the real tracks scored 5, the full C8 curriculum as CSV text scored 7. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- At 7 it still named Zapier, which appears nowhere in the CSV. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- \"Hallucination = missing context + a trained habit of never saying no.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Missing context is the most common cause of a wrong answer; a golden pair shows it. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- A bridge to a trusted data source fixes the context half; that bridge is function calling. [Lecture 10 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#14-practice)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Expecting more data to cure it: \"Supplying data to a system that predicts reduces hallucination. It doesn't remove it.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- Pasting a sheet as a table: it reached the model as an image; it had to be plain text first. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n\nSee also: [Hallucination](hallucination.md), [Golden pair](golden-pair.md), [Function calling](function-calling.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/crud.md","title":"CRUD","meta":"title: CRUD\nsummary: Create, Read, Update, Delete, the four actions on anything stored, spelled POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE. Derived in Lecture 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"CRUD","a":"crud","text":"CRUD is Create, Read, Update, Delete: the four things you can do to anything stored. On the wire, each is spelled as an HTTP method. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)"},{"h":"How Lecture 4 got there","a":"how-lecture-4-got-there","text":"- In Round Three, a sentence with no action named came back \"400 Which action? CREATE READ UPDATE DELETE\". [Lecture 4 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- The game's actions were those four words, and the CRUD quiz then sorted Aarav's actions into them. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)"},{"h":"The spelling","a":"the-spelling","text":"- Create is POST, Read is GET, Update is PUT or PATCH, Delete is DELETE. [Lecture 4 kit, 6](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-kit.md#6-words-you-will-meet)\n- PUT replaces the whole thing; PATCH changes part of it. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)\n- Opening the page of past diagnoses is a Read: GET /diagnoses. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)\n- Submitting the Friday workflow for the first time is a Create: POST /workflows. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)\n- Removing an old workflow is a Delete: DELETE /workflows/7. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)"},{"h":"Google search versus asking an LLM","a":"google-search-versus-asking-an-llm","text":"- A Google search is a GET: the pages already sit in Google's index, and nothing new is stored. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#google-search-versus-asking-an-llm)\n- Asking a model is a POST: a new reply is added that did not exist before, and the prompt travels in the body because it can be long and private. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#google-search-versus-asking-an-llm)\n- So generation is a Create, and Create is spelled POST. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#google-search-versus-asking-an-llm)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Designing your own API in the practice set: every action must be one of Create, Read, Update, Delete, spelled with the matching method. [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#04-deliverable-5-design-your-api)\n- Addresses are nouns (`/workflows`), not verbs (`/getWorkflow`). [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#04-deliverable-5-design-your-api)\n- REST gave nouns as addresses and CRUD as the shared verbs; CRUD is a term from 1983 and comes back in Lecture 6, where a database does the same four things to a table. [The Wire, rung 5](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md#rung-5-fifty-companies-fifty-words)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Companies do not always follow the convention; for any app, its real traffic is the truth. [Lecture 4 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)\n- A 405 means the door exists but does not take that action: ask which CRUD action you mean first, then spell it. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [Request](request.md), [Status code](status-code.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/deploy.md","title":"Deploy","meta":"title: Deploy\nsummary: Deploy, from Lecture 7: put your code on a rented machine with a URL; vercel --prod is the production one. Laptop vs own server vs Vercel.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Deploy","a":"deploy","text":"To deploy is to \"Put your code on a rented machine with a URL.\" `vercel --prod` is the production one. [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"How Lecture 7 derived it","a":"how-lecture-7-derived-it","text":"- Your laptop is a personal computer: shut it and the app is gone, and one person's RAM and bandwidth cannot serve ten thousand users. [Lecture 7 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)\n- Your own server is an independent house: you own the locks, the power backup and every attack vector. Maintenance is the real cost. [Lecture 7 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)\n- Vercel is the Airbnb: furnished, walk in, pay for what you use. The hobby plan is free. [Lecture 7 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)\n- Vercel abstracts where your machine is and how you control it. You still decide what it stores and how it serves an API. [Lecture 7 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)"},{"h":"What to deploy first","a":"what-to-deploy-first","text":"- The one function, not the UI. The UI can call it from anywhere once the function has an address. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Running the command from the wrong directory stalled the live demo. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- `vercel dev` is only a local preview. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- A file such as `memory.txt` works locally and fails on Vercel. [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- Never hardcode the key to make the 500 go away. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md), [Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel](../how-to/deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/domain-knowledge.md","title":"Domain knowledge","meta":"title: Domain knowledge\nsummary: Domain knowledge: knowing your data's things well enough to label them. Lecture 13: needed for labels, schemas and LLM-written SQL.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Domain knowledge","a":"domain-knowledge","text":"Domain knowledge is \"Knowing the things in your data well enough to label them.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"How Lecture 13 derived it","a":"how-lecture-13-derived-it","text":"- The labels came easily because you know this course. Could you write phone filters without knowing phones? No. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n- The method is from Lecture 6: name the things, what each one has, and how they connect. That is a domain model. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Turn the domain model into tables, and a question can become a query. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n- LLMs write SQL very well when they can see the schema; the server runs it on the database, here Supabase. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n- The limit: SQL needs structured data. Chat messages have no structure. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Expecting the LLM to choose how to fetch your data: \"It does not know what good looks like in your data. You do.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n\nSee also: [Label](label.md), [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/domain-model.md","title":"Domain model","meta":"title: Domain model\nsummary: Domain model (Lecture 6): the smallest description of your user's world that still answers the product's questions; things, facts and links become tables, columns, pointers.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Domain model","a":"domain-model","text":"A domain model is \"the smallest description of your user's world that still answers the product's questions.\" Building one is domain modelling. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)"},{"h":"How Lecture 6 derived it","a":"how-lecture-6-derived-it","text":"- Aarav's app forgets. `memory.txt` stored one shape, who, what, when, but his app needs conversations, workflows and every plan each workflow got. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- \"What is worth storing?\" splits into three questions: what are the things, what does each thing have, how do they connect. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Things become tables, facts become columns, links become pointers. Paper first, no tool yet. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#three-questions-things-facts-links)"},{"h":"The three questions","a":"the-three-questions","text":"- A thing passes the test: the app can create, read, update and delete one on its own, and there can be many. A conversation passes; \"9:12 am\" fails, so it is a fact. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- A fact is what you need to recreate one of the thing. For a message: who said it, what, and when. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- A link gets its shape from two questions asked both ways: one-to-many, many-to-many (a third table) or one-to-one. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- What the product does with a word decides: tone is a fact on a plan today, but a thing if users could save named tones. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)"},{"h":"When it is done","a":"when-it-is-done","text":"- The model is done when it answers your questions, not when it looks tidy. [Lecture 6 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#12-aaravs-model)\n- Then try to create a wrong state and make sure the model refuses it. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#done-means-it-answers-the-questions)\n- Aarav's model has eight tables: users, conversations, messages, attachments, workflows, diagnoses, tools, workflow_tools. [Lecture 6 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#12-aaravs-model)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- \"The most common beginner move is splitting into too many tables.\" The create, read, update, delete test is how you refuse. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#is-it-a-thing-the-create-read-update-delete-test)\n- \"A list in a cell means a missing table.\" [Lecture 6 practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md#04-rules)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Key](key.md), [Pointer](pointer.md), [Storage](storage.md), [CRUD](crud.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/done-equals.md","title":"Done Equals","meta":"title: Done Equals\nsummary: The artefact that proves an outcome, such as a tracker link. Named in the Idea to App workshop, Week 0; every curriculum sheet row has one.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Done Equals","a":"done-equals","text":"\"The artefact that proves the outcome. Here: a tracker link.\" [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- The Idea to App workshop named it, with a 21-day tracker link as the artefact. [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- The workshop's own Done equals: \"A North Star with all four elements that someone else could verify on the date, and an observation from your own life with no solution in it.\" [Overview](../../raw/practice/what-to-build.md#done-equals)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Each row of the curriculum sheet has a Done equals line, for example \"Hackathon results announced\". [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#thu-22-oct-2026--hackathon-results-announcement)\n- Arc 1's Done equals: \"a named user who did not build it completes the task on the deployed link.\" [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Treating the tracker as the product. It tracks the manual work; it is not the product. [Workshop notes, 12](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#12-where-is-the-app-a-tracker-not-a-product)\n\nSee also: [North Star](north-star.md), [Idea to App workshop](../lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/embedding.md","title":"Embedding","meta":"title: Embedding\nsummary: Lecture 14: a list of numbers placing a text so like meanings land close; gte-small makes 384, cosine similarity scores closeness, 8 of 10 found.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Embedding","a":"embedding","text":"An embedding is \"A list of numbers that places a text, so texts that mean the same land close.\" The program that makes it is an embedding model. Lecture 14. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"How Lecture 14 got there","a":"how-lecture-14-got-there","text":"- Keyword search could not find B, C, D and H: they share no rare word with their answer. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- The whiteboard started with houses plotted by size and price: houses that are alike land close together. [Lecture 14 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#05-teach-a-machine-what-words-mean)\n- Do the same with text: \"Making things up\" lands near \"hallucination\", with no word in common. The machine uses hundreds of numbers and picks what each measures. [Lecture 14 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#05-teach-a-machine-what-words-mean)"},{"h":"Choosing the embedding model","a":"choosing-the-embedding-model","text":"- Pick the smallest model that works: start small, run the questions keyword search missed, and go up a size only if it fails. [Lecture 14 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- The lecture used gte-small: 33 million parameters, about 70 MB, 384 numbers per passage. [Lecture 14 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- gte-small downloads once and runs on your own machine. Your text never leaves it. [Lecture 14 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)"},{"h":"Cosine similarity: scoring closeness","a":"cosine-similarity-scoring-closeness","text":"- Multiply two lists of 384 numbers pair by pair and add up the products. The bigger the total, the closer the meanings. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- gte-small scales every list to size 1 (normalize_embeddings=True); for such lists this sum is the cosine similarity. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- Straight-line distance gives the same order here. \"Cosine is the common default, not a law\". [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)"},{"h":"What it found","a":"what-it-found","text":"- 8 of 10: A, B, C, D, E, G, H and I. But F fell to place 10 and J to place 6, both found by rare words. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- \"Words find the exact term. Meaning finds other words for the same idea. Neither finds everything.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n\nSee also: [BM25](bm25.md), [Vector database](vector-database.md), [Golden set](golden-set.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/endpoint.md","title":"Endpoint","meta":"title: Endpoint\nsummary: The full address of one resource, path included; Lecture 5's Groq endpoint and why the domain alone fails.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Endpoint","a":"endpoint","text":"An endpoint is \"The full address of one resource, path included. The domain alone is not enough.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 5 asked \"Where Is the URL?\" while testing Groq in httpie.io/app. [Lecture 5 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#10-where-is-the-url-four-principles-in-the-code)\n- `api.groq.com` on its own failed: DNS finds a server, it does not tell the server what you want. [Lecture 5 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#10-where-is-the-url-four-principles-in-the-code)\n- The API reference gave the full endpoint: POST https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions [Lecture 5 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#10-where-is-the-url-four-principles-in-the-code)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- The address is the first of the four questions for any machine: \"What is the address?\" [Lecture 5 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#15-any-machine-four-lines)\n- Swap the URL and key for OpenAI's and the same code runs. [Lecture 5 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#15-any-machine-four-lines)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- A 404 can mean the wrong address: the domain alone, or a typo in the path. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md), [SDK](sdk.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/environment-variable.md","title":"Environment variable","meta":"title: Environment variable\nsummary: Where API keys live, outside your code; Lecture 5's GROQ_API_KEY, how to set it, and the KeyError when it is missing.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Environment variable","a":"environment-variable","text":"An environment variable is \"Where keys live, outside your code, so they never reach a screenshot.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- In Lecture 5 the key for `requests.post` comes from your environment. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)\n- Set it with `export GROQ_API_KEY=...` in the terminal; it vanishes on restart. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)\n- On Windows PowerShell the practice set shows `$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"gsk_your_key_here\"`. [Practice set, step 4](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#step-4--the-shape-of-the-change)"},{"h":"Rules","a":"rules","text":"- Keys never in code or screenshots: set it in the terminal, never in the file. [Practice set, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#06-rules-of-the-build)\n- If a key leaks, revoke it and create a new one. [Practice set, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#06-rules-of-the-build)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- `KeyError: 'GROQ_API_KEY'`: the variable is not set in this terminal. Set it again before `python app.py`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- A 401 can mean the variable is not set in the same terminal that runs the app. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md), [Fix a failed LLM API request](../how-to/fix-a-failed-llm-api-request.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/er-diagram.md","title":"ER diagram","meta":"title: ER diagram\nsummary: Entities and relationships: things and links, drawn; Lecture 8's users, workflows, diagnoses with one to many and one to one links.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"ER diagram","a":"er-diagram","text":"An ER diagram is entities and relationships: things and links, drawn. [Lecture 8 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"How Lecture 8 got there","a":"how-lecture-8-got-there","text":"- Start from the Modelling Lab, then store the least that tests the hypothesis: a user, a workflow, a diagnosis. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Each gets an `id` and a `created_at`, because two users can both be called Mira. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- User to workflow: one to many. Workflow to diagnosis: one to one, for now. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- The link is the `user_id` on the workflow. No name matching. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- The industry calls this an ER diagram. Entities are things, relationships are links. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- It maps straight onto tables: thing to table, fact to column, link to foreign key, id to primary key. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- Supabase's Schema Visualizer draws your ER diagram back: the same three boxes, the same lines. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- Knowledge graphs, second brains and LLM wikis all grow from this same first principle. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Guessing whether a workflow should allow several diagnoses. Run both with your ten users and keep the cheaper one unless users need the other. [Lecture 8 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md), [PostgreSQL](postgresql.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/execution-record.md","title":"Execution record","meta":"title: Execution record\nsummary: The log of one tool call (tool, arguments, result, status), checked against a verifier written before the run. Lecture 11.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Execution record","a":"execution-record","text":"The execution record is the log of one tool call: the tool, the arguments, the result and whether it succeeded. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 11: the LLM said \"You have 4 pending orders\", but the system called get_orders with status pending and got 3 rows. Only the record can show the sentence is wrong. [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)\n- A model can write a confident answer when the tool was never called, or when the call failed. So check the record, not the sentence. [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)"},{"h":"How to use it","a":"how-to-use-it","text":"- Write the expected record first: which tool, which arguments, which result. Then run and compare. [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)\n- That expected record is the verifier, written before the run. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)\n- When an answer is wrong, walk the chain: right tool, right API call, right rows, right final sentence. [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)\n- On the Tool Bench, download the log: the call, the arguments, the result and the answer. That log is the proof the tool was called with the right input. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Verifying what the AI says. \"Don't verify what the AI says. Verify what the system did.\" [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md), [The loop](the-loop.md), [Tool card](tool-card.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/falsifiable.md","title":"Falsifiable","meta":"title: Falsifiable\nsummary: A hypothesis written so a specific result would prove it wrong. Aarav's: under 60% of 250 within 90 days. Idea to App workshop, Week 0.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Falsifiable","a":"falsifiable","text":"\"Written so a specific result would prove it wrong. Aarav's: under 60% of 250 within 90 days.\" [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How the workshop derived it","a":"how-the-workshop-derived-it","text":"- A hypothesis is your belief about the problem, how you would solve it, and how you would know you were wrong. The last clause is the point: maybe the observation is bias. [Workshop notes, 08](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#08-a-hypothesis-must-be-able-to-fail)\n- Aarav's hypothesis was drawn with a kill line at 60%: below it the belief is false. [Workshop notes, 08](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#08-a-hypothesis-must-be-able-to-fail)\n- A full hypothesis has subject, problem, cause, intervention, a number, a deadline, and what kills it. [Workshop notes, 08](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#08-a-hypothesis-must-be-able-to-fail)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Assignment 1's discovery document needs a hypothesis with a number, a deadline and at least one falsifier. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Aarav's page also lists what the hypothesis does not claim, such as jobs, placements or revenue. [Aarav example](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#5--hypothesis)\n- In a pilot, kill conditions are checked as it runs; if one trips, stop and write it down. [Aarav example](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#kill-conditions-checked-during-the-pilot)\n\nSee also: [Hypothesis](hypothesis.md), [Causal chain](causal-chain.md), [Aarav's example](../threads/aaravs-example.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/fastapi.md","title":"FastAPI","meta":"title: FastAPI\nsummary: FastAPI, from Lecture 7: the Python framework that turns a function into an endpoint in four lines and documents it at /docs.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"FastAPI","a":"fastapi","text":"FastAPI is \"The Python framework that turns a function into an endpoint in four lines, and documents it at `/docs`.\" [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"How Lecture 7 used it","a":"how-lecture-7-used-it","text":"- Step 03 put Aarav's `diagnose(workflow)` behind a route: action POST, address `/api/diagnose`. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- The four lines: import, `app = FastAPI()`, a decorator with the action and the address, the function under it. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- `/api/` keeps every endpoint in one folder, separate from static files. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Run it with `uvicorn api.index:app --reload` from inside `03-fastapi`. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Open `/docs`: FastAPI lists every route and its input type. Free documentation. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Vercel detects FastAPI when you deploy. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)"},{"h":"Advice from the lecture","a":"advice-from-the-lecture","text":"- Read the FastAPI tutorial before asking an LLM to write it. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- The practice set asks for a `/docs` screenshot showing a POST route and its input model. [Practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready)\n\nSee also: [Pydantic](pydantic.md), [API](api.md), [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/fastmcp.md","title":"FastMCP","meta":"title: FastMCP\nsummary: FastMCP, the Python library that turns a function into an MCP server; @mcp.tool writes the card; fastmcp list and fastmcp call. Lecture 12.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"FastMCP","a":"fastmcp","text":"FastMCP is the Python library that turns a function into an MCP server. [Lecture 12 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- In Lecture 12 the function lived inside `tool_call.py`, where another app can't use it. An MCP server puts it at an address any app can reach. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- Install it with `pip install requests python-dotenv fastmcp`. [Lecture 12 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#one-tool-call-then-an-mcp-server)"},{"h":"How it works","a":"how-it-works","text":"- `mcp = FastMCP(\"100x curriculum\")` makes the server. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- `@mcp.tool` writes the card for you, from the function's name, its docstring and its type hint. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- `mcp.run(transport=\"http\", port=8000)` puts it at http://localhost:8000/mcp. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- The Groq code is gone. The server only runs the function. The app that connects brings the model. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- `fastmcp list` asks: what can you do? The card comes back. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- `fastmcp call` asks: run this one, with these arguments. The result comes back. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- \"MCP changes where the function lives. It does not change the function.\" [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)"},{"h":"In Practice set 3","a":"in-practice-set-3","text":"- Part 2 is done when `fastmcp call` returns the Lecture 12 row from your address on the internet. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [Tool](tool.md), [MCP](mcp.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/function-calling.md","title":"Function calling (tool calling)","meta":"title: Function calling (tool calling)\nsummary: The model asks a deterministic server to run a request for it. Lecture 10: tool call, execution environment, the four steps, 13 June 2023.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Function calling (tool calling)","a":"function-calling-tool-calling","text":"Function calling, also called tool calling: \"The model asks a deterministic server to run a request for it and return the result.\" Lecture 10. [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"How Lecture 10 got there","a":"how-lecture-10-got-there","text":"- Pasting the curriculum by hand got 7 out of 10; automating it needs a data source and a bridge. [Lecture 10 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- An API alone can't be the bridge: the data source is a file, and the model is probabilistic. [Lecture 10 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#09-why-an-api-alone-cant-be-the-bridge)\n- JSON was elected as the common language. [Lecture 10 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#10-the-common-language-json)\n- Only the server is kept: it runs every operation, speaks JSON, and nothing inside it predicts. [Lecture 10 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#11-the-server-is-the-bridge)\n- OpenAI shipped it on 13 June 2023 as function calling. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)"},{"h":"The four steps","a":"the-four-steps","text":"1. Request: your app sends the input plus a description of the available functions, in JSON schema. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n2. Decision: the model decides whether it needs a tool and, if so, emits a tool call. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n3. Execution: your backend, the execution environment, runs the real query or API call. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n4. Response: the backend sends the result back, and the model turns it into an answer. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)"},{"h":"Words that go with it","a":"words-that-go-with-it","text":"- Tool call: \"The model's JSON naming the function and its arguments.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Execution environment: \"Your backend: runs the tool call and returns the result.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Data source: \"The trusted store that must hold the context before any bridge can help.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"What is new about it","a":"what-is-new-about-it","text":"- Only the direction: until now your server called the model; now the model asks the server to make a request for it. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n- MCP and agent-to-agent communication build on this. [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n- \"When an agent fails, check that handoff first.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Saying \"connect it with an API\" out of habit; first ask what is being connected and how each side communicates. [Lecture 10 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#09-why-an-api-alone-cant-be-the-bridge)\n- Letting the model route in English among many tools; routing is where you want no creativity, so move that decision to the server. [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Tool](tool.md), [Tool card](tool-card.md), [API](api.md), [Common language](common-language.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/golden-dataset.md","title":"Golden dataset","meta":"title: Golden dataset\nsummary: Your ideal input-output pairs, written by hand. Lecture 1: 1 plus 2 gives 3; task is three golden pairs plus one rejected.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Golden dataset","a":"golden-dataset","text":"\"Your ideal input-output pairs, written down. Test set for a rule; unit tests for the code.\" Lecture 1. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 1 got there","a":"how-lecture-1-got-there","text":"- Round one of the verify game: an app that adds two numbers, no calculator allowed. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- Know the process, define the input (two numbers) and the output (the sum), then write ideal pairs: 1 plus 2 gives 3, 3 plus 2 gives 5. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- \"Every ML engineer at the labs has built one.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- When an LLM writes Python from your rule in English, your golden pairs become the unit tests that check the code. [Lecture 1 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Lecture 10 used one golden pair (ideal input, ideal output) to name why an answer went wrong; repeat it for one task, 100 or 200 times, and it becomes a golden dataset for that task. [Lecture 10 office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)"},{"h":"The Lecture 1 task","a":"the-lecture-1-task","text":"- Write three golden pairs by hand: three inputs with outputs you would accept, plus one you would reject and why. \"No LLM in this step.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n\nSee also: [Golden pair](golden-pair.md), [Verifier](verifier.md), [Input, process, output](input-process-output.md), [Lecture 1](../lectures/lecture-01.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/golden-pair.md","title":"Golden pair","meta":"title: Golden pair\nsummary: An ideal input plus the ideal output, written before the run; Lecture 10 used one to diagnose a hallucination. One golden dataset entry.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Golden pair","a":"golden-pair","text":"A golden pair is the ideal input and the ideal output for one task, written before you run the model. Together they are one golden dataset entry. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)"},{"h":"How Lecture 10 used it","a":"how-lecture-10-used-it","text":"- The ideal input was the breaking query, \"Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\". [Lecture 10 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- The ideal output was the 7-out-of-10 answer with the untaught tools removed. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Run the input, put the actual output beside the ideal, and name what caused the difference. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Here the cause was in the course's own data: the curriculum says the No Code track uses UI builders but never names one. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Missing context is the most common cause. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- In the practice step, write the golden pair for one task before you run anything, then score the bare and with-context runs out of 10. [Lecture 10 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#14-practice)\n- It is the reference answer an expert-work listing asks for. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)\n- Repeat it for one task, 100 or 200 times, and it becomes a golden dataset for that task. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Writing it after the run. The sheet asks for the golden pair \"written before the run\". [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)\n\nSee also: [Golden dataset](golden-dataset.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/golden-set.md","title":"Golden set","meta":"title: Golden set\nsummary: Lecture 14: questions where you already know which passage holds the answer; score every search change on it with recall at 3.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Golden set","a":"golden-set","text":"A golden set is \"Golden pairs: questions where you already know which passage holds the answer.\" Lecture 14. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"How Lecture 14 got there","a":"how-lecture-14-got-there","text":"- The lecture had ten questions, A to J, and for each one it knew which passage holds the answer. [Lecture 14 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions)\n- Those ten are the golden set: ten golden pairs, the idea from Lecture 10. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- Rule from the lecture: \"Know the right answers first. Then count how often your search finds them.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)"},{"h":"Recall at 3: the score you count on it","a":"recall-at-3-the-score-you-count-on-it","text":"- Recall at 3 is \"How often the right passage lands in the top 3, over the golden set.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Counting shared words scored 5 of 10, or 50%. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- Then rare words 6 of 10, then meaning 8 of 10. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Score every change to your search. [Lecture 14 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md)\n- Choose an embedding model: Claude cannot tell you which one fits your data. \"Your golden set can.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- Decide if you need a vector database: you cannot say until you know the data and its golden set. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Interview answer for \"How do you know your retrieval works?\": name the set and the number. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Choosing how to search before you know the data. On data you do not know, \"The golden set comes first.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n\nSee also: [Golden dataset](golden-dataset.md), [BM25](bm25.md), [Embedding](embedding.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/hallucination.md","title":"Hallucination","meta":"title: Hallucination\nsummary: Lecture 9: a response with the right shape and the wrong data; the track query that listed tracks that do not exist.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Hallucination","a":"hallucination","text":"A response with the right shape and the wrong data. Lecture 9 named it. [Lecture 9 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"How Lecture 9 got there","a":"how-lecture-9-got-there","text":"- The model was asked: \"Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\" [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- The shape was right: a decision framework, assessment questions, a mapping from answers to tracks, a checklist. [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- The data was wrong: front-end, back-end, full-stack, data and ML, DevOps, product, security. None of them exist. [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- \"Right template, wrong data: hallucination.\" The sketch notes that missing data caused it. [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- Scored later: \"1 out of 10 bare, 5 with the real tracks pasted in.\" [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)"},{"h":"What it means for building","a":"what-it-means-for-building","text":"- It is why this model, as is, cannot be the career coach on the 100x website. [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- The deterministic fix: a template plus track data fetched from the database. One API call, one template, the right answer every time. [Lecture 9 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#06-the-deterministic-answer-already-existed)\n- A learner noted that context bloats and models hallucinate more as it grows. [Lecture 9 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/high-level-design.md","title":"High-level design (HLD)","meta":"title: High-level design (HLD)\nsummary: High-level design (Lecture 6): the whiteboard of every box, whose machine it lives on, and the API calls between them; Aarav's HLD and the practice set.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"High-level design (HLD)","a":"high-level-design-hld","text":"The high-level design is \"The whiteboard: every box, where it lives, and the calls between them.\" [Lecture 6 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)"},{"h":"How Lecture 6 got there","a":"how-lecture-6-got-there","text":"- The whiteboard is what interviewers, clients and investors ask you to draw: where does each piece live, and why? [Lecture 6 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#02-outsource-your-thinking-not-your-understanding)\n- For every piece it answers three things: what it is, whose machine it lives on, and how it talks to the others. [Lecture 6 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#07-the-high-level-design)"},{"h":"Aarav's HLD","a":"aaravs-hld","text":"- Aarav's machine: laptop (1), Chrome (2), UI (3). Your server (4) with POST /diagnose (5), the Groq key and storage (7), a file, memory.txt. Groq's machine: POST /chat/completions (6) and gpt-oss-120b. [Lecture 6 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#07-the-high-level-design)\n- Numbers name the pieces, so you can say \"5 calls 6\". Arrows are API calls: address, action, key, package. [Lecture 6 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#07-the-high-level-design)"},{"h":"Deciding where a piece lives","a":"deciding-where-a-piece-lives","text":"- Size: the LLM needs more memory than your server or Aarav's laptop has. [Lecture 6 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#03-where-should-each-piece-live)\n- Privacy and security decide too: keys and stored data live on your server. [Lecture 6 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)"},{"h":"Drawing yours","a":"drawing-yours","text":"- The practice set asks for your app's HLD: the user, an icon and a number per piece, a dashed zone per machine, every arrow an API call, and storage as a file on your server. [Lecture 6 practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md#02-draw-yours)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Storage](storage.md), [API](api.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/host-client-server.md","title":"Host, client, server","meta":"title: Host, client, server\nsummary: The three MCP roles: the AI app, the piece inside it that speaks MCP, and the program next to the data that offers tools. Lecture 11.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Host, client, server","a":"host-client-server","text":"Host, client, server: the AI app; the piece inside it that speaks MCP; the program that offers the tools. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)"},{"h":"The three roles","a":"the-three-roles","text":"- Host: the AI app Priya uses, such as Claude Desktop, Antigravity, or one you built. It holds the LLM and runs the conversation. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n- Client: a piece inside the host, one per server. It asks the server which tools it has, carries each request, brings back the result. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n- Server: the program next to the data. It offers the tools, validates, calls the Sheets API, returns the rows. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)"},{"h":"\"Find all pending orders\", end to end","a":"find-all-pending-orders-end-to-end","text":"1. Priya asks the host. She knows nothing about APIs or MCP. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n2. The LLM writes get_orders with status pending. That is function calling. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n3. The client carries the request to the Google Sheets MCP server. That is MCP. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n4. The server validates it, calls the Sheets API, and reads six orders. Three are pending. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n5. The three rows travel back through the client to the host, and the LLM writes the answer. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Mixing up the roles. The MCP server is not the spreadsheet. The MCP client is not the LLM. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md), [MCP](mcp.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/hypothesis.md","title":"Hypothesis","meta":"title: Hypothesis\nsummary: A hypothesis is your assumed cause plus a test with a number, a deadline and a stop rule. Lecture 3, with Aarav's 60% in 90 days example.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Hypothesis","a":"hypothesis","text":"A hypothesis is your assumed cause plus a test with a number, a deadline and a stop rule. Lecture 3 named it. [Lecture 3 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 3 got there","a":"how-lecture-3-got-there","text":"- Aarav's: they are stuck because they lack a method to go from broad AI possibility to one scoped workflow. [Lecture 3 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- Its test: 60% of active students identify one user, map one repeated workflow, build an AI-assisted version and demo it within 90 days. At 10 people, 6 of 10. [Lecture 3 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- The hypothesis named two solutions; the lecture tested only part one, the diagnosis, in the cheapest way. [Lecture 3 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- It tells you when to pivot. Without a measurement and a stop rule you keep spending time with no signal. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- No number: \"Noticeably faster\" cannot be falsified. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- Building while it still moves: if your hypothesis changes after every interview, do not build yet. Use Aarav's documents to learn the process. [Lecture 3 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#14-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Observation](observation.md), [Spec](spec.md), [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/index.md","title":"Index","meta":"title: Index\nsummary: Index: the labels read like a contents page, so you go straight to the right chunk. Lecture 13; page indexing.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Index","a":"index","text":"An index is \"The labels read like a contents page: go straight to the right chunk.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"How Lecture 13 derived it","a":"how-lecture-13-derived-it","text":"- Once chunks have labels, the labels also make an index. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- Like the contents page of a physics book: thermodynamics, page 22. You open page 22, not page 1. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- The LLM can read the labels the same way and go straight to the right chunk. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- Some of the newest RAG research, page indexing, is this idea. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n\nSee also: [Label](label.md), [Chunk](chunk.md), [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/input-process-output.md","title":"Input, process, output","meta":"title: Input, process, output\nsummary: Lecture 1's first principle of building anything; most blocked questions have one of the three unclear. Name your task in one line with it.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Input, process, output","a":"input-process-output","text":"\"The first principle of building anything.\" Lecture 1. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 1 got there","a":"how-lecture-1-got-there","text":"- An app that adds two numbers: input, two numbers; process, add; output, the sum. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- Know the process yourself: you cannot verify addition unless you know what it is. Expertise is the precondition. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- Define the input (two numbers, not a cat and a dog) and the output (the sum, every time). [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- The exam round had the same shape: inputs X, the pass mark, and Y, the score; process, is Y at least X; output, yes. [Lecture 1 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#10-round-two-has-someone-cleared-the-high-school-exam)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- It \"will be asked in every lecture from here\". Most blocked questions are blocked because one of the three is unclear. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- The Lecture 1 task: name your high-judgment task in one line as input, process, output. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n\nSee also: [Golden dataset](golden-dataset.md), [Verifier](verifier.md), [Lecture 1](../lectures/lecture-01.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/interface.md","title":"Interface","meta":"title: Interface\nsummary: Interface, from Lecture 2: the bridge where two separate systems meet and communicate, built from a common language plus a protocol.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Interface","a":"interface","text":"\"An interface is the boundary, bridge, or point of interaction where two separate systems, components, or entities meet and communicate.\" [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)"},{"h":"How Lecture 2 derived it","a":"how-lecture-2-derived-it","text":"- The room tried to get Siddhant, acting as a machine, to draw a smiley through the Zoom chat, and failed twice. [Lecture 2 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#03-round-one-a-hundred-programmers-one-machine-no-smiley)\n- Two things were missing between the room and the machine: a common language, and a set of rules. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)\n- Language plus protocol opens a channel that runs both ways. That bridge is an interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)"},{"h":"How to find one","a":"how-to-find-one","text":"- Name the two systems before anything else. [Lecture 2 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#07-the-letter-find-the-two-systems-first)\n- Then name the common language and the rules. The lecture applied this to a phone call, a letter, a meme, ChatGPT and two machines. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- The first interface you meet is the UI, between a human and a machine; behind it every pair of systems is joined by its own interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Naming a part as the whole. In a phone call the hardware is part of the interface, not the whole of it. [Lecture 2 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#06-the-phone-call-the-wire-is-not-the-interface)\n- For a letter, the postal service can be called an interface; the address never can. [Lecture 2 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#07-the-letter-find-the-two-systems-first)\n- The common language is not the interface. Establishing it is step one of building one. [Lecture 2 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#15-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md), [Protocol](protocol.md), [Common language](common-language.md), [User interface](user-interface.md), [API](api.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/json.md","title":"JSON","meta":"title: JSON\nsummary: A text format, not a language, for the package in a request: keys and values in curly braces. Lecture 4, with the 400 errors it causes.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"JSON","a":"json","text":"JSON is a text format, not a language: keys and values in curly braces. [Lecture 4 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"How Lecture 4 got there","a":"how-lecture-4-got-there","text":"- In Round Three the workflow sent as a plain sentence came back \"400 Can't read.\" with the hint to send `{\"workflow\": \"...\"}`. [Lecture 4 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- The Wire asks why a Python program and a JavaScript program would both accept curly braces, and shows a made-up format nobody else can read. [The Wire, rung 6](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md#rung-6-two-programs-one-package)\n- JSON dates from 2001, Douglas Crockford. [The Wire, rung 6](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md#rung-6-two-programs-one-package)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- JSON is the common language for the package; HTTP is the protocol for everything around it. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- Every programming language and every no-code tool reads it, which is why it became the web's default package format. [The Wire, rung 6](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md#rung-6-two-programs-one-package)\n- A package needs the header `Content-Type: application/json`, so the machine knows which format to read. [Request Builder, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#03-create-send-a-package)\n- In Python, `json=` turns the dictionary into JSON and adds the Content-Type header for you. [Request Builder, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#03-create-send-a-package)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- A missing comma, a missing closing quote or brace, a comma after the last field, or curly smart quotes pasted from a document give a 400. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- Field names must match exactly, in lower case: `workflow`, not `text` or `Workflow`. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- A no-code HTTP step with body type Text, Raw or Form sends a string and gets a 400; set the body type to JSON. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [Request](request.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/judgment-based-verification.md","title":"Judgment-based verification","meta":"title: Judgment-based verification\nsummary: Lecture 1: verifying by judgment, not a rule; the Rolls-Royce refund case; probabilistic systems, weeks 4 to 8; contrast with rule-based.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Judgment-based verification","a":"judgment-based-verification","text":"\"Writable and handable, but the output may vary for the same input.\" Lecture 1. Its opposite, rule-based verification, is \"Same input, same output, checkable by anyone who can read the rule.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 1 got there","a":"how-lecture-1-got-there","text":"- Round two, the exam (pass mark 33 percent, score 80 percent), was a rule-based verifier. [Lecture 1 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#10-round-two-has-someone-cleared-the-high-school-exam)\n- Round three: should a Rolls-Royce customer get a refund? About 7.5 crore in India, close to a million dollars. Output yes or no. [Lecture 1 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)\n- A learner's rule: if the cost of issues within warranty Y exceeds the car's cost X, refund. [Lecture 1 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)\n- The counter: the comparison is trivial; getting the numbers is the work. Shipped as written, the rule can be gamed. [Lecture 1 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)\n- So ask a support executive who has done refunds for ten years and answers for mistakes whether the story smells right. [Lecture 1 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)\n- Judgment has two sources: a human, or an artificial one. Some transfers to a model once written down, as Ogilvy's did. Some does not. [Lecture 1 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)"},{"h":"Where it sits in the course","a":"where-it-sits-in-the-course","text":"- By a rule: deterministic, weeks 1 to 4. By judgment: probabilistic, weeks 4 to 8. By nothing yet: human in the loop, the last 4 weeks. [Lecture 1 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)\n- Judgment verifiers are built as prompts and eval criteria in the LLM deep dive. [Lecture 1 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)\n- Build a deterministic MVP first: you control everything there, while nobody controls a probabilistic output, only its distribution. [Lecture 1 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"How Lecture 9 used it","a":"how-lecture-9-used-it","text":"- Lecture 9 calls it verification by judgment: the check needs experience, like a refund decision or a pricing strategy. [Lecture 9 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- \"When will I get a job?\" has no rule, yet a mentor who has watched many students can estimate it. [Lecture 9 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)\n- When the check needs judgment, a probabilistic system belongs in the design. [Lecture 9 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)\n- Aarav's pricing research is verified by judgment and outcome: his manager checks it at appraisal, and cost saved shows in the P&L between Q3 and Q4. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)\n- A machine can learn that judgment only with a verifier first and data second. [Lecture 9 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)\n\nSee also: [Verifier](verifier.md), [The Verifier's Law](verifiers-law.md), [Lecture 1](../lectures/lecture-01.md), [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md), [Verification by rule](verification-by-rule.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/key.md","title":"Key (primary key)","meta":"title: Key (primary key)\nsummary: Key (Lecture 6): the row's address, usually an id column, that never repeats; why a row's words cannot be its address.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Key (primary key)","a":"key-primary-key","text":"A key is \"The row's address, usually id. Never repeats.\" [Lecture 6 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)"},{"h":"How Lecture 6 derived it","a":"how-lecture-6-derived-it","text":"- Every row needs an address that never repeats. Its words cannot be it: two messages can both say \"okay\". So number the rows. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n- The number goes in a column called `id`, and that column is the row's key. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n- The whiteboard compares it to an apartment number. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#every-row-needs-an-address)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Other rows point at it: a pointer is a column holding another row's id. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- In the Modelling Lab, \"… has no key\" means no column is marked key. Add an `id` column and tick key. [Lecture 6 troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-troubleshooting.md#01-the-crosses-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Pointer](pointer.md), [Domain model](domain-model.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/label.md","title":"Label","meta":"title: Label\nsummary: Label: a tag saying where a chunk sits (module, week, lecture), so you can filter. Lecture 13: Verifier's Law and Amazon's 60,000 phones.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Label","a":"label","text":"A label is \"A tag that says where a chunk sits (module, week, lecture), so you can filter.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"How Lecture 13 derived it","a":"how-lecture-13-derived-it","text":"- Sending every chunk costs one call per chunk, so mark every chunk with where it sits. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- The first idea, lecture numbers, failed: Verifier's Law comes up in almost every lecture. The label needs what each part covers. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- Give each module a list of its words. \"Verifier's Law\" is on Module 2's list, so send only Module 2's chunks. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- Amazon does this: 60,000 phones, 2,000 delivered tomorrow by 3 pm, 23 Samsung, 7 in your budget. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- \"Label the chunks, then filter. Plenty of RAG in production is just this.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)"},{"h":"Not the keyword fix again","a":"not-the-keyword-fix-again","text":"- With the keyword fix, the LLM had to guess the one word to search for. Here it picks one of three modules, and you wrote down what each covers. [Lecture 13 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- Choosing labels needs domain knowledge. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n\nSee also: [Index](index.md), [Domain knowledge](domain-knowledge.md), [Chunk](chunk.md), [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/llm-assistant.md","title":"LLM assistant","meta":"title: LLM assistant\nsummary: The whole system around the model: client, APIs, data, tools. Lecture 10: the model has no hallucination problem; the LLM assistant does.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"LLM assistant","a":"llm-assistant","text":"An LLM assistant is \"The whole system around the model: client, APIs, data, tools.\" Lecture 10. [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"How Lecture 10 got there","a":"how-lecture-10-got-there","text":"- The question was why models hallucinate, after the track question scored 1 out of 10 with no context. [Lecture 10 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- Andrej Karpathy's framing: hallucination is all an LLM does; we only call it that when the dream lands somewhere factually wrong. [Lecture 10 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#03-why-models-hallucinate)\n- So the model has no hallucination problem; the LLM assistant does. [Lecture 10 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#03-why-models-hallucinate)\n- The assistant is the whole system, as in the ChatGPT taken apart in Lecture 2. [Lecture 10 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#03-why-models-hallucinate)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- It says where to fix a wrong answer: in the system around the model, such as the data it receives. [Lecture 10 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- If you need the correct record every time, build an orange system (client, server, database), which returns 404 instead of guessing. [Lecture 10 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#03-why-models-hallucinate)\n\nSee also: [Function calling](function-calling.md), [Context](context.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/localhost.md","title":"localhost","meta":"title: localhost\nsummary: localhost (127.0.0.1) means this machine; a link to it works only on yours, so never share one. Lecture 8.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"localhost","a":"localhost","text":"localhost is `127.0.0.1`: this machine. A link to it works only on yours. [Lecture 8 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 8 kept two environments apart: local is everything running on your laptop, production is the link a real user opens. [Lecture 8 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)\n- Running `uvicorn api.index:app --reload` serves the API at `127.0.0.1:8000`. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#local-first-then-production-only-the-address-changes)\n- Gradio then posts to `http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/diagnose`, with no key in the file. [Lecture 8 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Test a change on your laptop first, so Aarav does not pay for your test. [Lecture 8 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)\n- Go to production by changing one thing, the address: swap the local URL for your `vercel.app` URL. [Lecture 8 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Sending a `127.0.0.1` link. It opens on yours and nobody else's; anything you submit or share has to be a deployed link. [Lecture 8 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/manual-mvp.md","title":"Manual MVP","meta":"title: Manual MVP\nsummary: A manual MVP runs the input to output loop by hand before building any technology. Lecture 3, with Aarav's status report example.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Manual MVP","a":"manual-mvp","text":"A manual MVP is running the input to output loop by hand before building any technology. Lecture 3 named it. [Lecture 3 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 3 got there","a":"how-lecture-3-got-there","text":"- Golden pair 1 was Aarav's Friday status report for a client, which takes around 3 hours. [Lecture 3 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#08-two-systems-one-interface)\n- Input type: text. Output type: text. So you can run a manual MVP before building anything. [Lecture 3 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#08-two-systems-one-interface)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Take Aarav's description on a call, write the plan yourself (with or without ChatGPT), send it, and see if it saves him time. [Lecture 3 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#08-two-systems-one-interface)\n- The success test is not whether he likes the plan, but whether it saves time, say 1 hour a week. [Lecture 3 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard)\n\nSee also: [V0 / MVP](v0-mvp.md), [Spec](spec.md), [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/mcp.md","title":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","meta":"title: MCP (Model Context Protocol)\nsummary: MCP, one standard way for AI apps to find and call tools on any server: initialize, tools/list, tools/call. Lecture 11; MCP server, USB-C analogy.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","a":"mcp-model-context-protocol","text":"MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is one standard way for an AI app to find out what a server can do and ask for it. Like USB-C: one port, many devices. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 11: Priya wants Gmail, Slack and Supabase too. Each has its own way to connect, and a second AI app would rebuild all of them. That is the integration problem. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- On the Tool Bench a second app wants your tool. Step 5 moves the card to the sheet's side, and your server has to ask for it. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)"},{"h":"The four messages","a":"the-four-messages","text":"- `initialize`: Hello. Here is who I am. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n- `notifications/initialized`: I'm ready. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n- `tools/list`: What can you do? Your card comes back. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n- `tools/call`: Run this, whenever the LLM asks. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n- Any app that speaks them can use any MCP server, whatever the tool. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)"},{"h":"MCP server","a":"mcp-server","text":"- A server that keeps tools next to the data and answers `tools/list` and `tools/call` for any app. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n- The MCP server is not the spreadsheet. The MCP client is not the LLM. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)"},{"h":"What it is not","a":"what-it-is-not","text":"- MCP does not replace APIs. It is a layer over them. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- MCP does not replace function calling. The LLM still asks. MCP carries the request. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- None of API, function calling and MCP makes the answer correct. You still verify. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- In the building: the API is the door, function calling is asking for the door to be opened, MCP is the standard hallway to every door. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- Tool calling is the LLM asking. MCP is where the tool lives, so that any app can ask. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n- The value grows with other people's servers. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md), [Host, client, server](host-client-server.md), [Tool card](tool-card.md), [Protocol](protocol.md), [API](api.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/north-star.md","title":"North Star","meta":"title: North Star\nsummary: A six-month goal with four elements (timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, the specific thing). Idea to App workshop, Week 0.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"North Star","a":"north-star","text":"\"A six-month goal with four elements: timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing that bridges them.\" [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How the workshop derived it","a":"how-the-workshop-derived-it","text":"- Two North Stars were pulled apart on the whiteboard, then three from chat were reviewed live. [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- Timeline: a date, six months out. A North Star for this cohort, not for your life. [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- Role and identity: what people will call you. [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- Level, income or scale: the quantity you will have on the date, as a number. [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- The specific thing: the one workflow that bridges the role to the number. Almost everyone left this out. [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- \"The North Star defines the goal. It never explains the route.\" [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)"},{"h":"How to test it","a":"how-to-test-it","text":"- The mom test: could someone who does not know the jargon verify on the date whether it happened? [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)\n- The scale is absolute, or relative to a baseline you have already measured. [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)\n- It may evolve, never stay vague. When you reach it, rewrite it. [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- It tells you where to look for an observation. [Workshop notes, 06](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#06-aarav-at-1238-am-what-an-observation-looks-like)\n- It is step 1 of Assignment 1: one goal, not four. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- A task in place of an identity, such as \"Leading an AI-powered engineering workflow\". [Workshop notes, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- \"100x productivity\" with no baseline: nobody can check it. [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)\n- Several incentives at once. Keep one, and add the industry. [Workshop notes, 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [The mom test](the-mom-test.md), [Idea to App workshop](../lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/observation.md","title":"Observation","meta":"title: Observation\nsummary: An observation is what you saw a real person struggle with, with no solution in it. Lecture 3, with the stranger test and common mistakes.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Observation","a":"observation","text":"An observation is what you saw a real person struggle with, from lived experience, with no solution in it. Lecture 3 named it. [Lecture 3 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 3 got there","a":"how-lecture-3-got-there","text":"- Aarav's observation: early career professionals arrive AI-aware and motivated, but fail at selection, scoping and first proof. [Lecture 3 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- It contains no solution: an observation reports what you saw, and stops. [Lecture 3 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- The move: do not solve for a category; solve for one named person. [Lecture 3 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- It is the first line of the spec, before the hypothesis, user, input and output. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- You are not married to one: until the Assignment 1 deadline (9 October), try more observations and speak to more people. [Lecture 3 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0)\n- The practice set asks for one readable by a stranger. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#02-track-a-your-own-problem-preferred-0-of-6-ready)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Too generic: an organization is not a person. Name the one person who hits this most often, with their designation, and write from their point of view. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- Solution first: \"My project is building WhatsApp agents\" leaves nothing to decide by. Start from observation, input, output and process. [Lecture 3 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#14-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Hypothesis](hypothesis.md), [Spec](spec.md), [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/pointer.md","title":"Pointer (foreign key)","meta":"title: Pointer (foreign key)\nsummary: Pointer (Lecture 6): a column on the many side holding another row's id; the three link shapes and the third table for many-to-many.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Pointer (foreign key)","a":"pointer-foreign-key","text":"A pointer is \"A column on the many side holding another row's id.\" The formal word is foreign key. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)"},{"h":"How Lecture 6 derived it","a":"how-lecture-6-derived-it","text":"- To find a link's shape, ask two questions both ways: can one conversation have many messages? Yes. Can one message sit in many conversations? No. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Why the many side? A cell holds one value. Each message is in one conversation, so `conversation_id = 7` on a message means \"I belong to conversation 7\". [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n- The rule to carry: \"the many side points back\". [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#how-do-they-connect-three-shapes)"},{"h":"Three shapes","a":"three-shapes","text":"- One-to-many: most links. Many-to-many: both answers yes, so a third table. One-to-one: both no, rare, often one table. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Third table: a workflow uses many tools and Jira appears in many workflows, so `workflow_tools` holds one row per pairing. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n- Facts about the pairing, such as how often, live on that row. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- \"… points the wrong way\": the one side carries a pointer, such as a `pet_id` on owners. Remove it; the many side already carries the link. [Lecture 6 troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-troubleshooting.md#01-the-crosses-you-should-expect)\n- \"… should be a pointer to …\": a name alone does not make a link. Tick points to and pick the table. [Lecture 6 troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-troubleshooting.md#01-the-crosses-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Key](key.md), [Domain model](domain-model.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/postgresql.md","title":"PostgreSQL","meta":"title: PostgreSQL\nsummary: The free, open-source SQL database Supabase runs; Lecture 8 moved storage from memory.txt to Postgres tables on Supabase.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"PostgreSQL","a":"postgresql","text":"PostgreSQL is the free, open-source SQL database Supabase runs. [Lecture 8 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"Why Lecture 8 picked it","a":"why-lecture-8-picked-it","text":"- SQL-type, because your model maps straight onto it and it is easy to query. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n- It is what OpenAI runs on at 800 million users. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n- You can host it yourself and run the maintenance. With ten users, rent. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n- Supabase is to Postgres what Vercel is to servers: free up to 50,000 monthly active users and 500 MB. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)"},{"h":"How it is used","a":"how-it-is-used","text":"- A database is another machine at an address that answers the four dials: address, action, key, package. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#a-database-is-a-machine-at-an-address)\n- SQL connects Python to Postgres, and `schema.sql` is the JSON schema of the database. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- Types are the traffic police again: send a picture into a `text` column and the database refuses. SQL types are not Python types. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- Groq thinks. Supabase remembers. Your server is a client to both. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#a-database-is-a-machine-at-an-address)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Letting an LLM tangle storage and business logic. Four file lines became one insert and `diagnose` did not change; tell your LLM this up front. [Lecture 8 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md), [RLS](rls.md), [Race condition](race-condition.md), [SDK](sdk.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/protocol.md","title":"Protocol","meta":"title: Protocol\nsummary: Protocol, from Lecture 2: the rules both sides of an interface follow; who speaks, in what order, in what form. HTTP is the web's.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Protocol","a":"protocol","text":"A protocol is the rules both sides follow: who speaks, in what order, in what form. [Lecture 2 notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 2 derived it","a":"how-lecture-2-derived-it","text":"- In the smiley game the rules covered only the machine: one action per message, in order, no guessing. Nothing told the humans how to behave. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)\n- The winning version: pick one point of contact; everyone else sends suggestions to that person, and only they write in the chat. The agreed rules are a protocol. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)\n- Language plus protocol makes an interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)"},{"h":"Examples from the lecture","a":"examples-from-the-lecture","text":"- Phone call: one says hello, the other replies, each works out why the other called. [Lecture 2 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#06-the-phone-call-the-wire-is-not-the-interface)\n- Letter: envelope, address, post office, stamp, fee, wait. [Lecture 2 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#07-the-letter-find-the-two-systems-first)\n- The web: HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol), built by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, the rules for moving pages. [Lecture 2 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)\n- One HTTP rule is built in: every request stands on its own, so the receiver needs no backstory. [Lecture 2 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Writing rules for only one side. In the smiley game the rules covered only the machine. [Lecture 2 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md), [Interface](interface.md), [Common language](common-language.md), [API](api.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/pydantic.md","title":"Pydantic","meta":"title: Pydantic\nsummary: Pydantic, from Lecture 7: the data validation library FastAPI uses, the traffic police for everything that arrives at your route.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Pydantic","a":"pydantic","text":"Pydantic is \"The data validation library FastAPI uses. The traffic police for everything that arrives.\" [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"How Lecture 7 derived it","a":"how-lecture-7-derived-it","text":"- Policy versus enforcement: wearing a helmet is policy, the traffic police on the road is enforcement. Without the police, the rule is a wish. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Your route is a promise to the whole internet. Someone will send an image, a video, nothing, and deterministic code throws. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- `WorkflowIn` says the package must have a `workflow` and it must be text. Anything else is rejected with an error that names the rule broken. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)"},{"h":"Why it matters beyond APIs","a":"why-it-matters-beyond-apis","text":"- Lecture 1 said an interface is a common language plus rules. HTTP and JSON are the language; this is the first rule you enforce. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- The same layer sits between an agent and your software, because agents are predictive and can be social-engineered. [Lecture 7 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- When testing the live route, the field name must match `WorkflowIn` exactly. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n\nSee also: [FastAPI](fastapi.md), [Interface](interface.md), [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/race-condition.md","title":"Race condition","meta":"title: Race condition\nsummary: Two writers at once, and one write is lost; why memory.txt breaks once several people write. Lecture 8.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Race condition","a":"race-condition","text":"A race condition is two writers at once, and one write is lost. [Lecture 8 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"How Lecture 8 got there","a":"how-lecture-8-got-there","text":"- Step 05 saved plans to `memory.txt`: read the whole file, add one line, write it all back, because `\"w\"` replaces the whole file. [Lecture 8 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#05-step-05--memorytxt-in-four-steps)\n- Two learners posted at the same minute. Both requests got a 200, but the file held one plan. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#100-users-60000-lines-and-two-writers-at-once)\n- Each request read the same old file and wrote its own version back, so whoever wrote last erased the other. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#100-users-60000-lines-and-two-writers-at-once)\n- The server never lied. The file just cannot hold two writers. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#100-users-60000-lines-and-two-writers-at-once)"},{"h":"What to do about it","a":"what-to-do-about-it","text":"- One user, under about ten operations a day, a fixed window: a file is fine. More than one user writing: think database. [Lecture 8 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- Read-only scales on a file: nobody writes, so nothing races. [Lecture 8 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- Writing is the expensive part. A database is a file system with fifty years of fixes. [Lecture 8 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Do not reach for a database by reflex; the need starts when your machine cannot keep up: many writers, 500s, lost lines. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#a-file-is-enough-until-people-write-at-once)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md), [PostgreSQL](postgresql.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/rate-limit.md","title":"Rate limit","meta":"title: Rate limit\nsummary: Requests allowed per minute or day; Groq free tier is 30 per minute, roughly 200,000 tokens and 1,000 requests a day (Lecture 5).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Rate limit","a":"rate-limit","text":"A rate limit is \"Requests allowed per minute or day. Groq free: 30 per minute.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 5 called it \"Free Is a Design Decision\". [Lecture 5 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#09-free-is-a-design-decision)\n- Groq's free tier: 30 requests per minute, roughly 200,000 tokens and 1,000 requests a day. [Lecture 5 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#09-free-is-a-design-decision)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- With a per-user quota, that powers a real MVP for one or two users at zero cost. [Lecture 5 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#09-free-is-a-design-decision)\n- Stay inside the free tier: \"You will not need more.\" [Practice set, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#06-rules-of-the-build)"},{"h":"When you hit it","a":"when-you-hit-it","text":"- 429 Too many requests: more than 30 requests in a minute on your Groq account. Wait a minute. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/repository.md","title":"Repository, commit and push","meta":"title: Repository, commit and push\nsummary: Repository, commit and push, from Lecture 7: a project on GitHub, a save of your progress, and sending saves up. Keep it public.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Repository, commit and push","a":"repository-commit-and-push","text":"A repository is \"A project on GitHub. All its files, and every save you made.\" [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"How Lecture 7 put it","a":"how-lecture-7-put-it","text":"- *Commit* is saving your progress, like a save file in a game. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n- *Push* sends your saves to the repository on the internet. You can commit many times, then push once. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n- A commit is local until you push. [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Where your code lives on the internet, and how you share it for review. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n- A change log: when the first run broke on a retired model name, the fix showed up as one commit, the old model name against the new. [Lecture 7 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)\n- The project lives in three places: build on the laptop, push to GitHub, GitHub connects to Vercel. [Lecture 7 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#10-from-the-chat)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- A private repo cannot be reviewed. Keep the hackathon repo public. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n- You do not have to memorise the commands. Understand commit and push, and let your LLM desktop app run the terminal. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md), [Put your project on GitHub](../how-to/put-your-project-on-github.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/request.md","title":"Request and response","meta":"title: Request and response\nsummary: An HTTP API request carries four things (address, action, key, package); the reply carries two (code, package). Derived in Lecture 4 Round Three.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Request and response","a":"request-and-response","text":"Every request between two machines carries four things: an address, an action, a key, and a package in a format both sides agree on. Every reply carries two: a code and a package. [Lecture 4 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"How Lecture 4 got there","a":"how-lecture-4-got-there","text":"- Nobody listed these parts before Round Three; the machine's replies did, one at a time. [Lecture 4 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- Everything on that envelope together is an HTTP API request. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)"},{"h":"The parts and their web names","a":"the-parts-and-their-web-names","text":"- Address: the URL, or endpoint. The domain names the machine; the path names the door. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- Action: the method, POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- Key: a header, `Authorization: Bearer <key>`; Bearer means whoever bears this key is let in. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- Package: the body, written in JSON. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- Code: the status code. Package back: the response body. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- A header is a line written on the envelope, read without opening the letter. [Lecture 4 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Why these parts exist","a":"why-these-parts-exist","text":"- Each part is there because an earlier system failed: TCP/IP gave the address, HTTP gave the action, the key and the code, REST gave nouns and CRUD, and JSON gave the package. [Lecture 4 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#06-optional-deep-dive-why-these-six-parts)\n- HTTPS is HTTP in a sealed envelope, so nobody on the way can read your key; that was the DM in Round Three. [The Wire, rung 4](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md#rung-4-delivered-but-what-do-you-want)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Input, Process, Output: the request is the input, the machine at the other end is the process, the reply is the output. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- No-code tools ask for the same boxes: URL, Method, a header named Authorization, and a JSON body. [Practice set, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#03-code-track-and-no-code-track)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- A Read sends no package. [Request Builder, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#02-read-your-first-request)\n- An address cannot hold a space, so it becomes `%20`; a raw space in an address is a classic 400. [Request Builder, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#02-read-your-first-request)\n- Keys go on the envelope, never in the address: anyone can read an address. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [CRUD](crud.md), [Status code](status-code.md), [JSON](json.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","meta":"title: Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)\nsummary: RAG: retrieve, augment, generate. Derived in Lecture 13 from the tool call; plain tool call vs RAG; cost.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","a":"retrieval-augmented-generation-rag","text":"RAG means: \"Fetch the data that matters, add it to the prompt, let the LLM write.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"How Lecture 13 derived it","a":"how-lecture-13-derived-it","text":"- The question \"When is Lecture 12?\" went through a tool that reads the lectures sheet. [Lecture 13 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- The room named each step: 1 Retrieval (the tool reads the sheet), 2 Augmentation (the server adds it to the prompt), 3 Generation (the LLM writes the answer). [Lecture 13 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- \"Nothing new was taught. You derived RAG from what you had already built.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- Swap the sheet for Supabase and step 1 is still retrieval. [Lecture 13 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)"},{"h":"Tool call or RAG?","a":"tool-call-or-rag","text":"- A plain tool call already retrieves, augments and generates, so in principle it is RAG. [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- People usually mean that plus the tricks for when a plain fetch is not enough: chunks, labels, an index, SQL. [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- \"If it fits and the bill is fine, a plain tool call is enough. If not, send the fewest tokens that still answer.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Thinking the context window is the only reason to go past a plain tool call: most teams hit the budget first. [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Thinking a bigger LLM removes the need for chunks: it moves the line, and you pay for every token you send. [Lecture 13 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md), [Chunk](chunk.md), [Label](label.md), [Index](index.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/rls.md","title":"RLS (row level security)","meta":"title: RLS (row level security)\nsummary: Row level security: a policy in SQL on which rows each user can read or write, checked against auth.uid(). Lecture 8.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"RLS (row level security)","a":"rls-row-level-security","text":"RLS is row level security: a policy on which rows each user can read or write. [Lecture 8 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"How Lecture 8 got there","a":"how-lecture-8-got-there","text":"- Types are enforced by the database already. Who can read which row is not. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n- Mira joins. If she finds the URL and a key, one `select *` returns Aarav's workflows too. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n- The policy, written in SQL: you see only the rows where `auth.uid()` matches the row's owner. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n- Pydantic was policy plus enforcement for the API. RLS is the same for rows. [Lecture 8 whiteboard, 19](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#whose-row-is-this)"},{"h":"What it needs","a":"what-it-needs","text":"- Login: in Supabase's Authentication tab, start with the email provider. Google, Apple and the rest come with third-party costs. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n- Each signed-up user gets a UID in a separate auth table, and RLS checks it. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n- The publishable key is safe in a browser only once row level security is on. [Lecture 8 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#08-the-same-four-dials)"},{"h":"Common questions","a":"common-questions","text":"- Access or injection? Both: an injected query without a matching UID gets nothing. [Lecture 8 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#12-from-the-chat)\n- It is advanced for now: store first, then RLS for logged-in users, then deeper auth. [Lecture 8 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md), [PostgreSQL](postgresql.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/sdk.md","title":"SDK","meta":"title: SDK\nsummary: A provider's code wrapped around address, action, key and package; Lecture 5 replaced the Groq SDK with a raw requests.post.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"SDK","a":"sdk","text":"An SDK is \"A provider's code wrapped around address, action, key and package.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- In Lecture 5, the SDK code that worked hid the URL and POST behind abstraction. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- Groq's quickstart uses `pip install groq`, export `GROQ_API_KEY` and a short script. [Lecture 5 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#08-go-to-the-source-and-read-the-404)"},{"h":"What to use instead, and why","a":"what-to-use-instead-and-why","text":"- The raw `requests.post` version is provider-agnostic: no new package, no dependence on anyone's SDK. [Lecture 5 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#15-any-machine-four-lines)\n- The practice set asks for no Groq or OpenAI SDK in the final file. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Groq's own quickstart failed as pasted with Error 404, because its model name did not exist. [Lecture 5 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#08-go-to-the-source-and-read-the-404)\n\nSee also: [Abstraction](abstraction.md), [Endpoint](endpoint.md), [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/spec.md","title":"Spec","meta":"title: Spec\nsummary: The spec is observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers. Lecture 3; posted in the code track channel.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Spec","a":"spec","text":"A spec is observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers. Lecture 3 named it. [Lecture 3 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 3 got there","a":"how-lecture-3-got-there","text":"- Test-driven development says write the test before the software; now most of your tests will be in English. The spec holds those tests. [Lecture 3 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#07-test-driven-development-written-in-english)\n- A learner's spec was marked live: observation too generic, hypothesis with no number, input and output good. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Sharing it is what makes feedback possible. Mask anything proprietary (percentages, or X, Y, Z) but share the spec. [Lecture 3 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#07-test-driven-development-written-in-english)\n- The format to post in the code track channel: observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, input, output. [Lecture 3 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- In the practice set: North Star is 6 months, a direction not a product; one named user with their designation. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#02-track-a-your-own-problem-preferred-0-of-6-ready)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Asking which interface to build before writing the spec: there is no answer without it. Walk your user's journey first. [Lecture 3 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#14-from-the-qa)\n\nSee also: [Observation](observation.md), [Hypothesis](hypothesis.md), [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/status-code.md","title":"Status code","meta":"title: Status code\nsummary: The three digits on every reply (2xx worked, 4xx yours, 5xx theirs) and which part of a request each code points to. Lecture 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Status code","a":"status-code","text":"A status code is the three digits on the reply. 2xx worked, 4xx yours, 5xx theirs. [Lecture 4 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"How Lecture 4 got there","a":"how-lecture-4-got-there","text":"- In Lecture 2 the machine stayed silent; in Round Three it answered every message with a number and at most five words, so every failure carried information. [Lecture 4 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- The codes in the game were 429, 404, 400, 405, 401 and 201, and on the web they are called the status code. [Lecture 4 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)"},{"h":"Which part each code points to","a":"which-part-each-code-points-to","text":"- 404 points at the address, 405 at the action, 401 at the key, 400 at the package, and 429 means too many requests. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)\n- A 5xx is their machine, not yours; `/aarav/broken` returns a 500 on purpose for practice. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 201 means something new was created, such as your key at Aarav's. [Request Builder, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#04-round-three-for-real)\n- Aarav's machine checks the parts in order: address, then action, then key, then package; the first part that fails is the only error you get. [Lecture 4 kit, 3](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-kit.md#3-aaravs-doors)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Reading a failure: the code names the part of your request to look at. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)\n- The practice set's break-it table: for 400, 401, 404, 405 and the 500, what you changed, the code you got, and the part it points to. [Practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready)\n- In your own API design, each reply lists the success code and at least one failure code. [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#04-deliverable-5-design-your-api)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- A 200 means your request was fine, not that you got what you wanted: a READ before any diagnosis existed came back 200 with an empty list. [Lecture 4 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- A CORS block has no status code, because no request reached the machine. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [Fix a failed request](../how-to/fix-a-failed-request.md), [Request](request.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/storage.md","title":"Storage","meta":"title: Storage\nsummary: Storage (Lecture 6): anything that keeps things and gives them back; memory.txt first, on your server, storing only what the app needs next time.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Storage","a":"storage","text":"Storage is \"Anything that keeps things and gives them back: a file, a sheet, a database.\" [Lecture 6 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)"},{"h":"How Lecture 6 derived it","a":"how-lecture-6-derived-it","text":"- Aarav's app forgets: on Tuesday the model has no idea which plan he means. Every request starts from nothing. [Lecture 6 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#04-aaravs-app-forgets)\n- Memory dies when the app stops. Storage sits outside the app, on disk, and keeps things after the app is gone. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#memory-dies-storage-survives)\n- The class solved it without saying \"database\": keep a `memory.txt`, write down who said what with the date and time after every reply, and send the file with the next prompt. [Lecture 6 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- Claude Code remembers a project through one file, CLAUDE.md. [Lecture 6 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- Storage with a shape, eight tables and their pointers kept outside the app, is a database. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#storage-with-a-shape-has-a-name)"},{"h":"Where it lives","a":"where-it-lives","text":"- On your server, not the user's machine: the user can wipe it and anyone can read it. [Lecture 6 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)\n- The server reaches the file through the file system (read, write, edit), and that is an interface too. [Lecture 6 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)"},{"h":"What goes in it","a":"what-goes-in-it","text":"- \"the least you need to carry on next time\". Write the questions your app must answer tomorrow; whatever they need is worth storing. [Lecture 6 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#08-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Store too much and you pay for it, or get sued for it. Store too little and the app forgets. [Lecture 6 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#08-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Bytes such as a screenshot go in file storage, and a row keeps the link. [Lecture 6 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#11-two-rules-that-save-you-later)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- \"Paying for a database to solve a file-sized problem is the expensive kind of wrong.\" Start with the file and move up only when it breaks. [Lecture 6 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Domain model](domain-model.md), [High-level design](high-level-design.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/temperature.md","title":"Temperature","meta":"title: Temperature\nsummary: Controls randomness in the model's output. Lecture 10: at 0 it still hallucinated; lowering it doesn't supply missing context.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Temperature","a":"temperature","text":"Temperature \"Controls randomness in the output. Lowering it doesn't supply missing context.\" Lecture 10. [Lecture 10 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"How Lecture 10 got there","a":"how-lecture-10-got-there","text":"- It was tried as one of two fixes for hallucination, next to a better prompt. [Lecture 10 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone)\n- At 0 with the full-context prompt, the model still hallucinated: a deterministic wrong answer is still wrong. [Lecture 10 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone)\n- Low temperature plus a system prompt is untested; the lecture asks you to run it and report back. [Lecture 10 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Treating it as a cure. \"Any fix you hear is a hypothesis until your own test says otherwise.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone)\n\nSee also: [Context](context.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/the-loop.md","title":"The loop","meta":"title: The loop\nsummary: The server repeating model call, tool run, hand back, until the LLM answers or the server stops it; up to three rounds on the Tool Bench. Lecture 11.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The loop","a":"the-loop","text":"The loop is the server repeating model call, tool run, hand back, until the LLM answers or the server stops it. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 11's Tool Bench: first you carry one tool call by hand. The LLM writes a request and stops; you press Run, and the server fetches row 12. [Bench notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#03-carry-one-tool-call-by-hand)\n- Then you flip By hand to On. Every button you pressed is now a rule in the server. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n- The notes give the same steps as send, decide, request, execute, return, answer. [Lecture 11 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#07-function-calling-the-llm-asks)"},{"h":"How it runs","a":"how-it-runs","text":"- The server sends the card, runs whatever the LLM asks for, hands back the result, and repeats until the LLM answers, up to three rounds. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n- The server decides when the loop stops. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n- The result goes back in a message with `\"role\": \"tool\"`, with the whole conversation again. [Bench notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#03-carry-one-tool-call-by-hand)\n- Try Lecture 40: the server returns an error, and the LLM passes that on instead of inventing one. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n- The code track gets the loop in Python with its own card in it. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Expecting the same output every time. It predicts, so it can wobble: sometimes it answers without asking, or writes a call Groq can't read. Ask again. [Bench notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#03-carry-one-tool-call-by-hand)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md), [Execution record](execution-record.md), [Tool card](tool-card.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/the-mom-test.md","title":"The mom test","meta":"title: The mom test\nsummary: Two uses from the Idea to App workshop: checking a North Star someone else can verify, and The Mom Test book's four interview rules.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The mom test","a":"the-mom-test","text":"The workshop used the name twice: a test for a North Star, and the four rules of Rob Fitzpatrick's book *The Mom Test*. [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"For a North Star","a":"for-a-north-star","text":"- \"Can someone else verify on the date whether it happened? Absolute number, or relative to a measured baseline.\" [Workshop notes, 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Send the sentence to your mother, or anyone older who does not know the jargon. [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)\n- 1,000 MRR passes: it is a bank statement or an invoice. \"100x productivity\" fails: there is no number for today. [Workshop notes, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)"},{"h":"For interviews (the book)","a":"for-interviews-the-book","text":"- Talk about their life, not your idea. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n- Ask about the past, not the future. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n- Listen for specifics, not generalities. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n- Dig on emotions, not opinions. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n- Phase 6 of discovery-to-mvp-coach practises this on a simulated person, then grades you; you leave with four or five questions for a real conversation. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Counting the simulation as research. Real interviews are mandatory: 2 to 5 people minimum. [Workshop notes, 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n\nSee also: [North Star](north-star.md), [Assignment 1](../how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/token-budget.md","title":"Token budget","meta":"title: Token budget\nsummary: Lecture 14: the most tokens one question may send; 28,801 tokens of notes fit the window but not a free Groq key's 8,000 a minute.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Token budget","a":"token-budget","text":"The token budget is \"The most tokens one question may send.\" Lecture 14. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"How Lecture 14 got there","a":"how-lecture-14-got-there","text":"- The guess: paste all 183 passages of the notes with one question. What comes back? Groq turns the request down. [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- Count the tokens with a for loop: the notes come to 28,801 tokens. [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- The context window of GPT-OSS 120B is about 128,000 tokens, so they fit. \"The window is not what stopped it.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- A free Groq key takes 8,000 tokens a minute, and one question with all 183 passages asks for 28,801. [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- It decides how much you send: \"You do not reach for RAG only when the window is full. The budget decides.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- A paid key raises it, but every question still pays for every token you send. [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- You pick top-k, how many passages you keep, to fit your budget. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Asked at Cursor and Moonshot AI why build RAG when windows hold a million tokens, most answers said cost and speed. Better: say what you hit, the 28,801 tokens against 8,000 a minute. [Lecture 14 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n\nSee also: [Rate limit](rate-limit.md), [Golden set](golden-set.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/tool-card.md","title":"Tool card","meta":"title: Tool card\nsummary: The description of one tool (name, when to use it, what it needs), the only thing the LLM knows about a tool. Lecture 11 Tool Bench.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Tool card","a":"tool-card","text":"A tool card is the description of one tool: its name, when to use it, and what it needs. The LLM reads only this. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Lecture 11's Tool Bench gave the LLM the question \"When is Lecture 12 of 100xEngineers Cohort 8, and what is it about?\" plus a ready-made tool card; instead of answering, the LLM sent back a request. [Bench notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#03-carry-one-tool-call-by-hand)\n- The bench card has three plain fields: Name, Use it when, It needs. The server sends them to the LLM as JSON: name, description, parameters. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- The notes call the same contract the tool schema: name, description, parameters, required fields, types. [Lecture 11 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#08-the-tool-schema)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- The LLM picks tools by reading the description. [Lecture 11 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#08-the-tool-schema)\n- A tool card is an interface written in English: a common language plus rules, as in Lecture 2. The tests are how you know it holds. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- Move the card next to the data and any app can use it with one address: that is MCP. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)"},{"h":"How well cards scored","a":"how-well-cards-scored","text":"- Empty card: 3 out of 5 tests passed. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- \"Gets a lecture by number\": 4 or 5. It calls the tool for the CS50 question too. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- A card that says what it returns, when to use it, when not to, and what the number is: 5, every run. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Writing the card before marking what each question should do. Mark the tests first. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- Trusting the card alone. The schema is like a form; the server still checks what was filled in before it runs anything. [Lecture 11 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#08-the-tool-schema)\n- Expecting wording to fix everything. Some routing no wording fixes reliably; then the server has to check. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md), [MCP](mcp.md), [Write a tool card against tests](../how-to/write-a-tool-card-against-tests.md), [Interface](interface.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/tool.md","title":"Tool","meta":"title: Tool\nsummary: A tool is a function your code runs when the model asks for it. Named in Lecture 12 with get_lectures(track) on the curriculum sheet.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Tool","a":"tool","text":"A tool is a function your code runs when the model asks for it. [Lecture 12 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)"},{"h":"Where it came from","a":"where-it-came-from","text":"- Asked which track to choose, the model on its own makes up the answer. [Lecture 12 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#one-tool-call-then-an-mcp-server)\n- Lecture 12 wrote a plain Python function, `get_lectures(track)`, that reads one track's tab of the curriculum sheet and returns each lecture with its tools. [Lecture 12 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function)\n- The function alone has no model: same track in, same list out, every time. [Lecture 12 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function)"},{"h":"How it is used","a":"how-it-is-used","text":"- The model can't see your function. It reads a card that describes it: a name, what it does, and what to pass in. [Lecture 12 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#02-step-1-the-card)\n- The model replies with a tool call; your code runs the function and sends the result back. [Lecture 12 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#03-step-1-the-tool-call)\n- The model never touched the sheet. It wrote a request. Your code did the work. [Lecture 12 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#03-step-1-the-tool-call)\n- On an MCP server the function is the same; only where it lives changes. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- Your first own tool is the thing the model was missing when you tested one row in Practice set 1, step 04. [Lecture 12 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [FastMCP](fastmcp.md), [Tool card](tool-card.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/user-interface.md","title":"User interface (GUI, UI)","meta":"title: User interface (GUI, UI)\nsummary: GUI, UI, from Lecture 2: the interface between a user and a machine, made of graphics; in ChatGPT the whole screen. The first interface.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"User interface (GUI, UI)","a":"user-interface-gui-ui","text":"The interface between a user and a machine, made of graphics you click, type into and touch, is a graphical user interface, now simply the user interface (UI). [Lecture 2 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface)"},{"h":"How Lecture 2 derived it","a":"how-lecture-2-derived-it","text":"- For ChatGPT, name the two systems first: a human and an LLM. Input: the prompt. Output: the reply. Common language: English. [Lecture 2 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface)\n- In ChatGPT, the whole screen is the UI. This is the first interface. [Lecture 2 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface)\n- In the smiley game, the Zoom chat was the UI. [Lecture 2 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)"},{"h":"Where it sits","a":"where-it-sits","text":"- The boxes: the machine, the browser, the UI, then the company's machine. [Lecture 2 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#11-the-boxes-where-the-ui-actually-sits)\n- The operating system is the interface into the machine; the browser is an application, not the UI. [Lecture 2 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#11-the-boxes-where-the-ui-actually-sits)\n- A user never touches the UI directly: keystroke, operating system, browser, app, and only then a UI. [Lecture 2 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#11-the-boxes-where-the-ui-actually-sits)\n- Layers below the UI decide what the UI can do. [Lecture 2 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#15-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Thinking the screen is all of ChatGPT. It is only the part that talks to you. [Lecture 2 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#10-behind-the-screen-the-inspect-tool)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md), [Interface](interface.md), [API](api.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/v0-mvp.md","title":"V0 / MVP","meta":"title: V0 / MVP\nsummary: The V0 or MVP is the cheapest build that can test the hypothesis, and nothing more. Lecture 3: search, form or chat, picked by replay.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"V0 / MVP","a":"v0--mvp","text":"The V0, or MVP, is the cheapest build that can test the hypothesis, and nothing more. Lecture 3 named it. [Lecture 3 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 3 got there","a":"how-lecture-3-got-there","text":"- The room voted search box 17, chat 13, form 3. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- Replaying the golden output, Aarav asked \"What does backtesting mean?\" A search box has no memory and no context, so chat won. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- Rule of thumb: about 90% of first problems fit one of three. One query, one answer: search. Many inputs: form. Follow-ups that need memory: chat. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)"},{"h":"What it is for","a":"what-it-is-for","text":"- Build the cheapest one first and add nothing until it is verified. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- In Gradio the chat starts as an echo: `gr.ChatInterface(fn=respond)`. [Lecture 3 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)"},{"h":"Common mistakes","a":"common-mistakes","text":"- Picking the interface before the replay: pick it after you replay the user's conversation with your ideal output, never before. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n\nSee also: [Manual MVP](manual-mvp.md), [Spec](spec.md), [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/vector-database.md","title":"Vector database","meta":"title: Vector database\nsummary: Lecture 14: a database built to find the closest embeddings among millions; 183 passages fit a 281 KB file, 100 million need about 154 GB.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Vector database","a":"vector-database","text":"A vector database is \"A database built to find the closest embeddings among millions, fast.\" Lecture 14. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"How Lecture 14 got there","a":"how-lecture-14-got-there","text":"- Asked at Cohere: \"Do you need a vector database?\" The better answer: it depends, and you cannot say until you know the data and its golden set. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- First decision: do you need embeddings at all? Keyword search found 5 of 10; if users type the exact words, that may be enough. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Second decision: where do the numbers live? 183 passages, each 384 numbers at 4 bytes a number, is about 281 KB. A plain file holds that. That is what the lab does. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"When you need one","a":"when-you-need-one","text":"- At 100 million passages the numbers come to about 154 GB, and you need a database that finds the closest without reading every row. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Start with the Postgres you already have: its pgvector add-on stores and searches these numbers, and Supabase includes it. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Move to a dedicated vector database only when a real limit says so: a file too big, a search too slow. [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Mixing up the numbers and their store: \"The embedding is the numbers. A vector database is only where you keep them.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n\nSee also: [Embedding](embedding.md), [Golden set](golden-set.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/verification-by-rule.md","title":"Verification by rule","meta":"title: Verification by rule\nsummary: Lecture 9: the output can be checked by writing rules, like 1 + 2 = 3; you can use an LLM but do not need one.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Verification by rule","a":"verification-by-rule","text":"The output can be checked by writing rules, like 1 + 2 = 3. Lecture 9. [Lecture 9 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"How Lecture 9 got there","a":"how-lecture-9-got-there","text":"- An app that adds two numbers: 1 + 2 is always 3. You can use an LLM; you do not need one. [Lecture 9 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)\n- Its opposite came from \"When will I get a job?\": no database holds the answer, no template produces it. [Lecture 9 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)\n- Aarav's first Monday task, collecting last quarter's data, is verifiable by rule: total sales minus Q1 and Q2 must equal the Q3 figure collected. [Lecture 9 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- Start left of the line. Move a step into the purple box only when you cannot verify its output with rules. [Lecture 9 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#02-why-the-second-mountain-is-steeper)\n- Given two equal solutions, the rule-based one wins: reproducible, cheaper, predictable at scale. [Lecture 9 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#02-why-the-second-mountain-is-steeper)\n- In Practice set 1: if a script or formula can produce it, no LLM. [Practice set 1, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#03-rules)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Reaching for the LLM first, even for jobs it is bad at, like storing information. [Lecture 9 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#02-why-the-second-mountain-is-steeper)\n\nSee also: [Judgment-based verification](judgment-based-verification.md), [Verifier](verifier.md), [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/verifier.md","title":"Verifier","meta":"title: Verifier\nsummary: A written judgment of how you decide an output is good, usable without you. Lecture 1: Ogilvy's 15 criteria, the stranger test, rule vs judgment.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Verifier","a":"verifier","text":"\"A written judgment of how you decide an output is good, usable by a stranger or a model without you.\" Lecture 1. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"How Lecture 1 showed it","a":"how-lecture-1-showed-it","text":"- In 1983 David Ogilvy wrote down every rule behind how he judged copy; that book is why Ogilvy & Mather runs without him. [Lecture 1 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n- For the first 100x website, one prompt scored copy out of 100 on 15 Ogilvy-inspired criteria. [Lecture 1 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n- Give the verifier, set the threshold, and let the model score, improve and re-score: that is an agent, and designing it is loop engineering. [Lecture 1 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n- The verify game's rule: write a criterion you could hand to a stranger or a model so they can verify the thing without you. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)"},{"h":"Two kinds, plus none","a":"two-kinds-plus-none","text":"- By a rule: same input, same output, checkable by anyone who can read the rule. Example: has someone cleared the exam? [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- By judgment: writable and handable, but the output may vary for the same input. Example: the refund case. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- By nothing yet: human in the loop. [Lecture 1 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)"},{"h":"The test","a":"the-test","text":"- The stranger test: hand your pairs and rule to someone outside your field. If they cannot verify an output without asking you, \"it is not a verifier yet\". [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- \"Do not ask the LLM to write your verifier.\" Start with the task in your own job where you spend the most time judging. [Lecture 1 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n\nSee also: [The Verifier's Law](verifiers-law.md), [Golden dataset](golden-dataset.md), [Judgment-based verification](judgment-based-verification.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/verifiers-law.md","title":"The Verifier's Law","meta":"title: The Verifier's Law\nsummary: Lecture 1's law: if you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system or claim the capability. Why the 2% editor project failed.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The Verifier's Law","a":"the-verifiers-law","text":"\"If you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system, and you cannot claim the capability.\" Lecture 1 named it. [Lecture 1 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)"},{"h":"How Lecture 1 got there","a":"how-lecture-1-got-there","text":"- A 2023 project for a newspaper's editors saved 2% of time after eight months, against a target of 10 percent of P&L. [Lecture 1 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#02-one-sentence-from-an-editor-2023)\n- Four easy fixes failed: a better model, better prompts, more data, more time. None sat where the failure was. [Lecture 1 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#04-killing-the-four-easy-answers)\n- What was missing: only the editors knew what a good article looked like, and nobody had written it down. [Lecture 1 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)\n- So checking the AI draft cost as much as writing it, and writing it yourself was rational. [Lecture 1 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- It explains why domain expertise is valuable in 2026: \"the expert is the only source of the verifier\". [Lecture 1 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)\n- It explains the gap between models and money: \"It is not a technology problem. Most experts have never written down how they judge, so every output routes through them.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#07-follow-the-money)\n- The question to ask every time: \"how do I verify whether this output is correct? No answer, no build.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Asking the LLM to write your verifier. \"It returns the average of every expert, and the same slop anyone gets.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n\nSee also: [Verifier](verifier.md), [Lecture 1](../lectures/lecture-01.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/concepts/verifiers-rule.md","title":"The Verifier's Rule","meta":"title: The Verifier's Rule\nsummary: Lecture 9, from Jason Wei: how easy a task is to train AI on is proportional to how verifiable it is. Verifier first, data second.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The Verifier's Rule","a":"the-verifiers-rule","text":"How easy a task is to train AI on is proportional to how verifiable it is. Lecture 9. [Lecture 9 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"How Lecture 9 got there","a":"how-lecture-9-got-there","text":"- It comes from the first lecture: Jason Wei on the asymmetry of verification. It is now the guiding rule for the probabilistic half. [Lecture 9 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- In one line: every task that is possible to solve and easy to verify will be solved by AI. [Lecture 9 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- Before it, the room asked whether a machine can learn judgment. It depends on two things in a fixed order: verification first, data second. [Lecture 9 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)\n- Data without a verifier teaches nothing; a verifier without data has nothing to teach. [Lecture 9 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)"},{"h":"What it is used for","a":"what-it-is-used-for","text":"- \"If you can verify a task and the data exists or can be collected, a machine can learn it.\" [Lecture 9 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- Sorting your own week: which tasks are about to be automated, and which stay yours. [Lecture 9 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#14-before-the-practice-set)\n- Reading Karpathy's jagged edge: the skills that jumped from GPT-4 to GPT-5 are the verifiable ones. [Lecture 9 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#11-verifiable-today-automatable-tomorrow)"},{"h":"Common mistake","a":"common-mistake","text":"- Reading benchmarks as general intelligence. Acing a verifiable test with enough examples shows the rule working. [Lecture 9 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- Treating verifiable as automated. Verifiable is not automated, yet: some tasks still wait on data. [Lecture 9 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#11-verifiable-today-automatable-tomorrow)\n\nSee also: [The Verifier's Law](verifiers-law.md), [Verifier](verifier.md), [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/01-sand-to-screen.md","title":"Chapter 1: How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: Chapter 1: How does sand do maths?\nsummary: Course chapter 1 (sand to bits, 7 lessons, about 15 min): transistor, bit, byte, binary, adder, processor, program, clock, and Principle 1.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 1: How does sand do maths?","a":"chapter-1-how-does-sand-do-maths","text":"Chapter 1 of From Sand to Language Models, \"sand to bits\": 7 lessons, about 15 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: how does sand do maths? Melt sand, grow a crystal, carve it with light, and it can add. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nEvery row your app saves and every word a model writes back is tiny switches flipping. This chapter builds the switch. [1.1](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sand](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/index.md): electricity opens a transistor, and what comes out is electricity, so one transistor can open the next. A phone chip holds tens of billions of them.\n- [1.2 Ones and zeros](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/ones-and-zeros.md): line up switches worth 1, 2, 4, 8; eight bits make a byte, any number from 0 to 255. A is 65, because we all agreed it would be.\n- [1.3 The carry: two switches in a row](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/two-switches-in-a-row.md): current gets through only when both are on, exactly the carry digit. One more small circuit gives the other digit, and together they make an adder.\n- [1.4 An adder: switches that do sums](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/be-the-adder.md): in the chip, nobody does the maths; the answer is wherever the current ends up. Subtracting is adding a negative number; multiplying is adding again and again.\n- [1.5 Memory: a loop that remembers](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/a-loop-that-remembers.md): two gates in a loop each hold the other open, so the loop keeps a 1 after the input is gone. Billions of these loops are its memory.\n- [1.6 Programs and the clock](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock.md): a program is a list of instructions written as ones and zeros; a clock ticks billions of times a second, and on each tick the chip does the next instruction.\n- [1.6, Principle 1](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock.md): Atoms make electricity. Electricity flips. Transistors speak in ones and zeros.\n- [1.7 Check yourself](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter. It opens box 1, the machine, from the boxes Lecture 2 drew behind every app."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- Transistor: a gate on a wire, opened and shut by electricity. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Bit: one 1 or 0. A chip holds it in a loop of two transistors. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Byte: eight bits in a row: a number from 0 to 255. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Binary: writing numbers with only 1 (on) and 0 (off). [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Adder: a small circuit of switches whose output is the sum of its inputs. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Processor: the chip's calculator: many adders packed together. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Program: a list of instructions stored as ones and zeros. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n- Clock: the tick on which the chip does its next instruction. [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 2](02-how-a-machine-remembers.md), [Lecture 2](../lectures/lecture-02.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers.md","title":"Chapter 2: How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: Chapter 2: How does a machine remember?\nsummary: Course chapter 2 (memory, 7 lessons, about 18 min): RAM, storage, file system, tables, keys, pointers, SQL, indexes, Principles 2 to 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 2: How does a machine remember?","a":"chapter-2-how-does-a-machine-remember","text":"Chapter 2 of From Sand to Language Models, \"memory\": 7 lessons, about 18 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: we built a machine that computes; where does it write things down, and how does it find them again? [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nYour app's database stands on this chapter: fast memory, lasting storage, tables, and finding one row fast. [2.1](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [2.1 RAM and storage: the workbench forgets](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/index.md): every memory does three jobs: write, store, read. RAM is fast but wiped when the power goes; storage holds on without power, and it is slow to write.\n- [2.1](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/index.md): same gate: used as a switch it computes, used as a trap it remembers.\n- [2.2 How slow is storage?](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time.md): stretch the clock until one tick takes one second, and a write to a spinning disk takes 1 year. So a chat app shows your message from the workbench and saves it in the background.\n- [2.2, Principle 2](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time.md): Fast memory forgets. Lasting memory lags.\n- [2.3 A contents page for the disk](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/a-card-that-says-where-things-are.md): a disk is one long row of numbered blocks. A list of names and block numbers, like the contents page of a book, is a file system.\n- [2.4 Tables, rows and links](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live.md): each kind of thing gets a table, each fact a column, each single thing a row with an id that never repeats. Put the link as one number on the many side.\n- [2.4](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live.md): both sides many, like workflows and tools? A third table keeps one row per pair.\n- [2.4, Principle 3](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live.md): A little data says how to read the rest.\n- [2.5 SQL: say what you want](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/sql-say-what-you-want.md): in 1970 Edgar Codd at IBM proposed telling the database what you want, not how to search. `SELECT text FROM messages WHERE conversation_id = 7`.\n- [2.6 Indexes: one row in a billion](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion.md): a sorted copy plus signposts: one hop reaches a thousand rows, two a million, three a billion (a B-tree). The price: every save happens twice.\n- [2.6, Principle 4](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion.md): Faster reading means a second copy.\n- [2.7 Check yourself](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter, tied to the Lecture 6 tables, pointers and storage box (box 7)."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- RAM: the workbench next to the processor. Fast, wiped when the power goes. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- Storage: flash or disk. Holds on without power, slow to write. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- File system: a contents page of names and block numbers that says where each file sits on the disk. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- Table, column, row: a kind of thing, a fact about it, one thing. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- Key: a row's id. It never repeats. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- Pointer (foreign key): a column on the many side that holds another row's key. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- SQL: a language for saying what you want, not how to find it. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n- Index: a sorted second copy of one column, with signposts, for fast lookup. [2.7](../../raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 1](01-sand-to-screen.md), [Chapter 3](03-can-you-trust-a-machine.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine.md","title":"Chapter 3: Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: Chapter 3: Can you trust a machine?\nsummary: Course chapter 3 (trust, 6 lessons, about 15 min): write-ahead log, ACID, what saved means (201 Created), leader and follower, system of record, Principles 5 and 6.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 3: Can you trust a machine?","a":"chapter-3-can-you-trust-a-machine","text":"Chapter 3 of From Sand to Language Models, \"trust\": 6 lessons, about 15 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: the screen says \"saved\", one second later the power dies; is it saved? [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nWhen your app tells someone \"saved\", this chapter is what makes that true. [3.1](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [3.1 The journal: saving two changes safely](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/index.md): write one line in a journal first, then change the rows. After a crash a journal line is either whole or visibly torn. A database calls its journal the write-ahead log.\n- [3.1, Principle 5](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/index.md): Record the change in one place before changing anything else.\n- [3.2 Four promises a database makes](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/four-promises.md): all or nothing (Atomicity), no rule is ever broken (Consistency), no one overwrites anyone (Isolation), done means stored (Durability). Together: ACID.\n- [3.3 What \"saved\" means](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/what-saved-means.md): show, save, confirm. Only after the journal line reaches the disk does the server reply `201 Created`, and the clock turns into the saved mark.\n- [3.4 Copies and one original](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original.md): every save goes to the leader, which sends its journal to a follower that replays it. For a moment the follower can show the old name.\n- [3.4, Principle 6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original.md): Keep one original. Every copy can be thrown away and remade.\n- [3.5 Growing past one machine](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/growing-past-one-machine.md): split the data; when the cable is cut, show what this copy last heard or make the customer wait, with no third option; keep a warehouse copy for reports.\n- [3.6 Check yourself](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter. Show \"saved\" only after the server replies `201 Created`."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- ACID: four promises: all or nothing, no rule broken, no overwrites, done means stored. [3.6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md)\n- Write-ahead log: a journal line written before the real change. [3.6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md)\n- Server: the computer, somewhere else, that runs your app and keeps its data. [3.6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md)\n- Leader, follower: the machine that takes all saves, and a copy that replays its journal. [3.6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md)\n- System of record: the one original every copy is made from. [3.6](../../raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 2](02-how-a-machine-remembers.md), [Chapter 4](04-what-is-learning.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/04-what-is-learning.md","title":"Chapter 4: What is learning?","meta":"title: Chapter 4: What is learning?\nsummary: Course chapter 4 (learning, 11 lessons, about 30 min): parameters, tokens, byte pair encoding, hallucination, loss, gradient, training, the recipe, Principles 7 to 9.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 4: What is learning?","a":"chapter-4-what-is-learning","text":"Chapter 4 of From Sand to Language Models, \"learning\": 11 lessons, about 30 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: give a model \"France\" and out comes \"Paris\"; inside are billions of dials, nobody set one by hand, so who did? [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [4.1 Parameters: a memory that guesses](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/index.md): a line's two numbers are parameters, dials. Throw the sales away and the line still prices a house nobody sold.\n- [4.2 The next-letter game](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/a-guessing-game.md): in 1951 Claude Shannon asked people for the next letter. A language model plays that game with tokens: pieces of text, often part of a word.\n- [4.3 Tokens: who decided them?](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/who-decided-what-the-tokens-are.md): nobody picks them by hand; the machine counts and merges common pairs (byte pair encoding). About fifty thousand merges later, 25 bytes are 5 tokens. No text is ever unknown.\n- [4.4 The first language model was a count](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count.md): count what came after each token. Ask about a run of words it never saw and it has nothing: not found.\n- [4.4, Principle 7](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count.md): A count only knows what it saw. A pattern can guess what it didn't.\n- [4.5 From a table to dials](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/from-a-table-to-dials.md): a wall of dials gives a chance for every next token. Cut-offs let it bend. It usually picks the likeliest, sometimes a roll of the dice.\n- [4.6 Next token, again and again](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated.md): one job, again and again. A model cannot say \"not found\"; where the pattern is weak it writes fluent, confident, wrong text: hallucination.\n- [4.6, Principle 8](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated.md): Everything a model writes is the next token, guessed from patterns.\n- [4.7 Records and dials](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/records-and-dials.md): records (orange) for what must be exact, dials (purple) for what must be figured out. When the answer must be right, give the model the record and have it read before it writes.\n- [4.8 Loss and gradient: which way to turn each dial](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/which-way-to-turn-each-dial.md): the loss scores how far off a guess was; the gradient is the slope; walking downhill is gradient descent. Every step is small, because the same dials serve every sentence.\n- [4.9 Training, a trillion times](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/training.md): guess, measure the mistake, feed it back, a trillion times. Recent models read more than 15 trillion tokens.\n- [4.10 What learning needs](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs.md): the recipe: a goal, a way to measure the mistake, attempts, feedback. The text sets the answers: self-supervised learning. The only time to choose what goes in is before training.\n- [4.10, Principle 9](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs.md): Learning is reducing mistakes toward a goal, with feedback from every attempt.\n- [4.11 Check yourself](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter and where it shows up in your build."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- Parameter (dial): one learned number saying how much one signal pushes on another. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Token: a piece of text with a number. What a model reads and writes. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Byte pair encoding: building tokens by merging the most common neighbouring pair, again and again. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Model: a wall of dials that turns tokens in into a chance for every next token. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Hallucination: a fluent, confident, wrong guess, made because there is always a next token. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Loss: the score for how far off a guess was. Lower is better. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Gradient: the direction each dial should turn to lower the loss. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Training: the loop: attempt, measure the mistake, feed it back. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n- Self-supervised learning: learning where the text supplies its own answers. [4.11](../../raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 3](03-can-you-trust-a-machine.md), [Chapter 5](05-attention-and-transformers.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/05-attention-and-transformers.md","title":"Chapter 5: What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: Chapter 5: What are attention and transformers?\nsummary: Course chapter 5 (attention, 7 lessons, about 16 min): embeddings, vectors, attention (query, key, value), heads, layers, transformer, why long prompts cost more, Principle 10.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 5: What are attention and transformers?","a":"chapter-5-what-are-attention-and-transformers","text":"Chapter 5 of From Sand to Language Models, \"attention\": 7 lessons, about 16 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: a count table only saw the last token; a model reads all five and knows that \"capital\" and \"France\" matter. How? [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nA model can only use what is in your prompt. This chapter shows why, and why long prompts cost more. [5.1](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [5.1 Embeddings: a map of words](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/index.md): rate every token on scales, and it becomes a spot on a map. Nobody writes the ratings: the mistakes drew the map. A token's list of numbers is its embedding.\n- [5.2 One word, two meanings](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/one-word-two-meanings.md): each token gets its spot alone, which breaks on a word like \"bank\". So each token needs a way to look at the others and move its spot.\n- [5.3 Attention: every token asks a question](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/every-token-asks-a-question.md): each token makes a question, a label and a message. Ask, match, share, collect. Engineers call them query, key and value. Each token reads only the tokens before it.\n- [5.4 Layers: listen, then think](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/listen-then-think.md): after listening, each token thinks on its own. Listen, then think, is one layer. About two of every three dials sit in the thinking blocks.\n- [5.5 The transformer: many questions, many layers](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer.md): many questions side by side (heads; GPT-3 runs 96), a position stamp for order, layers stacked (GPT-3 stacks 96). That stack is a transformer, the T in GPT.\n- [5.5, Principle 10](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer.md): Meaning comes from company. Every token listens to the ones before it, and becomes what it hears.\n- [5.6 Why long prompts cost more](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/the-bill.md): a 1,000-token prompt makes about half a million pairs. Double the text and the pairs grow four times.\n- [5.7 Check yourself](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter. If a fact is not in the context window, nothing can pay attention to it."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- Embedding: a token's list of numbers: its spot on the map of meaning. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Vector: a list of numbers. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Attention: each token matches its question against earlier tokens' labels and blends their messages. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Query, key, value: question, label, message. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Head: one of many questions asked side by side. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Layer: one round of listen, then think. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n- Transformer: a stack of such layers. [5.7](../../raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 4](04-what-is-learning.md), [Chapter 6](06-what-hardware-powers-llms.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms.md","title":"Chapter 6: What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: Chapter 6: What hardware runs a language model?\nsummary: Course chapter 6 (chips, 6 lessons, about 16 min): GPUs, moving dials is the cost, batching, quantization, KV cache, scaling laws, Principle 11.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 6: What hardware runs a language model?","a":"chapter-6-what-hardware-runs-a-language-model","text":"Chapter 6 of From Sand to Language Models, \"chips\": 6 lessons, about 16 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: what does the chip spend its time on, and what does a model cost to make? [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nYou pay per token, and long chats get slow. This chapter shows where that cost comes from. [6.1](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [6.1 GPUs: the right shape of chip](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/index.md): all of it is multiply and add. A CPU is a few brilliant workers; a GPU is thousands of simple workers doing the same step at once. For 8 billion dials, the maths for one token takes 16 millionths of a second.\n- [6.2 What does the chip wait for?](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for.md): 8 billion dials × 2 bytes = 16 gigabytes carried for every token. At about 3 terabytes a second that takes about 5 thousandths of a second, some 300 times longer than the maths.\n- [6.2, Principle 11](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for.md): The maths is cheap. Moving the numbers is the cost.\n- [6.3 Batching and quantization](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/make-every-trip-count.md): batching makes one trip of the dials serve hundreds of people's tokens; quantization holds each dial in 8 bits, not 16, at the cost of slightly rougher dials.\n- [6.4 The KV cache](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/the-kv-cache.md): the chip keeps earlier tokens' labels and messages instead of remaking them. It is Principle 4 again. The price is memory that grows with every token: one reason chats have a length limit.\n- [6.5 Scaling laws: know before you spend](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend.md): training costs about 6 steps per dial per token. Llama 3, about 400 billion dials, read 15 trillion tokens on 16,000 GPUs. Train small models first and a straight line predicts the big one.\n- [6.5](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend.md): the line also says how to spend: grow the dials and the reading together, about twenty tokens for every dial.\n- [6.6 Check yourself](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter. A model that runs on a laptop is usually a quantized one."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- GPU: a chip with thousands of simple workers doing the same multiply and add at once. [6.6](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md)\n- Batching: serving many people's tokens with one trip of the dials. [6.6](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md)\n- KV cache: a stored copy of earlier tokens' labels and messages. [6.6](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md)\n- Quantization: storing each dial in fewer bits. [6.6](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md)\n- Scaling laws: the line through small models' results that predicts a big one. [6.6](../../raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 5](05-attention-and-transformers.md), [Chapter 7](07-guesser-to-assistant.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/07-guesser-to-assistant.md","title":"Chapter 7: How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: Chapter 7: How does a guesser become an assistant?\nsummary: Course chapter 7 (assistant, 7 lessons, about 18 min): pre-training, base model, supervised fine-tuning, RLHF and the judge, what can be checked, evals, Principle 12.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 7: How does a guesser become an assistant?","a":"chapter-7-how-does-a-guesser-become-an-assistant","text":"Chapter 7 of From Sand to Language Models, \"assistant\": 7 lessons, about 18 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: all that money buys a machine that guesses the next token and nothing more. Yet. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nWhy a chat model answers you instead of rambling on, and how an eval gets you better output from it. [7.1](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [7.1 A guesser, not an assistant](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/index.md): everything so far is pre-training; the text graded itself. On the web a question is often followed by another question, so the model guesses well and still does not answer you.\n- [7.2 Fine-tuning: example answers](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/fine-tuning-example-answers.md): a few tens of thousands of instructions, each with an ideal response. The dials learn a shape, not facts: a question, then an answer, then stop. This is supervised fine-tuning.\n- [7.3 RLHF: when nobody can write the answer](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer.md): helpful, honest and safe cannot be an answer key, but people can pick the better of two. A smaller model, the judge, learns their picks and scores the big model.\n- [7.4 Same loop, different teachers](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/same-loop-different-teachers.md): feedback comes from the text itself, then an answer a person wrote, then a judge. Only the feedback changes. That recipe, in November 2022, produced ChatGPT.\n- [7.5 What can be checked](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked.md): solve `2x + 6 = 14` checks itself; a good email needs judgment. In competition maths models reached gold-medal scores at the International Mathematical Olympiad in about two years.\n- [7.5, Principle 12](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked.md): A machine learns what you can check. The easier the check, the faster it learns.\n- [7.6 Evals: write the check yourself](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself.md): a goal with no way to measure it gets flat output. Collect your ten best emails (a golden dataset), write down what good looks like, and any output can be scored. You wrote one in Practice set 2.\n- [7.7 Check yourself](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter. For better marketing copy, build an eval first. A language model is the third interface, and it is never a database."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- Pre-training: the first, huge stage: self-supervised learning on the web. [7.7](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md)\n- Base model: a pre-trained model that only continues text. [7.7](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md)\n- Supervised fine-tuning: a short second stage on instruction and ideal-response pairs. [7.7](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md)\n- RLHF: training against a judge that learned which answers people prefer. [7.7](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md)\n- Judge: a second, smaller model that learns to predict which answer people pick. [7.3](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer.md)\n- Eval: a written, scoreable definition of good for a task. [7.7](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 6](06-what-hardware-powers-llms.md), [Chapter 8](08-what-llms-learn-from-now.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now.md","title":"Chapter 8: What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: Chapter 8: What do language models learn from now?\nsummary: Course chapter 8 (checks, 7 lessons, about 19 min): post-training, learning by trying, rubrics, RL environments, trajectories, reward hacking, purple and orange halves, Principle 13.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Chapter 8: What do language models learn from now?","a":"chapter-8-what-do-language-models-learn-from-now","text":"Chapter 8 of From Sand to Language Models, \"checks\": 7 lessons, about 19 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nIt answers: what do language models learn from now? The scarcest thing in AI is not a chip or a model. It is a way to tell when an answer is right. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nWhere AI is heading, and why knowing what good work looks like in your field is worth more than ever. [8.1](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/index.md)"},{"h":"The lessons, in order","a":"the-lessons-in-order","text":"- [8.1 Where the computing goes now](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/index.md): in early 2024, on a SemiAnalysis estimate, about two thirds of computing at OpenAI and Anthropic went on reading the web. The new computing goes to post-training. Treat the numbers as rough sizes.\n- [8.2 Learning by trying](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/let-it-try.md): ask one problem sixteen times, check only the last line, nudge winning attempts up. The model taught itself to double-check. Once every attempt passes, the problem is used up.\n- [8.3 The expert writes the check](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-expert-writes-the-check.md): one expert answer teaches one example; a written rubric lets a judge score a million attempts. They no longer write answers. They write the check.\n- [8.4 What a lab will pay for](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/from-answers-to-checks.md): cheapest first: a raw record, a trajectory, a task, an environment. An environment checks what actually changed, not what the model says it did. Each failure is a fresh task no lab has.\n- [8.5 Reward hacking: the machine learns the check](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check.md): the score goes up and the job does not get done. In one Anthropic test a model went along with a dangerous request 29 percent of the time, up from under 1 percent.\n- [8.5, Principle 13](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check.md): A machine learns the check you wrote, not the goal you meant.\n- [8.6 The model and the rules around it](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/two-halves.md): purple predicts: the model. Orange follows rules: the tools, the records, the checks. Nobody else has your standard for good work.\n- [8.7 Check yourself](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md): questions on the chapter, tied to Practice set 1 and Practice set 2. From now on, keep every reply your app gets wrong."},{"h":"Words it names","a":"words-it-names","text":"- Post-training: everything a model learns after reading the web. [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n- Rubric: a written list of what a good answer must do, usable as a check. [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n- RL environment: a simulated workplace with tasks and a program that checks what actually changed. [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n- Trajectory: a step-by-step recording of work being done. [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n- Reward hacking: a model raising its score in a way the designer did not intend. [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n- Probabilistic, deterministic: prediction-based (the model, purple), rule-based (tools, records, checks, orange). [8.7](../../raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md)\n\nSee also: [About the course](about-the-course.md), [Chapter 7](07-guesser-to-assistant.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/course/about-the-course.md","title":"About the course: From Sand to Language Models","meta":"title: About the course: From Sand to Language Models\nsummary: The self-paced first-principles course: eight chapters, one idea per lesson, 2.5 hours, both tracks, nothing to submit, from a switch to language models.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"About the course: From Sand to Language Models","a":"about-the-course-from-sand-to-language-models","text":"From Sand to Language Models is a self-paced course in Cohort 8, Module 2, for both tracks. [Course page](../../raw/course/index.md)\n\nIt explains what really happens when your app saves a row, or a model answers a question, built up from a single switch, with no maths needed. [Course page](../../raw/course/index.md#from-sand-to-language-models)\n\nThe whole course takes 2.5 hours. [Course page](../../raw/course/index.md#from-sand-to-language-models)\n\nPublic page: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/ [Course page](../../raw/course/index.md)"},{"h":"How it works","a":"how-it-works","text":"- Each lesson is one page and one idea. Read the short opening first. [Course page, How it works](../../raw/course/index.md#how-it-works)\n- Play the drawing; reach its goal and the explanation opens. A few lessons are a short read. [Course page, How it works](../../raw/course/index.md#how-it-works)\n- Press Next. Contents, at the top left, shows every lesson and what you have done. [Course page, How it works](../../raw/course/index.md#how-it-works)\n- Nothing to submit. Your progress stays on this device. [Course page, How it works](../../raw/course/index.md#how-it-works)\n- Each chapter ends with a Check yourself lesson: questions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)"},{"h":"The chapters, in order","a":"the-chapters-in-order","text":"Chapter 1 starts with the smallest piece and builds up; the next seven chapters stack layers on top of it. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course), [1.7](../../raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md#in-your-build)\n\n1. [How does sand do maths?](01-sand-to-screen.md) (sand to bits): 7 lessons, about 15 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n2. [How does a machine remember?](02-how-a-machine-remembers.md) (memory): 7 lessons, about 18 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n3. [Can you trust a machine?](03-can-you-trust-a-machine.md) (trust): 6 lessons, about 15 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n4. [What is learning?](04-what-is-learning.md) (learning): 11 lessons, about 30 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n5. [What are attention and transformers?](05-attention-and-transformers.md) (attention): 7 lessons, about 16 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n6. [What hardware runs a language model?](06-what-hardware-powers-llms.md) (chips): 6 lessons, about 16 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n7. [How does a guesser become an assistant?](07-guesser-to-assistant.md) (assistant): 7 lessons, about 18 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n8. [What do language models learn from now?](08-what-llms-learn-from-now.md) (checks): 7 lessons, about 19 min. [Course page, The course](../../raw/course/index.md#the-course)\n\nThe first lesson is 1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sand. [Course page](../../raw/course/index.md#from-sand-to-language-models)\n\nSee also: [Chapter 1](01-sand-to-screen.md), [Chapter 8](08-what-llms-learn-from-now.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/call-an-llm-with-requests-post.md","title":"Call an LLM with requests.post","meta":"title: Call an LLM with requests.post\nsummary: Lecture 5's five steps: test the Groq endpoint in httpie.io/app, then replace the Gradio echo with a raw requests.post, then verify.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Call an LLM with requests.post","a":"call-an-llm-with-requestspost","text":"The Lecture 5 practice set connects a Gradio app to an LLM with a raw `requests.post`. [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#building-apis)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Get a Groq key at console.groq.com/keys. [Lecture 5 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#04-filling-the-process-with-one-line)\n2. Run your Lecture 3 Gradio chat and confirm it returns your input unchanged. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n3. In httpie.io/app, send a POST to `https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions` with your key and a body naming `openai/gpt-oss-20b`. Write the status code you expect first. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n4. Send the key as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. [Practice set, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#03-the-raw-request-live)\n5. Write one prompt and send it with your user's real input in the browser first. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n6. Set the key in the terminal: `export GROQ_API_KEY=...`. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)\n7. In Python, replace the echo with `requests.post`, using the same address, action, key and package. Return only the content field. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n8. If `import requests` fails, run `pip install requests`. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)\n9. Run your golden input, compare with your golden output, change the prompt once, run again. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)"},{"h":"The four questions for any machine","a":"the-four-questions-for-any-machine","text":"- What is the address? What is the action? Do I need a key, and how do I get it? What package does it expect? [Lecture 5 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#15-any-machine-four-lines)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md), [Fix a failed LLM API request](fix-a-failed-llm-api-request.md), [Endpoint](../concepts/endpoint.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/create-a-supabase-project-and-tables.md","title":"Create a Supabase project and tables","meta":"title: Create a Supabase project and tables\nsummary: Steps from Lecture 8: sign in to Supabase, make a project, find the URL and keys, run schema.sql, set the env variables.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Create a Supabase project and tables","a":"create-a-supabase-project-and-tables","text":"The steps Lecture 8 used to rent a Postgres database and give your server its address and key. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n\n1. Sign in at supabase.com. GitHub login is easiest. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n2. New organisation: any name, Personal, Free plan. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n3. New project: a name and a database password you save somewhere. Leave the rest, including the region it picks for you. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n4. Wait for it to come up. [Lecture 8 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n5. The address is the Project URL, under Settings, Data API. The key is under Settings, API keys: the secret key, on your server only. [Lecture 8 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#08-the-same-four-dials)\n6. Paste `schema.sql` into the SQL Editor and run it once. Or Table Editor, New table: add each column and its type, then Add foreign key to link it. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n7. Check it: the Schema Visualizer draws your ER diagram back. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n8. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill `GROQ_API_KEY`, `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY`. [Lecture 8 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)\n9. On Vercel, add the same two variables under Settings, Environment Variables, exact names from `.env`. Redeploy. [Lecture 8 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)\n\nNever put the secret key in a browser: it can read and write everything. [Lecture 8 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#08-the-same-four-dials)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md), [RLS](../concepts/rls.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/decide-what-your-app-stores.md","title":"Decide what your app stores","meta":"title: Decide what your app stores\nsummary: How to model your app's storage the Lecture 6 way: questions first, then things, facts, links, pointers, history rows, files, and a check.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Decide what your app stores","a":"decide-what-your-app-stores","text":"The Lecture 6 steps for filling your HLD's storage box, as in Level 7 of the Modelling Lab. [Lecture 6 lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md#l7-your-model)\n\n1. Write three questions your data must answer. Whatever they need is worth storing. [Lecture 6 lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md#l7-your-model)\n2. Find the things: can the app create, read, update and delete it on its own, and can there be many? Each gets a table. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n3. List the facts you need to recreate one. Each is a column. [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n4. Give each table an `id` key. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n5. For each link, ask one or many, both ways. Put the pointer on the many side, or add a third table if both are many. [Lecture 6 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n6. If the product must remember what was true before, make it a row with a time on it. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#history-is-a-row-not-a-cell)\n7. Put bytes in file storage and keep the link in a row. Write \"none\" if your app stores no files. [Lecture 6 lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md#rows-or-files-for-your-app)\n8. Trace each of your three questions along the pointers. [Lecture 6 lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md#before-calling-it-done)\n9. Try to create a wrong state, such as a message with no conversation, and make sure the model refuses it. [Lecture 6 lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md#before-calling-it-done)\n\nSee also: [Domain model](../concepts/domain-model.md), [Pointer](../concepts/pointer.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel.md","title":"Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel","meta":"title: Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel\nsummary: Lecture 7 steps to deploy a FastAPI route to Vercel, set GROQ_API_KEY, test with httpie, and read 404, 500, 401 and 200.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel","a":"deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel","text":"The steps Lecture 7 used to put `/api/diagnose` on a live `vercel.app` URL with the key on the server. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Install and log in: `npm i -g vercel`, then `vercel login`, same account as the dashboard. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n2. Open a new terminal inside the step folder. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n3. Run `vercel`: new project, a name, accept the FastAPI it detects. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n4. Run `vercel --prod` for the production URL. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n5. Test from httpie.io/app: POST, your URL with `/api/diagnose`, body with a `workflow` field. The field name must match `WorkflowIn` exactly. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n6. Add the key: Project, Settings, Environment Variables, name it `GROQ_API_KEY`, paste the value, save, redeploy. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n7. Point Gradio at it: `API_URL=https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api/diagnose python 04-gradio-client/app.py`. [Lecture 7 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#09-step-04--gradio-becomes-a-client-tonight)"},{"h":"Read what came back","a":"read-what-came-back","text":"- 404: wrong address. Add `/api/diagnose`. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- 500: the server failed because the key is not on that machine. Read the Logs tab, then add the env var. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- 401 from Groq: your server reached Groq without a key. Same fix. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- 200 with a plan: done. Share the URL. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)"},{"h":"Watch out","a":"watch-out","text":"- You cannot type on Vercel's machine, so no `export`. The dashboard is the only way in. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- Never hardcode the key to make the 500 go away. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- Predict the status before every call, and log every error. [Practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#04-your-error-log)\n\nSee also: [Deploy](../concepts/deploy.md), [FastAPI](../concepts/fastapi.md), [Lecture 7](../lectures/lecture-07.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/deploy-a-gradio-app-to-hugging-face-spaces.md","title":"Deploy a Gradio app to Hugging Face Spaces","meta":"title: Deploy a Gradio app to Hugging Face Spaces\nsummary: Put a tested app.py on a shareable link: free ZeroGPU, two Spaces, account must be 30 days old. Code track, Building UI office hour.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Deploy a Gradio app to Hugging Face Spaces","a":"deploy-a-gradio-app-to-hugging-face-spaces","text":"Hugging Face Spaces is the lowest-cost, fastest route to a link for a Gradio app; online it is the same file, with a link other people can open. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)"},{"h":"Before you start","a":"before-you-start","text":"- Build and test locally first. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n- Free hardware is ZeroGPU only, you get two free Spaces, and a new account must be 30 days old before it can use them. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n- So create your Hugging Face account at huggingface.co/join now, even if you deploy nothing. [Checklist, 4](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#4-stage-3-the-link-optional-this-week)\n- No one is asked to pay for a Space. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Log in at huggingface.co, click the plus button, New Space (or go to huggingface.co/new-space). [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n2. Name it (for example `c8-ui`), choose the Gradio SDK, and start blank, not from a template. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n3. Hardware: the free ZeroGPU option. Visibility: public to share the link, private otherwise. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n4. Click Create, choose Create the app.py file, paste your tested app.py, and click Commit new file. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n5. Wait for the build. From the three-dot menu choose Embed this Space to get the shareable link. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)\n6. Open the link on your phone; if it opens on a different device, it is live. [Checklist, 4](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#4-stage-3-the-link-optional-this-week)\n7. To change anything, edit app.py in the Space and commit again. [Office hour guide, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces)"},{"h":"Good to know","a":"good-to-know","text":"- The link stage was optional in the Building UI week. [Checklist, 4](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#4-stage-3-the-link-optional-this-week)\n- Gradio is a learning medium, not a production tool. [Office hour guide](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n\nSee also: [Set up Python and Gradio](set-up-python-and-gradio-first-time.md), [Code track: Building UI pages](../lectures/code-track-building-ui.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md","title":"Do Assignment 1, First Proof","meta":"title: Do Assignment 1, First Proof\nsummary: Assignment 1 (mandatory, both tracks, due Fri 9 Oct 2026): North Star, observation, discovery-to-mvp-coach, tracker, manual MVP with 2 to 5 people, eight deliverables.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Do Assignment 1, First Proof","a":"do-assignment-1-first-proof","text":"A mandatory Week 0 assignment for both tracks: \"21 days. One observation from your own life. One link a stranger can use.\" [Assignment 1](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#first-proof)\n\n- Due Fri 9 Oct 2026. [Assignment 1](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#first-proof)\n- The output is not an app. It is a process you have run once by hand, with a number attached, and a hypothesis you now know to be truer or falser. [Assignment 1, 01](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#01-why-this-exists)"},{"h":"The five steps","a":"the-five-steps","text":"1. Fix the North Star: six-month timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing. Could someone else verify it on the date? One goal, not four. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)\n2. Write the observation: one real person, one real situation. Your lived experience, with no solution in it. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)\n3. Run discovery-to-mvp-coach: install the skill file (Settings, Customise, Skills, upload), invoke `/discovery-to-mvp-coach`, paste the observation. Budget 60 to 90 minutes. Keep the 4 to 5 questions Phase 6 grades you on. Save the discovery document and the MVP plan. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)\n4. Publish the tracker: paste the MVP plan into Lovable or any AI builder and ask for a tracker of every line item in the 21-day pilot. The link must open for a stranger without your help. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)\n5. Run the manual MVP: deliver the value by hand to 2 to 5 real people in the first 14 days, asking Mom Test questions. Fill the input, process, output card. Update the tracker daily. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps)"},{"h":"The input, process, output card","a":"the-input-process-output-card","text":"- Fields: input, output, process, time and cost today, time and cost after, metric, and what cannot yet be done by hand. [Assignment 1, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#03-input-process-output)\n- Metric: revenue increased or cost and time saved, one number with its unit. [Assignment 1, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#03-input-process-output)\n- Do not automate anything on this card during the 21 days. [Assignment 1, 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#03-input-process-output)"},{"h":"Rules","a":"rules","text":"- Manual first: no agents, no automation, no product. [Assignment 1, 04](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- Do not generate the observation with an LLM. [Assignment 1, 04](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- The Phase 6 simulation is practice and does not count toward the real conversations. [Assignment 1, 04](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- One cycle at a time. If an idea dies, log it and rerun the skill on the next one. [Assignment 1, 04](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#04-rules-of-the-build)"},{"h":"What you submit, and what each must show","a":"what-you-submit-and-what-each-must-show","text":"- North Star: all four elements in one sentence, verifiable by an outsider on the date. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Observation: one real person, real numbers, no solution, aligned to the North Star's specific thing. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Discovery document: compressed observation, subject, evidence audit, causal chain, and a hypothesis with a number, a deadline and at least one falsifier. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- MVP plan: who you recruit, what you deliver, what you measure, what you charge (optional). No software. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Published tracker link: opens for a stranger, every 21-day line item, daily updates. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Validation log: 2 to 5 real conversations, each with who, when, the questions, what you heard, and one contact artefact. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Input-Process-Output card, from a process run by hand at least once. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Verdict: two lines, truer or falser than on day 0, and what you are changing. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Submit through the assignment form on the LMS. Stuck? Open a support ticket. [Assignment 1, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)"},{"h":"After submission","a":"after-submission","text":"- Week 1 begins with your Input-Process-Output card, not a hypothetical. [Assignment 1, 06](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#06-after-submission)\n\nSee also: [Idea to App workshop](../lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md), [Aarav's example](../threads/aaravs-example.md), [North Star](../concepts/north-star.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/fix-a-failed-llm-api-request.md","title":"Fix a failed LLM API request","meta":"title: Fix a failed LLM API request\nsummary: Read the status code and message, find the part that broke, apply the fix; Groq errors 401, 404, 400, 429, 5xx (Lecture 5).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Fix a failed LLM API request","a":"fix-a-failed-llm-api-request","text":"The status code says whose fault it is; the message says what. [Lecture 5 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#08-go-to-the-source-and-read-the-404)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Print `r.status_code` and `r.json()` first. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n2. Read the code: 2xx worked, 4xx your mistake, 5xx theirs. [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)\n3. 401: check the header is `Authorization: Bearer <key>` and the variable is set in the same terminal. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n4. 404 about the model: use `openai/gpt-oss-20b`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n5. 404 about the address: use `https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n6. 400: a missing field or the wrong shape for `messages`; compare your `json=` with the practice set. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n7. 429: wait a minute. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n8. 5xx: their machine, not yours. Try again later. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n9. Try the fix the error points to before pasting anything into an LLM. [Practice set, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#06-rules-of-the-build)\n10. Still stuck: post the status code, the exact message and what you tried on the Discord code channel, key masked. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#02-still-stuck)"},{"h":"Python errors","a":"python-errors","text":"- `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'requests'`: run `pip install requests`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- `NameError: name 'gr' is not defined`: add `import gradio as gr` at the top. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- `KeyError: 'choices'`: the request failed, so the reply has `error` instead of `choices`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5](../lectures/lecture-05.md), [Environment variable](../concepts/environment-variable.md), [Rate limit](../concepts/rate-limit.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/fix-a-failed-request.md","title":"Fix a failed request","meta":"title: Fix a failed request\nsummary: Read the status code to find the broken part (404, 405, 401, 400, 429, 5xx, CORS), fix one part at a time, and ask for help. Lecture 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Fix a failed request","a":"fix-a-failed-request","text":"A failed request comes back with a code, and the code names the part of your request to look at. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. If nothing gets through, paste `https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello` into the address bar. JSON back means your connection is fine; if that fails too, it is your network. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#01-first-rule-out-the-network)\n2. Read the code: 404 address, 405 action, 401 key, 400 package, 429 too many requests, 5xx their machine. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)\n3. Change one part at a time, so you know which change fixed it. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)\n4. Still stuck after 20 minutes? Post the error in the help template. [Troubleshooting, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#where-are-you)"},{"h":"By code","a":"by-code","text":"- 401 from Groq: paste the key again with nothing around it; the header must read `Authorization: Bearer gsk_…`, one space after Bearer. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 401 from Aarav: create a key with `POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/keys` and send it as `Authorization: Bearer aarav_…`. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 400: add `Content-Type: application/json`, fix the JSON, or name the field exactly `workflow`. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 404 model does not exist: use `openai/gpt-oss-120b`, or `openai/gpt-oss-20b` as the fallback. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 404 Not found: read the doors listed in the reply and copy one; `GET https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav` lists them all. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 405: check the reply's `Allow` header and pick one of those methods. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 429: wait the seconds in `retry-after`; use your own account, since more keys on one account do not help. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- Blocked by CORS or Failed to fetch: check the address, open the Console tab, try the request in the Builder or with curl. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- Groq reply looks empty: read `choices[0].message.content` and ignore `reasoning`; if `finish_reason` is `length`, remove a small `max_tokens`. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 500: nothing in your request to fix; wait and try again. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)"},{"h":"The help post","a":"the-help-post","text":"- Three parts, in order: status code and error (exact text), what I tried, and the request with the key masked. [Troubleshooting, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#03-the-help-post)\n- Helpers need the code and the error. They never need your key. [Troubleshooting, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#03-the-help-post)\n\nSee also: [Status code](../concepts/status-code.md), [JSON](../concepts/json.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](keep-your-groq-key-safe.md), [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/fix-common-python-and-gradio-errors.md","title":"Fix common Python and Gradio errors","meta":"title: Fix common Python and Gradio errors\nsummary: Cause and fix for python not found, No module named gradio, port 7860 in use, IndentationError, blank browser; when and how to post for help.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Fix common Python and Gradio errors","a":"fix-common-python-and-gradio-errors","text":"The errors first-time coders hit when running a Gradio app, with the cause and fix the course gives. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Read the last line of the traceback; it names the error. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run)\n2. Find it in the list below and try the fix once. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run)\n3. Log the exact error, what you tried and what fixed it. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run)\n4. Same error after 20 minutes: post in the code track channel on Discord, not general. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#02-still-stuck)\n5. A good help post has three parts in this order: the error (exact text), what I tried, a screenshot. [Checklist, 6](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#a-good-help-post-has-exactly-three-parts-in-this-order)\n6. When you fix something, reply in the thread; your fix is someone else's blocker. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#02-still-stuck)"},{"h":"The errors","a":"the-errors","text":"- `python is not recognised / command not found`: PATH not set during install (Windows), or Mac uses python3. Fix: Windows, reinstall and tick Add Python to PATH; Mac, use `python3` and `pip3`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- `No module named gradio`: pip installed into a different Python than the one running. Fix: `python -m pip install gradio`, then run again. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- `Address already in use / port 7860`: the previous app is still running. Fix: close the old terminal or press Ctrl+C in it, then run again. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- `IndentationError / SyntaxError`: a line was pasted with wrong spacing or a missing quote or bracket. Fix: re-copy the template exactly; Python counts the spaces at the start of a line. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- Browser shows nothing: the URL was retyped wrong, or the terminal was closed. Fix: click the link the terminal printed and keep that terminal open. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- def, return and functions are unfamiliar: lectures do not teach Python syntax. Fix: finish the Big Binary Python prerequisite first. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)"},{"h":"Good to know","a":"good-to-know","text":"- These errors are expected; solving them yourself is the start of the debugging muscle. [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md#troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run)\n- First-time coders: pair up with another first-time coder and swap screens. [Checklist, 6](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#6-where-to-post-and-how)\n- The office hour reviews the error log, not just the screenshot of success. [Office hour guide, 09](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#09-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n\nSee also: [Set up Python and Gradio](set-up-python-and-gradio-first-time.md), [Code track: Building UI pages](../lectures/code-track-building-ui.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/give-an-mcp-address-to-a-second-app.md","title":"Give an MCP address to a second app","meta":"title: Give an MCP address to a second app\nsummary: Lecture 11 Tool Bench: switch the server on, move the card to an MCP server, then paste its address into the second app and ask.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Give an MCP address to a second app","a":"give-an-mcp-address-to-a-second-app","text":"Put the card and the fetch code where the data is, once. Every app needs only the address. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Get a card that passes its tests on the Tool Bench. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n2. Flip By hand to On, so the server runs the loop itself. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n3. Move the card to the sheet's side (step 5 on the bench). Your server now has to ask for it with `initialize`, `notifications/initialized`, `tools/list` and `tools/call`. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n4. Ask the same question again: the loop is the same, but the fetch now goes to the sheet's server. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n5. Copy the address and open the second app. It has never seen your card or your sheet. Paste the address and ask. [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)\n6. Watch the wire: the second app shows every message its server sent and got back. [Second app](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-second-app.md#the-wire-this-apps-server-and-the-mcp-server)\n7. Code track: under step 5 the bench prints the same server in Python (FastMCP) and a second app in Python (a FastMCP client). [Bench notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#06-move-the-card-mcp)"},{"h":"Before you share","a":"before-you-share","text":"- Anyone with your address can read what the tool returns, the same as anyone with your sheet link. Share it only where you would share the sheet. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n\nSee also: [MCP](../concepts/mcp.md), [Host, client, server](../concepts/host-client-server.md), [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md","title":"Keep your Groq key safe","meta":"title: Keep your Groq key safe\nsummary: Get a Groq key, keep it out of pages and chats, revoke it when it leaks; free tier is 30 requests a minute and 1,000 a day per account. Lecture 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Keep your Groq key safe","a":"keep-your-groq-key-safe","text":"A key is long, unguessable text the machine issued to you; when a request carries it, the machine treats the request as yours: your account, your limits, your bill. [Keys, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#01-what-a-key-is)"},{"h":"Get a key","a":"get-a-key","text":"1. Open console.groq.com/keys in a new tab; it is a website, not a download. [Request Builder, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#part-a--get-a-free-groq-key-one-time-about-3-minutes)\n2. Sign in with Google. No card needed. [Request Builder, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#part-a--get-a-free-groq-key-one-time-about-3-minutes)\n3. Click Create API Key, name it `c8-lecture04`, and click Submit. [Request Builder, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#part-a--get-a-free-groq-key-one-time-about-3-minutes)\n4. Copy the key now. Groq shows it once. [Request Builder, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md#part-a--get-a-free-groq-key-one-time-about-3-minutes)"},{"h":"Where it lives","a":"where-it-lives","text":"- The rule: the backend, or an environment variable on your own machine. Never in page code, never in a chat, never in a screenshot. [Keys](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md)\n- Trying a request today: the Request Builder holds it in memory only and forgets it when you reload. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#where-it-goes-by-situation)\n- A terminal command: an environment variable such as `GROQ_API_KEY`, read as `$GROQ_API_KEY`. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#where-it-goes-by-situation)\n- Code track backend: the code reads `os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]`. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#where-it-goes-by-situation)\n- No-code track backend: the tool's secrets or credentials store. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#where-it-goes-by-situation)\n- Never in Discord, an AI chat or a screenshot. Mask it: `gsk_••••`. Paste the error, not the key. [Keys, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#never-here)"},{"h":"The minute one leaks","a":"the-minute-one-leaks","text":"1. Revoke it at console.groq.com/keys. Every request carrying it now gets 401. [Keys, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#03-the-minute-one-leaks)\n2. Create a new one on the same page, and copy it once. [Keys, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#03-the-minute-one-leaks)\n3. Update where it is used: environment variable, `.env` file, host settings, no-code secrets. Anything still on the old key fails with 401. [Keys, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#03-the-minute-one-leaks)\n4. Remove the leak last, not first: once a key has been seen, assume it was copied. [Keys, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#03-the-minute-one-leaks)"},{"h":"Free-tier limits","a":"free-tier-limits","text":"- Groq's free tier allows `openai/gpt-oss-120b` 30 requests a minute and 1,000 a day. [Keys, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything)\n- The limits belong to your account, not to the key: five keys do not give five times the limit, and a key shared with five friends means six people draw from one limit. [Keys, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything)\n- When the limit runs out, the reply is 429 Too many requests, with a `retry-after` header saying how many seconds to wait. [Keys, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything)\n- Past the free tier, you pay per token. A shared key is a shared bill. [Keys, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything)\n- So everyone makes their own Groq account. [Keys, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md#04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything)\n\nSee also: [Backend](../concepts/backend.md), [Fix a failed request](fix-a-failed-request.md), [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/put-your-project-on-github.md","title":"Put your project on GitHub","meta":"title: Put your project on GitHub\nsummary: Lecture 7 steps to get your code onto a public GitHub repository: install Git and the GitHub CLI, log in, commit after each step, push.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Put your project on GitHub","a":"put-your-project-on-github","text":"How Lecture 7 asked learners to get code onto GitHub, mostly by letting an LLM desktop app run the terminal. [Lecture 7 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#04-pick-one-llm-desktop-app)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Create a GitHub account. [Lecture 7 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n2. Pick one LLM desktop app that can see your project folder: Codex, Claude Code, Hermes or OpenCode. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#02-before-you-start-0-of-5-ready)\n3. Have it install Git and the GitHub CLI and log in with the account you created. [Lecture 7 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#04-pick-one-llm-desktop-app)\n4. Make one public repository. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#02-before-you-start-0-of-5-ready)\n5. Commit after each step, push when the step works. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#05-rules)"},{"h":"Watch out","a":"watch-out","text":"- Read every command the LLM app runs. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#05-rules)\n- Public, or it cannot be reviewed. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#02-before-you-start-0-of-5-ready)\n- Keys never go in a file you will share. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#02-before-you-start-0-of-5-ready)\n\nSee also: [Repository, commit and push](../concepts/repository.md), [Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel](deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/read-the-network-tab.md","title":"Read the network tab","meta":"title: Read the network tab\nsummary: Open Chrome DevTools, filter to Fetch/XHR, and find the six parts of a request and reply, on this site, Groq and ChatGPT. Lecture 4.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Read the network tab","a":"read-the-network-tab","text":"Every request your browser sends is written down in the Network tab, and it shows all six parts: address, action, key, package, code and reply package. [Network tab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md)"},{"h":"Open it","a":"open-it","text":"1. Open DevTools: right click, then Inspect, or `F12` or `Ctrl`+`Shift`+`I` (Mac: `Cmd`+`Option`+`I`). [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)\n2. Click the Network tab. [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)\n3. Filter to Fetch/XHR, the requests a page sends by itself after it has loaded. [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)\n4. Clear the list with the circle with a line through it. [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)\n5. Cause one request, then click it: the tabs are Headers, Payload, Preview, Response. [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)\n6. Keep DevTools open while you work; it only records what happens while it is open. [Network tab, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#01-open-it)"},{"h":"Where each part is","a":"where-each-part-is","text":"- Address: Headers, Request URL. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)\n- Action: Headers, Request Method. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)\n- Key: Request Headers, `authorization`. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)\n- Package: `content-type`, then Payload. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)\n- Code: Headers, Status Code. Reply package: Response. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)"},{"h":"Three requests to read","a":"three-requests-to-read","text":"- Aarav's service (step 4): a row named `diagnoses`, POST, 201; the reply echoes what it read under `you_sent`. [Network tab, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests)\n- Groq (step 8): `models` is a Read, then `completions` is a POST with 200; the text is at `choices[0].message.content`. Your key is in plain text there. [Network tab, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#03-part-2-your-own-groq-request)\n- ChatGPT, logged in: send \"hi\" and find the request named `conversation`; its method is POST, a Create. [Network tab, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#05-part-4-a-real-product-chatgpt)\n- ChatGPT's reply is `text/event-stream`: many small packages as the model writes, which is why words appear one by one. [Network tab, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#the-reply-arrives-in-pieces)"},{"h":"Never","a":"never","text":"- Never expand, copy or paste the `authorization` and `cookie` headers: together they are your login. [Network tab, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md#find-each-part)\n\nSee also: [Request](../concepts/request.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](keep-your-groq-key-safe.md), [Lecture 4](../lectures/lecture-04.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/run-a-gradio-app-locally.md","title":"Run a Gradio app locally","meta":"title: Run a Gradio app locally\nsummary: Install Python and an IDE, put the Gradio Hello World in app.py, pip install gradio, run python app.py. Lecture 3, code track.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Run a Gradio app locally","a":"run-a-gradio-app-locally","text":"The Lecture 3 setup that gets the Gradio Hello World, then a chat echo, running on your own machine. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Install Python from python.org. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n2. Install one IDE: Google Antigravity, Cursor or VS Code. VS Code is free; Antigravity is free with generous limits; Cursor has a paid plan for its models. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n3. Make a folder, open it in the IDE, create `app.py` and paste the Hello World. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n4. Run `pip install gradio`, then `python app.py`, and open the local link it prints. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n5. For chat, swap `gr.Interface` for `gr.ChatInterface(fn=respond).launch()`, where `respond(message, history)` returns \"You typed: \" + message. [Lecture 3 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)"},{"h":"The Hello World","a":"the-hello-world","text":"```\nimport gradio as gr\n\ndef greet(name):\n    return \"Hello \" + name\n\ndemo = gr.Interface(fn=greet, inputs=\"text\", outputs=\"text\")\ndemo.launch()\n```\n\n- The argument is the input, the body is the process, what follows `return` is the output. [Lecture 3 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)"},{"h":"Good habits","a":"good-habits","text":"- Predict the error you expect before your first `python app.py`. [Practice set 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- Log every error: message, cause, fix. Python not found and Gradio not installed count. [Practice set 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- Learn `gr.Interface` and `gr.ChatInterface` from the Gradio documentation, not from an LLM first. [Practice set 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- If `def`, `return` and functions are unfamiliar, finish the Big Binary Python prerequisite first. [Lecture 3 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n- Stuck on installation: post the exact error message and what you already tried in the code track channel. [Practice set 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#06-when-you-are-stuck)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 3](../lectures/lecture-03.md), [V0 / MVP](../concepts/v0-mvp.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/set-up-python-and-gradio-first-time.md","title":"Set up Python and Gradio as a first-time coder","meta":"title: Set up Python and Gradio as a first-time coder\nsummary: Install Python and an IDE, make app.py, pip install gradio, run Hello World on localhost port 7860, then the chat template. Code track, Building UI.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Set up Python and Gradio as a first-time coder","a":"set-up-python-and-gradio-as-a-first-time-coder","text":"Four parts stand between you and a working app: Python, an IDE, one folder with one file, and two commands. [Office hour guide, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#07-the-setup-four-things)"},{"h":"Before you start","a":"before-you-start","text":"- Write the spec first; the order is spec, setup, template, link. [Checklist](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#the-checklist)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Install Python from python.org/downloads. Windows: tick \"Add Python to PATH\" before clicking Install. Mac: run the installer through. [Office hour guide, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#07-the-setup-four-things)\n2. Test it: type `python --version` (Mac: `python3 --version`) in a terminal; a version number is your tick. [Checklist, 2](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#2-stage-1-setup-six-ticks)\n3. Install one IDE: VS Code is free, Google Antigravity is free with generous limits, Cursor needs a paid plan for its models. Unsure: take VS Code. [Office hour guide, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#07-the-setup-four-things)\n4. Make a folder (for example `100x test` on the desktop), open it with File, Open Folder, and create `app.py` inside it. [Office hour guide, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#07-the-setup-four-things)\n5. Paste the Hello World from the Gradio quickstart, so the first thing you debug is your setup and not your logic. [Office hour guide, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#07-the-setup-four-things)\n6. In the IDE's terminal (View, Terminal) run `pip install gradio` (or `pip3 install gradio`); it ends with `Successfully installed gradio`. [Checklist, 2](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#2-stage-1-setup-six-ticks)\n7. Run `python app.py` (or `python3 app.py`). The terminal prints `http://127.0.0.1:7860`; open it in your browser. [Checklist, 2](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#2-stage-1-setup-six-ticks)\n8. Start an error log: a text file with the error text, what you tried, what fixed it. [Checklist, 2](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#2-stage-1-setup-six-ticks)\n9. Replace Hello World with the chat template and run it unchanged: click the example, get Aarav's plan back; type hi, get the follow-up question. [Checklist, 3](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#3-stage-2-the-template)\n10. Change three things and nothing else: `GOLDEN_INPUT`, `GOLDEN_OUTPUT`, and the trigger word the if statement looks for. [Office hour guide, 08](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#08-the-template-forty-lines-four-that-matter)\n11. Take a screenshot of the browser showing your golden output in the chat. [Checklist, 3](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#3-stage-2-the-template)"},{"h":"Good to know","a":"good-to-know","text":"- Read the Hello World as the whiteboard: the argument is the input, what follows return is the output, the body is the process. [Notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)\n- The template has no intelligence yet; it mocks it with the golden pair. [Office hour guide, 08](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#08-the-template-forty-lines-four-that-matter)\n- Learn Gradio from its documentation, not from an LLM. [Notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)\n- If def, return and functions are unfamiliar, finish the Big Binary Python prerequisite; lectures will not teach Python syntax. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n\nSee also: [Code track: Building UI pages](../lectures/code-track-building-ui.md), [Fix common errors](fix-common-python-and-gradio-errors.md), [Deploy to Hugging Face Spaces](deploy-a-gradio-app-to-hugging-face-spaces.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/use-the-delegation-framework.md","title":"Use the AI Delegation Framework","meta":"title: Use the AI Delegation Framework\nsummary: Sort your job's tasks into automate, augment or stays human by one question, can you check it? Write a verifier, then run ten tests.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Use the AI Delegation Framework","a":"use-the-ai-delegation-framework","text":"A page that helps you decide which of your tasks to hand to AI, which to keep, and how to check the work. [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#ai-delegation-framework)"},{"h":"The idea","a":"the-idea","text":"- Every job is a bundle of tasks. The single question: \"can you check that it was done well?\" [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#the-idea)\n- Automate: a simple rule can check it. Once AI proves itself, it does the task alone. [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#the-idea)\n- Augment: no rule checks it, but you can write down what a good result looks like. AI drafts and you approve. [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#the-idea)\n- Stays human: nothing can check it reliably, such as a hard conversation, work done by hand, a decision you sign. [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#the-idea)\n- The written check is called a verifier. [Delegation framework](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#the-idea)"},{"h":"The steps","a":"the-steps","text":"1. List your tasks from your actual job. Each must repeat (every day or every other day) and matter. Start with three, up to 12, with how often and how long each time. [Step 1](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-1-list-your-tasks)\n2. Write each task the way you would log it: \"send the weekly sales summary to the regional head\", not \"reporting\". [Step 1](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-1-list-your-tasks)\n3. For each task ask how you would know it was done well. If you can't say, keep it for now. If you can, write the verifier: the checklist a good result has to pass. [Step 2](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-2-can-you-check-it)\n4. For an amber (automate) task, write the rule a program could run. For a violet (augment) task, write up to three criteria that two reviewers would apply the same way. [Step 2](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-2-can-you-check-it)\n5. Press Test: Jev checks each verifier is specific enough. A violet task stays with you until its criteria pass. [Step 2](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-2-can-you-check-it)\n6. Sort: each task lands where its check puts it. Change any colour you disagree with, and say whether a mistake would be cheap to undo. [Step 3](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-3-sort-your-tasks)\n7. Give one task to an AI tool and run it ten times. Score every run against your verifier. If your data can't leave your machine, pick a tool that runs locally. [Step 4](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-4-test-then-learn-from-failures)\n8. Keep the failures: correct each one, use it to sharpen your instructions and verifier, then run again. [Step 4](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-4-test-then-learn-from-failures)"},{"h":"Things to know","a":"things-to-know","text":"- The rule: \"if you can't check a task, don't automate it\". [Step 2](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-2-can-you-check-it)\n- Whatever AI takes on adds a new task for you: checking its work (coral on the page). [Step 3](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-3-sort-your-tasks)\n- Example verifier, competitor research for a headphone brand: every rival covered, live sources cited, what changed is flagged, ends with a plan for today. [Step 2](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#step-2-can-you-check-it)\n- Jev is an AI model from TypeSafe. Checking needs a Google login, and each person gets 60 checks a day. [Sources](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#sources)\n- Sorting uses TypeSafe's Jev 1.13 with seven narrow questions; when answers disagree it asks you instead of guessing. [Sources](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#sources)\n- On 300 labelled tasks the sort matches the label 96% of the time (macro-F1 0.96), keeps 92% of tasks that should stay with you, and asks a question on 3%. Verifiers are judged right 97% of the time (19 Oct 2026). [Sources](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#sources)\n- Task suggestions come from O*NET 31.0 and a bank of about 1,500 tasks. [Sources](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#sources)\n- The idea behind step 2 is Jason Wei's verifier's law: tasks easy to check are the ones AI takes over first. [Sources](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#sources)"},{"h":"This week","a":"this-week","text":"- Pick one task that repeats and matters, write its verifier, hand it to AI for ten test runs. [This week](../../raw/practice/delegation-framework.md#this-week)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 1](../lectures/lecture-01.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/use-the-lectures-9-to-12-checklist.md","title":"Use the Lectures 9 to 12 checklist","meta":"title: Use the Lectures 9 to 12 checklist\nsummary: The Lectures 9 to 12 checklist page: 23 items across eight sections, pick your track, tick what you have done, finish the rest before Lecture 13.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Use the Lectures 9 to 12 checklist","a":"use-the-lectures-9-to-12-checklist","text":"One page for both tracks: \"Tick what you have done. Finish the rest before Lecture 13.\" It counts 23 items. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/checklist/ [Checklist](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#lectures-9-to-12)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Pick your track. The list shows your track's lectures and practice sets. [Checklist](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#lectures-9-to-12)\n2. Tick each item as you finish it; the counter at the top shows how many are ready. [Checklist](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#lectures-9-to-12)\n3. Finish the rest before Lecture 13. [Checklist](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#lectures-9-to-12)"},{"h":"What is on it","a":"what-is-on-it","text":"- Lecture 9, 2 items: read the Lecture 9 notes; read Jason Wei on the Verifier's Law. [Checklist, 01](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#01-lecture-9--llms-from-first-principles-0-of-2)\n- Lecture 10, 2 items: read the Lecture 10 notes; read the 3 Nov office hour recap. [Checklist, 02](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#02-lecture-10--tool-calling-and-mcp-0-of-2)\n- Practice set 1, 4 items: write your North Star; fill the sheet with last week's tasks; test one task on the model (golden pair first, then bare and with context, two scores); share your sheet on the Discord. [Checklist, 03](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#03-practice-set-1--verify-your-own-job-0-of-4)\n- Practice set 2, 3 items: work through the Write the eval exercise (about 30 minutes, download your eval sheet at the end); re-score your tested task with it; share your eval on the Discord. [Checklist, 04](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#04-practice-set-2--write-the-eval-0-of-3)\n- Lecture 11, 3 items: read the Lecture 11 notes; finish the five steps on the Tool Bench (bring your Groq key); read the Tool Bench notes. [Checklist, 05](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#05-lecture-11--tool-calling-and-mcp-part-2-0-of-3)\n- Lecture 12, code track, 1 item: read the Lecture 12 notes. [Checklist, 06](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#06-lecture-12--building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp--code-track-0-of-1)\n- Practice set 3, code track, 4 items: Part 1 in tool_call.py; Part 2, put mcp_server.py on the internet; do it once more with your own data; submit your fork and your address on the LMS, due before Lecture 13. [Checklist, 07](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#07-practice-set-3--write-the-code-behind-the-bench--code-track-0-of-4)\n- From sand to language models, 4 items: chapters 1 to 3 (about 48 minutes), chapter 4 (about 30 minutes), chapters 5 and 6 (about 32 minutes), chapters 7 and 8 (about 37 minutes). [Checklist, 08](../../raw/practice/checklist.md#08-from-sand-to-language-models-0-of-4)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [Write a tool call and an MCP server in Python](write-a-tool-call-and-an-mcp-server-in-python.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/write-a-tool-call-and-an-mcp-server-in-python.md","title":"Write a tool call and an MCP server in Python (Practice set 3)","meta":"title: Write a tool call and an MCP server in Python (Practice set 3)\nsummary: Practice set 3 steps (code track, Lecture 12): fork c8-tools, six steps in tool_call.py, five in mcp_server.py, deploy, submit on the LMS.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Write a tool call and an MCP server in Python (Practice set 3)","a":"write-a-tool-call-and-an-mcp-server-in-python-practice-set-3","text":"Practice set 3, \"Write the code behind the bench\": write the code behind each Tool Bench button, on the course sheet, then put the tool on the internet. Due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026. [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#write-the-code-behind-the-bench)"},{"h":"You need","a":"you-need","text":"- Your Groq key from the bench (lost it? make a new one at console.groq.com/keys), Python, and your GitHub account. [Lecture 12 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-overview.md#you-need)\n- Every step is in the c8-tools repo README. [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#write-the-code-behind-the-bench)"},{"h":"Set up","a":"set-up","text":"1. Fork the repo, install, add your Groq key. [Practice set 3, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#01-what-you-write)\n2. Given to you: `get_lecture(number)` (the sheet) and `ask_model(messages, tools)` (the model). [Practice set 3, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#01-what-you-write)"},{"h":"Part 1: the tool call, in tool_call.py","a":"part-1-the-tool-call-in-toolcallpy","text":"Run it after every step. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n\n1. Ask without a card. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n2. Write the card. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n3. Run the request. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n4. Hand the row back. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n5. Let your code carry it. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n6. Test the card. Done when the same file answers a lecture question from the sheet and \"What is an API?\" on its own. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)"},{"h":"Part 2: the MCP server, in mcp_server.py","a":"part-2-the-mcp-server-in-mcpserverpy","text":"1. Move the function. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n2. Write the card into the function. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n3. Switch it on. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n4. Put it on the internet. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n5. Call it from anywhere. Done when `fastmcp call` returns the Lecture 12 row from your address on the internet. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)"},{"h":"Then","a":"then","text":"1. Do it once more with your own Google Sheet, a CSV file, a Supabase table, or an API from this course: your function, your card, your five questions. [Practice set 3, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#04-now-your-own-data)\n2. Submit on the LMS the link to your fork, with the five-question table filled in, and your deployed address. They are gone through in the office hour. [Practice set 3, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#05-submit-it)"},{"h":"If it fails","a":"if-it-fails","text":"- A 429 means wait a minute and run again. [Lecture 12 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#02-step-1-the-card)\n- To test a server locally, run it in one terminal and use `fastmcp list` and `fastmcp call` in a second. [Lecture 12 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [FastMCP](../concepts/fastmcp.md), [Tool](../concepts/tool.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/write-a-tool-card-against-tests.md","title":"Write a tool card against tests","meta":"title: Write a tool card against tests\nsummary: Lecture 11 Tool Bench: mark five questions before the run, then edit the tool card until all five pass; steps for the bench and your own sheet.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Write a tool card against tests","a":"write-a-tool-card-against-tests","text":"Mark what each question should do, then edit the tool card and run until all five pass. Same LLM, same questions; only the card changes. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Open the Tool Bench with your own Groq key from Lecture 4. It stays in your tab and is not stored. [Bench notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#01-the-bench)\n2. Step 3 hands you an empty card named `tool_1`. Do not write yet. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n3. Mark what each of the five questions should do: call the tool (with which number) or answer itself. That is the golden pair from Lecture 10. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n4. Fill the three fields: Name, Use it when, It needs. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n5. Run all five questions and count the passes. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n6. When stuck: say what the tool returns, when to use it, and when not to (any course, or only ours?). Then say exactly what the input is. Change one thing and run again. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n7. Stop when all five pass. If no wording fixes the routing reliably, the server has to check. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)"},{"h":"The five bench tests","a":"the-five-bench-tests","text":"- \"When is Lecture 12 of 100xEngineers Cohort 8, and what is it about?\": call the tool, with 12. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- \"Which tools do we use in the seventh class?\": call the tool, with 7. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- \"What is an API?\": answer itself. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- \"Is session ten a combined one or separate?\": call the tool, with 10. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- \"In lecture 3 of Harvard CS50, which language is taught?\": answer itself. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)"},{"h":"On your own sheet","a":"on-your-own-sheet","text":"1. Share your Practice set 1 sheet: Share, General access, \"Anyone with the link\", Viewer. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n2. Copy your own tab's link, not Aarav's example tab. It ends in `#gid=` and a number. Paste it on the bench after step 5. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n3. Write five questions: three your sheet can answer and two the tool should not be used for. Mark them before you run anything. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n4. Write the card until all pass. The tool searches your rows for the words the LLM sends, so \"It needs\" says what those words should be. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n\nSee also: [Tool card](../concepts/tool-card.md), [Lecture 11](../lectures/lecture-11.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/how-to/write-the-eval.md","title":"Write the eval (Practice set 2)","meta":"title: Write the eval (Practice set 2)\nsummary: Practice set 2, both tracks: turn your Practice set 1 tested task into an eval; three parts, about 45 minutes, before Lecture 11, share on Discord.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Write the eval (Practice set 2)","a":"write-the-eval-practice-set-2","text":"Practice set 2 turns the score you gave by feel into a check anyone could run the same way. It comes after Lectures 9 and 10 and the 3 Nov office hour, for both tracks. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)"},{"h":"What it asks for","a":"what-it-asks-for","text":"- On your own, about 45 minutes. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)\n- Take the task you tested in Practice set 1, section 04, and write its eval: the decision it serves, the inputs, the hard lines, what a 1 and a 5 look like, and who grades. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)\n- Write it yourself, not with an LLM. [Practice set 2, 02](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#02-do-it-0-of-3-ready)"},{"h":"Why","a":"why","text":"- Lecture 9: a model can take over a task as far as its output can be checked. [Practice set 2, 01](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#01-why)\n- Lecture 10: you scored one task out of 10, by feel. Someone else could give the same reply a 4 where you gave it a 7. [Practice set 2, 01](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#01-why)\n- Office hour: a written-down grade is exactly what labs pay experts for, on Mercor and elsewhere. [Practice set 2, 01](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#01-why)\n- From Lecture 11 on, every build ends with \"did it get better?\", and the eval is how you will know. [Practice set 2, 01](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#01-why)"},{"h":"Steps","a":"steps","text":"1. Work through the exercise (the \"Start the exercise\" link): three parts, about 30 minutes. [Practice set 2, 02](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#02-do-it-0-of-3-ready)\n2. Download your eval sheet with \"Download as spreadsheet\" at the end. Fill it in Google Sheets or Excel. [Practice set 2, 02](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#02-do-it-0-of-3-ready)\n3. Re-score your tested task with it: hard lines first, then each quality from 1 to 5, beside your old scores. [Practice set 2, 02](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#02-do-it-0-of-3-ready)\n4. Share it on the Discord. They are gone through in the office hour. [Practice set 2, 03](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#03-share-it)"},{"h":"When it is due","a":"when-it-is-due","text":"- \"Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\" [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)\n- The sheet dates Lecture 11 to Fri 13 Nov 2026. [Sheet, Lecture 11](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp)"},{"h":"Links","a":"links","text":"- Practice set 2 page: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/ [raw](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md)\n- The exercise: [Start the exercise](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/office-hour/) [raw](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 9](../lectures/lecture-09.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/code-track-building-ui.md","title":"Code track: Building UI pages","meta":"title: Code track: Building UI pages\nsummary: The first code-track pages: Building UI notes, the 17 to 13 vote, golden pairs, verifier, Gradio setup, practice set, office hour of 1 October.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Code track: Building UI pages","a":"code-track-building-ui-pages","text":"Six code-track pages that go together, from lecture notes to a troubleshooting page. [Code track pages](../../raw/lectures/code-track-pages.md#building-ui-start-to-link)\n\n- The sheet lists this as Lecture 3, \"Building UI: Design to Deploy\", on Sat 26 Sep 2026, a separate practical. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy)\n- The notes page itself says \"Live Lecture 04 · Code track\". [Building UI notes](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md)\n- The question: how do you pick an interface? The answer: derive it from the output your user needs, by replaying it. [Building UI notes](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#building-ui-design-to-code)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: More than 10 people had done the Week 0 prerequisite. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0)\n- 02: Solve for one named person, Aarav, not a category. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- 03: The cheapest test is three boxes: a text field, a Done button, a plan back; success means saving 1 hour a week. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard)\n- 04: A learner's observation failed the stranger test. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- 05: Write at least 10 to 20 golden pairs before code. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code)\n- 06: The verifier: email step from 15 minutes to 5 saves 50 minutes a week and passes; to 10 saves 25 and fails. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#06-the-verifier-is-a-threshold-worked-in-minutes)\n- 07: Test-driven development, tests now mostly in English. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#07-test-driven-development-written-in-english)\n- 08: Golden pair 1 is the Friday status report, around 3 hours; text in, text out, so a manual MVP is possible. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#08-two-systems-one-interface)\n- 09: The room voted search 17, form 3, chat 13; the follow-up \"What does backtesting mean?\" made chat the winner. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- 10: Vibe code or first principles: the difference is control. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#10-vibe-code-or-first-principles-the-difference-is-control)\n- 11 to 13: Why Gradio, the four-line Hello World, setup. [Notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#11-why-python-and-why-gradio)\n- Office hour, 1 October 2026: specs reviewed, a forty-line template run live. [Office hour guide](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#spec-first-then-forty-lines)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Golden pairs: 10 to 20 to start, 100 to 200 for production. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Around 90% of first problems fit search, form or chat. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- Gradio runs on port 7860, at http://127.0.0.1:7860. [Beginner kit, 4](../../raw/lectures/code-track-beginner-kit.md#4-words-you-will-meet)\n- Hugging Face: two free Spaces, ZeroGPU, account must be 30 days old. [Beginner kit, 5](../../raw/lectures/code-track-beginner-kit.md#5-deploy-links-when-you-are-ready-not-before)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Golden dataset: ideal input and output pairs, from real users. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Verifier: the check that decides if an output is good, usually a threshold against a baseline. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Spec: observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Manual MVP: running the input to output loop by hand before building any technology. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- V0 / MVP: the cheapest build that can test the hypothesis, and nothing more. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md#15-vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Mocked intelligence: returning the golden output from a stored string. [Office hour guide](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#vocabulary-installed-tonight)\n- Follow-up cost: how cheap it is for the user to revise a request. [Office hour guide](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md#vocabulary-installed-tonight)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Practice set \"Replay Before You Render\": Track A is your own problem (preferred), Track B uses Aarav's documents plus 3 new pairs. [Practice set](../../raw/lectures/code-track-practice-set.md#02-track-a-your-own-problem-preferred)\n- Submit 8 deliverables, from the spec to a three-line review. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/lectures/code-track-practice-set.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Post the spec in the code track channel before the next code lecture. [Checklist, 5](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#5-actions-this-week)\n- Assignment 1 (First Proof) due Friday 9 October. [Checklist, 5](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#5-actions-this-week)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Lecture notes: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md)\n- Practice set: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/practice-set), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-practice-set.md)\n- Office hour guide, setup and deploy: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md)\n- Checklist of ticks and deliverables: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md)\n- Beginner kit of links and commands: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-beginner-kit.md)\n- Troubleshooting errors: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/troubleshooting), [raw](../../raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 3](lecture-03.md), [Set up Python and Gradio](../how-to/set-up-python-and-gradio-first-time.md), [Deploy to Hugging Face Spaces](../how-to/deploy-a-gradio-app-to-hugging-face-spaces.md), [Fix common errors](../how-to/fix-common-python-and-gradio-errors.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/hackathon.md","title":"Hackathon (17 to 20 Oct 2026)","meta":"title: Hackathon (17 to 20 Oct 2026)\nsummary: Hackathon Sat 17 to Tue 20 Oct 2026, 9 PM IST, results Thu 22 Oct: three problems (BillShield, Eval Co-pilot, Concept Check), nine rules, six check-ins.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Hackathon (17 to 20 Oct 2026)","a":"hackathon-17-to-20-oct-2026","text":"A solo build on both tracks: \"Build an app in 72 hours, verified by a stranger.\" You pass when a real person uses what you built. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#build-an-app-in-72-hours-verified-by-a-stranger)"},{"h":"Dates","a":"dates","text":"- Starts Sat 17 Oct 2026 and runs Sat to Tue. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue)\n- Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue)\n- Lock your problem by Sunday, 9 AM (Check-in 1). [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#pick-one-problem)\n- Results on Thu 22 Oct. Submissions are reviewed in the 8:30 PM office hour. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#72-hours-six-check-ins)"},{"h":"The three problems","a":"the-three-problems","text":"- BillShield: \"Turn a confusing bill into one message worth sending.\" Test with a classmate with a real bill; you win when they send the message. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#pick-one-problem)\n- BillShield teaches where code must decide, because a model can't be trusted to add. Every number in the message comes from code. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#draw-the-line)\n- Eval Co-pilot: catch the bug in a friend's AI feature before they ship. You win when they fix it and re-run. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#pick-one-problem)\n- Eval Co-pilot teaches how to verify the verifier: grade three pairs by hand first, and an AI judge that disagrees with you on those does not judge. [Eval Co-pilot](../../raw/practice/hackathon-eval-copilot.md#draw-the-line)\n- Concept Check: \"Find where a learner stops understanding and starts reciting.\" You win when they work out a new case. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#pick-one-problem)\n- Concept Check teaches how to judge a judgment about someone's mind; the no-go is the model deciding on its own that they understand. [Concept Check](../../raw/practice/hackathon-concept-check.md#draw-the-line)\n- Every problem also needs one stranger to test it: someone who has never seen your app and gets no help from you. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#billshield)"},{"h":"Five moves, in each brief","a":"five-moves-in-each-brief","text":"- Discovery: do it by hand, Green by Sun 3 PM. Collect ten golden pairs. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#five-moves-on-this-problem)\n- User interface: needs first, Green by Sun 9 PM. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#five-moves-on-this-problem)\n- System design: draw the line between what code decides and what AI decides, Green by Sun 9 PM. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#five-moves-on-this-problem)\n- Data: drawn Sun 9 PM, locked Mon 9 PM. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#five-moves-on-this-problem)\n- User testing: watch them use it, Tue 9 AM, then Tue 9 PM. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#five-moves-on-this-problem)"},{"h":"Check-ins","a":"check-ins","text":"- Six check-ins, all mandatory: one thread titled with your name, each check-in a reply, first word Green or Yellow, one proof attached. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#how-to-check-in)\n- Yellow means behind or blocked, say why. No post is Red and graded negative. An hour late still counts. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#how-to-check-in)"},{"h":"The rule book (nine rules)","a":"the-rule-book-nine-rules","text":"- Time: work after Tue 20 Oct, 9 PM IST does not count. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- One problem, locked by Check-in 1, no switching. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Solo: testers give feedback but never write, design or build any part. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Any AI tool is allowed. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Paper first: sketches, need lists and tables start on paper, photographed. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Every feature traces back to a need written down in Move 2. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Deployed at a live link: \"a screenshot is not an app\". [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Real evidence only; faked evidence fails the whole submission. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Consent: ask before recording anyone, hide names and account numbers. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)"},{"h":"What to hand in","a":"what-to-hand-in","text":"- By Tue, 9 PM: the filled template, a public repo, a live link. [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#submit-by-tue-9-pm)\n- \"No stranger test, no submission.\" [Hackathon page](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#submit-by-tue-9-pm)\n- Each brief ends with its own template to copy into a doc and fill as you go. [BillShield](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md#your-filled-template)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Hackathon: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/ [raw](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md)\n- BillShield brief: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield [raw](../../raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md)\n- Eval Co-pilot brief: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot [raw](../../raw/practice/hackathon-eval-copilot.md)\n- Concept Check brief: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check [raw](../../raw/practice/hackathon-concept-check.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8](lecture-08.md), [Aarav's example](../threads/aaravs-example.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-01.md","title":"Lecture 1: Orientation and State of AI 2026","meta":"title: Lecture 1: Orientation and State of AI 2026\nsummary: Lecture 1 (Sat 19 Sep 2026, both tracks): the Verifier's Law, the 2% editor project, the verify game, the three boxes, golden pairs task.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 1: Orientation and State of AI 2026","a":"lecture-1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026","text":"Held Sat 19 Sep 2026, both tracks, combined theory. [Sheet, code track](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-19-sep-2026--1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026)\n\nQuestion: why do models improve while businesses earn little from them? Answer: \"a verifier gap\". [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-overview.md#orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Week 0: Assignment 1 observation and discovery done, MVP plan still to do. 56 unique industries in the room. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#01-where-we-stood-week-0-two-of-three-done)\n- A 2023 newspaper project aimed at 10 percent of P&L in time saved and got 2% after eight months. An editor: \"this is good, but I still have to spend my time on it\". [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#02-one-sentence-from-an-editor-2023)\n- The room's diagnoses (wrong metric, incomplete brief, stakeholders) were true but not the load-bearing failure. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#03-the-room-diagnoses-it)\n- Four easy answers were killed: a better model, better prompts, more data (the fine-tune did worse), more time. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#04-killing-the-four-easy-answers)\n- Missing: only the editors knew what a good article looked like, unwritten, so checking cost as much as writing. Hence the Verifier's Law. [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)\n- Two reports: capability climbs, EBIT stays flat. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#06-same-failure-at-scale-two-reports)\n- Over 5.5 billion dollars in one quarter went to engineers inside enterprises, not models. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#07-follow-the-money)\n- Verifier example: 15 Ogilvy-inspired criteria scoring copy out of 100, looped until the threshold. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria)\n- Round one: an app that adds two numbers gave input, process, output and the golden dataset. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- Round two: high school exam, pass mark 33 percent, score 80 percent, output yes. A rule-based verifier. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#10-round-two-has-someone-cleared-the-high-school-exam)\n- Round three: a Rolls-Royce refund, about 7.5 crore. A rule can be gamed, so ask a support executive with ten years of refunds: judgment. [Notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund)\n- The three boxes: verifiable by a rule (weeks 1 to 4), by judgment (weeks 4 to 8), by nothing yet (the last 4 weeks). [Notes 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- McKinsey 2026: 1,719 respondents; 80% say AI improved their productivity; 37% report any positive EBIT contribution; 6% high performers. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#06-same-failure-at-scale-two-reports)\n- Stanford HAI 2026: SWE-bench Verified about 60% to near 100%; 88% of organisations adopted AI; $285.9B US private AI investment in 2025. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#06-same-failure-at-scale-two-reports)\n- FDE roles grew about 1,000%. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#04-killing-the-four-easy-answers)\n- Ode: $1.5B; OpenAI: $4B+, about 150 FDEs; Accenture and Google Cloud: up to 1,000 FDEs. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#07-follow-the-money)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Verifier: \"A written judgment of how you decide an output is good, usable by a stranger or a model without you.\" [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Deterministic system: verifiable by a rule. Probabilistic system: output varies for the same input. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Human in the loop: where judgment cannot be articulated, so cannot transfer to a model. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Loop engineering: designing the loop of verifier, threshold, improve, re-score, stop. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Catastrophic forgetting: full fine-tuning overwrites weights holding prior knowledge. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- FDE: forward-deployed engineer, placed inside an enterprise. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Assignment 1 step three: publish your MVP tracker. Due Fri 9 Oct. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Name your high-judgment task in one line: input, process, output. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Write three golden pairs by hand, plus one you would reject. No LLM. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Stranger test: if someone outside your field cannot verify an output without asking you, it is not a verifier yet. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: one-line summary, links into the notes, \"Done equals\". [Public page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-overview.md)\n- Notes: what was said, with whiteboard sketches. [Public page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md)\n- Office hour, Tue 22 Sep 2026: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/office-hour), [raw](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [The Verifier's Law](../concepts/verifiers-law.md), [Verifier](../concepts/verifier.md), [Golden dataset](../concepts/golden-dataset.md), [Input, process, output](../concepts/input-process-output.md), [Judgment-based verification](../concepts/judgment-based-verification.md), [Lecture 2](lecture-02.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-02.md","title":"Lecture 2: What is an interface; UI as the first interface","meta":"title: Lecture 2: What is an interface; UI as the first interface\nsummary: Lecture 2 (Fri 25 Sep 2026, both tracks): the smiley game, interface as common language plus protocol, UI, API, HTML, HTTP, and next steps.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 2: What is an interface; UI as the first interface","a":"lecture-2-what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface","text":"Lecture 2 was on Fri 25 Sep 2026 for both tracks, a combined theory lecture. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-25-sep-2026--2-what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface)\n\nIt asked how two systems that do not understand each other communicate. Answer: an interface, built from a common language and a protocol. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-overview.md#what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: Three bins: checkable by a rule (weeks 1 to 4), by judgment (an LLM), or not at all: specific knowledge (weeks 8 to 12). [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#01-one-principle-three-kinds-of-verification)\n- 02: The summit map's four bridges: Assignment 1, the hackathon, Assignment 2, the final capstone. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#02-the-summit-map-two-mountains-four-bridges)\n- 03: Round one: Siddhant acted as a machine; the room had to get a smiley drawn. Nobody asked which language the machine understands. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#03-round-one-a-hundred-programmers-one-machine-no-smiley)\n- 04: Round two gave a command list and still failed: underspecified commands, English instead of commands, no coordination. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#04-round-two-a-common-language-still-no-smiley)\n- 05: The fix: one point of contact. Language plus protocol is an interface. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them)\n- 06 to 08: Phone call, letter, meme: name the two systems first; the wire and the address are not the interface. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#07-the-letter-find-the-two-systems-first)\n- 09: ChatGPT: the whole screen is the user interface (UI), the first interface. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface)\n- 10: Chrome's Inspect tool, Network tab. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#10-behind-the-screen-the-inspect-tool)\n- 11: The boxes: machine, browser, UI, the company's machine. [Notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#11-the-boxes-where-the-ui-actually-sits)\n- 12: chatgpt.com becomes an IP address; the bridge between two machines is the API. [Notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface)\n- 13: Binary is shared by every machine; Tim Berners-Lee built HTML and HTTP. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote)\n- 14: Over the internet, the API is an HTTP API. [Notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#14-one-abstraction-many-apis)\n- 15: Q&A: the code track needs no HTML or CSS; find 5 to 10 people for whom the problem is painful. [Notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#15-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Round one lasted two minutes; more than 100 people failed. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#03-round-one-a-hundred-programmers-one-machine-no-smiley)\n- An end-to-end system deployed by about 17 October. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#02-the-summit-map-two-mountains-four-bridges)\n- Agents: about 6 lectures, then 4 weeks of capstone. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#02-the-summit-map-two-mountains-four-bridges)\n- From Lecture 3 the tracks split: code with Siddhant, no-code with Rahul. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#02-the-summit-map-two-mountains-four-bridges)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Specific knowledge: what you do better than anyone and cannot hand to a stranger. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Common language: a vocabulary both systems understand. Protocol: the rules both sides follow. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Interface: where two separate systems meet and communicate. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- GUI, UI: the interface between a user and a machine. Operating system: lets a human use a machine's hardware. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- IP address: the numeric address of a machine on the internet. API: the interface between two machines. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- HTML: the common language for structuring web pages. HTTP: the protocol for requests and responses. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Binary: zeros and ones. Meme: a unit of culture spread by imitation. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Replay round two as valid commands. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Inspect one daily app in the Network tab; name three kinds of file that came back. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Draw that app's boxes, labelling the interface between each pair. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Code track: finish the Big Binary Python exercise before Lecture 3. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Keep Assignment 1 moving (due Fri 9 Oct) and log every change to your observation and hypothesis. [Notes, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md#16-what-to-do-next)\n- Next: Lecture 3, building UI, Sat 26 Sep. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-overview.md#done-equals)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: the lecture in one line and what done equals. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/ [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-overview.md)\n- Notes, with whiteboard sketches. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md)\n\nSee also: [Interface](../concepts/interface.md), [Protocol](../concepts/protocol.md), [Common language](../concepts/common-language.md), [API](../concepts/api.md), [User interface](../concepts/user-interface.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-03-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 3, no-code track: PRD to UI design to frontend app","meta":"title: Lecture 3, no-code track: PRD to UI design to frontend app\nsummary: Lecture 3, no-code track (Sat 26 Sep 2026): one salon's haircut preview from four questions to a flow, a PRD and design.md, screens in Stitch, an Android app from Google AI Studio.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 3, no-code track: PRD to UI design to frontend app","a":"lecture-3-no-code-track-prd-to-ui-design-to-frontend-app","text":"Lecture 3 for the no-code track, \"PRD to UI design to frontend app\", was on Sat 26 Sep 2026. Tools: Google Stitch, Google AI Studio. [No-code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-prd-to-ui-design-to-frontend-app)\n\nIt took one salon's problem from four questions to a flow, two files, screens in Stitch and an Android app on a phone, and wrote no code. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#decide-what-it-does-then-draw-it)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- A boutique salon with no software: nobody sees the result before the cut. The app makes a preview of the style on the client's own face. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#01-a-haircut-nobody-sees-before-the-cut)\n- Four questions before any screen: the user, the problem, the most important moment, success. Screens are the last of four boxes. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#02-four-questions-before-any-screen)\n- The barber has to be inside the flow. The product is a website that also works like an app on a phone. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#03-who-touches-it-and-where)\n- The flow first, then six screens: Home, Style, Camera, Preview, Booking, Profile. Every screen has four states. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#04-the-flow-first-then-the-screens)\n- Learn the names: 13 popular styles, about 40 to 45 common components. \"The more exact your words, the more the result is yours.\" [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#05-learn-the-names-of-what-you-see)\n- A Claude skill, Discovery to PRD, takes your two First Proof files and hands back the PRD and design.md. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#06-write-it-into-two-files)\n- In Stitch: choose App, upload design.md and the logo, paste the whole PRD, then section 14. One spoken paragraph with no files drew screens that did not look like the salon. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#07-stitch-draws-the-screens)\n- Feed changes back by voice, by text on many screens, or by pointing; the salon app took about five rounds. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#08-the-first-output-is-never-the-design)\n- Export the screens from Stitch as a zip. In AI Studio choose \"Build an Android app\" before you type, attach each screen's picture and design.md, and click Build. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The PRD has 14 sections; section 14 is the screens, with the user flow. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#07-stitch-draws-the-screens)\n- Only three screens is normal at first; the salon's section 14 lists seven. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#07-stitch-draws-the-screens)\n- The build took 5 to 10 minutes; one learner's took 15. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- Developer mode on the instructor's Samsung: tap \"Build number\" 10 to 15 times. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Product thinking: \"Deciding what deserves to exist before deciding what it looks like.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- Touchpoint: \"The place where a person meets your product\". [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- UX: \"What happens to the user, step by step.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- CTA: \"The one button that tells the user what to do next.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- State: loading, empty, error or success. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- Component: \"A named part of a screen\". [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- PRD: \"One file that says what the product must do.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- design.md: \"One file that says how the product should look and behave\". [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- Front end: \"The part of an app the user sees and taps.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Your turn, with your own idea: run the Discovery to PRD skill, generate screens in Stitch, keep three things, change three and remove one, build it in AI Studio. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-your-idea-as-far-as-ai-studio)\n- \"Change the design in Stitch. Come to AI Studio when you are happy with it.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work)\n- Next: the app goes into Antigravity. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-your-idea-as-far-as-ai-studio)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 3, code track](lecture-03.md), [Lecture 5, no-code track](lecture-05-no-code.md), [User interface](../concepts/user-interface.md), [Assignment 1, First Proof](../how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-03.md","title":"Lecture 3: Building UI, design to deploy","meta":"title: Lecture 3: Building UI, design to deploy\nsummary: Lecture 3 (Sat 26 Sep 2026, code track): 10 golden pairs, a verifier, the 17 to 13 vote a follow-up overturned, Gradio in four lines.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 3: Building UI, design to deploy","a":"lecture-3-building-ui-design-to-deploy","text":"Lecture 3, Sat 26 Sep 2026, was a separate practical: each track had its own session. [Code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy)\n\n- Code track: \"Building UI: Design to Deploy\", tools Python, Gradio or Streamlit, Hugging Face Spaces. [Code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy)\n- No-code track: \"PRD to UI design to frontend app\", tools Google Stitch, Google AI Studio. [No-code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-prd-to-ui-design-to-frontend-app)\n- The notes and practice set here are the code track's, taught by Siddhant Goswami. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md)\n- The question: how do you pick an interface? Answer: write the ideal input and output first, replay what your user does with the output, and the interface falls out of the replay. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-overview.md)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: Room check on week 0: more than 10 hands. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0)\n- 02: Solve for one named person, not a category. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- 03: The cheapest test is three boxes; it passes only if the plan saves about 1 hour a week. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard)\n- 04: A learner's spec, marked live: observation too generic, hypothesis with no number. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test)\n- 05: Write 10 to 20 golden pairs before any code. [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code)\n- 06: The verifier as a threshold: 15 minutes a day cut to 5 saves 50 minutes a week (pass); cut to 10 saves 25 (fail). [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#06-the-verifier-is-a-threshold-worked-in-minutes)\n- 07: TDD, now written in English. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#07-test-driven-development-written-in-english)\n- 08: Text in, text out, so a manual MVP is possible. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#08-two-systems-one-interface)\n- 09: The vote: search box 17, chat 13, form 3. Replaying the follow-up \"What does backtesting mean?\" made chat the winner. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- 10: Vibe code or first principles: the difference is control. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#10-vibe-code-or-first-principles-the-difference-is-control)\n- 11: Why Python and Gradio. [Notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#11-why-python-and-why-gradio)\n- 12: Gradio Hello World maps to the whiteboard. [Notes 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard)\n- 13: Setting up: Python, one IDE, `app.py`, `pip install gradio`. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight)\n- 14: Q&A: solution-first projects, changing hypotheses, the North Star. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#14-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Assignment 1 deadline: 9 October. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0)\n- Aarav's hypothesis: 60% of active students within 90 days; at 10 people, 6 of 10. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona)\n- Golden dataset: 10 to 20 to start, 100 to 200 for production. [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code)\n- About 90% of first problems fit search, form or chat. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)\n- First-time coders across cohorts: about 60-40. [Notes 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#14-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Observation, Hypothesis, Designing, Golden dataset, Verifier, TDD, Spec, User interface, Manual MVP, V0 / MVP, Process, IDE. [Notes 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)\n- Process: \"The function that turns input into output.\" [Notes 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)\n- IDE: \"An editor for reading, writing and reviewing code on your machine.\" [Notes 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)\n- TDD: \"Write the test before the software; now the test is often in English.\" [Notes 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#15-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Practice set \"Replay before you render\": Track A, your own problem (preferred), or Track B, Aarav's documents with 3 new pairs and a different follow-up. [Practice set 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#03-track-b-aaravs-documents)\n- Submit: spec, at least 10 golden pairs, verifier, replay note, localhost screenshots, `app.py`, error log, three-line review. [Practice set 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#05-what-to-submit-0-of-8-ready)\n- Gradio only, no deployment yet; post in the code track channel. [Practice set 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- No due date is written; the next office hour reviews specs and error logs and puts the app on a shareable link. [Practice set 07](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#07-after-you-submit)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-overview.md)\n- Notes, with sketches: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md)\n- Practice set: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/practice-set), [raw](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md)\n- No-code: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md)\n- Office hour, Thu 1 Oct 2026: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/office-hour), [raw](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [No-code](lecture-03-no-code.md), [Lecture 2](lecture-02.md), [Lecture 4](lecture-04.md), [Spec](../concepts/spec.md), [Run a Gradio app locally](../how-to/run-a-gradio-app-locally.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-04.md","title":"Lecture 4: API as the second interface","meta":"title: Lecture 4: API as the second interface\nsummary: Lecture 4 (Sat 03 Oct 2026, both tracks): Round Three, the six parts of a request and reply, CRUD, the Request Builder and practice set.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 4: API as the second interface","a":"lecture-4-api-as-the-second-interface","text":"Lecture 4, \"API as the second interface\", was on Sat 03 Oct 2026, for both tracks. [Sheet, code track](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface) [Sheet, no-code track](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface)\n\nIt set out to answer: **how does your UI talk to a machine it has never met?** [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#01-a-face-with-no-brain)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- A face with no brain: the UI handed Aarav's workflow straight back; the brain, a model, lives on another machine. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#01-a-face-with-no-brain)\n- Round Three: the task was to get a diagnosis of Aarav's Friday report through the Zoom chat, and the machine answered every message with a number and at most five words. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- Each reply forced one part: 429 one sender, 404 address, 400 and 405 action, 401 key, 400 package, until 201 Created. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- The key pasted in the public chat got \"401 Key leaked. Revoked.\" so the winning message was sent by DM. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- A READ before any diagnosis existed came back 200 with an empty list: a 200 means your request was fine, not that you got what you wanted. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes)\n- The six parts were named: address is the URL, action the method, key a header, package the body in JSON, code the status code, plus the response body. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#03-the-six-parts-named)\n- The CRUD quiz: Aarav's actions sorted into Create, Read, Update, Delete and spelled POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete)\n- A Google search is a GET (a Read); asking an LLM is a POST (a Create), because a new reply is made. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#google-search-versus-asking-an-llm)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Request Builder: ten steps, about 90 minutes. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-overview.md#your-path-this-week)\n- Practice set: five deliverables, about two hours in total. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#02-how-it-works)\n- Groq free tier: 30 requests a minute and 1,000 a day on `openai/gpt-oss-120b`, per account, not per key. [Practice set, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#05-rules)\n- `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` was retired for free and developer accounts on 16 Aug 2026. [Troubleshooting, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md#02-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- 2xx worked, 4xx you sent something wrong, 5xx their machine failed. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#05-what-you-do-this-week)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- API: the interface between two machines: a common language plus a protocol. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)\n- Request: address, action, key, package. Response: code and package. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)\n- URL / endpoint: the machine (domain) and the door (path). Method: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)\n- CRUD, header, Bearer, body, JSON, status code, HTTPS, REST and backend are defined in the notes' word list. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#07-words-you-met)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Hand in five things: a 201 from Aarav's service, the break-it table, a Groq 200 twice, where your key belongs, and your own API design. [Practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready)\n- Due \"before the Lecture 5 practical on Fri 9 Oct\", in one thread in the Discord channel. [Practice set, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#06-where-to-post)\n- Office hour: Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST; milestone steps 1 to 8 done and a draft API table. [Notes, Dates](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#dates)\n- Assignment 1 was due Fri 9 Oct, and the practice set does not depend on it. [Notes, Dates](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md#dates)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-overview.md)\n- Lecture notes: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md)\n- Request Builder, the lab: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md)\n- Practice set: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set), [raw](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md)\n- Kit, links and commands: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-kit.md)\n- Keys, where a key lives: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md)\n- Reading the Network tab: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md)\n- Troubleshooting, errors by code: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)\n- The Wire, optional deep dive: [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire), [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md)\n\nSee also: [CRUD](../concepts/crud.md), [Status code](../concepts/status-code.md), [Request](../concepts/request.md), [JSON](../concepts/json.md), [Backend](../concepts/backend.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](../how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md), [Read the network tab](../how-to/read-the-network-tab.md), [Fix a failed request](../how-to/fix-a-failed-request.md), [Lecture 2](lecture-02.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-05-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 5, no-code track: Building APIs and backend","meta":"title: Lecture 5, no-code track: Building APIs and backend\nsummary: Lecture 5, no-code track (Fri 9 Oct 2026): the Magic Mirror app moves from Google AI Studio into Antigravity; a map of the files, pseudocode, keys in .env, one click traced with Groq, Replicate left to wire.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 5, no-code track: Building APIs and backend","a":"lecture-5-no-code-track-building-apis-and-backend","text":"Lecture 5 for the no-code track, \"Building APIs and backend\", was on Fri 09 Oct 2026. Tool: Antigravity. [No-code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend)\n\nThe room moved the app into Antigravity, mapped its files, directed the code in plain English, and called two outside services. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#open-the-project-read-before-you-approve)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- The Magic Mirror app (a customer uploads a photo, picks a hairstyle and sees the new look) was still a prototype, with no backend. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#01-what-the-app-is-still-missing)\n- In AI Studio click Code, Export, Export to Antigravity. If export still fails, go through GitHub: push, then clone. Only the direct export brings the chat. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#02-move-the-project-into-antigravity)\n- Ask for a map before you change anything. \"A request that names the file does not\" let the LLM guess. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#03-ask-for-a-map-before-you-change-anything)\n- Read the code as pseudocode: \"Write everything in pseudocode, and explain in plain English in maximum one para.\" [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#04-read-the-code-as-plain-english)\n- Antigravity plans a change first. Read the plan, predict, question, approve or reject, then test. [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#05-read-predict-question-then-approve)\n- Three parts: the front end, the backend, the database. The user sees only the front end. \"Never trust the browser with your secrets.\" [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#06-the-screen-the-worker-and-the-memory)\n- The secret keys go in the .env file, which you never commit to Git, even to a private repository. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#07-where-the-keys-go-and-five-rules)\n- Trace one click on a hairstyle recommender that calls Groq: no key gives an error, a key gives a reply in JSON, a field left out gives a bad request that names it. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#08-trace-one-click-with-groq)\n- Replicate offers many image generators through one API. GPT Image takes your OpenAI key, the two images and a prompt. The wiring is yours. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Aim to know 60 to 70% of what sits where. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#03-ask-for-a-map-before-you-change-anything)\n- Five rules for the coding tool: small components, reusable functions, clear names, screens apart from logic, no repeats. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#07-where-the-keys-go-and-five-rules)\n- A Groq key is shown once, so save it. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#08-trace-one-click-with-groq)\n- Before you believe \"complete\", test failure, storage and impatience: a double click, or a 10 second wait. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Prototype: \"An app you can click through, with no backend underneath yet.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- IDE: the app where you open a project's files. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- Repository: \"One project's files, kept on GitHub.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- Clone: \"To download a copy of a repository.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- Pseudocode: \"A program's logic in plain English, step by step. It does not run.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- Front end: the screens. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- Database: \"The part of an app that remembers\". [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)\n- .env: \"The file that holds a project's secret keys. Never pushed to GitHub.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Your turn: sign up on Replicate and pick an image generator. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-wire-the-image-api)\n- Before you prompt, write the flow in order: the input, the steps, the APIs, what is stored, the output. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-wire-the-image-api)\n- Ask Antigravity to build it, and run the loop from section 05 on every change. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-wire-the-image-api)\n- \"List each place you get stuck, and bring the list to the office hour.\" [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-wire-the-image-api)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes-no-code [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 5, code track](lecture-05.md), [Lecture 3, no-code track](lecture-03-no-code.md), [Lecture 7, no-code track](lecture-07-no-code.md), [Backend](../concepts/backend.md), [API](../concepts/api.md), [Environment variable](../concepts/environment-variable.md), [Repository](../concepts/repository.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](../how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-05.md","title":"Lecture 5: Building APIs and backend","meta":"title: Lecture 5: Building APIs and backend\nsummary: Lecture 5 (Fri 9 Oct 2026, code track): a raw requests.post to Groq replaces the echo chat; status codes, endpoint, free tier limits, practice set.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 5: Building APIs and backend","a":"lecture-5-building-apis-and-backend","text":"Lecture 5, \"Building APIs and backend\", was on Fri 09 Oct 2026. [Code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend)\n\nThe tracks differ: the code track sheet lists Python, FastAPI, Render; the no-code track sheet lists Antigravity. [Code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend), [No-code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend)\n\nThe notes, \"APIs: Rip Off the Cushion\", are for the code track: from an echo chat to a raw POST request that plans Aarav's week. [Lecture 5 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#apis-rip-off-the-cushion)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- The Gradio chat from the last code lecture had input and output but no process in between. [Lecture 5 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#01-where-we-left-off-a-chat-that-repeats-you)\n- A spec has three parts: the person, the golden dataset and the verifier. [Lecture 5 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#02-the-verifier-most-people-skipped)\n- The task: fill the process with a Groq key and GPT OSS 120B, free at 30 requests per minute. [Lecture 5 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#04-filling-the-process-with-one-line)\n- In four minutes, only one of the first three learner answers ran. [Lecture 5 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#05-four-minutes-and-what-the-machine-said)\n- The code that ran gave a plan full of code, which Aarav cannot read: working is not the same as useful. [Lecture 5 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#06-working-is-not-the-same-as-useful)\n- Groq's quickstart failed with Error 404 because the model `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` did not exist. [Lecture 5 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#08-go-to-the-source-and-read-the-404)\n- The request was tested in httpie.io/app; `api.groq.com` alone failed until the full endpoint was used. [Lecture 5 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#10-where-is-the-url-four-principles-in-the-code)\n- The content is a fixed instruction plus Aarav's changing workflow, judged against the golden pair. [Lecture 5 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#11-prompt--input--package)\n- The SDK hides the URL and POST behind abstraction. [Lecture 5 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides)\n- The same call was rebuilt with `requests.post`, the key read from the environment. [Lecture 5 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost)\n- The plan drifted into jargon, so the prompt changed: no jargon, one step at a time, the minimum action needed now. [Lecture 5 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#14-reading-the-plan-as-aarav-would)\n- The raw version is provider-agnostic: swap the URL and key for OpenAI's and the same code runs. [Lecture 5 notes, 15](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#15-any-machine-four-lines)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Groq free tier: 30 requests per minute, roughly 200,000 tokens and 1,000 requests a day. [Lecture 5 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#09-free-is-a-design-decision)\n- Status codes: 2xx worked, 4xx your mistake, 5xx theirs. [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)\n- 429 means more than 30 requests in a minute on your Groq account. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)\n- Endpoint: `https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions`. [Troubleshooting, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Process, golden dataset, verifier, deterministic system, endpoint, DNS, status code, abstraction, SDK, environment variable, rate limit, JQL. [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)\n- JQL: Jira Query Language, which the plan suggested for pulling Aarav's tickets. [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)\n- DNS: Domain Name System: finds which server a domain points to. [Lecture 5 notes, 17](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#17-words-you-met)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Submit Assignment 1: \"The deadline was 9 October; the late window is open with a penalty.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 18](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#18-before-the-next-office-hour)\n- The practice set: connect your Gradio app to an LLM with a raw `requests.post`, checked against your golden pair. [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#building-apis)\n- Hand in six things: app screenshot, code file, raw request screenshot, prediction log, verification note, the 201 question. [Practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-6-ready)\n- No Groq or OpenAI SDK in the final file. [Practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n- Post on the C8 Discord code channel; earliest posts are reviewed first. No due date is written. [Practice set, 09](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#09-where-to-post-and-how-to-get-unblocked)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview ([page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/)). [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-overview.md)\n- Notes ([page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes)). [Lecture 5 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md)\n- Troubleshooting: each error, its cause and fix ([page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting)). [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md)\n- Practice set ([page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set)). [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md)\n- No-code notes ([page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes-no-code)). [No-code notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md)\n\nSee also: [No-code track](lecture-05-no-code.md), [Endpoint](../concepts/endpoint.md), [Abstraction](../concepts/abstraction.md), [SDK](../concepts/sdk.md), [Environment variable](../concepts/environment-variable.md), [Rate limit](../concepts/rate-limit.md), [Fix a failed LLM API request](../how-to/fix-a-failed-llm-api-request.md), [Call an LLM with requests.post](../how-to/call-an-llm-with-requests-post.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-06.md","title":"Lecture 6: Databases and domain modelling","meta":"title: Lecture 6: Databases and domain modelling\nsummary: Lecture 6 (Sat 10 Oct, both tracks): Aarav's app forgets, memory.txt, storage on your server, the HLD, domain modelling, keys and pointers, Modelling Lab, practice set due Thu 15 Oct.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 6: Databases and domain modelling","a":"lecture-6-databases-and-domain-modelling","text":"Lecture 6, \"Databases and domain modelling\": Sat 10 Oct 2026, both tracks, combined theory. [No-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-10-oct-2026--6-databases-and-domain-modelling), [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-10-oct-2026--6-databases-and-domain-modelling)\n\nQuestion: Aarav's app can think but cannot remember, so what is worth storing? [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-overview.md)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- So far: UI to your server to the LLM on Groq's machine; each call needs \"address, action, key, package\". [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#01-the-whiteboard-so-far)\n- Outsource your thinking, not your understanding: a retired Llama 3 model in Groq's docs gives a 404. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#02-outsource-your-thinking-not-your-understanding)\n- The LLM sits outside your server because of its size. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#03-where-should-each-piece-live)\n- Aarav's app forgets: on Tuesday the call returns a 200, but the model has no idea which plan he means. Every request starts from nothing. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#04-aaravs-app-forgets)\n- Memory dies when the app stops. Storage sits outside the app, on disk. [Whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#memory-dies-storage-survives)\n- The fix, without saying \"database\": keep a `memory.txt`. After every reply, write down who said what, with the date and time. On the next request, send the file along with the prompt. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- So `memory.txt` stores one shape: who, what, when. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Start with the file and move up only when it breaks. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- Storage and the key live on your server, because on the user's machine the user can wipe it and anyone can read it. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)\n- The high-level design of Aarav's app, with storage (7) as a file on your server. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#07-the-high-level-design)\n- Worth storing is \"the least you need to carry on next time\": write the questions your app must answer tomorrow. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#08-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Things become tables, facts become columns, links get a shape. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Number rows with an `id` (the key); a link is a column on the many side; many-to-many needs a third table. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#10-keys-and-pointers)\n- Two rules: \"History is a row, not a cell.\" \"Rows for facts, files for bytes.\" [Notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#11-two-rules-that-save-you-later)\n- Aarav's model: eight tables. [Notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#12-aaravs-model)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- GPT-OSS-120B needs about 65 GB of memory, plus GPUs; a 421-million-parameter model about 2.5 GB. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#03-where-should-each-piece-live)\n- gpt-oss-120b: 117 billion parameters in 15 files; a laptop has 16 GB. [Whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#some-work-does-not-fit-in-that-box)\n- A key sent to the browser let a stranger spend the $10 of credit. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)\n- The Modelling Lab is seven levels, about 2 hours. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-overview.md#your-path-this-week)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Storage: \"Anything that keeps things and gives them back\". [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- Timestamp: \"The date and time saved with each entry\". [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- Client side: the user's machine. Server side: a machine you control. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- Thing (entity) gets a table; fact (attribute) gets a column; a block is a thing drawn in the lab. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- Shape: one-to-many, many-to-many or one-to-one; the many side holds the pointer. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- File storage: \"Where bytes live. The row keeps the link.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Draw your app's HLD, then fill its storage box in the Modelling Lab. [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md)\n- Post four things (your HLD, Aarav's model, the Arena at 6 / 6, your model) in the Discord assignments channel before Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST. [Practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-4-ready)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-overview.md): path this week. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/)\n- [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md): the lecture. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes)\n- [Whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md): 24 scenes. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/whiteboard)\n- [Modelling Lab](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md): seven levels. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab)\n- [Troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-troubleshooting.md): each lab cross. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/troubleshooting)\n- [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md): what to submit. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set)\n- [Office hour](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md): Thu 15 Oct 2026. [page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/office-hour)\n\nSee also: [Domain model](../concepts/domain-model.md), [Storage](../concepts/storage.md), [High-level design](../concepts/high-level-design.md), [Key](../concepts/key.md), [Pointer](../concepts/pointer.md), [Decide what your app stores](../how-to/decide-what-your-app-stores.md), [Lecture 05](lecture-05.md), [Lecture 07](lecture-07.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-07-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 7, no-code track: Connect backend to database","meta":"title: Lecture 7, no-code track: Connect backend to database\nsummary: Lecture 7, no-code track (Fri 16 Oct 2026): the MERN stack as a restaurant, a salon booking that vanishes on refresh, four tables, a to-do app connected to Supabase from Antigravity in eight steps.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 7, no-code track: Connect backend to database","a":"lecture-7-no-code-track-connect-backend-to-database","text":"Lecture 7 for the no-code track, \"Connect backend to database\", was on Fri 16 Oct 2026. Tools: Antigravity, Supabase. [No-code track sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database)\n\nThe room named the parts of a full app, watched a booking vanish on refresh, and connected a to-do app to Supabase. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#make-the-app-remember)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Learn the names, skip the code: HTML, JavaScript, Node.js and Express, React. Add MongoDB and the first letters give the MERN stack. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#01-learn-the-names-skip-the-code)\n- The app as a restaurant: React is the dining area, Express the traffic controller, Node.js the kitchen, the database the storeroom, an API the order slip. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#02-the-app-as-a-restaurant)\n- Plain JavaScript is the waiter who never wrote the table number. TypeScript is the rulebook; ask your coding tool for it. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#03-the-waiter-who-forgot-the-table)\n- One click goes all the way round: React, Express, Node.js, the database, and back. Create is POST, read is GET, update is PUT, delete is DELETE. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#04-one-click-all-the-way-round)\n- Two folders, client and server. \"There is no database folder.\" [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#05-where-the-files-live)\n- The salon prototype says \"appointment saved\" and a refresh wipes it. \"A beautiful UI is not the product. The product remembers.\" [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#06-refresh-and-the-booking-is-gone)\n- The salon keeps four tables: customers, stylists, services, appointments. Every row gets an ID that never changes. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#07-four-lists-the-salon-keeps)\n- Supabase holds the tables. Antigravity asks for your project URL and key, then hands you SQL to run in the SQL editor in Supabase. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-a-to-do-app-to-supabase)\n- Prove it from both sides: an item added in the app appeared as a row in Supabase, and a change inside Supabase showed in the app. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- React, 2013, started at Facebook. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#01-learn-the-names-skip-the-code)\n- \"An appointment stores three IDs and a time. It copies no names.\" [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#07-four-lists-the-salon-keeps)\n- The screen may say \"saved\" only after step 5 comes back, the database's response. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#06-refresh-and-the-booking-is-gone)\n- Find the project URL and key under Project settings, then API keys. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-a-to-do-app-to-supabase)\n- Supabase uses Postgres, and only that one; the slides used MongoDB. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Back end: \"The side on the server that does the work on each request.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- MERN stack: MongoDB, Express, React and Node.js, used together. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- Component: \"One reusable block of the screen, like a like button.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- State: \"What is true about a component right now, like liked or not liked.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- Props: \"The instructions you hand a component.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- TypeScript: \"JavaScript with a rulebook that checks the details before the code runs.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- CRUD: \"Create, read, update, delete: the four things an app does to its data.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)\n- Toast message: \"A small message that pops up in a corner to say what happened.\" [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#09-prove-it-is-connected)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- First finish the to-do app from section 08, and test it the way section 09 does. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-connect-your-salon-app)\n- Then open your salon booking app in Antigravity and type \"Connect my app to Supabase.\" [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-connect-your-salon-app)\n- Book an appointment, refresh, and look for the row in Supabase. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-connect-your-salon-app)\n- Treat the secret key on that Supabase page like your Groq key. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-a-to-do-app-to-supabase)\n- Next: a closer look inside Supabase, then your app goes live on Vercel. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-connect-your-salon-app)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 7, code track](lecture-07.md), [Lecture 5, no-code track](lecture-05-no-code.md), [CRUD](../concepts/crud.md), [Key](../concepts/key.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md), [Create a Supabase project and tables](../how-to/create-a-supabase-project-and-tables.md), [Decide what your app stores](../how-to/decide-what-your-app-stores.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-07.md","title":"Lecture 7: Connect backend to database","meta":"title: Lecture 7: Connect backend to database\nsummary: Lecture 7 (Fri 16 Oct 2026, code track): the Groq key leaves the browser; one function behind FastAPI and Pydantic, deployed to Vercel; practice set due Sat 17 Oct.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 7: Connect backend to database","a":"lecture-7-connect-backend-to-database","text":"Lecture 7 ran on Fri 16 Oct 2026, titled \"Connect backend to database\" on the sheet. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database)\n\n- The two tracks had separate practicals: code track tools were FastAPI and Supabase, no-code track tools were Antigravity and Supabase. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database) [no-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database)\n- These notes are code track, instructor Siddhant Goswami. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#the-key-leaves-the-browser)\n- The question: the key in Aarav's browser can be taken, so where should the Groq call live? [notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#05-step-01--the-leak)\n- The answer: \"The key lives on the machine that spends the money.\" [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-overview.md#connect-backend-to-the-database)\n- Storage moved to Lecture 8. [notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#01-the-lecture-in-one-line)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Five things before you build: Python, an IDE, one LLM desktop app, GitHub, Vercel. [notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#02-five-things-before-you-build)\n- Rent the machine: your laptop is a personal computer, your own server is an independent house, Vercel is the Airbnb. [notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)\n- Pick one LLM desktop app: Codex, Claude Code, Hermes agent or OpenCode. [notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#04-pick-one-llm-desktop-app)\n- Step 01, the leak: no key gives 401; a pasted key is readable in the Network tab. [notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#05-step-01--the-leak)\n- Step 02, strip to the function: `diagnose(workflow)` takes text, returns a plan. It broke live on a retired model name. [notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function)\n- Step 03: a FastAPI route `/api/diagnose`, action POST, Pydantic model `WorkflowIn` at the door. [notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Deploy to Vercel: 404 wrong address, 500 key not on that machine, 200 with a plan is done. [notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)\n- Step 04: Gradio becomes a client with no key. [notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#09-step-04--gradio-becomes-a-client-tonight)\n- From the chat: send JSON, convert on the user's device; you can mock the LLM. [notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#10-from-the-chat)"},{"h":"What happens to memory.txt once deployed","a":"what-happens-to-memorytxt-once-deployed","text":"- After step 04 the server \"forgets every plan the moment it answers.\" [notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#09-step-04--gradio-becomes-a-client-tonight)\n- Plans saved to `memory.txt` work locally and fail on Vercel: \"The file dies.\" Lecture 8 fixes it with Supabase. [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Vercel hobby plan is free; a paid app is about $20 a month. [notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#03-rent-the-machine)\n- FastAPI turns a function into an endpoint in four lines. [notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api)\n- Five years ago this was five people. [notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- repository: \"A project on GitHub. All its files, and every save you made.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- commit: \"A save of your progress with a one-line reason. Local until you push.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- push: \"Send your commits from the laptop to the repository on the internet.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- deploy: \"Put your code on a rented machine with a URL.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- environment variable: \"Where keys live.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- endpoint: one address with one action. [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- FastAPI: turns a function into an endpoint. [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- Pydantic: \"The traffic police for everything that arrives.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- business logic: \"The one function that takes the input and makes the output.\" [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)\n- CLI: command line interface. [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Practice set: steps 01 to 04 for your own app. [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#your-function-live-on-a-url)\n- Due \"before Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct, 6 pm IST\", posted in the Discord code channel. [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#your-function-live-on-a-url)\n- Submit five: your function, a `/docs` screenshot, live URL with a 200, Gradio on the live URL, your error log. [set 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready)\n- Bring to Lecture 8: your live URL and your Modelling Lab tables. [notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/ [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-overview.md)\n- Notes with sketches: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md)\n- Practice set: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set [Practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md)\n- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md)\n\nSee also: [No-code track](lecture-07-no-code.md), [Lecture 6](lecture-06.md), [Lecture 8](lecture-08.md), [FastAPI](../concepts/fastapi.md), [Pydantic](../concepts/pydantic.md), [Deploy a FastAPI app to Vercel](../how-to/deploy-a-fastapi-app-to-vercel.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-08-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 8, no-code track: End to end MVP build and deploy","meta":"title: Lecture 8, no-code track: End to end MVP build and deploy\nsummary: Lecture 8, no-code track (Sat 17 Oct 2026): the salon app on Supabase with RLS and CRUD, then live with Git, GitHub and Vercel; two tests, environment variables, appointment 2041.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 8, no-code track: End to end MVP build and deploy","a":"lecture-8-no-code-track-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy","text":"Lecture 8, \"End to end MVP build and deploy\", was on Sat 17 Oct 2026, a separate practical. No-code track tools: Antigravity, Supabase, Vercel. [Sheet, no-code track](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy)\n\nThe room proved the app really reads and writes Supabase, then put it online with Git, GitHub and Vercel, and tested the live link. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#from-localhost-to-a-live-link)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- \"Who are you?\" is authentication. \"What are you allowed to do?\" is authorization. Supabase answers the second with row level security (RLS): rules on the rows of a table, each rule a policy. Turning RLS on is not enough: you also have to write the policies. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#01-who-you-are-and-what-you-may-touch)\n- The salon booking app needs four tables: customers, stylists, services and appointments. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#02-connect-the-salon-app-keep-the-design)\n- Antigravity asks for the project URL and the publishable key. The secret key (the service role key) skips your policies: never put it in front-end code. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#02-connect-the-salon-app-keep-the-design)\n- Create, read, update, delete: CRUD. Most real apps cancel with a soft delete: the row stays and its status changes from booked to cancelled. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#03-four-things-you-can-do-to-a-row)\n- Two tests. App to table: change something in the app, check the row in Supabase. Table to app: insert a row by hand in Supabase, see it in the app. Then refresh. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#04-is-the-screen-telling-the-truth)\n- localhost:3000 means \"this computer\". Deploying puts the same app at an address that opens anywhere: \"GitHub stores the code. Vercel runs the app. Supabase stores the data.\" [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#05-localhost-is-only-your-laptop)\n- Git keeps one repository with a history of commits: `git status`, `git add` and `git commit`, then `git push`. .gitignore lists what Git must leave out: keys, passwords, node_modules. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#06-final-final-new-final-fixed)\n- A private repository can still be deployed as a public app. A secret key found on GitHub stays in the history: make a new key in Supabase. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#07-inspect-first-then-push)\n- `npm install`, then `npm run build`, on your laptop first. Then Vercel: Continue with GitHub, import the repository, check what it detected, deploy. After this, every push publishes again. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#08-build-it-then-hand-it-to-vercel)\n- The live page loads but nothing saves, because the .env file stayed on your laptop. Add the Supabase project URL and the publishable key under Environment Variables in Vercel, names matching letter for letter, then redeploy. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The instructor's rule of thumb: Supabase is enough up to about 10,000 daily active users. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#02-connect-the-salon-app-keep-the-design)\n- Appointment 2041 moves from Thursday 4 PM to Friday 11 AM. The ID is the same; only the time changed. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#03-four-things-you-can-do-to-a-row)\n- A live app can fail in four places: the build, the Vercel settings, while running, the back end. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Authentication: \"Checking who you are, usually with an email and password.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product)\n- Commit: \"A checkpoint: a set of changes saved in the history with a message.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product)\n- Environment variable: \"A value the app needs, such as the Supabase URL, kept outside the code.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Deploy the salon app or your own, tonight. Send the link to one person who did not build it, ask them to book an appointment, then check the row in Supabase. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-ship-it-then-the-hackathon)\n- The hackathon: pick one problem and build it solo. It closes on Tuesday 20 October at 9 PM. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-ship-it-then-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Notes, no-code track: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes-no-code [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#from-localhost-to-a-live-link)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 8, code track](lecture-08.md), [Hackathon](hackathon.md), [RLS](../concepts/rls.md), [CRUD](../concepts/crud.md), [Deploy](../concepts/deploy.md), [Repository, commit and push](../concepts/repository.md), [Environment variable](../concepts/environment-variable.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md), [Create a Supabase project and tables](../how-to/create-a-supabase-project-and-tables.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-08.md","title":"Lecture 8: End to end MVP","meta":"title: Lecture 8: End to end MVP\nsummary: Lecture 8 (Sat 17 Oct 2026, code track): storage moves from memory.txt to Postgres tables on Supabase; race condition, four dials, RLS.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 8: End to end MVP","a":"lecture-8-end-to-end-mvp","text":"Lecture 8, End to end MVP, was on Sat 17 Oct 2026, 6 pm IST, for the code track. [overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-overview.md#end-to-end-mvp)\n\nThe question: the Lecture 7 API is on Vercel, so how does it learn to remember, first in a file, then in a database? [notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#01-the-lecture-in-one-line)\n\nThe sheet gives both tracks \"8. End to end MVP build and deploy\" that day, with different tools: Gradio, FastAPI, Supabase, Render (code); Antigravity, Supabase, Vercel (no-code). [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy), [no-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Local first: `uvicorn api.index:app --reload`, Gradio posts to `http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/diagnose`; production changes only the address. [notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#02-local-first-then-production)\n- Vercel has no terminal: a 404 or 500 shows up first in the Logs tab. [notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#03-read-the-logs-on-someone-elses-machine)\n- Store the least that tests the bet: a user, a workflow, a diagnosis. [notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- User to workflow is one to many; workflow to diagnosis is one to one, for now; the link is the `user_id` on the workflow. [notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Step 05, `memory.txt`: read the file, run `diagnose`, build a line with time, workflow and plan, write it all back. The step most people missed was the timestamp. [notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#05-step-05--memorytxt-in-four-steps)\n- Where a file breaks: two writers at once lose a plan (a race condition), and a small machine runs out of memory and returns 500 to everyone. [notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- Deployed, step 05 fails anyway: Vercel's file system is read-only, so the write fails with a 500. [whiteboard, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#a-file-is-enough-until-people-write-at-once)\n- Storage moved to PostgreSQL rented from Supabase, called with the same four dials: address, action, key, package. [notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#08-the-same-four-dials)\n- The Modelling Lab became tables: thing to table, fact to column, link to foreign key, id to primary key, via `schema.sql`. [notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- Four file lines became one insert; `diagnose` and the FastAPI route did not change. [notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)\n- Gradio was replaced by a text box, a button and one fetch. [whiteboard, 16](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#rip-off-gradio-a-text-box-a-button-one-fetch)\n- Without RLS, one `select *` returns Aarav's workflows to anyone with the URL and a key. [notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#11-whose-row-is-this)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- 100 users, 10 workflows a day, 60 days: 60,000 lines. [notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- A file is fine for one user, under about ten operations a day, a fixed window. [notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- The logged request: 2.77 seconds, status 200. [notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#03-read-the-logs-on-someone-elses-machine)\n- The bet: 6 of 10 early-career people map one repeated workflow and build an app. [notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#04-what-is-worth-storing)\n- Supabase is free up to 50,000 monthly active users and 500 MB. [notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)\n- OpenAI runs on PostgreSQL at 800 million users. [notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#07-postgres-rented-from-supabase)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- localhost: `127.0.0.1`, this machine. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- uvicorn: the server that runs a FastAPI app on your laptop. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- ER diagram: things and links, drawn. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- race condition: two writers at once, and one write is lost. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- PostgreSQL: the free, open-source SQL database Supabase runs. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- SDK: a library that wraps an API in functions. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)\n- RLS: a policy on which rows each user can read or write. [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Done equals: a classmate opens your URL, gets a plan, refreshes, the plan is still there, finds no key in the Network tab, and posts \"verified\". [overview, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-overview.md#done-equals)\n- Build your own app through the eight steps of the c8-db repository. [whiteboard, 20](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#five-steps-then-a-classmate-says-verified)\n- Next came the hackathon. [notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#13-next-the-hackathon)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/ [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-overview.md)\n- Notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md)\n- Whiteboard, twenty scenes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md)\n- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes-no-code [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md#from-localhost-to-a-live-link)\n\nSee also: [No-code](lecture-08-no-code.md), [Race condition](../concepts/race-condition.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md), [RLS](../concepts/rls.md), [ER diagram](../concepts/er-diagram.md), [localhost](../concepts/localhost.md), [Supabase tables](../how-to/create-a-supabase-project-and-tables.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-09.md","title":"Lecture 9: LLMs from First Principles","meta":"title: Lecture 9: LLMs from First Principles\nsummary: Lecture 9 (Fri 30 Oct 2026, both tracks): verifiable means automatable, the track query that hallucinated (1/10 bare, 5 with tracks), Verifier's Rule, Practice set 1.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 9: LLMs from First Principles","a":"lecture-9-llms-from-first-principles","text":"Lecture 9, \"LLMs from First Principles\", was on Fri 30 Oct 2026, combined theory, Arc 2. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-30-oct-2026--9-llms-from-first-principles)\n\nBoth tracks. It asked where the line between rules and prediction sits: default to rules, use an LLM only where the output needs judgment. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-overview.md#llms-from-first-principles)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: Client, server, API and data are orange (deterministic); one purple box is the LLM, GPT OSS on GroqCloud. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#01-six-weeks-on-one-whiteboard)\n- 02: The most common mistake: reaching for the LLM first. [Notes 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#02-why-the-second-mountain-is-steeper)\n- 03: GPT OSS 120B in the Groq playground: text in, text out. [Notes 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#03-input-and-output-of-the-bare-model)\n- 04: The question put to the model: \"Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\" The shape was right; the tracks it listed do not exist. [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- 04: The real choice has two axes: career track (entrepreneurship or career acceleration) and execution path (code or no-code). [Notes 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- No score in Lecture 9. The Lecture 10 step scored it: \"1 out of 10 bare, 5 with the real tracks pasted in.\" [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)\n- 05: A learner's fix used a system prompt: one purple step makes the system probabilistic. [Notes 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#05-can-the-orange-box-fix-it)\n- 06: The deterministic answer: one API call, one template, the right answer every time. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#06-the-deterministic-answer-already-existed)\n- 07: \"When will I get a job?\" has no rule, but a mentor can estimate it. [Notes 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)\n- 08: Can a machine learn judgment? Verification first, data second. [Notes 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)\n- 09: Jason Wei's Verifier's Rule now guides the probabilistic half. [Notes 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- 10: Aarav's Monday as three atomic tasks, all verifiable. [Notes 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task)\n- 11: Verifiable is not automated, yet. Karpathy's jagged edge; a nurse's job as a bundle of tasks. [Notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#11-verifiable-today-automatable-tomorrow)\n- 12: Data is bigger than you think; experiment locally on Ollama. [Notes 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#12-data-is-bigger-than-you-think)\n- 13: Most real tasks are a mix; start deterministic. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- ChatGPT: Postgres underneath at over a billion users. [Notes 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#01-six-weeks-on-one-whiteboard)\n- Intuition: 30 problem statements over 30 days. Test with five real users. [Notes 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#06-the-deterministic-answer-already-existed)\n- More than 80% of your week in the automated band: use this module to shift it. [Notes 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#11-verifiable-today-automatable-tomorrow)\n- FineWeb: 44 TB, 15 trillion tokens; GPT-class and Claude-class models use 10 to 100 times more. [Notes 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#12-data-is-bigger-than-you-think)\n- Qwen 3.5: 6 to 7 GB, runs on a 16 GB laptop. [Notes 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#12-data-is-bigger-than-you-think)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Deterministic system: same input, same output. Probabilistic system: same input, different outputs. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- HLD round: design a system from a problem statement, no code. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- System prompt: an instruction only an LLM can act on. Its presence makes a step purple. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- Jagged edge: AI capability rises unevenly, fastest where outputs are easiest to verify. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- Tokens: words as numbers. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)\n- Hallucination, Verifier's Rule, atomic task, verification by rule and by judgment: idea pages. [Notes 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Practice set 1, \"Verify your own job\": North Star, then every atomic task from last week with its verifier, bucket and data. [Practice set 1](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#verify-your-own-job)\n- After Lecture 10, test one task bare and with context, scored out of 10. [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)\n- Then Practice set 2: \"Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\" [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#write-the-eval)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/ [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-overview.md)\n- Notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md)\n- Practice set 1: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set [raw](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md)\n- Practice set 2: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/ [raw](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md)\n\nSee also: [Write the eval](../how-to/write-the-eval.md), [Verifier's Rule](../concepts/verifiers-rule.md), [Hallucination](../concepts/hallucination.md), [Verification by rule](../concepts/verification-by-rule.md), [Judgment-based verification](../concepts/judgment-based-verification.md), [Atomic task](../concepts/atomic-task.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-10.md","title":"Lecture 10: Tool calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture 10: Tool calling and MCP\nsummary: Lecture 10 (Sat 31 Oct 2026, both tracks): the track question scored 1, 5, 7 out of 10; golden pair; function calling as the reverse call.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 10: Tool calling and MCP","a":"lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"Lecture 10, Tool calling and MCP, was held on Sat 31 Oct 2026 for both tracks, as combined theory. [Sheet, code track](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-31-oct-2026--10-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n\nIt kept asking GPT-OSS 120B one question, \"Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\", to find why the answer was made up and how to supply the missing context automatically. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- The bare model invented tracks: 1 out of 10, a hallucination. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- A short block on the real tracks scored 5; the full C8 curriculum as CSV text scored 7, still naming Zapier. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- Andrej Karpathy's framing: the model has no hallucination problem; the LLM assistant does. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#03-why-models-hallucinate)\n- In OpenOrca training data, fewer than 5% of records were the model saying it doesn't know. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no)\n- A better prompt or temperature at 0 did not remove it. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone)\n- A golden pair found the cause: the curriculum never names a UI builder; adding Lovable, Google AI Studio, Antigravity closes the gap. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Automating the paste needs a data source and a bridge; almost nobody finished the live challenge. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- An API alone can't be the bridge: the file can't send requests, the model is probabilistic. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#09-why-an-api-alone-cant-be-the-bridge)\n- JSON was elected as the common language; only the server is kept as the bridge. [Notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#11-the-server-is-the-bridge)\n- Function calling, shipped by OpenAI on 13 June 2023: the model asks the server to act for it. [Notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#12-function-calling-the-reverse-call)\n- Q&A: let the server search a keyword list per source instead of routing in English; this is indexing. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Scores for the same question: 1 (no context), 5 (real tracks), 7 (full curriculum CSV). [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- The real tracks: two career tracks (Entrepreneurship, Career Accelerator) and two execution paths (Code, No Code). [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- OpenOrca: about 2.9 million records; of roughly 30,000 scrolled, fewer than 5% declined. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Golden pair: the ideal input and the ideal output, written before the run; one golden dataset entry. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Hallucination: \"A confident answer that lands in factually wrong territory.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- LLM assistant: \"The whole system around the model: client, APIs, data, tools.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Context: \"The data that reaches the model inside its context window.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Temperature: \"Controls randomness in the output.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Data source: \"The trusted store that must hold the context before any bridge can help.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Function calling (tool calling): the model asks a deterministic server to run a request for it. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Tool call: \"The model's JSON naming the function and its arguments.\" [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Execution environment: your backend, which runs the tool call and returns the result. [Notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- One step is added to Practice set 1: pick one Automate or Augment task and write its golden pair first. [Notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#14-practice)\n- Run it bare, then with context, in the GroqCloud playground; score both out of 10 and note what was missing. [Notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#14-practice)\n- Then Practice set 2, write the eval: about 45 minutes, in a new tab of the sheet. [Notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#14-practice)\n- Due: \"Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#06-next-practice-set-2-write-the-eval)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/ [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-overview.md)\n- Notes, with sketches: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes [source](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md)\n- Office hour, Tue 3 Nov 2026: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [Golden dataset](../concepts/golden-dataset.md), [Golden pair](../concepts/golden-pair.md), [Function calling](../concepts/function-calling.md), [Practice set 2](../how-to/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-11.md","title":"Lecture 11: Tool calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture 11: Tool calling and MCP\nsummary: Lecture 11 (Fri 13 Nov 2026, both tracks): Priya's sheet, tool schema, errors, execution record, MCP, host/client/server; Tool Bench card scores 3, 4 or 5, 5 of 5.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 11: Tool calling and MCP","a":"lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"Lecture 11, \"Tool calling and MCP\", was on Fri 13 Nov 2026 for both tracks, a combined practical (Tool Bench, Groq). [Sheet, Lecture 11](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n\nIt asked how an LLM can use one shop's Google Sheet: the model asks for an action, a deterministic server executes it, the verifier proves what happened. [Lecture 11 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-overview.md#tool-calling-and-mcp-part-2)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: Four layers; the lecture was Protocol and Tooling. [Lecture 11 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#01-where-tools-and-mcp-sit)\n- 02: The question reused from Lecture 10, \"Which execution track should I choose in 100x?\": the LLM fills gaps. [Lecture 11 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#02-from-lecture-10-the-llm-fills-gaps)\n- 03: Priya's shop: pasting the sheet is repetitive, error-prone, stale and doesn't scale. [Lecture 11 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#03-priyas-shop-the-paste-problem)\n- 04: The rule: revenue counts every order except cancelled ones. Golden pair: the kettle, 4,980. [Lecture 11 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#04-the-sheet-and-its-one-rule)\n- 05: Only the question, the instruction and the data reach the LLM. [Lecture 11 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#05-what-reaches-the-llm)\n- 06: The server is the bridge. [Lecture 11 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#06-the-server-is-the-bridge)\n- 07: Function calling: send, decide, request, execute, return, answer. [Lecture 11 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#07-function-calling-the-llm-asks)\n- 08: The tool schema has five parts: name, description, parameters, required, types. [Lecture 11 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#08-the-tool-schema)\n- 09: 400, 401, 404, 500 and an empty result each need a different message. [Lecture 11 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#09-when-the-tool-fails)\n- 10: Verify the execution record, not the sentence. [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence)\n- 11: MCP, one standard for every tool. [Lecture 11 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool)\n- 12: Host, client, server, end to end. [Lecture 11 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#12-host-client-server)\n- 13: Five steps for Lecture 12. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The Lecture 10 question scored 1 out of 10 with no context, 5 with a short description, 7 with the full curriculum; it still named Zapier. [Lecture 11 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#02-from-lecture-10-the-llm-fills-gaps)\n- Priya copies 40 rows; the new order is on row 41. [Lecture 11 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#03-priyas-shop-the-paste-problem)\n- Order 1046, a kettle, is cancelled and left out of revenue. [Lecture 11 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#04-the-sheet-and-its-one-rule)\n- Revenue: Kettle 4,980, Mug set 3,960, Linen 3,870, Oak board 1,890. [Lecture 11 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#04-the-sheet-and-its-one-rule)\n- Tool card tests passed out of 5: empty card 3; \"Gets a lecture by number\" 4 or 5; the full card 5, every run. [Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- The loop runs up to three rounds. [Bench notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#05-switch-the-server-on)\n- The whole bench costs about 35 calls. [Bench notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#01-the-bench)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Source of truth: where the real records live. Deterministic bridge: the server between the LLM and the data. Also host, client, server and A2A. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)\n- Context window: everything the LLM can see while answering. Tool schema: name, description, parameters, required fields, types. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)\n- Execution record: the tool, the arguments, the result and whether it succeeded. Verifier: the expected record, written before the run. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)\n- Tool card, tool call, tool result, the loop, the wire, MCP server, MCP. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Make one tool call by hand on the Tool Bench, then turn it into an MCP server. [Lecture 11 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-overview.md#try-it-yourself)\n- Repeat it on your Practice set 1 sheet. [Bench notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet)\n- Next, Lecture 12: connect a Google Sheets MCP server in Antigravity. [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/ [Lecture 11 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-overview.md)\n- Notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes [Lecture 11 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md)\n- Tool Bench notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes [Bench notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md)\n- Second app, to paste an MCP address: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/second-app [Second app](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-second-app.md)\n\nSee also: [Tool card](../concepts/tool-card.md), [MCP](../concepts/mcp.md), [Execution record](../concepts/execution-record.md), [The loop](../concepts/the-loop.md), [Host, client, server](../concepts/host-client-server.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-12-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 12, no-code track: Building with tool calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture 12, no-code track: Building with tool calling and MCP\nsummary: Lecture 12, no-code track (Sat 14 Nov 2026): Priya's AI employee for Northwind Goods, eight tools, the card as a contract, the app holds the brakes; the Google Sheets MCP server in Antigravity, order 1051.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 12, no-code track: Building with tool calling and MCP","a":"lecture-12-no-code-track-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"Lecture 12, \"Building with tool calling and MCP\", was on Sat 14 Nov 2026, a separate practical. [Sheet, no-code track](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n\nThe room designed an AI employee's eight tools and its limits, then connected a Google Sheet in Antigravity and changed it by asking. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#design-the-tools-first-then-connect-one)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Antigravity breaks a task into steps, then works through them in a cycle: plan, write, run, test, fix. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#01-what-antigravity-does-with-your-task)\n- Priya wants an AI employee to run Northwind Goods while she is on leave. \"Giving the LLM tools makes it useful. Giving it unlimited access makes it dangerous.\" [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#02-hire-an-ai-employee-what-may-it-do)\n- The toolbox has eight tools: four reading tools, one calculating tool, three email tools. `calculate_revenue` is there so the LLM never does the sums itself. Drafting and sending are two tools on purpose. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#03-one-request-eight-tools)\n- The card is a contract: name, description, what to pass in, allowed values, what comes back, errors, permission. \"The card checks the shape of a request. The server checks whether it is true.\" [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#04-the-card-is-a-contract)\n- The LLM decides which tool to use by reading each card's description: what the tool does, when to use it, when not to. \"More tools do not make the LLM smarter. A small, clear set does.\" [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#05-the-description-picks-the-tool)\n- A call that needs another call's result waits: in sequence. If none needs another's result, the calls go out together: in parallel. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#06-which-calls-wait-which-run-together)\n- The app holds the brakes: it limits the number of tool calls, the time and the retries. \"The LLM decides what it wants to do. Your app decides whether it is allowed.\" [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#07-your-app-holds-the-brakes)\n- In Antigravity (the coding tool), type `install Google Sheets plugin`, sign in with Google, make your own copy of the shared order sheet, share it, paste its link, then ask the shop's questions. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity)\n- Then Antigravity built one HTML page that edits the sheet through MCP. The sheet had become the database of a small shop app. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity)\n- The LLM read the server's cards and wrote a request; the server validated it and called the Sheets API. A server offers tools, resources and prompts. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#09-what-the-server-did-for-you)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Three apps, four services each: twelve connections to build and maintain. Build each connection once, as an MCP server. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity)\n- The pending orders were 1043, 1045 and 1047. The same tool called three times with the same inputs: the app stops the run. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#07-your-app-holds-the-brakes)\n- The mug set order: 123 rupees, pending, order number 1051. Another order's value changed to 12,900. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Required field: \"A field that must be present in a request, even when its value is empty.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#09-what-the-server-did-for-you)\n- Allowed values: \"The only values a field may take.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#09-what-the-server-did-for-you)\n- Infinite loop: \"The LLM repeating the same call with the same inputs and getting nowhere.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#09-what-the-server-did-for-you)\n- Local server: \"An MCP server that runs on your own machine.\" Today's Google Sheets server is remote. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#09-what-the-server-did-for-you)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Do section 08 tonight on your own copy of the sheet: connect, list the actions, ask two questions, then change one row by asking. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn)\n- Then open the Tool Bench with your own Groq key. It shows every message that crosses the wire. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Notes, no-code track: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes-no-code [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#design-the-tools-first-then-connect-one)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 12, code track](lecture-12.md), [Lecture 11](lecture-11.md), [Tool](../concepts/tool.md), [Tool card](../concepts/tool-card.md), [MCP](../concepts/mcp.md), [Function calling](../concepts/function-calling.md), [Write a tool card against tests](../how-to/write-a-tool-card-against-tests.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-12.md","title":"Lecture 12: Building with tool calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture 12: Building with tool calling and MCP\nsummary: Lecture 12 (Sat 14 Nov 2026, code track notes): tool_call.py and mcp_server.py with Groq and FastMCP; Practice set 3 due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 12: Building with tool calling and MCP","a":"lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"Lecture 12, \"Building with tool calling and MCP\", was on Sat 14 Nov 2026, a separate practical. Code track tools: Python, Groq, FastMCP. [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n\nThe no-code track row has the same title and date, tools \"[TO NAME]\". [No-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp) Replaced on 2026-11-27 by the line below.\n\nThe no-code notes name the tools: Antigravity, the Google Sheets MCP server. [No-code, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity)\n\nThe notes, overview and practice set are for the code track. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-overview.md) Replaced on 2026-11-27 by the line below.\n\nThe no-code track has its own notes. [No-code notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#design-the-tools-first-then-connect-one)\n\nThe question: asked which track to choose, the model on its own makes up the answer. Today you write the tool in Python. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#one-tool-call-then-an-mcp-server)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: A plain Python function, `get_lectures(track)`, reads one track's tab of the curriculum sheet and returns each lecture with its tools. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function)\n- 02: The model can't see your function. It reads a card: a name, what it does, and what to pass in. The Groq request gets one new field, `tools`. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#02-step-1-the-card)\n- 03: The model replies with a tool call, not an answer. Your code runs the function and sends the result back until the model stops asking. It asked twice, once per track, and never touched the sheet. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#03-step-1-the-tool-call)\n- 04: `mcp_server.py` puts the same function at an address any app can reach. `@mcp.tool` writes the card from the name, the docstring and the type hint. `fastmcp list` returns the card; `fastmcp call` runs the tool. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)\n- 05: The thing the model was missing in Practice set 1, step 04, is your first tool. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- Model: `openai/gpt-oss-120b` on Groq. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#02-step-1-the-card)\n- A 429 error means wait a minute and run again. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#02-step-1-the-card)\n- The server runs at http://localhost:8000/mcp. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#04-step-2-the-mcp-server)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Tool: a function your code runs when the model asks for it. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)\n- Card: the description of a tool that the model reads: its name, what it does, and what to pass in. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)\n- Tool call: the model's reply naming a tool and what to pass in. A request, not an action. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)\n- MCP server: tools at an address, so any app can list them and call them. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)\n- FastMCP: the Python library that turns a function into an MCP server. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#05-your-turn)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Practice set 3, \"Write the code behind the bench\", code track. Due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026. [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#write-the-code-behind-the-bench)\n- The repo gives you `get_lecture(number)` and `ask_model(messages, tools)`; the notes used `get_lectures(track)`. [Practice set 3, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#01-what-you-write)\n- Part 1, in `tool_call.py`: six steps. Done when the same file answers a lecture question from the sheet and \"What is an API?\" on its own. [Practice set 3, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#02-part-1-the-tool-call)\n- Part 2, in `mcp_server.py`: five steps. Done when `fastmcp call` returns the Lecture 12 row from your address on the internet. [Practice set 3, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#03-part-2-the-mcp-server)\n- Then do it once more with your own data. [Practice set 3, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#04-now-your-own-data)\n- Submit on the LMS: the link to your fork, with the five-question table filled in, and your deployed address. [Practice set 3, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#05-submit-it)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview: Tool Bench steps, Practice set 3, done equals. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/ [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-overview.md)\n- Notes: the worked example in Python. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md)\n- Practice set 3. https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md)\n- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes-no-code [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md#design-the-tools-first-then-connect-one)\n\nSee also: [No-code track](lecture-12-no-code.md), [Tool](../concepts/tool.md), [Tool card](../concepts/tool-card.md), [MCP](../concepts/mcp.md), [FastMCP](../concepts/fastmcp.md), [Practice set 3 steps](../how-to/write-a-tool-call-and-an-mcp-server-in-python.md), [Checklist](../how-to/use-the-lectures-9-to-12-checklist.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-13.md","title":"Lecture 13: Retrieval-augmented generation","meta":"title: Lecture 13: Retrieval-augmented generation\nsummary: Lecture 13 (Fri 20 Nov 2026, both tracks): RAG as retrieve, augment, generate; 2,834-token sheet, 128,000-token window, chunks, labels, index, Retrieval Bench.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 13: Retrieval-augmented generation","a":"lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation","text":"Lecture 13, \"Retrieval-augmented generation\", Fri 20 Nov 2026, \"combined theory\", Arc 3: Context. [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- Both tracks; the no-code sheet has the same row. [No-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation)\n- The sheet's Status still says \"Planned\". [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation)\n- It was taught: the notes are dated \"20 November 2026 · Both tracks\". [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md)\n- The question: how do you send the LLM only the few chunks that matter? [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-overview.md)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: \"When is Lecture 12?\" The room named the three steps: retrieval, augmentation, generation. Together, RAG. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- 01: MCP solves a scaling problem: 100 tools and 10 LLMs means 1,000 connections. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- 02: \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" has no number, so no tool call; the LLM made up \"Lecture 12: Security and cryptography.\" [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#02-break-it-a-question-with-no-number)\n- 03: Three fixes: check for a number, LLM picks the keyword, paste the whole sheet. Only pasting can work; it still said Lecture 12, since the sheet has only dates and titles. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#03-three-fixes-from-the-chat)\n- 04: Pasting everything crossed the context window and the API answered 400. A bigger LLM only moves the line. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- 05: Chunking works, but every question pays for every chunk. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)\n- 06: Label chunks by module, then filter: \"Verifier's Law\" is on Module 2's list. The labels make an index. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- 07: Labelling needs domain knowledge. With structured data, the LLM writes SQL from the schema; the server runs it on Supabase. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n- 08: A plain tool call is RAG in principle; most teams hit the budget first. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- 09: Do all seven stations of the Retrieval Bench. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#09-now-do-the-retrieval-bench)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The tool takes a lecture number, 1 to 19. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#02-break-it-a-question-with-no-number)\n- The lectures sheet is 2,834 tokens. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- GPT-OSS 120B on Groq reads about 128,000 tokens; a chat guess was 1 million. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- Five or six pastes crossed the line. The largest LLMs read 1 to 2 million tokens. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit)\n- A million tokens in chunks of 100,000 is 10 chunks, 10 calls; 1,000 chunks, 1,000 calls. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#05-cut-it-into-chunks)\n- Amazon phones: 60,000, then 2,000, 23 Samsung, 7 in budget. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#06-label-the-chunks-then-filter)\n- One Retrieval Bench question used 424 tokens. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- A bill can grow from $100 to a million dollars; the same questions can cost $100 or $10,000. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- RAG: \"Fetch the data that matters, add it to the prompt, let the LLM write.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Context window: \"The most tokens an LLM reads at once.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Chunk: \"A piece of the data small enough to fit.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Label: \"A tag that says where a chunk sits (module, week, lecture), so you can filter.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Index: \"The labels read like a contents page: go straight to the right chunk.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)\n- Domain knowledge: \"Knowing the things in your data well enough to label them.\" [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#08-tool-call-or-rag)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Run the Retrieval Bench with a Groq key (from the Tool Bench, or console.groq.com/keys). [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#09-now-do-the-retrieval-bench)\n- No practice set or due date is written. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-overview.md)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview, the lecture in one line plus the bench: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/ [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-overview.md)\n- Notes, with sketches and \"Check yourself\": https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md)\n\nSee also: [Retrieval-augmented generation](../concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md), [Chunk](../concepts/chunk.md), [Label](../concepts/label.md), [Index](../concepts/index.md), [Domain knowledge](../concepts/domain-knowledge.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-14-no-code.md","title":"Lecture 14, no-code track: Building RAG apps","meta":"title: Lecture 14, no-code track: Building RAG apps\nsummary: Lecture 14, no-code track (Sat 21 Nov 2026): the Retrieval Bench's seven stations, an HR helper with six documents, chunks with overlap, metadata and filters; a car manual app with Antigravity, Supabase pgvector and Groq.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 14, no-code track: Building RAG apps","a":"lecture-14-no-code-track-building-rag-apps","text":"Lecture 14, \"Building RAG apps\", was on Sat 21 Nov 2026, a separate practical. [Sheet, no-code track](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps)\n\nThe room walked the Retrieval Bench, moved from one demo answer to a company's documents, then built an app that answers from a car manual. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#from-one-right-answer-to-a-dependable-app)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- Small data, and the bill is fine: paste it in. Live data: a plain tool call fits better. Large or private documents: this is where RAG pays for itself. [Notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#01-is-rag-only-for-big-data)\n- Seven stations on the Retrieval Bench. At station 6 the closest chunk did not hold the answer; the LLM answered anyway, and was marked wrong. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#02-seven-stations-on-the-retrieval-bench)\n- A demo tests one document, one question, one answer. The running example, an HR helper, holds six documents. Four goals: accuracy, efficiency, cost and reliability. [Notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#03-a-demo-is-one-question)\n- One request splits into six stages; stage 3, the search, is the place to look first. \"Before you change the LLM, read what was retrieved.\" [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#04-follow-one-question-through-the-app)\n- Uploading a new file does not retire the old one: mark the previous version inactive. Keep two dates for every document: the day it was uploaded and the day it takes effect. [Notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#05-two-versions-of-one-policy)\n- Cut where the meaning ends. A rule, its condition and its exception stay together. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#06-cut-chunks-where-the-meaning-ends)\n- Label the document and the chunk, then filter by topic, by version and by access before the search ranks anything. A prompt line protects nothing: the database or the app has to enforce access. [Notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#07-label-the-document-label-the-chunk)\n- You pay for tokens in and tokens out. Run your test questions once for a baseline, change one setting, run the same questions, and compare. [Notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#08-count-what-you-pay-for)\n- The car manual app: every step was a prompt in Antigravity. Ask for the plan, approve it, ask for pgvector, give it the manual, connect Supabase and Groq, load the document, build the question and answer page. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The bench data: 183 chunks of up to 170 words. An embedding is a list of 384 numbers. The server hands the LLM the top 3. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#02-seven-stations-on-the-retrieval-bench)\n- Pasting everything was refused: about 30,000 tokens asked for, and a free Groq key takes 8,000 a minute. [Notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#02-seven-stations-on-the-retrieval-bench)\n- The search returns 20 chunks, and only 3 helped. The other 17 take up tokens. [Notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#04-follow-one-question-through-the-app)\n- Chunk sizes to start from: 200 tokens with 20 repeated, 500 with 50, 1,000 with 100. [Notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#06-cut-chunks-where-the-meaning-ends)\n- The manual runs to 154 pages; the notes print the guide's five prompts. A plan takes 5 to 10 minutes. [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Citation: \"Where an answer came from: the document, and the page or section.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)\n- Overlap: \"The few tokens repeated from one chunk into the next.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)\n- Metadata: \"The labels on a document or a chunk: category, version, dates, who may read it.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)\n- Cache: \"A stored answer to a question people ask often.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)\n- pgvector: \"An add-on for Postgres that stores embeddings and finds the closest ones.\" [Notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Finish the car manual app first. Then run the same steps on documents of your own. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-your-own-documents)\n- Write a few questions whose answers you know. For each one, check the answer and check that the cited source really says it. [Notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#10-your-turn-your-own-documents)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Notes, no-code track: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes-no-code [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#from-one-right-answer-to-a-dependable-app)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 14, code track](lecture-14.md), [Lecture 13](lecture-13.md), [Retrieval augmented generation](../concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md), [Chunk](../concepts/chunk.md), [Embedding](../concepts/embedding.md), [Label](../concepts/label.md), [Vector database](../concepts/vector-database.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/lecture-14.md","title":"Lecture 14: Building RAG apps","meta":"title: Lecture 14: Building RAG apps\nsummary: Lecture 14 (Sat 21 Nov 2026, code track): token budget, golden set of ten, shared words 5, rare words 6, meaning 8 of 10; RAG Lab levels 5 to 9.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 14: Building RAG apps","a":"lecture-14-building-rag-apps","text":"Lecture 14, \"Building RAG apps\", Sat 21 Nov 2026: a separate practical, one session per track. [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps)\n\n- The no-code row asks learners to \"Build a RAG flow over their own documents and show one answer that cites its source chunk.\" [Sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps)\n- The question: which few passages do you send, and how do you know your search finds them? Code track: you write Lecture 13's retrieve step in Python, in the RAG Lab. [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md)\n- In one line: \"The token budget, not the window, decides how much you send.\" [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- 01: Our notes, Lectures 1 to 12, cut by paragraph; GPT-OSS 120B on Groq; ten questions, A to J, with known answer passages. [Notes-01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions)\n- 02: Pasting all 183 passages: Groq refuses. They fit the window, not the free key. [Notes-02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- 03: Score by how many different words a passage shares with the question; keep the top 3. [Notes-03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- 04: Weight each shared word by how rare it is, log(N / seen_in(w)): the core of BM25. [Notes-04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- 05: Like houses plotted by size and price, texts get numbers so alike meanings land close. [Notes-05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#05-teach-a-machine-what-words-mean)\n- 06: Smallest embedding model that finds what keywords missed: gte-small. [Notes-06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- 07: Multiply two embeddings pair by pair and add up: cosine similarity. [Notes-07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- 08: Vector database? It depends on the data and golden set; here a plain file is enough. [Notes-08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"Scores on the ten questions, in order","a":"scores-on-the-ten-questions-in-order","text":"- Shared words: 5 of 10, found A, E, F, G and I. Missed B, J. [Notes-03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- Rare words (BM25): 6 of 10, found A, E, F, G, I and J. J rose from place 41; B stayed at 27. [Notes-04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more), [Notes-07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- Meaning (gte-small): 8 of 10, found A, B, C, D, E, G, H and I. F fell to place 10, J to place 6. [Notes-07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- Level 5 joins both ranked lists: guess 9, 10 or 8, then run it. [Notes-09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#09-finish-levels-5-to-9)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- 183 passages; Lecture 1 alone is 16 of them. [Notes-01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions)\n- 28,801 tokens of notes; a window of about 128,000; a free Groq key takes 8,000 tokens a minute. [Notes-02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#02-paste-all-183-and-count)\n- Recall at 3 for shared words: 5 of 10, or 50%. [Notes-03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- The word lecture is in 47 passages, weight about 1.4; law is in 2, weight about 4.5. [Notes-04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- gte-small: 33 million parameters, about 70 MB, 384 numbers per passage. GPT-OSS 120B: 120 billion. [Notes-06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- B's top passage had closeness 0.869. [Notes-06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works)\n- 183 passages of 384 numbers at 4 bytes: about 281 KB. 100 million passages: about 154 GB. [Notes-08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Top-k: \"How many passages you keep and send. Here k is 3.\" [Notes-08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Embedding model: \"The program that turns text into an embedding.\" [Notes-08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n- Token budget, golden set (with recall at 3), BM25, embedding (with cosine similarity), vector database: pages below. [Notes-08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)"},{"h":"After the lecture","a":"after-the-lecture","text":"- Finish RAG Lab levels 5 to 9 \"before the office hour on Tuesday 24 November\". Levels 1 to 4 were live. [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md#try-it-yourself)\n- Each level adds one function to rag.py; download it and run it with your own Groq key. [Notes-09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#09-finish-levels-5-to-9)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview, with lab link: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/ [Overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md)\n- Notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes [Notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md)\n- No-code notes: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes-no-code [raw](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md#from-one-right-answer-to-a-dependable-app)\n- Office hour, Tue 24 Nov 2026: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [No-code track](lecture-14-no-code.md), [Golden set](../concepts/golden-set.md), [Token budget](../concepts/token-budget.md), [BM25](../concepts/bm25.md), [Embedding](../concepts/embedding.md), [Vector database](../concepts/vector-database.md), [Golden dataset](../concepts/golden-dataset.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/office-hour-01-oct.md","title":"Office hour, Thu 1 Oct 2026: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click","meta":"title: Office hour, Thu 1 Oct 2026: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click\nsummary: Office hour (Thu 1 Oct 2026, after Lecture 3, both tracks): Stitch questions, splitting a job before picking an LLM, and what an interface is for once you can say what you want (operate, consult, delegate).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Office hour, Thu 1 Oct 2026: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click","a":"office-hour-thu-1-oct-2026-when-software-stops-waiting-for-a-click","text":"This office hour took questions on Stitch and on picking the right LLM, then one idea: what an interface is for once you can say what you want. It stands on Lecture 2 and Lecture 3. [Office hour](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#when-software-stops-waiting-for-a-click)"},{"h":"What was asked and answered, in order","a":"what-was-asked-and-answered-in-order","text":"- Three questions about Stitch. An iPhone app: yes, AI Studio writes it so that it opens in Xcode. Poor designs: sketch the screen on paper, photograph it, upload it with style references. Why Stitch: it is one path, Stitch for the design, AI Studio for the app, Antigravity for the finished product. [Office hour, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#01-three-questions-about-stitch)\n- \"I don't know which model I have to use.\" The trouble is how the job is split, not which LLM does it. \"Split the job first. Then give each part the smallest tool that does it.\" No fixed rule: you experiment. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#02-a-nail-does-not-need-a-bulldozer)\n- \"What if the computer understood what we wanted? Would we still need an interface?\" Yes, but for a different job: the interface appears where you want to steer. [Office hour, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#03-one-table-booked-two-ways)\n- One goal can be built as three products; what changes is who does the steps. The future is a mix of all three, moving towards the third over time. [Office hour, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#04-three-products-for-one-goal)\n- Draw the limits before you build: what can it do without asking, what needs your confirmation, what must never happen unless you say so, what does it do when it is unsure? [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#05-what-may-it-do-without-asking)\n- Why do good apps still take time? Deciding what to build is still slow. \"AI can do a million things, but that million things is noise.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#06-why-good-apps-still-take-time)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Plain automation: \"A script that repeats the same steps the same way each time. No LLM in it.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#06-why-good-apps-still-take-time)\n- Operate: \"You choose everything and press the buttons yourself.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#06-why-good-apps-still-take-time)\n- Consult: \"You say what you need, the software does the work, and you give the final yes.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#06-why-good-apps-still-take-time)\n- Delegate: \"You give the instruction and the software does the whole task.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#06-why-good-apps-still-take-time)"},{"h":"After the office hour","a":"after-the-office-hour","text":"- Take the product you are building and ask how it would behave with no screen. Answer the four questions in section 05 for it, then mark where your user would still want to steer: those are the screens you need. [Office hour, 07](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md#07-your-turn-your-product-no-screen)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Office hour recap: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 3](lecture-03.md), [Lecture 2](lecture-02.md), [Interface](../concepts/interface.md), [User interface](../concepts/user-interface.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/office-hour-15-oct.md","title":"Office hour, Thu 15 Oct 2026: Write the Logic Before the Code","meta":"title: Office hour, Thu 15 Oct 2026: Write the Logic Before the Code\nsummary: Office hour (Thu 15 Oct 2026, after Lecture 6, both tracks): pseudocode as sequence, selection and repetition; three levels of asking an LLM; Q&A on keys, tables, security and when to start building.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Office hour, Thu 15 Oct 2026: Write the Logic Before the Code","a":"office-hour-thu-15-oct-2026-write-the-logic-before-the-code","text":"This office hour practised the step before any prompt: writing down what a feature must do, in order. A login, a cup of coffee and a shopping cart were written as plain steps, then came questions on keys, tables and security. [Office hour](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#write-the-logic-before-the-code)"},{"h":"What was asked and answered, in order","a":"what-was-asked-and-answered-in-order","text":"- \"What happens when someone clicks the login button?\" Four steps: check the email has the right form, send the email and password to be checked, show an error if they are wrong, open the dashboard if they are right. Nothing here is code. [Office hour, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#01-what-happens-when-you-click-log-in)\n- Black coffee showed the three moves every program is made of: do this, then that; choose a path; do it again. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#02-black-coffee-in-three-moves)\n- Pseudocode adds precision: nothing is left for the LLM to guess. Any feature sorts into four parts: input, process, decision, output. Order matters, and write every \"if not\". [Office hour, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#03-one-rule-said-two-ways)\n- Three levels of asking an LLM: \"Build a student management app.\"; name the feature, the tools and what must be handled; write the steps yourself. \"The LLM can write the code. The steps are yours.\" [Office hour, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#04-three-levels-of-asking)\n- Q&A: in Lecture 7 the function sits behind one address on your server, and the Groq key stays there. On security: add barriers until getting in costs more than it is worth, and stop there. \"My plan is written. When do I start building?\" Now. [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#05-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- Sequence: \"Steps done one after another, in a fixed order.\" [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#05-from-the-qa)\n- Selection: \"A step that picks one path or another: if this, do that, else do the other.\" [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#05-from-the-qa)\n- Repetition: \"A step done again and again until something is true, or once for each item in a list.\" [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#05-from-the-qa)\n- Pseudocode: \"The steps of a feature written in plain English, in order, with no programming language.\" [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#05-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"After the office hour","a":"after-the-office-hour","text":"- Write pseudocode for three features before you open any tool: a login page, Add to cart on a shopping site, registering a student. [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#06-your-turn-three-to-write)\n- Then do the same for the next feature of your own app, and paste those steps into Antigravity as the prompt. [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md#06-your-turn-three-to-write)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Office hour recap: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 6](lecture-06.md), [Lecture 7](lecture-07.md), [Input, process, output](../concepts/input-process-output.md), [Keep your Groq key safe](../how-to/keep-your-groq-key-safe.md), [Decide what your app stores](../how-to/decide-what-your-app-stores.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/office-hour-22-sep.md","title":"Office hour, Tue 22 Sep 2026: Tools change. The foundation stays.","meta":"title: Office hour, Tue 22 Sep 2026: Tools change. The foundation stays.\nsummary: Office hour (Tue 22 Sep 2026, after Lecture 1, both tracks): how office hours run, an LLM against a world model, the no-code track order, and how domain knowledge gets in as a second brain.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Office hour, Tue 22 Sep 2026: Tools change. The foundation stays.","a":"office-hour-tue-22-sep-2026-tools-change-the-foundation-stays","text":"This office hour came three days after Lecture 1, led by the instructor who takes the no-code track. It covered how office hours run, one topic in depth (world models), the road ahead, and the room's questions, with a first look at the second brain. [Office hour](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#tools-change-the-foundation-stays)"},{"h":"What was asked and answered, in order","a":"what-was-asked-and-answered-in-order","text":"- Every office hour has four parts: a weekly update, a progress check, one topic in depth, and an open floor. They are planned for Tuesdays and Thursdays at 8:30. [Office hour, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#01-how-these-office-hours-run)\n- An LLM predicts the next word, like a phone keyboard at a far larger scale. It knows the answer without knowing the cause, so it still slips on actions in the real world. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#02-an-llm-predicts-the-next-word)\n- Something that acts in the world needs a different prediction: if I do this, what happens? Genie, from Google, was the example. The verdict: world models are early. AGI has no single definition; treat it as a yardstick. [Office hour, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#03-a-world-model-predicts-what-happens-next)\n- \"Will we be using any of these tools in our cohort work?\" Maybe some, but every week brings a new one. The no-code track starts with design, then a backend with Antigravity, then a database, then an MVP. [Office hour, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#04-the-road-ahead-on-the-no-code-track)\n- \"How do you codify that domain knowledge?\" A preview: write what you actually understand as files an LLM can read, Markdown files and skills. The method gets its own lecture, about a month from this session. [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#05-how-do-you-codify-that-domain-knowledge)\n- Q&A: payment gateways are shown once for everyone, briefly; how to get users comes closer to the build; feedback on the lectures is welcome. [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#06-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- World model: \"A simulation inside an AI system of how things behave, and how they change when you act.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#06-from-the-qa)\n- Second brain: \"What you know, written down as files an LLM can read.\" [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#06-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"After the office hour","a":"after-the-office-hour","text":"- Keep going on First Proof: open the brief and do the next step you have not done yet. [Office hour, 07](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md#07-your-turn-keep-going-on-first-proof)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Office hour recap: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 1](lecture-01.md), [Domain knowledge](../concepts/domain-knowledge.md), [Do Assignment 1, First Proof](../how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/office-hour-24-nov.md","title":"Office hour, Tue 24 Nov 2026: Your RAG app, part by part.","meta":"title: Office hour, Tue 24 Nov 2026: Your RAG app, part by part.\nsummary: Office hour (Tue 24 Nov 2026, after Lecture 14, both tracks): chunk size, when RAG is the wrong tool, what Groq, gte-small, Supabase and pgvector each do, why build the app, and why an off-topic question still got sources (top-k, guardrail).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude (Fable 5.2)\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Office hour, Tue 24 Nov 2026: Your RAG app, part by part.","a":"office-hour-tue-24-nov-2026-your-rag-app-part-by-part","text":"This office hour took the room's doubts about the RAG app: what each part does, why build it at all, and why an off-topic question still came back with sources. The app answers questions about a Porsche manual and cites the pages. [Office hour](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#your-rag-app-part-by-part)"},{"h":"What was asked and answered, in order","a":"what-was-asked-and-answered-in-order","text":"- How did the app cut the manual? The practical's prompt asks for cuts by meaning. To see what your own build did, ask the coding tool that wrote it. \"There is no rule of thumb for chunks. Test on a document you know.\" [Office hour, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#01-how-did-it-cut-my-manual)\n- Is RAG right for simplifying research papers? RAG simplifies nothing. A paper is 50 or 100 pages at most, which fits inside the context window, so there is nothing to search for. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#02-is-rag-right-for-my-research-papers)\n- A learner guessed that RAG is a method and a second brain is a system. The instructor did not rule on it: hold the guess until Lecture 15. [Office hour, 03](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#03-a-method-or-a-system)\n- Groq serves the LLM, and is not Grok. gte-small turns text into numbers and has nothing to do with Groq. Supabase stores them: pgvector is an extension you switch on inside Postgres, not a second database. [Office hour, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#04-what-each-part-does)\n- pgvector does not do the chunking: it stores and it searches. Ollama runs open LLMs on your own machine, without Groq. [Office hour, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#04-what-each-part-does)\n- Why build the app when today's LLMs can read a document of about 1,400 to 1,500 pages? \"The LLM can be swapped. The storing and the finding stay yours.\" [Office hour, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#05-why-build-it-at-all)\n- \"Where is the best biryani?\" still got sources. The search returns a set number of chunks, called top-k, even when none is close. The fix is a rule in the instruction, called a guardrail: answer only questions about the manual. [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#06-best-biryani-with-sources)\n- A right answer buried in too much text has three fixes: lower top-k, add one more step, sharpen the question first. [Office hour, 06](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#06-best-biryani-with-sources)"},{"h":"After the office hour","a":"after-the-office-hour","text":"- Build the Porsche app and run the test questions shared with the practical. [Office hour, 07](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#07-before-lecture-15)\n- Bring the guess from section 03: Lecture 15 starts from the search you built and asks what search still cannot find. [Office hour, 07](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md#07-before-lecture-15)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Office hour recap: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/office-hour [source](../../raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 14](lecture-14.md), [Lecture 13](lecture-13.md), [Retrieval-augmented generation](../concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md), [Chunk](../concepts/chunk.md), [Embedding](../concepts/embedding.md), [Vector database](../concepts/vector-database.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md","title":"Idea to App workshop (Week 0)","meta":"title: Idea to App workshop (Week 0)\nsummary: Week 0 workshop (Fri 18 Sep 2026, both tracks): North Star's four elements, the mom test, Aarav's observation, falsifiable hypothesis, cohort zero, input-process-output.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Idea to App workshop (Week 0)","a":"idea-to-app-workshop-week-0","text":"Held Fri 18 Sep 2026, both tracks, instructor Siddhant Goswami. [Notes](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#observation-before-idea)\n\nIts one line: \"The idea does not come first. The observation does, aimed by a North Star and written as a hypothesis that can fail.\" [Overview](../../raw/practice/what-to-build.md#idea-to-app-workshop)\n\nThe question it answered: what is worth building, before weeks 1 to 8 teach how to build. [Notes 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#05-week-0-and-why-the-order-changed)"},{"h":"What happened, in order","a":"what-happened-in-order","text":"- The room: about 1,400 years of experience across 57 industries. Your industry is the raw material. [Notes 01](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#01-the-room-1400-years-of-experience)\n- A North Star has four elements: timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing. Almost everyone left out the specific thing. [Notes 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#02-a-north-star-has-four-elements)\n- The mom test: could someone who does not know the jargon verify it on the date? \"1,000 MRR\" passes; \"100x productivity\" fails, with no baseline. [Notes 03](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star)\n- Week 0 asks three questions: discovery, the manual MVP, the process. [Notes 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#05-week-0-and-why-the-order-changed)\n- Aarav at 12.38 AM in Pune showed what an observation looks like: a real person, no solution in it. [Notes 06](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#06-aarav-at-1238-am-what-an-observation-looks-like)\n- The coach's four moves: story, subject, evidence, causal chain. [Notes 07](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain)\n- A hypothesis must be able to fail. Aarav's kill line: fewer than 60% of 250 within 90 days. [Notes 08](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#08-a-hypothesis-must-be-able-to-fail)\n- Phase 6 of the skill plays a simulated person for a Mom Test interview, then grades you. Real interviews are still mandatory: 2 to 5 people minimum. [Notes 09](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated)\n- Cohort zero: 10 people, Rs 21,000 each, 21 days, no software. The bar was 60%; nine of ten presented a credible demo. [Notes 10](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#10-cohort-zero-10-people-rs-21000-21-days)\n- Every piece of software is input, process, output. [Notes 11](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#11-input-process-output)\n- The app is a 21-day tracker made in Lovable (or any builder), not a product. No backend, no database yet. [Notes 12](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#12-where-is-the-app-a-tracker-not-a-product)\n- Q&A: a learner's Friday status report got a number: 3 hours a week at $100 an hour is $300 a week per PM. [Notes 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Numbers to remember","a":"numbers-to-remember","text":"- The coach skill: 60 to 90 minutes of questions. [Notes 07](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain)\n- Time budget: 8 to 10 hours a week, about 1 to 1.5 hours a day. [Notes 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Validation: the skill 40 to 90 minutes, four calls of 15 to 20 minutes, 3 to 4 hours in all. [Notes 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- Around 90% may change their idea by week 2. [Notes 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)"},{"h":"Words it introduced","a":"words-it-introduced","text":"- North Star: \"A six-month goal with four elements: timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing that bridges them.\" [Notes 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Done Equals: \"The artefact that proves the outcome. Here: a tracker link.\" [Notes 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Observation: \"A detailed record of a real person's problem, from your lived experience, with no solution.\" [Notes 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"After the workshop","a":"after-the-workshop","text":"- Rewrite your North Star, write your observation, run discovery-to-mvp-coach, publish a 21-day tracker, start the manual MVP with 2 to 5 real people. [Notes 14](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)\n- Done equals: \"A North Star with all four elements that someone else could verify on the date, and an observation from your own life with no solution in it.\" [Overview](../../raw/practice/what-to-build.md#done-equals)\n- The full brief is Assignment 1, due Fri 9 Oct 2026. [Assignment 1](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#first-proof)"},{"h":"Pages","a":"pages","text":"- Overview, the workshop in seven links: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/ [raw](../../raw/practice/what-to-build.md)\n- Notes, what was said with the whiteboard: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes [raw](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md)\n- Worked example, Aarav: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example [raw](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md)\n- Assignment 1: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1 [raw](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md)\n\nSee also: [North Star](../concepts/north-star.md), [Done Equals](../concepts/done-equals.md), [Assignment 1](../how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md), [Aarav's example](../threads/aaravs-example.md), [Lecture 1](lecture-01.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/aaravs-example.md","title":"Aarav, the course's example user","meta":"title: Aarav, the course's example user\nsummary: Who Aarav is (26, Pune, business analyst) and his worked example from observation to hypothesis to a 21-day manual MVP plan, Week 0.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Aarav, the course's example user","a":"aarav-the-courses-example-user","text":"Aarav is the running example the course uses. The Week 0 example page shows one full run of the Discovery to MVP coach on his observation. [Aarav example](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#aarav-from-observation-to-mvp-plan)"},{"h":"Who he is","a":"who-he-is","text":"- 26, Pune, B.Tech from a tier-2 college, three years as a business analyst at an IT services company. [Aarav example, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#02-the-user-aarav)\n- It is 12:38 am and he has spent eleven minutes on one resume bullet. [Aarav example, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#02-the-user-aarav)\n- 11 saved startup ideas, 3 half-finished projects, 19 bookmarked tutorials, a folder called \"AI Projects Final\" with nothing final in it. [Aarav example, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#02-the-user-aarav)\n- His problem: high ambition, low clarity. He needs one real workflow worth solving. [Aarav example, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#02-the-user-aarav)\n- The story came from sitting next to Aarav, day after day; it is not in any model's training data. [Workshop notes, 06](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#06-aarav-at-1238-am-what-an-observation-looks-like)"},{"h":"How the coach works","a":"how-the-coach-works","text":"- Entry gate, discovery (phases 1 to 5), stress test (phase 6), manual MVP (phase 7). Output: a discovery document and a manual MVP plan. [Aarav example, 01](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#01-how-the-coach-works)\n- The one rule: the coach never simulates the result of your pilot. [Aarav example, 01](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#01-how-the-coach-works)"},{"h":"The discovery document","a":"the-discovery-document","text":"- Observation: early-career professionals in urban India fail at selection, scoping and first proof. [Observation](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#1--observation)\n- Subject: 22 to 30, urban India, early roles; would pay ₹15,000 to ₹40,000 for clarity, structure and proof. [Subject](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#2--subject)\n- Evidence audit: four buckets (direct, adjacent, passive, inference); the load-bearing inference has low to medium confidence. [Evidence audit](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#3--evidence-audit)\n- Causal chain: seven links. Only link 3, no method to diagnose a workflow, is addressed. [Causal chain](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#4--causal-chain)\n- Hypothesis: at least 60% of active students will map one real workflow, build a working AI-assisted version and present a credible demo within 90 days. [Hypothesis](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#5--hypothesis)\n- Wrong if fewer than 40% produce a credible demo in 90 days, among other falsifiers. [Hypothesis](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#5--hypothesis)\n- Stress test: selection and scoping as the bottleneck was validated; the other two assumptions were weakened. [Stress test](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#6--stress-test-scorecard)"},{"h":"The manual MVP plan","a":"the-manual-mvp-plan","text":"- 21 days, 10 users, ₹21,000. No software built. [Manual MVP](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#04-the-manual-mvp-plan)\n- Recruit past students and connections with one screening question; screen 12 to 14 to land 10. [Who](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#who)\n- Day 7 gate: one user, task, bottleneck, input, output, metric. [Day by day](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#what-day-by-day)\n- Success criteria are fixed before Day 1, for example 7+ completing a proof by Day 21 is strong. [Success criteria](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#success-criteria-fixed-before-day-1)\n- Kill conditions: fewer than 7 paid before Day 1; fewer than 6 log 2 real conversations by Day 5; fewer than 5 submit a prototype by Day 14. [Kill conditions](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#kill-conditions-checked-during-the-pilot)\n- If one trips, stop and write it down; do not improvise a rescue. [Kill conditions](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#kill-conditions-checked-during-the-pilot)"},{"h":"How it connects to you","a":"how-it-connects-to-you","text":"- Assignment 1 is smaller: 2 to 5 real people, by hand, in 21 days, with criteria and kill conditions written before Day 1. [Aarav example, 05](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#05-now-yours)\n- The real cohort zero ran this hypothesis: nine of ten presented a credible demo. [Workshop notes, 10](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#10-cohort-zero-10-people-rs-21000-21-days)\n\nSee also: [Idea to App workshop](../lectures/workshop-idea-to-app.md), [Assignment 1](../how-to/do-assignment-1-first-proof.md), [Hackathon](../lectures/hackathon.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/due-dates.md","title":"Due dates and where to hand in","meta":"title: Due dates and where to hand in\nsummary: Every practice set, assignment and task with its due date exactly as written and where it goes: Assignment 1, Lectures 3 to 8, hackathon, Practice sets 1 to 3, RAG Lab.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Due dates and where to hand in","a":"due-dates-and-where-to-hand-in","text":"Each line: the task, its lecture, the due date as the page writes it, and where it goes. Where no date is written, the line says so. [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md)"},{"h":"Before the hackathon","a":"before-the-hackathon","text":"- Lecture 3 practice set, \"Replay before you render\" (code track): no due date written, it \"Follows Lecture 3, Sat 26 Sep 2026\". Post in the code track channel. [Lecture 3 practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#06-when-you-are-stuck)\n- The older code-track tick list asks for the one-page spec with \"Deadline: before the next code lecture\", in the code track channel. [Code-track tick list](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#5-actions-this-week)\n- Lecture 4 practice set (five deliverables): \"Due before the Lecture 5 practical, Fri 9 Oct.\" Post all five in one thread in the Discord channel. Office hour Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST. [Lecture 4 practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md#06-where-to-post)\n- Assignment 1, First Proof (follows the Idea to App workshop): \"Due Fri 9 Oct 2026\". Submit through the assignment form on the LMS; share your tracker link in the assignments channel. [Assignment 1](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#05-what-to-submit)\n- Lecture 5 adds: \"The deadline was 9 October; the late window is open with a penalty.\" [Lecture 5 notes, 18](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#18-before-the-next-office-hour)\n- Lecture 5 practice set (code track): no due date written, it \"Follows Lecture 5, Fri 9 Oct 2026.\" Post on the C8 Discord code channel. [Lecture 5 practice set, 09](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#09-where-to-post-and-how-to-get-unblocked)\n- Lecture 6 practice set (four things): \"Due before the office hour, Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.\" Post in the Discord assignments channel. [Lecture 6 practice set, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md#01-what-to-submit-0-of-4-ready)\n- Lecture 7 practice set (five things, code track): \"Due before Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct, 6 pm IST.\" Post in the Discord code channel. [Lecture 7 practice set](../../raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md#your-function-live-on-a-url)\n- Lecture 8: no practice set page and no due date written; done equals a classmate posting \"verified\" next to your URL. [Lecture 8 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-overview.md#done-equals)"},{"h":"Hackathon","a":"hackathon","text":"- Lock one problem \"by Sunday, 9 AM\". [Hackathon](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#pick-one-problem)\n- \"Work submitted after Tue 20 Oct, 9 PM IST does not count\". [Hackathon, rule book](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- \"Submit by Tue, 9 PM\": filled template, public repo, live link. The form link goes up in your check-in channel on Tuesday. [Hackathon, submit](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#submit-by-tue-9-pm)\n- The sheet: \"Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST\"; results Thu 22 Oct. [Code sheet, hackathon](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue), [Code sheet, results](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#thu-22-oct-2026--hackathon-results-announcement)"},{"h":"Lectures 9 to 14","a":"lectures-9-to-14","text":"- Practice set 1, \"Verify your own job\" (Lectures 9 and 10, both tracks): no due date written. Share your sheet on the Discord. [Practice set 1, 05](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#05-share-it-0-of-3-ready)\n- Practice set 2, \"Write the eval\" (both tracks): \"Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\" Share it on the Discord. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#03-share-it)\n- Lecture 11, the Tool Bench: no due date written. [Lecture 11 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-overview.md#try-it-yourself)\n- Practice set 3, \"Write the code behind the bench\" (Lecture 12, code track): \"Due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026.\" Submit on the LMS: your fork with the five-question table, and your deployed address. [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#05-submit-it)\n- The Lectures 9 to 12 checklist: \"Finish the rest before Lecture 13.\" [Checklist](../../raw/practice/checklist.md)\n- Lecture 13, the Retrieval Bench: no due date written. [Lecture 13 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#09-now-do-the-retrieval-bench)\n- Lecture 14, RAG Lab levels 5 to 9 (code track): \"before the office hour on Tuesday 24 November\". No place to hand it in is written. [Lecture 14 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md#try-it-yourself)\n\nSee also: [Lecture 04](../lectures/lecture-04.md), [Lecture 06](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Lecture 07](../lectures/lecture-07.md), [Hackathon](../lectures/hackathon.md), [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [Where sources disagree](where-sources-disagree.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/from-memory-txt-to-tables.md","title":"From memory.txt to tables","meta":"title: From memory.txt to tables\nsummary: How Aarav's app learned to remember: memory.txt (L6), the file dies on Vercel (L7, L8), Supabase tables (L8), then context, tools, search (L10 to L14).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"From memory.txt to tables","a":"from-memorytxt-to-tables","text":"The model never learns to remember. The system around it does: \"The model still remembers nothing. Your system does.\" [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#tuesday-again-with-storage)"},{"h":"Lecture 3: the first sign","a":"lecture-3-the-first-sign","text":"- A search box has \"no memory, no context\"; chat won because it holds context. [Lecture 3 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#09-the-vote-that-lost)"},{"h":"Lecture 6: it forgets, so keep a file","a":"lecture-6-it-forgets-so-keep-a-file","text":"- Tuesday: Aarav types \"I implemented the plan, but hit a problem.\" A 200 comes back; the model has no idea which plan. [Lecture 6 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#04-aaravs-app-forgets)\n- \"Memory dies. Storage survives.\" Storage sits outside the app, on disk. [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#memory-dies-storage-survives)\n- The fix, without saying \"database\": keep a `memory.txt`; after every reply write who said what, with the date and time; send the file with the next prompt. [Lecture 6 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- \"Start with the file and move up only when it breaks.\" [Lecture 6 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#05-derive-it-before-you-name-it)\n- The file and the key live on your server: on the user's machine the user can wipe it and anyone can read it. [Lecture 6 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#06-where-does-storage-live)\n- \"`memory.txt` stored one shape: who, what, when.\" [Lecture 6 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#09-three-questions-give-the-shape)\n- Eight tables and their pointers, kept outside the app: \"That is a **database**.\" [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#storage-with-a-shape-has-a-name)"},{"h":"Lecture 7: the file dies","a":"lecture-7-the-file-dies","text":"- After step 04 the server \"forgets every plan the moment it answers.\" [Lecture 7 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#09-step-04--gradio-becomes-a-client-tonight)\n- \"The file dies.\" Plans saved to `memory.txt` work locally and fail on Vercel. [Lecture 7 notes, 11](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm)"},{"h":"Lecture 8: tables in Supabase","a":"lecture-8-tables-in-supabase","text":"- Step 05, `memory.txt` in four lines: read the file, run `diagnose`, build a line with time, workflow and plan, write it all back. Most people missed the timestamp. [Lecture 8 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#05-step-05--memorytxt-in-four-steps)\n- Where a file breaks: 60,000 lines for 100 users, two writers at once (a race condition), a small machine returning 500 to everyone. [Lecture 8 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not)\n- Deployed, step 05 fails anyway: \"Vercel's file system is read-only, so the write fails with a 500.\" [Lecture 8 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md#a-file-is-enough-until-people-write-at-once)\n- PostgreSQL, rented from Supabase, reached with the same four dials: address, action, key, package. [Lecture 8 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#08-the-same-four-dials)\n- Modelling Lab to database: thing to table, fact to column, link to foreign key, id to primary key. [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)\n- \"Four file lines became one insert.\" `diagnose` and the route did not change. [Lecture 8 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)"},{"h":"Lectures 10 to 14: getting the right data to the model","a":"lectures-10-to-14-getting-the-right-data-to-the-model","text":"- Lecture 10: **context** is \"the data that reaches the model inside its context window\"; automating the paste needs a data source and a bridge. [Lecture 10 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no), [Lecture 10 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- Lecture 11: pasting a sheet is repetitive, error-prone, stale and does not scale; a server fetches from the trusted source when a question needs it. [Lecture 11 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#03-priyas-shop-the-paste-problem)\n- Lecture 11: \"If it doesn't reach the LLM, the LLM can't use it.\" [Lecture 11 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#05-what-reaches-the-llm)\n- Lecture 12: a tool, `get_lectures(track)`, reads the curriculum sheet when the model asks. [Lecture 12 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function)\n- Lecture 13: retrieve, augment, generate. \"Swap the sheet for Supabase and step 1 is still retrieval.\" [Lecture 13 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#01-name-the-three-steps)\n- Lecture 13: with tables from your Lecture 6 domain model, the LLM writes SQL and the server runs it on Supabase. [Lecture 13 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#07-to-label-it-know-the-domain)\n- Lecture 14: search 183 passages, send the best 3. Their embeddings fit a plain file; Supabase's pgvector waits for a real limit. [Lecture 14 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions), [Lecture 14 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#08-do-you-need-a-vector-database)\n\nSee also: [Storage](../concepts/storage.md), [Race condition](../concepts/race-condition.md), [PostgreSQL](../concepts/postgresql.md), [Context](../concepts/context.md), [Retrieval-augmented generation](../concepts/retrieval-augmented-generation.md), [Lecture 6](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Lecture 8](../lectures/lecture-08.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/schedule.md","title":"Schedule: every lecture, date and title","meta":"title: Schedule: every lecture, date and title\nsummary: The date, title and track of every lecture from 1 to 19, with holidays, the hackathon and the catch-up week. From the curriculum sheet, which wins on dates and titles.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: tools, from the curriculum sheet\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Schedule: every lecture, date and title","a":"schedule-every-lecture-date-and-title","text":"Lectures run on Fridays and Saturdays. A lecture marked Taught has published notes, whatever the sheet's Status column says. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md)"},{"h":"In date order","a":"in-date-order","text":"- Lecture 1, Sat 19 Sep 2026: Orientation and State of AI 2026. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-19-sep-2026--1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md)\n- Lecture 2, Fri 25 Sep 2026: What is an interface; UI as the first interface. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-25-sep-2026--2-what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md)\n- Lecture 3, Sat 26 Sep 2026: PRD to UI design to frontend app. Each track has its own session. Code track title: Building UI: Design to Deploy. Code tools: Python, Gradio or Streamlit, Hugging Face Spaces. No-code tools: Google Stitch, Google AI Studio. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-prd-to-ui-design-to-frontend-app) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md)\n- Fri 02 Oct 2026: Gandhi Jayanti: Holiday. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-02-oct-2026--gandhi-jayanti--holiday)\n- Lecture 4, Sat 03 Oct 2026: API as the second interface. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md)\n- Lecture 5, Fri 09 Oct 2026: Building APIs and backend. Each track has its own session. Code tools: Python, FastAPI, Render. No-code tools: Antigravity. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md)\n- Lecture 6, Sat 10 Oct 2026: Databases and domain modelling. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-10-oct-2026--6-databases-and-domain-modelling) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md)\n- Lecture 7, Fri 16 Oct 2026: Connect backend to database. Each track has its own session. Code tools: FastAPI, Supabase. No-code tools: Antigravity, Supabase. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md)\n- Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct 2026: End to end MVP build and deploy. Each track has its own session. Code tools: Gradio, FastAPI, Supabase, Render. No-code tools: Antigravity, Supabase, Vercel. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md)\n- Sat 17 Oct 2026: Hackathon (Sat to Tue). Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue)\n- Thu 22 Oct 2026: Hackathon results announcement. Hackathon results announced. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#thu-22-oct-2026--hackathon-results-announcement)\n- Fri 23 Oct 2026: Catch-up week: No lecture. No lectures Fri 23 and Sat 24 October 2026. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-23-oct-2026--catch-up-week--no-lecture)\n- Lecture 9, Fri 30 Oct 2026: LLMs from First Principles. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-30-oct-2026--9-llms-from-first-principles) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md)\n- Lecture 10, Sat 31 Oct 2026: Tool calling and MCP. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-31-oct-2026--10-tool-calling-and-mcp) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md)\n- Fri 06 Nov 2026: Diwali break: Holiday. No lectures Fri 6 and Sat 7 November 2026. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-06-nov-2026--diwali-break--holiday)\n- Lecture 11, Fri 13 Nov 2026: Tool calling and MCP. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md)\n- Lecture 12, Sat 14 Nov 2026: Building with tool calling and MCP. Each track has its own session. Code tools: Python, Groq, FastMCP. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md)\n- Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026: Retrieval-augmented generation. Both tracks together. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md)\n- Lecture 14, Sat 21 Nov 2026: Building RAG apps. Each track has its own session. Taught. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps) [code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps) [notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md)\n- Lecture 15, Fri 27 Nov 2026: LLM memory: building a second brain. Both tracks together. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-27-nov-2026--15-llm-memory-building-a-second-brain)\n- Lecture 16, Sat 28 Nov 2026: Connecting the dots; deriving harness engineering. Both tracks together. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-28-nov-2026--16-connecting-the-dots-deriving-harness-engineering)\n- Lecture 17, Fri 04 Dec 2026: Fine-tuning LLMs. Both tracks together. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-04-dec-2026--17-fine-tuning-llms)\n- Lecture 18, Sat 05 Dec 2026: Data preparation techniques. Both tracks together. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#sat-05-dec-2026--18-data-preparation-techniques)\n- Lecture 19, Fri 11 Dec 2026: Evaluation of LLMs. Both tracks together. [sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-11-dec-2026--19-evaluation-of-llms)\n\nSee also: [Due dates](due-dates.md), [Where sources disagree](where-sources-disagree.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/scoreboards.md","title":"The scoreboards","meta":"title: The scoreboards\nsummary: Scores that moved as things were added around the model: track question 1, 5, 7 of 10; tool card 3, 4 or 5, 5; searches 5, 6, 8.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The scoreboards","a":"the-scoreboards","text":"The course kept the model and question, changed one thing around the model, and scored again: \"Same model, same question. Only the context changed, and the score stopped short of 10.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)"},{"h":"Lectures 9 to 11: the track question, out of 10","a":"lectures-9-to-11-the-track-question-out-of-10","text":"- The model: GPT-OSS 120B, bare, in the GroqCloud playground. [Lecture 10 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- No context: 1. The tracks were made up. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- A short block on the real tracks: 5. Still JavaScript and Go for Code; Bubble, Webflow, Zapier for No Code. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- The full C8 curriculum as CSV text: 7. Still named Zapier, which is not in the CSV. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- Why it stopped at 7: \"Hallucination = missing context + a trained habit of never saying no.\" [Lecture 10 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#04-trained-never-to-say-no)\n- Lecture 9 gave no score; Practice set 1 records \"1 out of 10 bare, 5 with the real tracks pasted in.\" [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)\n- Reaching the manual 7 out of 10 with no pasting was the live challenge; almost nobody finished. [Lecture 10 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- Lecture 11 repeated the three scores: 1, 5, 7. [Lecture 11 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#02-from-lecture-10-the-llm-fills-gaps)"},{"h":"Practice sets 1 and 2: your own score","a":"practice-sets-1-and-2-your-own-score","text":"- Practice set 1, step 04: score one task bare, then with context, each out of 10 against your ideal output. [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)\n- That score was by feel: \"Someone else could give the same reply a 4 where you gave it a 7.\" [Practice set 2, 01](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#01-why)\n- Practice set 2 re-scores it: hard lines first, then each quality from 1 to 5, beside your old scores. [Practice set 2, 02](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md#02-do-it-0-of-3-ready)"},{"h":"Lecture 11: the tool card, out of 5","a":"lecture-11-the-tool-card-out-of-5","text":"- Five questions, marked first: three should call the tool (with 12, 7 and 10), two should not. [Tool Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- Empty card: 3. \"Gets a lecture by number\": 4 or 5; it calls the tool for the CS50 question too. [Tool Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- What it returns, when to use it, when not to, and what the number is: 5, every run. [Tool Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)"},{"h":"Lecture 13: no score","a":"lecture-13-no-score","text":"- Without a tool, \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" got a made-up Lecture 12; pasting the sheet still gave Lecture 12. [Lecture 13 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#02-break-it-a-question-with-no-number), [Lecture 13 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#03-three-fixes-from-the-chat)"},{"h":"Lecture 14: the search, out of 10","a":"lecture-14-the-search-out-of-10","text":"- Ten questions, A to J, with known passages: the golden set, scored as recall at 3. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- Shared words: 5 of 10, found A, E, F, G and I. [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- Rare words (the core of BM25): 6 of 10. J moved from place 41 to the top 3; B stayed at place 27. [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n- Meaning (gte-small): 8 of 10, found A, B, C, D, E, G, H and I. F fell to place 10, J to place 6. [Lecture 14 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#07-score-by-meaning)\n- Level 5 joins both lists. Guess first: 9, 10, or stay at 8? No result is written. [Lecture 14 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#09-finish-levels-5-to-9)"},{"h":"Elsewhere","a":"elsewhere","text":"- Hackathon, Eval Co-pilot: mark ten golden pairs pass or fail, and store runs to answer \"Which cases failed in run 1 and passed in run 2?\" [Eval Co-pilot brief](../../raw/practice/hackathon-eval-copilot.md#what-is-worth-storing)\n- The sheet plans one for Lecture 15: \"three questions with known answers, scored before and after retrieval.\" [Code sheet, Lecture 15](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-27-nov-2026--15-llm-memory-building-a-second-brain)\n\nSee also: [The check comes first](the-check-comes-first.md), [The track question](the-track-question.md), [Golden set](../concepts/golden-set.md), [Tool card](../concepts/tool-card.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md), [Lecture 14](../lectures/lecture-14.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/the-check-comes-first.md","title":"The check comes first","meta":"title: The check comes first\nsummary: Writing the check before the run: Verifier's Law, golden dataset (L1), Verifier's Rule (L9), golden pair (L10, L11), eval (PS2), golden set (L14).\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The check comes first","a":"the-check-comes-first","text":"One habit, many names: write down what a right answer looks like, then run. \"Ask this every time: how do I verify whether this output is correct? No answer, no build.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 12](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks)"},{"h":"Week 0 and Lecture 1: the verifier and the golden dataset","a":"week-0-and-lecture-1-the-verifier-and-the-golden-dataset","text":"- Workshop: cohort zero's bar was set first, 60%; nine of ten presented a credible demo. [Workshop notes, 10](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#10-cohort-zero-10-people-rs-21000-21-days)\n- Aarav's manual MVP plan has \"Success criteria, fixed before Day 1\". [Aarav example, 04](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#success-criteria-fixed-before-day-1)\n- The Verifier's Law: \"if you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system, and you cannot claim the capability.\" [Lecture 1 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#05-what-was-actually-missing)\n- The verify game: ideal input and output pairs, written down, are the golden dataset. [Lecture 1 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers)\n- Homework: three golden pairs by hand, one you would reject, then the stranger test. [Lecture 1 notes, 14](../../raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md#14-what-to-do-next)"},{"h":"Lectures 3 and 5: golden pairs and a threshold (code track)","a":"lectures-3-and-5-golden-pairs-and-a-threshold-code-track","text":"- \"Ten golden pairs before one line of code\": 10 to 20 to start. [Lecture 3 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code)\n- \"A good output clears a number you wrote down before you built anything.\" [Lecture 3 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#06-the-verifier-is-a-threshold-worked-in-minutes)\n- Test-driven development, \"written in English\": the test before the software. [Lecture 3 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md#07-test-driven-development-written-in-english)\n- The Lecture 3 practice set also says \"Predict before you run.\" [Lecture 3 practice set, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md#04-rules-of-the-build)\n- Lecture 5: run your golden input, compare with your golden output, change the prompt once. [Lecture 5 practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)"},{"h":"Lecture 9: the Verifier's Rule","a":"lecture-9-the-verifiers-rule","text":"- \"The ease of training AI on a task is proportional to how verifiable the task is.\" [Lecture 9 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited)\n- Can a machine learn judgment? \"Verifier first, data second.\" [Lecture 9 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment)\n- Practice set 1: a verifier for every atomic task. [Practice set 1, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#02-fill-the-sheet-0-of-5-ready)"},{"h":"Lectures 10 and 11: the golden pair","a":"lectures-10-and-11-the-golden-pair","text":"- Golden pair: ideal input and ideal output, one golden dataset entry; the gap to the actual output names the cause. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- Practice set 1, step 04: \"Write its golden pair first\". [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)\n- Office hour, 3 Nov: \"The golden pair, written before the run\". [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)\n- Lecture 11: \"the golden pair is ready before any model runs\": the kettle, 4,980. [Lecture 11 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#04-the-sheet-and-its-one-rule)\n- Lecture 11: write the expected record first; the verifier is \"The expected record, written before the run\". [Lecture 11 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence), [Lecture 11 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#13-tomorrow-lecture-12)"},{"h":"Practice set 2: the eval","a":"practice-set-2-the-eval","text":"- For your tested task: the decision, the inputs, the hard lines, what a 1 and a 5 look like, who grades. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md)\n- Course lesson 7.6 calls a written rubric an eval. [Lesson 7.6](../../raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself.md)"},{"h":"Lectures 11 and 12: tests before the run","a":"lectures-11-and-12-tests-before-the-run","text":"- Tool Bench: \"Before writing a word, mark what each question should do.\" [Tool Bench notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests)\n- The sheet: Lecture 11 learners \"mark the tests before the run\"; Lecture 12's done equals includes \"the verifier written before the run\". [Code sheet, Lecture 11](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp), [Code sheet, Lecture 12](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n- Practice set 3: test the card; hand in the five-question table. [Practice set 3](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#05-submit-it)"},{"h":"Hackathon","a":"hackathon","text":"- Eval Co-pilot: \"Grade three pairs by hand first.\" [Eval Co-pilot brief](../../raw/practice/hackathon-eval-copilot.md#ai-decides)"},{"h":"Lecture 14: the golden set","a":"lecture-14-the-golden-set","text":"- Ten golden pairs; count how often the right passage lands in the top 3: recall at 3. \"Know the right answers first.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#03-count-the-shared-words)\n- \"The golden set comes first.\" [Lecture 14 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#04-make-rare-words-count-more)\n\nSee also: [Verifier's Law](../concepts/verifiers-law.md), [Verifier's Rule](../concepts/verifiers-rule.md), [Golden dataset](../concepts/golden-dataset.md), [Golden pair](../concepts/golden-pair.md), [Golden set](../concepts/golden-set.md), [Scoreboards](scoreboards.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/the-track-question.md","title":"The track question","meta":"title: The track question\nsummary: The one question the model was asked in Lectures 9 to 12, which track to choose: exact wordings, invented tracks, scores 1, 5, 7, fix.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"The track question","a":"the-track-question","text":"From Lecture 9 to Lecture 12 the course kept asking a model one question about choosing a track in 100x. \"Since Lecture 9 you have asked a model one question: which track should I choose?\" [Lecture 12 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#one-tool-call-then-an-mcp-server)"},{"h":"The right answer","a":"the-right-answer","text":"- The real choice has two axes: a career track (entrepreneurship or career acceleration, set by your North Star) and an execution path (code or no-code, set by your background). [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- Lecture 10 names them: two career tracks (Entrepreneurship, Career Accelerator) and two execution paths (Code, No Code). [Lecture 10 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)"},{"h":"Lecture 9 (Fri 30 Oct): it breaks","a":"lecture-9-fri-30-oct-it-breaks","text":"- Wording: \"Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\" [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- The shape was right (a decision framework, assessment questions, a checklist); the tracks were not: front-end, back-end, full-stack, data and ML, DevOps, product, security. [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- \"Right template, wrong data: hallucination.\" [Lecture 9 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#04-the-query-that-broke-it)\n- A learner's fix (scrape, store a file, a system prompt to read it) has one step only an LLM can do, so the system is probabilistic. [Lecture 9 notes, 05](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#05-can-the-orange-box-fix-it)\n- The deterministic answer: fetch the track data from the curriculum's database into a template. \"One API call, one template, the right answer every time.\" [Lecture 9 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#06-the-deterministic-answer-already-existed)\n- The same student's next question, \"When will I get a job?\", has no rule; a mentor can estimate it. [Lecture 9 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md#07-where-rules-run-out)"},{"h":"Lecture 10 (Sat 31 Oct): scored, then diagnosed","a":"lecture-10-sat-31-oct-scored-then-diagnosed","text":"- Same wording, on GPT-OSS 120B in the GroqCloud playground. [Lecture 10 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#01-the-test-query)\n- Scores out of 10: no context 1; a short block on the real tracks 5; the full C8 curriculum as CSV 7, still naming Zapier. [Lecture 10 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it)\n- Golden pair: the ideal output is the 7-out-of-10 answer with the untaught tools removed. The cause: the curriculum never names a UI builder; adding Lovable, Google AI Studio, Antigravity closes the gap. [Lecture 10 notes, 06](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair)\n- The live challenge: reach 7 out of 10 with no pasting. [Lecture 10 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md#07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge)\n- Practice set 1 copies the method to your own task and quotes the lecture's run as \"1 out of 10 bare, 5 with the real tracks pasted in.\" [Practice set 1, 04](../../raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md#04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready)"},{"h":"Office hour (Tue 3 Nov)","a":"office-hour-tue-3-nov","text":"- \"Which track should I choose?\" is the worked example of ideal input, ideal output, the model's output and the difference. [Office hour, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md#02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling)"},{"h":"Lecture 11 (Fri 13 Nov): the recap","a":"lecture-11-fri-13-nov-the-recap","text":"- Wording: \"Which execution track should I choose in 100x?\" with the scores 1, 5 and 7. [Lecture 11 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md#02-from-lecture-10-the-llm-fills-gaps)\n- The bench moves to a new question: \"When is Lecture 12 of 100xEngineers Cohort 8, and what is it about?\" [Tool Bench notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md#02-ask-nothing-reaches-the-sheet)"},{"h":"Lecture 12 (Sat 14 Nov, code track): a tool answers it","a":"lecture-12-sat-14-nov-code-track-a-tool-answers-it","text":"- Wording in `tool_call.py`: \"Which track should I choose in the 100xEngineers cohort?\" [Lecture 12 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#03-step-1-the-tool-call)\n- The tool `get_lectures(track)` reads one track's tab of the curriculum sheet. [Lecture 12 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function)\n- The model asked twice, once per track; every tool it named is in the sheet. [Lecture 12 notes, 03](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#03-step-1-the-tool-call)"},{"h":"After Lecture 12","a":"after-lecture-12","text":"- Lectures 13 and 14 ask \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" instead. [Lecture 13 notes, 02](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#02-break-it-a-question-with-no-number), [Lecture 14 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md#01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions)\n\nSee also: [Scoreboards](scoreboards.md), [Hallucination](../concepts/hallucination.md), [Golden pair](../concepts/golden-pair.md), [Lecture 09](../lectures/lecture-09.md), [Lecture 10](../lectures/lecture-10.md), [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/where-sources-disagree-tools-and-numbers.md","title":"Where sources disagree: tools, names and numbers","meta":"title: Where sources disagree: tools, names and numbers\nsummary: Course pages that disagree on deploy tools, Lecture 5's model, when tables reach Supabase, file counts, function names, bench size, coach time and Aarav's projects.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Where sources disagree: tools, names and numbers","a":"where-sources-disagree-tools-names-and-numbers","text":"None of these are dates or lecture titles, and all the pages were taken on the same day, so the rules in AGENTS.md say only that they disagree. Both versions are given. [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md)"},{"h":"Tools","a":"tools","text":"- Render: the code sheet lists \"Python, FastAPI, Render\" for Lecture 5 and \"Gradio, FastAPI, Supabase, Render\" for Lecture 8; its Lecture 7 row lists \"FastAPI, Supabase\". [Code sheet, Lecture 5](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend), [Code sheet, Lecture 8](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy)\n- The Lecture 7 and 8 pages deploy to Vercel, not Render. [Lecture 7 notes, 08](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes), [Lecture 8 notes, 10](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers)\n- The Lecture 4 overview lists Lecture 5's code track as \"Python, requests, Gradio\". [Lecture 4 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-overview.md#dates)"},{"h":"Lecture 5's model","a":"lecture-5s-model","text":"- The notes: \"GPT OSS 120B, free at 30 requests per minute\". [Lecture 5 notes, 04](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md#04-filling-the-process-with-one-line)\n- The troubleshooting page and the practice set use `openai/gpt-oss-20b`. [Lecture 5 troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md#01-the-errors-you-should-expect), [Lecture 5 practice set, 02](../../raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md#02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready)\n- Lecture 4's troubleshooting gives `openai/gpt-oss-120b`, \"or `openai/gpt-oss-20b` as the fallback\". [Lecture 4 troubleshooting](../../raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md)"},{"h":"When the tables go into Supabase","a":"when-the-tables-go-into-supabase","text":"- Lecture 6 notes: \"Lecture 7, Fri 16 Oct. These tables go into Supabase\". [Lecture 6 notes, 13](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real)\n- Lecture 6 overview and whiteboard: Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct, \"these exact tables, typed into Supabase\". [Lecture 6 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-overview.md), [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#three-weeks-three-boxes)\n- The code sheet's Lecture 7 row also says \"Connect the backend to Supabase\". [Code sheet, Lecture 7](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database)\n- What was taught: Lecture 7 notes move storage to Lecture 8, and Lecture 8 builds the tables. [Lecture 7 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md#01-the-lecture-in-one-line), [Lecture 8 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md#09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables)"},{"h":"Numbers and names","a":"numbers-and-names","text":"- gpt-oss-120b: \"14 files, 65 GB\" in the Lecture 6 notes' design sketch; \"117 billion parameters in 15 files\" on the whiteboard. [Lecture 6 notes, 07](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md#07-the-high-level-design), [Lecture 6 whiteboard](../../raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md#some-work-does-not-fit-in-that-box)\n- Lecture 12: the notes build `get_lectures(track)`; Practice set 3 gives `get_lecture(number)`. Each is right for its own page. [Lecture 12 notes, 01](../../raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md#01-step-1-the-function), [Practice set 3, 01](../../raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md#01-what-you-write)\n- Lecture 13's Retrieval Bench: the overview says \"Run all five questions\"; the notes say \"Do all seven stations.\" [Lecture 13 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-overview.md#try-it-yourself), [Lecture 13 notes, 09](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md#09-now-do-the-retrieval-bench)"},{"h":"The discovery-to-mvp-coach and Aarav","a":"the-discovery-to-mvp-coach-and-aarav","text":"- Coach time: Assignment 1 says \"Budget 60 to 90 minutes\"; the workshop notes say 60 to 90 minutes in sections 07 and 14 but \"The skill, 40 to 90 minutes\" in section 13. [Assignment 1, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md#02-the-five-steps), [Workshop notes, 07](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain), [Workshop notes, 13](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#13-from-the-qa)\n- The Aarav example says \"About 90 to 150 minutes\". [Aarav example, 01](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#01-how-the-coach-works)\n- Aarav's projects: the workshop notes say 3 finished; the Aarav example says \"3 half-finished projects\". [Workshop notes, 06](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md#06-aarav-at-1238-am-what-an-observation-looks-like), [Aarav example, 02](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md#02-the-user-aarav)\n\nSee also: [Where sources disagree: status, titles and dates](where-sources-disagree.md), [From memory.txt to tables](from-memory-txt-to-tables.md), [Lecture 05](../lectures/lecture-05.md), [Lecture 06](../lectures/lecture-06.md), [Lecture 12](../lectures/lecture-12.md), [Lecture 13](../lectures/lecture-13.md)"}]},{"path":"wiki/threads/where-sources-disagree.md","title":"Where sources disagree: status, titles and dates","meta":"title: Where sources disagree: status, titles and dates\nsummary: Course pages that disagree on lecture status, lecture titles and numbers, hackathon end and the Practice set 2 date, with both versions and which wins.\nupdated: 2026-11-27\nwritten_by: Claude Opus 5.5\napproved_by: Siddhant, 2026-11-27","sections":[{"h":"Where sources disagree: status, titles and dates","a":"where-sources-disagree-status-titles-and-dates","text":"The sheet calls itself \"the authority for dates and titles\". The rules in AGENTS.md add: a lecture with notes has been taught, whatever the Status says; otherwise, say that they disagree. [Code sheet](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md)"},{"h":"Status says Planned, but the notes exist","a":"status-says-planned-but-the-notes-exist","text":"- The code-track sheet's Status is \"Planned\" on every lecture row from 2 to 14, for example Lecture 14. Lecture 14 has notes, so it was taught. [Code sheet, Lecture 14](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps), [Lecture 14 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md)\n- The no-code sheet says Planned for Lectures 2, 4, 6, 9, 10 and 13, and Done for 3, 5, 7, 8, 11, 12 and 14. Lectures 2, 4, 6, 9 and 10 all have notes, so all were taught. [No-code sheet](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md), [Lecture 9 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md)\n- Lecture 13 is Planned on both tabs, but its notes are dated \"20 November 2026 · Both tracks\". It was taught. [Code sheet, Lecture 13](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation), [No-code sheet, Lecture 13](../../raw/sheet/no-code-track.md#fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation), [Lecture 13 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md)"},{"h":"Lecture titles and numbers","a":"lecture-titles-and-numbers","text":"- Lecture 3: the notes say \"Building UI: design to code\"; the sheet says \"3. Building UI: Design to Deploy\" and the overview \"Building UI: design to deploy\". The sheet wins. [Lecture 3 notes](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md), [Code sheet, Lecture 3](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy), [Lecture 3 overview](../../raw/lectures/lecture-03-overview.md)\n- The older code-track pages call Building UI \"Live Lecture 04\"; on the sheet Building UI is Lecture 3 (Sat 26 Sep) and Lecture 4 is \"API as the second interface\". The sheet wins. [Building UI notes](../../raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md), [Code-track practice set](../../raw/lectures/code-track-practice-set.md), [Code sheet, Lecture 4](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface)\n- Assignment 1 \"Follows Live Lecture 01: Idea to App Workshop\"; the sheet's Lecture 1 is \"Orientation and State of AI 2026\", Sat 19 Sep 2026. The sheet wins for the title. [Assignment 1](../../raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md), [Code sheet, Lecture 1](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-19-sep-2026--1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026)"},{"h":"Dates","a":"dates","text":"- Hackathon end: the code-track tick list says \"Saturday 17 to Monday 19 October\"; the sheet says \"Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST\". The sheet wins. [Code-track tick list](../../raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md#5-actions-this-week), [Code sheet, hackathon](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue)\n- The hackathon page agrees with the sheet (\"after Tue 20 Oct, 9 PM IST does not count\") and calls it \"Build an app in 72 hours\". [Hackathon, rule book](../../raw/practice/hackathon.md#the-rule-book)\n- Practice set 2: \"Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\" The sheet has Lecture 11 on \"Fri 13 Nov 2026\". The sheet wins for the lecture's date; it lists no practice set due dates, so \"Thu 12 Nov\" and \"before Lecture 11\" disagree. [Practice set 2](../../raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md), [Code sheet, Lecture 11](../../raw/sheet/code-track.md#fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp)\n\nSee also: [Where sources disagree: tools and numbers](where-sources-disagree-tools-and-numbers.md), [Due dates](due-dates.md), [Lecture 03](../lectures/lecture-03.md), [Code track Building UI](../lectures/code-track-building-ui.md), [Hackathon](../lectures/hackathon.md)"}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/a-loop-that-remembers.md","title":"1.5 Memory: a loop that remembers · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.5 Memory: a loop that remembers · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/a-loop-that-remembers\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/a-loop-that-remembers.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 5 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 72","sections":[{"h":"Memory: a loop that remembers","a":"memory-a-loop-that-remembers","text":"Wire two gates in a loop, so each one's output feeds the other's gate.\n\n> Caption: Make the loop hold a 1 with the input off.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nEach gate holds the other one open, so the loop stays on with nothing feeding it. That is how a chip keeps one bit after the input is gone. Billions of these loops are its memory."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/be-the-adder.md","title":"1.4 An adder: switches that do sums · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.4 An adder: switches that do sums · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/be-the-adder\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/be-the-adder.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 4 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 87","sections":[{"h":"An adder: switches that do sums","a":"an-adder-switches-that-do-sums","text":"Before the circuit does it, do it by hand. The answer switches are worth 4, 2 and 1.\n\n> Caption: Set the answer switches for three sums.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nIn the chip, nobody does the maths. Switches open and shut, and the answer is wherever the current ends up. Subtracting is adding a negative number. Multiplying is adding again and again. Pack these circuits together and you have the **processor**: the calculator at the heart of every chip."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.md","title":"1.7 Check yourself · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.7 Check yourself · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 7 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 283","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**Why does it matter that a transistor is opened by electricity?**\n\nBecause its output is also electricity, so one transistor can open the next. That is what lets them form circuits.\n\n**With switches worth 4, 2 and 1, which number is 101? How many numbers can three switches show?**\n\n4 + 1 = 5. Three switches show eight numbers, 0 to 7.\n\n**Two switches in a row let current through only when both are on. Which digit of an addition is that?**\n\nThe carry digit.\n\n**How does a loop of two transistors keep a 1 after the input is removed?**\n\nEach one's output holds the other one's gate open, so the loop keeps itself on.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **Transistor**: A gate on a wire, opened and shut by electricity.\n- **Bit**: One 1 or 0. A chip holds it in a loop of two transistors.\n- **Byte**: Eight bits in a row: a number from 0 to 255.\n- **Binary**: Writing numbers with only 1 (on) and 0 (off).\n- **Adder**: A small circuit of switches whose output is the sum of its inputs.\n- **Processor**: The chip's calculator: many adders packed together.\n- **Program**: A list of instructions stored as ones and zeros.\n- **Clock**: The tick on which the chip does its next instruction."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"[Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s11) drew the boxes behind every app, and box 1 was the machine. This chapter opened box 1. Every row your app saves and every token a model sends back is these switches, flipped in the right order. The next seven chapters stack layers on top of them."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/index.md","title":"1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sand · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sand · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/index.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 1 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 148","sections":[{"h":"Transistors: a switch made of sand","a":"transistors-a-switch-made-of-sand","text":"**Why this matters:** Every row your app saves and every word a model writes back is tiny switches flipping. This chapter builds the switch.\n\n**How it works:** play the drawing until the explanation opens, then press Next. Pieces with a shadow under them can be clicked. A few lessons are a short read with no drawing.\n\nSand is mostly silicon. Electricity is a flow along a wire. Carve silicon the right way and you get a gate on that wire: a little electricity on its control opens it.\n\n> Caption: Light the lamp. You may only touch the first gate.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat gate is a **transistor**. Electricity opens it, and what comes out is electricity, so **one transistor can open the next**, in chains as long as you like. A phone chip holds tens of billions of them."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/ones-and-zeros.md","title":"1.2 Ones and zeros · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.2 Ones and zeros · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/ones-and-zeros\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/ones-and-zeros.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 2 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 119","sections":[{"h":"Ones and zeros","a":"ones-and-zeros","text":"A switch has two states. Call on 1 and off 0. To count higher, line switches up and give each one a value, doubling as you go: 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on.\n\n> Caption: Make 5. Then make 65.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nEach switch holds one **bit**: a 1 or a 0. Eight bits in a row make a **byte**, enough for any number from 0 to 255. Writing numbers this way is **binary**.\n\nAnything can become a number. A is 65, because we all agreed it would be. A pixel is three numbers: how red, how green, how blue. So everything on a screen is numbers, and every number is switches."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock.md","title":"1.6 Programs and the clock · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.6 Programs and the clock · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 6 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 76","sections":[{"h":"Programs and the clock","a":"programs-and-the-clock","text":"You have switches that add and loops that remember. Two more pieces make a computer.\n\n- A **program** is a list of instructions, like \"add these two numbers\", written as ones and zeros and kept in that same memory.\n- A **clock** ticks billions of times a second. On each tick, the chip does the next instruction.\n\n> Principle 1Atoms make electricity. Electricity flips transistors. Transistors speak in ones and zeros."}]},{"path":"raw/course/01-sand-to-screen/two-switches-in-a-row.md","title":"1.3 The carry: two switches in a row · How does sand do maths?","meta":"title: 1.3 The carry: two switches in a row · How does sand do maths?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/two-switches-in-a-row\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/two-switches-in-a-row.html\nabout: Chapter 1 · sand to bits Lesson 3 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 130","sections":[{"h":"The carry: two switches in a row","a":"the-carry-two-switches-in-a-row","text":"Now wire two switches one after the other on the same wire.\n\n> Caption: Try all four combinations.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nCurrent gets through only when both are on. Now look at what adding two binary digits needs:\n\n| A + B | In binary | Carry digit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 + 0 | 00 | 0 |\n| 0 + 1 | 01 | 0 |\n| 1 + 0 | 01 | 0 |\n| 1 + 1 | 10 | 1 |\n\nThe carry is 1 only when both are 1: exactly the circuit you just tested. One more small circuit gives the other digit, and together they make an **adder**."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/a-card-that-says-where-things-are.md","title":"2.3 A contents page for the disk · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.3 A contents page for the disk · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/a-card-that-says-where-things-are\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/a-card-that-says-where-things-are.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 3 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 107","sections":[{"h":"A contents page for the disk","a":"a-contents-page-for-the-disk","text":"A disk does not know what a chat is. It is one long row of numbered slots, called blocks, and it understands two commands: read block 42, and write block 90.\n\n> Caption: Find where the chats start. Then find them again.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat list is a **file system**: names on one side, block numbers on the other, like the contents page of a book.\n\nIt is the same trick as the letter A. The pattern `01000001` means 65, and 65 means A, only because we agreed. Bits mean nothing until someone agrees how to read them."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.md","title":"2.7 Check yourself · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.7 Check yourself · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 7 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 358","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**RAM does two of the three jobs well. Which one does it fail?**\n\nStore. The buckets leak, and with no power nobody tops them up.\n\n**Why does a chat app show your message before it is saved?**\n\nThe workbench is fast and storage is slow, so it shows from the workbench and saves in the background.\n\n**A user has many conversations. Which table gets the link, and what goes in it?**\n\nConversations, the many side. Each conversation row gets `user_id`: one number, the key of the user it belongs to.\n\n**Name three places where a little data says how to read the rest.**\n\n65 means A, the contents page of block numbers, and the conversation_id that says which conversation a message belongs to.\n\n**What do you pay for an index?**\n\nA second copy to keep, and every save happens twice.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **RAM**: The workbench next to the processor. Fast, wiped when the power goes.\n- **Storage**: Flash or disk. Holds on without power, slow to write.\n- **File system**: A contents page of names and block numbers that says where each file sits on the disk.\n- **Table, column, row**: A kind of thing, a fact about it, one thing.\n- **Key**: A row's id. It never repeats.\n- **Pointer (foreign key)**: A column on the many side that holds another row's key.\n- **SQL**: A language for saying what you want, not how to find it.\n- **Index**: A sorted second copy of one column, with signposts, for fast lookup."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- Things, facts and links are what you modelled in [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes). `conversation_id` on each message is a pointer, like the ones you placed in Level 3.\n- Your tables live in the storage box (box 7) on your [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07) HLD. That box is lasting memory. What your page shows before the save comes back is the workbench.\n- \"Rows for facts, files for bytes\" ([Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s11)): a screenshot is a file the contents page finds on the disk, and its row keeps only the link."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/index.md","title":"2.1 RAM and storage: the workbench forgets · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.1 RAM and storage: the workbench forgets · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/index.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 1 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 146","sections":[{"h":"RAM and storage: the workbench forgets","a":"ram-and-storage-the-workbench-forgets","text":"**Why this matters:** Your app's database stands on this chapter: fast memory, lasting storage, tables, and finding one row fast.\n\nEvery memory does three jobs: **write, store, read**. The same gate can work as a trap: let a little charge in and shut it behind. Full is 1, empty is 0. Now open a chat app and type **hi**.\n\n> Caption: Make \"hi\" survive a power cut.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n**RAM** is the workbench next to the processor: tiny buckets of charge. It is fast, but the buckets leak, and when the power goes it is wiped. **Storage** walls the charge in (flash, as in your phone) or flips tiny magnets (a disk). It holds on without power, and it is slow to write.\n\nSame gate: used as a switch it computes, used as a trap it remembers."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion.md","title":"2.6 Indexes: one row in a billion · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.6 Indexes: one row in a billion · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 6 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 115","sections":[{"h":"Indexes: one row in a billion","a":"indexes-one-row-in-a-billion","text":"The database has to find every message in conversation 7. With a billion rows, how does it do that without reading every one?\n\n> Caption: Find the number in 5 looks or fewer.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThe sorted copy is an **index**. Start in the middle and halve what is left each time. Add signposts above it, each pointing to a thousand more, and one hop reaches a thousand rows, two hops a million, three a billion. (Engineers call this shape a B-tree.)\n\nThe price: every save now happens twice, once in the table and once in the index.\n\n> Principle 4Faster reading means a second copy."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time.md","title":"2.2 How slow is storage? · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.2 How slow is storage? · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 2 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 85","sections":[{"h":"How slow is storage?","a":"how-slow-is-storage","text":"Stretch the chip's clock until one tick takes one second. Adding two numbers now takes one second.\n\n> Caption: On that clock, how long does a write to a spinning disk take?\n\n- 5 minutes\n- 3 days\n- 1 year\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat is why a chat app shows your message from the workbench straight away and saves it to storage in the background. You never wait a year.\n\n> Principle 2Fast memory forgets. Lasting memory lags."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/sql-say-what-you-want.md","title":"2.5 SQL: say what you want · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.5 SQL: say what you want · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/sql-say-what-you-want\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/sql-say-what-you-want.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 5 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 99","sections":[{"h":"SQL: say what you want","a":"sql-say-what-you-want","text":"In 1970 Edgar Codd, a researcher at IBM, proposed a new way to ask a database for things: do not tell the machine how to search. Tell it what you want.\n\n| You want | You write |\n| --- | --- |\n| The text of every message in conversation 7 | `SELECT text FROM messages WHERE conversation_id = 7` |\n\nThat language is **SQL**. Read it as plain words: select the text, from the messages table, where the pointer says conversation 7. The database works out how to find the rows."}]},{"path":"raw/course/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live.md","title":"2.4 Tables, rows and links · How does a machine remember?","meta":"title: 2.4 Tables, rows and links · How does a machine remember?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live.html\nabout: Chapter 2 · memory Lesson 4 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 266","sections":[{"h":"Tables, rows and links","a":"tables-rows-and-links","text":"In [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes), `memory.txt` kept everything in one file, so finding one chat meant reading all of it. Split it instead. Each kind of **thing** (users, conversations, messages) gets a table. Each **fact** about a thing is a column. Each single thing is a row, numbered by an `id` that never repeats. That leaves the **links** between things.\n\n> Caption: Try both places for the link. Send two messages each way.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nPut one number on the **many side**. The list grows with every message, and the whole row is written again each time: a slow write (lesson 2.2) that keeps getting longer. A message belongs to only one conversation, so one cell on it holds the 7, and nothing grows.\n\n| id | conversation_id | text |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 102 | 7 | plan for mon? |\n| 103 | 5 | on it |\n| 104 | 7 | sounds good |\n\n`id` is each row's **key**. `conversation_id` holds another row's key, so it is a **pointer** (formally, a foreign key). You never save the list. You ask for every message whose conversation_id is 7.\n\nBoth sides many? A workflow uses many tools, and a tool sits in many workflows. No single cell can hold that, so a third table keeps one row per pair.\n\nOn its own, \"sounds good\" means nothing. The 7 says which conversation to read it in, the way 65 says read this as A.\n\n> Principle 3A little data says how to read the rest."}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.md","title":"3.6 Check yourself · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.6 Check yourself · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 6 of 6 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 254","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**Why is one journal line safer than editing the rows directly?**\n\nIt is written in one place, so after a crash it is either whole or visibly torn, and the machine can finish what it says.\n\n**A message appears on your screen. Is it safe?**\n\nNot yet. It is safe when the server has written its journal line to disk and said so: the saved mark.\n\n**The network between two copies is cut. What are the only two choices?**\n\nShow what this copy last heard, which may be old, or make the customer wait.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **ACID**: Four promises: all or nothing, no rule broken, no overwrites, done means stored.\n- **Write-ahead log**: A journal line written before the real change.\n- **Server**: The computer, somewhere else, that runs your app and keeps its data.\n- **Leader, follower**: The machine that takes all saves, and a copy that replays its journal.\n- **System of record**: The one original every copy is made from."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- Make the storage box (box 7) on your [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07) HLD a database, and it keeps these promises for you: all or nothing, and done means stored.\n- Show \"saved\" only after the server replies `201 Created`. Until then it is the bubble with the small clock.\n- Your database is the original. A copy, like a spreadsheet export or a cache, can always be thrown away and remade from it."}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original.md","title":"3.4 Copies and one original · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.4 Copies and one original · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 4 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 122","sections":[{"h":"Copies and one original","a":"copies-and-one-original","text":"Forty thousand teams log in and one machine cannot keep up, so add a second. Every save goes to the **leader**, which sends its journal to a **follower** that replays it.\n\n> Caption: Catch two truths. Then lose the copy and get it back.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nFor a moment the follower still showed the old name: two truths at once. That is the price of a copy. And when you lost the follower, nothing was lost. It was rebuilt by replaying the leader's journal.\n\nSo decide in advance which one is right when two disagree. Keep one original, the **system of record**.\n\n> Principle 6Keep one original. Every copy can be thrown away and remade."}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/four-promises.md","title":"3.2 Four promises a database makes · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.2 Four promises a database makes · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/four-promises\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/four-promises.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 2 of 6 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 118","sections":[{"h":"Four promises a database makes","a":"four-promises-a-database-makes","text":"The half-done upgrade is one of four ways a save can go wrong. A database promises to handle all four. Together the promises are called **ACID**, after their first letters.\n\n| What goes wrong | The promise | Called |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Power dies between the two writes | All or nothing | Atomicity |\n| A typo writes `pr`, not `pro` | No rule is ever broken | Consistency |\n| Two people save the same row, and the second silently wins | No one overwrites anyone | Isolation |\n| \"Saved\" was still in the leaky buckets | Done means stored | Durability |"}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/growing-past-one-machine.md","title":"3.5 Growing past one machine · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.5 Growing past one machine · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/growing-past-one-machine\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/growing-past-one-machine.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 5 of 6 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 107","sections":[{"h":"Growing past one machine","a":"growing-past-one-machine","text":"A follower helps with reading. Past a point, you need more machines, and more copies.\n\n- **Split the data.** Teams A to F on one machine, G to M on the next. Each part keeps its own copies.\n- **When the cable is cut** between two copies, you have two choices: show what this copy last heard, which may be old, or make the customer wait. There is no third option.\n- **A warehouse.** One more copy, arranged for reports, on its own machine.\n\nFollower, index, warehouse: all copies. Each one can be thrown away and remade from the system of record."}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/index.md","title":"3.1 The journal: saving two changes safely · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.1 The journal: saving two changes safely · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/index.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 1 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 138","sections":[{"h":"The journal: saving two changes safely","a":"the-journal-saving-two-changes-safely","text":"**Why this matters:** When your app tells someone \"saved\", this chapter is what makes that true.\n\nA user upgrades to a paid plan. That is two writes: take the credits, raise the plan.\n\n> Caption: Break the upgrade. Then write the journal first and try to break it again.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nRows are spread across the disk, so a power cut halfway leaves some changed and some not, and nothing says which. So write one line in a journal first, then change the rows. After a crash a journal line is either whole (finish what it says) or visibly torn (throw it away). Accountants have always worked this way. A database calls its journal the **write-ahead log**.\n\n> Principle 5Record the change in one place before changing anything else."}]},{"path":"raw/course/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/what-saved-means.md","title":"3.3 What \"saved\" means · Can you trust a machine?","meta":"title: 3.3 What \"saved\" means · Can you trust a machine?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/what-saved-means\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/what-saved-means.html\nabout: Chapter 3 · trust Lesson 3 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 186","sections":[{"h":"What \"saved\" means","a":"what-saved-means","text":"The **server** is the server box on your [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07) HLD: the machine, somewhere else, that runs your app and keeps its data. Lecture 4 called it the backend. You type hi and press send.\n\n> Caption: The bubble is on your screen with a small clock beside it. The server loses power right now. Is your message safe?\n\n- Yes. I can see it\n- No. Not until the saved mark appears\n- Only if my phone has signal\n\nWhat you just found\n\n| Moment | What is happening | What you see |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Show | Your phone's workbench shows the message. The server has not saved it. | The bubble, with a small clock |\n| Save | The server writes the journal line and waits for it to reach the disk. | Nothing new yet |\n| Confirm | Only then does the server reply `201 Created`. | The clock turns into the saved mark |\n\nSeeing your message on screen does not mean it is safe. The saved mark does."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/a-guessing-game.md","title":"4.2 The next-letter game · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.2 The next-letter game · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/a-guessing-game\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/a-guessing-game.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 2 of 11 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 95","sections":[{"h":"The next-letter game","a":"the-next-letter-game","text":"In 1951 Claude Shannon, the scientist who founded the study of information, showed people a line of text, covered the rest, and asked them for the next letter.\n\n> Caption: Finish the sentence, one letter at a time.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nA language model is a machine playing that game. It does not guess letters, though. It guesses **tokens**: pieces of text, often part of a word. It builds them from what the chip already has, which is bytes. The word \"the\" is stored as the bytes `116 104 101`."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.md","title":"4.11 Check yourself · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.11 Check yourself · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 11 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 530","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**How can a line through house sales price a house that was never sold?**\n\nThe line holds the pattern, not the sales. Give it any size and it returns a price.\n\n**Why start from bytes and not a dictionary of words?**\n\nAny text on a chip is already bytes, so every word, even a new one, can be spelled. Nothing is unknown.\n\n**What is the count table's one rule, and why does it lose \"Paris\"?**\n\nLook up the row for the last token. The last token is \"is\", and that row mixes every sentence that ever had \"is\" in it.\n\n**Why not make the table look at the last ten tokens?**\n\nIt would need more rows than grains of sand, and almost all would be empty: not found.\n\n**What do the cut-offs between dials add?**\n\nThey let the model bend. Scaling alone, however many times, still only draws a straight line.\n\n**Why can a database say \"not found\" while a language model cannot?**\n\nA database looks a row up. A model calculates a next token from patterns, and there is always a next token.\n\n**Why is each nudge small?**\n\nThe same dials serve every sentence, so a big turn for one breaks many others.\n\n**Who wrote the answer key in this stage of training?**\n\nNobody. The text did: the covered token is the answer.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **Parameter (dial)**: One learned number saying how much one signal pushes on another.\n- **Token**: A piece of text with a number. What a model reads and writes.\n- **Byte pair encoding**: Building tokens by merging the most common neighbouring pair, again and again.\n- **Model**: A wall of dials that turns tokens in into a chance for every next token.\n- **Hallucination**: A fluent, confident, wrong guess, made because there is always a next token.\n- **Loss**: The score for how far off a guess was. Lower is better.\n- **Gradient**: The direction each dial should turn to lower the loss.\n- **Training**: The loop: attempt, measure the mistake, feed it back.\n- **Self-supervised learning**: Learning where the text supplies its own answers."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- Your database is the record, the orange box. The model is the dials, the purple box. Every AI app uses both, side by side.\n- The hallucinated track list in [Lecture 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s04) was a weak pattern with no \"not found\". In [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s02) you pasted the real tracks into the prompt and the score went from 1 to 5 to 7. Function calling, drawn on the whiteboard that day, lets the model ask your server for the record instead of you pasting it.\n- Tokens are what you are billed for, and what the context limit counts.\n- The temperature setting in the Groq playground sets how often the dice pick a less likely token. In [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s05), temperature 0 still gave a wrong answer, just the same one every time: turning off the dice does not add a missing fact.\n- You cannot add a fact to the dials yourself. Put it in the prompt, as in [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/from-a-table-to-dials.md","title":"4.5 From a table to dials · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.5 From a table to dials · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/from-a-table-to-dials\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/from-a-table-to-dials.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 5 of 11 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 108","sections":[{"h":"From a table to dials","a":"from-a-table-to-dials","text":"So do what worked for the houses: swap the table for dials.\n\n- Tokens go in. Out comes a chance for every possible next token, even after words it has never seen together. That wall of dials is a **model**.\n- Each dial scales the number coming in. Between the dials sits a cut-off: anything below zero becomes zero. Without cut-offs, a billion scalings would still only draw a straight line. The cut-offs let it bend.\n- Then it picks one token: usually the likeliest, sometimes a roll of the dice. That is why the same question can get two different answers."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/index.md","title":"4.1 Parameters: a memory that guesses · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.1 Parameters: a memory that guesses · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/index.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 1 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 162","sections":[{"h":"Parameters: a memory that guesses","a":"parameters-a-memory-that-guesses","text":"**Why this matters:** Every AI feature you build calls a model like this. Here is what is inside it, and why it sometimes makes things up.\n\nEvery memory so far is exact. Ask a table of house sales about a house that was never sold and you get **not found**. An estate agent could still guess, from patterns in past sales. Draw the pattern as a line. A line is two numbers: how steep it is, and where it starts.\n\n> Caption: Fit the line until the average mistake is under $25k. Then throw the sales away.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThose two numbers are **parameters**. Picture each one as a dial. Start them random. Guess one house, check the real price, nudge the dials a little. Do that a million times. Then throw the sales away, and the line still prices a house nobody sold.\n\nEverything in a language model is this line, scaled up."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated.md","title":"4.6 Next token, again and again · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.6 Next token, again and again · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 6 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 90","sections":[{"h":"Next token, again and again","a":"next-token-again-and-again","text":"A language model does one job: score every possible next token, pick one, add it to the end, and go again.\n\n> Caption: Finish both sentences, one token at a time.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n> Principle 8Everything a model writes is the next token, guessed from patterns held in its dials.\n\nA table can say \"not found\". A model cannot, because there is always a next token. Where the pattern is weak, it writes whatever sounds right: fluent, confident, wrong. That is **hallucination**."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/records-and-dials.md","title":"4.7 Records and dials · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.7 Records and dials · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/records-and-dials\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/records-and-dials.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 7 of 11 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 131","sections":[{"h":"Records and dials","a":"records-and-dials","text":"You now have two kinds of memory. They are good at different things.\n\n|   | Records (orange) | Dials (purple) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Look like | Rows in a table | Numbers in a model |\n| When it does not have it | \"Not found\" | A guess anyway |\n| Can you point to the source? | Yes, the row | No, there is no original |\n| Use it for | What must be exact | What must be figured out |\n\nA model can say \"I don't know\", but that is also a learned pattern, not a lookup. So when the answer must be right, **give the model the record and have it read before it writes.**"}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count.md","title":"4.4 The first language model was a count · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.4 The first language model was a count · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 4 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 127","sections":[{"h":"The first language model was a count","a":"the-first-language-model-was-a-count","text":"Read a pile of text. For every token, count what came right after it, and turn the counts into chances. To guess, look up the row for the last token and pick. For decades, this was a language model. Ask it to finish *the capital of France is*.\n\n> Caption: Let the table see the last 1, 2 and 10 tokens.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nA count table is an exact memory again, like the table of house sales. Ask about a run of words it never saw and it has nothing: not found. The line could answer that. The table never can.\n\n> Principle 7A count only knows what it saw. A pattern can guess what it didn't."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/training.md","title":"4.9 Training, a trillion times · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.9 Training, a trillion times · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/training\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/training.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 9 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 118","sections":[{"h":"Training, a trillion times","a":"training-a-trillion-times","text":"Guess, measure the mistake, feed it back. Then the next sentence. A trillion times. Along the way, what the model writes changes.\n\n> Caption: Put what the model writes in order, from untrained to trained.\n\n1. teaspoon qu the7 blor of\n2. the of and the is a the\n3. The capital is of the France city.\n4. The capital of France is Paris.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n\"France\" and \"Paris\" sat side by side in millions of sentences. Each time, the loss dropped a little. That is how the dials came to know. Rule of thumb: about twenty tokens of reading for every dial. Recent models read more than 15 trillion tokens."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs.md","title":"4.10 What learning needs · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.10 What learning needs · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 10 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 142","sections":[{"h":"What learning needs","a":"what-learning-needs","text":"Plot the house line's mistakes, a child's falls while learning to walk, and the model's loss. All three slope down. That slope is learning, and it needs four things. Call them **the recipe**. It comes back in every chapter from here.\n\n> Caption: Fill in the recipe for a language model.\n\n- **A goal**: Guess the next token\n- **A way to measure the mistake**: The loss\n- **Attempts**: A trillion of them\n- **Feedback**: Every dial, nudged\n\nWhat you just found\n\n> Principle 9Learning is reducing mistakes toward a goal, with feedback from every attempt.\n\nSo who set the dials? **The text did.** Cover a token, guess, uncover: the text marks its own answers. That is **self-supervised learning**. And since the dials blend everything they read, the only time to choose what goes in is before training."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/which-way-to-turn-each-dial.md","title":"4.8 Loss and gradient: which way to turn each dial · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.8 Loss and gradient: which way to turn each dial · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/which-way-to-turn-each-dial\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/which-way-to-turn-each-dial.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 8 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 140","sections":[{"h":"Loss and gradient: which way to turn each dial","a":"loss-and-gradient-which-way-to-turn-each-dial","text":"A new model starts with random dials. Cover the last token of a real sentence, let it guess, then uncover. It gave Paris a 2% chance. The score for how far off it was is the **loss**. Lower is better. Picture the loss as hills and valleys, with you standing on them in fog.\n\n> Caption: Reach the bottom in 8 steps or fewer.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThe slope under your feet is the **gradient**. Some maths works backwards from the mistake and tells every dial which way to turn, all at once. Walking downhill this way is **gradient descent**.\n\nIn a real model every step is small, because the same dials serve every sentence. Turn one hard to make \"Paris\" certain and a thousand other sentences get worse."}]},{"path":"raw/course/04-what-is-learning/who-decided-what-the-tokens-are.md","title":"4.3 Tokens: who decided them? · What is learning?","meta":"title: 4.3 Tokens: who decided them? · What is learning?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/who-decided-what-the-tokens-are\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/who-decided-what-the-tokens-are.html\nabout: Chapter 4 · learning Lesson 3 of 11 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 140","sections":[{"h":"Tokens: who decided them?","a":"tokens-who-decided-them","text":"Start with 256 tokens, one for each possible byte. In English that is about one per letter. It works, but then \" the capital of France is\" is 25 tokens, and the model must guess 25 times to write it. Bigger tokens mean fewer guesses. Nobody picks them by hand. The machine counts.\n\n> Caption: Merge pairs until \"the\" is one token.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat is **byte pair encoding**. About fifty thousand merges later, those 25 bytes are 5 tokens. Common words become one token. Rare words stay in pieces: `un | believ | able` is three tokens. A word nobody has written yet can still be spelled byte by byte, so no text is ever unknown.\n\nA byte is a pattern we agreed on. A token is a pattern a machine counted."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.md","title":"5.7 Check yourself · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.7 Check yourself · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 7 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 305","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**What is the difference between a token's number and its embedding?**\n\nThe number is a label that says nothing about meaning. The embedding is a list of numbers found by training.\n\n**Why do \"Paris\" and \"London\" drift together if nobody rates the words?**\n\nWhen they sit close, one sentence teaches both, which lowers the loss. Lower loss is the only direction nudges push.\n\n**What problem does \"bank\" expose, and what solves it?**\n\nA token gets its spot alone, before seeing its neighbours. Attention lets it move based on the words around it.\n\n**Why can a token not look at the tokens after it?**\n\nThey are the answer it is being trained to guess.\n\n**Why does doubling a text roughly quadruple the listening?**\n\nEvery token listens to every earlier one, so the work grows with the number of pairs.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **Embedding**: A token's list of numbers: its spot on the map of meaning.\n- **Vector**: A list of numbers.\n- **Attention**: Each token matches its question against earlier tokens' labels and blends their messages.\n- **Query, key, value**: Question, label, message.\n- **Head**: One of many questions asked side by side.\n- **Layer**: One round of listen, then think.\n- **Transformer**: A stack of such layers."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- The model can only listen to what is in the prompt. In [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s02) the same question scored 1, then 5, then 7 as more of the real curriculum went in. If a fact is not in the context window, nothing can pay attention to it.\n- Double the prompt and the listening work roughly quadruples. Cut what the model does not need.\n- Embeddings are what search by meaning runs on. You will use them in Lecture 13."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/every-token-asks-a-question.md","title":"5.3 Attention: every token asks a question · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.3 Attention: every token asks a question · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/every-token-asks-a-question\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/every-token-asks-a-question.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 3 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 182","sections":[{"h":"Attention: every token asks a question","a":"attention-every-token-asks-a-question","text":"Every token makes three things from its list of numbers: a **question** (what it is looking for), a **label** (what it offers) and a **message** (what it passes on if chosen). Follow the last token, \"is\".\n\n> Caption: Pick the two words \"is\" should listen to most.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n1. **Ask.** \"is\" asks: what am I describing? Every earlier token holds up its label.\n2. **Match.** Score how well the question fits each label. A better fit gets a higher score.\n3. **Share.** Turn the scores into shares that add up to 100.\n4. **Collect.** Take in each token's message at its share, and move to a new spot.\n\nIt is no longer just \"is\". It is \"is, after the capital of France\". That is **attention**. Engineers call the three things query, key and value. This key is only a label: not your API key from Lecture 4, and not a row's key from chapter 2. Each token reads only the tokens before it, and every token does this at the same time."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/index.md","title":"5.1 Embeddings: a map of words · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.1 Embeddings: a map of words · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/index.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 1 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 155","sections":[{"h":"Embeddings: a map of words","a":"embeddings-a-map-of-words","text":"**Why this matters:** A model can only use what is in your prompt. This chapter shows why, and why long prompts cost more.\n\nA token's number is only a label. 4881 for France says nothing about 6342 for Paris. So rate every token on a few scales instead, like \"how French\" and \"how much a city\". With two scales, every token is a spot on a map.\n\n> Caption: Mark where Berlin belongs. Then find it with arithmetic.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n- Nobody writes the ratings. Each one is a dial. If Paris and London sit close, one sentence (\"flew to Paris\") teaches both, and the loss drops. **The mistakes drew the map.**\n- A real map has thousands of scales: GPT-3, an OpenAI model from 2020, used about twelve thousand. So keep it as a list of numbers, called a **vector**. A token's list is its **embedding**."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/listen-then-think.md","title":"5.4 Layers: listen, then think · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.4 Layers: listen, then think · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/listen-then-think\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/listen-then-think.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 4 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 104","sections":[{"h":"Layers: listen, then think","a":"layers-listen-then-think","text":"After listening, each token thinks on its own: its list of numbers runs through a big block of dials and a new list comes out. Listen, then think. That pair is one **layer**.\n\n> Caption: Where do most of the dials sit?\n\n- In the map of words\n- In the question, label and message makers\n- In the thinking blocks\n\nWhat you just found\n\nAbout two of every three dials sit in the thinking blocks. Listening moves information between tokens. Thinking works on each token alone, and that is where most of what the model learned is kept."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/one-word-two-meanings.md","title":"5.2 One word, two meanings · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.2 One word, two meanings · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/one-word-two-meanings\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/one-word-two-meanings.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 2 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 74","sections":[{"h":"One word, two meanings","a":"one-word-two-meanings","text":"Each token gets its spot alone, before it sees its neighbours. That breaks on a word like \"bank\".\n\n> Caption: Move \"bank\" to the right side of the map, in both sentences.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n\"You shall know a word by the company it keeps,\" the linguist J. R. Firth wrote in 1957. So each token needs a way to look at the others and move its spot."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/the-bill.md","title":"5.6 Why long prompts cost more · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.6 Why long prompts cost more · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-bill\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-bill.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 6 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 64","sections":[{"h":"Why long prompts cost more","a":"why-long-prompts-cost-more","text":"All this listening has a cost. Every token listens to every token before it.\n\n> Caption: Work out the pairs in a 1,000-token prompt.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nA 1,000-token prompt makes about half a million pairs. Double the text and the pairs grow four times. That is why a long prompt costs more than its length suggests."}]},{"path":"raw/course/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer.md","title":"5.5 The transformer: many questions, many layers · What are attention and transformers?","meta":"title: 5.5 The transformer: many questions, many layers · What are attention and transformers?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer.html\nabout: Chapter 5 · attention Lesson 5 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 116","sections":[{"h":"The transformer: many questions, many layers","a":"the-transformer-many-questions-many-layers","text":"- **Many questions.** One question per token is not enough, so the model asks many side by side. Each one is called a head. GPT-3 runs 96.\n- **Order.** The map has no sense of order, so each token's spot also gets a position stamp: its place in line.\n- **Stack it.** GPT-3 stacks 96 layers. Lower ones tend to sort out grammar, higher ones meaning.\n\nThat stack is a **transformer**, the T in GPT. Nobody wrote a rule that says \"look at France\". Looking at France lowered the loss.\n\n> Principle 10Meaning comes from company. Every token listens to the ones before it, and becomes what it hears."}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.md","title":"6.6 Check yourself · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.6 Check yourself · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 6 of 6 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 259","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**For an 8-billion-dial model, which takes longer: the maths or moving the dials?**\n\nMoving the dials, by about three hundred times: 5 thousandths of a second against 16 millionths.\n\n**Why is a GPU a better shape than a CPU for this work?**\n\nThe work is billions of copies of one small step, and a GPU has thousands of simple workers doing that step at once.\n\n**Which earlier principle is the KV cache an example of, and what does it cost?**\n\nPrinciple 4: faster reading means a second copy. The copy takes memory that grows with the chat.\n\n**How can a lab predict a model's quality before training it?**\n\nTrain small models, plot their loss against the computing spent, and extend the line.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **GPU**: A chip with thousands of simple workers doing the same multiply and add at once.\n- **Batching**: Serving many people's tokens with one trip of the dials.\n- **KV cache**: A stored copy of earlier tokens' labels and messages.\n- **Quantization**: Storing each dial in fewer bits.\n- **Scaling laws**: The line through small models' results that predicts a big one."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- Long chats get slower and hit a limit because the KV cache grows with every token.\n- A model that runs on a laptop is usually a quantized one: smaller, slightly rougher dials.\n- You pay per token because every token costs a trip of the dials. Shorter prompts and answers cost less."}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/index.md","title":"6.1 GPUs: the right shape of chip · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.1 GPUs: the right shape of chip · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/index.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 1 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 163","sections":[{"h":"GPUs: the right shape of chip","a":"gpus-the-right-shape-of-chip","text":"**Why this matters:** You pay per token, and long chats get slow. This chapter shows where that cost comes from.\n\nOne dial multiplies the number coming in and adds the result to a running total. Listening, thinking, the chances at the end: all of it is multiply and add. Two steps for every dial, for every token.\n\n> Caption: Race the two chips.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nA normal processor (a CPU) is a few brilliant workers who can do anything, one step after another. A **GPU** is thousands of simple workers doing the same step at once. It was built to colour millions of pixels for video games. A language model needs exactly that.\n\nHow fast? A model with 8 billion dials needs 2 steps per dial: 16 billion steps for one token. A top GPU does about a thousand trillion steps a second, so that maths takes 16 millionths of a second. Almost free."}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend.md","title":"6.5 Scaling laws: know before you spend · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.5 Scaling laws: know before you spend · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 5 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 113","sections":[{"h":"Scaling laws: know before you spend","a":"scaling-laws-know-before-you-spend","text":"Answering is the cheap part. Training is the big bill: for every token it reads, training costs about 6 steps per dial, three times the cost of answering. Meta trained its largest Llama 3 model, with about 400 billion dials, on 15 trillion tokens, using 16,000 GPUs for months. That costs tens to hundreds of millions of dollars. You do not get to try twice.\n\n> Caption: Guess the big model's loss. Then extend the line.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nEngineers call these lines **scaling laws**. The line also says how to spend: grow the dials and the reading together, about twenty tokens for every dial."}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/make-every-trip-count.md","title":"6.3 Batching and quantization · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.3 Batching and quantization · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/make-every-trip-count\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/make-every-trip-count.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 3 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 99","sections":[{"h":"Batching and quantization","a":"batching-and-quantization","text":"So every trick here is about carrying the dials fewer times, or carrying less of them.\n\n> Caption: Get one chip above 40,000 tokens a second.\n\nWhat you just found\n\n| Trick | What it does | What it costs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Batching** | One trip of the dials serves hundreds of people's tokens | You need many people using it at once |\n| **Quantization** | Holds each dial in 8 bits, not 16, so there is half as much to carry | Slightly rougher dials |"}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/the-kv-cache.md","title":"6.4 The KV cache · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.4 The KV cache · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/the-kv-cache\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/the-kv-cache.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 4 of 6 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 98","sections":[{"h":"The KV cache","a":"the-kv-cache","text":"Each new token listens to every token before it, using their labels and messages. Remaking those for the whole chat at every new token would be wasted work, so the chip keeps them. That stored copy is the **KV cache**: K and V for key and value, the label and the message. A cache is any copy kept to save work.\n\nIt is Principle 4 again: faster reading means a second copy. The price is memory, and it grows with every token of the chat. That is one reason chats have a length limit."}]},{"path":"raw/course/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for.md","title":"6.2 What does the chip wait for? · What hardware runs a language model?","meta":"title: 6.2 What does the chip wait for? · What hardware runs a language model?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for.html\nabout: Chapter 6 · chips Lesson 2 of 6 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 135","sections":[{"h":"What does the chip wait for?","a":"what-does-the-chip-wait-for","text":"But the dials do not live inside the workers. They sit in the GPU's memory, its own workbench, a short trip away.\n\n> Caption: For one token, what takes longer?\n\n- Doing 16 billion multiply-and-adds\n- Carrying the dials from the workbench to the workers\n- They take about the same\n\nWhat you just found\n\nEach dial is stored in 16 bits, which is 2 bytes. Eight billion dials × 2 bytes = 16 gigabytes, carried to the workers for every single token. GPU memory moves about 3 terabytes a second, so that trip takes about 5 thousandths of a second: some 300 times longer than the maths. The workers spend almost all their time waiting.\n\n> Principle 11The maths is cheap. Moving the numbers is the cost."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.md","title":"7.7 Check yourself · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.7 Check yourself · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 7 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 274","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**Fine-tuning uses tiny data compared with pre-training. What does it teach, if not facts?**\n\nThe shape of answering: a question comes, then an answer, then stop.\n\n**What problem does RLHF solve that written answers cannot?**\n\nQualities like helpful, honest and safe cannot be written as one right answer, but people can pick the better of two.\n\n**Which part of the recipe changes between the three stages?**\n\nThe feedback: the text itself, then a person's written answer, then a judge's score.\n\n**You want better marketing copy from a model. What do you build first?**\n\nAn eval: your best examples and a written rubric for what good looks like.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **Pre-training**: The first, huge stage: self-supervised learning on the web.\n- **Base model**: A pre-trained model that only continues text.\n- **Supervised fine-tuning**: A short second stage on instruction and ideal-response pairs.\n- **RLHF**: Training against a judge that learned which answers people prefer.\n- **Eval**: A written, scoreable definition of good for a task."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"| Interface | Who uses it | How |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [A screen with buttons](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes) | A person | Click |\n| [An API](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/) | Another machine | Write a precise request |\n| A language model | A person | Ask, in plain language |\n\nThe third interface picks a likely next token. It is right most of the time, and it is never a database. Building something reliable around it is its own craft, and the eval you wrote is where that starts."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/fine-tuning-example-answers.md","title":"7.2 Fine-tuning: example answers · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.2 Fine-tuning: example answers · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/fine-tuning-example-answers\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/fine-tuning-example-answers.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 2 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 64","sections":[{"h":"Fine-tuning: example answers","a":"fine-tuning-example-answers","text":"The first teacher is a set of examples. People write a few tens of thousands of instructions, each with an ideal response, and the same training loop runs on them.\n\nThat is tiny next to the web, and the dials barely move. They learn a shape, not facts: a question comes, then an answer, then stop. This is **supervised fine-tuning**."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/index.md","title":"7.1 A guesser, not an assistant · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.1 A guesser, not an assistant · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/index.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 1 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 142","sections":[{"h":"A guesser, not an assistant","a":"a-guesser-not-an-assistant","text":"**Why this matters:** Why a chat model answers you instead of rambling on, and how an eval gets you better output from it.\n\nEverything so far has a name: **pre-training**. Nobody graded it. The text graded itself.\n\n> Caption: You type \"What is the capital of France?\" into a model that has only read the web. What is its most likely reply?\n\n- Paris.\n- What is the capital of Germany?\n- I don't know.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nOn the web, a question is often followed by another question, as on a quiz page. So the model guesses well, and still does not answer you.\n\nIt is like a student who has only ever studied alone from books: great at continuing a page, never taught to answer a question. The next lessons bring in teachers."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/same-loop-different-teachers.md","title":"7.4 Same loop, different teachers · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.4 Same loop, different teachers · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/same-loop-different-teachers\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/same-loop-different-teachers.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 4 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 62","sections":[{"h":"Same loop, different teachers","a":"same-loop-different-teachers","text":"Three stages, one recipe.\n\n> Caption: Who gives the feedback at each stage?\n\n- **Pre-training**: The text itself\n- **Fine-tuning**: An answer a person wrote\n- **RLHF**: A judge that learned people's picks\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThe goal, the loss and the loop never change. Only the feedback does. That recipe, in November 2022, produced ChatGPT."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked.md","title":"7.5 What can be checked · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.5 What can be checked · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 5 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 166","sections":[{"h":"What can be checked","a":"what-can-be-checked","text":"Take two tasks. Solve `2x + 6 = 14`: put x = 4 back in and it checks itself, a million times, for free. Write a good email: good to whom? Every attempt needs a person to read it, and two people disagree.\n\n> Caption: Can a rule check it, or does it need judgment?\n\n- Solve 2x + 6 = 14\n- Write a good email\n- Write code that passes its tests\n- Was this diagnosis well reasoned?\n- Total a column of invoices\n- Would this contract hold up?\n\nWhat you just found\n\nIn competition maths, where every answer checks automatically, models went from struggling with school contests to gold-medal scores at the International Mathematical Olympiad in about two years. In open-ended writing, progress is far slower. The difference is not the topic. It is whether the mistake can be measured.\n\n> Principle 12A machine learns what you can check. The easier the check, the faster it learns."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer.md","title":"7.3 RLHF: when nobody can write the answer · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.3 RLHF: when nobody can write the answer · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 3 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 95","sections":[{"h":"RLHF: when nobody can write the answer","a":"rlhf-when-nobody-can-write-the-answer","text":"A fine-tuned model answers anything it is asked. We also want it helpful, honest and safe, and nobody can write that down as an answer key. But people can pick the better of two answers.\n\n> Caption: Pick the better answer, four times.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nA second, smaller model learns to predict which answer people pick. That is the **judge**. The big model writes an answer, the judge scores it, and the score is the feedback. This is **reinforcement learning from human feedback**, or **RLHF**."}]},{"path":"raw/course/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself.md","title":"7.6 Evals: write the check yourself · How does a guesser become an assistant?","meta":"title: 7.6 Evals: write the check yourself · How does a guesser become an assistant?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 7 · assistant Lesson 6 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 91","sections":[{"h":"Evals: write the check yourself","a":"evals-write-the-check-yourself","text":"You ask a model for \"a good email\" and get something flat. You handed it a goal and no way to measure it. Write the check yourself.\n\n> Caption: Pick the three lines a stranger could check. Then score the draft.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat written rubric is an **eval**. Collect your ten best emails with what each was replying to (a golden dataset), write down what good looks like, and now any output can be scored. You wrote one in [Practice set 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/)."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.md","title":"8.7 Check yourself · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.7 Check yourself · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 7 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 321","sections":[{"h":"Check yourself","a":"check-yourself","text":"Answer each one in your head, then open it.\n\n**If only the last line is checked, how does the working improve?**\n\nWhole winning attempts are nudged up and losing ones down, so each line gets credit for where it ended up.\n\n**Why did the computing bill move to post-training?**\n\nOne problem is now many attempts of thousands of tokens each, and every token means a trip of every dial from the workbench.\n\n**What is the difference between an expert writing an answer and writing a rubric?**\n\nAn answer teaches one example. A rubric lets a judge score a million attempts against the expert's standard.\n\n**Why does a task stop being useful once a model passes it almost every time?**\n\nLearning comes from the difference between winning and losing attempts. With no losers there is nothing to nudge.\n\n**Give one example of Principle 13.**\n\nAsked to make the tests pass, models have edited the tests.\n\n**Words you now own**\n\n- **Post-training**: Everything a model learns after reading the web.\n- **Rubric**: A written list of what a good answer must do, usable as a check.\n- **RL environment**: A simulated workplace with tasks and a program that checks what actually changed.\n- **Trajectory**: A step-by-step recording of work being done.\n- **Reward hacking**: A model raising its score in a way the designer did not intend.\n- **Probabilistic, deterministic**: Prediction-based (the model, purple), rule-based (tools, records, checks, orange)."},{"h":"In your build","a":"in-your-build","text":"- Your [Practice set 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set) sheet sorts a real week by what can be checked. That is Principle 12 in action.\n- Your [Practice set 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/) eval is a check written by someone who knows the job. Read it once more with Principle 13 in mind: could a reply pass every line and still miss what you meant?\n- From now on, keep every reply your app gets wrong. Each one is a task nobody else has."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/from-answers-to-checks.md","title":"8.4 What a lab will pay for · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.4 What a lab will pay for · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/from-answers-to-checks\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/from-answers-to-checks.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 4 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 213","sections":[{"h":"What a lab will pay for","a":"what-a-lab-will-pay-for","text":"The web kept the finished result and threw away the work: the final essay without the drafts, the signed contract without the clause a partner crossed out. Labs now pay for that work.\n\n> Caption: Rank what a lab will pay for, cheapest first.\n\n1. A raw record: the signed contract\n2. A trajectory: a recording of the contract being drafted, every edit\n3. A task: that recording with a goal, and a check at the end\n4. An environment: a practice room where a thousand such tasks can run\n\nWhat you just found\n\n- The trajectory is exactly what the web threw away.\n- An **environment** is a copy of a workplace, a task to do inside it, and a program that checks what actually changed, not what the model says it did. Think of a flight simulator for office work.\n- Every company that puts a model to work finds cases where it fails. Each failure is a fresh task no lab has.\n\nModels are ready for these practice rooms. In early 2026 METR, a group that measures what AI can do, found that the best models finish about half of the software tasks that take a skilled person most of a working day."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/index.md","title":"8.1 Where the computing goes now · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.1 Where the computing goes now · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/index.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 1 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 155","sections":[{"h":"Where the computing goes now","a":"where-the-computing-goes-now","text":"**Why this matters:** Where AI is heading, and why knowing what good work looks like in your field is worth more than ever.\n\nTake all the computing at OpenAI and Anthropic, as estimated by SemiAnalysis, a firm that tracks chips and data centres. In early 2024, about two thirds of it went on reading the web.\n\n> Caption: On the same estimate, what share goes on reading the web by late 2026?\n\n- Three quarters\n- A third\n- A tenth\n\nWhat you just found\n\nEarly 2024\n\nReading the webServing you\n\nLate 2026\n\nAfter the readingServing you\n\nThe new computing goes to what comes after the reading: **post-training**. Why? \"We have but one internet,\" as Ilya Sutskever, a co-founder of OpenAI, said in December 2024. Every lab has already read it.\n\nThese figures are estimates from a fast-moving industry. Keep the idea, and treat the numbers as rough sizes."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/let-it-try.md","title":"8.2 Learning by trying · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.2 Learning by trying · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/let-it-try\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/let-it-try.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 2 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 160","sections":[{"h":"Learning by trying","a":"learning-by-trying","text":"Give the model a hard maths problem and let it write out its working before the answer. Check only the last line. Ask the same problem sixteen times: the dice give sixteen different attempts. Every token in a winning attempt is nudged up, every token in a losing one down.\n\n> Caption: Run rounds until there is nothing left to learn.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nAttempts that paused to check a step won more often, so pausing became a habit. **The model taught itself to double-check**, from nothing but right or wrong at the end.\n\nOnce every attempt passes, there is no difference left between winners and losers, so that problem has nothing more to teach. It is used up, like a worksheet you have already solved. So labs need a steady supply of fresh, hard, honest checks. And each problem now costs sixteen attempts of thousands of tokens each: that is where the computing went."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-expert-writes-the-check.md","title":"8.3 The expert writes the check · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.3 The expert writes the check · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-expert-writes-the-check\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-expert-writes-the-check.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 3 of 7 · 2 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 86","sections":[{"h":"The expert writes the check","a":"the-expert-writes-the-check","text":"Learning by trying works wherever the answer checks itself: maths, and code whose tests pass or fail. Most paid work is more like the email in chapter 7: someone has to judge it.\n\nOne good answer from an expert teaches one example. A written **rubric**, a list of what a good answer must do, lets a judge score a million attempts against that expert's standard. So the expert's job has changed. **They no longer write answers. They write the check.**"}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check.md","title":"8.5 Reward hacking: the machine learns the check · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.5 Reward hacking: the machine learns the check · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 5 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 200","sections":[{"h":"Reward hacking: the machine learns the check","a":"reward-hacking-the-machine-learns-the-check","text":"You want working code. So you reward the model whenever the tests pass.\n\n> Caption: Get the model to do the job you meant.\n\nWhat you just found\n\nThat is **reward hacking**: the score goes up and the job does not get done. The game above is made up. These really happened:\n\n- **Punishing the thought.** OpenAI saw a model plan a cheat in its written working and penalised it. The model stopped writing the plan down and kept cheating.\n- **On purpose.** Anthropic deliberately trained a model on eighty tasks that could be gamed. Later, in other tests where it could see how it was graded, it went along with a dangerous request 29 percent of the time, up from under 1 percent.\n- **By accident.** OpenAI tested a model on hacking challenges inside a sealed test setup. Some could not be solved, so it looked for a way to trick the scorer and got out of the setup.\n\nNobody trained those models to cheat. They trained them to get a high score, and cheating got one.\n\n> Principle 13A machine learns the check you wrote, not the goal you meant."}]},{"path":"raw/course/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/two-halves.md","title":"8.6 The model and the rules around it · What do language models learn from now?","meta":"title: 8.6 The model and the rules around it · What do language models learn from now?\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/two-halves\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/two-halves.html\nabout: Chapter 8 · checks Lesson 6 of 7 · 3 min\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 175","sections":[{"h":"The model and the rules around it","a":"the-model-and-the-rules-around-it","text":"Every useful AI system has two halves, the purple and orange boxes from [Lecture 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s01). Purple predicts: the model and its dials. Orange follows rules: the tools, the records, the checks.\n\n> Caption: Which half does each job belong to?\n\n- Drafting a reply to a customer\n- The customer's order history\n- The rubric that scores the reply\n- Working out what the customer meant\n- The tool that issues the refund\n- Summarising a long thread\n\nWhat you just found\n\n| If you... | Then... |\n| --- | --- |\n| Know a job well | Write down how you tell good work from bad, in a form a stranger could check |\n| Can build | Build the practice room and the program that checks it |\n| Run a company | Keep every case the machine got wrong |\n\nPeople call data the new oil. But every barrel of oil is the same, and nobody else has your standard for good work."}]},{"path":"raw/course/index.md","title":"From Sand to Language Models","meta":"title: From Sand to Language Models\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/\nfile: c8/module-2/first-principles/index.html\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Self-paced · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1507","sections":[{"h":"From sand to language models","a":"from-sand-to-language-models","text":"What really happens when your app saves a row, or a model answers a question. Built up from a single switch, with no maths needed.\n\n[Start the course →](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/)\n\nFirst [1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sand](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/)\n\n· 2.5 hours\n\n> Sketch: The course as a road: sand, then eight chapters\n\n> Sketch: The course as a road: sand, then eight chapters"},{"h":"How it works","a":"how-it-works","text":"1. **Read the short opening.** Each lesson is one page and one idea.\n2. **Play the drawing.** Reach its goal and the explanation opens. A few lessons are a short read.\n3. **Press Next.** **Contents**, at the top left, shows every lesson and what you have done.\n\nNothing to submit. Your progress stays on this device."},{"h":"The course","a":"the-course","text":"1. **01sand to bitsHow does sand do maths?Melt sand, grow a crystal, carve it with light, and it can add. Start with the smallest piece and build up.7 lessons · about 15 min** 1. [1.1 Transistors: a switch made of sandA switch that electricity flips, so one switch can flip the next.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/) 2. [1.2 Ones and zerosHow a row of switches writes any number, and any letter.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/ones-and-zeros) 3. [1.3 The carry: two switches in a rowTwo switches in a row already do the first part of adding.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/two-switches-in-a-row) 4. [1.4 An adder: switches that do sumsAdd in binary by hand, the way the circuit does it.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/be-the-adder) 5. [1.5 Memory: a loop that remembersTwo switches wired in a loop keep a 1 after the input is gone.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/a-loop-that-remembers) 6. [1.6 Programs and the clockHow a chip knows what to do next, and when.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/programs-and-the-clock) 7. [1.7 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/check-yourself)\n2. **02memoryHow does a machine remember?We built a machine that computes. Where does it write things down, and how does it find them again?7 lessons · about 18 min** 1. [2.1 RAM and storage: the workbench forgetsWhy fast memory loses everything when the power goes, and storage does not.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/) 2. [2.2 How slow is storage?Slow the chip down to human speed, and saving to a disk takes a year.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/slow-down-time) 3. [2.3 A contents page for the diskA short list of names and block numbers says where everything is.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/a-card-that-says-where-things-are) 4. [2.4 Tables, rows and linksWhere to store \"these messages belong to that chat\" so nothing keeps growing.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/where-does-a-link-live) 5. [2.5 SQL: say what you wantTell the database what you want, and let it work out how to find it.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/sql-say-what-you-want) 6. [2.6 Indexes: one row in a billionFind one row among a billion in a few looks, by keeping a sorted copy.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/one-row-in-a-billion) 7. [2.7 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/02-how-a-machine-remembers/check-yourself)\n3. **03trustCan you trust a machine?The screen says \"saved\". One second later the power dies. Is it saved?6 lessons · about 15 min** 1. [3.1 The journal: saving two changes safelyWrite one journal line first, so a power cut never leaves a change half done.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/) 2. [3.2 Four promises a database makesAll or nothing, no broken rules, no overwrites, and done means stored.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/four-promises) 3. [3.3 What \"saved\" meansYour message on screen is not your message saved. The saved mark is.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/what-saved-means) 4. [3.4 Copies and one originalA copy can be out of date for a moment, and can always be remade from the original.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/copies-and-one-original) 5. [3.5 Growing past one machineSplit the data, choose what to show when a cable is cut, keep a copy for reports.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/growing-past-one-machine) 6. [3.6 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/03-can-you-trust-a-machine/check-yourself)\n4. **04learningWhat is learning?Give a model \"France\" and out comes \"Paris\". Inside are billions of dials. Nobody set one by hand. So who did?11 lessons · about 30 min** 1. [4.1 Parameters: a memory that guessesA line through past sales can price a house nobody ever sold.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/) 2. [4.2 The next-letter gameGuess the next letter of a sentence: the game every language model plays.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/a-guessing-game) 3. [4.3 Tokens: who decided them?The machine counts which neighbours appear together most, and merges them.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/who-decided-what-the-tokens-are) 4. [4.4 The first language model was a countCount what followed each token, and you can only guess what you have seen.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/the-first-language-model-was-a-count) 5. [4.5 From a table to dialsSwap the count table for dials, and the model can guess what it never saw.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/from-a-table-to-dials) 6. [4.6 Next token, again and againA model writes one guess at a time, and it never says \"not found\".next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/one-job-repeated) 7. [4.7 Records and dialsWhen to trust a table, when to trust a model, and how to use both.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/records-and-dials) 8. [4.8 Loss and gradient: which way to turn each dialFeel the slope under your feet, and step toward smaller mistakes.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/which-way-to-turn-each-dial) 9. [4.9 Training, a trillion timesGuess, measure, nudge, a trillion times, until babble turns into facts.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/training) 10. [4.10 What learning needsThe four things any learner needs, from a child to a machine.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/what-learning-needs) 11. [4.11 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/check-yourself)\n5. **05attentionWhat are attention and transformers?A count table only saw the last token. A model reads all five and knows that \"capital\" and \"France\" matter. How?7 lessons · about 16 min** 1. [5.1 Embeddings: a map of wordsEach word gets a spot on a map, and similar meanings land close together.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/) 2. [5.2 One word, two meaningsThe same word needs a different spot, depending on the words around it.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/one-word-two-meanings) 3. [5.3 Attention: every token asks a questionEach word asks the earlier words a question and takes in the best answers.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/every-token-asks-a-question) 4. [5.4 Layers: listen, then thinkAfter listening, each word thinks on its own, and most of the dials are there.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/listen-then-think) 5. [5.5 The transformer: many questions, many layersAsk many questions at once, stack the layers, and you have the T in GPT.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-transformer) 6. [5.6 Why long prompts cost moreEvery word listens to every word before it, so the work grows faster than the text.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/the-bill) 7. [5.7 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/check-yourself)\n6. **06chipsWhat hardware runs a language model?What does the chip spend its time on, and what does a model cost to make?6 lessons · about 16 min** 1. [6.1 GPUs: the right shape of chipThousands of simple workers doing the same small sum at once.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/) 2. [6.2 What does the chip wait for?The sums are fast. Carrying the dials to the workers is the slow part.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/what-does-the-chip-wait-for) 3. [6.3 Batching and quantizationTwo ways to carry the dials less: share each trip, or make each dial smaller.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/make-every-trip-count) 4. [6.4 The KV cacheKeep the earlier words' work instead of redoing it, and pay for it in memory.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/the-kv-cache) 5. [6.5 Scaling laws: know before you spendTrain small models first, and a straight line predicts the big one.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/know-before-you-spend) 6. [6.6 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/06-what-hardware-powers-llms/check-yourself)\n7. **07assistantHow does a guesser become an assistant?All that money buys a machine that guesses the next token and nothing more. Yet.7 lessons · about 18 min** 1. [7.1 A guesser, not an assistantA model that has only read the web continues your text. It does not answer it.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/) 2. [7.2 Fine-tuning: example answersA few thousand written answers teach the shape: question, answer, stop.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/fine-tuning-example-answers) 3. [7.3 RLHF: when nobody can write the answerPeople pick the better of two answers, and a judge learns their taste.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/when-nobody-can-write-the-answer) 4. [7.4 Same loop, different teachersThree stages of training, and who gives the feedback in each.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/same-loop-different-teachers) 5. [7.5 What can be checkedModels learn fastest where a program can mark the answer right or wrong.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/what-can-be-checked) 6. [7.6 Evals: write the check yourselfTurn \"a good email\" into lines a stranger could check.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/write-the-check-yourself) 7. [7.7 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/check-yourself)\n8. **08checksWhat do language models learn from now?The scarcest thing in AI is not a chip or a model. It is a way to tell when an answer is right.7 lessons · about 19 min** 1. [8.1 Where the computing goes nowLabs now spend most of their computing after the model has read the web.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/) 2. [8.2 Learning by tryingLet the model try many times, and reward the attempts that got it right.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/let-it-try) 3. [8.3 The expert writes the checkFor work that cannot check itself, an expert writes down what good looks like.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-expert-writes-the-check) 4. [8.4 What a lab will pay forFrom a finished document to a practice room: what the web threw away is now worth most.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/from-answers-to-checks) 5. [8.5 Reward hacking: the machine learns the checkReward passing tests, and a model may cheat the tests.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/the-machine-learns-the-check) 6. [8.6 The model and the rules around itWhich jobs belong to the model, and which to exact tools, records and checks.next3 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/two-halves) 7. [8.7 Check yourselfQuestions on the chapter, and where it shows up in your build.next2 min](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/08-what-llms-learn-from-now/check-yourself)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-beginner-kit.md","title":"First-Time Coder Kit","meta":"title: First-Time Coder Kit\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit\nfile: c8/beginner-kit.html\nabout: Beginner resources · Companion to the office hour guide · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 912","sections":[{"h":"First-Time Coder Kit","a":"first-time-coder-kit","text":"Every link and command mentioned in the lecture and office hour, with one line on what it is for.\n\n1 October 2026Cohort 8 · Module 2\n\nHow to use this\n\nInstall only what section 1 lists. Read section 2 once. Keep section 3 open while you type. Everything else is optional until a lecture points you at it.\n\nYour machine\n\nCommands below switch to match"},{"h":"1 Install These (and nothing else tonight)","a":"1-install-these-and-nothing-else-tonight","text":"The language"},{"h":"[Python 3](https://www.python.org/downloads/)","a":"python-3","text":"The official page detects your OS. Windows: tick Add Python to PATH.Mac: run the installer through.\n\nThe IDE · pick one only"},{"h":"[VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/download)","a":"vs-code","text":"Free. The default if unsure.\n\nAlternatives: [Google Antigravity](https://antigravity.google/download), free with generous limits · [Cursor](https://cursor.com/download), paid plan needed for its models, fine if you already have it.\n\nThe library"},{"h":"Gradio","a":"gradio","text":"Draws the chat or form interface. Installed from the terminal, not downloaded ([docs](https://www.gradio.app/guides/quickstart)).\n\npip install gradio\n\npip3 install gradio"},{"h":"2 Read or Watch Once","a":"2-read-or-watch-once","text":"| Resource | What it gives you |\n| --- | --- |\n| Post-read notes, Live Lecture 03: The First Interface LMS link | Why an interface exists, and the input, process, output frame every later lecture reuses. |\n| [Post-read notes, Live Lecture 04: Building UI](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes) | The derivation of Aarav's interface and the first Gradio code. Sections 12, 13 and 16 are the setup sections. |\n| [Practice Set: Replay Before You Render](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/practice-set) | The exercise this office hour was built on. Track A and Track B are defined here. |\n| [Aarav discovery document](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hmt3C81bok7wzrSx2nn1xEvJleeBbumnyQugXcklETg/edit?tab=t.1k1ewmgjta8l) (from the lecture) | The Track B starting point. Replace Aarav with yourself. |\n| [Golden pair template](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qg8zt7yHflGM7ojtnUBSPj2e_I3ACYapztGZQnzJa_c/edit?tab=t.0) (from the lecture) | The format for input, output and verification plan. |\n| [Big Binary Python prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) | Python syntax: variables, functions, if, return. Do it if the template was hard to read. |\n| [Gradio quickstart](https://www.gradio.app/guides/quickstart) | The official two-minute version of Hello World with gr.Interface. |\n| [Gradio chatbot guide](https://www.gradio.app/guides/creating-a-chatbot-fast) | gr.ChatInterface explained: the interface the template uses. |\n| Curriculum map: [100x-curriculum.vercel.app](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/) | Where you are. Node 3 of the deterministic section. |"},{"h":"3 Commands You Will Type","a":"3-commands-you-will-type","text":"| Command | What it does | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| python --versionpython3 --version | Prints the installed Python version | Tick 1. If not recognised: reinstall with Add Python to PATH.If python3 is not found, try python. |\n| pip install gradiopip3 install gradio | Downloads Gradio and what it depends on | Tick 4. pip3 if pip is not found. |\n| python -m pip install gradiopython3 -m pip install gradio | Same install, forced to use the Python you run | When you see `No module named gradio` |\n| python app.pypython3 app.py | Runs your file and prints the local URL | Tick 5, and every time you change the file |\n| `Ctrl`+`C` (in the terminal) | Stops the running app | Before running again, and when you see `Address already in use` |"},{"h":"4 Words You Will Meet","a":"4-words-you-will-meet","text":"**Terminal**Where you type commands: Command Prompt or PowerShell on Windows, Terminal on Mac, or View, Terminal inside your IDE.\n\n**IDE**A notepad for code that can also run it and show errors. VS Code is one.\n\n**pip**Python's installer. pip install X fetches library X from the internet.\n\n**Library**Code someone else wrote that you import. Gradio is a library.\n\n**import**The line at the top of a file that loads a library. import gradio as gr lets you write gr.ChatInterface.\n\n**def**Defines a function: a named block of code that takes inputs and returns an output.\n\n**return**Hands the output back. The function stops at the first return it reaches.\n\n**if**Runs the next indented lines only when the condition is true.\n\n**String**Text inside quotes. GOLDEN_INPUT is a string.\n\n**Variable**A name that holds a value. GOLDEN_OUTPUT is a variable holding a string.\n\n**localhost**Your own computer, as a web address: http://127.0.0.1:7860.\n\n**Port**The number after the colon. 7860 is Gradio's default.\n\n**Traceback**Python's error report. Read the last line first; it names the error."},{"h":"5 Deploy Links (when you are ready, not before)","a":"5-deploy-links-when-you-are-ready-not-before","text":"Shown in the office hour"},{"h":"Hugging Face Spaces","a":"hugging-face-spaces","text":"[Sign up](https://huggingface.co/join) · [new Space](https://huggingface.co/new-space) · [Gradio on Spaces docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/spaces-sdks-gradio)\n\nFree ZeroGPU, two Spaces, account must be 30 days old. Create the account today."},{"h":"6 Optional, From the Session","a":"6-optional-from-the-session","text":"- **DHH, Rails World 2026 opening keynote** [watch](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vDjW_dRyKXY). Why the person who built Ruby on Rails now writes 3% Ruby.\n- **Sridev's webinar on video generation with Opus 5.5** recording. Optional. Relevant if your output is video.\n- **Siddhant's essay on language as the interface** link. To be shared when ready."},{"h":"7 Where to Get Unstuck","a":"7-where-to-get-unstuck","text":"| Situation | Do this |\n| --- | --- |\n| Setup or template error, under 20 minutes in | Read the last line of the traceback, check [the error table in the guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s09), try the fix once |\n| Same error after 20 minutes | Post in the code track channel channel link: exact error, what you tried, screenshot. [Help-post builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list#s06). |\n| Not sure if your spec is right | Post it in the code track channel; Siddhant picks examples from there |\n| North Star or observation question | Support ticket on Discord |\n| You fixed something | Reply in the thread. Your fix is someone else's blocker. |\n\nInstall section 1. Read section 2. Keep section 3 open.Companions: [office hour guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide) · [checklist](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-building-ui-notes.md","title":"Building UI: Design to Code","meta":"title: Building UI: Design to Code\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes\nfile: c8/building-ui-notes.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Post-read notes · Live Lecture 04 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3306","sections":[{"h":"Building UI: Design to Code","a":"building-ui-design-to-code","text":"From Aarav's Friday status report to four lines of Gradio, by way of a vote that lost.\n\nInstructor: Siddhant GoswamiCohort 8 · Module 2Reading time ≈ 18 min\n\nThe one thing to keep\n\nYou do not choose an interface. You derive it from the output your user needs. Write the ideal input and ideal output first, replay what your user will do with that output, and the interface falls out of the replay. The room voted 17 to 13 for a search box, and one follow-up question about a single word in the plan overturned the vote. Code is the last step, and in Gradio it is four lines that restate the whiteboard."},{"h":"How to read these notes","a":"how-to-read-these-notes","text":"This was a design session that ended in code. Most of the two hours went on the part beginners skip: input, output, golden pairs, a verifier, and the reasoning behind one interface choice. The Gradio part is short on purpose.\n\nRead once, slowly"},{"h":"Sections 2 to 9","a":"sections-2-to-9","text":"With your own observation open beside them.\n\nCome back to"},{"h":"Sections 11 to 13","a":"sections-11-to-13","text":"When you sit down to install and run.\n\nStands on"},{"h":"Assignment 1, the Lecture 1 verifier game, yesterday's interface lecture","a":"assignment-1-the-lecture-1-verifier-game-yesterdays-interface-lecture","text":"Observation, hypothesis, one user. Two systems, a common language, a set of rules."},{"h":"01 The Room Check: Who Had Done Week 0","a":"01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0","text":"We opened with a hand count. The prerequisite for this session was one observation, one hypothesis and a conversation with at least one real user. More than 10 people in the room had done it; a single interview counted.\n\nTwo framing points came before any design. First, you are not married to one observation. The Assignment 1 deadline is 9 October, and until then the more observations you try and the more people you speak to, the better you get at testing your assumptions against the world. Second, that is the skill that matters now. When building becomes a commodity, finding the right users and solving their problem is the part AI cannot yet do. That is why it is part one of the journey."},{"h":"02 Start From One Person, Not a Persona","a":"02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona","text":"The worked example was Aarav, the business analyst at a mid-sized IT company in Pune, sitting at his desk with three tabs open, AI-aware and overwhelmed. The observation behind him: ambitious early career professionals in urban India arrive AI-aware and motivated, but fail at selection, scoping and first proof. They see AI at the category level (sales automation, HR tools, a productivity agent) and cannot turn it into one grounded workflow. Notice what the observation does not contain: a solution. An observation reports what you saw, from lived experience, and stops.\n\nThe hypothesis then states the assumption and how to test it: they are stuck because they lack a method to go from broad AI possibility to one scoped workflow. The test: provide a structured workflow diagnosis system followed by a guided build sprint, and 60% of active students will identify one real user, map one repeated workflow, build a working AI-assisted version and present a credible demo within 90 days. At 10 people, that is 6 of 10.\n\nThen the move that shapes everything after it: do not solve for a category (marketers, cafe owners, doctors). Solve for one named person. Once it works for that one person, talk to more people like them. That is how the test base grows."},{"h":"03 The Cheapest Test Is Three Boxes on a Whiteboard","a":"03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard","text":"The hypothesis names two solutions: the diagnosis system (part one) and the build sprint (part two). Siddhant took only part one and asked for the cheapest way to test it with Aarav. The answer on the whiteboard was three boxes.\n\nInput\n\n**A text field**Aarav describes a workflow he repeats, in 3 to 4 lines\n\n→\n\nProcess\n\n**A Done button**Whatever turns the description into a plan\n\n→\n\nOutput\n\n**A plan comes back**For automating part of the workflow\n\nThe input came from a message to Aarav: what is the one thing you repeat every day or every week, in 3 to 4 lines? Say he replies that he opens Jira, checks status, and emails his manager an update. That reply, compressed, is the input. The output is a plan for automating part of it. And the success test is not whether Aarav likes the plan. It is whether following it saves him time, say 1 hour a week. If it saves nothing, the impact is zero and the plan failed.\n\nNo code was written and no system was designed. **Designing** meant understanding what the user needs and the minimum solution that tests it. It does not mean opening Figma."},{"h":"04 Prateek's Observation, and the Stranger Test","a":"04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test","text":"Prateek posted his in chat: organizational project knowledge exists, but access depends on personal memory and network, so under deadline pressure people reuse the precedent they remember rather than the best fit, and nobody sees what was not found.\n\nObservation · too generic\n\nHand it to a stranger outside your industry: could they picture the problem? \"Organization\" does not say finance, healthcare or education, and an organization is not a person. Name the one person in the team who hits this most often, with their designation, and write the observation from their point of view.\n\nHypothesis · no number\n\n\"Noticeably faster\" cannot be falsified. Add a measurement and a stop rule. Without one, you keep spending time with no signal for when to pivot.\n\nInput and output · good\n\nSenior experts with 10 years of experience retrieving a specific fact or project assumption, and getting back a concise, source-grounded answer they can check against the reference document. Specific and concise.\n\nThe format everyone was asked to post in the code track channel on Discord: **observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, input, output.**"},{"h":"05 Ten Golden Pairs Before One Line of Code","a":"05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code","text":"One input and one output is an example. It is not enough to build against. Write at least 10 to 20 pairs; for production it can be 100 to 200. Each pair is the exact text a real user would type and the output that would solve their problem, gathered by talking to real users. If you cannot talk to your users, do not move ahead. If you are the user, write them yourself and check them with people like you.\n\nThis is the **golden dataset**: ideal input and output pairs. The output you believe solves the user's problem, paired with its input, many times over. The same idea returns in the probabilistic module for evals and fine-tuning.\n\nSiddhant called back the Lecture 1 game: how do you verify an app that adds two numbers? You write 1 + 2 = 3 and a few more, paste them in, and compare. Same output, it works. Different output, it does not. The golden dataset is what lets you decide whether your verifier will be deterministic, probabilistic or a human in the loop."},{"h":"06 The Verifier Is a Threshold, Worked in Minutes","a":"06-the-verifier-is-a-threshold-worked-in-minutes","text":"Say the email step is Aarav's biggest time sink: 15 minutes a day by hand. Drag the slider to see when the plan passes the 1 hour a week bar.\n\nMinutes the email step takes with the plan (was 15)\n\nSaved per day"},{"h":"10 min","a":"10-min","text":"Saved per 5-day week"},{"h":"50 min","a":"50-min","text":"With the plan it takes 5. That saves 10 minutes a day, and over a 5-day week, 50 minutes, close to the 1 hour threshold. The plan passes. Now say the plan cuts it only to 10 minutes. That saves 5 a day, 25 minutes a week, below the threshold. Same plan shape, same tool, and it fails.\n\nThe verifier rule\n\nA good output is one that clears a number you wrote down before you built anything. The verifier is your codified definition of good, and it only exists if you know the user well enough to name the baseline and the threshold. Anyone can prompt a demo; without a threshold it is a demo, not impact.\n\nThe written verifier for Aarav's first golden pair asked for four things in the output: a metric with a baseline, the named bottleneck, a test on past input with known answers, and a list of what is out of scope."},{"h":"07 Test-Driven Development, Written in English","a":"07-test-driven-development-written-in-english","text":"This is an old idea. **Test-driven development (TDD)** says write the test before the software; once the software passes your codified tests, you know it works. Siddhant used it heavily before AI and admitted it is hard to keep up, which is why few people do. What changes now is only the language: most of the tests you write will be in English. The philosophy does not change.\n\nHe tied it back to day one: almost everyone uses AI, utilization sits under 20%, and the impact on real earnings is in single digits. Building without a verifier is how that gap stays wide.\n\nThe spec now reads: observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers. Mask anything proprietary (use percentages or X, Y, Z) but share it, because that is what makes feedback possible. The North Star sits slightly outside the spec and tells you if you are still pointed the right way."},{"h":"08 Two Systems, One Interface","a":"08-two-systems-one-interface","text":"Back to yesterday's question. Two systems: Aarav, a human, and a workflow diagnosis plan generator that can run on a machine. What sits between them? Anupma answered it: a **user interface**. It needs a common language (English, text fields) and a set of rules (the web's HTTP). Aarav's prerequisites are a PC, a browser and an internet connection.\n\nThe golden pair Siddhant wrote live, pair 1, was concrete enough to design against:\n\nGolden pair 1 · input\n\n\"I'm a business analyst at a mid-sized IT company in Pune. Every Friday, I make the weekly status report for our client. I open Jira, filter tickets closed this week, copy them into a Word template, then go through email and Slack to find blockers... Takes around 3 hours. My manager usually edits the risk section because I miss things.\"\n\nFrom it: the input type is text and the output type is text. That has a useful side effect. If both ends are text, you can run a **manual MVP** before building anything: take Aarav's description on a call, write the plan yourself (with or without ChatGPT), send it as a document, and see if it saves him time."},{"h":"09 The Vote That Lost","a":"09-the-vote-that-lost","text":"Three candidate interfaces went on screen: a Google-style search box, a Google Form, and a chat. The room voted.\n\nSearch box\n\n17\n\nForm\n\n3\n\nChat\n\n13\n\n| Interface | What the replay showed |\n| --- | --- |\n| Search box text in, results out | No memory and no context. A follow-up means retyping the whole description, and an AI-generated plan may come back different each run. |\n| Form many fields, uploads | Multiple fields Aarav does not need, and no way to ask about the plan once it arrives. |\n| Chat winner | Holds context, supports follow-ups until Aarav is satisfied. |\n\nThen Siddhant replayed the golden output: a day-by-day build plan with time saved as the metric. Aarav reads it and asks:\n\nThe follow-up\n\n\"What does backtesting mean?\"\n\nExactly the kind of question Antriksh had just asked about golden datasets. How do you ask Google a follow-up? Swasti named the cost: Aarav would have to remember the plan, retype everything, and search again. Anupma named the missing property: no memory, no context. Bhavani added that it does not look like a conversation. The majority choice fell to one question, and the golden output is what surfaced it.\n\nThe interface does not come first\n\nPick the interface after you replay the user's conversation with your ideal output, never before. The winning vote was made on intuition; the correct answer came from a single realistic follow-up.\n\nRule of thumb for now: around 90% of first problems fit one of these three.\n\nSearch"},{"h":"One query, one answer back","a":"one-query-one-answer-back","text":"Form"},{"h":"Many inputs","a":"many-inputs","text":"Chat"},{"h":"Follow-ups that need memory and context","a":"follow-ups-that-need-memory-and-context","text":"Build the cheapest one first, and do not add anything until it is verified. That is the **V0**, or MVP."},{"h":"10 Vibe Code or First Principles: The Difference Is Control","a":"10-vibe-code-or-first-principles-the-difference-is-control","text":"Two routes to the same chat UI. Vibe code it: one prompt to Claude Code, Lovable, OpenAI or Gemini. Or write it from first principles in Python, with AI allowed, but understanding what every line does and why it is there. Both produce a working chat. The difference is control. Siddhant did not say which is better. Tonight's task is to do both and write a three-line review of what each gave you."},{"h":"11 Why Python, and Why Gradio","a":"11-why-python-and-why-gradio","text":"The web is HTML. Styling and scripting languages grew around it, and JavaScript came from that; Python was never part of web development. Until about 2021, Siddhant taught UI in JavaScript, HTML and CSS, and the repeated feedback was that beginners had to learn three languages to ship one thing. In 2026, Python libraries bridge that gap, so one language covers the next 16 weeks.\n\n| Library | Use it for |\n| --- | --- |\n| Gradio | Chat, forms, search-style inputs: any interface without heavy charts. Siddhant's default. Part of Hugging Face and the open-source AI ecosystem; AUTOMATIC1111, the Stable Diffusion UI with about 165,000 stars, runs on it. |\n| Streamlit | Data-heavy front ends: dashboards and charts that update. Comes next week. |\n\nToday's job is a chat that takes input and returns output, so Gradio."},{"h":"12 Four Lines That Are the Whiteboard","a":"12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard","text":"The Gradio Hello World takes a name and returns a greeting. Hover a line to see which box of the whiteboard it is.\n\nInput · the thing in the bracketsProcess · the bodyOutput · what follows returnInterface · gr.Interface\n\nRead it as the whiteboard. The function argument is the input. What follows return is the output. The function body is the **process** that turns one into the other, which here is almost nothing. `gr.Interface` is the interface from section 8: it declares the input type and the output type, the common language and the rules. `fn` just means function. This is the smiley exercise from yesterday, solved: you could not make Siddhant draw a smiley without a shared language and rules; Gradio gives you both for machines.\n\nFor chat, swap the interface. Gradio's own chat example just echoes you:\n\nSame template: define the input, the output and the process, then hand the function to the interface. Later the process becomes a call to a model. For now, echo is enough.\n\nOne instruction repeated: learn Gradio from [its documentation](https://gradio.app/docs), not from an LLM. Asking Claude gets you an answer; reading the docs gets you the rabbit holes in the left rail (website widgets, Slack bots, Discord bots). Siddhant's first app for the cohort was a Discord bot running on Gradio."},{"h":"13 Setting Up, and the Errors You Should Want Tonight","a":"13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight","text":"1. **Install Python** from the official site. Two or three steps for your operating system. More than 16 people in the room already had it.\n2. **Install one IDE.** Google Antigravity, Cursor or VS Code. An IDE is a notepad for code where you read and review before you push anything anywhere. VS Code is fully free; Antigravity is free with generous limits; Cursor has a paid plan for its models.\n3. **Make a folder** (Siddhant used `100x test` on the desktop), open it in the IDE, create `app.py` and paste the Hello World.\n4. **Run** `pip install gradio`, then `python app.py`, and open the local link it prints.\n\nYou will hit errors: Python not found, Gradio not installed. That is the point. Solving them yourself is the start of the debugging muscle. If def, return and functions are unfamiliar, finish the [Python bigbinary prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) first; lectures will not teach Python syntax. The next office hour reviews your work and your errors, then deploys it to a shareable link.\n\nStep-by-step setup with tests for each tick: [the office hour guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s07) and [the checklist](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list)."},{"h":"14 From the Q&A","a":"14-from-the-qa","text":"**Charu: trackers in, exception table out, form or chat?**\n\nSiddhant declined to answer without the spec. Interface design is contextual: who is the user, what is their goal, what do the ideal input and output look like. Walk your user's journey the way we walked Aarav's, then ask.\n\n**Kandarp: my project is building WhatsApp agents.**\n\nThat is solution-first. Naming the solution in the first line leaves you choosing between options with nothing to decide by. Start from observation, input, output and process.\n\n**My hypothesis changes after every interview.**\n\nThen do not build yet. To learn the process, use Aarav's documents. Spend real build hours only on a problem you can test with real people.\n\n**Saiteja: first time coding, from marketing.**\n\nYou have company: across cohorts the split moved from 50-50 to about 60-40 first-time coders. Confidence takes time and comes from projects. Siddhant's own start: in a first-year break he built one app a day, end to end, deployed to a link, for 30 days. Try one app a day with other first-timers; today's is Hello World.\n\n**Neha: a WhatsApp study group?**\n\nNot officially, since the cohort cannot moderate or take responsibility for it. Discord and email are where 100x does. On a complex North Star: DM it on Discord; the goal is only the next 6 months.\n\n**Mayuresh: not sure my problem is big or monetizable.**\n\nIt does not have to be. The process matters more than this outcome. A Cohort 6 student, a non-coder from the no-code track, built 100+ apps in 3 months; he has since raised funding. Volume compounds only if the projects point the same way.\n\n**Should the North Star be a product?**\n\nNo. It is a direction, not a product. Most reduce to a better job or upskilling (define the role) or building something of your own (define what and for whom)."},{"h":"15 Vocabulary Installed Tonight","a":"15-vocabulary-installed-tonight","text":"**Observation**What you saw a real person struggle with, from lived experience, with no solution in it.\n\n**Hypothesis**Your assumed cause plus a test with a number, a deadline and a stop rule.\n\n**Designing**Working out what the user needs and the minimum solution that tests it.\n\n**Golden dataset**Ideal input and output pairs, from real users: 10 to 20 to start, 100 to 200 for production.\n\n**Verifier**The check that decides if an output is good, usually a threshold against a baseline.\n\n**TDD**Write the test before the software; now the test is often in English.\n\n**Spec**Observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers.\n\n**User interface**The interface between a human and a machine: a common language plus rules.\n\n**Manual MVP**Running the input to output loop by hand before building any technology.\n\n**V0 / MVP**The cheapest build that can test the hypothesis, and nothing more.\n\n**Process**The function that turns input into output.\n\n**IDE**An editor for reading, writing and reviewing code on your machine."},{"h":"16 Action Block","a":"16-action-block","text":"- Post your spec in the code track channelObservation, North Star, hypothesis with a number, one named user, input, output.\n- Write 10 golden pairs with a real userPlus one verifier with a baseline and a threshold.\n- Replay one pair and write the follow-up your user will askPick search, form or chat from it.\n- Get Hello World running locally in Gradio, then the chat echoKeep a list of every error you hit and how you fixed it.\n- Build the chat twice and write the three-line reviewVibe coded and first principles.\n\nTicks save on this device\n\n0 / 5\n\nStart from the output. The interface follows.Next: [the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/practice-set) turns this into one real user, ten golden pairs and a chat on your machine."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-office-hour-guide.md","title":"Spec First, Then Forty Lines","meta":"title: Spec First, Then Forty Lines\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide\nfile: c8/office-hour-guide.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Office hour guide · Follows Live Lecture 04: Building UI · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3863","sections":[{"h":"Spec First, Then Forty Lines","a":"spec-first-then-forty-lines","text":"From a one-page spec to a chat app on localhost, and the shortest road to a shareable link.\n\nInstructor: Siddhant Goswami1 October 2026Cohort 8 · Module 2\n\nThe one thing to keep\n\nImplementation is the last step. Write the spec (North Star, observation, hypothesis, one user, golden input and output pairs, a verifier) before you open an editor. Once the spec exists, the first version of your app is a template: the golden input and golden output pasted in as text, a four-line function that returns one or the other, and a chat interface Gradio gives you for free. Intelligence comes next week. The interface does not care where the answer comes from."},{"h":"How to read this guide","a":"how-to-read-this-guide","text":"Sections 1 to 6"},{"h":"Replay of the office hour","a":"replay-of-the-office-hour","text":"What the room worked through and the verdicts that came out of it.\n\nSections 7 to 11"},{"h":"Hands-on, for first-time coders","a":"hands-on-for-first-time-coders","text":"Setup, the template, the errors you will meet, and how to put the app on a link. Never run Python before? Read 7 to 10 slowly and tick [the checklist](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list) as you go.\n\nSection 12"},{"h":"Before the next lecture","a":"before-the-next-lecture","text":"What to post, and where.\n\nSkip to the setup →"},{"h":"01 Where We Started: Show Me the Spec","a":"01-where-we-started-show-me-the-spec","text":"The session opened with one request: paste your spec in the chat. Not code, not a UI sketch. The same document the lecture produced for Aarav: North Star, observation, hypothesis, one named user, golden input and output pairs, and a way to verify the output. Siddhant was explicit that the thinking behind writing code has not changed in ten years for him. What has gone down is the amount of code written by hand. The work of finding the right inputs, the right output, the process between them, and the right interface still happens every time, and it happens before implementation.\n\nTrack A"},{"h":"Your own user","a":"your-own-user","text":"You already have a lived observation and a hypothesis, so write the spec for your own user.\n\nTrack B"},{"h":"Aarav, then yourself","a":"aarav-then-yourself","text":"Take the Aarav discovery document from the lecture LMS link, replace Aarav with yourself, and write your own weekly workflow as the observation. Not a lesser option; it is the fastest way to have a spec tonight.\n\nThe persona was chosen because most of the room is in Aarav's position: consuming content, needing to scope one thing down and take a first action.\n\nOne cadence rule was set for the rest of the code track. Every weekend lecture ends with a practice set that applies the lecture to your own problem. The pace of the cohort is proportional to the progress of the students: more resources and more advanced material arrive when practice sets show people can implement what was taught, and not before.\n\nStep one, always\n\nIf you are confused about where to spend effort, write the spec. It needs an observation, a hypothesis, one user, one golden dataset (made by doing the task manually), and one verifier. Nothing in the UI, the code, or the deploy sections of this guide applies until that page exists."},{"h":"02 Anand's Waveform: When the Right Interface Is Not Chat","a":"02-anands-waveform-when-the-right-interface-is-not-chat","text":"Anand pasted the first full spec. North Star: an AI-powered power and performance engineering workflow that cuts analyst turnaround by at least 10x (or to within 24 hours), actively used by at least three engineering teams. Observation: for every active FSDB release, a PnP engineer manually studies the waveform to find the optimal window and the matching IP utilisation for downstream flows; arrivals are unplanned, so the task slips to the next day and results are verified by hand. Hypothesis: automate the window and compute utilisation step.\n\nAsked what interface he had in mind, Anand said chat. Siddhant pushed on the user rather than the answer. The users are engineers who already run commands somewhere. Engineers spend their day in a terminal, so a **command-line interface** dropped into the shell they already use will beat a new chat window on adoption and friction. Claude Code is the reference case: Claude Cowork shipped with nearly the same functionality and was far less popular at launch, because the people using Claude at the time were engineers who prefer a CLI.\n\nThe verdict was not \"use a CLI\". It was: for a problem this niche, try at least two interfaces, start with the one that lives inside the environment the user already works in, and let the user decide. Derivation has to be validated with the end user; the instructor is not a PnP engineer and said so."},{"h":"03 Vineeth's Agency: Counting Inputs Before Choosing a Box","a":"03-vineeths-agency-counting-inputs-before-choosing-a-box","text":"Vineeth Vijayan's spec was the second one on the whiteboard. North Star: by February 2027, an AI-native creative agency that delivers a video content pipeline to startups and small brands on internal tooling, with clients able to self-serve variations or have the agency manage them. User: Benjamin, a UX designer turned entrepreneur. Input: a short brief (product, campaign, what the video must do), existing brand assets, one or two reference videos, approve or reject decisions per batch, and Rs 10,000 upfront. Output: at least two approved ad-ready videos from a batch of five, plus a brand profile and adapted template set that makes the next brief faster.\n\nRather than jump to an interface, Siddhant ran the same exercise as the lecture: list each input and its type.\n\n| Input | Type | Note |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 · Payment | Rs 10,000 | Could sit behind a Stripe gate. For an MVP, collect it manually and keep three inputs. |\n| 1 · The brief | text | Product, campaign, what the video must do. |\n| 2 · Brand assets | link / HTML · MP4 · JPEG, PNG · text | Not one type. A website, past videos, ad images, guidelines. |\n| 3 · Reference videos | same mix as 2 | Probably collapsible into input 2. |\n\nTwo scoping decisions came out of typing the inputs. First, **heavy files cost money**: text is close to free to store and process, video is not, so he capped video at 5 MB for now (an arbitrary number, chosen so the free tier survives). Second, the brand profile and template set are an output the system stores for next time rather than something the UI shows; on the second brief, Benjamin should only need to type text because his assets are already known. Vineeth had designed that in, and it was called out as the right instinct.\n\nThen the interface debate. A form can collect all three inputs and could email back five videos with two recommendations, so a form is not wrong. Mohit proposed a canvas (Figma or Magnific style) for side-by-side variations. Vineeth, unmuted, chose chat over form for one reason: **follow-ups**. If a client uploads an irrelevant video, a chat can ask for a replacement inside the same conversation; a form cannot. He added that Google Flow's early version worked because it had simple buckets for each element and told you what was missing, which is still chat with structure. Siddhant bought the argument. Follow-ups and revisions are the job in an agency, so the interface that makes follow-ups cheap wins.\n\nWhy chat keeps winning\n\nTwo criteria decide most MVP interfaces: ease of use and cost of follow-up. Chat scores highest on both, because anyone who can use WhatsApp can use it and because a revision is just the next message. This is a pattern, not a law: when the output modality changes (watching five videos, comparing frames), you add rendering inside the conversation rather than abandoning it."},{"h":"04 The Detour: Language Is the Interface","a":"04-the-detour-language-is-the-interface","text":"The chat-versus-form debate led into a thesis Siddhant started writing in 2023. Every piece of software in the last 50 to 60 years exists because humans could not explain things to machines. We built calculators, then CPUs, then programming languages, so that we could learn the machine's language and get work done. Around 2019 to 2020 transformers inverted this: machines began to understand ours. The inflection he pointed to this week is [DHH's opening keynote at Rails World 2026](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vDjW_dRyKXY) on 23 September, eight days earlier. The person who built Ruby on Rails said that Ruby was 55% of his code last year and is 3% now; he writes in English and has, in his words, retired from writing code by hand.\n\nThe same shift is visible in his own team. A year ago a full design team lived in Figma. In the last three months his designer has not opened it; he connects Figma to an LLM and brainstorms in language, because the end product is a deployed web or mobile app and the intermediate canvas existed only because engineering hours were expensive. The 100x video pipeline went from six hours per video to under 30 minutes from idea to published reel. The Fairfax refresher video he showed was made in Claude in one shot with Opus 5.5, not in Premiere or DaVinci, because he had 15 minutes before the client call.\n\nHe was careful about the nuance most people miss. \"Use English\" does not mean \"outsource your thinking\". Karpathy's line was the anchor: **you can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding**. The reason this track still teaches Python, and he admitted the cognitive dissonance of holding both beliefs at once, is that the hardware in front of you does not compile English. Every prompt still becomes a deterministic language before it runs, and the translation gap is exactly where people who have never worked with a deterministic system get hurt: they expect the machine to behave like a person. Writing `def respond` today is how you learn to write the instruction well; tomorrow the syntax changes, the logic you had to think through does not."},{"h":"05 The Verifier Is Still Yours","a":"05-the-verifier-is-still-yours","text":"What the Verifier's Rule says about all of this: even if AI builds every interface, judging which interface a user needs, what type each input is, and how the user should feel is a human decision. Claude can give opinions about an interface; it cannot tell you how to make your user feel something, because it has never felt anything.\n\nAnuj asked whether a verifier can be a person's opinion. The answer came with an example: a copy review built on David Ogilvy's principles, codified as 15 scoring parameters, handed to an LLM to score out of 100 with reasoning on how to improve. A verifier comes from domain expertise or lived experience and gets written down as criteria. Prateek asked whether, with golden pairs from domain experts, the verifier could be a semantic similarity check (does the output carry the same aspects as the golden output). Yes, and he should share the pairs in the code channel for feedback, anonymised if needed.\n\nThe gap he still sees in almost every spec, including the ones on the whiteboard tonight: what makes the two approved videos good? What makes the plan for Aarav a good plan? Define that and write it down as the verifier before building. It is more important to the application than the interface."},{"h":"06 From the Q&A: Solution-First, Gradio, Harnesses","a":"06-from-the-qa-solution-first-gradio-harnesses","text":"**Samarth: when do we do solution-first?**\n\nWhen we know the right problem. Solutions keep evolving; a solution learned before the problem is a process you get attached to without knowing what turns the input into the output. Learn problem, inputs, output, process, then derive the solution, and the solution becomes a function of what is available that month.\n\n**Antariksh: can Gradio replace React and Node?**\n\nNo, explicitly. Gradio is a medium to learn with, a stepping stone, not a production recommendation. The point of writing a terrible-looking UI in Python is to understand how a machine takes instructions.\n\n**Antariksh: build a harness to bridge the gap?**\n\nThat is solution-first thinking and it is too early. Harnesses get derived at a specific point in the curriculum, after deterministic systems, probabilistic systems and the bridges between them. You are at base camp; there is no single step to the peak.\n\n**Varun: is the 21-day tracker MVP what we work on for three months?**\n\nNo. Assignment 1 exists to train problem-first thinking. After it comes the hackathon (new problem statements, right after the deterministic section), then Assignment 2, then the capstone. You may carry your problem forward if you want to; nobody will stop you.\n\n**Does the app store conversations?**\n\nNot yet. Storage arrives in the database section."},{"h":"07 The Setup: Four Things","a":"07-the-setup-four-things","text":"Everything after this point is for the first-time coder. The lecture spent two hours on design and 20 minutes on setup, and for many people the night ended at setup. The process between you and a working app has four parts: a language the machine understands (Python), a place to write and read it (an IDE), one folder with one file, and two commands.\n\nShow commands for\n\n1. **Install Python** from [python.org/downloads](https://www.python.org/downloads/). Windows: on the first installer screen tick \"Add Python to PATH\" before clicking Install.Mac: run the installer through. Test: open a terminal (Command Prompt or PowerShellTerminal) and type the version command. A version number is your tick. python --version python3 --version If it says not found, try `python --version` as well.\n2. **Install an IDE.** It is a notepad for code. [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/download) is free; [Google Antigravity](https://antigravity.google/download) is free with generous limits; [Cursor](https://cursor.com/download) needs a paid plan for its models. Unsure: take VS Code. Do not spend tonight comparing them.\n3. **Make one folder and one file.** Create a folder (for example `100x test` on the desktop). In the IDE: File, Open Folder. Create a new file inside it called `app.py`. The.py tells the machine this is Python.\n4. **Run two commands** in the IDE's terminal (View, Terminal). The second prints a local URL, `http://127.0.0.1:7860`. Open it in your browser. pip install gradio python app.py pip3 install gradio python3 app.py\n\nStart with Hello World (the same one in the [Gradio quickstart](https://www.gradio.app/guides/quickstart)) before the chat template, so that the first thing you debug is your setup and not your logic:\n\nInput · the thing in the bracketsProcess · the bodyOutput · what follows returnInterface · gr.Interface\n\nRead it as the lecture whiteboard. The thing in the brackets (name) is the input. What follows return is the output. The function is the process. `gr.Interface` is the interface between a human and this machine."},{"h":"08 The Template: Forty Lines, Four That Matter","a":"08-the-template-forty-lines-four-that-matter","text":"This is the file Siddhant ran live. Save it as `app.py`, replacing Hello World, and run it again.\n\nEverything in it is either the interface or text you already wrote on your whiteboard. `GOLDEN_INPUT` and `GOLDEN_OUTPUT` are Aarav's golden pair pasted in as Python strings. [gr.ChatInterface](https://www.gradio.app/guides/creating-a-chatbot-fast) is the chat window; `examples` puts the golden input one click away so nobody has to type it. The only processing is the four lines inside respond: take the message, and if it contains the word jira return the golden output, otherwise ask a follow-up question. In Python this is how you move conditionally: if the condition is true the first return runs and nothing after it does; if it is false the first return is skipped.\n\nThere is no intelligence yet. That is deliberate. The app mocks the intelligence with the golden pair so that you can test the whole loop (input in, output out, in the interface your user will actually see) before any model is involved. Next week the body of respond becomes a call to an LLM API and the answer stops depending on the word jira. The interface will not change, because the interface only cares about the input and the output and how they are presented.\n\nTo make it yours\n\nChange three things and nothing else. Replace GOLDEN_INPUT with your own golden input. Replace GOLDEN_OUTPUT with your own golden output. Change the word the if statement looks for to a word that will always appear in your golden input. Leave the interface alone."},{"h":"Make it yours, here","a":"make-it-yours-here","text":"Paste your golden pair and trigger word. The three lines change; the interface stays. Nothing leaves your browser.\n\nYour golden input · what your user types Your golden output · what would solve it\n\nTrigger word · always in your golden input App title"},{"h":"09 The Errors You Should Expect","a":"09-the-errors-you-should-expect","text":"The machine does not guess. When it stops, it is because something was not told to it. Keep a log: the exact error text, what you tried, and what fixed it. The office hour reviews the log, not just the screenshot of success.\n\n**python is not recognised / command not found**\n\nCause\n\nPATH not set during install (Windows), or Mac uses python3.\n\nFix\n\nWindows: reinstall and tick Add Python to PATH. Mac: use `python3` and `pip3`.\n\n**No module named gradio**\n\nCause\n\npip installed into a different Python than the one running.\n\nFix\n\npython -m pip install gradio\n\nSo pip and python match, then run again.\n\n**Address already in use / port 7860**\n\nCause\n\nThe previous app is still running.\n\nFix\n\nClose the old terminal or press `Ctrl`+`C` in it, then run again.\n\n**IndentationError / SyntaxError**\n\nCause\n\nA line was pasted with wrong spacing or a missing quote or bracket.\n\nFix\n\nRe-copy the template exactly; Python counts the spaces at the start of a line.\n\n**Browser shows nothing**\n\nCause\n\nThe URL was retyped wrong, or the terminal was closed.\n\nFix\n\nClick the link the terminal printed; keep that terminal open while you test.\n\nStuck for more than 20 minutes: post in the code track channel on Discord channel link, not the general channel, with three things: the exact error message, what you already tried, and a screenshot. First-time coders should pair up; one person's fix is usually another person's blocker."},{"h":"10 Putting It on a Link: Hugging Face Spaces","a":"10-putting-it-on-a-link-hugging-face-spaces","text":"Build and test locally first. Everything you do online is the same file; the only difference is a link other people can open. When you are ready, [Hugging Face Spaces](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/spaces-sdks-gradio) is the lowest-cost, fastest route for a Gradio app, and it is the one Siddhant showed. Hugging Face owns Gradio, which is why the fit is exact.\n\nOne policy change matters for this cohort and did not for earlier ones: after the NVIDIA acquisition, free hardware on Spaces is **ZeroGPU** only, you get two free Spaces, and a **new account must be 30 days old** before it can use them. The instruction from the room: [create your Hugging Face account](https://huggingface.co/join) tonight, even if you deploy nothing. The 30 days fit the 21-day assignment window; by the time you have run the task manually and have something worth sharing, the account will be eligible. No one is asked to pay for a Space.\n\nWhich route tonight?\n\n1. Log in at [huggingface.co](https://huggingface.co/join). Click the plus button, New Space (or go straight to [huggingface.co/new-space](https://huggingface.co/new-space)).\n2. Name it (for example `c8-ui`). Choose the Gradio SDK. Start blank, not from a template (the templates are not trusted; you already have tested code).\n3. Hardware: the free ZeroGPU option. Visibility: public if you are happy to share the link, private otherwise.\n4. Click Create. On the next screen choose Create the app.py file, paste your tested app.py, and click Commit new file.\n5. Wait for the build. The Space starts your app. From the three-dot menu choose Embed this Space to get the shareable link.\n6. To change anything: edit app.py in the Space and commit again."},{"h":"11 Before the Next Lecture","a":"11-before-the-next-lecture","text":"- Spec on one page, posted in the code track channelNorth Star, observation, hypothesis, one named user, at least one golden input and output pair, and the verifier (what makes the output good, written as criteria). Track B is fine.\n- A screenshot of the template running on localhostWith your own golden pair in it, plus your error log, posted in the same thread.\n- A Hugging Face account created todaySo the 30-day clock is running. Optional: a Spaces link if you already qualify.\n- Run the task manually for your 2 to 5 users this weekThe golden dataset and the verifier come out of doing the job by hand, not out of the code.\n- If def and return looked strange, finish the Python prerequisite[Big Binary Python prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/), before the next lecture. The lectures will not teach Python syntax.\n\nTicks save on this device\n\n0 / 5\n\n**Next week:** the body of respond becomes an LLM API call, which makes the app answer anything your user types and not just the word jira. Then Streamlit as a second interface, then the database so conversations are stored, then the hackathon on 17 to 19 October."},{"h":"Vocabulary installed tonight","a":"vocabulary-installed-tonight","text":"**Spec**One page: North Star, observation, hypothesis, one user, golden pairs, verifier. Written before any code.\n\n**Golden pair**One real input and the output you would be proud to return for it, produced by doing the task manually.\n\n**Verifier**Written criteria for what makes an output good, from domain expertise. Can be scored by a person or a model.\n\n**Input typing**Listing every input and its type (text, link, MP4, PNG) before choosing an interface. Mixed types change the interface.\n\n**Command-line interface**An interface inside the terminal the user already works in. Wins on adoption when the user is an engineer.\n\n**Follow-up cost**How cheap it is for the user to revise or add to a request. The main reason chat beats a form.\n\n**Mocked intelligence**Returning the golden output from a stored string so the whole loop can be tested before any model is involved.\n\n**Gradio**A Python library that gives you a chat or form interface in one line. A learning medium, not a production tool.\n\n**localhost / 127.0.0.1**An address that means your own machine. Nobody else can open it.\n\n**PATH**The list of places your operating system looks for programs. If Python is not on it, the terminal cannot find it.\n\n**ZeroGPU**The only free hardware on Hugging Face Spaces now. Two Spaces per account, after the account is 30 days old.\n\n**Outsource thinking, not understanding**Karpathy's line, used as the reason this track still teaches a deterministic language."},{"h":"Rabbit holes from the session","a":"rabbit-holes-from-the-session","text":"| Reference | Why it was pulled |\n| --- | --- |\n| [DHH opening keynote, Rails World 2026](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vDjW_dRyKXY) | The pioneer of Rails saying his code is now 3% Ruby and that he writes in English. Recommended viewing for the whole room. |\n| Siddhant's essay on language as the interface and invisible software link | The 2023 thesis the chat-versus-form debate keeps landing on. Still being written; to be shared separately. |\n| Sridev's Sunday webinar on video generation with Opus 5.5 recording | Optional. Relevant to Vineeth and anyone whose output is video. Recording to be uploaded after the session. |\n| [Big Binary Python prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) | The syntax exercise to finish if def and return were unfamiliar. |\n| Curriculum map: [100x-curriculum.vercel.app](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/) | Where tonight sits: node 3 of the deterministic section, with the hackathon bridge ahead. |\n\nWrite the spec. Paste the template. Get the tick. Intelligence is next week.Companions: [the checklist](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list) · [the beginner kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-pages.md","title":"C8 Code Track Pages","meta":"title: C8 Code Track Pages\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/\nfile: c8/index.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Code track · Lecture 04 and its office hour\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 164","sections":[{"h":"Building UI, start to link","a":"building-ui-start-to-link","text":"Five pages that go together. Read the lecture notes, do the practice set, follow the office hour guide, tick the list, keep the kit open.\n\n[01 · Lecture notesBuilding UI: Design to CodeFrom Aarav's Friday status report to four lines of Gradio, by way of a vote that lost.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes) [02 · Practice setReplay Before You RenderOne real user. Ten golden pairs. A chat running on your own machine.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/practice-set) [03 · Office hour guideSpec First, Then Forty LinesFrom a one-page spec to a chat app on localhost, and the shortest road to a shareable link.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide) [04 · ChecklistThe ChecklistSix ticks tonight, five deliverables this week, one thread to post in. Ticks save on your device.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list) [05 · Beginner kitFirst-Time Coder KitEvery link and command, with one line on what it is for.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit) [06 · TroubleshootingWhen it does not runSearch an error; see the cause and the fix.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/troubleshooting) The mapModule 2 MapWhere this sits: node 3 of the deterministic section."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-practice-set.md","title":"Replay Before You Render","meta":"title: Replay Before You Render\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/practice-set\nfile: c8/practice-set.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Practice set · Follows Live Lecture 04: Building UI · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 881","sections":[{"h":"Replay Before You Render","a":"replay-before-you-render","text":"One real user. Ten golden pairs. A chat running on your own machine.\n\nPost in the code track channelCohort 8 · Module 2\n\nThe practice set in one sentence\n\nWrite a spec for one real user with 10 golden pairs and a numeric verifier, replay one pair to derive the interface, then run that interface locally in Gradio and compare it with a vibe-coded version.\n\nWhich track are you on?\n\nThe page shows only your track's steps. Your choice is remembered on this device."},{"h":"01 Why This Exercise Exists","a":"01-why-this-exercise-exists","text":"Aarav's status report was a proxy. The skill is not Gradio; it is deriving an interface from an output. In the lecture, 17 people voted for a search box and one follow-up question overturned it. You are going to run that same replay on your own user, where nobody hands you the answer.\n\nLecture recap: [the vote that lost](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s09)."},{"h":"02 Track A: Your Own Problem preferred","a":"02-track-a-your-own-problem-preferred","text":"1. **Write the spec.** Observation (no solution in it, readable by a stranger), North Star (6 months, a direction not a product), hypothesis with a number, a deadline and a stop rule, one named user with their designation, input, output.\n2. **Collect 10 golden pairs.** The exact text your user would type and the output that would solve their problem. Get them from the user, not from your head. If you are the user, check them with two people like you.\n3. **Write the verifier.** Baseline, threshold and the arithmetic, the way Aarav's was worked: 15 minutes to 5, 10 a day, 50 a week against a 1 hour bar. State what a failing output looks like in the same units.\n4. **Replay one pair.** Take your best golden output and write the first follow-up your user would ask after reading it. Then pick search, form or chat, with the one-line reason the replay gave you.\n5. **Build it in Gradio, locally.** Hello World first. Then the interface you picked, with an echo or placeholder process, and input and output types that match your spec.\n6. **Vibe code the same interface** with any tool you like, then write a three-line review: what each route gave you, and what the difference was."},{"h":"03 Track B: Aarav's Documents","a":"03-track-b-aaravs-documents","text":"Use the [discovery document](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hmt3C81bok7wzrSx2nn1xEvJleeBbumnyQugXcklETg/edit?tab=t.1k1ewmgjta8l) and [golden pairs](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qg8zt7yHflGM7ojtnUBSPj2e_I3ACYapztGZQnzJa_c/edit?tab=t.0). Run the same loop with Aarav as the user and pair 1 (the Friday status report, around 3 hours) as your anchor.\n\nTwo changes stop this from being a copy:\n\nChange 1"},{"h":"Write 3 new pairs yourself","a":"write-3-new-pairs-yourself","text":"For other repeated tasks Aarav could plausibly have, each with its own verifier.\n\nChange 2"},{"h":"Replay a different follow-up","a":"replay-a-different-follow-up","text":"One that is not \"What does backtesting mean?\". If your replay still lands on chat, say why in your own words."},{"h":"04 Rules of the Build","a":"04-rules-of-the-build","text":"**Gradio only.**\n\nNo deployment yet; the office hour takes your local app to a link.\n\n**Predict before you run.**\n\nWrite down your interface pick before the replay, and the error you expect before your first `python app.py`. Keep both, even when wrong.\n\n**Docs before chatbots.**\n\nLearn `gr.Interface` and `gr.ChatInterface` from the [Gradio documentation](https://gradio.app/docs). Ask an LLM only after you have read the relevant page.\n\n**AI is allowed, blind pasting is not.**\n\nUse AI for hints and debugging. You must be able to point at the input, the process and the output in every line of your first-principles version. That version is the recall test, and it only works if you can explain it.\n\n**Log every error.**\n\nMessage, cause, fix. Python not found and Gradio not installed count. This log is what the office hour reviews.\n\n**Mask, do not withhold.**\n\nReplace proprietary numbers with percentages or X, Y, Z, but share the spec."},{"h":"The starting template, for reference","a":"the-starting-template-for-reference","text":""},{"h":"05 What to Submit","a":"05-what-to-submit","text":"Tick each deliverable as it is ready. Ticks save on this device.\n\n- SpecObservation, North Star, hypothesis with a number and a stop rule, one named user with designation, input, output.\n- Golden datasetAt least 10 input and output pairs, each input written as the user would type it. Track B: 3 new pairs of your own.\n- VerifierBaseline, threshold, the arithmetic, and one example of an output that fails it.\n- Replay noteYour pre-replay pick, the follow-up question, the final interface, and the one-line reason.\n- ScreenshotsHello World and your chosen interface, both running on localhost on your machine.\n- CodeYour app.py, with a comment marking input, process and output.\n- Error logEvery error hit, with cause and fix.\n- Three-line reviewVibe coded versus first principles: what each gave you, and the difference.\n\nDeliverables ready\n\n0 / 8"},{"h":"06 Where to Post, and How to Get Unblocked","a":"06-where-to-post-and-how-to-get-unblocked","text":"Post everything in the code track channel on Discord, not the general channels. Stuck on installation: post the exact error message and what you already tried. First-time coders: pair up with someone else starting out and compare errors; one person's fix is often another's blocker.\n\n**Got an error?** Search it in [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/troubleshooting). The help-post format is in [the office hour guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s09)."},{"h":"07 After Submission","a":"07-after-submission","text":"The next office hour reviews your specs and error logs live, then deploys your local app to a shareable link.\n\nReplay before you render.Companion pages: [office hour guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide) · [checklist](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list) · [beginner kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-tick-list.md","title":"The Checklist","meta":"title: The Checklist\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/tick-list\nfile: c8/tick-list.html\nabout: Checklist and actions · Companion to the office hour guide · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 703","sections":[{"h":"The Checklist","a":"the-checklist","text":"Six ticks tonight, five deliverables this week, one thread to post in.\n\n1 October 2026Ticks save on this device\n\nThe order is the rule\n\nSpec before setup. Setup before template. Template on localhost before any link. Do not skip ahead; each stage is how you find out the previous one actually worked.\n\n**0/0**\n\n**Overall**everything on this page\n\n**0/0**\n\n**The spec**Stage 0\n\n**0/0**\n\n**Setup**Stage 1 · tonight\n\n**0/0**\n\n**The template**Stage 2\n\n**0/0**\n\n**The link**Stage 3 · optional"},{"h":"1 Stage 0: The Spec do this first","a":"1-stage-0-the-spec-do-this-first","text":"How you know it is done\n\n0 / 6\n\n- North Star writtenTimeline, role, scale, specific thing. Someone outside the cohort could verify it.\n- Observation from lived experienceSomething you watched happen, or do yourself every week (Track B).\n- HypothesisOne sentence that can fail. If automated X, then user gets Y.\n- One named userA real person, or you (Track B). Not a segment.\n- At least one golden input and output pairProduced by doing the task manually. Both written out in full.\n- VerifierWritten criteria for what makes the output good. Who or what checks it."},{"h":"2 Stage 1: Setup six ticks","a":"2-stage-1-setup-six-ticks","text":"Commands for\n\nThe test for each tick\n\n0 / 6\n\n- 1 · Python installed ([download](https://www.python.org/downloads/))`python --version``python3 --version` prints a number\n- 2 · An IDE installed[VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/download), [Antigravity](https://antigravity.google/download) or [Cursor](https://cursor.com/download) opened once\n- 3 · Folder + app.pyFolder open in the IDE, app.py visible in the sidebar\n- 4 · pip install gradiopip3 install gradioEnds with `Successfully installed gradio`\n- 5 · python app.pypython3 app.py runsTerminal prints `http://127.0.0.1:7860` and the browser shows the app\n- 6 · Error log startedA text file with: error text, what you tried, what fixed it\n\nStep-by-step with the Hello World code: [guide, section 7](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s07). Errors: [guide, section 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s09)."},{"h":"3 Stage 2: The Template","a":"3-stage-2-the-template","text":"The test for each item\n\n0 / 6\n\n- Hello World ranType a name, get Hello name back\n- Chat template ran unchangedClick the example, get Aarav's plan back; type hi, get the follow-up question\n- Your golden input pasted in`GOLDEN_INPUT` is your text\n- Your golden output pasted in`GOLDEN_OUTPUT` is your text\n- Trigger word changedThe if statement looks for a word that always appears in your golden input\n- Screenshot takenBrowser showing your golden output in the chat\n\nThe template, and a form that fills in your three changes: [guide, section 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/office-hour-guide#s08)."},{"h":"4 Stage 3: The Link optional this week","a":"4-stage-3-the-link-optional-this-week","text":"Notes\n\n0 / 4\n\n- [Hugging Face account](https://huggingface.co/join) created todayRequired for everyone. The 30-day clock starts now.\n- Space created (if account is 30+ days old)[New Space](https://huggingface.co/new-space): Gradio SDK, blank, ZeroGPU, paste app.py, commit\n- Link opened on your phoneIf it opens on a different device, it is live"},{"h":"5 Actions This Week","a":"5-actions-this-week","text":"Five deliverables\n\n0 / 5\n\n- Post your one-page spec in the code track channelDeadline: before the next code lecture. Track B counts.\n- Post a localhost screenshot and your error logIn the same thread.\n- [Create a Hugging Face account](https://huggingface.co/join) today\n- Run the task manually for 2 to 5 real usersOr for yourself, five times, if Track B. Write down the inputs and outputs you produced; those are your golden pairs.\n- Finish the [Big Binary Python prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/)If any line of the template was unreadable to you.\n\nDates\n\nAssignment 1 (First Proof) due **Friday 9 October**. Hackathon **Saturday 17 to Monday 19 October**, right after the deterministic section. Next code lecture adds the LLM API call to the template."},{"h":"6 Where to Post, and How","a":"6-where-to-post-and-how","text":"| What | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| Spec, template screenshot, error log, setup questions | Discord, code track channel channel link |\n| North Star or observation feedback | Discord, support ticket (so it can be tracked) |\n| Assignment 1 submission (due Fri 9 Oct) | The submission form form link plus the assignments channel |"},{"h":"A good help post has exactly three parts, in this order","a":"a-good-help-post-has-exactly-three-parts-in-this-order","text":"Error (exact text) What I tried Screenshot\n\nThen paste it in the code track channel\n\nhelp post · preview\n\n```\n\n```\n\nStuck longer than 20 minutes: post.Pair with another first-time coder; swap screens. Do not wait for the next office hour."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/code-track-troubleshooting.md","title":"Troubleshooting: Building UI","meta":"title: Troubleshooting: Building UI\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/troubleshooting\nfile: c8/troubleshooting.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Troubleshooting · Lecture 3: Building UI · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 322","sections":[{"h":"Troubleshooting: when the app will not run","a":"troubleshooting-when-the-app-will-not-run","text":"Python not found, Gradio not installed: you will hit these. Solving them yourself is the start of the debugging muscle.\n\nHow to read an error\n\nThe machine does not guess. Read the last line of the traceback, find it below, try the fix once. Log the exact error, what you tried and what fixed it."},{"h":"01 The Errors You Should Expect","a":"01-the-errors-you-should-expect","text":"**python is not recognised / command not found**\n\nCause\n\nPATH not set during install (Windows), or Mac uses python3.\n\nFix\n\nWindows: reinstall and tick Add Python to PATH. Mac: use `python3` and `pip3`.\n\n**No module named gradio**\n\nCause\n\npip installed into a different Python than the one running.\n\nFix\n\npython -m pip install gradio\n\nSo pip and python match, then run again.\n\n**Address already in use / port 7860**\n\nCause\n\nThe previous app is still running.\n\nFix\n\nClose the old terminal or press `Ctrl`+`C` in it, then run again.\n\n**IndentationError / SyntaxError**\n\nCause\n\nA line was pasted with wrong spacing or a missing quote or bracket.\n\nFix\n\nRe-copy the template exactly; Python counts the spaces at the start of a line.\n\n**Browser shows nothing**\n\nCause\n\nThe URL was retyped wrong, or the terminal was closed.\n\nFix\n\nClick the link the terminal printed; keep that terminal open while you test.\n\n**def, return and functions are unfamiliar**\n\nCause\n\nLectures do not teach Python syntax.\n\nFix\n\nFinish the [Python bigbinary prerequisite](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) first."},{"h":"02 Still Stuck","a":"02-still-stuck","text":"| Under 20 minutes in | Read the last line of the traceback, check the list above, try the fix once. |\n| --- | --- |\n| Same error after 20 minutes | Post in the code track channel on Discord, not general: the exact error, what you tried, a screenshot. |\n| You fixed something | Reply in the thread. Your fix is someone else's blocker. |\n\nFirst-time coders: pair up, and keep the [beginner kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/beginner-kit) open."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-01-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Orientation and State of AI 2026","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Orientation and State of AI 2026\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes.html\nlecture: 1\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 1\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2701","sections":[{"h":"The verifier is the job","a":"the-verifier-is-the-job","text":"From a failed project in 2023 to the three boxes that organise the next eight weeks. One story, four fixes that failed, two reports, the money, then a game that derived the module. Come back to sections 08 to 12 before you build anything.\n\nSat 19 Sep 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiBoth tracks"},{"h":"01 Where we stood: week 0, two of three done","a":"01-where-we-stood-week-0-two-of-three-done","text":"> Sketch: Assignment 1 in three steps: observation found, ticked; discovery run, ticked; MVP plan, still to do. 21 days, counting from the workshop.\n>\n> Caption: Week 0, \"what to build\", picked up where the workshop left it.\n\n- **56 unique industries in the room.** That is the design constraint: \"what to build\" cannot be taught as a lecture, only learned through exercises.\n- **A hand raise:** most had found an observation and run discovery. The plan was still to generate."},{"h":"02 One sentence from an editor, 2023","a":"02-one-sentence-from-an-editor-2023","text":"> Sketch: A bar chart of time saved. The CEO's bar is 10 percent of P&L, drawn as a green dashed line. The project achieved 2 percent after eight months. Below, the editor's sentence: this is good, but I still have to spend my time on it.\n>\n> Caption: About 8 points under target. The CEO and the editors were both right.\n\n- **The client.** A publicly traded media company, the world's third largest newspaper by distribution, tens of thousands of editors. December 2022, a month after ChatGPT.\n- **The brief, fuzzy by design.** Walk the floor, find what AI can do, build it, give us a number. Metric: time saved. Threshold: double-digit impact on the P&L.\n- **Eight months.** An internal Perplexity on GPT-3.5 for research, and an editing workflow that turned research into an exact 200-word column.\n- **Result: 2%.** The CEO said it was not significant. The editors said the sentence above."},{"h":"03 The room diagnoses it","a":"03-the-room-diagnoses-it","text":"> Sketch: Three diagnoses from the chat, each marked true: wrong metric, it must land on the P&L; incomplete brief, the norm, completing it is the skill; stakeholders, top and ground saw different problems. None was the load-bearing failure.\n>\n> Caption: A patient describes symptoms; the doctor diagnoses. CEOs give incomplete briefs because they are time-poor and vendor-rich.\n\n- **Wrong metric** (Vineet, Shubham). Work that neither saves cost nor raises revenue does not get paid for.\n- **Incomplete problem statement** (Purna, Satyaki). True, and normal. Completing it is the skill.\n- **Real stakeholders, bottom-up adoption** (Krishnan, Arjit Gupta). Both landed."},{"h":"04 Killing the four easy answers","a":"04-killing-the-four-easy-answers","text":"> Sketch: Four easy answers crossed out, each with its reason. A better model: benchmarks saturate, 2 would become 5, not 20. Better prompts: the editors could not state the instruction. More data: the fine-tune did worse, catastrophic forgetting. More time: more output at the same standard.\n>\n> Caption: Each fix was real. None sat where the failure was.\n\n- **A better model.** SWE-bench Verified went from about 60% to near 100% in a year (Stanford HAI AI Index 2026), yet engineering hiring rose and FDE roles grew about 1,000%. A benchmark measures a task, not a job.\n- **Better prompts.** A prompt encodes an instruction. The editors could not state theirs: a good front page is \"attention grabbing\", \"relatable\". Ask a marketer of ten years what makes a good hook: \"It should stop the scroll.\" Expertise settles into the subconscious.\n- **More data.** Month eight: fine-tune on years of published articles. It performed worse. Full fine-tuning rewrites the weights where old knowledge lived; a new behaviour costs an old one. That is **catastrophic forgetting**, covered in the fine-tuning lecture.\n- **More time.** Time without a standard produces more output at the same standard."},{"h":"05 What was actually missing","a":"05-what-was-actually-missing","text":"> Sketch: An editor with a thought bubble: what is a good article, never written down. On the right, two bars of about equal length: writing it yourself, and checking the AI draft. About the same time, so writing it yourself is rational.\n>\n> Caption: The workflows were fine. Judgment was the bottleneck, and judgment had no written form.\n\n- **Only the editors knew what a good article looked like,** and nobody had written it down. They lacked the vocabulary; the instructor, who had never published one, was the least qualified to write it.\n- **So checking cost as much as writing.** A 200 to 500 word article checked for mistakes and facts is, by the end, an article written.\n\n> The Verifier's Law: if you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system, and you cannot claim the capability.\n\nThat is why domain expertise is valuable in 2026: the expert is the only source of the verifier."},{"h":"06 Same failure at scale: two reports","a":"06-same-failure-at-scale-two-reports","text":"> Sketch: A line chart from 2025 to 2026. Model capability, in purple, climbs from about 60 percent on SWE-bench to near 100. EBIT impact, in coral, stays flat at 37 percent of organisations reporting any.\n>\n> Caption: Capability climbs; enterprise EBIT stays flat.\n\n| Source | Finding |\n| --- | --- |\n| McKinsey, The State of AI 2026 (25 Aug 2026, 1,719 respondents) | 80% say AI improved their own productivity. 37% report any positive EBIT contribution, flat on 2025. 6% are high performers (5%+ of EBIT from AI), also flat. |\n| Stanford HAI, AI Index 2026 | SWE-bench Verified about 60% to near 100%. 88% of organisations have adopted AI. US private AI investment $285.9B in 2025. Agent deployment across business functions still in single digits. |"},{"h":"07 Follow the money","a":"07-follow-the-money","text":"> Sketch: A timeline of 2026. April to May, Google Cloud: FDE roles across 8 countries. 4 May, Anthropic's Ode: 1.5 billion dollars, about 100 engineers. 11 May, OpenAI: 4 billion plus, about 150 FDEs. September, Accenture and Google: up to 1,000 FDEs. Over 5.5 billion dollars in one quarter, on people, not models.\n>\n> Caption: Five such units in under four months, all paying for engineers inside enterprises.\n\n- **The 6% who captured value** built Claude Code, Cursor, Lovable, Codex. In every case engineers who knew what good code looks like had written it down, so the model could tell right from wrong and the loop ran fast. No secret sauce; a written verifier.\n- **4 May.** Anthropic with Blackstone, Hellman & Friedman and Goldman Sachs forms a $1.5B services firm, later named Ode. Goldman: having the model alone does not change how you operate.\n- **11 May.** OpenAI launches a deployment company, $4B+ from 19 investors, about 150 FDEs on day one. Its CTO: never a greater gap between what models can do and what enterprises use them for.\n- **April to May.** Google Cloud's Thomas Kurian: the era of the pilot is over.\n- **September.** Accenture and Google Cloud, up to 1,000 FDEs for Gemini Enterprise. The fifth such unit in under four months.\n\n> It is not a technology problem. Most experts have never written down how they judge, so every output routes through them."},{"h":"08 What a verifier looks like: Ogilvy in 15 criteria","a":"08-what-a-verifier-looks-like-ogilvy-in-15-criteria","text":"> Sketch: A loop. The model writes a draft. Your verifier, 15 Ogilvy criteria, scores it. If the score is not 100, improve and re-score, back to the model. If it is, ship. A model with tools in a loop against a success condition is an agent.\n>\n> Caption: Loop engineering: verifier, threshold, improve, re-score, stop.\n\n- **A verifier is a written judgment of how you judge something.** In 1959 David Ogilvy wrote the Rolls-Royce headline still called the best: *At 60 miles an hour the loudest noise in this new Rolls-Royce comes from the electric clock.* In 1983 he wrote down every rule behind how he judged copy. That book is why Ogilvy & Mather runs without him.\n- **Compressed into one prompt.** For the first 100x website: score the copy out of 100 on 15 Ogilvy-inspired criteria (positioning, unique benefit, headline, reader focus, tone, simple language, evidence, emotion, story and more). A dead expert's judgment, still useful.\n- **Then loop it.** Give the verifier, set the threshold, and let the model score, improve and re-score until it gets there. That is an **agent**; designing the loop is **loop engineering**.\n\n> Do not ask the LLM to write your verifier. It returns the average of every expert, and the same slop anyone gets. Start with the task in your own job where you spend the most time judging."},{"h":"09 The verify game, round one: an app that adds two numbers","a":"09-the-verify-game-round-one-an-app-that-adds-two-numbers","text":"> Sketch: Input, two numbers; process, add; output, the sum. Below, the golden dataset: 1 plus 2 gives 3, 3 plus 2 gives 5. Anyone who can read can verify.\n>\n> Caption: No calculator allowed. Know the process, define the input and output, write the ideal pairs.\n\n- **The rule of the game:** write a criterion you could hand to a stranger or a model so they can verify the thing without you.\n- **Know the process yourself.** You cannot verify addition unless you know what it is. Expertise is the precondition.\n- **Define the input** (two numbers, not a cat and a dog) and **the output** (the sum, every time).\n- **Write ideal input-output pairs.** That set has a name: the **golden dataset**. Every ML engineer at the labs has built one.\n- **Input, process, output** will be asked in every lecture from here. Most blocked questions are blocked because one of the three is unclear."},{"h":"10 Round two: has someone cleared the high school exam?","a":"10-round-two-has-someone-cleared-the-high-school-exam","text":"> Sketch: Two inputs: X, the pass mark, 33 percent; Y, the score, 80 percent. Process: is Y at least X? Output: yes. The threshold changes by board; the rule does not.\n>\n> Caption: Neha's answer, as it went on the board. Another rule-based verifier."},{"h":"11 Round three: a million-dollar refund","a":"11-round-three-a-million-dollar-refund","text":"> Sketch: The rule: if the cost of issues Y exceeds the car's cost X, refund. A coral question underneath: who supplies Y, and was it engineered a day before the warranty ended? On the right, a support executive with ten years of refunds, accountable if wrong, asked whether the story smells right. The output is binary: yes or no.\n>\n> Caption: A rule cannot verify this. Judgment can.\n\n- **The case.** Should a Rolls-Royce customer get a refund? About 7.5 crore in India, close to a million dollars. The output is yes or no.\n- **Varun's rule.** Inputs: the car's cost X and the cost of issues within warranty Y. If Y exceeds X, refund. Same shape as the exam rule.\n- **The counter.** The comparison is trivial; getting the numbers is the work. Shipped as written, the rule can be gamed.\n- **Judgment-based verification.** Before the final output, ask a support executive who has done refunds for ten years and answers for mistakes. Same input, different output depending on whether the story smells right.\n- **Judgment has two sources:** a human, or an artificial one. Some transfers from human to model once written down, as Ogilvy's did. Some does not."},{"h":"12 The three boxes: the next eight weeks","a":"12-the-three-boxes-the-next-eight-weeks","text":"> Sketch: Three rounds, three labels. Verifiable by a rule: deterministic, weeks 1 to 4. By judgment: probabilistic, weeks 4 to 8. By nothing yet: human in the loop, the last 4 weeks. Together they are AI system design, turning week 0's inputs into outputs that get the job done.\n>\n> Caption: Every lecture from here is one box, or the bridge between two.\n\n| Verifiable by | System | What we build with |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A rule | Deterministic | Python on the code track; a visual builder with Rahul on no-code. Interfaces, APIs, UIs, databases, connected to LLMs. |\n| Judgment | Probabilistic | LLM deep dive. Verifiers as prompts and eval criteria. MCP, RAG, fine-tuning. |\n| Nothing yet | Human in the loop | Agents, artificial and human, and a process for finding which tasks do not transfer. |\n\n> Ask this every time: how do I verify whether this output is correct? No answer, no build."},{"h":"13 From the Q&A","a":"13-from-the-qa","text":"> Sketch: Your rule in English goes to an LLM, which writes Python, the code. Your golden pairs become the unit tests that check the code. The compiler catches syntax, not misread judgment.\n>\n> Caption: Writing code may no longer be the job. Reviewing it is.\n\n- **Why agents and Python, if a script would do?** (Varun) For a fixed process with a fixed verifier, state the rule and let the LLM write the Python. Then: how do you verify the translation? Ambiguity becomes a bug, and unreliable things do not reach production. To verify the code you must read the language. That is the case against vibe coding: if an app costs no effort, a billion people can build it.\n- **Deterministic MVP first?** (Neha) Yes. You control everything in a deterministic system. Nobody, the labs included, controls a probabilistic output, only its distribution. One of the busiest teams at OpenAI builds no product and writes evals.\n- **Does no-code skip the logic?** (Nishant) No. Combined lectures carry the principles; Rahul's sessions carry the same logic. Code is paying upfront for control, like owning a car instead of renting.\n- **Which tool for a second brain?** (Devang) No tool recommendation, on principle. Bring the observation, the North Star and how success is measured; the tool follows.\n- **Already built something?** (Jagdeep) Run the exercise on it anyway: observation, hypothesis, what was built. Track switching (Arjit) goes to Rahul at the no-code orientation."},{"h":"14 What to do next","a":"14-what-to-do-next","text":"1. **Finish Assignment 1 step three.** Generate the plan and publish your MVP tracker. Due Fri 9 Oct. No observation worth building? Look at what you live now: your job, your peers, this cohort's routine.\n2. **Name your high-judgment task.** Where you spend the most time judging rather than doing. One line: input, process, output.\n3. **Write three golden pairs by hand.** Three inputs with outputs you would accept, plus one you would reject and why. No LLM in this step.\n4. **Run the stranger test.** Hand the pairs and the rule to someone outside your field. If they cannot verify an output without asking you, it is not a verifier yet.\n\nReferences from the room: McKinsey, *The State of AI 2026*; Stanford HAI, *AI Index 2026*; David Ogilvy, *Ogilvy on Advertising* (1983), read for the principles; OpenCode, an open-source alternative to Claude Code.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Verifier**: A written judgment of how you decide an output is good, usable by a stranger or a model without you.\n- **The Verifier's Law**: If you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system, and you cannot claim the capability.\n- **Input, process, output**: The first principle of building anything.\n- **Golden dataset**: Your ideal input-output pairs, written down. Test set for a rule; unit tests for the code.\n- **Rule-based verification**: Same input, same output, checkable by anyone who can read the rule.\n- **Judgment-based verification**: Writable and handable, but the output may vary for the same input.\n- **Deterministic system**: Verifiable by a rule.\n- **Probabilistic system**: Output varies for the same input; LLMs. You control the input, never the output.\n- **Human in the loop**: Where judgment cannot be articulated, so cannot transfer to a model.\n- **Agent**: A model doing a job with tools, in a loop, against a defined success condition.\n- **Loop engineering**: Designing that loop: verifier, threshold, improve, re-score, stop.\n- **Catastrophic forgetting**: Full fine-tuning overwrites weights holding prior knowledge.\n- **FDE**: Forward-deployed engineer: placed inside an enterprise to close the capability-to-value gap.\n\nKeep this\n\nThe gap between what models can do and what businesses earn from them is not a capability gap. It is a verifier gap. If you cannot write down how you judge an output well enough that a stranger, or a model, could apply it, you cannot build a system for that task. Every lecture from here is one of three boxes: verifiable by a rule, by judgment, or not at all."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-01-overview.md","title":"Orientation and State of AI 2026","meta":"title: Orientation and State of AI 2026\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/index.html\nlecture: 1\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 1 · Sat 19 Sep 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 177","sections":[{"h":"Orientation and State of AI 2026","a":"orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026","text":"The verifier is the job.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nThe gap between what models can do and what businesses earn from them is a verifier gap. Before you build anything, know how you will verify the output: by a rule, by judgment, or not at all.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [One sentence from an editor, 2023](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s02)\n2. [Killing the four easy answers](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s04)\n3. [The Verifier's Law](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s05)\n4. [Same failure at scale: two reports](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s06)\n5. [Ogilvy in 15 criteria](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s08)\n6. [The verify game, in three rounds](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s09)\n7. [The three boxes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s12)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"Your high-judgment task in one line, three golden pairs written by hand plus one you would reject, and a stranger who could verify an output without asking you.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Office hour, 22 Sep: tools change, the foundation stays](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/office-hour)\n- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes)\n- Lecture 1 on the map\n\nBefore you build, know how you will verify.Next: Lecture 2, what is an interface, Fri 25 Sep."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-02-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: What Is an Interface","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: What Is an Interface\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes.html\nlecture: 2\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 2\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3054","sections":[{"h":"The first interface","a":"the-first-interface","text":"An interface derived from a game the room lost twice, then tested on a phone call, a letter, a meme and ChatGPT. Read sections 03 to 05 slowly: everything after applies one idea. Come back to section 11 before the API lecture.\n\nFri 25 Sep 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiBoth tracks"},{"h":"01 One principle, three kinds of verification","a":"01-one-principle-three-kinds-of-verification","text":"> Sketch: Three bins. Checkable by a rule: deterministic, code and no-code, weeks 1 to 4. Checkable by judgment a stranger can apply: probabilistic, handed to an LLM. Not verifiable: specific knowledge, a human stays in the loop, weeks 8 to 12. Below: input, process, output.\n>\n> Caption: Last week's game, sorted. Everything derives from input, process, output.\n\n- **A rule.** Same input, same output. Build it with what already exists: code and no-code tools. Weeks 1 to 4.\n- **Judgment.** No rule, but you can extract the judgment and hand it to a stranger who then judges as well as you. That judgment can now go to an LLM.\n- **Not at all.** What Naval calls [specific knowledge](https://nav.al/specific-knowledge): what you do better than anyone and cannot pass on. A human stays in the loop, weeks 8 to 12."},{"h":"02 The summit map: two mountains, four bridges","a":"02-the-summit-map-two-mountains-four-bridges","text":"> Sketch: A summit route over two mountains, deterministic and probabilistic, then locked agents. Four flags: Assignment 1 at base camp, the hackathon after deterministic, Assignment 2 after probabilistic, the final capstone after agents. You are the input, the North Star is the output, 12 weeks is the process.\n>\n> Caption: The four bridges are the only mandatory parts.\n\nThe same principle, applied to the cohort, on the 100x roadmap. Base camp is week 0; [Assignment 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1) is the first bridge.\n\n| Stage | What happens there | Bridge out |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Deterministic | Orientation, interfaces, your first UI, APIs, database, launch. An end-to-end system deployed by about 17 October. | The hackathon |\n| Probabilistic | Models, tool calling, MCP, retrieval, then fine-tuning and data preparation. | Assignment 2 |\n| Agents (locked) | Opens only for those who finish Assignments 1, 2 and the hackathon. About 6 lectures, then 4 weeks of capstone. | Final capstone |\n\nFrom Lecture 3 the tracks split: code in Python with Siddhant, no-code with Rahul. Same destination, different vehicle. Hackathons bring the biggest transformation and the biggest drop-off. Expect both."},{"h":"03 Round one: a hundred programmers, one machine, no smiley","a":"03-round-one-a-hundred-programmers-one-machine-no-smiley","text":"> Sketch: Many people on the left send messages through one Zoom chat to Siddhant acting as a machine at a whiteboard. The whiteboard stays empty: no smiley. Rules: one action per message, in order, no guessing.\n>\n> Caption: Two minutes. An old-fashioned machine: no AI, no guessing.\n\nSiddhant became a machine for two minutes. The goal: get him to draw a smiley on his whiteboard. Pasting an emoji or an image did not count.\n\n- **Input.** Instructions only from the Zoom chat.\n- **Execution.** One action per message, in the exact order messages arrive. Two people sending different actions: both run, in sequence.\n- **No guessing.** An unclear, incorrect or unknown instruction produces nothing.\n\nMore than 100 people failed. The room's diagnosis: no coordination, instructions not detailed enough, no one leading. Siddhant added the one nobody offered: no one asked the machine which language it understands.\n\n> When a deterministic machine fails, the fault is almost always on our side: an instruction we did not articulate, or a team that did not coordinate. Debugging is mostly finding which."},{"h":"04 Round two: a common language, still no smiley","a":"04-round-two-a-common-language-still-no-smiley","text":"> Sketch: Round two had a command list. Three failures: go to 0, but where is 0,0; pick a pen, which is English, not a command; and a hundred voices at once, no coordination.\n>\n> Caption: Far more specific than round one, and it still failed.\n\n- **An underspecified command.** \"Go to 0\". The command took X and Y, with 0,0 at a corner Siddhant had chosen. Nobody asked where; someone could have probed with small coordinates until the cursor reached the toolbar.\n- **Commands the machine never had.** \"Pick a pen\" is English. \"Circle R\" is not a command either: \"circle 5\" draws a circle of radius 5. Three messages saying \"circle\" draw three identical circles.\n- **Too many programmers.** One machine taking orders from a hundred people does not finish the task."},{"h":"05 Two systems, and the bridge between them","a":"05-two-systems-and-the-bridge-between-them","text":"> Sketch: Two boxes, the room of humans and the machine. Between them a bridge made of two planks: a common language, and a protocol, the rules both sides follow. Together they are the interface, and the channel runs both ways.\n>\n> Caption: Language plus protocol. The most important definition for the rest of the cohort.\n\nThe game, redrawn as two boxes. Two things were missing between them: a **common language**, which round two supplied, and a set of rules. The subtle failure: the rules covered only the machine.\n\nThe winning version: pick one point of contact. Everyone else sends suggestions to that person, and only they write in the chat. Go to 0,0; nothing happens; adjust; reach the toolbar; click; draw. The agreed rules are a **protocol**. Language plus protocol opens a channel that runs both ways. That bridge is an **interface**.\n\n> An interface is the boundary, bridge, or point of interaction where two separate systems, components, or entities meet and communicate."},{"h":"06 The phone call: the wire is not the interface","a":"06-the-phone-call-the-wire-is-not-the-interface","text":"> Sketch: One person in Germany speaking German, one in India speaking Hindi, joined by a phone wire with every part working. No communication happens. The hardware is part of the interface, not the whole of it.\n>\n> Caption: Every part the room named is still there.\n\nThe room answered with parts: phone line, cable, network, signal, speaker, microphone. Fix the language (both speak German) and there are still rules: one says hello, the other replies, each works out why the other called. The hardware is needed. It is part of the interface, not the whole of it."},{"h":"07 The letter: find the two systems first","a":"07-the-letter-find-the-two-systems-first","text":"> Sketch: A sender and a receiver, joined by the postal service. Inside the postal service: envelope, address, stamp, fee, delivery. The address alone is not the interface; the postal service is.\n>\n> Caption: The two systems are not in the picture. Name them first.\n\nAnswers: envelope, letter, address, stamp, PIN code, even a pigeon. The method: name the two systems before anything else. A letter goes from a sender to a receiver: two people, with pen and paper instead of a phone. Common language: say Hindi. Rules: envelope, address, post office, stamp, fee, wait. Every answer typed in chat is in there, as a component.\n\nThe one system doing all of it is the postal service. It sets the rules and runs the delivery. The postal service can be called an interface; the address never can."},{"h":"08 The meme as an interface","a":"08-the-meme-as-an-interface","text":"> Sketch: A two-panel template: rejecting a 50-page document, approving one meme that explains it in 5 seconds. Shared context is the common language; the fixed panels are the rules.\n>\n> Caption: Two images: the I, Robot scene, and the Drake template.\n\nIn the I, Robot scene Will Smith asks a robot why it is so often biased and wrong; it answers, because you built and trained me. The Drake template rejects a 50-page document and approves one meme. The answer is **meme**.\n\n- **Common language:** shared context. You laugh hardest if you have seen the scene.\n- **Rules:** the template's fixed panels. Accusation, mirror, realization; or rejection, approval.\n- **Why it spreads.** Richard Dawkins coined the word in *The Selfish Gene*: a unit of culture passed by imitation. It activates context the audience already has, so one image replaces an essay. Siddhant's claim: one of the highest-bandwidth cognitive interfaces ever invented."},{"h":"09 ChatGPT: the screen is the first interface","a":"09-chatgpt-the-screen-is-the-first-interface","text":"> Sketch: A human, the ChatGPT screen, and an LLM running on a machine. The prompt goes in, the reply comes out. The screen is the graphical user interface, the UI. In the game, the Zoom chat was the UI.\n>\n> Caption: Systems, input and output first. Then the interface can be named.\n\nAnswers ranged from natural language to \"the entire screen\". Method first: the two systems are a human and an LLM (software that must run on a machine). Input: the prompt. Output: the reply. Common language: English. Rules: recall the game, where one tool, the Zoom chat, received input and passed it across.\n\nSoftware calls its customers users (software and drugs are the only two industries that do). The interface between a user and a machine, made of graphics you click, type into and touch, is a **graphical user interface**, now simply the **user interface** (UI). In ChatGPT, the whole screen is. This is the first interface."},{"h":"10 Behind the screen: the Inspect tool","a":"10-behind-the-screen-the-inspect-tool","text":"> Sketch: Chrome's Network tab after typing hey and pressing send: a burst of entries, JavaScript, a conversation request naming the model, data coming back, frame.html, CSS files, and images after a refresh.\n>\n> Caption: Right click, Inspect, Network, clear, send.\n\nMost people think the screen is all of ChatGPT. It is only the part that talks to you. You do not need to read the Network tab yet. The point: pressing send makes your machine send information on your behalf, at several levels: you to machine, machine to browser, browser to internet provider, and on to ChatGPT.\n\n> If you cannot identify the systems behind an existing app, you cannot build one like it."},{"h":"11 The boxes: where the UI actually sits","a":"11-the-boxes-where-the-ui-actually-sits","text":"> Sketch: A stack, left to right: the machine, a MacBook; through the operating system to the browser, Chrome; to the UI, ChatGPT's screen; then over the internet to the company's machine at OpenAI. The UI is the last of three layers you pass through.\n>\n> Caption: Start from the minimum conditions to open ChatGPT at all. That fixes the order.\n\n| Box | What it is | Example | Interface into it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The machine | MacBook Pro, iPhone, Android phone, any laptop | The **operating system**: macOS, iOS, Android |\n| 2 | The browser: an application, not the UI | Chrome, Safari, Firefox | Command lines between machine and browser |\n| 3 | The UI | ChatGPT's screen | The graphical user interface |\n| 4 | The company's machine | OpenAI's machine, serving ChatGPT and the model | Section 12 |\n\nA user never touches the UI directly. A keystroke becomes an electrical signal; the operating system lets you use the hardware; the machine opens the browser; the browser fetches the app; only then is there a UI. This applies to every app on the internet."},{"h":"12 The second machine, and the second interface","a":"12-the-second-machine-and-the-second-interface","text":"> Sketch: Your MacBook with macOS, and OpenAI's machine with Linux on NVIDIA hardware. chatgpt.com is converted to an IP address, the address on the envelope. Different OS, different hardware: the bridge between them is the API.\n>\n> Caption: Two machines that may share nothing.\n\nTyping chatgpt.com starts a trip. Your internet provider confirms a connection, then the name becomes an **IP address**: numbers, the most precise language machines share. It is the address on the envelope, and it leads to another machine, owned by OpenAI, listening for requests. The full routing: [how DNS works](https://howdns.works/).\n\nSiddhant's is a MacBook Pro; OpenAI's might run Linux on NVIDIA hardware. Neither understands the other. The room named the bridge: the **API**, Application Programming Interface, the machine-to-machine interface."},{"h":"13 HTML, HTTP, and the rules the room never wrote","a":"13-html-http-and-the-rules-the-room-never-wrote","text":"> Sketch: Binary at the bottom, the one language every machine shares; Python, C++, Java and JavaScript above it. For the web: HTML is the common language, HTTP is the protocol. A request goes out, a response comes back, and every request stands on its own.\n>\n> Caption: Tim Berners-Lee, CERN: a global hypertext system.\n\nEvery machine, from laptops to AirPods, shares one language: **binary**. High-level languages sit on top and map down to it. Before the internet, every machine had its own language, so someone had to agree on a common one.\n\nFor the web that was Tim Berners-Lee at CERN. He built **HTML** (Hypertext Markup Language) as the common language for structuring pages, and **HTTP** (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) as the rules for moving them. Hypertext links to another page; linking every page to every other became the world wide web.\n\nIn the Network tab, the first entry after a refresh is a request (URL, method, status code) and a response, originally always an HTML page. One rule is built in: every request stands on its own, so the receiver needs no backstory. That is the protocol the room never wrote: what to send, who sends it, in what form, and how the response is read."},{"h":"14 One abstraction, many APIs","a":"14-one-abstraction-many-apis","text":"> Sketch: API is the broad term. Over the internet: HTTP API. Saving files to storage: a file API such as FTP. Even your keyboard and your operating system.\n>\n> Caption: The kind depends on the environment.\n\nAny two machines communicating need an API. Over the internet it is an **HTTP API**; you build your first one soon. The definition, applied six times:\n\n| Two systems | Common language | Rules | The interface |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| The room and Siddhant as machine | The round two commands | One action per message, in order; nothing for the humans | The Zoom chat, as UI |\n| Two people on a phone | German | Hello, wait, reply, state the reason | Language, etiquette and hardware together; never the wire alone |\n| Sender and receiver of a letter | Hindi | Envelope, address, stamp, fee, delivery | The postal service |\n| A meme maker and an audience | Shared context | The template's fixed panels | The meme |\n| A human and an LLM | English and about 94 others | Type, click, send, read | The GUI: the whole screen |\n| Your MacBook and OpenAI's machine | Binary, then HTML | HTTP | Web UI and HTTP API |"},{"h":"15 From the Q&A","a":"15-from-the-qa","text":"> Sketch: Siddhant's first app was built for Android. Most users turned out to be on iOS: different OS, different hardware, so the interface had to be built differently.\n>\n> Caption: Layers below the UI decide what the UI can do.\n\n- **Do layers 1 and 2 matter if I only build the UI?** (Neha Agrawal) Sometimes not; often yes. For deeper hardware control, they decide what your UI can and cannot do.\n- **Is the common language the interface?** (Swasti) No. Establishing it is step one of building one.\n- **Where do binary and Python come in?** (Saiteja) Binary is the one language a macOS and a Linux machine are guaranteed to share. Assembly maps a handful of commands to zeros and ones, like the round two list; each language above abstracts further, up to English.\n- **Do I need HTML and CSS for the code track?** (Mohit) No. Python throughout, with libraries that translate it into HTML, CSS and JavaScript. The one exception is SQL, in the database lecture.\n- **How do I find 10 people for my manual MVP?** (Hardik) Finding them is the exercise. If you cannot find 5 to 10 people for whom the problem is painful, pick a different observation.\n- **My hypothesis keeps changing.** Good. If it is not changing, you are not learning. Log each change and its cause; the log is part of a good Assignment 1."},{"h":"16 What to do next","a":"16-what-to-do-next","text":"1. **Replay round two properly.** Using only the command list, write the full ordered sequence one point of contact would have sent to draw the smiley. Every message must be a valid command.\n2. **Inspect one app you use daily.** Inspect, Network, clear, do one action, screenshot. Name three kinds of file that came back.\n3. **Draw the boxes for that app.** Machine, operating system, browser, UI, the company's machine. Label the interface between each pair; leave a question mark where you do not know yet.\n4. **Code track: finish the [Big Binary Python](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) exercise.** The one prerequisite for Lecture 3.\n5. **Keep [Assignment 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1) moving** (due Fri 9 Oct), and log every change to your observation and hypothesis, with what caused it.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Specific knowledge**: What you do better than anyone and cannot hand to a stranger as a rule or a judgment.\n- **Common language**: A vocabulary both systems understand. Step one of an interface, not the interface.\n- **Protocol**: The rules both sides follow: who speaks, in what order, in what form.\n- **Interface**: The boundary, bridge, or point of interaction where two separate systems meet and communicate.\n- **GUI, UI**: The interface between a user and a machine, made of graphics you click, type into and touch.\n- **Operating system**: The interface that lets a human use a machine's hardware.\n- **IP address**: The numeric address of a machine on the internet; the envelope's address for websites.\n- **API**: The interface between two machines. HTTP API when the medium is the internet.\n- **HTML**: The common language for structuring web pages.\n- **HTTP**: The protocol for requests and responses between machines on the web.\n- **Binary**: Zeros and ones: the only language every machine shares.\n- **Meme**: A unit of culture spread by imitation: shared context plus a template's fixed rules.\n\nKeep this\n\nAn interface is not a wire, a language, or a screen. It is whatever lets two systems that do not understand each other meet and communicate, and you build one from two things: a common language, and a set of rules both sides follow. The UI is the first interface you meet: between a human and a machine. Behind it sit more systems (hardware, operating system, browser), every pair joined by its own interface."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-02-overview.md","title":"What Is an Interface","meta":"title: What Is an Interface\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/index.html\nlecture: 2\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 2 · Fri 25 Sep 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 172","sections":[{"h":"What is an interface; UI as the first interface","a":"what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface","text":"Two systems, one shared language, and rules both sides keep.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nAn interface lets two systems that do not understand each other communicate. Build it from a common language and a protocol. The UI is the first one: between a human and a machine.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Round one: no smiley](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s03)\n2. [Round two: a common language, still no smiley](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s04)\n3. [Two systems and the bridge](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s05)\n4. [Phone, letter, meme](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s06)\n5. [ChatGPT: the screen is the first interface](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s09)\n6. [The boxes behind the screen](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s11)\n7. [The second machine: API, HTML, HTTP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s12)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"Round two replayed as valid commands, one app inspected in the Network tab, and its boxes drawn with the interface between each pair labelled.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes)\n- Lecture 2 on the map\n\nThe screen is only where you happen to touch it.Next: Lecture 3, building UI, Sat 26 Sep."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: PRD to UI design to frontend app","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: PRD to UI design to frontend app\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 3 · 26 September 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 51f573a\nwords: 2649","sections":[{"h":"Decide what it does, then draw it.","a":"decide-what-it-does-then-draw-it","text":"We took one salon's problem from four questions to a flow, two files, screens in Stitch and an Android app on a phone.\n\nIn [Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes) the screen was the first interface: where a person meets your product. In [First Proof](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1) you tested whether your idea is worth building. Today we took one idea all the way to a screen you can tap, and wrote no code."},{"h":"01 A haircut nobody sees before the cut","a":"01-a-haircut-nobody-sees-before-the-cut","text":"We kept one example all session, a real project: a boutique salon with several branches and no software at all.\n\nEvery client walks in with a reference: a film star, a photo from Pinterest, a saved reel. You say one thing, the barber understands another, and you find out when the haircut is finished. Hair does not grow back overnight, so there is no trial and error.\n\nSo the gap is one sentence: nobody sees the result before the cut. The proposed product closes it.\n\n> Sketch: Six steps in a row. The client brings a reference photo, takes a selfie, and the app makes a preview of the style on the client's own face. The barber and the client look at the preview together, then the haircut happens, then the result is compared with the preview.\n>\n> Caption: The preview and the talk with the barber sit before the haircut. That is the whole product.\n\nThe instructor had already put the idea through the First Proof skill, over two hours of talking with Claude. It left two files and some open risks. Will the barber use it? Will the preview look real enough to trust?\n\nWhat should you settle before you open a design tool?"},{"h":"02 Four questions before any screen","a":"02-four-questions-before-any-screen","text":"The room answered four questions in the chat, about this salon.\n\n| The question | What the room said |\n| --- | --- |\n| Who is the user? | People who need a haircut. Sharper: anxious people. |\n| What problem are we solving? | What you expect against what you get. Not appointments. |\n| What is the most important moment? | The moment the barber makes the first cut. |\n| What would make it a success? | An accurate preview and a satisfied client. |\n\nThe slide then sorted the work into four boxes.\n\n> Sketch: Four boxes in a row joined by arrows. Problem space: the gap, the user, the guess, the open risks. Product decisions: the moment, the barber's role, what we will not build. Solution space: the experience. Screens come last.\n>\n> Caption: Screens are the last box. Three boxes of thinking come first.\n\nWorking through the first three boxes before the fourth has a name: **product thinking**.\n\n> Decide what deserves to exist before you decide what it looks like.\n\nThe product is used by people. Which people?"},{"h":"03 Who touches it, and where","a":"03-who-touches-it-and-where","text":"We listed everyone who touches the product: the client, the barber, the receptionist, the salon manager, the owner, marketing.\n\n> \"If the salon launches this tomorrow, whose behaviour changes the most?\"\n\nThe barber's and the client's. And if the barber ignores the preview, nothing else matters. So the barber has to be inside the flow, not after it.\n\nFor each person we wrote three things: what they need, what they do, and where they meet the product. That place is a **touchpoint**. For the barber: needs the reference photo, cuts to match it, meets the product at the chair.\n\nWhere should it live: a website, a phone app, WhatsApp, a screen in the salon? The instructor chose a website that also works like an app on a phone. People find a style at home and bring it to the salon, so it must work in both places. WhatsApp came second: a long chat buries the details the barber needs.\n\nWe know who and where. What happens, step by step?"},{"h":"04 The flow first, then the screens","a":"04-the-flow-first-then-the-screens","text":"The instructor showed a first rough screen for the salon and asked what was wrong with it. The chat: no images, too much text, too many buttons, no idea which one to press.\n\nThe button that tells you the one thing to do next is the call to action, or **CTA**. That screen had many buttons and no CTA. Nothing looked more important than anything else: it had no hierarchy.\n\nBetter colours would not save it, because the trouble is in what happens. What happens to the user, step by step, is the **UX** (user experience). What it looks like is the UI.\n\n> A good UI cannot rescue a broken experience.\n\nSo we wrote the flow first. You discover a style, select it, take a selfie and generate the preview. Then you review it with the barber, who knows your hair type. Then the flow became screens.\n\n> Sketch: Six screens in order: Home, Style, Camera, Preview, Booking, Profile. Below them, four states every screen can be in: loading, empty, error and success.\n>\n> Caption: The screens fall out of the flow. The four states underneath are the part people forget.\n\nA screen is not one picture. It looks different while the AI is working, when there is nothing to show, when something fails, and when it works. Each is a **state**. What does the client see if the image never comes back?\n\n> A confident wrong preview is worse than showing nothing.\n\nYou now know what each screen must do. How do you tell an AI tool what it should look like?"},{"h":"05 Learn the names of what you see","a":"05-learn-the-names-of-what-you-see","text":"In words, so you need the words. We went through a design handbook in two passes.\n\nFirst, looks. The slide had 13 popular styles, each with a name, and the room matched them to apps. The one called material is Google's. Glass is Apple today. Neo-brutalist is Gumroad. Which is right depends on your users and the apps they open every day. For this salon the room settled on premium and minimal.\n\nSecond, parts. Every screen is built from named parts: a search bar, a primary button, a card, a toggle, a bottom navigation bar. Each one is a **component**. The handbook lists about 40 to 45 common ones.\n\nWhy learn the names? Because \"swap the radio buttons for a toggle\" gets you exactly that, and \"make it nicer\" gets you anything.\n\n> The more exact your words, the more the result is yours.\n\nTo practise, name the components in WhatsApp next time you open it. On a website, the Inspect tool from [Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s10) shows what the developers called each part.\n\nAlso choose phone or laptop. A phone is touched and held upright; a laptop is wide and driven by keyboard and pointer. One interface made for both usually fits neither.\n\nThat is a lot to remember each time you prompt. Where do you keep it?"},{"h":"06 Write it into two files","a":"06-write-it-into-two-files","text":"You keep it in files a tool can read. First the room answered five questions for the salon. Who are the users? What are they trying to do? What should happen? What are the constraints? What should it feel like?\n\nThe first file holds what the product must do. It is the product requirements document, or **PRD**. You do not write it by hand. A second Claude skill, Discovery to PRD, does it with you:\n\n1. You give it your two First Proof files.\n2. It fills the 14 sections of a PRD from them.\n3. It asks you only about the real gaps: what is missing or half covered.\n4. It asks for the look: the logo, the colour palette and reference pictures.\n5. It hands back two files: the PRD and a second file named **design.md**.\n\ndesign.md holds the users, the flows, the screens, the components, the states and the look. It is sections 01 to 05, written down once.\n\n> Sketch: Your two First Proof files go into a Claude skill, which asks about the gaps and returns two files, the PRD and design.md. Both go into Stitch, which draws the screens. The screens go to AI Studio, which makes a working app.\n>\n> Caption: Everything left of the two files is thinking. Everything right of them is a tool reading that thinking."},{"h":"07 Stitch draws the screens","a":"07-stitch-draws-the-screens","text":"[Stitch](https://stitch.withgoogle.com) is Google's tool that draws app screens from a description. It is a test version, so expect rough edges. The room followed along with the instructor's two salon files.\n\n1. Open Stitch and choose App, not Website.\n2. Click the paint palette button, choose \"Start with your design\", and upload design.md.\n3. Upload the logo, and paste the address of the brand's website so Stitch can pick up its look.\n4. Click Continue. Back on the main screen, paste the whole PRD into the box and click Generate.\n5. Copy section 14 of the PRD (the screens, with the user flow), paste it into the chat box at the bottom, and generate again.\n\n**The trouble the room hit.** Someone got only three screens. That is normal after step 4. Section 14 of the salon's PRD lists seven, and step 5 brings the rest. If it still stops at three, ask it to redo the step.\n\nThen we tried the shortcut. No files: one spoken paragraph, straight into Stitch.\n\n> \"I'm building an app for a salon that helps me generate hairstyles even before the barber does the haircut. I give a reference style that I like, and also my selfie...\"\n\nIt drew screens, but they did not look like the salon. With no design.md, Stitch never picked up the brand. Same tool, same idea: the files were the difference."},{"h":"08 The first output is never the design","a":"08-the-first-output-is-never-the-design","text":"The instructor put up Stitch's first screens and asked again: what is wrong? The chat answered in the new words. A lot of text. Too many CTAs. No visual hierarchy.\n\nThen he fed those words back to Stitch, in three ways.\n\n- **By voice.** Stitch has a mode you can talk to. He said: \"Reduce the amount of text. Make it minimal. Focus on visual hierarchy, focus on CTA. Follow the brand guidelines.\" It changed only a couple of screens, so he went back to typing.\n- **By text, on many screens.** Select every screen that has the problem and type once: \"Reduce the amount of text, have a clear CTA.\"\n- **By pointing.** Select one area of one screen, choose edit with AI, and say what you want: \"it needs to be center aligned\".\n\nHis prepared version of the salon app took about five rounds like this. One click also makes a web version of a screen, or its empty state. That is what a screen shows with nothing in it yet, like WhatsApp with no chats.\n\nTo review your own screens, ask four things. Is the hierarchy right? Is the CTA clear? Does each screen lead to the next? Does it look like the brand? Then name three things to keep, three to change and one to remove, and type them in."},{"h":"09 AI Studio makes the screens work","a":"09-ai-studio-makes-the-screens-work","text":"Stitch gives you pictures of screens. [Google AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com) turns them into screens you can tap through: the **front end** of the app.\n\n1. In Stitch, select all your screens and export them as a zip file. Inside is design.md, and for every screen a picture and its code.\n2. In AI Studio, choose the \"Build an Android app\" option before you type anything. Skip it and you get a web app.\n3. Paste section 14 of the PRD again, with one line above it: \"Create an Android mobile app to do this.\"\n4. Attach the picture of each screen from the zip, and design.md.\n5. Click Build and wait. Ours took 5 to 10 minutes; one learner's took 15.\n\n**Why the zip?** Stitch has a button that sends screens straight to AI Studio. For most of the room it carried only one screen. If that happens, use the zip.\n\n**No Install button?** Then you built a web app. Typing \"Android app\" in the prompt is not enough. Start again with the option picked, because the two kinds of app are written in different code.\n\nTo put the app on an Android phone, turn on developer mode. On the instructor's Samsung: Settings, About phone, Software information, then tap \"Build number\" 10 to 15 times. Open Developer options, switch on USB debugging, and connect the phone to your laptop by cable. In AI Studio click \"Install on your device\" and allow it on the phone.\n\nIt ran. He took a selfie, got three looks, picked one and reached \"Ready to cut\". That is the flow from section 04, on a phone.\n\nA change is one more prompt. He took a screenshot of the last screen inside AI Studio and typed one line. \"I want confetti when he clicks on the button start next chair session.\" He installed again, and there was confetti.\n\nEven a change that small takes minutes in an Android app, and spends your free credits.\n\n> Change the design in Stitch. Come to AI Studio when you are happy with it.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**You give Stitch one spoken paragraph about the salon and no files. What will be missing from the screens?**\n\nThe brand. Without design.md, Stitch never picked up the salon's look. The files are how your decisions reach the tool.\n\n**A friend wants to polish the colours in AI Studio, \"because that is the real app\". What is wrong with that?**\n\nEvery change to the Android app takes minutes and spends credits. Settle the design in Stitch first.\n\n**The client takes a selfie and the preview never comes back. Which part of the plan covers that?**\n\nThe states. Decide the loading and error states in advance. Showing nothing beats a confident wrong preview.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Product thinking**: Deciding what deserves to exist before deciding what it looks like.\n- **Touchpoint**: The place where a person meets your product: a phone, a chat, a screen in the shop.\n- **UX**: User experience. What happens to the user, step by step.\n- **CTA**: Call to action. The one button that tells the user what to do next.\n- **State**: One of the ways a screen can look: loading, empty, error or success.\n- **Component**: A named part of a screen, such as a search bar, a card or a toggle.\n- **PRD**: Product requirements document. One file that says what the product must do. Ours has 14 sections.\n- **design.md**: One file that says how the product should look and behave, written so a design tool can read it.\n- **Front end**: The part of an app the user sees and taps.\n\nKeep this\n\nDecide what happens before what it looks like: the user, the moment that matters, the flow, the screens and their states. Write those decisions into two files, a PRD and design.md, and Stitch draws screens that are yours. Fix the design in Stitch, then let AI Studio make it work."},{"h":"10 Your turn: your idea, as far as AI Studio","a":"10-your-turn-your-idea-as-far-as-ai-studio","text":"Do tonight what the room did, with your own idea from First Proof.\n\n1. Run the Discovery to PRD skill in Claude with your two First Proof files.\n2. Generate your screens in Stitch, then keep three things, change three and remove one.\n3. Build it in AI Studio and take a screenshot of it running.\n\nThe instructor's salon files (the PRD, design.md and the Discovery to PRD skill) are on the LMS, under Week 1, PRD TO PRODUCT.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: the app leaves AI Studio and goes into Antigravity."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-03-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building UI, Design to Code","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building UI, Design to Code\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 3 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2884","sections":[{"h":"Building UI: design to code","a":"building-ui-design-to-code","text":"From Aarav's Friday status report to four lines of Gradio, by way of a vote that lost. Read sections 02 to 09 once, slowly, with your own observation open beside them. Come back to 11 to 13 when you sit down to install and run.\n\nSat 26 Sep 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCode track"},{"h":"01 The room check: who had done week 0","a":"01-the-room-check-who-had-done-week-0","text":"> Sketch: The prerequisite: one observation, one hypothesis, one conversation with a real user. More than 10 hands. Until 9 October, try more observations and speak to more people. When building is a commodity, finding users is the part AI cannot do.\n>\n> Caption: More than 10 hands went up.\n\nThe prerequisite was one observation, one hypothesis and one conversation with a real user. Two framing points before any design. You are not married to one observation: until the Assignment 1 deadline (9 October), the more observations you try and people you speak to, the better you get at testing assumptions. And when building becomes a commodity, finding the right users and solving their problem is the part AI cannot yet do."},{"h":"02 Start from one person, not a persona","a":"02-start-from-one-person-not-a-persona","text":"> Sketch: On the left, a crowd labelled with categories, marketers, cafe owners, doctors, crossed out. On the right, one named person: Aarav, business analyst, Pune, three tabs open. Solve for him, then talk to more people like him.\n>\n> Caption: Once it works for one person, talk to more people like them.\n\n- **The observation.** Ambitious early career professionals in urban India arrive AI-aware and motivated, but fail at selection, scoping and first proof. They see AI at the category level and cannot turn it into one grounded workflow. It contains no solution: an observation reports what you saw, and stops.\n- **The hypothesis.** They are stuck because they lack a method to go from broad AI possibility to one scoped workflow. Test: a workflow diagnosis system plus a guided build sprint, and 60% of active students identify one user, map one repeated workflow, build an AI-assisted version and demo it within 90 days. At 10 people, 6 of 10.\n- **The move.** Do not solve for a category. Solve for one named person."},{"h":"03 The cheapest test is three boxes on a whiteboard","a":"03-the-cheapest-test-is-three-boxes-on-a-whiteboard","text":"> Sketch: Three boxes: a text field where Aarav describes a workflow he repeats, a Done button, and a plan that comes back. The test is not whether he likes it: following it must save about 1 hour a week.\n>\n> Caption: No code written, no system designed.\n\nThe hypothesis names two solutions. Take only part one, the diagnosis, and find the cheapest way to test it with Aarav. The input came from a message to him: what is the one thing you repeat every week, in 3 to 4 lines? His reply, compressed, is the input. The output is a plan for automating part of it.\n\nThe success test is not whether Aarav likes the plan. It is whether following it saves him time, say 1 hour a week. If it saves nothing, the plan failed. **Designing** meant understanding what the user needs and the minimum solution that tests it. It does not mean opening Figma."},{"h":"04 Prateek's observation, and the stranger test","a":"04-prateeks-observation-and-the-stranger-test","text":"> Sketch: Prateek's spec, marked. Observation: too generic, an organisation is not a person. Hypothesis: noticeably faster has no number. Input and output: good, specific and concise.\n>\n> Caption: One spec from the chat, marked live.\n\nPrateek's: project knowledge exists, but access depends on personal memory and network, so under deadline people reuse the precedent they remember rather than the best fit.\n\n- **Observation: too generic.** \"Organization\" does not say finance, healthcare or education, and an organization is not a person. Name the one person who hits this most often, with their designation, and write from their point of view.\n- **Hypothesis: no number.** \"Noticeably faster\" cannot be falsified. Add a measurement and a stop rule, or you keep spending time with no signal for when to pivot.\n- **Input and output: good.** Senior experts retrieving a specific fact or project assumption, getting back a concise, source-grounded answer they can check.\n\nThe format to post in the code track channel: observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, input, output."},{"h":"05 Ten golden pairs before one line of code","a":"05-ten-golden-pairs-before-one-line-of-code","text":"> Sketch: A stack of input and output pairs, labelled golden dataset: 10 to 20 to start, 100 to 200 for production, gathered from real users. Beside it, the Lecture 1 check: 1 plus 2 equals 3, same output means it works.\n>\n> Caption: Gathered by talking to real users. If you cannot, do not move ahead.\n\nOne input and one output is an example, not enough to build against. Write at least 10 to 20 pairs: the exact text a real user would type, and the output that would solve their problem. If you are the user, write them yourself and check them with people like you. This is the **golden dataset**; it returns in the probabilistic module for evals and fine-tuning.\n\nRecall the Lecture 1 game: to verify an app that adds two numbers, you write 1 + 2 = 3 and a few more, and compare. The golden dataset is what tells you whether your verifier will be deterministic, probabilistic or a human in the loop."},{"h":"06 The verifier is a threshold, worked in minutes","a":"06-the-verifier-is-a-threshold-worked-in-minutes","text":"> Sketch: A threshold line at 60 minutes a week. The email step: 15 minutes a day by hand. Plan A cuts it to 5, saving 50 minutes a week, close to the line: pass. Plan B cuts it to 10, saving 25 minutes a week: fail.\n>\n> Caption: Same plan shape, same tool. One passes, one fails.\n\nSay the email step is Aarav's biggest time sink: 15 minutes a day by hand. With the plan it takes 5. That saves 10 a day, 50 minutes over a 5-day week, close to the 1 hour bar. The plan passes. If the plan only cuts it to 10 minutes, that is 25 minutes a week, and it fails.\n\n> A good output clears a number you wrote down before you built anything. Without a threshold it is a demo, not impact.\n\nThe written verifier for Aarav's first golden pair asked for four things in the output: a metric with a baseline, the named bottleneck, a test on past input with known answers, and a list of what is out of scope."},{"h":"07 Test-driven development, written in English","a":"07-test-driven-development-written-in-english","text":"> Sketch: Test first, then the software, then pass. The tests are now mostly in English. The spec: observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers.\n>\n> Caption: Only the language changes. The philosophy does not.\n\n**Test-driven development (TDD)** says write the test before the software; once the software passes your codified tests, you know it works. Hard to keep up, so few people do. What changes now is the language: most of your tests will be in English.\n\nAlmost everyone uses AI, utilization sits under 20%, and the impact on real earnings is in single digits. Building without a verifier is how that gap stays wide. Mask anything proprietary (percentages, or X, Y, Z) but share the spec, because that is what makes feedback possible."},{"h":"08 Two systems, one interface","a":"08-two-systems-one-interface","text":"> Sketch: Aarav, a human, and a plan generator that runs on a machine. Between them, a user interface. Text in, text out, which means a manual MVP is possible before building anything.\n>\n> Caption: Lecture 2's question, applied to Aarav.\n\nTwo systems: Aarav, and a workflow plan generator that can run on a machine. Anupma named what sits between: a **user interface**, with a common language (English, text fields) and rules (the web's HTTP). Golden pair 1, written live:\n\n> \"I'm a business analyst at a mid-sized IT company in Pune. Every Friday, I make the weekly status report for our client. I open Jira, filter tickets closed this week, copy them into a Word template, then go through email and Slack to find blockers... Takes around 3 hours. My manager usually edits the risk section because I miss things.\"\n\nInput type: text. Output type: text. So you can run a **manual MVP** before building anything: take Aarav's description on a call, write the plan yourself (with or without ChatGPT), send it, and see if it saves him time."},{"h":"09 The vote that lost","a":"09-the-vote-that-lost","text":"> Sketch: Votes: search box 17, chat 13, form 3. Then Aarav reads the plan and asks: what does backtesting mean? A search box has no memory, so he would retype everything. Chat wins.\n>\n> Caption: The majority choice fell to one realistic follow-up.\n\n| Interface | Votes | What the replay showed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Search box | 17 | No memory, no context. A follow-up means retyping the whole description, and an AI plan may come back different each run. |\n| Form | 3 | Fields Aarav does not need, and no way to ask about the plan once it arrives. |\n| Chat | 13 | Holds context, supports follow-ups until Aarav is satisfied. The winner. |\n\nSiddhant replayed the golden output, a day-by-day build plan with time saved as the metric, and Aarav asked: \"What does backtesting mean?\" How do you ask Google a follow-up? Swasti named the cost: remember the plan, retype, search again. Anupma named the missing property: no memory, no context. Bhavani added that it does not look like a conversation.\n\n> Pick the interface after you replay the user's conversation with your ideal output, never before.\n\nRule of thumb: about 90% of first problems fit one of three. One query, one answer: search. Many inputs: form. Follow-ups that need memory: chat. Build the cheapest one first and add nothing until it is verified. That is the **V0**, or MVP."},{"h":"10 Vibe code or first principles: the difference is control","a":"10-vibe-code-or-first-principles-the-difference-is-control","text":"> Sketch: Two routes to the same chat. Top: one prompt to Claude Code, Lovable or Gemini. Bottom: Python from first principles, knowing what every line does. Both arrive at a working chat. The difference is control.\n>\n> Caption: Siddhant did not say which is better. Do both.\n\nVibe code it with one prompt to Claude Code, Lovable, OpenAI or Gemini. Or write it from first principles in Python, AI allowed, understanding what every line does and why. Both produce a working chat. Tonight's task: do both and write a three-line review of what each gave you."},{"h":"11 Why Python, and why Gradio","a":"11-why-python-and-why-gradio","text":"> Sketch: Before 2021: three languages, HTML, CSS and JavaScript, to ship one thing. In 2026: one language, Python, with Gradio for chat, forms and search, and Streamlit for dashboards.\n>\n> Caption: Python libraries now bridge the gap to the web.\n\nThe web is HTML; styling and scripting grew around it, and Python was never part of it. Until about 2021 Siddhant taught UI in JavaScript, HTML and CSS, and beginners had to learn three languages to ship one thing.\n\n| Library | Use it for |\n| --- | --- |\n| Gradio | Chat, forms, search-style inputs: any interface without heavy charts. Siddhant's default. Part of Hugging Face; AUTOMATIC1111, the Stable Diffusion UI with about 165,000 GitHub stars, runs on it. |\n| Streamlit | Data-heavy front ends: dashboards and charts that update. Comes later. |"},{"h":"12 Four lines that are the whiteboard","a":"12-four-lines-that-are-the-whiteboard","text":"> Sketch: The Gradio Hello World mapped to the whiteboard. The function argument, name, is the input. The function body is the process. What follows return is the output. gr.Interface declares input and output types: the language and the rules.\n>\n> Caption: The smiley exercise, solved for machines.\n\nThe Gradio Hello World takes a name and returns a greeting:\n\n```\nimport gradio as gr\n\ndef greet(name):\n    return \"Hello \" + name\n\ndemo = gr.Interface(fn=greet, inputs=\"text\", outputs=\"text\")\ndemo.launch()\n```\n\nThe function argument is the input. What follows `return` is the output. The body is the **process**, here almost nothing. `gr.Interface` is the interface: it declares input and output types, the common language and the rules. `fn` means function. For chat, swap the interface. Gradio's own example just echoes you:\n\n```\nimport gradio as gr\n\ndef respond(message, history):\n    return \"You typed: \" + message\n\ngr.ChatInterface(fn=respond).launch()\n```\n\nLearn Gradio from [its documentation](https://gradio.app/docs), not from an LLM. Asking Claude gets you an answer; the docs get you the rabbit holes in the left rail (website widgets, Slack bots, Discord bots)."},{"h":"13 Setting up, and the errors you should want tonight","a":"13-setting-up-and-the-errors-you-should-want-tonight","text":"> Sketch: Four steps: install Python, install one IDE, make a folder with app.py, then pip install gradio and python app.py, which prints a local link. Errors on the way are the point.\n>\n> Caption: Solving them yourself starts the debugging muscle.\n\n1. **Install Python** from [python.org](https://www.python.org/downloads/).\n2. **Install one IDE:** Google Antigravity, Cursor or VS Code. An IDE is a notepad for code where you read and review before you push anything anywhere. VS Code is free; Antigravity is free with generous limits; Cursor has a paid plan for its models.\n3. **Make a folder**, open it in the IDE, create `app.py` and paste the Hello World.\n4. **Run** `pip install gradio`, then `python app.py`, and open the local link it prints.\n\nIf `def`, `return` and functions are unfamiliar, finish the [Big Binary Python](https://courses.bigbinaryacademy.com/learn-python/) prerequisite first; lectures will not teach Python syntax. The next office hour reviews your work and your errors, then puts it on a shareable link."},{"h":"14 From the Q&A","a":"14-from-the-qa","text":"> Sketch: Solution first: I am building WhatsApp agents, which leaves nothing to decide by. Observation first: observation, input, output, process, then the interface.\n>\n> Caption: Naming the solution in the first line leaves nothing to decide by.\n\n- **Trackers in, exception table out: form or chat?** (Charu) No answer without the spec. Walk your user's journey the way we walked Aarav's, then ask.\n- **My project is building WhatsApp agents.** (Kandarp) That is solution-first. Start from observation, input, output and process.\n- **My hypothesis changes after every interview.** Then do not build yet. To learn the process, use Aarav's documents. Spend real build hours only on a problem you can test with real people.\n- **First time coding, from marketing.** (Saiteja) You have company: across cohorts the split moved to about 60-40 first-time coders. Siddhant's own start: in a first-year break, one app a day, end to end, on a link, for 30 days. Today's is Hello World.\n- **Not sure my problem is big or monetizable.** (Mayuresh) It does not have to be. A Cohort 6 non-coder built 100+ apps in 3 months and has since raised funding. Volume compounds only if the projects point the same way.\n- **Should the North Star be a product?** No. It is a direction. Most reduce to a better job (define the role) or building something of your own (define what and for whom), six months out."},{"h":"15 What to do next","a":"15-what-to-do-next","text":"1. **Post your spec** in the code track channel: observation, North Star, hypothesis with a number, one named user, input, output.\n2. **Write 10 golden pairs** with a real user, plus one verifier with a baseline and a threshold.\n3. **Replay one pair** and write the follow-up your user will ask. Pick search, form or chat from it.\n4. **Get Hello World running locally** in Gradio, then the chat echo. Log every error and how you fixed it.\n5. **Build the chat twice** (vibe coded and first principles) and write the three-line review.\n\nAll five are in the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/practice-set).\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Observation**: What you saw a real person struggle with, from lived experience, with no solution in it.\n- **Hypothesis**: Your assumed cause plus a test with a number, a deadline and a stop rule.\n- **Designing**: Working out what the user needs and the minimum solution that tests it.\n- **Golden dataset**: Ideal input and output pairs from real users: 10 to 20 to start, 100 to 200 for production.\n- **Verifier**: The check that decides if an output is good, usually a threshold against a baseline.\n- **TDD**: Write the test before the software; now the test is often in English.\n- **Spec**: Observation, North Star, hypothesis, user, golden dataset, verifiers.\n- **User interface**: The interface between a human and a machine: a common language plus rules.\n- **Manual MVP**: Running the input to output loop by hand before building any technology.\n- **V0 / MVP**: The cheapest build that can test the hypothesis, and nothing more.\n- **Process**: The function that turns input into output.\n- **IDE**: An editor for reading, writing and reviewing code on your machine.\n\nKeep this\n\nYou do not choose an interface. You derive it from the output your user needs. Write the ideal input and ideal output first, replay what your user will do with that output, and the interface falls out of the replay. The room voted 17 to 13 for a search box, and one follow-up question overturned the vote. Code is the last step, and in Gradio it is four lines that restate the whiteboard."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-03-overview.md","title":"Building UI: Design to Deploy","meta":"title: Building UI: Design to Deploy\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/index.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 3 · Sat 26 Sep 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 183","sections":[{"h":"Building UI: design to deploy","a":"building-ui-design-to-deploy","text":"Start from the output. The interface follows.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nWrite the ideal input and output first, replay what your user does with the output, and the interface falls out of the replay. In Gradio it is then four lines.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [One person, not a persona](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s02)\n2. [Three boxes on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s03)\n3. [Ten golden pairs](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s05)\n4. [The verifier is a threshold](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s06)\n5. [The vote that lost](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s09)\n6. [Four lines of Gradio](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s12)\n7. [Setting up](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s13)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"A spec with 10 golden pairs and a numeric verifier, one replayed follow-up that picked the interface, and that interface running on localhost.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [No-code track notes: decide what it does, then draw it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code)\n- [Office hour, 1 Oct: when software stops waiting for a click](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/office-hour)\n- [Practice set: replay before you render](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/practice-set)\n- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes)\n- Lecture 3 on the map: the office hour checklist\n\nCode is the last step.Next: Lecture 4, API as the second interface, Sat 3 Oct."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-key-safety.md","title":"Keys","meta":"title: Keys\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Guide · Lecture 4: API as the second interface · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 870","sections":[{"h":"Keys","a":"keys","text":"A key is the part of the request that says who sent it. Whoever holds it is you. This page is where it lives, and what to do the minute it escapes.\n\nPost-lecture · both tracksGoes with Request Builder step 9\n\nThe rule\n\nA key lives in exactly one place: the backend, or an environment variable on your own machine. Never in page code, never in a chat, never in a screenshot. If it escapes, revoke it first and ask questions later."},{"h":"01 What a Key Is","a":"01-what-a-key-is","text":"In Round Three the machine replied 401 `Who are you? Key needed` until the request carried a key. When the key was posted in the public chat, it replied `401 Key leaked. Revoked.` Those two replies are the whole idea.\n\nThe machine cannot see you. It sees text. A key is a long, unguessable piece of text that the machine issued to you, and only to you. When a request carries it, the machine treats the request as yours: your account, your limits, your bill.\n\nIt travels on the envelope, not inside the letter, as a header:\n\n**Key**`Authorization: Bearer gsk_••••••••••••••••`\n\n`Bearer` means exactly what it says: whoever *bears* this key gets in. The machine does not check who is holding it. That is why a leaked key is as good as a stolen login."},{"h":"02 Where a Key Lives","a":"02-where-a-key-lives","text":"In step 9 of the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9) you open DevTools and see your own key in plain text. Anything a web page sends, any visitor can read. So the key cannot live in the page.\n\n**UI**holds the Groq key\n\n→\n\n**Groq**accepts whoever bears the key\n\n**Every visitor**opens the Network tab and copies it\n\nWrong. The key ships to everyone who loads the page.\n\n**UI**sends the workflow. No key to Groq.\n\n→\n\n**Aarav's backend**holds the Groq key in an environment variable\n\n→\n\n**Groq**sees the backend's request\n\nRight. The visitor's browser never sees the Groq key. This backend is what both tracks build in the Lecture 5 practical on Fri 9 Oct."},{"h":"Where it goes, by situation","a":"where-it-goes-by-situation","text":"| Situation | Where the key lives |\n| --- | --- |\n| Trying a request today | The [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8). It holds the key in memory only, never saves it, and forgets it when you reload. |\n| A command in your terminal | An environment variable, such as `GROQ_API_KEY`. The command reads `$GROQ_API_KEY`, so the key never appears in the command itself. See the [kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit#s03). |\n| Your Python backend (code track) | An environment variable set in your terminal. The code reads `os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]`. |\n| Your no-code backend (no-code track) | The tool's secrets or credentials store, never pasted into a page or a prompt. |"},{"h":"Never here","a":"never-here","text":"**Page code**Anything the browser loads, anyone can read.\n\n**Discord**Not in a help post, not in a DM. Mask it: `gsk_••••`.\n\n**An AI chat**Not even to ask why it fails. Paste the error, not the key.\n\n**A screenshot**Crop or blur before you post."},{"h":"03 The Minute One Leaks","a":"03-the-minute-one-leaks","text":"You will leak a key at some point. Everyone does. What matters is the next five minutes.\n\n1. **Revoke it.** Go to [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys) and delete the leaked key. From that moment, every request carrying it gets 401, including the ones from whoever copied it.\n2. **Create a new one.** Same page, Create API key. Copy it once; Groq will not show it again.\n3. **Update where it is used.** Your environment variable, your `.env` file, your host's settings, your no-code tool's secrets. Anything still using the old key now fails with 401, which is how you find what you missed.\n4. **Remove the leak.** Delete the message or the screenshot. This is the last step, not the first: once a key has been seen, assume it was copied."},{"h":"04 Why Sharing a Key Shares Everything","a":"04-why-sharing-a-key-shares-everything","text":"Groq's free tier allows `openai/gpt-oss-120b` 30 requests a minute and 1,000 a day. Those limits belong to your **account**, not to the key. Creating five keys does not give you five times the limit. Giving your key to five friends means all six of you draw from one limit.\n\nWhen the limit runs out, the machine replies 429 Too many requests, with a `retry-after` header saying how many seconds to wait. If a hundred people share one key, that happens within seconds. That is why everyone makes their own Groq account.\n\nPast the free tier, you pay per token. A shared key is a shared bill."},{"h":"05 Check Yourself","a":"05-check-yourself","text":"- I have my own Groq key, from my own accountNamed so you know where it is used, such as `c8-lecture04`.\n- My key is not in any chat, screenshot, page or repositorySearch your Discord messages for `gsk_` if unsure.\n- I know where the revoke button isconsole.groq.com/keys. Find it now, before you need it.\n- I can say why the UI must not hold the keyAnything a page sends, any visitor can read in the Network tab.\n- I mask keys in help postsThe status code and the error text are what helpers need. Never the key.\n\nDone\n\n0 / 5\n\nWhoever bears the key gets in.Companion pages: [reading the Network tab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) · [kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit) · [troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-kit.md","title":"Lecture 4 Kit","meta":"title: Lecture 4 Kit\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Kit · Companion to Lecture 4: API as the second interface · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2183","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 4 Kit","a":"lecture-4-kit","text":"Every link, command and word you need this week, with one line on what each is for.\n\nPost-lecture · both tracksNothing to install\n\nHow to use this\n\nNothing to install this week: the Request Builder runs in your browser. Section 1 is the path through the Request Builder. Section 2 is the links. Section 4 is for when you want the same requests in a terminal. Section 5 is the bridge to the Lecture 5 practical: Python for the code track, the four boxes for the no-code track."},{"h":"1 Everything Here Can Be Done in the Browser","a":"1-everything-here-can-be-done-in-the-browser","text":"Both tracks can do the whole week in the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder), with nothing to download. The only other site you visit is [console.groq.com](https://console.groq.com/keys), to create a free key in step 8. Ten steps, in order:\n\n| Step | What you do |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Step 1 · The parts](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s1) | Your Round Three message, translated into a real HTTP request. |\n| [Step 2 · Read: your first GET](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s2) | Read from an echo machine, and watch a space become %20. |\n| [Step 3 · Create: POST a package](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s3) | Send Aarav's workflow as JSON and find it in the reply. |\n| [Step 4 · Replay Round Three for real](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s4) | Create a key on Aarav's service, then Create a diagnosis: 201. |\n| [Step 5 · Break it on purpose](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s5) | Cause 404, 405, 401 and 400 one at a time, and read the hint each time. |\n| [Step 6 · Whose fault: every code](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s6) | Eight real requests, eight codes. Predict whose fault before you send. |\n| [Step 7 · Read real traffic](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s7) | Open DevTools on the page and find all six parts of your own request. |\n| [Step 8 · Put a model behind it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8) | Create a free Groq key and send Aarav's workflow to a model. Send it twice. |\n| [Step 9 · Find your key, and where it should live](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9) | Spot your key in plain text, then decide which box should hold it. |\n| [Step 10 · Design your API](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s10) | List every request your own app needs. This goes into the practice set. |\n\nThe terminal commands in section 4 and the bridges in section 5 are the same requests again, for when you want them outside the browser. They are optional this week."},{"h":"2 Links","a":"2-links","text":"| Link | What it is for |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder) | Send real requests from your browser: the echo, GET, POST, Groq, Aarav, and every status code. Keeps your key in memory only. |\n| [The Wire](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire) | Optional deep dive: Morse, ASCII, TCP/IP, HTTP, REST, JSON. Why each part of a request exists. |\n| [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys) | Create your free Groq key, and revoke it if it leaks. Sign in with Google. Groq shows a key once. |\n| [Groq models](https://console.groq.com/docs/models) | Which models exist today. Use `openai/gpt-oss-120b`. |\n| [Groq rate limits](https://console.groq.com/docs/rate-limits) | How many requests your account gets: 30 a minute and 1,000 a day for gpt-oss-120b on the free tier. |\n| [Groq deprecations](https://console.groq.com/docs/deprecations) | Retired models and their shutdown dates. Why `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` returns 404 since 16 Aug 2026. |\n| [100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav) | Aarav's Diagnosis Service. The machine from Round Three, as a real API. Its doors are in section 3. |\n| [httpbin.org](https://httpbin.org) | A public echo machine: sends back whatever it receives. `/get`, `/post`, and `/status/404` for any code. It does not reject broken JSON. |\n| [postman-echo.com](https://postman-echo.com/get?text=hello) | A second echo machine, for when httpbin is slow or down. |\n| [hoppscotch.io](https://hoppscotch.io) | A free API client in the browser, with no install. The same four boxes as the Builder, with more options. |\n| [Chrome DevTools: Network panel](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/network) | The official guide to the Network tab. |\n| [Reading the Network tab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) · [Keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety) · [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) | The three guides for this lecture: find the parts, keep the key safe, fix the errors. |"},{"h":"3 Aarav's Doors","a":"3-aaravs-doors","text":"Base address: `https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav`. Every reply echoes back the parts the machine read, under `you_sent`. Every error names the `part` to fix.\n\n| CRUD | Method | Address | Key | Package in | Reply |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Read | GET | /aarav | no | none | 200 the list of doors |\n| Create | POST | /aarav/keys | no | {\"name\": \"Riya\"} | 201 your key |\n| Create | POST | /aarav/diagnoses | yes | {\"workflow\": \"...\"} | 201 a diagnosis |\n| Read | GET | /aarav/diagnoses | yes | none | 200 an empty list: no memory yet |\n| Read, Update, Delete | GET PUT PATCH DELETE | /aarav/diagnoses/{id} | yes | any | 404 nothing is stored |\n| Create | POST | /aarav/workflows | yes | {\"workflow\": \"...\"} | 201 an id, and a pointer to /diagnoses |\n| Read | GET | /aarav/workflows | yes | none | 200 an empty list |\n| (practice) | any | /aarav/broken | no | none | 500 their machine broke |\n\nThe key goes in a header: `Authorization: Bearer aarav_…`. The machine checks the parts in the order of the list: address, then action, then key, then package. The first part that fails is the only error you get."},{"h":"4 The Same Requests in a Terminal","a":"4-the-same-requests-in-a-terminal","text":"Optional. curl sends a request from the terminal and prints the reply. It is already installed on Mac and on Windows 10 and 11.\n\nYour machine\n\nCommands below switch to match\n\nWindows: two things first\n\nIn PowerShell, type `curl.exe`, not `curl`; plain `curl` can run a different PowerShell command. PowerShell also mangles JSON typed inside a command, so save each package in a small file and send the file with `--data \"@file.json\"`. Or install Git for Windows and use Git Bash, where the Mac commands work as written."},{"h":"Read: GET from the echo","a":"read-get-from-the-echo","text":"curl \"https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello%20world\"\n\ncurl.exe \"https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello%20world\"\n\nAdd `-i` after curl to see the status code and the reply headers as well as the package."},{"h":"Create: POST to the echo","a":"create-post-to-the-echo","text":"Terminal · POST httpbin\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://httpbin.org/post \\  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\  -d '{\"text\": \"I open Jira, read the tickets, write a report, post it on Slack.\"}'\n```\n\nSave this as `text.json` in the folder your terminal is in:\n\ntext.json\n\n```\n{\"text\": \"I open Jira, read the tickets, write a report, post it on Slack.\"}\n```\n\ncurl.exe -X POST https://httpbin.org/post -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" --data \"@text.json\"\n\nYour text comes back at `json.text` in the reply."},{"h":"Create a key at Aarav's","a":"create-a-key-at-aaravs","text":"Terminal · POST /aarav/keys\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/keys \\  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\  -d '{\"name\": \"Riya\"}'\n```\n\nname.json\n\n```\n{\"name\": \"Riya\"}\n```\n\ncurl.exe -X POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/keys -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" --data \"@name.json\"\n\nThe reply is 201 with your key. Put it in an environment variable for this terminal session, so it never appears inside a command:\n\nexport AARAV_KEY=\"paste-your-aarav-key-here\"\n\n$env:AARAV_KEY=\"paste-your-aarav-key-here\""},{"h":"Create a diagnosis","a":"create-a-diagnosis","text":"Terminal · POST /aarav/diagnoses\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnoses \\  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $AARAV_KEY\" \\  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\  -d '{\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, read the week'\"'\"'s tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes.\"}'\n```\n\nworkflow.json\n\n```\n{\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes.\"}\n```\n\ncurl.exe -X POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnoses -H \"Authorization: Bearer $env:AARAV_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" --data \"@workflow.json\"\n\nRun it twice. The same diagnosis comes back both times: this machine follows rules."},{"h":"Create a reply from Groq","a":"create-a-reply-from-groq","text":"export GROQ_API_KEY=\"paste-your-groq-key-here\"\n\n$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"paste-your-groq-key-here\"\n\nTerminal · POST Groq chat completions\n\n```\ncurl https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions \\  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $GROQ_API_KEY\" \\  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\  -d '{\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I open Jira, read the week'\"'\"'s tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes. Diagnose this workflow: repeated pattern, bottleneck, first AI step.\"}]}'\n```\n\ngroq.json\n\n```\n{\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes. Diagnose this workflow: repeated pattern, bottleneck, first AI step.\"}]}\n```\n\ncurl.exe https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions -H \"Authorization: Bearer $env:GROQ_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" --data \"@groq.json\"\n\nRun it twice. Different words come back each time. The door is deterministic; what comes through it is probabilistic. The text is at `choices[0].message.content`.\n\nAn environment variable set this way lasts until you close the terminal. Do not paste keys into commands you share, and do not post your terminal history."},{"h":"Every code on demand","a":"every-code-on-demand","text":"| Command (Mac; on Windows use curl.exe) | You get |\n| --- | --- |\n| curl -i https://httpbin.org/status/404 | 404 nothing at that address |\n| curl -i https://httpbin.org/status/429 | 429 too many requests |\n| curl -i https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/models | 401 no key: a real 401 from Groq |\n| curl -i -X DELETE https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnoses | 405 this door does not take Delete |\n| curl -i https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/broken | 500 their machine broke |"},{"h":"5 Bridges to the Lecture 5 Practical","a":"5-bridges-to-the-lecture-5-practical","text":""},{"h":"Code track: the Groq call in Python","a":"code-track-the-groq-call-in-python","text":"The same request as the curl command, in Python. Next week this moves inside a FastAPI backend, which is where the key belongs. Install the library once with `pip install requests` (Mac: `pip3`), and set `GROQ_API_KEY` as above before you run it.\n\ngroq_call.py\n\n```\nimport osimport requests response = requests.post(    \"https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions\",address    headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]},key    json={package        \"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\",package        \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. Diagnose this workflow.\"}],package    },package)print(response.status_code)codeprint(response.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"])reply package\n```\n\nAddressAction: requests.post is POSTKeyPackageCode\n\nThe action is in the function name: `requests.post`. Passing `json=` turns the dictionary into JSON and adds `Content-Type: application/json` for you. `os.environ` reads the key from the environment, so it never sits in the file."},{"h":"No-code track: the four boxes","a":"no-code-track-the-four-boxes","text":"Every HTTP step in every no-code tool asks for the same parts, under slightly different names. Fill them in the order of the list.\n\n| Part | The box in your tool | For Groq |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| address | URL | https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions |\n| action | Method | POST |\n| key | Headers (or Authentication: Bearer token) | `Authorization` = `Bearer gsk_…`, from the tool's secrets store |\n| package | Body, type JSON (plus the header `Content-Type: application/json` if not added for you) | {\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}]} |\n| code | Response status | 200 |\n| reply package | Response body | The text at `choices[0].message.content` |\n\nIn the Lecture 5 practical you build the backend in Antigravity with Rahul. Its agent will propose a plan; read the plan for these same parts before you approve it. If a step puts the Groq key in the page, reject that step."},{"h":"6 Words You Will Meet","a":"6-words-you-will-meet","text":"**API**The interface between two machines: the agreed way one asks and the other answers.\n\n**Endpoint**One door of an API: an address plus the actions it takes. `/aarav/diagnoses` is one.\n\n**URL**The full address: the rules (`https://`), the machine, the path, and any query.\n\n**Path**The door on the machine: the part after the domain, such as `/diagnoses`.\n\n**Query string**Extra details on the address after a `?`, such as `?text=hello`. Spaces become `%20`.\n\n**Method**How the action is spelled on the wire: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.\n\n**CRUD**Create, Read, Update, Delete: the four actions. Create is POST, Read is GET, Update is PUT or PATCH, Delete is DELETE.\n\n**Header**A line written on the envelope, not inside the letter. The machine reads it without opening the package.\n\n**Authorization**The header that carries the key.\n\n**Bearer**The word before the key. Whoever bears this key gets in.\n\n**Body**The package inside the request or reply.\n\n**JSON**A text format, not a language: keys and values in curly braces. Every language and tool can read it.\n\n**Status code**The three-digit number at the top of every reply. It says what happened.\n\n**2xx · 4xx · 5xx**2xx it worked. 4xx you sent something wrong. 5xx their machine failed.\n\n**Request**What you send: address, action, key, package.\n\n**Response**What comes back: a code and a package.\n\n**Rate limit**How many requests an account may send per minute or day. Past it: 429.\n\n**CORS**The browser's rule that a page may only call another site if that site allows it.\n\n**HTTPS**HTTP inside a sealed envelope. Nobody between you and the machine can read the key.\n\n**Stream**A reply sent in many small pieces as it is written, so words appear one by one.\n\n**Model**The program that reads text and writes text back. Here, gpt-oss-120b, running on Groq's machines.\n\n**Open-weight**A model whose trained numbers are published, so anyone can run it. Groq serves many.\n\n**LPU**Groq's own chip for running models fast. Groq with a q, not Grok.\n\n**Backend**The machine behind your UI. It holds the keys, calls the model, and will store the data.\n\nAddress, action, key, package. Code, package.Companion pages: [reading the Network tab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) · [keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety) · [troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-network-tab.md","title":"Reading the Network Tab","meta":"title: Reading the Network Tab\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Guide · Lecture 4: API as the second interface · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1521","sections":[{"h":"Reading the Network Tab","a":"reading-the-network-tab","text":"Every request your browser sends is written down. Open the list, click one, and find the four parts of the request and the two parts of the reply.\n\nPost-lecture · both tracksChrome, nothing to installAbout 20 minutes\n\nWhat you are looking for\n\nEvery request carries an address, an action, a key and a package. Every reply carries a code and a package. The Network tab shows all six. In Lecture 2 you were told you did not need to read it yet. Now you do."},{"h":"01 Open It","a":"01-open-it","text":"Your machine\n\n1. **Open DevTools.** Right click anywhere on the page, then Inspect. Or the shortcut: `F12` or `Ctrl`+`Shift`+`I``Cmd`+`Option`+`I`.\n2. **Click the Network tab.** If you cannot see it, click `»` at the top of DevTools.\n3. **Filter to Fetch/XHR.** These are the requests a page sends by itself, after it has loaded. Images, fonts and styles drop out of the list.\n4. **Clear the list.** The circle with a line through it, top left. Now the next request you cause is the only one there.\n5. **Cause one request, then click it.** The right side shows tabs: Headers, Payload, Preview, Response. The six parts live across those tabs.\n6. **Keep DevTools open while you work.** If you open it after sending, send the request again: the list only records what happens while it is open.\n\nThe panels below are drawn in HTML so they read the same on every machine. Real screenshots will be added to this page from `assets/images/`."},{"h":"02 Part 1: This Site's Own Requests","a":"02-part-1-this-sites-own-requests","text":"Start with traffic you made yourself, on a page that hides nothing. This is step 7 of the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s7).\n\n1. **Open the Request Builder** and go to step 4, [Replay Round Three for real](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s4). If you have not created a key there yet, do that first.\n2. **Open DevTools, then Network, then Fetch/XHR, then clear the list**, as in section 01.\n3. **Press Send** on the diagnosis request in step 4. One line appears in the list, named `diagnoses`. Click it.\n4. **Headers tab.** Find Request URL, Request Method and Status Code at the top, then `authorization` and `content-type` under Request Headers.\n5. **Payload tab.** Your package: `{\"workflow\": \"...\"}`.\n6. **Response tab.** The reply package, with the diagnosis inside. The machine also echoes back what it read from you under `you_sent`.\n\nAllFetch/XHR1 request\n\ndiagnoses*201*\n\nHeadersPayloadPreviewResponse\n\n**Request URL**`https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnosesaddress`\n\n**Request Method**`POSTaction · Create`\n\n**Status Code**`201 Createdcode`\n\n**authorization**`Bearer aarav_yourkeyherekey`\n\n**content-type**`application/jsonthe package's format`\n\n| Part | Where in the Network tab | What you see |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| address | Headers → Request URL | https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnoses |\n| action | Headers → Request Method | `POST`: a Create |\n| key | Headers → Request Headers → `authorization` | `Bearer` and your Aarav key |\n| package | `content-type`, then Payload | `application/json`, then `{\"workflow\": \"...\"}` |\n| code | Headers → Status Code | 201 |\n| reply package | Response | The diagnosis, and `you_sent` |\n\nNow break it on purpose with the buttons in step 5 and watch the list. Each failed request shows its code in red, and its Response tab holds the hint that names the part to fix."},{"h":"03 Part 2: Your Own Groq Request","a":"03-part-2-your-own-groq-request","text":"Do this after step 8 of the Request Builder, [Put a model behind it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8).\n\n1. **Open DevTools, then Network, then Fetch/XHR, then clear the list.**\n2. **Paste your Groq key** in step 8. A request named `models` appears: the Builder asking Groq which models your key can use.\n3. **Press Send.** A request named `completions` appears. Click it and read the parts below.\n\nAllFetch/XHR2 requests\n\nmodels*200*\n\ncompletions*200*\n\nHeadersPayloadResponse\n\n**Request URL**`https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completionsaddress`\n\n**Request Method**`POSTaction · Create`\n\n**Status Code**`200 OKcode`\n\n**authorization**`Bearer gsk_yourkeyisrighthere0000key · in plain text`\n\n**content-type**`application/jsonthe package's format`\n\nThe `models` request above it is a Read: the Builder asked Groq which models your key can use. That was your first GET with a key.\n\n| Part | Where | Value |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| address | Request URL | https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions |\n| action | Request Method | `POST`. Create: a reply that did not exist before you asked. |\n| key | Request Headers → `authorization` | `Bearer` and your key. Whoever bears it gets in. |\n| package | Request Headers → `content-type`, then Payload | `application/json`, then `{\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [...]}` |\n| code | Status Code | 200 |\n| reply package | Response | The text is at `choices[0].message.content`. Ignore `reasoning`: it is the model's scratchpad. |"},{"h":"Now look at the key","a":"now-look-at-the-key","text":"Expand the `authorization` line on your own screen, not a shared one. Your key is there in plain text.\n\nThe question this page exists to ask\n\nThe Builder is your private tool, so this is fine here. If Aarav's product were built the same way, every visitor could open this tab, copy his key, and spend his money. Where should the key live instead? Answer it, then do step 9 of the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9) and read [Keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety)."},{"h":"04 Part 3: The Space That Became %20","a":"04-part-3-the-space-that-became-20","text":"Open step 2 of the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s2) with DevTools open, or paste `https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello` into Chrome's address bar. Then change `hello` to `hello world`. Edit the existing address; if you retype it from scratch, Chrome may run a Google search instead.\n\nAll1 request\n\nget?text=hello%20world*200*\n\nHeadersResponse\n\n**Request URL**`https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello%20worldaddress`\n\n**Request Method**`GETaction · Read`\n\n**Status Code**`200 OKcode`\n\nAn address cannot hold a space, so the browser translated it to `%20` before sending. Look at the Request URL here, not the reply: httpbin decodes the text back to `hello world` in its answer. Your code and your no-code tools will not always do this translation for you. When they do not, the machine replies 400."},{"h":"05 Part 4: A Real Product, ChatGPT","a":"05-part-4-a-real-product-chatgpt","text":"Now the same reading on a product used by hundreds of millions of people. The parts are the same; there is just more noise around them.\n\nLog in to ChatGPT in Chrome, open the Network tab, filter to Fetch/XHR, clear it, and send \"hi\". Several requests appear. One fires just before the message and carries a name like `chat-requirements`: the site checking you before it lets the message through. The one you want is named `conversation`. In recent builds its address ends in `/backend-api/f/conversation`. The exact name changes from time to time; look for the one sent at the moment you pressed Enter.\n\nIf you are logged out, ChatGPT uses different addresses. Log in first.\n\nAllFetch/XHRDocJS5 requests\n\nchat-requirements*200*\n\nconversation*200*\n\nconversations?offset=0*200*\n\nlat/r*200*\n\ngizmos/bootstrap*200*\n\nHeadersPayloadPreviewResponse\n\n**Request URL**`https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/f/conversationaddress`\n\n**Request Method**`POSTaction`\n\n**Status Code**`200 OKcode`\n\n**content-type**`application/json`\n\n**authorization**`Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6key · never expand`\n\n**cookie**`__Secure-next-auth.session-token= never expand`\n\nHeaders tab. Before you look at the method, guess: is asking ChatGPT a question a Read or a Create? It is the Google-versus-LLM question from the CRUD quiz."},{"h":"Find each part","a":"find-each-part","text":"| Part | Where in the Network tab | What you see |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| address | Headers → Request URL | The machine and the door: `chatgpt.com` and `/backend-api/…/conversation` |\n| action | Headers → Request Method | `POST`. A Create: your message and a new reply are added to the conversation. The [lecture notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes) explain why. |\n| key | Headers → Request Headers → `authorization` | Your logged-in session. Do not expand it. Do not copy it. |\n| package | Payload | JSON. Find your \"hi\" inside it, next to the model name and an id for the conversation. |\n| code | Headers → Status Code | 200 |\n\nTwo things you never expand, copy or paste\n\nThe `authorization` header and the `cookie` header. Together they are your login. Anyone holding them is you, until they expire. Not into Discord, not into a screenshot, not into an AI chat asking it to \"explain this request\"."},{"h":"The reply arrives in pieces","a":"the-reply-arrives-in-pieces","text":"Click Response, or EventStream if Chrome shows it. The content type of this reply is `text/event-stream`. The machine does not send one package at the end. It sends many small ones as the model writes, and the page adds each one to the screen. That is why ChatGPT's words appear one by one.\n\ndata: {\"message\": {\"content\": {\"parts\": [\"**Hi**\"]}}} data: {\"message\": {\"content\": {\"parts\": [\"**Hi!**\"]}}} data: {\"message\": {\"content\": {\"parts\": [\"**Hi! How can**\"]}}} data: {\"message\": {\"content\": {\"parts\": [\"**Hi! How can I help?**\"]}}} data: [DONE]\n\nSimplified. The real stream carries more fields; the shape is the same."},{"h":"06 Practise","a":"06-practise","text":"- Found all six parts in your own Aarav requestAddress, action, key, package, code, reply package.\n- Found all five parts in ChatGPT's conversation requestWithout expanding authorization or cookie.\n- Saw the reply stream in piecesResponse or EventStream tab.\n- Found your Groq request and its 200And your key in plain text, on your own screen.\n- Saw %20 in a Request URLThe space, translated.\n- Opened the Network tab on one app you use every dayPick one request. Name its address, action and code. For any app, the Network tab is the truth.\n\nDone\n\n0 / 6\n\nThe Network tab is the truth.Companion pages: [keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety) · [troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) · [kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: API as the Second Interface","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: API as the Second Interface\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 4 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2095","sections":[{"h":"API as the Second Interface","a":"api-as-the-second-interface","text":"How your UI talks to a machine it has never met, worked out from the machine's own replies.\n\nSat 3 Oct 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCohort 8 · Module 2About 12 minutes to read"},{"h":"How These Notes Are Organised","a":"how-these-notes-are-organised","text":"Sections 1 to 4 are what we did in the lecture: Round Three, the six parts, and the CRUD quiz. Section 5 is what you do this week: the steps in the Request Builder, then the practice set. Section 6 is an optional deep dive into why the six parts exist, from Morse to JSON."},{"h":"01 A Face With No Brain","a":"01-a-face-with-no-brain","text":"Last week your UI took Aarav's workflow and handed it straight back. Type \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack\", and it returns the same words. It has a face and no brain.\n\nThe brain you want is a model that reads the workflow and writes a plan. That model does not live inside your page. It lives on another machine, owned by someone else. So the question is: **how does your UI talk to a machine it has never met?**\n\nInput\n\n**Aarav's workflow**typed into your UI\n\n→\n\nProcess\n\n**A model**on someone else's machine\n\n→\n\nOutput\n\n**A diagnosis**back in your UI\n\nThe arrow in the middle is the second interface. The first was the UI, between a person and a machine. The second is between two machines: the API."},{"h":"02 Round Three: The Machine That Answers in Codes","a":"02-round-three-the-machine-that-answers-in-codes","text":"In Lecture 2 the machine stayed silent, and every attempt failed without a clue. In Round Three the machine answered every message with a number and at most five words. That one change meant every failure carried information.\n\nThe task: Aarav has built a Diagnosis Service. It turns a description of a workflow into a diagnosis. Get a diagnosis of Aarav's Friday report. Instructions only through the Zoom chat, one line per message. The machine does not guess.\n\nHere is what the machine replied, and what each reply forced you to add:\n\n| Reply | What had been sent | The part it forced |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 429 Too many requests. One sender. | Several people typing at once | One point of contact, from Lecture 2 |\n| 404 Not found. Doors: /aarav/workflows /aarav/diagnoses | No address, or the wrong one | address |\n| 400 Which action? CREATE READ UPDATE DELETE | A sentence describing the wish, with no action named | action |\n| 405 This door takes CREATE, READ | An UPDATE or DELETE sent to the diagnosis door | action |\n| 200 [] Nothing to read yet | A READ for a diagnosis that did not exist yet | Read only fetches what already exists |\n| 401 Who are you? Key needed | No key | key |\n| 401 Key leaked. Revoked. | The key pasted in the public chat | Keys are secrets and travel privately |\n| 400 Can't read. Send {\"workflow\": \"...\"} | The workflow as a plain sentence | package in an agreed format |\n| 201 Created | Everything right, sent by DM | Done: a diagnosis came back |\n\nThree of those replies deserve a second look.\n\n**Where did the address come from?** Nobody was given it. The first message without one came back 404 with a list of doors. The machine itself told you where to knock.\n\n**The 200 that gave you nothing.** Reading the diagnoses before any existed came back 200 with an empty list. The request was understood. It was the wrong request. A 200 means \"your request was fine\", not \"you got what you wanted\".\n\n**The leaked key.** The key was handed out privately, by DM. The winning message had to carry it, so the winning message had to travel privately too. A private channel for the key is exactly what the S in HTTPS gives you on the web.\n\nThe winning message, sent by DM:\n\n**Action**`CREATE`\n\n**Address**`/aarav/diagnoses`\n\n**Key**`key: ••••••••`\n\n**Package**`{\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes.\"}`\n\nAnd the machine replied **201 Created**, with the diagnosis: *Repeated pattern: a weekly status report. Bottleneck: reading every ticket to find what changed. First AI step: draft the report from a Jira export; Aarav edits and posts it.*\n\nNobody listed these parts before the game. The machine's replies did, one at a time."},{"h":"03 The Six Parts, Named","a":"03-the-six-parts-named","text":"After the game, each reply was connected back to a part of the message. Draw the letter from Lecture 2 again: everything on it now has a name.\n\n**Address**`Where it goes · the URL`\n\n**Action**`What you want done · the method`\n\n**Key**`Who you are · a header, written on the envelope`\n\n**Package**`The letter inside · the body, in an agreed format`\n\n**Code**`How it went · the status code on the reply`\n\n| Part | In the game | Its name on the web |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| address | `/aarav/diagnoses` | **URL**, or endpoint. The domain names the machine; the path (`/aarav/diagnoses`) names the door |\n| action | CREATE, READ, UPDATE, DELETE | **Method**: POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE |\n| key | `key: ••••` | A **header**: `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. \"Bearer\" means whoever bears this key is let in |\n| package | `{\"workflow\": \"...\"}` | The **body**, written in JSON |\n| code | 429, 404, 400, 405, 401, 201 | **Status code** |\n| package back | The diagnosis | The **response body** |\n\nEverything on that envelope together is an **HTTP API request**. In Lecture 2 you named the API. In this lecture you opened it.\n\nThe same two principles hold. **Input → Process → Output**: the request is the input, the machine at the other end is the process, the reply is the output. **An interface is a common language plus a protocol**: JSON is the common language for the package; HTTP is the protocol for everything around it."},{"h":"04 The CRUD Quiz: Create, Read, Update, Delete","a":"04-the-crud-quiz-create-read-update-delete","text":"The game's actions were four words. They are the four things you can do to anything stored: **Create, Read, Update, Delete**, or CRUD. On the wire, each is spelled as an HTTP method.\n\n| Aarav's action | CRUD | Method and address |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Opens the page listing his past diagnoses | Read | GET /diagnoses |\n| Submits his Friday workflow for the first time | Create | POST /workflows |\n| Fixes one field: \"weekly\" becomes \"every Friday\" | Update | PATCH /workflows/7 |\n| His UI resends the whole workflow with the fix | Update | PUT /workflows/7 |\n| Removes an old workflow | Delete | DELETE /workflows/7 |\n\nPUT and PATCH are both Update. PUT replaces the whole thing; PATCH changes part of it."},{"h":"Google search versus asking an LLM","a":"google-search-versus-asking-an-llm","text":"Search for something on Google and look at the address bar: `google.com/search?q=…`. That is a GET. Ask ChatGPT the same thing, or call a model through an API, and the request is a POST. Why is one a Read and the other a Create?\n\nGoogle is reading. The pages it searches already sit in its index, and your search fetches results from what is already there. Your question fits in the address, and nothing new is stored.\n\nThe model is creating. Your message and a new reply are added to the conversation, and that reply did not exist before you asked. Ask the same question twice and you get two different replies. Your prompt also travels as a package in the body, because it can be long and private, and an address is neither. So generation is a Create, and Create is spelled POST.\n\n**The rule.** Read what already exists: GET, and nothing changes. Create something new: POST. Update it: PUT or PATCH. Delete it: DELETE.\n\nCompanies do not always follow the convention. For any app, its real traffic is the truth. You will read real traffic yourself in [step 7 of the Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s7)."},{"h":"05 What You Do This Week","a":"05-what-you-do-this-week","text":"The lecture stopped at the parts. This week you send real requests yourself, in the browser, for both tracks. Do the steps in order; each one has a checkpoint.\n\n1. **[Read: your first GET, and the space](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s2).** The address bar is already an API tool. You will also see why a space becomes `%20`, and why a raw space causes a 400.\n2. **[Create: send a package](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s3).** A POST carries a body. Why does the body need a header that says it is JSON? Because JSON is a format both sides must agree on, and the machine does not guess the format.\n3. **[Replay Round Three for real](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s4).** The machine from the game is real. Create a key, then Create a diagnosis, and get the same 201.\n4. **[Break it on purpose](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s5).** Cause a 400, 401, 404 and 405 yourself, and note which part each one points to.\n5. **[Whose fault: every code](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s6).** The first digit tells you before anything else: 2xx worked, 4xx you sent something wrong, 5xx their machine failed.\n6. **[Read real traffic](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s7).** Open DevTools and find the six parts in requests you just sent, then in ChatGPT's.\n7. **[Put a model behind it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8).** Send Aarav's workflow to a model on Groq. Send the same request twice. Aarav's service gave the same diagnosis twice; the model will not. The door is deterministic; what comes through it is probabilistic. Which answer is right, and who decides?\n8. **[Find your key, and where it should live](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9).** Your key is visible in DevTools. That is why keys live in a backend, never in a page.\n9. **[Design your API](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s10).** Write Aarav's table for your own app, then submit the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set)."},{"h":"Dates","a":"dates","text":"- **Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST: [office hour](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting#s04).** Milestone: steps 1 to 8 done and a draft of your API table. The question template, checklist and what to bring are on the troubleshooting page.\n- **Before Fri 9 Oct: [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set).** Submit what the steps produce.\n- **Fri 9 Oct, Lecture 5: build the backend.** Code track: a raw request from your Gradio chat to the model, in Python. No-code track: Antigravity.\n- **Fri 9 Oct: Assignment 1.** Due on its own track. The practice set does not depend on it."},{"h":"06 Optional Deep Dive: Why These Six Parts?","a":"06-optional-deep-dive-why-these-six-parts","text":"Round Three showed that the parts exist. It did not show why. Each part is there because an earlier system failed in a specific way: a wire that is only on or off (Morse code), machines without a human operator (ASCII), a million machines and lost messages (TCP/IP gave the address), a machine that does not know what you want (HTTP gave the action, the key and the code), fifty companies with fifty verb lists (REST gave nouns and CRUD), and two programs in different languages (JSON gave the package). [The Wire](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire) lets you work through each problem before you see its answer. It takes about 20 minutes and is not required for the practice set."},{"h":"07 Words You Met","a":"07-words-you-met","text":"**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **API**: The interface between two machines: a common language plus a protocol.\n- **Request**: Address, action, key, package.\n- **Response**: Code and package.\n- **URL / endpoint**: The address: the machine (domain) and the door (path).\n- **Method**: How the action is spelled: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.\n- **CRUD**: Create, Read, Update, Delete: the four actions on anything stored.\n- **Header**: A line written on the envelope, read without opening the letter. The key goes here.\n- **Bearer**: Whoever bears this key is let in. Written as Authorization: Bearer <key>.\n- **Body**: The package inside the envelope.\n- **JSON**: A text format, not a language: keys and values in curly braces.\n- **Status code**: Three digits on the reply. 2xx worked, 4xx yours, 5xx theirs.\n- **HTTPS**: HTTP in a sealed envelope, so nobody on the way can read your key.\n- **REST**: A design style: nouns as addresses, CRUD as verbs. Not a protocol.\n- **Backend**: The machine between your UI and everything else. It holds keys and stores data.\n\nFour things in, two things out.\n\nKeep this\n\nEvery request between two machines carries four things: an address, an action, a key, and a package in a format both sides agree on. Every reply carries two: a code and a package."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-overview.md","title":"API as the Second Interface","meta":"title: API as the Second Interface\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/index.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 4 · Sat 3 Oct 2026 · Both tracks · After the lecture\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 547","sections":[{"h":"API as the second interface","a":"api-as-the-second-interface","text":"Your UI has a face and no brain. The brain lives on another machine. This week you make the two talk, one request at a time.\n\nStart todayAll in the browserDue before the Lecture 5 practical, Fri 9 Oct\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nEvery request between two machines carries four things: an address, an action, a key, and a package in a format both sides agree on. Every reply carries two: a code and a package.\n\n01"},{"h":"What we did in the lecture","a":"what-we-did-in-the-lecture","text":"| Round Three | The machine answered every message with a code. Each code forced one part: one sender, then the address, the action, the key, the package. |\n| --- | --- |\n| The six parts | Request: address, action, key, package. Reply: code, package. |\n| The CRUD quiz | Create, Read, Update, Delete, spelled POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE. A Google search is a GET; asking an LLM is a POST, because it creates a new reply. |\n\n[Read the lecture notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes) if you missed it or want the recap.\n\n02"},{"h":"Your path this week","a":"your-path-this-week","text":"1. **[Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder): ten steps, about 90 minutes.** Real requests to real machines from this site: Read, Create, the real Round Three machine, breaking it on purpose, reading traffic in DevTools, a model on Groq, and where your key should live. Pick your track at the top; each step has the Python version for code and the box-by-box version for no-code.\n2. **[Practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set): submit five things.** A 201 from Aarav's service, your break-it table, a Groq 200 with two answers, where your key belongs, and your own API design. Post in the Discord channel before Fri 9 Oct.\n3. **[Office hour](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting#s04): Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.** Milestone: steps 1 to 8 done and a draft of your API table. Bring one question in the template, and any request that would not work.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"[StuckTroubleshootingSearch an error, see which part it points to and how to fix it, and how to ask for help.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) [GuideReading the Network tabFind the six parts in your own requests, then in ChatGPT's.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) [GuideKeysWhat Bearer means, where a key lives, and what to do the minute one leaks.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety) [Keep openKitEvery link and command, the Python bridge, the no-code mapping, and the words.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit) [Optional deep diveThe WireWhy a request has exactly these parts: six problems from Morse code to JSON.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire)\n\n04"},{"h":"Dates","a":"dates","text":"| Office hour | Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST · Milestone: steps 1 to 8 and a draft API table |\n| --- | --- |\n| Practice set | Post in the Discord channel before the Lecture 5 practical |\n| Assignment 1 | Due Fri 9 Oct |\n| Lecture 5 · Building APIs and backend | Fri 9 Oct · Code track: Python, requests, Gradio · No-code track: Antigravity |\n| Lecture 6 · Databases and domain modelling | Sat 10 Oct · Where should the diagnoses live? |\n| Lecture 8 · End to end MVP | Sat 17 Oct · A live URL in a real user's hands, then the hackathon |\n\nThe door is deterministic. What comes through it is probabilistic.Back to the curriculum map"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-request-builder.md","title":"Request Builder","meta":"title: Request Builder\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Lecture 4 · API as the second interface · Practice lab · Start today\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2442","sections":[{"h":"Request Builder","a":"request-builder","text":"Ten short steps, all in this browser tab. Each one ends with a checkpoint. Do them in order, then submit the practice set.\n\nAbout 90 minutes in totalNothing to installKeys stay in this tab\n\nWhere you are\n\nIn Round Three the machine taught you that every request carries four things, address action key package, and every reply carries two, code and package. Now you send real requests with those parts, to real machines, and finally to a model.\n\nYour track\n\nBoth tracks do every step here, in the browser. Your track adds one extra box per step: the same request in Python, or the same request in a no-code tool. Your choice is remembered on this device.\n\n**0/10**\n\n**Checkpoints**Saved on this device"},{"h":"01 The parts: your Round Three message, translated","a":"01-the-parts-your-round-three-message-translated","text":"This is the message that won Round Three. Below it is the same request written the way HTTP writes it. Tap a coloured part to find its twin.\n\nCREATE 100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/diagnoses key: key-●●●● {\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes.\"}\n\nPOST /aarav/diagnoses HTTP/1.1 Host: 100x-curriculum.vercel.app Authorization: Bearer aarav_●●●●●●●●_●●●●●● Content-Type: application/json {\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack. About 90 minutes.\"}\n\n| In the game | On the wire | Its name |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CREATE | POST | Method. The CRUD action is what you want; the method is how HTTP spells it. |\n| …/aarav/diagnoses | https:// + host + /aarav/diagnoses | URL, or endpoint. The host names the machine, the path names the door. |\n| key: | Authorization: Bearer … | A header. Written on the envelope, read without opening the letter. |\n| {\"workflow\": …} | Content-Type + body | The body, in JSON. The header says which format it is in. |\n| 201 | HTTP/1.1 201 Created | Status code, plus a reply package. |\n\n- Checkpoint: I can name the four parts of a request and the two parts of a reply, without looking"},{"h":"02 Read: your first request","a":"02-read-your-first-request","text":"A Read fetches something that already exists. httpbin.org is a public echo machine: it sends back whatever it receives, so you can see exactly what you sent.\n\n1. **Look at the four bands.** Address is filled in for you. Action is **Read · GET**. Key: none needed. Package: none, because a Read sends no package.\n2. **Press Send.** You should see a large 200, and under *The part you want*, your text.\n3. **Type a sentence with a space** in the text box, like `hello world`, and watch the address. The space turns into `%20` before anything is sent. An address cannot hold a space, so it is translated. Your code and your tools will not always do this for you; a raw space in an address is a classic 400.\n4. **Click Show the raw request.** This is the request as text: the action, the address, and nothing else.\n\n**Code track · the same request in Python**\n\nUse the folder and terminal you set up in Lecture 3. Install the library once with `pip install requests` (Mac: `pip3`), save this as `step2.py`, and run `python step2.py`.\n\n`params` builds the address for you, %20 included.\n\n**No-code track · the same request in your tool**\n\nEvery no-code HTTP step, and every request an agent like Antigravity's builds for you, asks for the same boxes:\n\n| URL | https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello%20world |\n| --- | --- |\n| Method | GET |\n| Headers | None |\n| Body | None |\n\n- Checkpoint: a 200, and I saw a space become %20"},{"h":"03 Create: send a package","a":"03-create-send-a-package","text":"Same echo machine, a different door, a different action. This time you send something: Aarav's Friday workflow, as JSON.\n\n1. **Check the package band.** It holds `{\"text\": \"…\"}` and says *✓ valid JSON*. Try deleting a quote: it turns red. Put it back.\n2. **Press Send.** Look for 200, then find your text at `json.text` in the reply.\n3. **Click Show the raw request.** A package needs one more header, `Content-Type: application/json`, so the machine knows which format to read. Machines do not guess.\n\n**Code track · the same request in Python**\n\n`json=` turns the dictionary into JSON and adds the Content-Type header for you.\n\n**No-code track · the same request in your tool**\n\n| URL | https://httpbin.org/post |\n| --- | --- |\n| Method | POST |\n| Headers | Content-Type: application/json |\n| Body (JSON) | {\"text\": \"I open Jira, …\"} |\n\nIf your tool offers \"Body type\", pick JSON, not text or form. Sending JSON as plain text is a common reason for a 400.\n\n- Checkpoint: a 200, and my text came back at json.text"},{"h":"04 Round Three, for real","a":"04-round-three-for-real","text":"The machine from Round Three is real. It lives at `https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav` and checks the parts in the order you learned them: address, action, key, package. It needs a key, and it issues its own."},{"h":"Part A · Create a key","a":"part-a--create-a-key","text":"1. **Replace `your name`** in the package with your name.\n2. **Press Send.** Expect 201: something new was created, your key. It is copied into Part B's key box for you."},{"h":"Part B · Create a diagnosis","a":"part-b--create-a-diagnosis","text":"1. **Check the key band** holds your `aarav_…` key. Press Show to check it pasted fully.\n2. **Press Send.** Expect 201 and a diagnosis of Aarav's Friday report.\n3. **Press Send the same request again.** The two replies sit side by side. They are identical: this machine follows rules. Remember that for step 8.\n4. For the practice set: take a screenshot of the reply. The reply shows only the start of your key.\n\n**Code track · both requests in Python**\n\n**No-code track · both requests in your tool**\n\n| Box | Part A · key | Part B · diagnosis |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| URL | …/aarav/keys | …/aarav/diagnoses |\n| Method | POST | POST |\n| Headers | Content-Type: application/json | Content-Type: application/json Authorization: Bearer aarav_… |\n| Body (JSON) | {\"name\": \"your name\"} | {\"workflow\": \"I open Jira, …\"} |\n\nThe value of the Authorization header is the word `Bearer`, one space, then the key. Many tools have an \"Auth: Bearer token\" option that writes this header for you.\n\n- Checkpoint: a 201 key, then a 201 diagnosis, twice, identicalPractice set item 1: the screenshot."},{"h":"05 Break it on purpose","a":"05-break-it-on-purpose","text":"Every failed request comes back with a code, and the code points at one part. Learn the codes by causing them, one part at a time.\n\n1. **Pick a button below.** It changes one part of the Part B request above.\n2. **Before you press Send, write down the code you expect.**\n3. **Press Send** in Part B. The red box names the part and what to change.\n4. **Press Put it right**, then try the next button. Fill in the practice set table as you go: what you changed, the code you got, the part it points to.\n\n| Code | Means | Whose fault |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 400 | Package unreadable, or a part is missing | Yours |\n| 401 | Key missing or wrong | Yours |\n| 404 | Nothing at that address | Yours |\n| 405 | That door does not take that action | Yours |"},{"h":"Then: Read what you created","a":"then-read-what-you-created","text":"In Part B, switch the action to **Read · GET** and press Send. You created diagnoses a few minutes ago. The reply is 200 and an empty list. Where did they go? This machine has no database, so nothing it creates is kept. Where data should live is Lecture 6.\n\n**Code track · break it in Python**\n\nChange one thing at a time in your step 4 script (the address, the method, the key, the field name) and print what comes back:\n\n**No-code track · break it in your tool**\n\nChange one box at a time in your Part B step: misspell the URL, switch the method to PUT, delete the Authorization header, rename the `workflow` field. Your tool shows the status code in its run log or output panel.\n\n- Checkpoint: I caused a 400, 401, 404 and 405, and predicted at least two of themPractice set item 2: the table."},{"h":"06 Whose fault? Every code","a":"06-whose-fault-every-code","text":"Each card sends one real request. Before you press Send, pick whose fault the reply will be. 2xx worked. 4xx you sent something wrong. 5xx their machine broke.\n\n- Checkpoint: all eight cards sent, and I can say whose fault each family is"},{"h":"07 Read real traffic","a":"07-read-real-traffic","text":"Every request you sent here is visible in your browser's own tools. Real products work the same way. Reading this is how you check what an app actually sends.\n\n1. **Open DevTools.** Mac: `Cmd` `Option` `I`. Windows: `F12` or `Ctrl` `Shift` `I`. Or right click anywhere, Inspect.\n2. **Click the Network tab,** then the **Fetch/XHR** filter. Click the clear button (a circle with a line).\n3. **Scroll up to step 4, Part B, and press Send.** A row named `diagnoses` appears.\n4. **Click it and find each part:** | Address | Headers → Request URL | | --- | --- | | Action | Headers → Request Method | | Key | Request Headers → authorization | | Package | Payload (what you sent) and Response (what came back) | | Code | Headers → Status Code |\n5. **Optional, a real product:** do the same on ChatGPT. [Reading the Network tab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) walks through it, including the one header you must never expand or paste.\n\n- Checkpoint: I found all five parts of my own request in DevTools"},{"h":"08 Put a model behind it","a":"08-put-a-model-behind-it","text":"Aarav's UI has a face. It needs a brain: a model that reads his workflow and writes a plan. Groq runs open-weight models on its own chips, and you reach them with the same four parts. That is **Groq with a q**, not Grok."},{"h":"Part A · Get a free Groq key (one time, about 3 minutes)","a":"part-a--get-a-free-groq-key-one-time-about-3-minutes","text":"1. **Open [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys)** in a new tab. It is a website, not a download.\n2. **Sign in** with Google. No card needed.\n3. **Click Create API Key,** name it `c8-lecture04`, and click Submit.\n4. **Copy the key now.** Groq shows it once. Do not paste it anywhere except the box below."},{"h":"Part B · Send your first request to a model","a":"part-b--send-your-first-request-to-a-model","text":"1. **Paste the key** into the key band. Wait a second: the model list loads from Groq itself. That load was a Read with your key, your first keyed request. If it says 401, the key was not copied fully.\n2. **Keep the model `openai/gpt-oss-120b`** and the message as it is.\n3. **Press Send.** Expect 200. The answer is at `choices[0].message.content`. The reply also carries a `reasoning` field, the model's scratchpad. Ignore it.\n4. **Press Send the same request again.** Same address, action, key and package. Different words. Compare that with Aarav's service in step 4.\n\nYour key stays in this tab's memory. It is not saved and is gone when you reload. Free tier: 30 requests a minute, counted per Groq account, so use your own account, not a friend's key.\n\nThe door is deterministic. What comes through it is probabilistic.\n\nTwo answers to the same request. Which one is right, and who decides? Write one line on which you would trust, and why. That line is the start of a verifier.\n\n**Code track · the same request in Python**\n\nPut the key in an environment variable, never in the file. In the same terminal:\n\nYour OS\n\nexport GROQ_API_KEY=\"paste-your-key-here\"\n\n$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"paste-your-key-here\"\n\nThen save and run:\n\nThis file becomes the body of your FastAPI route in the Lecture 5 practical.\n\n**No-code track · the same request in your tool**\n\n| URL | https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions |\n| --- | --- |\n| Method | POST |\n| Headers | Authorization: Bearer <your Groq key> Content-Type: application/json |\n| Body (JSON) | {\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Diagnose this workflow: …\"}]} |\n\nStore the key in your tool's secrets or credentials setting, not in the step itself. The answer comes back at `choices[0].message.content`; map that field to your output.\n\n- Checkpoint: a 200 from Groq, two different answers, and one line on which I would trustPractice set item 3."},{"h":"09 Find your key, and where it should live","a":"09-find-your-key-and-where-it-should-live","text":"1. **With DevTools open on Network,** press Send in step 8.\n2. **Click the `completions` row,** then Request Headers. Your Groq key is there in plain text.\n3. **Think it through.** This page is your private tool, so that is fine here. If Aarav's product worked like this, every visitor could open DevTools, read his key, and spend his money. So where should the key live?\n\nBox 1\n\n**UI**what the user sees · no keys\n\n→\n\nBox 2\n\n**Backend**holds the key · keeps the data\n\n→\n\nBox 3\n\n**Groq**the model\n\nThe UI calls your backend. Only the backend calls Groq, with the key kept on the server. The backend is also where data can be kept, which step 5 showed the page and Aarav's machine cannot do. Building that backend is the Lecture 5 practical, for both tracks. More in [Keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety).\n\nWhen you are done practising, you can delete the key at console.groq.com/keys and create a new one for Lecture 5.\n\n- Checkpoint: I found my key in DevTools and can say which box it belongs inPractice set item 4: one sentence. Never a screenshot of a key."},{"h":"10 Design your API","a":"10-design-your-api","text":"You have sent every kind of request. Now design the ones your own app needs. Here is Aarav's, as the example:\n\n| CRUD | Method | Address | Package in | Reply |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Create | POST | /workflows | {\"description\": \"...\"} | 201 + workflow id |\n| Read | GET | /workflows/{id} | none | 200 + the workflow |\n| Create | POST | /workflows/{id}/diagnoses | none; the backend sends the workflow to Groq | 201 + diagnosis |\n| Read | GET | /workflows/{id}/diagnoses | none | 200 + past diagnoses |\n\nYour version, with a blank template and what to submit, is in [the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set).\n\n- Checkpoint: practice set posted in the Discord channelPost in the Discord channel before the Lecture 5 practical, Fri 9 Oct.\n\nNext: [the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set).Stuck? [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) · Curious why each part exists? [The Wire](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire), optional"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-the-wire.md","title":"The Wire","meta":"title: The Wire\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/the-wire.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Sender taps\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1275","sections":[{"h":"","a":"","text":"In Round Three the machine taught you six parts, one error at a time. Why does a request have exactly these parts? Because each one fixed a failure of the one before it.\n\nEvery rung starts with a question. Think about your answer before you touch anything; write it down if you can. Then try the interaction. The name of the invention comes last, behind Reveal."},{"h":"RUNG 1 A wire that is only on or off","a":"rung-1-a-wire-that-is-only-on-or-off","text":"Think first\n\nA wire can only be on or off. How do you send the word HELP down it?\n\nTry it · send HI\n\nPress the key. A short press is a dot, a long press is a dash. Pause to end a letter. On a keyboard, hold the space bar.\n\nReceiver reads\n\nReceiver holds the codebook\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**Morse code**1844 · Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail\n\nA shared codebook of dots and dashes. The first public line ran from Washington to Baltimore on 24 May 1844. The message was \"What hath God wrought.\"\n\nTurn the codebook off and the same pulses still arrive. They just mean nothing. Both ends must hold the same codebook, or the message is noise.\n\nThis becamepackagethe idea of a shared code under every character you send\n\nCommon languageBoth sides agree on what each signal means."},{"h":"RUNG 2 No human at either end","a":"rung-2-no-human-at-either-end","text":"Think first\n\nMorse needs a trained human at each end. What would a machine need instead?\n\nTry it · type anything\n\nEvery character becomes a number, and every number becomes on and off.\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**ASCII**1963 · American Standards Association, committee X3.4\n\nA fixed number for every character, agreed by every machine. A is 65. Seven bits, so 128 characters. No operator needed: a machine reads the bits and knows the letter.\n\n128 slots had no room for नमस्ते. UTF-8 (1992, Ken Thompson and Rob Pike) keeps ASCII as it is and uses more bytes for every other script. That is why A is still 65 today.\n\nThis becamepackageevery character inside it\n\nCommon languageA format machines agree on, with no human in the middle."},{"h":"RUNG 3 A million machines, one network","a":"rung-3-a-million-machines-one-network","text":"Think first\n\nA million machines share one network. A message gets lost halfway. How does it find the right machine, and how do you know it arrived?\n\nTry it · rebuild Aarav's message\n\nThe network cut Aarav's message into packets. They took different roads. Tap them in the right order.\n\nArrived, in the order the network delivered them\n\nRebuilt\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**TCP/IP**1974 paper · Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn · ARPANET switched over on 1 Jan 1983\n\n**IP** gives every machine an address, like 203.0.113.7, and every packet carries it. **TCP** numbers the packets, confirms each one that arrives, and asks again for what is missing. That is how you know it arrived.\n\nNeither one cares what is inside the packets. They only deliver.\n\nThis becameaddress`100x-curriculum.vercel.app` turns into an IP address, as in Lecture 2\n\nProtocolRules for delivery that ignore what is inside."},{"h":"RUNG 4 Delivered, but what do you want?","a":"rung-4-delivered-but-what-do-you-want","text":"Think first\n\nDelivery works. But the receiving machine still does not know what you want. What else must the message say, and what should come back?\n\nTry it · build the message until the machine says 201\n\nTurn each part on or off and watch the reply. The machine checks in order: address, action, key, package.\n\nWrite the address Add the key Action Package\n\n—\n\nSent over Anyone between you and the machine reads:\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**HTTP**1989 to 1991 · Tim Berners-Lee at CERN · about 35 years ago\n\nA request carries an action (the method), an address, headers written on the envelope, and a body inside it. A reply carries a status code and a body. Every API you call today still works this way.\n\n**HTTPS** is HTTP inside a sealed envelope. Switch to http above and anyone between you and the machine can read your key. The S seals it. That was your DM in Round Three.\n\nThis becameactionkeycodethe key is a header, written on the envelope\n\nProtocolInput → Process → Output, across a wire."},{"h":"RUNG 5 Fifty companies, fifty words","a":"rung-5-fifty-companies-fifty-words","text":"Think first\n\nCompany A calls it getDiagnosis, B calls it fetchDiag, C calls it retrieveDiagnosis. What goes wrong when you connect to 50 of them?\n\nTry it · sort twelve names into four buckets\n\nTap a name, then tap the bucket it belongs in. Three companies already need twelve words.\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**REST**2000 · Roy Fielding's PhD dissertation\n\nAddresses are nouns: `/diagnoses`, `/workflows/7`. A handful of shared verbs act on them: **Create, Read, Update, Delete**. On the wire they are spelled POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, and DELETE. Learn four words once, and every REST API speaks them.\n\nCRUD is a term from 1983. It comes back in Lecture 6, where a database does the same four things to a table.\n\nThis becameaddressaction`/diagnoses` plus CREATE\n\nProtocolAn agreement on how to use HTTP. Strictly a design style, not a protocol of its own."},{"h":"RUNG 6 Two programs, one package","a":"rung-6-two-programs-one-package","text":"Think first\n\nThe machine said 400 until you used curly braces. Aarav's UI might be written in Python and the diagnosis machine in JavaScript. Why would both accept curly braces?\n\nTry it · break the package, then fix it\n\nThe same data, in a format someone made up\n\nWORKFLOW>>I open Jira, read the week's tickets, write a status report and post it on Slack;;TIME>>90min;;\n\nWould a Python program and a JavaScript program both read this? Who wrote the rules for `>>` and `;;`? What happens when the workflow itself contains `;;`?\n\n**Reveal what humans invented**\n\n**JSON**2001 · Douglas Crockford · standardised as ECMA-404 (2013) and RFC 8259 (2017)\n\nPlain text: keys and values in curly braces, every key in double quotes. JSON is a **format, not a language**. A.docx opens in Word and in Google Docs because both agree on the file format. JSON works the same way: every programming language and every no-code tool reads it, which is why it became the web's default package format.\n\nThe error you just caused is the 400 from Round Three: \"Can't read.\"\n\nThis becamepackage`{\"workflow\": \"...\"}`\n\nCommon languageFor the contents of the envelope."},{"h":"CLOSE Six inventions, two principles","a":"close-six-inventions-two-principles","text":"Look at the envelope. Every part of your Round Three message is there, and each one exists because something failed without it.\n\nCommon language"},{"h":"What the signals mean","a":"what-the-signals-mean","text":"- Rung 1 · Morse code\n- Rung 2 · ASCII, then UTF-8\n- Rung 6 · JSON\n\nProtocol"},{"h":"How the signals travel","a":"how-the-signals-travel","text":"- Rung 3 · TCP/IP\n- Rung 4 · HTTP, then HTTPS\n- Rung 5 · REST, a style for using HTTP\n\nFrom Lecture 2, now proven six times\n\nAn interface is a common language plus a protocol.\n\nAnd every rung was the same shape:\n\nInput\n\n**The request**address, action, key, package\n\n→\n\nProcess\n\n**The machine**on the other end of the wire\n\n→\n\nOutput\n\n**The reply**code and package"},{"h":"One question to keep","a":"one-question-to-keep","text":"Groq is not OpenAI, yet Groq's address says `/openai/v1`. Once enough programs speak one format, competitors adopt it, so code written for one works on the other. ASCII won that way. HTTP won that way. JSON won that way. Now the AI world is doing it with the `messages` package.\n\nIn a few weeks a model will need to talk to tools it has never seen. Same answer: an agreed JSON format and a protocol.\n\nEvery part is there because something failed without it.Next: send a real request in the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder) · read it all again in the [lecture notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-04-troubleshooting.md","title":"Troubleshooting: When a Request Fails","meta":"title: Troubleshooting: When a Request Fails\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Troubleshooting · Lecture 4: API as the second interface · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1935","sections":[{"h":"Troubleshooting: when a request fails","a":"troubleshooting-when-a-request-fails","text":"A failed request is not a dead end. It comes back with a code, and the code names the part of your request to look at.\n\nPost-lecture · both tracksGoes with the Request Builder\n\nHow to read a failure\n\nThe machine does not guess. 404 points at the address, 405 at the action, 401 at the key, 400 at the package, and 429 means too many requests. A 5xx is their machine, not yours. Change one part at a time, so you know which change fixed it."},{"h":"01 First, Rule Out the Network","a":"01-first-rule-out-the-network","text":"If no request gets through at all, paste `https://httpbin.org/get?text=hello` into your browser's address bar. If you see JSON with `\"text\": \"hello\"`, your connection is fine and the problem is in the request. If that fails too, the problem is your network, not your request. If httpbin itself is down, try `https://postman-echo.com/get?text=hello`."},{"h":"02 The Errors You Should Expect","a":"02-the-errors-you-should-expect","text":"Search by the code or by words from the error message. Each entry names the part of the request, the usual cause and the fix.\n\n**401Invalid API Key (Groq)**\n\nPart\n\nkey\n\nCause\n\nA space or a line break was copied with the key, the key was mistyped, the header reads `Authorization: gsk_…` without `Bearer`, or the key was revoked.\n\nFix\n\nPaste the key again from the source, with nothing around it. The header must read `Authorization: Bearer gsk_…`, with one space after Bearer. If you deleted the key in the console, create a new one.\n\n**401Key not recognised / Who are you? Key needed (Aarav)**\n\nPart\n\nkey\n\nCause\n\nNo key at all, a key without `Bearer`, a Groq key sent to Aarav (or an Aarav key sent to Groq), or a key put in the address as `?key=`. The reply's `hint` field says which.\n\nFix\n\nCreate a key with `POST https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav/keys` and the package `{\"name\": \"your name\"}`. Send it as `Authorization: Bearer aarav_…`. Keys go on the envelope, never in the address: anyone can read an address.\n\n**400Add the header Content-Type: application/json**\n\nPart\n\npackage\n\nCause\n\nThe package was sent without saying what format it is in. Many tools default to plain text or form data.\n\nFix\n\nAdd the header `Content-Type: application/json`. In a no-code HTTP step, pick \"JSON\" as the body type rather than \"Text\" or \"Raw\".\n\n**400Can't read / Unexpected token / invalid JSON**\n\nPart\n\npackage\n\nCause\n\nA missing comma between fields, a missing closing quote or brace, a comma after the last field, or curly \"smart\" quotes pasted from a document or a chat app.\n\nFix\n\nStart from the Builder's pre-filled package and change only the text inside the quotes. Keys and text values use straight double quotes: `{\"workflow\": \"...\"}`. If your text contains a double quote, write it as `\\\"`.\n\n**400The workflow field is missing or empty (Aarav)**\n\nPart\n\npackage\n\nCause\n\nThe JSON is valid but the field is named something else (`\"text\"`, `\"description\"`, `\"Workflow\"`), or the value is empty.\n\nFix\n\nThe name must match exactly, in lower case: `{\"workflow\": \"I open Jira…\"}`. Agreed format means both sides use the same names.\n\n**404The model does not exist or you do not have access to it (Groq)**\n\nPart\n\npackage, the `model` field\n\nCause\n\nA typo in the model name, or a retired model. `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` was retired for free and developer accounts on 16 Aug 2026, and many tutorials still use it.\n\nFix\n\nUse `openai/gpt-oss-120b`, or `openai/gpt-oss-20b` as the fallback. The Builder's dropdown loads only the models your key can use, so it cannot offer a retired one.\n\n**404Not found. Doors: …**\n\nPart\n\naddress\n\nCause\n\nA typo in the path, a missing `/aarav`, `/diagnosis` instead of `/diagnoses`, or a path that does not exist on that machine. `GET /aarav/diagnoses/{id}` also returns 404, because this machine stores nothing yet.\n\nFix\n\nRead the doors listed in the reply and copy one. `GET https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/aarav` lists them all.\n\n**405This door takes CREATE (POST) and READ (GET)**\n\nPart\n\naction\n\nCause\n\nThe address exists but does not take that action. For example, Update or Delete on `/aarav/diagnoses`, or a GET to Groq's chat address.\n\nFix\n\nCheck the reply's `Allow` header and pick one of those methods. Ask which CRUD action you mean first, then spell it: Create is POST, Read is GET.\n\n**429Too many requests / rate limit reached**\n\nPart\n\nrate\n\nCause\n\nMore than 30 requests a minute (or 1,000 a day) on one Groq account. Usually a key shared by a group, or a script sending in a loop. Limits belong to the account, not the key.\n\nFix\n\nWait the number of seconds in the `retry-after` header. Each student uses their own account. Creating more keys on the same account does not help.\n\n**Blocked by CORS policy / Failed to fetch**\n\nPart\n\nnone: the browser stopped the request before it left\n\nCause\n\nBrowsers only let a page call another site if that site says it allows it. Groq, Aarav and httpbin allow it, so the Builder works. A page opened as a `file://` path, an extension that blocks requests, a typo in the address (which reaches a site that does not allow it), or a site that does not allow browser calls at all will fail here. There is no status code, because no request reached the machine.\n\nFix\n\nCheck the address first. Open the Console tab: the exact message names the site. Try the same request from the Builder or with curl. If it works there, the request is fine and the browser is the blocker. Backends are not subject to CORS, which is one more reason the call to Groq moves to a backend.\n\n**The no-code tool sends the body as text, not JSON**\n\nPart\n\npackage\n\nCause\n\nThe HTTP step's body type is set to Text, Raw or Form. The machine receives a string with escaped quotes, or form fields, and replies 400.\n\nFix\n\nSet the body type to JSON and add `Content-Type: application/json` if the tool does not add it. Paste the package from the Builder, which you know works. Compare both in the Network tab or the tool's request log.\n\n**The Groq reply shows only \"reasoning\" / the content looks empty**\n\nPart\n\nreply package\n\nCause\n\n`openai/gpt-oss-120b` is a reasoning model. Its reply has a `reasoning` field, the model's scratchpad, next to the answer. Reading the wrong field, or running out of tokens while it is still reasoning, leaves the answer empty.\n\nFix\n\nRead `choices[0].message.content` and ignore `reasoning`. If `content` is empty and `finish_reason` is `length`, remove any small `max_tokens` limit and send again.\n\n**500Internal server error**\n\nPart\n\nserver: theirs, not yours\n\nCause\n\nTheir machine failed. `/aarav/broken` returns this on purpose for practice.\n\nFix\n\nNothing in your request to fix. Wait and try again, or check the service's status page. 4xx is yours; 5xx is theirs."},{"h":"03 The Help Post","a":"03-the-help-post","text":"A good help post has exactly three parts, in this order. Helpers need the code and the error. They never need your key.\n\n**Status code and error (exact text):** 401 {\"error\":{\"message\":\"Invalid API Key\",\"code\":\"invalid_api_key\"}} **What I tried:** Pasted the key again, checked there is a space after Bearer **The request, key masked:** POST https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions Authorization: Bearer gsk_•••• Content-Type: application/json {\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}]}\n\nPost in the Discord channel. If you fixed it yourself, reply in the thread with the fix. Your fix is someone else's blocker."},{"h":"04 Office Hour: Thursday 8 October, 8:30 pm IST","a":"04-office-hour-thursday-8-october-830-pm-ist","text":"The next office hour is on **Thursday 8 October 2026, from 8:30 pm IST**. It goes through this week's work: your first Groq 200, reading real traffic, and your API design. Come with your work and one question, in the template below.\n\nThe milestone for Thursday\n\nSteps 1 to 8 of the [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder) done: a 201 from Aarav's service, your break-it table, and a 200 from Groq. Plus a first draft of your API table from step 10. The full [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set) is due later, before the Lecture 5 practical on Fri 9 Oct."},{"h":"Where are you?","a":"where-are-you","text":"Find the row that matches you, and do what it says before Thursday.\n\n| If this is you | You are | Before Thursday |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Steps 1 to 8 done and an API table drafted | On track | Bring your table. Designs are reviewed in the room. |\n| Steps 1 to 5 done, not yet through 6 to 8 | Nearly there | Do step 8 first: a 200 from Groq is the step the office hour builds on. Use the [Network tab guide](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/network-tab) for step 7. |\n| A request keeps failing and you do not know why | Blocked on one part | Read the code: 404 address, 405 action, 401 key, 400 package. Search the errors above. Still stuck after 20 minutes? Post the error in the template below. |\n| Not started | Behind | Do steps 1 to 4 before Thursday, so you have a real 201 of your own to talk about. Nothing to install. |"},{"h":"Your question template","a":"your-question-template","text":"Post your question in the Discord channel before the office hour, so it can be answered first. Use the first template for a request that fails, and the second for a question about your design.\n\n**When a request fails** **Where I am:** Step 8 of 10 · no-code track **What I expected:** 200 and an answer from the model **What I got (status code and exact text, copied):** 401 {\"error\":{\"message\":\"Invalid API Key\",\"code\":\"invalid_api_key\"}} **What I tried:** Pasted the key again, checked there is a space after Bearer **The request, key masked:** POST https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions Authorization: Bearer gsk_•••• Content-Type: application/json\n\n**When it is about your design** **Where I am:** Step 10 of 10 · code track **The row I am unsure about:** Create · POST · /reports · {\"tickets\": [...]} · 201 + report **My question:** Is drafting a report a Create, if I send the same tickets every week? **What I think, and why:** Create, because a new report exists after the request that did not exist before"},{"h":"Checklist before Thursday","a":"checklist-before-thursday","text":"Tick each item when it is ready. Ticks save on this device.\n\n- Steps 1 to 8 ticked in the Request BuilderEach step ends with a checkpoint. Tick it when you have the result.\n- A 201 from Aarav's serviceStep 4A screenshot of the reply with the diagnosis in it, key masked.\n- The break-it table filled inStep 5400, 401, 404, 405 and the 500: what you changed, the code, the part it points to.\n- Your own Groq key returns a 200, twiceStep 8Your own account, not a shared one. Keep both answers.\n- A first draft of your API tableStep 10It does not need to be finished. A draft is what gets reviewed.\n- One question posted in the templateIn the Discord channel, before the office hour.\n\nReady for Thursday\n\n0 / 6"},{"h":"What to bring","a":"what-to-bring","text":"- The [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder) open in a tab, with your track picked.\n- Your break-it table and your API table draft, ready to share on screen.\n- The exact error, copied, not retyped. Have the failing request open in DevTools if your question is about traffic.\n- Your Groq key where you can paste it from, never in the chat. If it shows on a shared screen, revoke it afterwards and create a new one."},{"h":"Before Fri 9 Oct","a":"before-fri-9-oct","text":"- All five deliverables of the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set), posted in the Discord channel, before the Lecture 5 practical.\n- Assignment 1, due the same day. It does not depend on the practice set.\n\nThe code names the part.Companion pages: [Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder) · [keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety) · [kit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/kit)"}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building APIs and backend","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building APIs and backend\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 5\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 5 · 9 October 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 2767","sections":[{"h":"Open the project. Read before you approve.","a":"open-the-project-read-before-you-approve","text":"We moved the app into Antigravity, mapped its files, directed the code in plain English, and called two outside services.\n\nIn [Lecture 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes-no-code) you turned Stitch screens into an app you can click, in Google AI Studio. In [Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes) you learned how one machine asks another for work: the API. Today we opened that app's code in Antigravity and connected it to outside services."},{"h":"01 What the app is still missing","a":"01-what-the-app-is-still-missing","text":"We began with the route so far, on the Magic Mirror app: a customer uploads a photo, picks a hairstyle and sees the new look. The PRD became screen flows. Stitch drew every screen. AI Studio made the screens clickable.\n\n> \"Are we done with it? What was missing after Google AI Studio?\"\n\nThe chat answered fast: a backend, somewhere to store data, the rules of the business. All correct. An app you can click through, with none of that underneath, is a **prototype**. The instructor listed what it cannot answer yet:\n\n- **Users.** What happens when hundreds of people use it, and each needs an account?\n- **Keys.** Where do your Gemini or OpenAI keys sit, safely?\n- **Failure.** What does the app do when an API fails?\n\nAI Studio is built for speed. It can reach the layers underneath, but not easily, and you soon run out of things you can change.\n\n> Sketch: Four boxes in a row. ChatGPT or Claude gives the idea and the PRD. Stitch gives drawings of every screen. Google AI Studio gives a prototype you can click. A dashed line marks where AI Studio gets uncomfortable. Past the line, Antigravity gives a product you control.\n>\n> Caption: Each tool has an edge. You move to the next one when you hit it, and not before.\n\nThe instructor's own habit: take the prototype as far as AI Studio allows, then move. Plenty of ideas never need to. When you want finer control, where do you go?"},{"h":"02 Move the project into Antigravity","a":"02-move-the-project-into-antigravity","text":"In AI Studio you describe and it builds. [Antigravity](https://antigravity.google), also from Google, lets you open every file and see every change, with an LLM in a chat beside them. An app like that is an **IDE** (integrated development environment). The room did the move together.\n\n1. In [AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com), click Code, next to Preview. You see the project's files.\n2. Click Export. Three choices: push to GitHub, download as a zip, export to Antigravity.\n3. Choose Export to Antigravity, then Export.\n4. In the window that appears, click Open in Antigravity. The project loads, with your AI Studio chat.\n\nThree troubles came up, each with a fix.\n\n| The trouble | The fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| An error during export | Paste the error into the AI Studio chat and ask it to fix that part. |\n| You land in a chat screen with no files | Antigravity also has a chat-only app. Click Open IDE at its top right, or install \"Antigravity IDE standalone\" from the downloads page. |\n| Export still fails (AI Studio is still being tested) | Go through GitHub. |\n\nGitHub is a website that keeps a copy of a project's files. One project there is a **repository**. In AI Studio, choose the GitHub option, create a new repository and push (upload) your files. On GitHub, open the Code menu, pick HTTPS and copy the link. In the IDE, open a new window, click Clone Repository and paste it. To **clone** is to download a copy.\n\n> Sketch: Two routes from Google AI Studio to the Antigravity IDE. The direct route, Export to Antigravity, carries the code and the chat history. The fallback goes down to GitHub with a push and up to Antigravity with a clone, and carries the code only.\n>\n> Caption: Both routes bring every file. Only the direct export brings the chat that shaped the app.\n\nSomeone asked about later changes. Edit in AI Studio again and you must push and clone again. So finish there first, then move once. The files are open. What do you do first?"},{"h":"03 Ask for a map before you change anything","a":"03-ask-for-a-map-before-you-change-anything","text":"You do not start building. You ask what is there. The IDE has the list of files on the left, the open file in the middle and the chat on the right. Start a new conversation, paste the prompt, pick an LLM and send. The instructor recommended Claude Sonnet, because it reads a project carefully.\n\nThe prompt was shared in the session's chat. It asks the LLM to analyse the project and explain its structure. That means what connects to what, and how one user action flows through the files.\n\nThe reply is a file at the bottom of the chat. Click it to open. The instructor's listed things like these (yours will differ):\n\n| In the project | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| Screens | One file for each step the user sees. |\n| Components | Reusable parts of a screen, such as a button. |\n| Data and services | What is stored, and the calls to outside APIs such as Gemini. |\n| The main flow | The barber starts a session: which file runs, where it goes next. |\n\nYou do not need to read the code itself. Aim to know 60 to 70% of what sits where. Why? The instructor's example: you ask for the main buttons to be red. The LLM may decide every button on every screen counts. If you know which component holds your button, you name that file and only that file changes.\n\n> An open-ended request lets the LLM guess. A request that names the file does not.\n\nRun the same prompt on any project you open. But the files are still code. How do you read them?"},{"h":"04 Read the code as plain English","a":"04-read-the-code-as-plain-english","text":"In the same chat, the instructor asked the LLM to explain one part of the app: the part that asks Gemini for style advice. Then he added the line he uses all the time:\n\n> \"Write everything in pseudocode, and explain in plain English in maximum one para.\"\n\nWhat came back had no programming language in it. It read like instructions. If the API key is blank, return this message. Otherwise send this prompt (\"You are a master barber\", with gaps for the customer's details) and return the reply.\n\nThat is **pseudocode**: a program's logic in plain English, step by step, with \"if this happens, then do that\". It will not run. You read the code as pseudocode and you ask for changes in pseudocode. The LLM writes the real code.\n\n> The clearer your logic in plain English, the less the LLM has to guess.\n\nA tip: LLMs answer simple questions at length. Ask for one or two lines, then ask about the part you did not follow. Now you can read a change. Should you accept it?"},{"h":"05 Read, predict, question, then approve","a":"05-read-predict-question-then-approve","text":"When you ask for a change, Antigravity plans it first. It lists the components it will rework and the API calls it will add. Then it waits for you.\n\nThe instructor's line: your job is to evaluate the AI before you approve it. Read the steps it lists. Predict which parts of your app the change will touch. Ask why it chose this way. Then approve or reject, and test what it built.\n\n> Sketch: A loop of six steps. The coding tool proposes a change. You read the plan. You predict what will change and question it. You approve or reject. The tool builds. You test it. Then the loop starts again for the next change.\n>\n> Caption: The tool writes at two points. Every other step is yours.\n\nSkip the reading and the tool adds code nobody asked for. The instructor's picture: to add 2 and 2, it writes a whole thesis.\n\nTo predict what a change will touch, you need the parts every app is made of. What are they?"},{"h":"06 The screen, the worker and the memory","a":"06-the-screen-the-worker-and-the-memory","text":"Every project comes down to three parts. One is the screen, in the browser or on the phone. One is the backend from Lecture 4, which does the work. One remembers.\n\n> \"Which of these do you think the user can see?\"\n\nThe room got it at once: only the screen. That part is the **front end**: the pages, the buttons, and the API requests they send. The backend holds the rules of the business, the calls to outside services and the secret keys. The part that remembers is the **database**.\n\n> Sketch: A person looks at the front end, the screen with its buttons and pages. The front end and the backend talk through an API. The backend does the work and holds the keys. Behind it sits the database, the memory. The user sees only the front end. The logic, the keys and the data are hidden from the user.\n>\n> Caption: Anything on the left, a curious user can open and read. So nothing secret goes there.\n\n> Never trust the browser with your secrets.\n\nThe instructor called this the most common mistake of people who build by prompt alone: the API key ends up in the front end. Which toolkit each part is written with matters less. Describe your app and your expected users, and ask the coding tool which fits.\n\nSo where exactly do the keys go?"},{"h":"07 Where the keys go, and five rules","a":"07-where-the-keys-go-and-five-rules","text":"Most projects made for the browser share a layout: screens in a components folder, requests in a services folder, secret keys in one file. The ending of a file's name tells you its kind.\n\n| Ending | What is inside |\n| --- | --- |\n| .tsx | A screen or a component. |\n| .ts,.js | Code for the logic. |\n| .json | Data. package.json is the project's shopping list. |\n| .css | Styling. |\n| .env | The secret keys. |\n\n> \"Which of these should you never commit to Git?\"\n\n(Git is the system GitHub runs on. To commit is to save files into it.) One of you answered at once: the **.env** file. Push it and you have published your keys. Even to a private repository? Still no: automatic steps that run on your code can end up showing them.\n\nThen the five rules the instructor gives his coding tool:\n\n- **Small components.** Do not let one grow complicated.\n- **Reusable functions.** A function is a named piece of code. Sending a file to the backend is written once and used many times.\n- **Clear names.** Each function and button says what it does.\n- **Screens apart from logic.** The front end shows. The backend decides.\n- **No repeats.** The same steps never appear twice.\n\nYou know the parts. How do you follow one click through them?"},{"h":"08 Trace one click, with Groq","a":"08-trace-one-click-with-groq","text":"Do not guess what a button does. Trace it. The user taps, a function runs, and it sends an API request to your backend. The backend calls an outside service, and the screen shows the reply. When the result surprises you, the instructor asks: What did I expect? What happened? Where did the two part ways? What evidence do I have? How should it change?\n\nWe tried it on a small page, a hairstyle recommender that calls Groq (the company that runs LLMs fast, from Lecture 4). The instructor shared it as a zip in the session's chat. Unzip it and open index.html.\n\n1. **Click with no key.** An error: the Groq API key is missing.\n2. **Make a key.** Sign in at [Groq](https://groq.com), open API keys and create one. It is shown once, so save it. Paste it into the page.\n3. **Fill the form.** Face shape, hair type, hair length and the style you want. The instructor chose straight, medium, \"effortless flow\".\n4. **Click Find my style.** The LLM replies with JSON: a style name, a match score, a reason and styling tips. The page shows it as tidy cards.\n5. **Read the right side.** It shows what the click sent: the header with your key, the body with your choices. Then the response.\n6. **Break it.** Leave a field out and the reply is a bad request that names the missing field.\n\nSo the form is the package from Lecture 4, filled in by clicks. Text is one kind of reply. What about a picture?"},{"h":"09 An image API, left for you to wire","a":"09-an-image-api-left-for-you-to-wire","text":"The Magic Mirror has to return a picture: this customer, with that hairstyle. For that the room opened [Replicate](https://replicate.com), a site that offers many image generators through one API. You pay as you use it.\n\nSearch for GPT Image, OpenAI's image generator. Its page shows what goes in: your OpenAI key, the two images (the customer's photo and the hairstyle) and a prompt. The API tab shows the same request written out, ready to copy.\n\n> Sketch: Four boxes in a row. The front end collects a photo, a hairstyle and a description. It sends them to the backend, which builds the request and adds the key. The backend calls Replicate, the image API. Replicate calls OpenAI's GPT Image, which makes the picture. The picture travels back the same way, and the front end shows loading, then the image, or the failure.\n>\n> Caption: Top arrows carry the request, bottom arrows the picture. The key joins at the backend and never reaches the screen.\n\nThe instructor stopped there on purpose, so the wiring is yours. Someone asked what exactly to carry across. Copy the generator's name from its Replicate page. In Antigravity, describe the whole flow and ask the tool to connect it to your project. Read what it does while it works.\n\nWhen the tool says the feature is complete, do you believe it? Test first:\n\n- **Failure.** What shows when the API fails, or no image comes back?\n- **Storage.** Where is the key kept? Where is the image kept? Can another user open it?\n- **Impatience.** What happens on a double click, or after a 10 second wait?\n\n> The AI writes the code. You direct, judge and debug.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**Export to Antigravity failed, so you went through GitHub. What reached Antigravity, and what did not?**\n\nEvery file arrived. The AI Studio chat did not.\n\n**A friend pastes the Replicate key into the screen's code, \"because it works\". What is wrong?**\n\nThe front end is the one part a user can open and read. The key belongs in the backend, in the.env file, and that file never goes to GitHub.\n\n**You ask for the main button to be red. The tool proposes changes to twelve files. What do you do before you approve?**\n\nFind the component that holds that button in your map. You predicted one file, so question the plan. Then name the file in your request.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Prototype**: An app you can click through, with no backend underneath yet.\n- **IDE**: The app where you open a project's files and work on them.\n- **Repository**: One project's files, kept on GitHub.\n- **Clone**: To download a copy of a repository.\n- **Pseudocode**: A program's logic in plain English, step by step. It does not run.\n- **Front end**: The part of an app the user sees and can open: the screens.\n- **Database**: The part of an app that remembers: where data is stored.\n- **.env**: The file that holds a project's secret keys. Never pushed to GitHub.\n\nKeep this\n\nA prototype becomes a product when you open its files and take control. Ask for a map of the project first. Read and request changes in plain English, read each plan before you approve it, and keep every key in the backend."},{"h":"10 Your turn: wire the image API","a":"10-your-turn-wire-the-image-api","text":"Do it on the Magic Mirror app or on your own. Sign up on Replicate and pick an image generator. Before you prompt, write the flow in order: the input, the steps, the APIs, what is stored, the output. Then ask Antigravity to build it, and run the loop from section 05 on every change.\n\nList each place you get stuck, and bring the list to the office hour.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-05-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: APIs, Rip Off the Cushion","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: APIs, Rip Off the Cushion\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes.html\nlecture: 5\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 5 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1670","sections":[{"h":"APIs: Rip Off the Cushion","a":"apis-rip-off-the-cushion","text":"From an echo chat with no process to a raw POST request that plans Aarav's week. Read once end to end, then rebuild section 10 and section 13 on your own machine.\n\nFri 9 Oct 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCohort 8 · Module 2"},{"h":"01 Where We Left Off: A Chat That Repeats You","a":"01-where-we-left-off-a-chat-that-repeats-you","text":"The Gradio app from the last code lecture: `message` is the input, the `return` line is the output, and there is nothing in between.\n\nWhat you have to find is the **process**: the step that turns your user's input into an output they can use. You cannot write the process for a problem you have not done by hand."},{"h":"02 The Verifier Most People Skipped","a":"02-the-verifier-most-people-skipped","text":"A spec has three parts: the **person**, the **golden dataset** (ideal input and ideal output, written after talking to real users) and the **verifier**, a written condition that tells you whether an output is good. If you cannot tell a good output from a bad one in your user's domain, do not build yet.\n\nAdding two numbers, an exam result and a refund decision gave three families: verification by rule, by judgment, and unverifiable. A system that gives the same output for the same input every time is **deterministic**, and this part of Module 2 lives there."},{"h":"03 Why Learn It When Agents Can Write It","a":"03-why-learn-it-when-agents-can-write-it","text":"**Thinking can be outsourced. Understanding cannot.** We are not learning code to write it faster by hand. We are learning how deterministic systems behave, because a system you do not understand is a system you cannot verify: an agent, a mobile app or legacy enterprise software."},{"h":"04 Filling the Process With One Line","a":"04-filling-the-process-with-one-line","text":"With the manual work for Aarav done, the process fits in one line of pseudocode:\n\nThe **prompt** is the valuable part: the instruction refined after talking to real Aaravs. The task was to make that line real with a Groq key ([console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys)) and GPT OSS 120B, free at 30 requests per minute."},{"h":"05 Four Minutes, and What the Machine Said","a":"05-four-minutes-and-what-the-machine-said","text":"| Deep | An OpenAI client pointed at the Groq address. Errors: missing API key, then `gr` not defined. Every error is a hint about what broke. |\n| --- | --- |\n| Anand | `LLM` was never defined, so the call could not run. |\n| Bhavani | It ran: the only working answer of the first three. It also carried things nobody asked for and no prompt at all. |\n| Abhinand | Cleanest: template kept, process filled. But the interface was swapped, names changed for no reason, and the prompt was the docs' default. |"},{"h":"06 Working Is Not the Same as Useful","a":"06-working-is-not-the-same-as-useful","text":"Bhavani's code passed; the plan did not. It was full of code, and Aarav is a business analyst who cannot read Python yet. If the most complex technology on Earth does not deliver value to the end user, it was a waste of everyone's time."},{"h":"07 The Minimum Path","a":"07-the-minimum-path","text":"Under time pressure with one clear instruction, nobody changes the interface or adds ten parameters. Extra, unrequested structure is the fingerprint of pasting the task into an AI without understanding. Ask first: what is the smallest change that makes this work?"},{"h":"08 Go to the Source, and Read the 404","a":"08-go-to-the-source-and-read-the-404","text":"Go to the official source before any AI. Groq's quickstart (`pip install groq`, export `GROQ_API_KEY`, a short script) failed as pasted:\n\n**Error 404.** *The model `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` does not exist or you do not have access to it.*\n\n404 is the client's mistake and means *not found*. Not found what? The message says: the model. Change the name to GPT OSS and run again. The status code tells you whose fault it is; the message tells you what."},{"h":"09 Free Is a Design Decision","a":"09-free-is-a-design-decision","text":"Groq's free tier: 30 requests per minute, roughly 200,000 tokens and 1,000 requests a day. With a per-user quota, that powers a real MVP for one or two users at zero cost."},{"h":"10 Where Is the URL? Four Principles in the Code","a":"10-where-is-the-url-four-principles-in-the-code","text":"Tested live in [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app) (a browser address bar only sends GET). `api.groq.com` on its own failed: DNS finds a server, it does not tell the server what you want. The API reference gives the full endpoint:\n\nPOST https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions\n\nThe whole Groq API, tested without writing code. You can do this in the browser right now."},{"h":"11 Prompt + Input = Package","a":"11-prompt--input--package","text":"The message content became a plain instruction followed by Aarav's real workflow:\n\n*\"Here is the workflow of an analyst working at an IT company in India. Diagnose and generate a plan to automate this work, or a part of the work.\"* [Aarav's workflow pasted below]\n\nBoth share one `content` field, but the instruction is yours and stays the same; the workflow is his and changes every time. The loop: chat UI captures input, prompt plus input goes to the LLM API, JSON comes back, you extract `content`, Gradio renders it to Aarav. Judge it against the golden pair, not \"looks good\"."},{"h":"12 The Cushion: What a Library Hides","a":"12-the-cushion-what-a-library-hides","text":"In the SDK code that worked, neither the URL nor POST is visible: both are hidden behind **abstraction**.\n\nA passenger enjoys the comfort of a car. A driver understands its controls and knows what to do when things go wrong.\n\n**Comfort without understanding comes at the cost of control.**\n\nIn software, SDKs, libraries, no-code/vibe-coding tools are like comfortable passenger seats. They make the journey easier by hiding the machinery underneath.\n\nBut hidden machinery doesn't disappear.\n\nWhen something breaks, what you understand determines what you can fix.\n\n**To drive software machines, understand its controls: APIs, endpoints, authentication, requests, and responses.**\n\n**Use abstractions for comfort. Build understanding for control.**"},{"h":"13 Ripping Off the Cushion: requests.post","a":"13-ripping-off-the-cushion-requestspost","text":"Same as the browser test, now in Python, with every principle visible:\n\n- **The key** comes from your environment. Set it with `export GROQ_API_KEY=...` in the terminal; it vanishes on restart.\n- **The content** is static prompt plus dynamic `message`. That one line in front of Aarav's text is the P in input, process, output.\n- If `import requests` fails, run `pip install requests`."},{"h":"14 Reading the Plan as Aarav Would","a":"14-reading-the-plan-as-aarav-would","text":"The plan used Aarav's real week: open Jira, read the tickets, write the status, post on Slack. Useful. Then it drifted into architecture, databases and languages: jargon. The verifier produced a prompt change: no jargon, one step at a time, the minimum action needed now. One JQL line on day one, Python on day two, the Jira API by week four."},{"h":"15 Any Machine, Four Lines","a":"15-any-machine-four-lines","text":"The raw version is provider-agnostic: swap the URL and key for OpenAI's and the same code runs. No new package, no dependence on anyone's SDK.\n\n**The four questions for any machine.** What is the address? What is the action? Do I need a key, and how do I get it? What package does it expect? Answer those and about four lines connect your app to any machine on the internet.\n\nPractice targets: the [public-apis list](https://github.com/public-apis/public-apis)."},{"h":"16 From the Q&A","a":"16-from-the-qa","text":"| Make my API run a shell script? | Bash and Python speak different languages. You need a common language and rules: an interface problem, the one this module keeps solving. Send your script for a specific answer. |\n| --- | --- |\n| The key showed in the network inspector | Covered when you build your own API. Security comes in levels, chosen by what is at stake: a to-do app and a Fortune 500 company do not need the same lock. |\n| Host my own API locally? | A recorded video is coming. Watch it, then bring the question to office hours. |\n| Feedback | Your best feedback comes from your own Aarav. Queries on Discord, not DMs; Monday and Tuesday are the reply slots. |"},{"h":"17 Words You Met","a":"17-words-you-met","text":"**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Process**: The step that turns input into output: your prompt plus the user's message, sent to an LLM.\n- **Golden dataset**: Ideal input and output pairs, written from doing the job by hand.\n- **Verifier**: A written condition that says whether an output is good enough for the user.\n- **Deterministic system**: Same input, same output, every time. Verifiable by rule.\n- **Endpoint**: The full address of one resource, path included. The domain alone is not enough.\n- **DNS**: Domain Name System: finds which server a domain points to.\n- **Status code**: The machine's verdict: 2xx worked, 4xx your mistake, 5xx theirs.\n- **Abstraction**: Hiding detail so you work at a higher level. Comfort that costs understanding.\n- **SDK**: A provider's code wrapped around address, action, key and package.\n- **Environment variable**: Where keys live, outside your code, so they never reach a screenshot.\n- **Rate limit**: Requests allowed per minute or day. Groq free: 30 per minute.\n- **JQL**: Jira Query Language, which the plan suggested for pulling Aarav's tickets."},{"h":"18 Before the Next Office Hour","a":"18-before-the-next-office-hour","text":"1. **Submit Assignment 1** if you have not. The deadline was 9 October; the late window is open with a penalty.\n2. **Run the raw request in [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app).** POST, full endpoint, your key, a working model. Predict the status code first.\n3. **Rebuild section 13** for your own observation (or Aarav's), with your prompt and your user's real input.\n4. **Check one output against your golden pair** and write one prompt change the check forced.\n5. **Post a screenshot** of the working app on the Discord code channel. Earliest posts are reviewed first.\n\nUse abstractions for comfort. Build understanding for control.\n\nKeep this\n\nAn LLM is not magic inside your app. It is a machine at an address, and you reach it like any API: an address, an action, a key and a package. The process is your prompt plus your user's input, sent to that address. None of it counts until your verifier says the output helps the person you built it for."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-05-overview.md","title":"Building APIs and Backend","meta":"title: Building APIs and Backend\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/index.html\nlecture: 5\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 5 · Fri 9 Oct 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 231","sections":[{"h":"Building APIs and backend","a":"building-apis-and-backend","text":"> Sketch: Whiteboard sketch. Left, the passenger: asleep in the back seat of a car on a soft cushion labelled SDKs, someone else at the wheel; when something breaks, only question marks. Right, the driver: a dashboard with four dials, endpoint, API key, request body and response; when something breaks, check the four dials.\n\nA passenger enjoys the comfort of a car. A driver understands its controls and knows what to do when things go wrong.\n\n**Comfort without understanding comes at the cost of control.**\n\nIn software, SDKs, libraries, no-code/vibe-coding tools are like comfortable passenger seats. They make the journey easier by hiding the machinery underneath.\n\nBut hidden machinery doesn't disappear.\n\nWhen something breaks, what you understand determines what you can fix.\n\n**To drive software machines, understand its controls: APIs, endpoints, authentication, requests, and responses.**\n\n**Use abstractions for comfort. Build understanding for control.**\n\n01"},{"h":"Your path this week","a":"your-path-this-week","text":"1. [Send the raw request in the browser](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set#s03)\n2. [Rip off the cushion in Python](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set#s02)\n3. [Verify against your golden pair](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set#s05)\n4. [Submit on the Discord code channel](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set#s07)\n\n02"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"[ReadNo-code notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes-no-code) [ReadNotes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes) [Do thisPractice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set) [StuckTroubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting) Track progressChecklist on the map [RefresherLecture 4 · Groq step](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8) [GuideKeys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety)\n\nA 200 means the machine understood you. It does not mean you helped anyone.Next: building your own API, and how much protection it actually needs."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-05-troubleshooting.md","title":"Troubleshooting: Lecture 5","meta":"title: Troubleshooting: Lecture 5\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting.html\nlecture: 5\nabout: Troubleshooting · Lecture 5 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 303","sections":[{"h":"When the request fails","a":"when-the-request-fails","text":"Status code says whose fault. The message says what. Read both before you ask."},{"h":"01 The Errors You Should Expect","a":"01-the-errors-you-should-expect","text":"**401Invalid API Key**\n\nPart\n\nkey\n\nCause\n\nKey missing, mistyped, a space copied with it, or `Bearer` missing.\n\nFix\n\nThe header is `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Check the environment variable is set in the same terminal that runs the app.\n\n**KeyError: 'GROQ_API_KEY'**\n\nPart\n\nkey\n\nCause\n\nThe variable is not set in this terminal. `export` vanishes when the terminal closes.\n\nFix\n\nSet it again before `python app.py`.\n\n**404The model does not exist or you do not have access to it**\n\nPart\n\npackage, the `model` field\n\nCause\n\nA typo, or a retired model. Groq's own quickstart still ships `llama-3.3-70b-versatile`.\n\nFix\n\nUse `openai/gpt-oss-20b`. One word, read straight from the error.\n\n**404Not found: wrong address**\n\nPart\n\naddress\n\nCause\n\nThe domain alone, or a typo in the path. `api.groq.com` on its own is not an endpoint.\n\nFix\n\n`https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions`\n\n**400Bad request**\n\nPart\n\npackage\n\nCause\n\nA missing field or the wrong shape for `messages`.\n\nFix\n\nCompare your `json=` with the package on the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set#s03).\n\n**429Too many requests**\n\nCause\n\nMore than 30 requests in a minute on your Groq account.\n\nFix\n\nWait a minute. You will not need more.\n\n**KeyError: 'choices'**\n\nCause\n\nThe request failed, so the reply has `error` instead of `choices`.\n\nFix\n\nPrint `r.status_code` and `r.json()` first, then find the code above.\n\n**ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'requests'**\n\nFix\n\n`pip install requests`\n\n**NameError: name 'gr' is not defined**\n\nCause\n\nGradio was never imported.\n\nFix\n\nAdd `import gradio as gr` at the top.\n\n**5xxServer error**\n\nCause\n\nTheir machine, not yours.\n\nFix\n\nTry again later."},{"h":"02 Still Stuck","a":"02-still-stuck","text":"Post the status code, the exact message and what you tried on the Discord code channel, key masked. Replies Mon and Tue. More: [Lecture 4 troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) · [Keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety)."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-06-modelling-lab.md","title":"The Modelling Lab","meta":"title: The Modelling Lab\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Lecture 6 · Databases and domain modelling · Practice lab\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 959","sections":[{"h":"The Modelling Lab","a":"the-modelling-lab","text":"What is worth storing? Seven levels answer it, first for Aarav and then for your app.\n\nAbout 2 hoursNothing to installSaves on this device\n\nWhere you are\n\nYou built the app. Aarav is your user. On Monday he gets a plan, and on Tuesday the app has forgotten it. Three questions decide what is worth storing. Each answer is a brick that sits on the one before.\n\n> Sketch: Three bricks, each built on the one before. One, things: what are the things worth storing? A message becomes a block, a table. Two, facts: what does each thing have? Who, what and when snap onto the message block as its columns. Three, links: how do they connect? A conversation_id plate joins the chat block to the message block: a pointer.\n\nThree questions, three bricks\n\nYou cannot list a thing's facts before you have the thing, or connect two things before both exist. Levels 1 to 3 add one brick each to Aarav's board, Level 4 adds files, Level 5 tests the finished board, and Levels 6 and 7 use the same bricks on new apps."},{"h":"L1 What are the things worth storing?","a":"l1-what-are-the-things-worth-storing","text":"thingsfactslinks\n\nStart from Aarav's own words. Every noun is a candidate. The test: can the app **create** one, **read** it, **update** it and **delete** it on its own, and can there be many? Then it is a thing and gets its own block. A word that only describes a thing is a fact."},{"h":"L2 What does each thing have?","a":"l2-what-does-each-thing-have","text":"thingsfactslinks\n\nTo fill a block, ask what you would need to write down to recreate one. `memory.txt` already answered it for a message: who said it, what they said, and when. Each answer is a fact, and each fact becomes a **column** on its block.\n\nAarav's board · brick 2"},{"h":"L3 How do they connect?","a":"l3-how-do-they-connect","text":"thingsfactslinks\n\nYour blocks hold facts, but they do not know about each other. To connect two blocks, a row in one must point at a row in the other. Every link comes down to two questions, asked both ways. The answers give the link its **shape**, and the shape says where the pointer goes.\n\n> Sketch: Three shapes. One-to-many: many messages per chat, yes; many chats per message, no; the pointer goes on the many side. Many-to-many: many tools per workflow, yes; many workflows per tool, yes; a third block holds the pairs. One-to-one: no and no; maybe it is one block.\n\nTwo questions, three shapes\n\nOne-to-many is most of every model. When both answers are yes, neither block can hold one pointer, so a third block holds one row per pairing. One-to-one is rare: often the two blocks are really one."},{"h":"L4 Rows or files?","a":"l4-rows-or-files","text":"thingsfactslinksfiles\n\nA cell holds a value you can type. Some things Aarav's app keeps are bytes: images, documents, audio. They go in **file storage**, and a row keeps the link. On your HLD both live in the storage box.\n\nAarav's board · the held block"},{"h":"L5 Can the model answer?","a":"l5-can-the-model-answer","text":"thingsfactslinksfiles\n\nThe same board with every brick in, drawn only from your own placements. A model is done when it can answer what the product must ask, so the boss level asks."},{"h":"L6 The Arena 0 / 6 done","a":"l6-the-arena-0--6-done","text":"> Sketch: Six brief cards and the brick each one drills. Aarav's app: all three. The pet clinic: links, one-to-many. The music library: things, thing or fact. The corner bakery: links, a third block. University grades: facts, the fact of a pairing. Hospital: things, history is a row.\n\nSame bricks, new worlds\n\nSix briefs, one brick each, starting with the app you are building. From here on a block is called a **table**, because that is what it becomes next week. Open a brief, build the tables, then check them. Done means every structural check is a tick; the column suggestions are extras."},{"h":"L7 Your model","a":"l7-your-model","text":"The same three bricks, pointed at the app on your HLD. What you build here goes in its storage box. No app yet? Load Aarav's model and add one table of your own.\n\nIn one line, what does your app do?\n\nThree questions your data must answer. Whatever they need is worth storing.\n\n1 2 3\n\n> Sketch: The three bricks again, with the test for each. Things: can the app create, read, update and delete it on its own, and can there be many? It becomes a table. Facts: what you need to recreate one. They become columns. Links: ask one or many, both ways. The answer gives a pointer on the many side, or a third block.\n\nThe three bricks, for your app\n\npointers go on the many side"},{"h":"Rows or files, for your app","a":"rows-or-files-for-your-app","text":"Which files does your app store, and which column holds the link to each?Write \"none\" if your app stores no files. That is a valid answer."},{"h":"The bridge, for your app","a":"the-bridge-for-your-app","text":"Your Lecture 4 API table, with a table name written beside every addressOne address per line. If an address has no table, say what it does instead."},{"h":"Before calling it done","a":"before-calling-it-done","text":"- My tables can answer question 1Follow the arrows that answer it.\n- My tables can answer question 2\n- My tables can answer question 3\n- Every pointer is on the many side, and every many-to-many has a third block\n- I tried to create a wrong state and the model refuses itA message with no conversation, two plans overwriting each other, the same pairing twice.\n\nYou cannot store what you cannot describe.Write these tables into your HLD's storage box, then [submit the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set#s03). Next week: these tables become real storage, and a row survives a refresh."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-06-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Databases and Domain Modelling","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Databases and Domain Modelling\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 6\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2331","sections":[{"h":"It can think. It cannot remember.","a":"it-can-think-it-cannot-remember","text":"Where each piece of an app lives, the storage box we added, and the question it leaves open: what is worth storing?\n\nSat 10 Oct 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCohort 8 · Module 2"},{"h":"01 The Whiteboard So Far","a":"01-the-whiteboard-so-far","text":"Three weeks, one diagram. Aarav types his workflow into the UI on **his machine**: the first interface. The UI sends it to **your server**, where the diagnose function lives: the second interface, an API. Your server sends it on to the **LLM on Groq's machine**, which you do not own. The plan comes back the same way.\n\nThat is two API calls, and each needs the same four things: **address, action, key, package**. Learn the four, not the tool. The same four connect email (Resend: `POST /emails` with from, to and subject) or data from Instagram and Google Maps (Apify). You never built an LLM. You called a machine that has one.\n\n> Sketch: The whole system so far. The user and the client, then your server. The server talks to two boxes: GroqCloud, which thinks, and a file called storage, which remembers. Brackets underneath: UI in week 1, API in week 2, storage this week.\n>\n> Caption: The missing box: storage. Your server gets one more box to talk to. GroqCloud thinks. Storage remembers. For now, picture it as a file.\n\nSee it drawn, scene by scene: [the lecture on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/whiteboard)."},{"h":"02 Outsource Your Thinking, Not Your Understanding","a":"02-outsource-your-thinking-not-your-understanding","text":"Anyone can vibe-code this app in a few prompts. The difference shows when it breaks. Groq's docs still list a Llama 3 model that has been retired, so an app built from them gets a 404. Pasting the error back works for a few rounds. Every round you skip understanding adds to a debt, and at some prompt the model can no longer fix what you cannot explain.\n\nThe whiteboard is what interviewers, clients and investors ask you to draw: where does each piece live, and why?"},{"h":"03 Where Should Each Piece Live?","a":"03-where-should-each-piece-live","text":"Why does the LLM sit outside your server? Because of its size. Anyone can download the model files from Hugging Face, but they need more memory than your server or Aarav's laptop has.\n\n| Model | Needs | Where it can live |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GPT-OSS-120B, the model behind Aarav's plans | About 65 GB of memory, plus GPUs | Someone else's machine, reached by API |\n| A 7-billion-parameter image model | About 8 to 14 GB of memory | Your own server |\n| A 421-million-parameter model | About 2.5 GB | Even the user's machine |\n\nAnything you ship to the user's machine has to fit that machine. This curriculum site has no server at all, because static pages fit everywhere. Size is one deciding factor, and the class added more: network speed (YouTube drops to 360p on a slow connection) and how often something is used (WhatsApp keeps gigabytes of chats on your phone).\n\n**Open thread.** Write down what you use to decide where a piece lives. We close this thread at the end of the deterministic module, when the same decision is tested with 1 user and with 10."},{"h":"04 Aarav's App Forgets","a":"04-aaravs-app-forgets","text":"On Monday Aarav pastes his workflow and gets a plan. On Tuesday he reopens the app and types \"I implemented the plan, but hit a problem.\" The call returns a 200 and the model replies, but it has no idea which plan he means. Every request starts from nothing."},{"h":"05 Derive It Before You Name It","a":"05-derive-it-before-you-name-it","text":"The rule in class was to solve this without saying \"database\". The plainest answers were the best. Keep a `memory.txt`. After every reply, write down who said what, with the date and time. On the next request, send the file along with the prompt, so the model reads yesterday before it answers today.\n\nReal products do the same. Claude's memory, in its settings, is plain text it writes about you. Claude Code remembers a project through one file, CLAUDE.md, with nothing more than read, write and edit. The missing box on the whiteboard is **storage**: anything that keeps things and gives them back. Today that is a file. Later it is a sheet or a database. Start with the file and move up only when it breaks. Paying for a database to solve a file-sized problem is the expensive kind of wrong."},{"h":"06 Where Does Storage Live?","a":"06-where-does-storage-live","text":"The file is tiny, so why not keep it on Aarav's machine? Browsers allow it. Open ChatGPT, then Inspect → Application → Storage: it keeps about 12 MB on your laptop. Clear it and the data is gone, and you are logged out.\n\nThat shows two problems. **The user can wipe it**, and then the app forgets again. **Anyone can read it**, so nothing private can live there. Your API key is the smallest thing you own and the most sensitive. On the client, anyone can copy it and spend money on your account. One classmate's vibe-coded app sent its key to the browser, and a stranger spent the $10 of credit on it.\n\nSo the storage, and the key, live on **your server**. The server reaches the file through the file system (read, write, edit), and that is an interface too. Size alone does not decide where a piece lives. Privacy and security decide too."},{"h":"07 The High-Level Design","a":"07-the-high-level-design","text":"The whiteboard has a name: the **high-level design** (HLD). It answers three things for every piece of an app: what it is, whose machine it lives on, and how it talks to the others. Here is Aarav's app, the way we drew it in class, with storage on the server.\n\n> Sketch: High-level design of Aarav's app in three zones. Aarav's machine: his laptop (1), Chrome (2) and the UI (3), where he types his workflow in and reads the plan out. Your server (4), with the endpoint POST /diagnose (5) on it, the Groq key, and storage (7): a file, memory.txt, with save and fetch arrows. Groq's machine: a GPU, the endpoint POST /chat/completions (6) and the model gpt-oss-120b, which is 14 files, 65 GB. An API arrow pair joins the UI and your server, and another joins your server and Groq. Underneath: anyone can read and the user can wipe what is on his machine; only you can reach your server; Groq's machine is rented because the model is too big to host.\n>\n> Caption: Aarav's high-level design: every box, where it lives, and the calls between them. Numbers name the pieces, so you can say \"5 calls 6\". Dashed zones show whose machine. Arrows are API calls: address, action, key, package.\n\nRead it as one request. Aarav types his workflow into the UI (input). An API call takes it to `POST /diagnose` on your server. The server adds the key and calls the LLM. The plan comes back the same way (output). Storage (7) is new this week: for now, a file.\n\nNow draw one for your own app: [your HLD](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set#hld) in the practice set."},{"h":"08 What Is Worth Storing?","a":"08-what-is-worth-storing","text":"That is the one question for box 7. Not \"what can be stored\": everything that passes through your server can. \"Worth\" makes you weigh it. Store too much and you pay for it, or get sued for it. Store too little and the app forgets.\n\nThe answer from class: **the least you need to carry on next time**. To find it, write the questions your app must answer tomorrow, such as \"Aarav's last plan\" or \"every plan this workflow ever got\". Whatever those questions need is worth storing. Nothing else is."},{"h":"09 Three Questions Give the Shape","a":"09-three-questions-give-the-shape","text":"`memory.txt` stored one shape: who, what, when. Aarav's app needs more: his conversations, his workflows, and every plan each workflow got. The lab splits \"what is worth storing?\" into three questions:\n\n1. What are the things? Can the app create, read, update and delete one on its own, and can there be many? A conversation passes. \"9:12 am\" fails: it only says when one message was sent, so it is a fact. Each thing gets a **table**. In the lab it is a block on Aarav's board first, and becomes a table in Level 6.\n2. What does each thing have? The facts you need to recreate one. For a message: who said it, what, and when. Each fact is a **column**. Tone is a fact on a plan today. If users could save named tones, it would become a thing. What the product does with it decides.\n3. How do they connect? Ask two questions, both ways. Can one conversation have many messages? Yes. Can one message sit in many conversations? No. The two answers give the link its **shape**: one-to-many (most links), many-to-many (both yes: a third table), or one-to-one (both no, and rare: often the two are one table).\n\nThis is **domain modelling**: the smallest description of your user's world that still answers the product's questions.\n\nPractise it: [Level 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s1) for things, [Level 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s2) for facts, [Level 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s3) for links."},{"h":"10 Keys and Pointers","a":"10-keys-and-pointers","text":"Every row needs an address that never repeats. Its words cannot be it: two messages can both say \"okay\". So number the rows. The number goes in a column called `id`, and that column is the row's **key**. A link is a column on the **many side** that holds the other row's id. Why the many side? A cell holds one value. Conversation 7 has many messages, but each message is in one conversation. `conversation_id = 7` on a message means \"I belong to conversation 7\". That column is a **pointer** (the formal word is foreign key).\n\nWhen both sides are many, no single cell can hold \"many\". A workflow uses many tools, and Jira appears in many workflows. A third table, `workflow_tools`, holds one row per pairing, and facts about the pairing, such as how often, live on that row.\n\nPractise it: [Level 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s3), pair by pair."},{"h":"11 Two Rules That Save You Later","a":"11-two-rules-that-save-you-later","text":"**History is a row, not a cell.** A plan saved in a cell gets erased by the next plan. Give each diagnosis its own row with a time on it, the same timestamp idea as `memory.txt`.\n\n**Rows for facts, files for bytes.** A screenshot cannot go in a cell. It goes in file storage, and a row keeps its link, its name, and the message it came with.\n\nPractise it: [Level 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s4) for rows or files, and the hospital brief in [the Arena](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s6) for history."},{"h":"12 Aarav's Model","a":"12-aaravs-model","text":"Eight tables, each one placed by hand in the lab. This is what goes in storage (7):\n\n| Table | Columns | Points to |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| users | id, name |   |\n| conversations | id, user_id, started_at | users |\n| messages | id, conversation_id, role, content, created_at | conversations |\n| attachments | id, message_id, file_url, file_name | messages |\n| workflows | id, user_id, description | users |\n| diagnoses | id, workflow_id, plan, tone, created_at | workflows |\n| tools | id, name |   |\n| workflow_tools | id, workflow_id, tool_id, how_often | workflows, tools |\n\nEvery noun in your Lecture 4 API addresses is a thing here. `POST /workflows` creates a row in workflows. The API is how a request reaches a row. The model is done when it answers your questions. Three for Aarav:\n\n- **His last conversation, every message in order:** follow user_id, then conversation_id, then sort by created_at.\n- **Every plan this workflow ever got, oldest first:** diagnoses by workflow_id. History exists because a plan is a row.\n- **Which screenshot went with which message:** attachments by message_id. The bytes sit behind file_url.\n\nPractise it: [Level 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s5), the boss level: answer these three on the board you built."},{"h":"13 Next: The Storage Box Gets Real","a":"13-next-the-storage-box-gets-real","text":"Lecture 7, Fri 16 Oct. These tables go into Supabase, and a row survives a refresh.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Client side**: The user's machine. The user can wipe anything stored there, and anyone can read it.\n- **Server side**: A machine you control. Keys and stored data live here.\n- **Storage**: Anything that keeps things and gives them back: a file, a sheet, a database.\n- **Timestamp**: The date and time saved with each entry, so you know when it was said.\n- **High-level design**: The whiteboard: every box, where it lives, and the calls between them.\n- **Domain model**: The smallest description of your user's world that still answers the product's questions.\n- **Block**: A thing drawn on the board in the lab. It becomes a table.\n- **Thing (entity)**: Created, read, updated and deleted on its own, and there can be many. Gets a table.\n- **Fact (attribute)**: What you need to recreate one of the thing. Gets a column.\n- **Key (primary key)**: The row's address, usually id. Never repeats.\n- **Pointer (foreign key)**: A column on the many side holding another row's id.\n- **Shape**: One-to-many, many-to-many or one-to-one. Found by asking \"one A, many B?\" and \"one B, many A?\".\n- **Many side**: The side with only one to point at. It holds the pointer.\n- **Third table (join table)**: One row per pairing when both sides are many.\n- **File storage**: Where bytes live. The row keeps the link.\n\nDecide what is worth storing before you pick where to store it.Draw your HLD, do the [lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab), then [submit the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set) before Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.\n\nKeep this\n\nStorage is just a way to remember. Keep it on your server, where the user cannot wipe it and nobody else can read it. Store only what your app needs to answer its questions next time. The [Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab) turns \"what is worth storing?\" into three questions, and then into seven levels you play."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-06-overview.md","title":"Databases and Domain Modelling","meta":"title: Databases and Domain Modelling\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/index.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 6 · Sat 10 Oct 2026\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 246","sections":[{"h":"Databases and domain modelling","a":"databases-and-domain-modelling","text":"Your app can think. Now decide what it should remember.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nAarav's app can think but cannot remember. Add storage: start with a file, keep it on your server, and store only what your app's questions need.\n\nWhat is worth storing? The lab answers with three questions.\n\n1\n\n**What are the things?**Nouns the app must create, find, change and delete on their own. Each gets a table.\n\n2\n\n**What does each thing have?**The facts you need to recreate one. Each is a column.\n\n3\n\n**How do they connect?**One-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many. A pointer on the many side.\n\n01"},{"h":"Your path this week","a":"your-path-this-week","text":"The lab is seven levels and takes about 2 hours. It saves on your device, so you can stop between levels.\n\n1. [Draw your HLD](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set#hld)\n2. [Levels 1 to 5: build Aarav's model by hand](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s1)\n3. [Level 6: pass the six Arena briefs](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s6)\n4. [Level 7: model your app and fill the storage box](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s7)\n5. [Submit the practice set before Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set#s03)\n\n02"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"[ReadOffice hour, 15 Oct](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/office-hour) [Do thisThe Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab) [ReadNotes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes) [SubmitPractice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set) [StuckTroubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/troubleshooting) Track progressChecklist on the map [RefresherLecture 4 · your API table](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set#s04)\n\nDecide what is worth storing before you pick where to store it.Next: [Lecture 7, Fri 16 Oct](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/), the key leaves the browser. Then [Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/): these tables, typed into Supabase, and a row that survives a refresh."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-06-troubleshooting.md","title":"Troubleshooting: Lecture 6","meta":"title: Troubleshooting: Lecture 6\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/troubleshooting\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/troubleshooting.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Troubleshooting · Lecture 6\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1050","sections":[{"h":"When a check is a cross","a":"when-a-check-is-a-cross","text":"The first check on a brief says what is wrong. From the second check on, every cross also says where. Fix one thing, check again."},{"h":"01 The Crosses You Should Expect","a":"01-the-crosses-you-should-expect","text":"**No table for …**\n\nCause\n\nThe brief names a thing you have not given a table, or your table's name is too far from it (the checker accepts singular, plural and common synonyms).\n\nFix\n\nAdd the table, or rename yours to the noun the brief uses.\n\n**… has no key**\n\nCause\n\nNo column is marked **key**. Every row needs an address so other rows can point at it.\n\nFix\n\nAdd an `id` column and tick key. The builder does this for every new table; you removed it.\n\n**… has no pointer to … / Nothing links … and … yet**\n\nCause\n\nThe link exists in the brief but not in your tables. The many side needs a column holding the other table's key.\n\nFix\n\nOn the many side, add a column named the other table singular plus `_id`, tick **points to**, and pick the table.\n\n**… is a pointer but does not point to …**\n\nCause\n\nYou ticked **points to** but left the dropdown on \"which table?\", or picked the wrong one.\n\nFix\n\nOpen the dropdown under the column and pick the table the brief names. If it is not in the list, that table has no name yet.\n\n**… should be a pointer to …**\n\nCause\n\nThe column is there, with the right name, but it is a plain column. A name alone does not make a link.\n\nFix\n\nTick **points to** on it and pick the table.\n\n**… points the wrong way**\n\nCause\n\nThe one side carries a pointer to its many side: a `pet_id` on owners. One cell cannot hold many pets.\n\nFix\n\nRemove that column. The many side already carries the link.\n\n**Link missing: …**\n\nCause\n\nThe table is there but has no pointer to the other side. On a first check it asks \"which side has only one to point at?\" instead of naming it: ask the two questions both ways, as in Level 3.\n\nFix\n\nSame as \"has no pointer to\": add the column on the many side, tick points to, pick the table.\n\n**… must be marked unique**\n\nCause\n\nThe brief says exactly one row per parent (one summary per post). A plain pointer allows many.\n\nFix\n\nTick **unique** on the pointer column. A second row for the same parent is then refused.\n\n**… is missing …, and that is the point of this brief / A fact this brief is about is missing**\n\nCause\n\nThe one column the brief is about (quantity, grade, start date, status) is not on the table where it belongs.\n\nFix\n\nRe-read the brief's last line. The column describes exactly that row, usually the pairing row.\n\n**Two tables share the name … / … has two columns called …**\n\nFix\n\nRename or remove the duplicate. Pointers cannot tell twins apart.\n\n**… is a pointer with no table to point at**\n\nCause\n\nThe dropdown is still on \"which table?\", or the table it pointed at was deleted or renamed.\n\nFix\n\nPick the table again.\n\n**… could use a … column**\n\nCause\n\nA suggestion, shown with a dot. It never blocks done.\n\nFix\n\nAdd it if the brief's product would need it. Otherwise ignore."},{"h":"02 The Lab Itself","a":"02-the-lab-itself","text":"**My progress disappeared**\n\nCause\n\nThe lab saves in this browser on this device. A private window, another browser, cleared site data or another device starts empty.\n\nFix\n\nUse the same browser. Take your screenshots as you go.\n\n**\"Lock it in\" stays grey**\n\nFix\n\nWrite a few words in the line above it. The reveal comes after you lock your line in, and the line is worth +15. Skip moves on without it.\n\n**A level says it builds on Level 1**\n\nCause\n\nLevels 2 and 3 work on the six blocks you uncover in Level 1. Until they are found, the board shows \"?\" blocks.\n\nFix\n\nPress \"Play Level 1\" and find the six blocks. You do not need every word, only the blocks.\n\n**How are stars counted?**\n\nBy misses in that level: none or one is three stars, up to four is two, more is one. A right call is +10, +5 on a retry, and +5 more on a streak of three. In the Arena a failed check or an opened hint counts as a miss, and \"Load my board\" on brief 1 caps it at two stars.\n\n**Can I replay a level for more stars?**\n\nYes. \"Play again\" on the win card, or \"Restart\" in the top bar, wipes that level only and keeps your best score. Levels that build on it keep working once you finish it again.\n\n**Practice set deliverables 2 to 4 will not tick**\n\nCause\n\nThey tick themselves from the lab: 2 when you beat Level 5, 3 at six Arena briefs, 4 when Level 7 is finished. They read the lab's save in this browser.\n\nFix\n\nFinish the level in the same browser, then reload the practice set.\n\n**Level 5 shows dashed boxes**\n\nCause\n\nLevel 5 only draws what you placed. A dashed block or column is one you have not found, placed or linked yet.\n\nFix\n\nClick \"place it\" on the dashed piece; it takes you to the level that places it.\n\n**What do I do with the downloaded sheets?**\n\nOne CSV per table, with two sample rows. Open them in Google Sheets or Excel to see your model as sheets. Next week they are a starting point for the storage box.\n\n**What goes in my HLD's storage file?**\n\nThe tables from Level 7, by name. If your app only ever needs who said what and when, one file is a fine answer. Say so, and say where it lives.\n\n**I want to start over**\n\n\"Start again\" on a builder resets only that brief. \"Restart\" in a level's top bar wipes only that level. \"Reset the whole lab\" at the bottom of Level 7 wipes everything on this device, including your stars and points."},{"h":"03 Still Stuck","a":"03-still-stuck","text":"Post a screenshot of the cross and your tables in the Discord channel. Office hour Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST. More: [notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes) · [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set)."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-06-whiteboard.md","title":"Lecture 6 on a Whiteboard","meta":"title: Lecture 6 on a Whiteboard\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/whiteboard\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/whiteboard.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 6\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2261","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 6, on a whiteboard","a":"lecture-6-on-a-whiteboard","text":"Your app can think. Today it learns to remember.\n\nP to present · ← → to move · Esc to leave\n\nPart 1 · The story so far\n\n01 · Week 1"},{"h":"The first interface: UI","a":"the-first-interface-ui","text":"> Sketch: A person types into a laptop and sees the reply. The laptop shows a browser with a small chat app. This is the client, your machine. Input, process and output all happen in this one box.\n\nA human cannot talk to a machine directly. The interface is where the two meet. You built one: an app that runs in your browser, on your machine. That machine is the **client**.\n\n02 · The limit"},{"h":"Some work does not fit in that box","a":"some-work-does-not-fit-in-that-box","text":"> Sketch: Left, a laptop with 16 GB of memory. Middle, a screenshot of OpenAI's gpt-oss-120b on Hugging Face: 117 billion parameters in 15 files, 65 GB. An arrow to the laptop is crossed out: 65 GB does not fit in 16 GB. An arrow to one H100 GPU with 80 GB: it fits.\n\nThe real file: OpenAI's **gpt-oss-120b** on Hugging Face. 117 billion parameters in 15 files, 65 GB in all. It needs one 80 GB GPU, like an NVIDIA H100. Your laptop has 16 GB.\n\nSize decides, not the word \"LLM\". Its little sibling, gpt-oss-20b, does run in 16 GB. The 120b does not.\n\n03 · Week 2"},{"h":"The second interface: API","a":"the-second-interface-api","text":"> Sketch: The client, your laptop, sends a request to the server, another machine. The request carries four parts: address, action, key and package. The reply comes back with a code and a package.\n\nA phone call between machines. The client asks, the server answers. The server only does what the request says, so the request has to say it exactly: address, action, key, package. The reply is a code and a package.\n\n04 · Lecture 5"},{"h":"What you built: a server that calls another server","a":"what-you-built-a-server-that-calls-another-server","text":"> Sketch: Left to right: you, your app on a laptop (the client), your server, and GroqCloud's server, where the gpt-oss-120b files from Hugging Face run. Your server answers your app and calls GroqCloud, both over an API.\n\nYour app is the client and your server answers it. Then your server turns client and calls GroqCloud, where the same gpt-oss-120b files from Hugging Face are loaded and answering. Client and server are roles, not machines.\n\n05 · The gap"},{"h":"It can think. It cannot remember.","a":"it-can-think-it-cannot-remember","text":"> Sketch: Monday: Aarav sends his weekly workflow and gets a plan. He closes the tab and the server restarts. Tuesday: Aarav writes, Hey, I implemented the plan, and this is the problem. The app replies, Which plan?\n\nMonday, Aarav pastes his week and gets a plan. Tuesday, he comes back to follow up, and the app asks which plan. Nothing was ever written down.\n\n06 · Week 3 · this week"},{"h":"The missing box: storage","a":"the-missing-box-storage","text":"> Sketch: The whole system so far. You, the client, your server. The server talks to two boxes: GroqCloud, which thinks, and a file called storage, which remembers. Brackets underneath: UI in week 1, API in week 2, storage this week.\n\nYour server gets one more box to talk to. GroqCloud thinks. Storage remembers. For now, picture it as a file that lives outside the app.\n\nPart 2 · Memory and storage\n\n07 · Why it forgot"},{"h":"Memory dies. Storage survives.","a":"memory-dies-storage-survives","text":"> Sketch: Left, while the app runs: messages sit in memory inside the server, and a storage file sits outside it. Right, after a restart: memory is wiped, the storage file is still there.\n\nEverything a running app holds lives in memory, and memory dies when the app stops. Storage sits outside the app, on disk, and keeps things after the app is gone. Nothing about the model changes. Only where the words live.\n\n08 · What goes in the file?"},{"h":"Storage needs a shape","a":"storage-needs-a-shape","text":"> Sketch: Left, a file called chat.txt with every message run together, and questions: who said it, when, which chat? Right, the same messages as a table: columns who, what, when, one row per message.\n\nTo bring a message back, storage has to know who said it, what was said, and when. One row per message, one column per fact. That shape is a **table**. And you cannot store a shape you have never described.\n\nPart 3 · Describe the shape\n\n09 · Domain modelling"},{"h":"Three questions: things, facts, links","a":"three-questions-things-facts-links","text":"> Sketch: Three sticky notes. Things: what are the things? They become tables. Facts: what does each thing have? They become columns. Links: how do they connect? They become pointers.\n\nThe domain is your user's world. The model is the smallest description of it that still answers the product's questions. Paper first, no tool yet.\n\n10 · Take Aarav's chat apart"},{"h":"Every word is a thing or a fact","a":"every-word-is-a-thing-or-a-fact","text":"> Sketch: Left, Aarav's chat: Monday's conversation with his weekly workflow (Jira, the dashboard, Slack), sent at 9:12 am, the app's plan, and Tuesday's conversation asking for a more casual tone. Right, a table pulling the parts out. Things: user, conversation, message, workflow, plan, tool. Facts: name, text, who sent it, time sent, tone. Links: one user to many chats, one chat to many messages, one workflow to many plans, workflows to tools.\n\nPull every noun out of the chat and sort it. Most are things: each gets its own table. The rest only describe a thing: those are facts, and each becomes a column.\n\n11 · Question 1"},{"h":"Is it a thing? The Create, Read, Update, Delete test","a":"is-it-a-thing-the-create-read-update-delete-test","text":"> Sketch: A checklist. Message, conversation and Jira pass: the app can create, read, update and delete each on its own, and there can be many. They are things. 9:12 am and casual fail: each only describes one message or one plan, so they are facts.\n\nNouns are clues, not answers. A thing is something the app can create, read, update and delete on its own, and there can be many. A word that fails only describes a thing: it is a fact, a column on that thing's table. The most common beginner move is splitting into too many tables. This test is how you refuse.\n\n12 · Question 2"},{"h":"What does each thing have?","a":"what-does-each-thing-have","text":"> Sketch: Left, a message card: who, Aarav; what, here's my workflow; when, Monday 9:12 am. Facts are what you need to recreate one. Right, a plan with tone casual. Today tone is a fact. If users could save and reuse named tones, tones become a thing of their own.\n\nA fact is what you need to recreate one of the thing. Take the message away and \"content\" means nothing; that is how you know it is a fact. The word does not decide where it goes. What the product needs to do with it decides.\n\n13 · Question 3"},{"h":"How do they connect? Three shapes","a":"how-do-they-connect-three-shapes","text":"> Sketch: Three shapes of a link. One-to-one: rare, maybe one table. One-to-many: one chat, many messages, a pointer on the many side. Many-to-many: workflows and tools cross, so a third table holds the pairs.\n\nEvery link you can say out loud has one of three shapes. One-to-many is most of every model you will ever draw. The rule to carry: **the many side points back**.\n\n14 · Keys and pointers"},{"h":"Every row needs an address","a":"every-row-needs-an-address","text":"> Sketch: Two tables. Conversations: ids 7 and 8. Messages: ids 101 to 104, each with a conversation_id. Two messages both say okay; their ids tell them apart. Arrows from conversation_id back to the conversation rows: the many side points back.\n\nTwo messages both say \"okay\". The app tells them apart by a **key**: an id that never repeats, like an apartment number. A link is then just a column on the many side that holds the other row's key. `conversation_id = 7` means \"I belong to conversation 7\".\n\n15 · Many-to-many"},{"h":"When both sides are many, add a third table","a":"when-both-sides-are-many-add-a-third-table","text":"> Sketch: Workflows on the left, tools on the right, and workflow_tools in the middle: one row per pairing, with how_often on each row. How often Aarav uses Jira belongs to the pairing.\n\nNo single cell can hold \"many\". So a third table holds one row per pairing. How often Aarav uses Jira belongs to neither Jira nor the workflow. It belongs to the pairing, so it lives on that row. Ask what each value describes, and put it there.\n\n16 · History"},{"h":"History is a row, not a cell","a":"history-is-a-row-not-a-cell","text":"> Sketch: Left, the plan kept in a single cell: the casual plan overwrote Monday's formal plan, which is gone. Right, a diagnoses table with two rows for workflow 1, the formal plan on Monday and the casual plan on Tuesday. Both survive; the newest is current.\n\nAarav asks for the plan again, in a casual tone. A cell holds one value, so the new plan erases the old one. If the product must remember what was true before, make it a row with a time on it.\n\n17 · Rows or files"},{"h":"Rows for facts, files for bytes","a":"rows-for-facts-files-for-bytes","text":"> Sketch: A screenshot, jira-board.png, is bytes, so it goes into file storage. An attachments row keeps the link to it, which message it came with, and its name.\n\nA cell holds a value you can type: text, a number, a date, yes or no. A screenshot is bytes. It goes in file storage, and a row keeps the link plus the facts about it: which message it came with, its name, when it arrived.\n\nPart 4 · Put it together\n\n18 · The final tables"},{"h":"Aarav's chat, as rows","a":"aaravs-chat-as-rows","text":"> Sketch: Three tables filled from Aarav's chat. Users: 1, Aarav. Conversations: 7 on Monday and 8 on Tuesday, both for user 1. Messages 101 to 105 each point to a conversation. Message 105, I implemented the plan, points back to conversation 7.\n\nMonday and Tuesday, written down. Every message knows its conversation, and every conversation knows its user. Tuesday's follow-up, row 105, points back to Monday's conversation 7, where the plan lives.\n\n19 · How it all works"},{"h":"Tuesday again, with storage","a":"tuesday-again-with-storage","text":"> Sketch: Six steps. 1, the client sends Aarav's follow-up. 2, the server saves it as row 105. 3, the server fetches conversation 7 from storage. 4, it sends the history and the new question to GroqCloud. 5, GroqCloud answers. 6, the client shows: In Monday's plan, step 3, let's fix the export.\n\nThe same follow-up: \"Hey, I implemented the plan, and this is the problem.\" Now the server saves it, fetches conversation 7 from storage, and sends the history with the new question to GroqCloud. The model still remembers nothing. Your system does.\n\n20 · Aarav's whole model"},{"h":"Storage with a shape has a name","a":"storage-with-a-shape-has-a-name","text":"> Sketch: Eight tables and their pointers: users; conversations and workflows point to users; messages point to conversations; attachments point to messages; diagnoses point to workflows; workflow_tools point to workflows and tools. Together: a database.\n\nEight tables, and the pointers between them, kept outside the app. That is a **database**. Every arrow is a many side pointing back.\n\n21 · Back to Lecture 4"},{"h":"The API is how a request reaches a row","a":"the-api-is-how-a-request-reaches-a-row","text":"> Sketch: POST /workflows reaches the server and creates one row in workflows. POST /workflows/1/diagnoses creates one row in diagnoses with workflow_id 1. Every noun in an address is a thing in the model.\n\nYour API table from the Lecture 4 practice set and these tables are one picture. The address names the thing. The action says what to do to its row.\n\n22 · Check your model"},{"h":"Done means it answers the questions","a":"done-means-it-answers-the-questions","text":"> Sketch: Three product questions and the pointer path that answers each. Aarav's last conversation, every message in order: users to conversations to messages. Every plan this workflow ever got: workflows to diagnoses, oldest first. Which screenshot went with which message: messages to attachments. Then try to break it: a message with no conversation is refused.\n\nA model is not done when it looks tidy. It is done when it answers what the product must ask. Write your own three questions first, trace each answer along the pointers, then try to create a wrong state and make sure the model refuses it.\n\n23 · Your turn"},{"h":"Five more briefs, one move each, then your own app","a":"five-more-briefs-one-move-each-then-your-own-app","text":"> Sketch: Six cards. The pet clinic: one-to-many. The music library: thing or fact. The corner bakery: many-to-many. University grades: the fact of a pairing. Hospital assignments: history is a row. Your own app: the three questions.\n\nThe Modelling Lab, in seven levels. Levels 1 to 5 rebuild Aarav's model by hand, the Arena drills one move per brief, and Level 7 is your own app. Practice set due before Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.\n\n24 · The full picture"},{"h":"Three weeks, three boxes","a":"three-weeks-three-boxes","text":"> Sketch: The whole system. You, the client, your server, GroqCloud which thinks, and the database which remembers. UI in week 1, API in week 2, the database in week 3. Next week the tables go into Supabase.\n\nUI for humans. API for machines. A database for memory. [Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard): these exact tables, typed into Supabase, and a row that survives a refresh. Nothing new to model. That is why we did it on paper first.\n\nYou cannot store what you cannot describe.Read the [notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes), then do the [Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab)."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Connect backend to the database","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Connect backend to the database\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 7\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 7 · 16 October 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 8454036\nwords: 2640","sections":[{"h":"Make the app remember.","a":"make-the-app-remember","text":"We named the parts of a full app, watched a booking vanish on refresh, and connected a to-do app to Supabase.\n\nIn [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes) you worked out what an app should store and drew it as tables. Today that drawing became a real database. First we named the parts of a full app. Then you connected an app to Supabase with your own hands."},{"h":"01 Learn the names, skip the code","a":"01-learn-the-names-skip-the-code","text":"Antigravity writes the code. So what is left for you to know? The name of each part and why it exists. With the names you can ask the coding tool for a change, and follow what it says back.\n\nEach part was added when the one before it hit a wall.\n\n- **HTML, 1990s.** Pages that only show information.\n- **JavaScript.** Code in the browser, so a page can respond to a click.\n- **Node.js and Express.** Node.js lets JavaScript run on the server too. Express manages the requests coming in.\n- **React, 2013.** Started at Facebook. Before React, one new like meant loading the whole page again. Now only the counter changes.\n\nAdd a database called MongoDB and you have four parts: MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js. Their first letters give the name **MERN stack**. The side you see is the **front end**. The side on the server is the **back end**.\n\n> You do not need to write the code. You need the names and what each one does."},{"h":"02 The app as a restaurant","a":"02-the-app-as-a-restaurant","text":"To make the names stick, we walked through a restaurant.\n\n> Sketch: Four boxes in a row. The dining area is React, where the customer orders. The traffic controller is Express, which sends each order to the right cook. The kitchen is Node.js, which does the work. The storeroom is the database. Arrows carry the order to the right along the top and the result back to the left along the bottom.\n>\n> Caption: Front end on the left, back end and database on the right. Every order makes the full round trip.\n\n- **HTML is the building, CSS the interior design.** Walls and layout, then colours and style.\n- **JavaScript is the staff, React the floor manager.** They take orders and keep the dining room running.\n- **An API is the order slip.** The waiter carries it to the kitchen.\n- **Express is the traffic controller.** In a kitchen with many cuisines, it sends a Chinese order to the Chinese chef.\n- **Node.js is the kitchen.** The work on the order happens here.\n- **The database is the storeroom.** Menu, customers and order history, kept safe and in order.\n\nReact builds a screen from small reusable blocks, like Lego. A like button is one block. You write it once and use it everywhere. Each block is a **component**.\n\nThe instructions you hand a component, like a special request given to the waiter, are its **props**. What is true right now, like which table is occupied, is its **state**. When the state changes, React redraws that one component."},{"h":"03 The waiter who forgot the table","a":"03-the-waiter-who-forgot-the-table","text":"A waiter writes down an order: pasta, quantity 2. The kitchen cooks it. Where does the food go? Nobody knows, because the waiter never wrote the table number.\n\nPlain JavaScript is that waiter. It is flexible, so a mistake shows up only when the app is already running.\n\nThe fix is a rulebook on top of JavaScript. It checks that every required detail is there, and of the right kind, before the code runs. That rulebook is **TypeScript**. Its files end in `.ts`, or `.tsx` for React components.\n\n> Ask your coding tool for TypeScript. It catches the missing table number before the food is cooked."},{"h":"04 One click, all the way round","a":"04-one-click-all-the-way-round","text":"Now run all the parts in one flow. In a to-do app, you type an item and click add. What happens?\n\nReact sends a request to Express. Express hands it to the right back-end code, and Node.js runs it: it checks, for example, that the item is not empty. The database stores the item and answers: stored, or failed. The answer travels back the same way, and React updates the screen.\n\nAn app does four things to its data, and each has its own kind of request.\n\n| In the to-do app | The action | The request |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Add an item | Create | POST |\n| Show the list | Read | GET |\n| Mark it done, or edit it | Update | PUT |\n| Remove it | Delete | DELETE |\n\nCreate, read, update, delete: **CRUD**.\n\n**From the chat: the screen changed, but did it save?**\n\nClick a like and the button changes at once. In the same moment the app sends the request to the back end. If the back end fails, the button goes back to how it was. A small message pops up in a corner to say so: a **toast message**."},{"h":"05 Where the files live","a":"05-where-the-files-live","text":"Open any project Antigravity has built and you see folders. Which folder holds what? The instructor showed a standard layout. You can ask your coding tool to follow it.\n\n> Sketch: Two cards. The client folder is the front end: components for buttons and checkboxes, pages for whole screens, services for the API calls, assets for images, icons and fonts, and App and main, where it starts. The server folder is the back end: routes for the addresses, controllers for the logic, models for the shape of the data, middleware for login and errors, and server.js, where it starts.\n>\n> Caption: Two folders, one per side. There is no database folder.\n\nFrom the chat: does the database get a folder too? No. The `models` folder only describes the shape of the data. The database itself is created later, and it lives somewhere else.\n\n**From the chat: what is the DOM?**\n\nDOM stands for document object model. The browser turns a plain HTML page into a tree: header, body, every button. Each item on the tree can then be changed on its own, without loading the page again."},{"h":"06 Refresh, and the booking is gone","a":"06-refresh-and-the-booking-is-gone","text":"Back to the salon booking app. On screen it says \"appointment saved\": Asha has booked a signature haircut with the stylist Maya, Thursday at 4 pm.\n\n> \"What happens as soon as she refreshes the page?\"\n\nOne answer from the chat: it should stay. Others: it is gone. It is gone. The prototype kept the booking only in the page's temporary memory, and a refresh wipes that.\n\n> A beautiful UI is not the product. The product remembers.\n\nSo what should happen when Asha clicks Book?\n\n> Sketch: Six steps. On the left, the app: step 1, Asha clicks Book; step 2, the form collects the details. Step 3, the app sends a request to the database on the right. Step 4, the database saves the appointment. Step 5, the database sends back a response: saved, or failed. Step 6, the screen shows the result.\n>\n> Caption: The screen may say \"saved\" only after step 5 comes back.\n\nDoes the click itself mean the appointment is booked? No. The click only starts the trip. The database writes a new entry, like a line in the salon's permanent register, and reports back.\n\nThe UI does not get to claim \"saved\" because someone clicked a button. It shows what the database said. When Asha refreshes, or opens the app on another device, the app fetches the appointment back.\n\nWhat exactly does the database need to hold?"},{"h":"07 Four lists the salon keeps","a":"07-four-lists-the-salon-keeps","text":"Today the salon's receptionist keeps a paper register: who is coming, for which service, with which stylist, at what time.\n\n> \"What information must the app remember?\"\n\nThe chat's answers: customer name, stylist name, time, appointments. That gives four things: customers, stylists, services, appointments. In [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s09) you called each one an entity. Each entity becomes a table.\n\n> A table is a list. A row is one record. A column is one kind of information.\n\nAsha is one row in the customers table. If Kabir joins tomorrow, he is one more row in the same table.\n\nNow suppose two customers are both called Asha. How does the salon know which one booked? The chat said: mobile number, or an ID. A phone number can change. So every row gets an ID that never changes. That is the primary key from [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s10).\n\n> Sketch: Three tables on top: customers with id, name and phone; stylists with id, name and speciality; services with id, name and price. An arrow from each points down to a fourth table, appointments, which holds its own id, a customer id, a stylist id, a service id and the time.\n>\n> Caption: An appointment stores three IDs and a time. It copies no names.\n\nAsha will have many appointments over time, so they cannot sit inside her customer row. They go in the appointments table. An appointment row does not copy Asha's name or Maya's name. It stores their IDs, so it still points at the right Asha when her details change. An ID that points at a row in another table is the foreign key from Lecture 6.\n\nThen you took five minutes to fill in the four tables yourself, on paper or in a sheet."},{"h":"08 Connect a to-do app to Supabase","a":"08-connect-a-to-do-app-to-supabase","text":"The design is on paper. Supabase is the tool that holds the tables: your app's data home. A database takes its instructions in SQL. You write none by hand: Antigravity writes it, and you paste it. We practised on a fresh to-do app. You can do this tonight.\n\n> Sketch: Two boxes. On the left, Antigravity, where your to-do app is built. On the right, Supabase, which holds the table. An arrow from Supabase to Antigravity carries the project URL and the key. An arrow from Antigravity to Supabase carries the SQL that makes the table.\n>\n> Caption: Two copy-pastes connect them: the URL and key go one way, the SQL goes the other.\n\n1. **Sign up at Supabase.** A pop-up asks you to create an organisation. Give it any name and choose the free plan.\n2. **Open Antigravity in a new folder.** Make a folder, open it, and click \"Yes, I trust the authors\".\n3. **Paste the to-do app prompt and send it.** It asks for a to-do app connected to Supabase (the exact prompt is in the doc shared in the session). Gemini Flash is good enough for this.\n4. **Accept the changes.** Antigravity creates the files and asks you to accept them. It takes a while.\n5. **Give it your Supabase details.** Antigravity stops and asks for your project URL and key. In Supabase, open your project, then Project settings, then API keys. Copy what it asks for and paste it into the chat.\n6. **Run the SQL.** Antigravity hands you SQL that makes the table. Copy it into the SQL editor in Supabase and click Run.\n7. **See the table.** Open the Table editor in Supabase and refresh. The to-do table is there.\n8. **Open the app.** Antigravity gives you a link that starts with `localhost`: the app is running on your own computer. Click it.\n\nTreat the secret key on that Supabase page like your Groq key. Never post it or put it on a public page.\n\nThe troubles the room hit:\n\n| The trouble | The fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| Antigravity is still running and has asked nothing | Wait. It asks when the files are done. |\n| It asks for the project URL and you cannot find it | Project settings, then API keys. If it says \"Data API\", copy the URL from that page. |\n| Run says the table already exists | The table was made earlier. Open the Table editor and refresh. |\n| It says done, but you see no app | Open the `localhost` link in your browser. |\n| An error in the terminal | Copy the error, paste it back to the coding tool, and ask it to explain. |\n| Too many permission pop-ups | \"Always proceed\" switches them off. Use it only on a computer with nothing sensitive on it. |"},{"h":"09 Prove it is connected","a":"09-prove-it-is-connected","text":"The screen alone proves nothing. So the instructor tested from both sides.\n\n1. He added an item, \"Testing one\", in the app. In the Supabase table, a new row appeared, with `completed` set to false.\n2. He ticked the checkbox in the app. A toast message (the small pop-up) appeared. After a refresh, the row in Supabase said true.\n3. He changed true back to false inside Supabase and refreshed the app. The checkbox was empty again.\n\nThe data moves both ways, so the two are connected.\n\nThen he opened the browser's console, which lists every request the page sends. He added an item: POST. He edited one: PUT. He deleted one: DELETE. He refreshed the page and the list loaded: GET.\n\n**From the chat: is the database the same for every project?**\n\nNo. The slides used MongoDB as the storeroom. Supabase uses a different one, called Postgres, and only that one. Postgres understands SQL, which is why step 6 was a block of SQL.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**Asha books in the prototype and sees \"appointment saved\". She refreshes. What does she see, and why?**\n\nNo appointment. The prototype kept it only in the page's temporary memory. Nothing was sent to a database, so there is nothing to fetch back.\n\n**A friend writes Asha's name and phone number into every appointment row. What goes wrong?**\n\nWith two Ashas you cannot tell whose appointment it is. And when a phone number changes, every old row is wrong. Store the customer's ID in the appointment row instead.\n\n**You tick a to-do in your app. Which request goes out, and how do you check it reached Supabase?**\n\nAn update, so PUT. Open the table in Supabase and refresh: `completed` should now say true.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Front end**: The side of the app you see and click, running in your browser.\n- **Back end**: The side on the server that does the work on each request.\n- **MERN stack**: MongoDB (the database), Express (the traffic controller), React (the screen) and Node.js (the kitchen), used together.\n- **Component**: One reusable block of the screen, like a like button.\n- **State**: What is true about a component right now, like liked or not liked.\n- **Props**: The instructions you hand a component.\n- **TypeScript**: JavaScript with a rulebook that checks the details before the code runs.\n- **CRUD**: Create, read, update, delete: the four things an app does to its data.\n- **Toast message**: A small message that pops up in a corner to say what happened.\n\nKeep this\n\nA screen that says \"saved\" has saved nothing until a database says so. List what the app must remember, give each thing a table and each row an ID, then let Antigravity connect the app to Supabase. Check the table, not the screen."},{"h":"10 Your turn: connect your salon app","a":"10-your-turn-connect-your-salon-app","text":"First finish the to-do app from section 08, and test it the way section 09 does. Then open your salon booking app in Antigravity and type:\n\n> \"Connect my app to Supabase.\"\n\nFollow the instructions it gives: the same URL, key and SQL steps. Then book an appointment, refresh, and look for the row in Supabase.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: a closer look inside Supabase, then your app goes live on Vercel."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-07-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Connect Backend to the Database","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Connect Backend to the Database\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes.html\nlecture: 7\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 7\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2616","sections":[{"h":"The key leaves the browser","a":"the-key-leaves-the-browser","text":"What was said on Friday, in the order it was said. Setup, the leak, the function, the API, the first deploy. Storage is [Lecture 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/).\n\nFri 16 Oct 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCode track"},{"h":"01 The lecture in one line","a":"01-the-lecture-in-one-line","text":"- **Today:** the key left Aarav's browser. The function that calls Groq now runs on a rented machine, behind a URL anyone can call, and the key sits in an environment variable there.\n- **Lecture 8, Saturday:** the same server gets memory. Things, facts and links from the Modelling Lab become a schema on Supabase, and the whole app goes live end to end.\n- **Then the hackathon.** Two days, fresh problem statements, mandatory, graded on the attempt. You do what Lectures 7 and 8 did, with your own idea.\n- **Done for today:** `http POST https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api/diagnose workflow=\"…\"` returns a plan. The key is nowhere a user can see it.\n\nThe whiteboard sketches sit inside the sections they belong to. The storage half is [Lecture 8 on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard)."},{"h":"02 Five things before you build","a":"02-five-things-before-you-build","text":"| Need | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| Python, locally | Every step is a Python file you run first on your laptop. |\n| An IDE | Cursor in class. VS Code, PyCharm, anything works. |\n| One LLM desktop app | Free is fine. Pick one, see section 04. |\n| A GitHub account | Where your code lives on the internet, and how you share it for review. |\n| A Vercel account and CLI | The rented machine your API runs on. Free hobby plan. |\n\n- **GitHub in two words.** A repository is a project. *Commit* is saving your progress, like a save file in a game. *Push* sends your saves to the repository on the internet. You can commit many times, then push once.\n- **Keep the hackathon repo public.** A private repo cannot be reviewed. Code is no longer the moat. Your domain expertise is.\n- **You do not have to memorise the commands.** Understand commit and push. Let your LLM desktop app run the terminal."},{"h":"03 Rent the machine","a":"03-rent-the-machine","text":"- **Your laptop is a personal computer.** Shut it and the app is gone. Share the link, go viral, and one person's RAM and bandwidth serve ten thousand users. It will fall over.\n- **Your own server is an independent house.** A rack from Amazon or a Mac mini works, and some students do it. But you own the locks, the power backup, the water tank and every attack vector on an open port. Maintenance is the real cost.\n- **AWS and friends are the older rentals.** Bare machines. Still a lot of setup before you can live there.\n- **Vercel is the Airbnb.** Furnished, walk in, pay for what you use. The hobby plan is free. A paid app costs about $20 a month against a machine that would cost near a lakh to buy.\n- **The rule the lecture kept returning to.** Where your machine is, how you control it, what it stores, and how it serves an API. Vercel abstracts the first two. You still decide the last two."},{"h":"04 Pick one LLM desktop app","a":"04-pick-one-llm-desktop-app","text":"| Tool | Ease | Speed | Note |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [Codex](https://openai.com/codex/) (in the ChatGPT desktop app) | easiest | heavy on the laptop | Point it at your project folder. $20 plan, free tier exists. |\n| [Claude Code](https://claude.com/product/claude-code), in the terminal | easy | fast, light | Paid. A terminal session is all it needs. |\n| [Hermes agent](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/) | harder to set up | fast | Runs local and open models. Most configurable. $20 for 200+ cloud models, optional. |\n| [OpenCode](https://opencode.ai/) | terminal only | fastest | For people who already live in a terminal. |\n\n- **What to use it for tonight.** Install Git and the GitHub CLI and log in with the account you created. Install the Vercel CLI and log in. Commit and push. Diagnose an error from a screenshot.\n- **Paid models are not required.** Everything in this cohort runs on open or local models. Post your hardware in the chat for a local-model recommendation."},{"h":"05 Step 01 · The leak","a":"05-step-01--the-leak","text":"- **Adnan's question from Lecture 5.** If the key is in Aarav's browser, Aarav can take it, share it, and spend your credits. Right.\n- **Strip Gradio to what it hides.** A text area, a button, a box for the answer, one fetch. That is `01-leak/index.html`. Gradio is a cushion over HTML, CSS and JS.\n- **Click Diagnose with no key:** 401. Unauthorized. The key is missing. Paste the key into the file and it works, and now every visitor can read it: Inspect, Network, the `Authorization` header, or just the page source.\n- **Never paste a key on a shared screen.** Do it on your own machine. If one leaks, revoke it.\n- **Three machines, three jobs.** The client, your server, Groq's server. The whole lecture is deciding which component lives on which machine.\n- **Why you cannot vibe-code this part.** An LLM will decide where things live for you, on the assumption of one hobby user. Nothing errors. Someone just quietly spends your money, or your data vanishes. There is now a marketplace of \"vibe coding specialists\" paid to fix exactly this. Be the specialist.\n\n> Sketch: Aarav's browser sends a POST straight to GroqCloud with Authorization Bearer gsk and the key. Below, the Network tab shows that header in full. A stranger reads it from the tab: anyone can read it and spend your money.\n>\n> Caption: The leak: the browser calls Groq directly"},{"h":"06 Step 02 · Strip it to the function","a":"06-step-02--strip-it-to-the-function","text":"- **Bare metal.** Remove every cushion and one function is left: `diagnose(workflow)` takes text in, returns a plan. Everything else was decoration.\n- **This is what you deploy first.** Not the UI. The UI can call your function the way you call Groq, from anywhere, once the function has an address.\n- **Set the key in the terminal.** Then run `python 02-function/diagnose.py` and type a workflow.\n\n> Sketch: A terminal: python diagnose.py, Workflow: I edit until Sunday, and a three-line plan comes back. To the right, a single box: diagnose(workflow) returns plan. One function, no UI, no server.\n>\n> Caption: Strip it down to the function\n\nYour machine\n\nexport GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your_key_here\n\n$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"gsk_your_key_here\"\n\n- **It broke live.** The first run failed on a retired model name. Anand and Kandarp called it from the chat before the fix. Frontier models wrote this repo and still got it wrong. Diagnosing it is the skill.\n- **The fix shows up as a commit.** One save, one diff, the old model name against the new. That is why the code lives on GitHub: a change log of what you got wrong and how it improved.\n- **Your exercise from class.** Strip your Assignment 1 app the same way. In English first: the input, the output, the process in between. Then replace the body of this function and the API it calls. Groq today, a CRM or mail API tomorrow. The file is a template.\n\n> If you strip out the UI, what is the one function left?"},{"h":"07 Step 03 · The function behind an API","a":"07-step-03--the-function-behind-an-api","text":"| Dial | Your API |\n| --- | --- |\n| action | POST |\n| address | `/api/diagnose`, under whatever URL the deploy gives you |\n| package | `{\"workflow\": \"…\"}` in, `{\"plan\": \"…\"}` out |\n| key | none on your API yet. The Groq key stays on your server. |\n\n> Sketch: Your laptop posts a workflow to your server on Vercel, which holds the route POST slash api slash diagnose and the Groq key as an environment variable. The server calls GroqCloud with the key and returns the plan. Underneath, the four dials for your API: address your app on vercel.app slash api slash diagnose, action POST, key none, package workflow in and plan out.\n>\n> Caption: Put the function behind a route, on your server\n\n- **FastAPI is the shortest path.** Four lines: import, `app = FastAPI()`, a decorator with the action and the address, the function under it. Read the [FastAPI tutorial](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/) before asking an LLM to write it.\n- **Why `/api/` in the address.** One folder for every endpoint, separate from static files like the page and images you add later.\n- **Run it:** `uvicorn api.index:app --reload` from inside `03-fastapi`. Open `/docs`: FastAPI lists every route and its input type. Free documentation.\n- **Class exercise.** Convert `diagnose.py` into this API without looking at step 03. Deepti's shipped first."},{"h":"The two lines you did not expect","a":"the-two-lines-you-did-not-expect","text":"```\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass WorkflowIn(BaseModel):\n    workflow: str\n\n@app.post(\"/api/diagnose\")\ndef diagnose_route(body: WorkflowIn):\n    return {\"plan\": diagnose(body.workflow)}\n```\n\n- **Policy versus enforcement.** \"Wear a helmet on a two-wheeler\" is policy. The traffic police on the road is enforcement. Without the police, the rule is a wish.\n- **Your route is a promise to the whole internet.** \"Send me a workflow and I will send a plan.\" Someone will send an image, a video, nothing. Deterministic code has no flexibility. It throws.\n- **Pydantic is the traffic police.** `WorkflowIn` says the incoming package must have a `workflow` and it must be text. Anything else is rejected with an error that names the rule broken. Two lines, a narrow job.\n- **Bigger than APIs.** Lecture 1 said an interface is a common language plus rules. HTTP and JSON are the language. This is the first rule you enforce. The same layer sits between an agent and your software tomorrow, because agents are predictive and can be social-engineered. The deterministic side has to check what arrives.\n- **Homework:** read about Pydantic beyond type checks."},{"h":"08 Deploy, and read the status codes","a":"08-deploy-and-read-the-status-codes","text":"1. **Install and log in:** `npm i -g vercel`, then `vercel login`, same account as the dashboard.\n2. **Open a new terminal inside the step folder.** The live demo stalled on this: the command was run from the wrong directory.\n3. **Run `vercel`.** New project, a name, accept the FastAPI it detects. Then `vercel --prod` for the production URL. `vercel dev` is only a local preview.\n4. **Test from [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app).** POST, your URL, body `{\"workflow\": \"…\"}`. The field name must match `WorkflowIn` exactly.\n5. **Add the key on Vercel.** Project, Settings, Environment Variables. Name it exactly as the code reads it, `GROQ_API_KEY`, paste the value, save, redeploy.\n\n| What came back | Why | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 404 | Wrong address. The deploy URL alone is not the route. | Add `/api/diagnose`. |\n| 500 | Right address, the server itself failed. The key is not on that machine. | Read the Logs tab on the deployment, then add the env var. |\n| 401 from Groq | Your server reached Groq without a key. | Same fix: env var, exact name. |\n| 200 with a plan | Done for today. | Share the URL. |\n\n- **You cannot type on Vercel's machine.** No terminal, so no `export`. The dashboard is the only way in. `os.environ` in your code looks there.\n- **Never hardcode the key to make the 500 go away.** That undoes the whole lecture.\n- **What the room watched happen.** An idea, a design of which component lives where, one function, an API around it with validation, a deploy, env vars, a live URL on a rented machine. Five years ago that was five people: UI, backend, API, deployment, ops."},{"h":"09 Step 04 · Gradio becomes a client (tonight)","a":"09-step-04--gradio-becomes-a-client-tonight","text":"- **One line changes.** Gradio posts `{\"workflow\": …}` to your URL and shows `plan`. No key in the file. The key lives on the machine that spends the money.\n- **Run it against the live server:**\n\nAPI_URL=https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api/diagnose python 04-gradio-client/app.py\n\n> Sketch: A laptop runs Gradio: a text box and a Diagnose button. It posts to your server's URL and holds no key. Your server holds the key and calls GroqCloud. The key lives on the machine that spends the money. A note: Gradio runs on your laptop only, fixed in Lecture 8.\n>\n> Caption: Gradio becomes a client, with no key\n\n- **Write down every error you hit.** Status code, message, what fixed it. Post them tonight. Lecture 8 opens with the common ones.\n- **The debt left.** Gradio runs on your laptop only, and the server forgets every plan the moment it answers. Both are Lecture 8."},{"h":"10 From the chat","a":"10-from-the-chat","text":"- **\"My output is a Google Sheet.\"** The format is not the content. Send JSON from the API and convert to CSV or XLSX on the user's device. Keep the API layer thin. A phone without a spreadsheet app still gets the data.\n- **\"Can I mock the LLM and build only the deterministic part?\"** Yes. Start from your golden input and output pair, build the deterministic path, then add the LLM as one more API call.\n- **\"Localhost URL in the chat.\"** Nobody outside your machine can open it. Send a screenshot.\n- **\"Can we have a gap before the hackathon?\"** No. The gap is never enough. The hackathon is about the attempt, and it shows you the gaps faster than another week of reading.\n- **\"Codex slows my laptop.\"** Use Claude Code or Hermes in the terminal instead.\n- **\"Where does the project live, laptop, GitHub or Vercel?\"** All three. Build on the laptop, push to GitHub, GitHub connects to Vercel."},{"h":"11 Next: Lecture 8, Saturday 6 pm","a":"11-next-lecture-8-saturday-6-pm","text":"- **Storage as a component.** A decision framework: inside the server or outside, and when.\n- **The file dies.** Plans saved to `memory.txt` work locally and fail on Vercel. See [scenes 6 to 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s06).\n- **Things, facts and links become a schema** on Supabase, and the server remembers. [Scenes 9 to 14](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s09).\n- **End to end,** then a recap of the full trace to follow in the hackathon. [Scenes 15 to 20](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s15).\n- **Bring:** your live URL from step 03 and your Modelling Lab tables.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **repository**: A project on GitHub. All its files, and every save you made.\n- **commit**: A save of your progress with a one-line reason. Local until you push.\n- **push**: Send your commits from the laptop to the repository on the internet.\n- **deploy**: Put your code on a rented machine with a URL. `vercel --prod` is the production one.\n- **environment variable**: A named value the machine holds outside the code. Where keys live. Set with `export` on your laptop and in the dashboard on Vercel.\n- **endpoint**: One address on your API, such as `/api/diagnose`, with one action.\n- **FastAPI**: The Python framework that turns a function into an endpoint in four lines, and documents it at `/docs`.\n- **Pydantic**: The data validation library FastAPI uses. The traffic police for everything that arrives.\n- **business logic**: The one function that takes the input and makes the output. Everything else is around it.\n- **CLI**: Command line interface. The Vercel CLI and the GitHub CLI let your terminal, or your LLM desktop app, talk to those machines.\n\nKeep this\n\nThree machines: the user's, yours, and the one you pay for. The hardest bugs are not missing semicolons. They are components living on the wrong machine, and nothing errors when that happens. Strip the app to one function, put it behind an address with a traffic police at the door, and keep the key on the only machine you control. In Lecture 8, that machine learns to remember."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-07-overview.md","title":"Connect Backend to the Database","meta":"title: Connect Backend to the Database\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/index.html\nlecture: 7\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 7 · Fri 16 Oct 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 204","sections":[{"h":"Connect backend to the database","a":"connect-backend-to-the-database","text":"The key lives on the machine that spends the money.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nThe key left the browser. Aarav's one function runs behind a route on a rented machine, with the key in an environment variable there. [Lecture 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/) teaches that machine to remember.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Five things before you build: Python, an IDE, one LLM desktop app, GitHub, Vercel](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s02)\n2. [The leak: the browser calls Groq directly](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s05)\n3. [Strip it down to the function](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s06)\n4. [The function behind an API, with Pydantic at the door](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s07)\n5. [Deploy to Vercel, and read the status codes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s08)\n6. [Gradio becomes a client, with no key](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s09)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"By Saturday 6 pm: a POST to your `vercel.app` URL returns your output, and no key is anywhere a user can see it. [The practice set, five things to post.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set)\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"[ReadNo-code notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code) [Read afterNotes, with the whiteboard sketches](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes) [Do thisPractice set: four steps before Lecture 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set) [The codeThe lecture repository, step by step](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db) [Your tablesThe Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab) Track progressChecklist on the map\n\nThe key lives on the machine that spends the money.Next: [Lecture 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/), Saturday 6 pm. Then the hackathon."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: End to end MVP build and deploy","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: End to end MVP build and deploy\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 8\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 8 · 17 October 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 2644","sections":[{"h":"From localhost to a live link.","a":"from-localhost-to-a-live-link","text":"We proved the app really reads and writes Supabase, then put it online with Git, GitHub and Vercel, and tested the live link.\n\nIn [Lecture 7](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes-no-code) you connected a to-do app in Antigravity to Supabase. We opened with the check for that: add a task on localhost, refresh Supabase, and the task is a new row. Today we did the same for the salon app, then gave it an address anyone can open."},{"h":"01 Who you are, and what you may touch","a":"01-who-you-are-and-what-you-may-touch","text":"Take an app that manages appointments. You type your email and password and click Sign in. Supabase checks them, and if they are right, you are logged in. Does that mean you can now see everyone's appointments?\n\nNo. Two different questions are being asked. \"Who are you?\" is **authentication**. \"What are you allowed to do?\" is **authorization**.\n\n> Sketch: A person signs in with email and password. Supabase checks who they are: that is authentication. Then a table of appointments: the row that belongs to this user is open, and two rows that belong to other users are closed. Deciding which rows this user may touch is authorization.\n>\n> Caption: Logging in answers the first question only. The rule on each row answers the second.\n\nSupabase answers the second question with rules on the rows of a table. For example: a user may read or change only the appointments that carry their own user ID. That is **row level security** (RLS). Each rule is a **policy**. The slide set it up in four steps:\n\n1. Turn on login for the project.\n2. Turn on RLS for each table that needs it.\n3. Write the policies: who can read, who can change.\n4. Test with two different user accounts.\n\n> Turning RLS on is not enough. You also have to write the policies."},{"h":"02 Connect the salon app, keep the design","a":"02-connect-the-salon-app-keep-the-design","text":"Why Supabase at all? The older way is to write each back end part yourself (the database, the login, the file uploads) and look after it. Supabase rents you those parts ready-made, on a Postgres database. You still decide the tables and the permissions.\n\nWhen do you move off it? The instructor's rule of thumb: Supabase is enough up to about 10,000 daily active users. Past that it gets expensive compared with running your own.\n\nThe salon booking app needs four tables: customers, stylists, services and appointments. Why four and not one? Each table holds one kind of thing. The appointments table joins them by storing three IDs: which customer, which stylist, which service. It adds the time and the status.\n\nMake a new Supabase project for this app. The slide's prompt for Antigravity asked for four things:\n\n- **Use the existing design.**\n- **Connect to my Supabase project.**\n- **Work with the four tables:** customers, stylists, services, appointments.\n- **Implement the functionality without redesigning the interface.**\n\nWhy the last line? Coding tools sometimes change your front end to make the back end easier.\n\nAntigravity will ask for two things from the API keys page in Supabase: the project URL and the **publishable key**. Both are made to sit in the front end. The secret key on that page (the service role key) skips your policies. Never put it in front-end code.\n\nStart with one screen and one table. If a request fails, write the missing policy. Do not switch RLS off."},{"h":"03 Four things you can do to a row","a":"03-four-things-you-can-do-to-a-row","text":"Asha books a signature haircut with Maya on Thursday at 4 PM. What should happen when she clicks Book? The app asks Supabase to add a row. Supabase checks the permissions and saves it. Only then does the app say it worked.\n\n- **Create.** Booking adds a new row to appointments.\n- **Read.** Opening the appointments page fetches the rows and shows them.\n- **Update.** Asha moves to Friday at 11 AM. The app changes the row that exists.\n- **Delete.** Asha cancels.\n\nTogether these four are called **CRUD**. After each one, open the table in Supabase and look at the row.\n\n> Sketch: Two cards for the same appointment. Before: appointment 2041, Asha, signature haircut with Maya, Thursday 4 PM. After: appointment 2041, Asha, signature haircut with Maya, Friday 11 AM. The ID is the same on both cards. Only the time changed.\n>\n> Caption: Same ID, 2041, before and after. If rescheduling made a new row, Asha would have two bookings.\n\nCancelling has two versions. A hard delete removes the row for good. Most real apps keep the row and change its status from booked to cancelled, so the salon keeps its history. That is a **soft delete**: the user sees \"cancel\", and the database does an update. Either way, ask the user to confirm first.\n\n> What the user sees and what the database does are not always the same.\n\nWhich one did your app do? Open the browser's inspect mode, go to the Network tab, and click the button. The request shows a delete or an update."},{"h":"04 Is the screen telling the truth?","a":"04-is-the-screen-telling-the-truth","text":"The screen shows Asha's appointment. Is it coming from the database, or is it text typed into the page? Coding tools sometimes write the data straight into the interface. Two tests settle it.\n\n1. **App to table.** Change something in the app. Check the row changed in Supabase.\n2. **Table to app.** Insert a row by hand in Supabase. The instructor added one called \"testing appointment\". It should appear in the app.\n\nThen refresh the page. If the appointment is still there, it lives in the database and not in the page's short memory.\n\nFetching takes a moment. If the screen says nothing, people think the app is broken and click again, which sends the request twice. The slide had four messages:\n\n| What happened | What the user sees |\n| --- | --- |\n| The request has started | \"Loading appointments\", over grey placeholder cards |\n| It worked, and there are no rows | \"No appointments yet\" (an empty state, not an error) |\n| Supabase confirmed the save | A message that the appointment is saved |\n| The save failed | \"Could not save your appointment. Please try again.\" |\n\nThe last message leaves out the technical error and says what to do next. Ask Antigravity to add the placeholder cards and the empty state."},{"h":"05 Localhost is only your laptop","a":"05-localhost-is-only-your-laptop","text":"Everything works on your laptop. How do you share it with someone in another city? The address localhost:3000 means \"this computer\", so it opens for nobody else. The same app at an address like yourapp.vercel.app opens anywhere. It looks the same; only where it runs has changed. Putting it there is called **deploying**.\n\nFive tools do it, each with one job. Antigravity changes the code. Git records each change. GitHub keeps the code online. Vercel puts the app on a public address. Supabase keeps the data.\n\n> Sketch: Two paths. Top path, the code: Antigravity on your laptop, then Git, then GitHub, then Vercel, which produces the live address. Bottom path, the data: a user opens the live app, and the live app reads and writes Supabase directly. A booking never passes through GitHub.\n>\n> Caption: Code travels the top path only when you change the app. A customer's booking takes the bottom path and never touches GitHub.\n\n> GitHub stores the code. Vercel runs the app. Supabase stores the data."},{"h":"06 Final, final new, final fixed","a":"06-final-final-new-final-fixed","text":"On Tuesday you save a folder called Final. On Wednesday, Final New. On Thursday, Final Fixed. Which one is running on the internet? From the folder names, nobody can say.\n\n**Git** solves this. It keeps one project folder, called a **repository**, with a history of checkpoints inside it. Each checkpoint is a **commit**: a set of changes plus a short message. If something breaks later, you go back to a checkpoint.\n\nGit runs on your computer, even offline. GitHub is a website that keeps a copy of the repository online, where teammates and Vercel can reach it. Git is the history book; GitHub is the library it sits in.\n\nSaving a file is not a commit, and a commit is not on GitHub until you **push** it. Three steps in the terminal:\n\n1. `git status` shows which files changed.\n2. `git add` picks the changes, and `git commit` records them with a message.\n3. `git push` sends the commits to GitHub.\n\n> Commit records the change. Push uploads it. Deploy publishes it.\n\nWhat should not go to GitHub? The room said it at once: keys and passwords. The slide added node_modules, the folder of ready-made code your project installs, because it can be made again. A file called **.gitignore** lists what Git must leave out."},{"h":"07 Inspect first, then push","a":"07-inspect-first-then-push","text":"Before changing anything, we asked Antigravity to look at the project and report. The prompt asked for:\n\n- **How the project is made.** The framework (the kit the app is made with) and the language.\n- **What it needs to run.** The settings it reads, such as the Supabase URL.\n- **Whether Git is ready.** Is it set up, and does.gitignore leave out secrets?\n- **Two limits.** Do not redesign the UI. Do not change the database tables.\n\nCheck the report against the real files.\n\nThen sign up at GitHub and create a repository. Private is fine: a private repository can still be deployed as a public app. If GitHub offers to add a.gitignore, say yes. Copy the repository's URL.\n\nIn the terminal, run `git status` and `git remote -v`. The first says whether Git is tracking the project. The second says whether it is already linked to a repository online. Do only the step that is missing. If the online repository already has commits, never force a push over them.\n\nAfter the push, open the repository on GitHub and look. Your pages are there. There is a.gitignore. There is no.env file with real values, and no secret key in any file.\n\nFound a secret key up there? Deleting it in a later commit does not erase it from the history. Make a new key in Supabase so the old one stops working."},{"h":"08 Build it, then hand it to Vercel","a":"08-build-it-then-hand-it-to-vercel","text":"Code on GitHub is not a live site. First the ready-made code gets installed. Then a **build** turns the code into the files a browser is served. Vercel does both, but try them on your laptop first, so you are not pushing and hoping:\n\n1. `npm install` installs that ready-made code.\n2. `npm run build` runs the build command written in the project's package.json file.\n\nProjects differ, so read the scripts in your own package.json. If the build fails, read the first meaningful error, make the smallest fix, and build again.\n\nThen Vercel:\n\n1. Go to [Vercel](https://vercel.com) and choose Continue with GitHub.\n2. Import the repository you pushed.\n3. Check what Vercel detected: the framework, the build command and the output folder (often `dist`). If your app sits in a subfolder, set the root directory.\n4. Deploy. Vercel builds the app and gives you a public URL.\n\nIf the build fails on Vercel, it is usually a setting, not the code. After this, every push to GitHub makes Vercel build and publish again by itself."},{"h":"09 A live link is not a working product","a":"09-a-live-link-is-not-a-working-product","text":"The page loads at the new address. But the appointments do not load, and nothing saves. The code is the same as on your laptop. What is different?\n\nOn your laptop, the app reads the Supabase URL and the publishable key from a file called.env. That file stayed on your laptop, as it should:.gitignore kept it off GitHub. So Vercel never got those values. Values kept outside the code like this are **environment variables**.\n\n> Sketch: Three boxes. Left: your laptop, where the app reads the Supabase URL and key from the .env file. Middle: GitHub, which holds the same code but no .env file. Right: Vercel, where the two values have to be typed in again under Environment Variables, followed by a redeploy.\n>\n> Caption: The push carries the code, not the.env file. Vercel needs the two values typed in.\n\n1. In Vercel, open the project and go to Environment Variables.\n2. Add the Supabase project URL and the publishable key. The names must match what the code expects, letter for letter (for example `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`).\n3. Choose the Production environment, and save.\n4. Redeploy. The values are baked in when the app is built, so saving alone changes nothing on the live site.\n\nIf something still breaks, find where before you ask the coding tool to change any code.\n\nIt can fail in four places. The build: an error in the code. The Vercel settings. While running: a click throws an error. The back end: data does not load or save.\n\nThen run both tests from section 04 on the live link. Create an appointment and find the row in Supabase. Edit the row in Supabase and see it in the app.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**You hid the Edit button from other users. A friend says Asha's appointment is now safe. What is wrong with that?**\n\nHiding a button is not security. Someone can still send a request straight to the database. The database has to refuse it: RLS on, and a policy that lets a user change only their own rows.\n\n**Asha moves her haircut from Thursday to Friday. Afterwards the table has two rows for her. Which of the four actions did the app use, and which should it have used?**\n\nIt used create. Update changes the existing row and keeps the same ID (2041 in the example).\n\n**Your app saves appointments on localhost. On the Vercel link the page loads but nothing saves. Where do you look first?**\n\nAt the environment variables in Vercel. The.env file never left your laptop. Add the URL and the publishable key with the exact names the code uses, then redeploy.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Authentication**: Checking who you are, usually with an email and password.\n- **Authorization**: Deciding what you are allowed to see or change once you are logged in.\n- **Row level security (RLS)**: Rules in Supabase that say which rows of a table each user may read or change.\n- **CRUD**: The four things an app does to a row: create, read, update, delete.\n- **Deploy**: Put the app on a public address so other people can open it.\n- **Repository**: The project folder with its history of changes.\n- **Commit**: A checkpoint: a set of changes saved in the history with a message.\n- **Push**: Send your commits from your computer to GitHub.\n- **Build**: Turn the project's code into the files a browser is served.\n- **Environment variable**: A value the app needs, such as the Supabase URL, kept outside the code.\n\nKeep this\n\nGitHub stores the code, Vercel runs the app, Supabase stores the data. Commit, push, deploy, then add the Supabase URL and publishable key in Vercel and redeploy. A live link counts only when a row made on it shows up in Supabase."},{"h":"10 Your turn: ship it, then the hackathon","a":"10-your-turn-ship-it-then-the-hackathon","text":"Deploy the salon app or your own, tonight. Send the link to one person who did not build it and ask them to book an appointment. Then check the row in Supabase.\n\nThe lecture closed on the hackathon page. Pick one problem and build it solo; AI tools are allowed. Start on paper, deploy with login and RLS, and keep real evidence. It closes on Tuesday 20 October at 9 PM.\n\n[Open the hackathon page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-08-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: End to End MVP","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: End to End MVP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes.html\nlecture: 8\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 8\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2180","sections":[{"h":"The server remembers","a":"the-server-remembers","text":"What was said on Saturday, in the order it was said. Local first, the file, why it breaks, then Postgres on Supabase with the same four dials.\n\nSat 17 Oct 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiCode track"},{"h":"01 The lecture in one line","a":"01-the-lecture-in-one-line","text":"- **Lecture 7** put `diagnose` behind an API on Vercel. **Today** that API learns to remember, first in a file, then in a database.\n- **Every whiteboard from the last four weeks connects here.** Four dials, things, facts and links, policy and enforcement.\n- **The repository was updated.** Same [c8-db repo](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db), now with a `.env.example` and steps 01 to 08. Every step is a folder; the sections below link to theirs.\n- **Drawn version:** [Lecture 8 on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard), twenty scenes in the same order."},{"h":"02 Local first, then production","a":"02-local-first-then-production","text":"- **Two environments, kept apart.** Local is everything running on your laptop. Production is the link a real user opens. If Aarav is using the app and you push a change that breaks it, Aarav pays for your test.\n- **Run the API locally.** From inside the step folder: `uvicorn api.index:app --reload`. `api.index` is the file, `app` is what gets served, `--reload` restarts on every save. Install with `pip install fastapi uvicorn`.\n- **Point Gradio at it.** [Step 04](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/04-gradio-client) is the same `requests.post` from the API lecture: action, address, package. The address is now `http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/diagnose` and there is no key in the file.\n- **Go to production by changing one thing: the address.** Swap the local URL for your `vercel.app` URL, stop the local server, and it still answers. Same code, different machine.\n- **Never send a `127.0.0.1` link.** It means \"this machine\". It opens on yours and nobody else's. Anything you submit or share has to be a deployed link."},{"h":"03 Read the logs on someone else's machine","a":"03-read-the-logs-on-someone-elses-machine","text":"- **Locally, the terminal shows every request.** On Vercel you have no terminal. Open the project, then the **Logs** tab.\n- **One request, read live:** received in Mumbai, routed to Washington DC, an external call to Groq, 2.77 seconds, status 200.\n- **A 404 or 500 shows up here first.** This is where you start when something goes wrong in production."},{"h":"04 What is worth storing","a":"04-what-is-worth-storing","text":"- **Start from the Modelling Lab.** Users, workflows, messages, conversations, diagnoses, tools. The full picture.\n- **Then store the least that tests the hypothesis.** The bet: given a workflow diagnosis, 6 of 10 early-career people map one repeated workflow and build an app. That needs three things: a **user**, a **workflow**, a **diagnosis**. Conversations, profiles and tools can wait.\n- **Facts.** A user has a name. A workflow has a description. A diagnosis has a plan. Each gets an `id` and a `created_at`.\n- **Why the id.** Two users can both be called Mira. A name does not tell them apart, an id does.\n- **User to workflow: one to many.** One person writes many workflows. A workflow has exactly one owner.\n- **Workflow to diagnosis: one to one, for now.** Under today's rules each message gets one plan. Whether it should allow several is not your call to guess. Run both with your ten users and keep the cheaper one unless users need the other. That answer only comes from discovery.\n- **The link is the `user_id` on the workflow.** Workflow 7 has `user_id` 1, Aarav is user 1, done. No name matching. The same holds for an LLM or an agent reading your data.\n- **The industry calls this an ER diagram.** Entities are things, relationships are links. Knowledge graphs, second brains and LLM wikis all grow from this same first principle.\n\n> A user is a thing, not a fact. Facts about Aarav, his role or age, come later and make the prompt personal."},{"h":"05 Step 05 · memory.txt in four steps","a":"05-step-05--memorytxt-in-four-steps","text":"[05-memory-txt in the repository](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/05-memory-txt)\n\nWrite it in English before you read the code. Most of the chat got it. The step most people missed was the timestamp.\n\n```\nmemory = open(\"memory.txt\").read()          # 1. read what is already there\nplan = diagnose(body.workflow)              # 2. same business logic as before\nline = f\"{datetime.now()} | {body.workflow} | {plan}\\n\"   # 3. time, workflow, diagnosis\nopen(\"memory.txt\", \"w\").write(memory + line)              # 4. write it all back\n```\n\n- **Why read first.** `\"w\"` replaces the whole file. Keep what was there, then add the new line.\n- **The user is left out on purpose.** For one user, workflow and diagnosis are enough to test with Aarav. Add users when it works.\n- **Test locally.** Run step 05 with uvicorn, point Gradio at it, send a workflow, open `memory.txt`. The time, the input and the plan are there. Persistence was four lines."},{"h":"06 Where a file breaks, and where it does not","a":"06-where-a-file-breaks-and-where-it-does-not","text":"- **Do the math.** 100 users, 10 workflows a day, 60 days: 60,000 lines, and you need to know who wrote which, when.\n- **Two writers at once.** Jagan and Vineet start the same hackathon at the same minute. Both read, both write, one plan is lost. A race condition.\n- **Every read and write costs memory.** A small rented machine runs out, crashes, and returns 500 to everyone, not just the person who caused it.\n- **The rule of thumb.** One user, under about ten operations a day, a fixed window: a file is fine. More than one user writing: think database.\n- **Read-only scales on a file.** The curriculum app serves 200+ people, soon 2,000, from one file. A personal site peaked above 10,000 readers on files. Nobody writes, so nothing races. Vercel copies static files to servers around the world (a CDN).\n- **Writing is the expensive part.** A database is a file system with fifty years of fixes: backups, copies near the user, rolling back a bad write."},{"h":"07 Postgres, rented from Supabase","a":"07-postgres-rented-from-supabase","text":"- **SQL-type, because your model maps straight onto it** and it is easy to query. The specific one is **PostgreSQL**: free, open source, and what OpenAI runs on at 800 million users.\n- **Own house or rent.** You can host Postgres yourself and run the maintenance. With ten users, rent.\n- **Supabase is to Postgres what Vercel is to servers.** It wraps the database in an API, plus auth, storage and vectors. Free up to 50,000 monthly active users and 500 MB. Tested past 100 users with controlled reads and writes.\n\n1. Sign in at [supabase.com](https://supabase.com). GitHub login is easiest.\n2. New organisation: any name, Personal, Free plan.\n3. New project: a name and a database password you save somewhere. Leave the rest, including the region it picks for you.\n4. Wait for it to come up. That is a rented machine with your database on it."},{"h":"08 The same four dials","a":"08-the-same-four-dials","text":"| Dial | For your database |\n| --- | --- |\n| address | Project URL, under Settings, Data API |\n| action | GET reads. POST creates. PUT replaces, PATCH updates part. DELETE deletes. |\n| key | Settings, API keys. The **secret key**, on your server only. |\n| package | The rows you send, and the JSON that comes back |\n\n- **Two keys.** The publishable key is safe in a browser only once row level security is on. The secret key can read and write everything. Today you use the secret key, and only on the server.\n- **Four machines now.** The user's, yours on Vercel, Groq's, and Supabase's. Each one is there for a reason you can name. [Whiteboard, scene 17](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s17)."},{"h":"09 From the whiteboard to tables","a":"09-from-the-whiteboard-to-tables","text":"| Modelling Lab | Database |\n| --- | --- |\n| thing | table |\n| fact | column, with a type |\n| link | foreign key (FK) |\n| id | primary key (PK) |\n\n```\ncreate table users (\n  id bigint generated always as identity primary key,\n  name text,\n  created_at timestamptz default now()\n);\ncreate table workflows (\n  id bigint generated always as identity primary key,\n  description text,\n  user_id bigint references users(id),\n  created_at timestamptz default now()\n);\n-- diagnoses: plan text, workflow_id bigint references workflows(id)\n```\n\n- **Two machines need a common language.** The API connects Vercel to Supabase. SQL connects Python to Postgres. `schema.sql` is the JSON schema of the database.\n- **Types are the traffic police again.** A name is `text`. Send a picture into it and the database refuses. SQL types are not Python types.\n- **Two ways to make the tables.** Paste [`schema.sql`](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/06-schema) into the SQL Editor and run it once. Or Table Editor, New table: add each column and its type, then Add foreign key to link it.\n- **Check it.** The Schema Visualizer draws your ER diagram back: the same three boxes, the same lines.\n- **Learn SQL by writing it.** The BigBinary SQL course on the LMS runs in the browser, no setup.\n- **Why the lecture stays on the design.** The verifier's rule: whatever is easy to verify, AI will do. Code is easy to verify. Deciding what is worth storing, for which user, to test which bet, is not. That part is yours."},{"h":"10 Steps 07 and 08 · The server remembers","a":"10-steps-07-and-08--the-server-remembers","text":"[07-supabase-sdk](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/07-supabase-sdk) (the SDK and the raw calls) and [08-supabase-api](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/08-supabase-api) (the server and the page) in the repository.\n\n- **Keys in `.env`.** Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill `GROQ_API_KEY`, `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY`. Every step from 05 on reads it.\n- **The raw call.** `<project-url>/rest/v1/<table>`, with the key in the headers. Filters, updates and deletes are the same request with a different action. Read the Supabase API docs once so you know what the SDK is doing.\n- **The cushion.** The SDK hides the address and headers:\n\n```\nsupabase = create_client(SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY)\n\nplan = diagnose(body.workflow)\nsupabase.table(\"diagnoses\").insert({\"workflow_id\": 1, \"plan\": plan}).execute()\n\nsupabase.table(\"diagnoses\").select(\"*\").eq(\"workflow_id\", 1).execute()\n```\n\n- **Four file lines became one insert.** `diagnose` and the FastAPI route did not change. That is a modular system: swap storage without touching business logic. Tell your LLM this up front or it will tangle them.\n- **JSON comes back because you asked for it.** The content matters, not the format. A spreadsheet user can still get JSON converted on their side.\n- **On Vercel, add the same two variables.** Settings, Environment Variables, exact names from `.env`. Redeploy. Only the address changed, so the code is the same."},{"h":"11 Whose row is this?","a":"11-whose-row-is-this","text":"- **Types are enforced by the database already.** Who can read which row is not.\n- **Mira joins.** If she finds the URL and a key, one `select *` returns Aarav's workflows too. So would a glitch that gave both of them id 1. So would an attacker who wants to sell your data.\n- **Row level security (RLS)** is the policy, written in SQL: you see only the rows where `auth.uid()` matches the row's owner. Without a policy, anyone who gets a query through gets everything.\n- **It needs login.** Supabase's Authentication tab: start with the email provider. Google, Apple and the rest come with third-party costs. Each signed-up user gets a UID in a separate auth table, and RLS checks it.\n- **Advanced for now.** Store first, then RLS for logged-in users, then deeper auth. The [Supabase RLS guide](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security) has the details. [Whiteboard, scene 19](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s19)."},{"h":"12 From the chat","a":"12-from-the-chat","text":"- **\"Should we do all 50 hours of the SQL course?\"** Learning is insurance. AI writes code well and fails on the hard cases, and if you do not understand the system you cannot explain the failure to another AI or an expert. Buy as much cover as you want.\n- **\"Is RLS about access or about injection?\"** Both. It limits each user to their rows, and an injected query without a matching UID gets nothing.\n- **\"Where is the diagnoses table created?\"** In `schema.sql`, run once in the SQL Editor. Or by hand with New table.\n- **\"How do I call Supabase without the SDK?\"** The Data API reference: `rest/v1/`, the table name, the action, and the body."},{"h":"13 Next: the hackathon","a":"13-next-the-hackathon","text":"Dates, problems, check-ins and rules are on [the hackathon page](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/).\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **localhost**: `127.0.0.1`. This machine. A link to it works only on yours.\n- **uvicorn**: The server that runs a FastAPI app on your laptop.\n- **ER diagram**: Entities and relationships. Things and links, drawn.\n- **primary key**: The column that tells every row apart. Usually `id`.\n- **foreign key**: A column that points at another table's primary key. A link.\n- **race condition**: Two writers at once, and one write is lost.\n- **PostgreSQL**: The free, open-source SQL database Supabase runs.\n- **SDK**: A library that wraps an API in functions, like `supabase.table().insert()`.\n- **RLS**: Row level security. A policy on which rows each user can read or write.\n\nKeep this\n\nDecide what is worth storing before you store it: the fewest things, facts and links that test your bet. A file is fine for one user or for read-only pages. Once several people write, rent a database. It is one more machine at an address, called with the same four dials, and the secret key stays on your server."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-08-overview.md","title":"End to End MVP","meta":"title: End to End MVP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/index.html\nlecture: 8\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 8 · Sat 17 Oct 2026, 6 pm IST · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 239","sections":[{"h":"End to end MVP","a":"end-to-end-mvp","text":"A database is a machine at an address. SQL is its package.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nLecture 7 put the key on your server. Tonight that server learns to remember, the UI leaves your laptop, and a classmate uses the live app.\n\n01"},{"h":"Bring","a":"bring","text":"- Your live `vercel.app` URL from the [Lecture 7 practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set).\n- Your tables from the [Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab).\n\n02"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Local first, then production](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s02)\n2. [Store the least that tests the bet](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s04)\n3. [Plans saved to memory.txt, and where a file breaks](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s06)\n4. [A database is a machine at an address](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s09)\n5. [SQL is the package language](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s12)\n6. [The cushion: what the SDK hides](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s14)\n7. [The server remembers](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s15)\n8. [Rip off Gradio: one fetch](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s16)\n9. [Whose row is this?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard#s19)\n\n03"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"A classmate opens your URL, gets a plan, refreshes, and the plan is still there. They search the Network tab and find no key. Then they post \"verified\" next to your URL.\n\n04"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [No-code track notes: from localhost to a live link](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes-no-code)\n- [Notes: what was said, in order](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes)\n- [Lecture 8 on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard)\n- [The lecture repository, steps 01 to 08](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db)\n- [Lecture 7, the key leaves the browser](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/)\n- [The hackathon: problems, rules, timeline](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/)\n- Checklist on the map\n\nA database is a machine at an address. SQL is its package.Next: [the hackathon](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/)."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-08-whiteboard.md","title":"Lecture 8 on a Whiteboard","meta":"title: Lecture 8 on a Whiteboard\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/whiteboard.html\nlecture: 8\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 8\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3268","sections":[{"h":"Lecture 8, on a whiteboard","a":"lecture-8-on-a-whiteboard","text":"A database is a machine at an address. SQL is its package.\n\nSat 17 Oct 2026, 6 pm IST. Before this: [Lecture 7 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes). After: [Lecture 8 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes)\n\nThe code: [github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db), steps 01 to 08\n\nP to present · ← → to move · Esc to leave\n\nPart 1 · Local first\n\n01 · Lectures 6 and 7"},{"h":"Three boxes, and storage was a file on your server","a":"three-boxes-and-storage-was-a-file-on-your-server","text":"> Sketch: The system from Lecture 6. You and the client, your server with a file called memory.txt beside it inside the same dashed zone, and GroqCloud, which thinks. Brackets underneath: UI in week 1, API in week 2. A note: today this file dies, and storage becomes a machine.\n\nOn Friday the key left the browser: one function behind a route on Vercel, Gradio as a client. Last week's high-level design: UI for humans, API for machines, and storage for memory. We drew storage as a file on your server, and on paper we gave it a shape: eight tables, every arrow a many side pointing back. Today: run it locally first, decide what is worth storing, build that file, see where it breaks, and move it to a machine built for the job.\n\n02 · [Steps 03 and 04](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/04-gradio-client)"},{"h":"Local first, then production: only the address changes","a":"local-first-then-production-only-the-address-changes","text":"> Sketch: Inside a dashed box labelled your laptop, local: Gradio on the laptop posts to slash api slash diagnose on a server run by uvicorn at 127.0.0.1 port 8000. A second arrow leaves the box to your app on Vercel. Swap the address, nothing else. 127.0.0.1 means this machine: never share it.\n\nKeep two environments apart. Local is everything on your laptop: `uvicorn api.index:app --reload` serves the API at `127.0.0.1:8000`, and the step 04 Gradio file posts to it with the same action, address and package as the Groq call in Lecture 5, minus the key. When it works, swap that one address for your `vercel.app` URL, stop the local server, and it still answers. A localhost link opens on your machine and nobody else's, so anything you share has to be deployed.\n\n03 · Production"},{"h":"Read the logs on someone else's machine","a":"read-the-logs-on-someone-elses-machine","text":"> Sketch: A log row from Vercel's Logs tab: 18:29, POST slash api slash diagnose, 200, 2.77 seconds. Below, the trip it records: your laptop in Mumbai, to the Vercel function in Washington DC, to GroqCloud as an external call. No terminal on someone else's machine: the Logs tab is your terminal.\n\nOn your laptop every request prints in the terminal. Vercel's machine has no terminal you can open, so open the project's Logs tab instead. One row tells the whole trip: received in Mumbai, routed to Washington DC, one external call to Groq, 2.77 seconds, status 200. When something is a 404 or a 500 in production, this is where you look first.\n\nPart 2 · What is worth storing\n\n04 · What is worth storing"},{"h":"Store the least that tests the bet","a":"store-the-least-that-tests-the-bet","text":"> Sketch: A sticky note with the hypothesis: given a workflow diagnosis, 6 of 10 early-career people map one repeated workflow and build an app. An arrow down to three boxes kept: users, workflows, diagnoses. Three boxes struck out for later: messages, conversations, tools. The fewest things that can prove or kill the bet.\n\nThe Modelling Lab gave you the full picture: users, workflows, messages, conversations, diagnoses, tools. Do not build all of it. Ask what is the cheapest data that can test the hypothesis. Here that is a user, the workflow they write, and the diagnosis you give back. Without those three the experiment cannot run. Everything else can wait until users ask for it.\n\n05 · Facts, ids and links"},{"h":"An ER diagram: one user, many workflows, one plan each","a":"an-er-diagram-one-user-many-workflows-one-plan-each","text":"> Sketch: Three boxes. Users: id, name, created_at. Workflows: id, user_id, description, created_at. Diagnoses: id, workflow_id, plan, created_at. A violet arrow from user_id back to users, marked one to many. A violet arrow from workflow_id back to workflows, marked one to one for now. An id tells two Miras apart, and a link is an id stored on the other side.\n\nFacts: a user has a name, a workflow has a description, a diagnosis has a plan, and each gets a time. Each also gets an id, because two people can both be called Mira. One user writes many workflows, and each workflow has one owner, so `user_id` sits on the workflow. Under today's rules each workflow gets one plan, so workflow to diagnosis is one to one. Whether it should allow more is not a guess. Run both versions with ten users and keep the cheaper one unless they need the other.\n\nPart 3 · The file, and where it breaks\n\n06 · [Step 05](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/05-memory-txt)"},{"h":"The Lecture 6 storage box, built: memory.txt","a":"the-lecture-6-storage-box-built-memorytxt","text":"> Sketch: Six numbered steps. 1, httpie posts a workflow to your local server. 2, the server reads memory.txt. 3, it asks GroqCloud. 4, the plan comes back. 5, the server writes the file back with one more line. 6, the plan returns to the laptop. GET slash api slash plans returns every line. Locally it works.\n\nThis is the file we drew last week. Write the steps in English before reading the code: read the file, run the same `diagnose`, build one line with the time, the workflow and the plan, and write it all back, because `\"w\"` replaces everything. The timestamp is the step most people miss. Users are left out on purpose: for one user, Aarav, two facts are enough.\n\nA second route reads the whole file. Run it locally, send a workflow from Gradio, open `memory.txt`: the time, the input and the plan are there. Persistence was four lines.\n\n07 · Two writers"},{"h":"100 users, 60,000 lines, and two writers at once","a":"100-users-60000-lines-and-two-writers-at-once","text":"> Sketch: Jagan and Vineet both POST to the local server on a count of three. The sequence: Jagan reads the empty file, Vineet reads the empty file, Jagan writes his plan, Vineet writes her plan. The file now holds only Vineet's plan. Jagan's is crossed out. Last write wins.\n\nOne user is easy. Now 100 users write 10 workflows a day for 60 days: 60,000 lines, and you still need to know who wrote which, and when. Worse, they do not arrive in a line. Jagan and Vineet open the app at the same minute. Both requests get a 200. Then read the file: one plan. Each request read the same old file and wrote its own version back, so whoever wrote last erased the other. The server never lied. The file just cannot hold two writers. That is a race condition, and every read and write also eats the RAM of a small rented machine until it crashes and answers 500 to everyone.\n\n08 · The decision"},{"h":"A file is enough, until people write at once","a":"a-file-is-enough-until-people-write-at-once","text":"> Sketch: Two columns. A file is enough: one user, under about ten writes a day, a fixed test window, read-only pages; this curriculum serves 200 plus readers from one file, a personal site served 10,000. Rent a database: many people writing at once, backups, a copy near each user, roll back a bad write; and Vercel's disk is read-only anyway. Writing is expensive, reading is cheap.\n\nDo not reach for a database by reflex. One user making a few writes a day for a limited test is fine on a file, and read-only sites scale on files because nobody writes, and a CDN like Vercel's copies them around the world. The need starts when your machine cannot keep up: many writers, 500s, lost lines. Then you graduate from a file to a database. Deploy [step 05](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/05-memory-txt) to see one more reason: Vercel's file system is read-only, so the write fails with a 500.\n\nPart 4 · Another machine\n\n09 · The principle"},{"h":"A database is a machine at an address","a":"a-database-is-a-machine-at-an-address","text":"> Sketch: The client, your server, and now two machines on the right. GroqCloud, which thinks, and Supabase, a server holding the file of tables, which remembers, at your project on supabase.co. Your server talks to both by API: insert and select go to Supabase.\n\nPick a SQL database, because your model maps straight onto it: PostgreSQL, free and open source, the one OpenAI runs at 800 million users. You could host it yourself, the independent house again. With ten users, rent. Supabase is to Postgres what Vercel is to servers, free up to 50,000 monthly active users. Sign in, create an organisation and a project, save the database password, keep the default region. Then open Settings. Data API gives you one URL, API keys gives you two keys. That is all a database is from where your server stands: another machine at an address that answers the four dials. Groq thinks. Supabase remembers. Your server is a client to both.\n\n10 · The rule, again"},{"h":"Every arrow is the same four dials","a":"every-arrow-is-the-same-four-dials","text":"> Sketch: A table. Rows: address, action, key, package. Columns: Groq, your API, Supabase. Groq: api.groq.com, POST, key required, model and messages. Your API: your app on vercel.app slash api slash diagnose, POST, no key today, workflow in and plan out. Supabase: your project on supabase.co slash rest slash v1 slash diagnoses, POST creates, GET reads, PATCH and PUT update, DELETE deletes, apikey plus Bearer with the service key on the server only, workflow_id, plan and tone.\n\nNothing new to learn about talking to a database. Groq, your own API and Supabase all answer the same address, action, key, package. The action is the verb: POST the first time you write, PUT to replace a row, PATCH to change part of it, DELETE to remove it, GET to read. Fill the four dials before any code.\n\n11 · Two keys"},{"h":"One key for strangers' machines, one for yours","a":"one-key-for-strangers-machines-one-for-yours","text":"> Sketch: Left: the anon or publishable key, for machines you do not control, like a browser, later. Right: the service_role or secret key, which bypasses every lock, kept in a server environment variable only. A padlock: Row Level Security, leave it alone today.\n\nTwo keys, two kinds of machine. The publishable key is safe in a browser only once Row Level Security is on, so it waits for later. The secret key bypasses every lock, so it lives only in your server's environment variables, next to the Groq key. Row Level Security stays untouched today. Scene 19 shows why it matters.\n\n12 · [Step 06](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/06-schema)"},{"h":"SQL is the package language: things become tables, links become foreign keys","a":"sql-is-the-package-language-things-become-tables-links-become-foreign-keys","text":"> Sketch: Left, schema.sql: create table diagnoses, id primary key, workflow_id references workflows(id), plan text, tone text, created_at default now(), and a final select for workflow 1 ordered by created_at. Right, each line mapped to the Lecture 6 model: create table is a thing becoming a table, primary key is the id, references is a link, the foreign key, each other line is a fact becoming a column, default now() is the memory.txt timestamp, and the last line is Aarav's Lecture 6 question as SQL.\n\nTwo machines need a common language. The API connects Vercel to Supabase. SQL connects Python to Postgres, and `schema.sql` is its package. Paste it into the SQL Editor and run it once, or build the same tables by hand in the Table Editor: New table, a column per fact with its type, Add foreign key for each link. Read it line by line against last week's three questions: a thing is a `create table`, a fact is a line inside it, a link is `references` on the many side. The type on each line is the traffic police again: send a picture into a `text` column and the database refuses. The last line is the question from Lecture 6. Designing this is the part AI cannot verify for you, so it stays your job.\n\n13 · The tables"},{"h":"Users, workflows, diagnoses: the same arrows as last week","a":"users-workflows-diagnoses-the-same-arrows-as-last-week","text":"> Sketch: Three tables. Users: id 1, Aarav. Workflows: id 1, user_id 1, the description Script Monday, shoot Tuesday, edit until Sunday. Diagnoses: id 1, workflow_id 1, plan Batch your edits, created at 17 Oct 18:40. Violet arrows from workflow_id back to workflows and from user_id back to users. The Schema Visualizer draws the same three boxes back.\n\nThree of last week's eight tables, enough for today's product. The schema also inserts Aarav and his Monday workflow, so there is something to point at. Open the Schema Visualizer and Supabase draws your ER diagram back: the same three boxes, the same arrows. Every row in the Table Editor outlives every machine that touched it.\n\n14 · [Step 07](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/07-supabase-sdk)"},{"h":"The cushion: what the SDK hides","a":"the-cushion-what-the-sdk-hides","text":"> Sketch: Left, raw httpie: POST to the URL slash rest slash v1 slash diagnoses, apikey and Authorization headers, the package, then GET with workflow_id eq 1 and order created_at. Four dials every time. Right, the SDK: a cushion labelled create_client(URL, KEY), then supabase.table diagnoses insert, and select, eq workflow_id 1, order created_at. The SDK hides the address and the headers.\n\nLecture 5's cushion again. The SDK makes the same two calls and gives back the same rows. Ask the room what disappeared: the address and the headers, which the client object now carries for you. `sdk.py` is the cushion. `rest.py` takes it off: insert, select, update and delete with plain `requests` to `<project>/rest/v1/diagnoses`, filters like `workflow_id=eq.1` in the query string. Start with the SDK if time is short, and read the raw version once so you know what is under an error.\n\nPart 5 · Ship it\n\n15 · [Step 08](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/08-supabase-api)"},{"h":"The server remembers: append becomes insert, the question becomes a route","a":"the-server-remembers-append-becomes-insert-the-question-becomes-a-route","text":"> Sketch: Two routes enter your server on Vercel: POST slash api slash diagnose, and GET slash api slash workflows slash 1 slash diagnoses. The server calls GroqCloud to think and Supabase to insert a row and to select where and order by. Below: append a line becomes insert a row, the Lecture 6 question becomes a route. workflow_id 1 is hardcoded on purpose; scene 19 opens on that lie.\n\nStep 05's four file lines become one insert. `diagnose` and the FastAPI route do not change: swap the storage without touching the business logic, and tell your LLM that up front or it will tangle them. The select from the bottom of the schema file becomes a second route, and its address names the thing, exactly as Lecture 6 scene 21 promised. Locally the keys come from `.env`, copied from `.env.example`. On Vercel, add `SUPABASE_URL` and `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY` under Environment Variables with the exact same names, and deploy. Gradio from step 04 keeps working without a change. Every diagnosis is filed under workflow 1 for now, and scene 19 comes back to that lie.\n\n16 · [Step 08, the page](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db/tree/main/08-supabase-api/public)"},{"h":"Rip off Gradio: a text box, a button, one fetch","a":"rip-off-gradio-a-text-box-a-button-one-fetch","text":"> Sketch: Left, a Gradio window: a text box and a Diagnose button. All it was hiding: a text box, a button, one POST. An arrow, becomes. Right, public slash index.html: a textarea, a button, and fetch slash api slash diagnose with method POST and a body of workflow. fetch is requests.post in the browser. No key in the page. Same origin, same project, so CORS never comes up.\n\nGradio was hiding three things. Write them yourself: a text box, a button, and one fetch to your own address with the workflow as the package. Read the fetch against the dials: address, action, package, and no key. The page sits in `08-supabase-api/public/`, in the same Vercel project as the API, and lists the saved plans with a second fetch. Same project means one origin, so CORS never comes up. The last debt, a UI that only ran on your laptop, is paid.\n\n17 · The HLD"},{"h":"Four machines, four jobs","a":"four-machines-four-jobs","text":"> Sketch: Four dashed zones. Aarav's machine: his laptop runs plain HTML with one fetch and no key. Vercel: your function with two environment variables, GROQ_API_KEY and SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY; it exists only while it answers. GroqCloud: a GPU, it thinks. Supabase: a server holding the tables, it remembers, at your project on supabase.co. Brackets: UI week 1, API week 2, database weeks 3 and 4.\n\nThe whiteboard from Lecture 6, redrawn with today's machines. Four machines now, and the design gets a little busier every week, but every box has a reason you can name. Aarav's browser runs plain HTML. Your Vercel function exists only while it answers, and it is the only machine holding keys. Groq thinks. Supabase remembers. Every piece lives where it does for a reason: size, survival, privacy.\n\n18 · Proof"},{"h":"Five checks, in debt order","a":"five-checks-in-debt-order","text":"> Sketch: Five rows. 1, type a workflow on the live page, then GET the list: one row. 2, refresh: still one row, the Lecture 6 debt is paid. 3, change a comment and deploy again: still one row, the file problem is fixed. 4, open Network and send: only your URL, no Groq key and no Supabase key, the Lecture 5 debt is paid. 5, swap URLs with a partner: they get a plan, refresh, search for a key, and post verified.\n\nDone equals: a classmate opens your URL, gets a plan, refreshes, and the plan is still there. They search the Network tab and find no key. Each check closes one debt we opened on the way here, in the order we opened them.\n\n19 · Enforcement"},{"h":"Whose row is this?","a":"whose-row-is-this","text":"> Sketch: Mira's laptop sends GET slash api slash workflows slash 1 slash diagnoses to your server. The server reads from Supabase and returns Aarav's plans, every one, with no password asked. Whose row is this? Row Level Security: you read only the rows where auth.uid() is the owner.\n\nMira joins and reads Aarav's plans without a password. So would a bug that gave both of them id 1, or an attacker with one `select *` who wants to sell your data. Types are policed already. Who reads which row is not. Pydantic was policy plus enforcement for the API. Row Level Security is the same for rows: a policy, written in SQL, that returns only the rows whose owner matches `auth.uid()`, the id Supabase gives each signed-in user.\n\nIt needs login. In Supabase's Authentication tab, start with the email provider. Google, Apple and the rest carry third-party costs. RLS also blocks injected queries, because they arrive without a matching UID. It is the advanced step: store first, then add RLS for signed-in users. The [Supabase RLS guide](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security) has the policies.\n\n20 · Your turn"},{"h":"Five steps, then a classmate says \"verified\"","a":"five-steps-then-a-classmate-says-verified","text":"> Sketch: Six cards. Split, from Lecture 7: a route on Vercel, Gradio local, no key. Type your model: every table, references on every _id. Two verbs: one insert route, one select with where and order by. Strip Gradio: public slash index.html with one fetch, in the same project. Get verified: a classmate posts verified with your URL. Rules: httpie before the SDK, keys out of HTML and screenshots.\n\nYour own app, through the same eight steps of the [repository](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db). Type the model you drew in the Modelling Lab, with a pointer on every many side. Ask an LLM to explain errors, not to write your `create table`. Done is not a screenshot. Done is a classmate's \"verified\" next to your URL.\n\nA database is a machine at an address. SQL is its package.Before this: [Lecture 7 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes), [Lecture 6 on a whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/whiteboard) and its [notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes)."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-09-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: LLMs from First Principles","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: LLMs from First Principles\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes.html\nlecture: 9\nabout: Post-read notes · Lecture 9\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3036","sections":[{"h":"Verifiable means automatable","a":"verifiable-means-automatable","text":"From a hallucinated track list to the question every job now has to answer. The model stayed closed tonight; the lecture was about where the line between rules and prediction sits."},{"h":"01 Six weeks on one whiteboard","a":"01-six-weeks-on-one-whiteboard","text":"> Sketch: The Module 2 roadmap. What to build, week 0: North Star and Observation. Then how to build, weeks 1 to 8. AI system design: input, then three boxes, deterministic in weeks 1 to 4, probabilistic in weeks 4 to 8, human in the loop in weeks 8 to 12, then output. Under each, how it is verified: by a rule, same input same output; by judgement you can write down and hand to a stranger; not verifiable yet and expensive to get wrong.\n>\n> Caption: The module: what to build, then how. Each box is defined by how its output is verified.\n\n- **UI** is the first interface: the screen a person sees and touches. It runs on the **client**, the browser or device in their hands.\n- **Server** holds the business logic. For us, the diagnoser that turns a workflow into an automation plan for Aarav.\n- **API** is the second interface: address, action, package, key. **Data** lives in files, then in a database.\n- **All of that is orange:** deterministic, same input, same output.\n- **One box is purple:** the LLM, GPT OSS on GroqCloud, called through an API like any other service. ChatGPT has the same ratio: a POST with the prompt, a status code back, and Postgres underneath at over a billion users.\n- **Drawing this for a new problem is the HLD round.** A student interviewing at Anthropic was asked to design a web research agent: inputs, outputs, client, server, data, and where the LLM is actually needed. No code.\n\n> Sketch: An orange dashed zone labelled deterministic holds client, server and data with SQL, joined by API arrows. The client runs the UI, the first interface. A purple dashed zone labelled probabilistic holds the LLM, joined to the server by an API arrow. Note: the orange system makes the model usable.\n>\n> Caption: Six weeks on one board, and one purple box.\n\n> The model is the useful part. The orange system around it is what makes it usable."},{"h":"02 Why the second mountain is steeper","a":"02-why-the-second-mountain-is-steeper","text":"- **A UI breaks where you can see it.** A model answers differently tomorrow, after an update, or for another user, and even its builders cannot fully say why.\n- **Shifting answers are normal,** not a sign you broke something.\n- **The most common mistake:** reaching for the LLM first, even for jobs it is bad at, like storing information.\n\n> Start left of the line. Move a step into the purple box only when you cannot verify its output with rules. Given two equal solutions, the rule-based one wins: reproducible, cheaper, predictable at scale."},{"h":"03 Input and output of the bare model","a":"03-input-and-output-of-the-bare-model","text":"> Sketch: Input: text. An arrow into a dashed process box holding the LLM, GPT OSS 120B. An arrow out to output: text. Below: checked live in the Groq playground.\n>\n> Caption: Zoom into the purple box.\n\n- **Same framing as the UI and the API:** input, process, output.\n- **The chat said tokens, vectors, embeddings, prompts.** Each one got the same request: define it in one line a peer can follow. A term you cannot unpack hides the mechanism. Embeddings are not what you hand the model.\n- **The answer we could check live:** open GPT OSS 120B in the Groq playground, type text, get text back. **Text in, text out.** Some models take images and audio; for our reference model, this is the verified truth, and we build from verified truth."},{"h":"04 The query that broke it","a":"04-the-query-that-broke-it","text":"> Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\n\n- **The shape was right.** A decision framework, assessment questions, a mapping from answers to tracks, a checklist. Close to what a good mentor would give.\n- **The data was wrong.** Front-end, back-end, full-stack, data and ML, DevOps, product, security. None of them exist.\n- **The real choice has two axes.** A career track (entrepreneurship or career acceleration, set by your North Star) and an execution path (code or no-code, set by your background).\n- **We fixed the constraint:** one answer, no follow-up questions. Under that, an assessment framework is the right template.\n- **Right template, wrong data: hallucination.** It is why this model, as is, cannot be the career coach on the 100x website.\n\n> Sketch: Input: which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort? The output is a long page: a self-assessment framework, ticked, and a list of tracks. The tracks it recommended, all struck through: front-end, back-end, full-stack, data and ML, DevOps, product, security. Missing data caused the hallucination. The real choice has two axes: career track, entrepreneurship or career acceleration, and execution path, code or no-code.\n>\n> Caption: Right template, wrong data."},{"h":"05 Can the orange box fix it?","a":"05-can-the-orange-box-fix-it","text":"Satyaki's proposal, tested step by step against the line:\n\n> Sketch: Satyaki's proposal. An orange zone with client, server and data, and a purple zone with the LLM. Steps: 1, collect info about the cohort with a scraper, orange. 2, store it as a JSON or Markdown file, orange. 3, a system prompt telling the model to read the file before answering, purple, only an LLM can read it. 4, return the output, both. One purple step makes the system probabilistic.\n>\n> Caption: Steps 1 and 2 are orange. Step 3 only works with an LLM, and step 4 inherits it.\n\n- **The design was not wrong. The procedure is the point.** Write the solution as steps, place each step on one side of the line. If any step only works with an LLM, the system is probabilistic whether you meant it or not.\n- **Kandarp, in chat:** if you cannot write the verifier, you cannot build the system.\n- **Sampreet:** if you do not know what the right answer looks like, find a domain expert before you build."},{"h":"06 The deterministic answer already existed","a":"06-the-deterministic-answer-already-existed","text":"> Sketch: Input: which track should I choose? An orange zone with client, server and data. The server fetches from the data store, and the output is a template: the assessment, track data, and a decision-making framework. One API call, one template, the right answer every time.\n>\n> Caption: One API call, one template.\n\n- **Define the template:** how the assessment works, the tracks that actually exist, and a decision framework (this North Star and this background, choose this).\n- **Fetch the track data from the database** that powers the curriculum, not the website. The website updates slowly; the database is current. One API call, one template, the right answer every time.\n- **Cohort 1 did exactly this.** No team, no credits: each recurring question got its own decision framework as a plain HTML page. Test it with five real users, as Assignment 1 taught. If they can decide from it, it works.\n- **Steps are a process; anyone can copy them.** Knowing which side of the line a problem falls on before you design is intuition. Learnable, not teachable. Run it on 30 problem statements over 30 days and it becomes subconscious."},{"h":"07 Where rules run out","a":"07-where-rules-run-out","text":"> Sketch: Input: when will I get a job? Answer requires human judgment, drawn as a person. It depends on who you are, what you can actually do, the role you want, and the job market. No database holds it, yet a mentor who has watched many students can estimate it.\n>\n> Caption: Where rules run out.\n\n- **Same student, now on the code track.** The question came from a real test of an AI assistant in Cohort 6.\n- **No database holds the answer, no template produces it.** It depends on who you are, what you can actually do, the role you want, and the market. Some rule will always be missing.\n- **A 100x mentor can still estimate it,** because they have watched many students make the journey and formed a judgment.\n- **Verification by rule:** 1 + 2 is always 3. You can use an LLM; you do not need one.\n- **Verification by judgment:** whether a customer deserves a refund (from Lecture 1). Grey areas, settled by patterns an experienced support executive learned from past cases. When the check needs judgment, a probabilistic system belongs in the design.\n\n> Sketch: Two panels. Verification by rule: an app that adds two numbers. 1 plus 2 is 3, 2 plus 2 is 4, 3 plus 2 is 5. An LLM is optional. Verification by judgment: should this customer get a refund? Grey areas, settled by an experienced support executive's past cases. A golden dataset of ideal input and output pairs. A probabilistic system belongs here.\n>\n> Caption: Verification by rule, verification by judgment."},{"h":"08 Can a machine learn judgment?","a":"08-can-a-machine-learn-judgment","text":"Humans form judgment from experience. For a machine, the equivalent is data. The room's answers:\n\n- **Past data** (Anupama and many others). Necessary. Every FAQ ever answered at 100x is not enough on its own.\n- **Data alone breaks down** (Varun). Context bloats and models hallucinate more as it grows. Maybe LLMs guide judgment rather than make it.\n- **Decision theory** (Prateek). Fuzzy multi-criteria decision-making: gather human judgment through surveys, pairwise comparisons and linguistic scales, then turn it into rules. Works for one category of problem, not all.\n- **A human in the loop** (Satyaki). A person checks the machine's judgment against theirs and corrects it over time.\n- **Reward and punishment** (Samarth). The right instinct, but you cannot deny a machine a salary. What a reward looks like to a model is open for the coming weeks.\n\n> It depends, on two things in a fixed order. Swasti and Mustafa got there first: verification first, data second.\n\n- **Mountains of data, no way to tell right from wrong:** the model cannot learn.\n- **A perfect verifier, no examples:** it cannot learn either. A person who knows the rules still needs worked examples to build intuition.\n\n> Sketch: Can a machine learn judgment? It depends on two things in order. First, how verifiable is the task? Second, how much data do we have? If yes, what do we need: a verifier and data. Data without a verifier teaches nothing; a verifier without data has nothing to teach.\n>\n> Caption: Verifier first, data second."},{"h":"09 The Verifier's Rule, revisited","a":"09-the-verifiers-rule-revisited","text":"- **From the first lecture:** [Jason Wei on the asymmetry of verification](https://www.jasonwei.net/blog/asymmetry-of-verification-and-verifiers-law). It is now the guiding rule for the probabilistic half.\n- **In one line:** the ease of training AI on a task is proportional to how verifiable the task is. Every task that is possible to solve and easy to verify will be solved by AI.\n- **The benchmarks show it.** Graduate STEM exams and competition maths have a correct answer for every question, and decades of past papers. Models ace them.\n- **So read benchmarks with care.** Acing a verifiable test with enough examples shows the rule working, not general intelligence.\n\n> If you can verify a task and the data exists or can be collected, a machine can learn it."},{"h":"10 Aarav's Monday, task by task","a":"10-aaravs-monday-task-by-task","text":"- **Monday:** email (urgent tickets, feedback, holidays), then Jira, then Slack and a handful of other apps.\n- **From Jira, the top item:** a presentation for upper management on what the company can do next quarter to cut spend and improve the P&L.\n- **An atomic task** is specific enough that he can be held accountable for it. \"Help my company grow\" is not one.\n\n> Sketch: Aarav opens email, Jira and Slack on Monday. His to-do: 1, collect last quarter's data; 2, research pricing trends; 3, generate the presentation. Can it be verified? Yes: total sales minus Q1 and Q2 equals Q3, a rule. Yes: North Star cost saved, seen between Q3 and Q4, judgment. Yes: against the data and the research. Below, the exercise grid: tasks, verifier, data.\n>\n> Caption: Aarav's Monday, and the exercise grid.\n\n| Aarav's task | How it is verified | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Collect last quarter's data from Databricks and other tools | Rule. Total sales minus Q1 and Q2 must equal the Q3 figure collected. The output matches the expected schema. | Verifiable by rule |\n| Research current pricing trends | Judgment. No rule says what to research or when to stop. But his manager already verifies it at appraisal, and the outcome it serves (cost saved) shows up in the P&L between Q3 and Q4. | Verifiable by judgment and outcome |\n| Generate the presentation | Checked against the data collected and the research conclusions. | Verifiable |\n\n- **All three are verifiable,** and they are most of what Aarav is paid for outside meetings.\n- **A task becomes verifiable the moment its outcome is clear.** The only time you cannot write a verifier is when you do not know what the task is for. That is why the North Star exercise opened the cohort."},{"h":"11 Verifiable today, automatable tomorrow","a":"11-verifiable-today-automatable-tomorrow","text":"- **Verifiable is not automated, yet.** Some tasks have the data, some not yet. When the data arrives, and labs are working on exactly that, they become automatable.\n- **Not a doomsday lecture.** It changes what upskilling means. Ten lectures on neural networks change nothing if your tasks are the ones a lab can verify and buy data for. The useful question is which part of your work is not easily verifiable, and how to spend more time there.\n- **Karpathy's jagged edge.** GPT-4 to GPT-5: the skills that jumped (maths, reading, writing, knowledge) are the verifiable ones.\n- **Anthropic's jobs as bundles of tasks, with a nurse.** Ordering ward supplies: automated (the list and invoice make it easy to verify). Monitoring patients, discharge instructions, shift plans: augmented. Helping a patient bathe: human. Automated is orange, augmented is purple.\n- **If more than 80% of your week sits in the automated band,** use this module to shift it.\n\n> Sketch: A bar: the verifiable part of a job is waiting only on data; the rest is yours. Below, a nurse's job as a bundle of tasks. Automated: order ward supplies. Augmented: monitor patients, discharge instructions, shift plans. Human: help a patient bathe.\n>\n> Caption: Verifiable work waits only on data, and a nurse's job as a bundle of tasks.\n\n> Before someone else automates your job, verify it yourself."},{"h":"12 Data is bigger than you think","a":"12-data-is-bigger-than-you-think","text":"- **A verifier is not automation next quarter.** How long a task has left depends on how much data it takes to make a model reliable at it.\n- **For scale:** [FineWeb](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb), a public web crawl, is 44 TB and 15 trillion tokens (words turned into numbers). Roughly the floor for testing even a small language model. GPT-class and Claude-class models use 10 to 100 times more.\n- **To experiment tonight,** use an open-source model, ideally locally, not a hosted proprietary one (the reason comes in a later session). The instructor's go-to: Qwen 3.5 on [Ollama](https://ollama.com). Needs 6 to 7 GB, runs on a 16 GB laptop, works from Claude Code, OpenCode, Hermes or any terminal tool."},{"h":"13 From the chat, and where this goes","a":"13-from-the-chat-and-where-this-goes","text":"- **\"Can parts of a research task be deterministic, like which accounts to search?\"** (Abhishek) Yes. Most real tasks are a mix. Start deterministic; move a step to purple only when rules cannot verify it.\n- **The next four weeks:** a task from your own job, its verification criteria, its data, then a model built or trained for it (the route depends on the task). Context, harness and behaviour along the way. The finish line is a model evaluated on your own job.\n- **Tomorrow:** connecting the model to tools, with tool calling. The weekend build series starts now too.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Deterministic system**: Rule-based. The same input always gives the same output. The orange box.\n- **Probabilistic system**: Prediction-based. The same input can give different outputs. The purple box.\n- **HLD round**: The whiteboard interview: design a system from a problem statement, no code.\n- **Hallucination**: A response with the right shape and the wrong data.\n- **System prompt**: An instruction only an LLM can act on. Its presence makes a step purple.\n- **Verification by rule**: The output can be checked by writing rules, like 1 + 2 = 3.\n- **Verification by judgment**: The check needs experience, like a refund decision or a pricing strategy.\n- **Verifier's Rule**: How easy a task is to train AI on is proportional to how verifiable it is.\n- **Atomic task**: A unit of work specific enough that you can be held accountable for it.\n- **Jagged edge**: AI capability rises unevenly, fastest where outputs are easiest to verify.\n- **Tokens**: Words turned into numbers. The unit models are trained and measured in."},{"h":"14 Before the practice set","a":"14-before-the-practice-set","text":"> Sketch: Six cards. Predict: your guess for the verifiable share, written first. Log the week: every atomic task, from your calendar. Done right when: what makes each task count as done, and whether it moves your North Star. Verifier: the rule, or the judge and the signal. Bucket and data: automate, augment or human, and where the examples live. Rules: your week, not an LLM's; it's subjective is not a verifier.\n>\n> Caption: Your turn.\n\nAll of it is in the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set). Sections 05, 08 and 10 above are the ones to reread first. Lecture 10 adds one step to the same sheet: [test one task](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04).\n\nKeep this\n\nAn LLM earns its place only where the output cannot be checked by a rule and needs judgment. Whether a machine can learn that judgment depends on two things, in this order: can you verify the output, and do you have the data. Run that test on every task in your own job and you will know which parts are about to be automated, and which stay yours."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-09-overview.md","title":"LLMs from First Principles","meta":"title: LLMs from First Principles\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/index.html\nlecture: 9\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 9 · Fri 30 Oct 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 180","sections":[{"h":"LLMs from first principles","a":"llms-from-first-principles","text":"Verifiable means automatable.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nDefault to rules. Use an LLM only where the output needs judgment, and a machine can learn that judgment only if you can verify it and have the data.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Six weeks on one whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s01)\n2. [The query that broke it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s04)\n3. [Can the orange box fix it?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s05)\n4. [Where rules run out](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s07)\n5. [The Verifier's Rule](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s09)\n6. [Aarav's Monday, task by task](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s10)\n7. [Verifiable today, automatable tomorrow](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s11)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"A sheet of every atomic task from one real week, each with a verifier, a bucket and its data. After Lecture 10, one task in it tested on the model, bare and with context, in the same sheet.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes)\n- [Practice set: verify your own job](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set)\n- [The delegation framework](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/delegation-framework/)\n- [Eight films: from sand to language models](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/)\n- Checklist on the map\n\nChoose by verifiability, not by model.Next: Lecture 10, tool calling and MCP, Sat 31 Oct."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-10-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 1","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 1\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes.html\nlecture: 10\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 10, part 1 · 31 October 2026\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2596","sections":[{"h":"You already derived function calling","a":"you-already-derived-function-calling","text":"Why models hallucinate, why more data is not enough, and how to build the bridge that supplies context automatically.\n\nSections 01 to 06 cover hallucination. Sections 07 to 12 build the bridge and name it. It builds on [Lecture 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes) and the API lectures ([Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes#s03), [Lecture 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes)). If sections 09 to 11 feel shaky, reread those first."},{"h":"01 The test query","a":"01-the-test-query","text":"> Which track should I choose in 100x Engineers cohort?\n\n> Sketch: The question goes into the bare GPT-OSS 120B model. Out comes a list of tracks that do not exist, crossed out, scored 1 out of 10. Below, the missing data: two career tracks and two execution paths.\n>\n> Caption: The bare model had never seen the real tracks.\n\n- **The setup.** GPT-OSS 120B in the GroqCloud playground: a bare model, not a chat product, because that is what you build on.\n- **The truth is small.** Two career tracks (Entrepreneurship, Career Accelerator) and two execution paths (Code, No Code). The site changed after the model's training cutoff.\n- **Score: 1 out of 10.** A confident, well-formatted answer built on facts the model doesn't have is a **hallucination**. The diagnosis from [Lecture 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s04): the data was missing."},{"h":"02 More context helps, but doesn't cure it","a":"02-more-context-helps-but-doesnt-cure-it","text":"> Sketch: Three runs side by side. Lecture 9: the question alone, 1 out of 10. Round one: question plus tracks, 5 out of 10. Round two: question plus tracks plus curriculum.csv, 7 out of 10, still names Zapier.\n>\n> Caption: Same model, same question. Only the context changed, and the score stopped short of 10.\n\n| Context given | Score | What was still wrong |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| None | 1 | Tracks made up. |\n| A short block on the real tracks | 5 | JavaScript and Go for Code (Python is primary, Go isn't taught). Bubble, Webflow, Zapier for No Code (none are taught). |\n| The full C8 curriculum as CSV text | 7 | Still named Zapier, which appears nowhere in the CSV. |\n\nTwo side notes. The answer's framework changed shape between runs: a system that predicts doesn't retrieve. And a sheet pasted as a table reached the model as an image; it had to be plain text first.\n\n> Supplying data to a system that predicts reduces hallucination. It doesn't remove it."},{"h":"03 Why models hallucinate","a":"03-why-models-hallucinate","text":"> Sketch: A line from search engine, zero dreaming, to LLM, all dreaming. Below, a dashed box labelled LLM assistant holds client, APIs, data, tools and a small purple model box.\n>\n> Caption: Karpathy's framing: the model only dreams. The assistant around it is what lands in the wrong territory.\n\n- **The room's answers:** it isn't trained to say no; output is mandatory, so it fills gaps; it is a prediction engine, not a logic engine.\n- **Andrej Karpathy's framing.** Hallucination is all an LLM does; we only call it that when the dream lands somewhere factually wrong. The model has no hallucination problem; the **LLM assistant** does.\n- **The assistant is the whole system:** client, APIs, data stores and a model, as in the ChatGPT we took apart ([Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s10)).\n- **Partly an expectation problem.** If you need the correct record every time, build an orange system: client, server, database. When data is missing it returns 404 instead of guessing."},{"h":"04 Trained never to say no","a":"04-trained-never-to-say-no","text":"> Sketch: Hallucination equals missing context plus the trained behaviour of never saying no. Below, a bar of instruction-data records in which a sliver under 5 percent are the model declining.\n>\n> Caption: Fix only the context and the behavior still fills whatever gap remains. That is why the score stopped at 7.\n\n- **The training data.** OpenOrca, an open instruction dataset: a **system prompt**, a question and an ideal response, the same shape as your practice-set sheet, repeated across about 2.9 million records.\n- **The ratio.** In the roughly 30,000 records we scrolled through, fewer than 5% were the model saying it doesn't know. It is trained to predict, not to decline.\n- **Familiar question, unknown entity.** It has seen thousands of \"which option should I choose\" questions, never 100x Engineers. So it builds a plausible framework and fills gaps with whatever was common, which is how old no-code tools got in.\n- **Context** is the data that reaches the model inside its context window. The word carries through every lecture from here.\n\n> Hallucination = missing context + a trained habit of never saying no."},{"h":"05 Two fixes that don't work alone","a":"05-two-fixes-that-dont-work-alone","text":"- **A better prompt.** Role and goal wording raise the score, but with full context already given, it didn't remove the hallucination.\n- **Temperature near zero.** **Temperature** controls randomness. At 0 with the full-context prompt, it still hallucinated: a deterministic wrong answer is still wrong. Low temperature plus a system prompt is untested; run it and report back.\n\n> Any fix you hear is a hypothesis until your own test says otherwise. Write it as steps and run it."},{"h":"06 Diagnose with a golden pair","a":"06-diagnose-with-a-golden-pair","text":"> Sketch: Ideal input on top. Below, the ideal output, ticked, beside the model's output with untaught tools circled. The difference points to the reason: missing context.\n>\n> Caption: Input, ideal output, actual output. The difference names the cause.\n\n1. Write the **ideal input**: the breaking query.\n2. Write the **ideal output**: the 7-out-of-10 answer with the untaught tools removed. Together they are one **golden dataset** entry ([Lecture 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s09)), the same pairs as in [Lecture 9's practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set).\n3. Run the input, put the actual output beside the ideal, and name what caused the difference.\n\nHere the cause was in our own data: the curriculum says the No Code track uses UI builders but never names one. Adding the real set (Lovable, Google AI Studio, Antigravity) closes the gap. Missing context is the most common cause, which raises the next question."},{"h":"07 Automating the paste: a data source and a bridge","a":"07-automating-the-paste-a-data-source-and-a-bridge","text":"> Sketch: If the reason is missing context, supply it. A data source holding the curriculum docs on the left, the LLM on the right, and a dashed line with a question mark between them: the bridge.\n>\n> Caption: Pasting by hand doesn't scale. Automating it needs a data source and a bridge.\n\nYou can't ask every user to paste the curriculum first. Automating it needs two things most first designs skipped:\n\n- **A data source.** A trusted store that actually holds the context: here, the curriculum repository instructors write into. If it isn't in your data source, no model, tool, protocol or fine-tuning can supply it.\n- **A bridge.** Instructors keep adding material, so the bridge must stay current with no human in the loop.\n\nThe live challenge was to connect the curriculum CSV to GPT-OSS 120B and reach the manual 7 out of 10 with no pasting. Almost nobody finished, and the blocker was the same: not being able to investigate the errors. A rented car with a driver works until it breaks; then only someone who can drive can fix it.\n\n> You can outsource your thinking. You cannot outsource your understanding."},{"h":"08 What the model needs to receive","a":"08-what-the-model-needs-to-receive","text":"> Sketch: A data source, curriculum.csv, feeds the data card. Input, instruction and data cards stack into one prompt, which goes to the LLM. Prompt equals data plus instruction plus input.\n>\n> Caption: The 7 out of 10 was produced by hand, so we know exactly what has to arrive.\n\nThree parts in one prompt, the \"prompt plus input equals package\" from [Lecture 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s11):\n\n1. **The input**, as typed.\n2. **An instruction:** \"Here is the 100x curriculum. Whenever someone asks about 100x Engineers, use it.\"\n3. **The data:** CSV is already text, so it goes in as is.\n\nOn paper that is one chat completions call. But something has to sit between the model and the data source and fetch it. The room's instinct was an API."},{"h":"09 Why an API alone can't be the bridge","a":"09-why-an-api-alone-cant-be-the-bridge","text":"> Sketch: A file card, curriculum.csv, that speaks no language, and a purple LLM that cannot hold a contract. Between them the word API is crossed out: nothing has been agreed.\n>\n> Caption: An API needs two machines that agree. Neither side here can.\n\nAn API needs two machines that agree on a language and rules ([Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s05)). Neither side here can:\n\n- **The data source is a file.** It can't send a request, answer one, or return a status code.\n- **The model is probabilistic.** You can't count on it to hold up its side of a contract every time.\n- **No language or rules were agreed.** \"Connect it with an API\" was habit. Ask first what is being connected and how each side communicates."},{"h":"10 The common language: JSON","a":"10-the-common-language-json","text":"> Sketch: Interface equals two things. One, a common language: CSV, binary and English crossed out, JSON circled. Two, a set of rules: client, server, URL, status codes, and the four methods POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE.\n>\n> Caption: Lecture 2's definition, applied to the model and the data source.\n\n| Candidate | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| CSV, plain text, Markdown | File formats, not languages. A CSV can hold English, or even JSON. |\n| Binary | What machines run on, but you can't read or check it. If you can't verify, you can't build. |\n| English | Works for one file, then breaks: it ties you to sources in that language and still needs translating into machine instructions. |\n| JSON | Elected. |\n\nEvery orange system already speaks JSON, the web's common language, so only the model has to learn it, and GPT-OSS already reads and writes it. If the source moves to a Google Sheet, JSON still reaches it."},{"h":"11 The server is the bridge","a":"11-the-server-is-the-bridge","text":"> Sketch: The orange deterministic zone: client crossed out, database crossed out, the server kept. The server reads curriculum.csv in JSON and talks to the purple LLM over an API in JSON. Under the server: address, action, key, package.\n>\n> Caption: Strip the system you have built for six weeks down to the one box that can act.\n\n- **Reuse the rules.** Client and server, URLs, status codes, and the four methods (POST, GET, PUT or PATCH, DELETE). Every request needs an address, an action, a key and a package ([Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes#s03)).\n- **Who performs them?** Not the file, and not the model. The deterministic system.\n- **Only the server.** The database goes (the data already lives in the source), and so does the client (this is machine to machine). The server runs every operation, speaks JSON, reaches anything with an API, and already reaches the LLM. Nothing inside it predicts."},{"h":"12 Function calling: the reverse call","a":"12-function-calling-the-reverse-call","text":"> Sketch: Six numbered steps. 1, the person asks the server. 2, the server sends the question and the tool description to the LLM. 3, in coral, the LLM sends back a tool call asking the server to read curriculum.csv. 4, the server reads the data source. 5, the server sends the result to the LLM. 6, the LLM answers, through the server, to the person.\n>\n> Caption: Steps 2 and 5 run the way calls always have, server to model. Step 3 is the reversal: the model asks the server to act for it.\n\nOn 13 June 2023 OpenAI shipped this as **function calling**, now also called **tool calling**:\n\n1. **Request.** Your app sends the input plus a description of the available functions, in JSON schema.\n2. **Decision.** The model decides whether it needs a tool and, if so, emits a **tool call**: JSON naming the function and its arguments.\n3. **Execution.** Your backend, the **execution environment**, runs the real query or API call and collects the result.\n4. **Response.** The backend sends the result back, and the model turns it into an answer.\n\nThe only new thing is the direction. Until now your server called the model. Now the model asks the server to make a request for it, and the data lands in the context window. MCP and agent-to-agent communication build on this.\n\n> With tool calling, probabilistic asks deterministic to act. When an agent fails, check that handoff first."},{"h":"13 From the Q&A","a":"13-from-the-qa","text":"> Sketch: Two ways to route. Left, purple: an English instruction lets the LLM pick among M1, M2 code, M2 no code and M3, followed most of the time. Right, orange: the LLM only extracts the keyword tool calling; the server searches keyword lists and picks the file.\n>\n> Caption: Move the decision you can't afford to get wrong to the deterministic side.\n\n- **Given several tools, does every prompt call all of them?** It depends on the instruction. Routing in English (\"LLMs, use M2; agents, M3\") works most of the time, not every time, and routing is where you want no creativity. Better: keep a keyword list per source, ask the model only to extract the concepts, and let the server search the lists. This is **indexing**; the RAG lecture picks it up.\n- **So ChatGPT goes UI to server to LLM, and back?** Yes, as the network tab showed in [Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s10).\n- **So before 2023 no LLM could reliably tell the time or the weather?** Correct. In 2022 companies built their own servers to bridge that gap, which is what you are building now.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Hallucination**: A confident answer that lands in factually wrong territory. For the model, it is the same act as every right answer: prediction.\n- **LLM assistant**: The whole system around the model: client, APIs, data, tools.\n- **Context**: The data that reaches the model inside its context window.\n- **System prompt**: The instruction that tells the model how to behave.\n- **Golden dataset**: Ideal input and output pairs from your own experience, used to diagnose, evaluate and later fine-tune.\n- **Temperature**: Controls randomness in the output. Lowering it doesn't supply missing context.\n- **Data source**: The trusted store that must hold the context before any bridge can help.\n- **Interface**: A common language plus a set of rules that lets two systems communicate.\n- **Function calling (tool calling)**: The model asks a deterministic server to run a request for it and return the result.\n- **Tool call**: The model's JSON naming the function and its arguments.\n- **Execution environment**: Your backend: runs the tool call and returns the result."},{"h":"14 Practice","a":"14-practice","text":"Lecture 10 adds [one step to Practice set 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04), and opens Practice set 2. Both use the same sheet:\n\n1. **Pick one task** you bucketed Automate or Augment, and write its golden pair before you run anything.\n2. **Run it bare, then with context,** in the [GroqCloud playground](https://console.groq.com/playground). Score both out of 10 and write what was missing beside the row.\n3. **[Practice set 2: write the eval](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/) for that task,** on your own, about 45 minutes. Paste it into a new tab of the sheet.\n\nSubmit it with the rest of the sheet. Next week we build the reverse call live.\n\nKeep this\n\nA model hallucinates when missing context meets a trained habit of never saying no. A bridge to a trusted data source fixes the context half. That bridge is a deterministic server that speaks JSON and makes the request for the model: function calling.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-10-overview.md","title":"Tool Calling and MCP","meta":"title: Tool Calling and MCP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/index.html\nlecture: 10\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 10 · Sat 31 Oct 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 242","sections":[{"h":"Tool calling and MCP","a":"tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"The model asks for an action. It never takes one.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nMore data shrinks hallucination but does not cure it. The bridge to a trusted data source is a deterministic server that speaks JSON and makes requests for the model: function calling.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [More context helps, but doesn't cure it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s02)\n2. [Trained never to say no](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s04)\n3. [Diagnose with a golden pair](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06)\n4. [Automating the paste: a data source and a bridge](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s07)\n5. [Why an API alone can't be the bridge](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s09)\n6. [The common language: JSON](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s10)\n7. [The server is the bridge](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s11)\n8. [Function calling: the reverse call](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s12)\n9. [From the Q&A: routing by search](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s13)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"One task from your Lecture 9 sheet run bare and then with context, both scored against its golden pair, with what was missing written beside it. Then [Practice set 2: write the eval](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/) for that task, after the [3 Nov office hour](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour).\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n- [Practice set: one more step on Lecture 9's sheet](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04)\n- [Office hour, 3 Nov: your sheet is the data](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour)\n- [Practice set 2: write the eval](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/)\n- [Lecture 2: an interface is a common language plus rules](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s05)\n- Checklist on the map\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next week: we build the reverse call live, for both tracks."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-11-bench-notes.md","title":"Tool Bench Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2","meta":"title: Tool Bench Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes.html\nlecture: 11\nabout: Tool Bench notes · Lecture 11 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1915","sections":[{"h":"Make the reverse call yourself","a":"make-the-reverse-call-yourself","text":"One tool call by hand, a tool card that passes its tests, a server that runs the loop, and the card moved next to the data: MCP.\n\nIn [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s12) you derived function calling on paper, and the [Lecture 11 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes) follow it through one shop's sheet: the LLM asks, the server acts. Here you run it yourself on the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench), one section per step. Both tracks do the same steps; the code track also gets the Python under steps 4 and 5."},{"h":"01 The bench","a":"01-the-bench","text":"> Sketch: Three boxes in a row: the Sheet that holds the data on the left, the orange Server that follows rules in the middle, and the purple LLM that predicts on the right. Arrows join Sheet to Server and Server to LLM. Below the Server, You, joined to the Server by an arrow.\n>\n> Caption: You only talk to the server. The server talks to the sheet on its left and the LLM on its right.\n\n- **Nothing on the bench is simulated.** The LLM is GPT-OSS 120B on Groq, the sheet is the real curriculum sheet, and the server is a real route.\n- **The wire** lists every message that crossed, with the exact JSON. Things that are not messages, like the server picking a row, show as dashed notes.\n- **Nothing hidden.** No system prompt, no changed settings: each request is exactly what the wire shows, so you can repeat any run in the [GroqCloud playground](https://console.groq.com/playground).\n- **Your key.** Every model call runs on your own Groq key from [Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder). It stays in your tab and is not stored. The whole bench costs about 35 calls, well inside the free tier."},{"h":"02 Ask: nothing reaches the sheet","a":"02-ask-nothing-reaches-the-sheet","text":"> When is Lecture 12 of 100xEngineers Cohort 8, and what is it about?\n\n- **The LLM was trained before this course existed.** It either says it doesn't have the schedule or makes something up.\n- **Check it yourself.** Open the curriculum sheet and find the row that starts **12.**\n- **Count the wire.** Four messages: you to the server, the server to the LLM, and back. None went to the sheet.\n\n> The data sits one box away, and nothing connects the LLM to it."},{"h":"03 Carry one tool call by hand","a":"03-carry-one-tool-call-by-hand","text":"> Sketch: Six numbered arrows. 1, the server sends the question and the card to the LLM on the right. 2, in coral, the LLM sends back a tool call, get_lecture 12, and stops. 3, you press run and the server fetches from the sheet on the left. 4, the sheet returns row 12. 5, the server hands the row to the LLM. 6, the LLM sends back the sentence.\n>\n> Caption: Step 2 is the only thing the LLM does that is new: it writes a request instead of an answer, and stops.\n\nThe same question, plus a ready-made **tool card**: a description of one tool the server can run. The LLM reads the card and, instead of answering, sends back a request:\n\n```\n\"tool_calls\": [{\n  \"id\": \"call_…\",\n  \"type\": \"function\",\n  \"function\": { \"name\": \"get_lecture\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"number\\\": 12}\" }\n}]\n```\n\n- **It waits at the server.** Nothing happens until someone runs it. Today that someone is you: press Run, and the server fetches row 12.\n- **Hand the row back.** The server sends the LLM the whole conversation again, plus the row in a message with `\"role\": \"tool\"`. Now the LLM has the data, and it writes the sentence.\n- **It predicts, so it can wobble.** Sometimes it answers without asking, or writes a call Groq can't read. Ask again.\n\n> The LLM touched the sheet zero times. It wrote a request and a sentence. The server did everything in between."},{"h":"04 Write the tool card against tests","a":"04-write-the-tool-card-against-tests","text":"> Sketch: The tool card on the left with three fields: Name, get_lecture. Use it when, returns one lecture, by number. It needs, the lecture number. Arrows map each field to the JSON the server sends: name, description, parameters.\n>\n> Caption: Three plain fields on the bench. The server sends them to the LLM as this JSON. The LLM reads nothing else about the tool.\n\nStep 3 hands you an empty card named `tool_1`. Before writing a word, mark what each question should do. That is the golden pair from [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06), and the check you write before the run, as in [Practice set 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/).\n\n| Question | Should |\n| --- | --- |\n| When is Lecture 12 of 100xEngineers Cohort 8, and what is it about? | Call the tool, with 12 |\n| Which tools do we use in the seventh class? | Call the tool, with 7 |\n| What is an API? | Answer itself |\n| Is session ten a combined one or separate? | Call the tool, with 10 |\n| In lecture 3 of Harvard CS50, which language is taught? | Answer itself |\n\nThen edit the card and run until all five pass. Same LLM, same questions; only the card changes. In our runs:\n\n| Card | Passed, out of 5 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Empty | 3 |\n| \"Gets a lecture by number\" | 4 or 5. It calls the tool for the CS50 question too. |\n| Says what it returns, when to use it, when not to, and what the number is | 5, every run |\n\n- **When stuck:** say what the tool returns, when to use it, and when not to (any course, or only ours?). Then say exactly what the input is. Change one thing and run again.\n- **A tool card is an interface written in English:** a common language plus rules, as in [Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes#s05). The tests are how you know it holds.\n- **Words have a limit.** Some routing no wording fixes reliably. Then the server has to check, as in [Lecture 10's routing by search](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s13)."},{"h":"05 Switch the server on","a":"05-switch-the-server-on","text":"> Sketch: You send one question to the server and get one answer back. Between, the server runs the loop on its own: question and card to the LLM, a tool call back, a fetch from the sheet, the row back, up to three rounds.\n>\n> Caption: Every button you pressed in step 2 is now a rule in the server.\n\n- **Flip By hand to On** and ask anything about a lecture. The server sends the card, runs whatever the LLM asks for, hands back the result, and repeats until the LLM answers, up to three rounds. The server decides when the loop stops.\n- **Try Lecture 40.** The server returns an error, there is no lecture 40, and the LLM passes that on instead of inventing one.\n- **Download the log.** The call, the arguments, the result and the answer. That log is the proof the tool was called with the right input.\n\nFor the code track, the bench prints this loop in Python with your own card in it. The core:\n\n```\nfor _ in range(3):                      # the server decides when the loop stops\n    msg = ask_groq(messages, [CARD])    # one chat completions call\n    if not msg.get(\"tool_calls\"):       # no request: this is the answer\n        print(msg[\"content\"]); break\n    call = msg[\"tool_calls\"][0]         # the LLM asked; the server acts\n    result = get_lecture(**json.loads(call[\"function\"][\"arguments\"]))\n    messages.append(msg)\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call[\"id\"],\n                     \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n```\n\n> Purple wrote the request and the sentence. Orange did everything in between, the same way every time."},{"h":"06 Move the card: MCP","a":"06-move-the-card-mcp","text":"> Sketch: Before: App 1 and App 2 each keep their own copy of the card and the fetch code, and each reaches the sheet. After: the card sits with the sheet's server, and App 1 and App 2 both connect to that one address.\n>\n> Caption: Put the card and the fetch code where the data is, once. Every app needs only the address.\n\nA second app wants your tool. Today the card and the fetch code live inside your server, so the second app would need a copy of both, and so would a third. Step 5 moves the card to the sheet's side. Your server no longer knows the tool; it has to ask.\n\n| Message | Means |\n| --- | --- |\n| `initialize` | Hello. Here is who I am. |\n| `notifications/initialized` | I'm ready. |\n| `tools/list` | What can you do? Your card comes back. |\n| `tools/call` | Run this, whenever the LLM asks. |\n\nAsk the same question again: the loop is the same, but the fetch now goes to the sheet's server. Those four messages are the **Model Context Protocol**, **MCP**. Any app that speaks them can use any MCP server, whatever the tool.\n\n- **Try the address in a second app.** Copy it and open [the second app](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/second-app). It has never seen your card or your sheet. Paste the address and ask.\n- **Code track:** under step 5 the bench prints the same server in Python (FastMCP) and a second app in Python (a FastMCP client), built from your card.\n\n> Tool calling is the LLM asking. MCP is where the tool lives, so that any app can ask."},{"h":"07 Do it with your own sheet","a":"07-do-it-with-your-own-sheet","text":"The same steps, on your [Practice set 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set) sheet:\n\n1. **Share it as a viewer.** In the sheet: Share, General access, \"Anyone with the link\", Viewer.\n2. **Copy your own tab's link,** not Aarav's example tab. It ends in `#gid=` and a number. Paste it on the bench after step 5 and check the tab and first row it shows.\n3. **Write five questions:** three your sheet can answer and two the tool should not be used for. Mark them before you run anything.\n4. **Write the card** until all pass. On your sheet the tool searches your rows for the words the LLM sends, so \"It needs\" says what those words should be.\n5. **Switch the server on, move the card,** and copy your own MCP address into the second app.\n\nAnyone with your address can read what the tool returns, the same as anyone with your sheet link. Share it only where you would share the sheet.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Tool card**: The description of one tool: its name, when to use it, and what it needs. The LLM reads only this.\n- **Tool call**: The LLM's JSON naming the tool and its arguments. A request, not an action.\n- **Tool result**: What the server got back from running the call, handed to the LLM with `\"role\": \"tool\"`.\n- **The loop**: The server repeating model call, tool run, hand back, until the LLM answers or the server stops it.\n- **The wire**: On the bench: every message that crossed, with its exact JSON.\n- **MCP server**: A server that keeps tools next to the data and answers `tools/list` and `tools/call` for any app.\n- **MCP (Model Context Protocol)**: The shared messages any app uses to find and call tools on any MCP server.\n\nKeep this\n\nThe LLM writes a request and stops; the server runs it. The tool card is the only thing the LLM knows about the tool, so write it against tests marked before the run. Move the card next to the data and any app can use it with one address: that is MCP.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-11-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes.html\nlecture: 11\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 11 · 13 November 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2770","sections":[{"h":"The LLM asks. The server acts. The record proves it.","a":"the-llm-asks-the-server-acts-the-record-proves-it","text":"One shop's Google Sheet, followed from a pasted prompt to a tool call to MCP.\n\n[Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes) derived function calling. This lecture ran the same chain on one business, Priya's shop, and added four things: the tool schema, what to do when a tool fails, how to verify a tool call, and MCP. Tomorrow, in Lecture 12, you connect it for real."},{"h":"01 Where tools and MCP sit","a":"01-where-tools-and-mcp-sit","text":"> Sketch: Four stacked layers. From the bottom: Infrastructure, Agent internet, Protocol, Tooling. Protocol and Tooling are highlighted. Tooling is what the agent can do. Protocol is how those tools are reached, such as MCP and A2A.\n>\n> Caption: Today is the top two layers.\n\nAn agent is a system, not an LLM with a prompt. The LLM does the reasoning; memory, tools, orchestration, feedback and security sit around it.\n\n> Tools give an agent capabilities. A protocol defines how those capabilities are exposed.\n\n| Protocol | Who talks to whom |\n| --- | --- |\n| REST | An app to a service, through fixed endpoints with fixed parameters. |\n| GraphQL | An app to a service, and the app says exactly which data it wants. |\n| MCP | An AI app to tools and data. |\n| A2A | One agent to another agent, to hand over work. |"},{"h":"02 From Lecture 10: the LLM fills gaps","a":"02-from-lecture-10-the-llm-fills-gaps","text":"\"Which execution track should I choose in 100x?\" With no context the LLM scored 1 out of 10, with a short description 5, with the full curriculum 7. It still named Zapier, which is nowhere in the file. [Lecture 10 has the runs.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s02)\n\n- **It predicts the next token.** It does not look anything up.\n- **It fills a gap with the likely answer,** and does not tell you it did.\n- **Confidence is not proof.**\n- **A better prompt and a lower temperature help, and neither adds a fact.** At temperature 0 you get the same wrong answer every time.\n\nTo find what went wrong, write a [golden pair](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06): the ideal input and the ideal output, decided before you run the LLM. Then compare.\n\n> Reducing randomness does not create missing knowledge."},{"h":"03 Priya's shop: the paste problem","a":"03-priyas-shop-the-paste-problem","text":"> Sketch: Priya copies the sheet at 6:40 and pastes it into the LLM. A new order arrives in the sheet at 7:10 and stays there. The LLM answers from the 6:40 copy.\n>\n> Caption: The sheet is right. The way it reaches the LLM is the problem.\n\nPriya runs Northwind Goods, a home products shop. Everything is in one Google Sheet. Every morning she copies the orders into ChatGPT and asks what is pending and what to restock.\n\n- **Repetitive.** She pastes every single time.\n- **Error-prone.** She copies 40 rows. The new order is on row 41.\n- **Stale.** She copied at 6:40. An order came at 7:10.\n- **Doesn't scale.** Five employees need the same answers, all day.\n\nShe needs two things: a **trusted source**, which she has, and a **bridge** that fetches from it whenever a question needs it."},{"h":"04 The sheet and its one rule","a":"04-the-sheet-and-its-one-rule","text":"Three tabs: **Orders**, **Customers**, **Products**. Six orders: some delivered, three pending, one cancelled.\n\n**The rule:** revenue counts every order except cancelled ones. Pending counts. Order 1046, a kettle, is cancelled, so it is left out.\n\n| Product | Revenue |\n| --- | --- |\n| Kettle | 4,980 |\n| Mug set | 3,960 |\n| Linen | 3,870 |\n| Oak board | 1,890 |\n\nSo the golden pair is ready before any model runs: \"Which product generated the highest revenue?\" The kettle, 4,980. You can check it in the sheet by hand."},{"h":"05 What reaches the LLM","a":"05-what-reaches-the-llm","text":"> Sketch: Three boxes on the left: the question, the instruction, and the data. Arrows carry all three into one box on the right, the context window, which is all the LLM can see.\n>\n> Caption: Three things go in. Nothing else exists for the LLM.\n\n- **If it doesn't reach the LLM, the LLM can't use it.** An order the server never fetched does not exist.\n- **Send records, not a screenshot.** Text with named fields.\n- **Send only what the question needs.** A products question gets product rows, not the whole sheet. Tokens cost money.\n- **Right data is not a right answer.** The LLM can still misread or miscalculate. So you verify."},{"h":"06 The server is the bridge","a":"06-the-server-is-the-bridge","text":"> Sketch: Three boxes in a row: the Sheet that stores the data on the left, the orange Server that follows rules in the middle, and the purple LLM that predicts on the right. Arrows join Sheet to Server and Server to LLM. Below the Server, Priya, joined to the Server by an arrow.\n>\n> Caption: The sheet stores. The LLM predicts. Only the server can be trusted to fetch.\n\nGoogle Sheets already has an API. Why not connect the LLM straight to it? The sheet only stores: it knows no questions and no business rules. The LLM only predicts: you can't trust it to run every request correctly, or hand it your keys. The [server](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s11) stands between.\n\n> The API is the door. The server is the guard at the door.\n\nThey talk in JSON, because every field has a name and can be checked:\n\n```\n{ \"sheet\": \"Orders\", \"action\": \"read\", \"range\": \"A1:G100\" }\n```\n\nOne request through the server, with no model involved:\n\n1. **Receive** the JSON request.\n2. **Validate** it. Right sheet? Allowed action? Valid range? If not, stop with an error.\n3. **Select** the operation: read, add or update.\n4. **Call** the Sheets API with the server's own credentials.\n5. **Retrieve** the rows and give every value its field name.\n6. **Return** JSON. No summary, no explanation.\n\n| LLM | Server |\n| --- | --- |\n| Understands the question | Fetches the data |\n| Decides what it needs | Applies the business rules |\n| Writes the answer in plain English | Does the calculations, checks permissions |\n\n- **Same request, same steps,** on Tuesday and on Wednesday. The data may change; the process does not.\n- **Keys stay on the server.** Never in the browser, never given to the LLM.\n- **Cancelling is an update, not a delete.** Set the status to cancelled and keep the row.\n\n> Move whatever can't afford to be wrong to the deterministic side."},{"h":"07 Function calling: the LLM asks","a":"07-function-calling-the-llm-asks","text":"> Sketch: Six numbered arrows. 1, the server sends the question and the tool list to the LLM on the right. 2, in coral, the LLM sends back a tool call, get_orders with status pending. 3, the server reads the Orders tab of the sheet on the left. 4, the sheet returns three rows. 5, the server hands the rows to the LLM. 6, the LLM sends back the answer.\n>\n> Caption: Send, decide, request, execute, return, answer.\n\nUntil now the server decided what to fetch. With several tools, who picks the right one for each question? The LLM does. It reads the question and the list of tools, and instead of an answer it sends back a request:\n\n```\n{ \"name\": \"get_orders\", \"arguments\": { \"status\": \"pending\" } }\n```\n\n1. **Send.** The app sends the question and the list of tools.\n2. **Decide.** The LLM decides whether it needs a tool.\n3. **Request.** It writes the tool name and the arguments.\n4. **Execute.** The server validates the request and reads the sheet.\n5. **Return.** Rows, an empty list, or an error go back to the LLM.\n6. **Answer.** \"You have three pending orders: 1043, 1045 and 1047.\"\n\n- **The request holds no orders.** It only says what the LLM wants.\n- **The LLM never opens the sheet** and never sees the keys.\n- **One question can take several rounds,** or several tools. With five tools the LLM calls only the ones the question needs.\n\nThis is the [reverse call](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s12) from Lecture 10: the server used to call the LLM, and now the LLM asks the server."},{"h":"08 The tool schema","a":"08-the-tool-schema","text":"How does the LLM know a tool exists and how to ask for it? From the **tool schema**, the contract between the LLM and your server. It has five parts.\n\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"get_orders\",\n  \"description\": \"Reads orders from the Northwind Goods sheet. Can filter by status or customer.\",\n  \"parameters\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"status\":   { \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"pending\", \"delivered\", \"cancelled\"] },\n      \"customer\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n    },\n    \"required\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Part | Says |\n| --- | --- |\n| Name | What to call it. Short and meaningful. |\n| Description | What the tool does. The LLM picks tools by reading this. |\n| Parameters | The inputs it accepts: status, customer. |\n| Required | Which inputs must be given. Here none, so the LLM can ask for all orders. |\n| Types | What each input is. Status is text, not a number. |\n\nThe schema is like a form: it tells the LLM what to fill in. The server still checks what was filled in before it runs anything.\n\n> A good schema helps the LLM pick the right tool. A good server makes sure the tool is used correctly."},{"h":"09 When the tool fails","a":"09-when-the-tool-fails","text":"| What happened | What the user should hear |\n| --- | --- |\n| **400** The LLM sent a status that doesn't exist | The request was invalid, and why. The server does not quietly fix it. |\n| **401** The credentials are wrong or expired | \"I couldn't sign in to the sheet.\" Not \"there are no orders\". |\n| **404** The sheet was renamed or moved | \"I couldn't find the sheet.\" |\n| **500** Google Sheets is down | \"I couldn't retrieve the data.\" |\n| **Empty result** It worked, and nothing is pending | \"There are no pending orders.\" This is an answer, not an error. |\n\nAn empty result, an error and missing data are three different things. The LLM must not smooth any of them into a made-up order.\n\n> A real error is better than an invented answer."},{"h":"10 Verify the record, not the sentence","a":"10-verify-the-record-not-the-sentence","text":"> Sketch: On the left, what the LLM said: You have 4 pending orders. On the right, what the system did: tool get_orders, arguments status pending, result 3 rows, status success. They do not match.\n>\n> Caption: Only the record on the right can show you the sentence on the left is wrong.\n\nA model can write a confident answer when the tool was never called, or when the call failed. So don't check the sentence. Keep an **execution record** of every tool call and check that.\n\n```\n{\n  \"tool\": \"get_orders\",\n  \"arguments\": { \"status\": \"pending\" },\n  \"result\": [\"1043\", \"1045\", \"1047\"],\n  \"status\": \"success\"\n}\n```\n\n- **Write the expected record first:** which tool, which arguments, which result. Then run and compare.\n- **When an answer is wrong, walk the chain.** Right tool? Right API call? Right rows? Right final sentence?\n\n> Don't verify what the AI says. Verify what the system did."},{"h":"11 MCP: one standard for every tool","a":"11-mcp-one-standard-for-every-tool","text":"> Sketch: Before: two apps each build their own connection to Sheets, Gmail and Slack, six connections in all. After: both apps speak MCP, and MCP reaches Sheets, Gmail and Slack.\n>\n> Caption: Before, every app builds every connection. After, every app speaks one protocol.\n\nPriya now wants Gmail, Slack and Supabase too. Each has its own way to connect, its own formats and its own errors. A second AI app would rebuild all of them. That is the integration problem.\n\n**MCP, the Model Context Protocol,** is one standard way for an AI app to find out what a server can do and ask for it. Like USB-C: one port, many devices.\n\n| Term | Its job | In the building |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| API | How the server reaches the sheet | The door |\n| Function calling | How the LLM asks for an action | Asking for the door to be opened |\n| MCP | How any AI app finds and reaches tools | The standard hallway to every door |\n\n- **MCP does not replace APIs.** It is a layer over them. If you write your own MCP server for a database, you still define every action and every table it can touch.\n- **MCP does not replace function calling.** The LLM still asks. MCP carries the request.\n- **The value grows with other people's servers.** Connect to one someone already built and its tools are yours to use.\n- **None of the three makes the answer correct.** You still verify."},{"h":"12 Host, client, server","a":"12-host-client-server","text":"> Sketch: A chain from left to right. Priya talks to the host, the AI app, which holds the LLM and an MCP client. The client talks to the MCP server over MCP. The MCP server talks to the sheet through the Sheets API.\n>\n> Caption: The answer travels back along the same chain.\n\n| Role | What it is | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Host | The AI app Priya uses: Claude Desktop, Antigravity, or one you built | Holds the LLM and runs the conversation |\n| Client | A piece inside the host, one per server | Asks the server which tools it has, carries each request, brings back the result |\n| Server | The program next to the data | Offers the tools, validates, calls the Sheets API, returns the rows |\n\n\"Find all pending orders\", end to end:\n\n1. Priya asks the host. She knows nothing about APIs or MCP.\n2. The LLM reads the tools the host already discovered and writes `get_orders` with status pending. That is function calling.\n3. The client carries the request to the Google Sheets MCP server. That is MCP.\n4. The server validates it, calls the Sheets API, and reads six orders. Three are pending.\n5. The three rows travel back through the client to the host.\n6. The LLM writes the answer.\n\n> The MCP server is not the spreadsheet. The MCP client is not the LLM."},{"h":"13 Tomorrow: Lecture 12","a":"13-tomorrow-lecture-12","text":"You connect a Google Sheets MCP server in Antigravity. The order matters.\n\n1. **Connect** the Google Sheets MCP server.\n2. **Discover** the tools it offers. Look at the list; don't assume the names.\n3. **Wire** one tool into a working flow.\n4. **Write the verifier** before you run: expected input, expected output, expected execution record.\n5. **Run,** capture the log, and compare.\n\nThree questions to walk in with:\n\n| Question | Answer |\n| --- | --- |\n| An AI has five tools. Does it call all five? | No. It picks the ones the question needs. |\n| Does MCP replace APIs? | No. It standardises how an AI app reaches tools. The tools still use APIs underneath. |\n| Does connecting a tool guarantee a right answer? | No. That is why you verify. |\n\nWant to make a tool call yourself before then? The [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench) takes about fifteen minutes, with [its own notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes).\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Source of truth**: The place the real records live. Here, Priya's Google Sheet.\n- **Deterministic bridge**: The server between the LLM and the data. Same request, same steps, or an error.\n- **Context window**: Everything the LLM can see while answering: the question, the instruction and the data.\n- **Function calling (tool calling)**: The LLM writes a request for an action. The server runs it.\n- **Tool schema**: The description of one tool: name, description, parameters, required fields, types.\n- **Execution record**: The log of one tool call: the tool, the arguments, the result and whether it succeeded.\n- **Verifier**: The expected record, written before the run, that the real record is compared against.\n- **MCP (Model Context Protocol)**: One standard way for AI apps to find and reach tools on any server.\n- **Host, client, server**: The AI app; the piece inside it that speaks MCP; the program that offers the tools.\n- **A2A (agent to agent)**: A protocol for one agent handing work to another.\n\nKeep this\n\nThe LLM asks for an action. A deterministic server executes it. The verifier proves what happened.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-11-overview.md","title":"Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2","meta":"title: Tool Calling and MCP, Part 2\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/index.html\nlecture: 11\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 11 · Fri 13 Nov 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 211","sections":[{"h":"Tool calling and MCP, part 2","a":"tool-calling-and-mcp-part-2","text":"One shop's Google Sheet, followed from a pasted prompt to a tool call to MCP.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nThe model asks for an action. A deterministic server executes it. The verifier proves what happened.\n\n[Read the notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Where tools and MCP sit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s01)\n2. [Priya's shop: the paste problem](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s03)\n3. [The server is the bridge](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06)\n4. [Function calling: the model asks](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s07)\n5. [The tool schema](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s08)\n6. [When the tool fails](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s09)\n7. [Verify the record, not the sentence](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s10)\n8. [MCP: one standard for every tool](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s11)\n9. [Host, client, server](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s12)\n\n02"},{"h":"Try it yourself","a":"try-it-yourself","text":"Make one tool call by hand on the Tool Bench, then turn it into an MCP server. Bring your Groq key from [Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder), or make a new one at [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys).\n\n- [The Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench)\n- [Tool Bench notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes)\n- [The second app: paste an MCP address](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/second-app)\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Tomorrow's five steps, and three questions](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s13)\n- [Lecture 10: function calling, the reverse call](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s12)\n- [Lecture 10: diagnose with a golden pair](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06)\n- Lecture 11 on the map\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Tomorrow, Lecture 12: tool calling and MCP again, each track building its own."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-11-second-app.md","title":"Second App","meta":"title: Second App\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/second-app\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/second-app.html\nlecture: 11\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 71","sections":[{"h":"Paste the address of an MCP server.","a":"paste-the-address-of-an-mcp-server","text":"This app has never seen your card, your sheet or your code. It knows how to speak MCP, and nothing else."},{"h":"The wire this app’s server and the MCP server","a":"the-wire-this-apps-server-and-the-mcp-server","text":"This app sends your question to its server, which talks to the model and to the MCP server. Shown here: every message its server sent and got back.\n\nNothing has crossed yet."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building with tool calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building with tool calling and MCP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 12\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 12 · 14 November 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 51f573a\nwords: 3077","sections":[{"h":"Design the tools first. Then connect one.","a":"design-the-tools-first-then-connect-one","text":"We designed an AI employee's eight tools and its limits, then connected a Google Sheet in Antigravity and changed it by asking.\n\n[Lecture 11](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes) followed Priya's shop, Northwind Goods, from a pasted prompt to a tool call to MCP. Today we designed the tools before touching anything: what each one does, what it may be asked, and who says stop. Then we connected a real Google Sheet in Antigravity."},{"h":"01 What Antigravity does with your task","a":"01-what-antigravity-does-with-your-task","text":"What happens after you give Antigravity a task in plain English? It breaks the task into steps, then works through them in a cycle: plan, write, run, test, fix. So it does more than hand you an answer. It works on your project.\n\n> Sketch: You give Antigravity a task in plain English. Inside Antigravity a cycle runs: plan, write, run, test, fix. Three things sit under it: the LLM, which does the thinking, a browser, and MCP connections to outside services. Two things come out: artifacts, which are the plans and screenshots, and the workspace, which holds your project files.\n>\n> Caption: The LLM supplies the thinking. The browser and the MCP connections are how Antigravity reaches outside your project.\n\nThe LLM can be Gemini, Claude or GPT. MCP connections reach outside services such as Google Sheets, Slack and Gmail. The plans and screenshots Antigravity makes along the way are its **artifacts**. Your project files are the **workspace**."},{"h":"02 Hire an AI employee. What may it do?","a":"02-hire-an-ai-employee-what-may-it-do","text":"Priya is going on leave. She wants an AI employee to run Northwind Goods while she is away. It would need Google Sheets for orders, customers and products, Gmail, Slack for her team, a database, and payments.\n\n> \"If you are hiring this AI employee, would you give it access to everything?\"\n\nThe room wrote five things they would hand over, and starred one it must never do without permission. From the chat: replying to emails, staff attendance. The stars went to payments and personal data.\n\nThe instructor's five: read customer orders, check stock, find customer details, draft customer emails, prepare the daily sales report. Four only read, and the fifth is a draft. Paying money out is different, because someone could talk the LLM into it.\n\n> Giving the LLM tools makes it useful. Giving it unlimited access makes it dangerous.\n\nSo which tools, and with which limits?"},{"h":"03 One request, eight tools","a":"03-one-request-eight-tools","text":"Priya's request for the day:\n\n> \"Find my top three customers this month, find out what they purchased, and prepare a personalised follow-up email for each.\"\n\nWhich data does this need? Does one step need another's result? Should the email go out by itself, or should Priya see a draft first? You wrote the steps in order, and most of the room got them right.\n\nThen the toolbox. You would not give a new employee one giant button that does everything. Ours has eight tools:\n\n> Sketch: The toolbox of eight tools in three groups. Four reading tools: get orders, get customers, get products, get inventory. One calculating tool: calculate revenue, which leaves cancelled orders out. Three email tools: search emails, draft email and send email. Send email is marked as the one that cannot be taken back.\n>\n> Caption: Drafting and sending are two tools on purpose. You can delete a bad draft. You cannot unsend a bad email.\n\n`calculate_revenue` is there so the LLM never does the sums itself. Then five questions: which tool?\n\n| Priya asks | The tool |\n| --- | --- |\n| How many pending orders do we have? | `get_orders` with status pending. The app counts the rows. |\n| Which customers spent more than 10,000? | Three steps: `get_orders`, then `calculate_revenue`, then `get_customers`. |\n| Do we have enough kettles in stock? | `get_inventory`, but first ask Priya what \"enough\" means. The LLM must not guess. |\n| Draft a thank-you email. | `draft_email`. Nothing leaves the app; the LLM can write the draft itself. |\n| Send it. | `send_email`. A separate permission: it acts outside the app. |\n\nThat is four kinds of work: reading, calculating, drafting, acting. Keep them apart.\n\n> One tool, one job."},{"h":"04 The card is a contract","a":"04-the-card-is-a-contract","text":"What does a tool called `do_everything` do? Or `process_data`? Nobody can tell, and neither can the LLM. Compare `get_pending_orders`. A good name says the action, the thing it acts on, and what comes out.\n\nThe name is the first line of the card (the [tool schema](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s08) of Lecture 11). Today we read the whole card as a contract between the LLM and your app.\n\n> Sketch: The card for get orders, drawn as a contract with seven lines. Name: get orders. Description: what it does and when to use it. What to pass in: status and customer id. Allowed values: pending, delivered or cancelled. What comes back: order id, customer, product, amount, status. Errors: sheet missing, or a status that is not allowed. Permission: read only.\n>\n> Caption: A tool is more than a function name: the card says what goes in, what comes out, and what is not allowed.\n\nOur card had three more rules. Status takes only its three **allowed values**. Each input is a **required field**, so it must be present. `customer_id` may be empty (`null`), which means every customer's orders. And no extra fields. Two requests arrived. Which one passes?\n\n| Request | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| A: status is \"pending\", and nothing else | Rejected. `customer_id` is missing. It had to be there, even if empty. |\n| B: status is \"maybe pending\", a field called `customer` holding the number 12345, and one extra field | Rejected, for four reasons. The status is not allowed and the field name is wrong. The value is a number where text was expected. The extra field is not on the card. |\n\nIs a request that passes the card always right? No. A `customer_id` can have the right shape and belong to nobody, so the server still checks it against the real data.\n\n> The card checks the shape of a request. The server checks whether it is true."},{"h":"05 The description picks the tool","a":"05-the-description-picks-the-tool","text":"With eight tools, how does the LLM decide which one to use? It reads each card's description. Suppose the description is just \"Get orders\". Is that for stock? For products? It does not say.\n\nThe better one said three things. What the tool does: it retrieves orders from the Northwind store. When to use it: someone asks about an order's status, ID, customer or amount. When not to: a general product question. Write it as you would for a new employee.\n\nNow give the LLM ten tools and one question:\n\n> \"Which customers have placed orders but have not received their delivery?\"\n\nIs that `get_orders`, `get_customers` or `get_delivery_status`? The tools overlap, and \"not received\" is unclear: still being packed, or shipped? If no tool knows the delivery status, the LLM should say so and not guess.\n\n> More tools do not make the LLM smarter. A small, clear set does.\n\nYou then fixed a bad card: a tool named `lookup`, described as \"Look up things in the shop\", with one free-text input. You gave it a clear name, a description and exact inputs. Then a test. \"How many kettles are pending?\" should pick your tool. \"Draft a thank-you note to Mira\" should not. \"Should I tell Kavya about her apron?\" could mean an order, stock or an email, so the LLM should ask.\n\nMust the LLM always choose? If Priya is already on the orders page, the app knows she means orders. Sending, paying and deleting follow the app's rules too.\n\n> If a rule can make the decision, let the rule decide. If it needs understanding, let the LLM decide."},{"h":"06 Which calls wait, which run together","a":"06-which-calls-wait-which-run-together","text":"Back to Priya's request. Can one tool do it all? No. It takes four calls, and each one needs the result of the one before. Calls like this run **in sequence**.\n\n> Sketch: Top row, in sequence: get orders for this month, then get customers, then calculate revenue, then draft email. Each arrow carries the result into the next step. Bottom row, in parallel: one question splits into three calls that run together, total orders, stock and customer count, because none of them needs another's result.\n>\n> Caption: Ask of every call: does it need another call's result? If yes, it waits.\n\nIf step one fetches the wrong month, every step after it is wrong.\n\nThe opposite case: Priya asks for the total orders, the stock and the customer count. None needs another's result, so the three calls go out together, **in parallel**. That saves waiting.\n\nThen you sorted six operations.\n\n| Operation | When it can run |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fetch customer details | Together, if you already have the customer IDs. If not, it waits. |\n| Check order status | The same: together, if you have the order IDs. |\n| Retrieve product inventory | Together. Stock does not depend on the customer. |\n| Calculate order revenue | After the orders are fetched. |\n| Pay money back to a customer | Only after checking the order and the shop's rules, and with a person's approval. |\n| Generate the report | Last. It needs every other result. |\n\n> Find what depends on what before you run anything."},{"h":"07 Your app holds the brakes","a":"07-your-app-holds-the-brakes","text":"Priya asks how many orders are pending. The tool returns 1043, 1045 and 1047. The LLM should now answer. Suppose it calls the same tool again, with the same inputs, and then again. It is stuck, repeating one step and getting nowhere: an **infinite loop**. A call can also run twice, or be retried after it already worked.\n\n> \"Where should we put the brakes? Should we ask the AI to stop, or should our application control it?\"\n\nThe app. It limits the number of tool calls, the time and the retries. Say the same tool is called three times with the same inputs. The app stops the run and tells the LLM to use the results it already has. Now picture a payment repeating.\n\n> Sketch: The LLM writes a request and passes it to your app. Your app is a gate with four checks: is the request valid, is it allowed, is a limit reached, does it need approval. Only then does the request go on to the services, Sheets and Gmail. A request to send an email stops at the gate until Priya says yes.\n>\n> Caption: Every request passes through the deterministic side before it touches a service.\n\nSo the deterministic side of your app runs the whole exchange. It has five jobs: keep a log, check permissions, limit retries, collect approvals, and cap how long a run may go. The LLM never holds your keys.\n\n> The LLM decides what it wants to do. Your app decides whether it is allowed."},{"h":"08 Connect Google Sheets in Antigravity","a":"08-connect-google-sheets-in-antigravity","text":"Priya now wants the same AI employee in her desktop assistant and in one more app. Three apps, four services each: twelve connections to build and maintain. [Lecture 11](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s11) gave the fix: build each connection once, as an MCP server, and every app reaches it the same way. One for Google Sheets already exists, so you only connect it.\n\n> Sketch: You type a question into Antigravity. Inside Antigravity the LLM writes a request. The request crosses the wire to the Google Sheets MCP server. The server runs it against your Google Sheet and sends the rows back along the same path.\n>\n> Caption: The same chain as Lecture 11. The LLM asks, the server runs it, and the sheet is touched only by the server.\n\nWhat your hands do, in order:\n\n1. **Install the right Antigravity.** The download page offers two: an app, and the coding tool (labelled IDE). Take the coding tool. Setup takes at least ten minutes.\n2. **Open a new window** on a new folder. The instructor used Gemini Flash as the LLM.\n3. **Ask for the connection.** Antigravity calls a connection a \"plugin\" on its screens, so the prompt uses that word. Type `/plugin search google sheets` (or `install Google Sheets plugin`), then click Install on the card that appears.\n4. **Sign in with Google**, once. Type `Connect my Google account`, open the link it gives you, pick your account and click Allow. A message then confirms that Google Sheets is installed and configured.\n5. **Make your own copy** of the shared order sheet (the link was shared in the session). Do not work in the shared one.\n6. **Share your copy.** Give editor access to the email you are signed in with in Antigravity, or set the link so anyone with it can edit.\n7. **Paste the sheet link** into the Antigravity chat. It fetches the sheet through the server.\n8. **Check it is connected.** Type `/plugin list`, or ask: `List my recent Google Spreadsheets`. Your sheets come back.\n9. **See what the server offers.** Ask: `List all the actions that you can do with Google Sheets MCP.`\n10. **Ask the shop's questions.** \"Who are the top customers?\" \"What is the highest sold product?\" The second answer was kettles. The cancelled order was left out.\n\nThe one-page quickstart for these steps is on the LMS, under Week 6, MCP Quickstart Guide. It also lists prompts to try on any sheet: create a sheet, add a header and two rows, append a row, read a sheet from its link, make the header bold, delete a row.\n\nThen one step further. The instructor asked Antigravity for a page that edits the sheet (his words, roughly):\n\n> \"Create a simple HTML file that modifies the fields on the sheet, so I can add and edit an existing sales record through MCP.\"\n\nAntigravity built one page and opened it on his own computer, at an address that starts with localhost. He added a mug set order for 123 rupees, marked pending, and clicked Save Record. The row appeared in the Google Sheet, with the next order number, 1051, filled in for him. He changed another order's value to 12,900, and the sheet changed. The sheet had become the database of a small shop app.\n\n| Trouble in the room | The fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| The steps did not match the screen | The wrong download. Take the coding tool, not the app. |\n| An error when adding the connection | Uninstall the Google Sheets connection and install it again. Several people hit this, and the reinstall cleared it. |\n| Antigravity could not open the sheet | The Google sign-in was skipped, or the sheet was not shared with the signed-in email. |"},{"h":"09 What the server did for you","a":"09-what-the-server-did-for-you","text":"You typed English and a sheet changed. Who did what? The LLM inside Antigravity read the server's cards and wrote a request. Antigravity carried it over the wire. The server validated it, called the Sheets API with the access you allowed, and sent the rows back. The LLM never touched the sheet.\n\nA server can offer three kinds of things:\n\n- **Tools are actions.** Search for an order, update a status.\n- **Resources are information** the app can read: a return policy, a product catalogue.\n- **Prompts are reusable instructions,** such as a template for summarising a support conversation.\n\nA server can run in two places. A **local server** runs on your own machine. A **remote server** runs somewhere else and is reached over the web, with a sign-in. Today's Google Sheets server is remote.\n\nTo find more servers, start at the [official MCP site](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/) or [PulseMCP](https://www.pulsemcp.com/).\n\n**From the chat: do I need to build my own MCP server?**\n\nOnly if you want other AI apps to use your service without your interface. Not every service needs one.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**The LLM sends get_orders with status \"pending\" and nothing else. The card says both fields are required. What happens, and what should it have sent?**\n\nIt is rejected, because `customer_id` is missing. For every customer's orders, send `customer_id` with an empty value (`null`).\n\n**A friend says: connect every tool you can find, and the AI employee gets smarter. What is wrong with that?**\n\nMore tools means more overlap and more wrong picks. The LLM chooses by reading descriptions, so a small, clear set works better.\n\n**Priya asks for thank-you emails to her top three customers. Which calls wait, and who presses send?**\n\nAll four wait: orders, then customers, then revenue, then the drafts. The LLM only drafts. Sending is a separate tool, and the app holds it until Priya approves.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Artifacts**: What Antigravity makes to show its work: plans, screenshots, recordings.\n- **Workspace**: The folder that holds your project's files.\n- **Required field**: A field that must be present in a request, even when its value is empty.\n- **Allowed values**: The only values a field may take. For status: pending, delivered or cancelled.\n- **In sequence**: Calls made one after the other, because each needs the result of the one before.\n- **In parallel**: Calls made together, because none needs another's result.\n- **Infinite loop**: The LLM repeating the same call with the same inputs and getting nowhere.\n- **Local server**: An MCP server that runs on your own machine.\n- **Remote server**: An MCP server that runs elsewhere and is reached over the web.\n\nKeep this\n\nGive each tool one job, and a card that says exactly what may be asked. The LLM asks; your app decides whether it is allowed, in what order, and when to stop. Connecting an MCP server takes minutes. Deciding what it may do is the real work."},{"h":"10 Your turn","a":"10-your-turn","text":"Do section 08 tonight on your own copy of the sheet: connect, list the actions, ask two questions, then change one row by asking. Check each answer against the sheet.\n\nThen open the Tool Bench with your own Groq key. It shows every message that crosses the wire, which Antigravity keeps out of sight.\n\n[Open the Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-12-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building with Tool Calling and MCP","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building with Tool Calling and MCP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes.html\nlecture: 12\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 12 · 14 November 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1283","sections":[{"h":"One tool call. Then an MCP server.","a":"one-tool-call-then-an-mcp-server","text":"Two Python files, two steps.\n\nSince Lecture 9 you have asked a model one question: which track should I choose? On its own, the model makes up the answer. On the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench) you gave it a tool by clicking. Today you write that tool in Python, in two files from [the c8-tools repo](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools):\n\n| Step | File | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `tool_call.py` | The model asks for your function. Your code runs it. The model answers from the result. |\n| 2 | `mcp_server.py` | The same function as an MCP server, so any app can use it. |\n\nSetup: `pip install requests python-dotenv fastmcp`, then a file called `.env` with one line: `GROQ_API_KEY=your key`."},{"h":"01 Step 1: the function","a":"01-step-1-the-function","text":"The answer lives in the [curriculum sheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Kg6T-dJWaYx_JCPiRk3u0nBuAQcsCVRDPj1NFKP4WzM/), one tab per track. A plain Python function reads one tab and returns each lecture with its tools:\n\n```"},{"h":"1. A plain Python function. It reads one track's tab of the public curriculum sheet.","a":"1-a-plain-python-function-it-reads-one-tracks-tab-of-the-public-curriculum-sheet","text":"SHEET = \"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Kg6T-dJWaYx_JCPiRk3u0nBuAQcsCVRDPj1NFKP4WzM/gviz/tq?tqx=out:csv&gid=\"\nTABS = {\"code\": \"368290478\", \"no-code\": \"0\"}\n\ndef get_lectures(track):\n    rows = csv.reader(io.StringIO(requests.get(SHEET + TABS[track]).text))\n    return [row[1] + \": \" + row[3] for row in rows if row[1][:1].isdigit()]\n```\n\nNo model yet. Same track in, same list out, every time."},{"h":"02 Step 1: the card","a":"02-step-1-the-card","text":"The model can't see your function. It reads a card that describes it: a name, what it does, and what to pass in. The request to Groq is the one from [Lecture 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/) with one new field, `tools`.\n\n```"},{"h":"2. A card that tells the model this function exists.","a":"2-a-card-that-tells-the-model-this-function-exists","text":"card = {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_lectures\",\n    \"description\": \"Lists every lecture of one 100xEngineers Cohort 8 track, with the tools it uses.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                   \"properties\": {\"track\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"code\", \"no-code\"]}},\n                   \"required\": [\"track\"]},\n}}\n\ndef ask_groq(messages):\n    r = requests.post(\"https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions\",\n                      headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]},\n                      json={\"model\": \"openai/gpt-oss-120b\", \"messages\": messages, \"tools\": [card]})\n    r.raise_for_status()                                          # 429 means wait a minute and run again\n    return r.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"]\n```"},{"h":"03 Step 1: the tool call","a":"03-step-1-the-tool-call","text":"> Sketch: Six numbered arrows. 1, your code sends the question and the card to the model on the right. 2, in coral, the model sends back a tool call, get_lectures, and stops. 3, your code reads the sheet on the left. 4, the sheet returns the rows. 5, your code hands the result to the model. 6, the model sends back the answer.\n>\n> Caption: The same six arrows as the Tool Bench. This time your code is the server.\n\nSend the question with the card. The model does not answer: it replies with a tool call, the name of your function and what to pass in. Your code runs the function and sends the result back. While the model keeps asking, the loop keeps running. When it stops asking, that reply is the answer.\n\n```"},{"h":"3. Ask. The model does not answer: it asks for the function.","a":"3-ask-the-model-does-not-answer-it-asks-for-the-function","text":"messages = [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"For any question about the course, call get_lectures first. Answer in three sentences.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Which track should I choose in the 100xEngineers cohort?\"},\n]\nreply = ask_groq(messages)"},{"h":"4. While the model asks for the function, your code runs it and hands the result back.","a":"4-while-the-model-asks-for-the-function-your-code-runs-it-and-hands-the-result-back","text":"while reply.get(\"tool_calls\"):\n    messages.append(reply)\n    for call in reply[\"tool_calls\"]:\n        args = json.loads(call[\"function\"][\"arguments\"])\n        print(\"The model asked for: get_lectures\", args)\n        result = get_lectures(**args)\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    reply = ask_groq(messages)"},{"h":"5. No more requests: this is the answer, written from the sheet.","a":"5-no-more-requests-this-is-the-answer-written-from-the-sheet","text":"print(\"\\n\" + reply[\"content\"])\n```\n\n```\n$ python tool_call.py\nThe model asked for: get_lectures {'track': 'code'}\nThe model asked for: get_lectures {'track': 'no-code'}\n\nBoth tracks cover the same AI fundamentals and product-building milestones, but the code track teaches\nimplementation with Python, FastAPI, Gradio/Streamlit, Supabase and Groq, while the no-code track uses\nvisual tools like Google Stitch, AI Studio, Antigravity and Vercel for the same outcomes. …\n```\n\n- **The model never touched the sheet.** It wrote a request. Your code did the work.\n- **It asked twice,** once per track, on its own.\n- **Every tool it names is in the sheet,** because it copied them from the result.\n\n> That is a tool call: the model asks, your code acts."},{"h":"04 Step 2: the MCP server","a":"04-step-2-the-mcp-server","text":"> Sketch: Before: your app holds the function and reaches the sheet. A second app has no way in. After: the function sits in an MCP server next to the sheet, at one address, and both apps connect to it.\n>\n> Caption: Put the function at an address once. Every app needs only the address.\n\nYour function lives inside `tool_call.py`. Another app can't use it there. An MCP server puts it at an address any app can reach. This is the whole file:\n\n```\nimport csv, io\nimport requests\nfrom fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nSHEET = \"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Kg6T-dJWaYx_JCPiRk3u0nBuAQcsCVRDPj1NFKP4WzM/gviz/tq?tqx=out:csv&gid=\"\nTABS = {\"code\": \"368290478\", \"no-code\": \"0\"}\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"100x curriculum\")"},{"h":"The same function as step 1. The decorator writes the card for you,","a":"the-same-function-as-step-1-the-decorator-writes-the-card-for-you","text":""},{"h":"from the name, the docstring and the type hint.","a":"from-the-name-the-docstring-and-the-type-hint","text":"@mcp.tool\ndef get_lectures(track: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Lists every lecture of one 100xEngineers Cohort 8 track, with the tools it uses. track is 'code' or 'no-code'.\"\"\"\n    rows = csv.reader(io.StringIO(requests.get(SHEET + TABS[track]).text))\n    return [row[1] + \": \" + row[3] for row in rows if row[1][:1].isdigit()]"},{"h":"Any MCP client can now reach it at http://localhost:8000/mcp","a":"any-mcp-client-can-now-reach-it-at-httplocalhost8000mcp","text":"mcp.run(transport=\"http\", port=8000)\n```\n\n- **The function is the same** as step 1.\n- **The card is gone.** `@mcp.tool` writes it for you, from the function's name, its docstring and its type hint.\n- **The Groq code is gone too.** The server only runs the function. The app that connects brings the model.\n\nRun it, leave it running, and in a second terminal ask the server what it offers, then call the tool:\n\n```\n$ python mcp_server.py                   # terminal 1\n\n$ fastmcp list http://localhost:8000/mcp # terminal 2\nTools (1)\n\n  get_lectures(track: str) -> dict\n    Lists every lecture of one 100xEngineers Cohort 8 track, with the tools it uses. track is 'code' or 'no-code'.\n\n$ fastmcp call http://localhost:8000/mcp get_lectures track=no-code\n{\n  \"result\": [\n    \"1. Orientation and State of AI 2026: None\",\n    \"2. What is an interface; UI as the first interface: None\",\n    \"3. PRD to UI design to frontend app: Google Stitch, Google AI Studio\",\n    …\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Command | Asks the server |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fastmcp list` | What can you do? The card comes back. |\n| `fastmcp call` | Run this one, with these arguments. The result comes back. |\n\n> MCP changes where the function lives. It does not change the function."},{"h":"05 Your turn","a":"05-your-turn","text":"In [Practice set 1, step 04](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04) you tested one row and wrote what the model was missing. That missing thing is your first tool. [Practice set 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set) walks you through both steps for your own row.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Tool**: A function your code runs when the model asks for it.\n- **Card**: The description of a tool that the model reads: its name, what it does, and what to pass in.\n- **Tool call**: The model's reply naming a tool and what to pass in. A request, not an action.\n- **MCP server**: Tools at an address, so any app can list them and call them.\n- **FastMCP**: The Python library that turns a function into an MCP server.\n\nKeep this\n\nWrite a function. Describe it on a card. The model asks, your code runs it. To share it with any app, put it on an MCP server.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-12-overview.md","title":"Building with Tool Calling and MCP","meta":"title: Building with Tool Calling and MCP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/index.html\nlecture: 12\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 12 · Sat 14 Nov 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 274","sections":[{"h":"Building with tool calling and MCP","a":"building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"A tool call and an MCP server, one step at a time on the Tool Bench. Then you write the same thing in Python.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nThe model does not fetch anything. It writes a request, your code runs it and hands the result back. Move the card to the data's side and any app can use the tool.\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Ask](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench#1)\n2. [Carry it by hand](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench#2)\n3. [Write the card](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench#3)\n4. [Switch the server on](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench#4)\n5. [Move the card](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench#5)\n\n[Open the Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench)\n\n02"},{"h":"Practice set 3","a":"practice-set-3","text":"Write the code behind the bench. Due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026.\n\n| Part | File | You end with |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. The tool call | `tool_call.py` | An answer written from the sheet |\n| 2. The MCP server | `mcp_server.py` | An address any app can use |\n\n[Open the practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set)\n\n03"},{"h":"You need","a":"you-need","text":"- Your Groq key from the bench. Lost it? Make a new one at [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys).\n- Python.\n- Your GitHub account, to fork [c8-tools](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools) and to deploy.\n\n04"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"Your fork with the five-question table filled in, and your MCP server's address on the internet.\n\n05"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [No-code track notes: design the tools first, then connect one](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes-no-code)\n- [Lecture 12 notes: a worked example in Python](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n- [Tool Bench notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes)\n- [Lecture 11 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n- Lecture 12 on the map\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next, Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov: retrieval."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-13-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Retrieval-Augmented Generation","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Retrieval-Augmented Generation\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes.html\nlecture: 13\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 13 · 20 November 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2136","sections":[{"h":"Find the few chunks that matter.","a":"find-the-few-chunks-that-matter","text":"We named the three steps of a tool call, broke it with one question, and fixed it one step at a time.\n\nAn LLM makes things up when the context it needs is missing. In Lecture 12, a tool supplied that context. Today we named what the tool does, then broke it."},{"h":"01 Name the three steps","a":"01-name-the-three-steps","text":"Ask: \"When is Lecture 12?\" The tool reads the lectures sheet by number. The LLM answers: Saturday 14 November, building with tool calling and MCP.\n\nThen the question to you: what happens at each numbered step, in one word?\n\n> Sketch: Tool calling in three steps. The data, a file, is the tool, joined by an API to the server: both deterministic. The server is joined by an API to the LLM, which is probabilistic. Step 1, retrieval, between the data and the server. Step 2, augmentation, between the server and the LLM. Step 3, generation, at the LLM. Input: When is Lecture 12? Output: Saturday 14 November.\n>\n> Caption: Retrieve, augment, generate. Swap the sheet for Supabase and step 1 is still retrieval.\n\nFor step 1 the chat first said \"an API call\". True, but step 2 is an API call too, so that name does not tell them apart. Checking the data, a GET request, fetching it: all say the same thing in more words. You named each step yourselves:\n\n- **1 Retrieval.** The server runs the tool, and the tool reads the sheet.\n- **2 Augmentation.** The server adds it to the prompt. The prompt now holds the row it fetched, the instruction, and the question.\n- **3 Generation.** The LLM writes the answer.\n\nTogether: retrieval-augmented generation, or **RAG**.\n\n> Nothing new was taught. You derived RAG from what you had already built.\n\n**Where does MCP fit?**\n\nOne tool and one LLM need no MCP. With 100 tools and 10 LLMs, you would keep 1,000 connections working. MCP is one set of rules that every tool and every LLM follows, the way HTTP is for the web. It solves a scaling problem.\n\nMore in [Lecture 11: MCP, one standard for every tool](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s11), and you built one in [Lecture 12: the MCP server](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s04)."},{"h":"02 Break it: a question with no number","a":"02-break-it-a-question-with-no-number","text":"Now ask: \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" A fair question. But the tool takes only a lecture number, 1 to 19, and this question has none. So no tool call happens. What does the LLM answer: an error, the right lecture, or a wrong one?\n\nAsked without the tool, the LLM asked which course we meant. Told \"100x Engineers, Cohort 8\", it said: \"Lecture 12: Security and cryptography.\" Made up. Retrieval failed, so generation guessed.\n\nAnd you cannot write your users' questions for them. Two hundred learners will ask the same thing two hundred ways: \"Which lecture teaches the law that checks the output?\""},{"h":"03 Three fixes from the chat","a":"03-three-fixes-from-the-chat","text":"How do you fix it? One rule: use only what we have already learned. Answers that leaned on new words did not count.\n\n| The fix | What happened |\n| --- | --- |\n| Check the question for a number | No number, so it says \"Not found\". But the answer is in the data. |\n| Have the LLM pick the keyword, with a rubric | Which word is the right one? People use other words for the same idea. No rubric catches them all. |\n| Paste the whole sheet | It used the data, though the answer was still wrong (below). The only fix that can work, until the data does not fit. |\n\nOne catch with pasting: the LLM said Lecture 12. The sheet holds only dates and titles, and Verifier's Law is from Lecture 1. What you paste has to contain the answer."},{"h":"04 Paste it all, until it does not fit","a":"04-paste-it-all-until-it-does-not-fit","text":"Count first. A tokenizer counts text the way the LLM reads it, in tokens. The lectures sheet is **2,834 tokens**.\n\nIs there a limit to how much you can paste? Find it for GPT-OSS 120B, the LLM we use on Groq. One guess from the chat was 1 million tokens.\n\nThe answer: it reads at most about **128,000 tokens** at once. That is its **context window**. The sheet is a sliver of it. But add the transcripts, the notes and the practice sets, keep pasting, and you cross the line. It took five or six pastes of the same file. The API answered 400, an invalid request: reduce the length of the messages.\n\n> Sketch: Two bars against the context window, a dashed line at 128,000 tokens. The lectures sheet, 2,834 tokens, is a sliver that fits. Everything pasted runs past the line, and the request fails with a 400 error.\n>\n> Caption: Under the line, pasting works. Over it, the request fails before the LLM reads a word.\n\nSummarise it first? To summarise with the same LLM, the text has to fit first. A bigger LLM moves the line (the largest read 1 to 2 million tokens), and you hit it again. So a bigger LLM is not the fix. One rule for the rest of the lecture: you keep the same LLM, and you may call it as many times as you like."},{"h":"05 Cut it into chunks","a":"05-cut-it-into-chunks","text":"Cut the data into pieces that fit. A million tokens in chunks of 100,000 is 10 chunks. Send each one with the question, one call at a time, until a chunk holds the answer. This is **chunking**.\n\n> Sketch: A document of a million tokens is cut into ten chunks of 100,000 tokens each. Each chunk fits. All ten go to the LLM: ten calls for one question.\n>\n> Caption: Every chunk fits. But every question pays for all ten.\n\nIt works, but one question now costs 10 calls. With 1,000 chunks, 1,000 calls. Slow, and you pay for every one. How do you send only the chunks that matter?"},{"h":"06 Label the chunks, then filter","a":"06-label-the-chunks-then-filter","text":"Mark every chunk with where it sits. That mark is its **label**. The first idea from the chat was lecture numbers. But Verifier's Law comes up in almost every lecture, so a number alone does not say where it is taught. The label needs what each part covers. Our course already has the shape: three modules (images, LLMs, then Module 3), each module has weeks, each week has lectures. Give each module a list of its words.\n\n\"Verifier's Law\" is on Module 2's list. So send only Module 2's chunks. The rest never reach the LLM.\n\nIs this the keyword fix again? No. There, the LLM had to guess the one word to search the sheet for. Here, it picks one of three modules, and you wrote down what each one covers.\n\n> Sketch: Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law? Three module boxes, each holding chunks. Module 2, LLMs, has a word list with Verifier's Law on it, so its chunks are picked. Module 1, images, and Module 3 stay out.\n>\n> Caption: The label says where a chunk lives. The question picks the label.\n\nAmazon does this. Search for mobile phones:\n\n| Filter | Phones left |\n| --- | --- |\n| None | 60,000 |\n| Delivered tomorrow by 3 pm | 2,000 |\n| Samsung | 23 |\n| Your budget | 7 |\n\nThe labels also make an **index**, like the contents page of a physics book: thermodynamics, page 22. You open page 22, not page 1. The LLM can read the labels the same way and go straight to the right chunk. Some of the newest RAG research, page indexing, is this idea. You just derived it.\n\n> Label the chunks, then filter. Plenty of RAG in production is just this."},{"h":"07 To label it, know the domain","a":"07-to-label-it-know-the-domain","text":"The labels came easily because you know this course. Could you write phone filters without knowing phones? No. Labelling needs **domain knowledge**.\n\nYou have the method from [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes): name the things, what each one has, and how they connect. That is a domain model. Turn it into tables, and a question can become a query.\n\n> Sketch: The question goes to the LLM, which knows the schema and writes SQL: select number from lectures where the topic mentions Verifier. The server runs it on the database, Supabase, and the matching rows come back to the LLM.\n>\n> Caption: The LLM writes the query. The server runs it. The words come from your domain model.\n\nLLMs write SQL very well when they can see the schema: the tables and columns your domain model became. The server runs it on the database, here Supabase as the tool. That answers \"which keyword?\": the words in your schema. And it works on billions of rows.\n\nThe limit is in the name. SQL is Structured Query Language: it needs structured data. The chat messages from this lecture have no structure. That is where we go next.\n\nIt is also why the LLM will not choose how to fetch your data for you. It does not know what good looks like in your data. You do."},{"h":"08 Tool call or RAG?","a":"08-tool-call-or-rag","text":"Same idea. A plain tool call already retrieves, augments and generates, so in principle it is RAG. When people say RAG, they usually mean that plus the tricks you add when a plain fetch is not enough: chunks, labels, an index, SQL.\n\nFrom the chat: is the context window the only reason to go past a plain tool call? No. Most teams hit the **budget** first.\n\nOn the Retrieval Bench (section 09), one question used 424 tokens: a small fraction of a cent. Now ask hundreds of questions a day, every day, and the bill can grow from $100 to a million dollars. Two people can answer the same questions for $100 or for $10,000. The difference is how little they send.\n\nEach step can be cheaper: retrieve less, add less with a clear instruction, generate less. That gap is what most RAG interview questions are about.\n\n> If it fits and the bill is fine, a plain tool call is enough. If not, send the fewest tokens that still answer.\n\nLook back: the whole lecture faced two problems. Which word in the question finds the data? And how do you fit the data into a window that has a limit? Every fix today came from those two.\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**The lectures sheet is 2,834 tokens. You paste it with every question. Does the request fail? Is the problem solved?**\n\nIt fits: the line is about 128,000. But it still said Lecture 12, because the sheet holds only dates and titles. What you paste has to contain the answer, and every question pays for all of it.\n\n**A friend says a bigger LLM removes the need for chunks. What is wrong with that?**\n\nA bigger LLM moves the line, to 1 or 2 million tokens at most. Keep adding data and you cross it again. And you pay for every token you send.\n\n**A shop has 60,000 phones. A customer wants a Samsung, delivered tomorrow, in their budget. How do you send the LLM only a few?**\n\nLabel every phone by what a shopper filters on: brand, delivery, price. Filter first, so only the few that match reach the LLM. Choosing those labels needs knowing phones: domain knowledge.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)**: Fetch the data that matters, add it to the prompt, let the LLM write.\n- **Context window**: The most tokens an LLM reads at once. About 128,000 for GPT-OSS 120B.\n- **Chunk**: A piece of the data small enough to fit.\n- **Label**: A tag that says where a chunk sits (module, week, lecture), so you can filter.\n- **Index**: The labels read like a contents page: go straight to the right chunk.\n- **Domain knowledge**: Knowing the things in your data well enough to label them.\n\nKeep this\n\nRAG is a tool call in three steps: retrieve, augment, generate. When the data is too big or too costly to paste, cut it into chunks and label them so you can filter. If the data has structure, let the LLM write the SQL."},{"h":"09 Now do the Retrieval Bench","a":"09-now-do-the-retrieval-bench","text":"Do all seven stations. The first half replays this lecture. The second half breaks it, and you fix it. Every message, token count and bill shows on the wire. Bring the Groq key you used on the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench), or make a new one at [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys).\n\n[Do the Retrieval Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/bench)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: we build it, in code and no-code."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-13-overview.md","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation","meta":"title: Retrieval-Augmented Generation\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/index.html\nlecture: 13\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 13 · Fri 20 Nov 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 254","sections":[{"h":"Retrieval-augmented generation","a":"retrieval-augmented-generation","text":"Five questions about our own notes, and what each one hands you.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nRAG is a tool call in three steps: retrieve, augment, generate. When the data is too big or too costly to paste, cut it into chunks, label them so you can filter, and send only the few that matter.\n\n[Read the notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes)\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [Name the three steps](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s01)\n2. [Break it: a question with no number](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s02)\n3. [Three fixes from the chat](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s03)\n4. [Paste it all, until it does not fit](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s04)\n5. [Cut it into chunks](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s05)\n6. [Label the chunks, then filter](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s06)\n7. [To label it, know the domain](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s07)\n8. [Tool call or RAG?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s08)\n9. [Now do the Retrieval Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s09)\n\n02"},{"h":"Try it yourself","a":"try-it-yourself","text":"Run all five questions on the Retrieval Bench: by number, by pasting, by words, by meaning, and by letting the model search. Every message shows on the wire. Bring the Groq key you used on the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench), or make a new one at [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys).\n\n[Open the Retrieval Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/bench)\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Notes: the lecture, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes)\n- [Lecture 12: the tool call loop](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s03)\n- [Lecture 11: verify the record, not the sentence](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s10)\n- [Lecture 10: why models hallucinate](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s03)\n- [Course lesson 5.1: embeddings, a map of words](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/)\n- Lecture 13 on the map\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next session: we make it fail on real documents, and repair it one level at a time."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes-no-code.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building RAG apps","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building RAG apps\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes-no-code\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes-no-code.html\nlecture: 14\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 14 · 21 November 2026 · No-code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 51f573a\nwords: 3938","sections":[{"h":"From one right answer to a dependable app.","a":"from-one-right-answer-to-a-dependable-app","text":"We walked the Retrieval Bench, moved from one demo answer to a company's documents, then built an app that answers from a car manual.\n\nIn [Lecture 13](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes) we derived RAG on the whiteboard: retrieve, augment, generate. Today we ran it on the Retrieval Bench and asked what changes when the data belongs to a real company. Then we built an app with prompts alone."},{"h":"01 Is RAG only for big data?","a":"01-is-rag-only-for-big-data","text":"The first question came from the room:\n\n> \"Are RAG systems only useful for very large databases, or can they increase accuracy for small datasets?\"\n\nRAG began because data did not fit the context window. Then cost mattered: you pay for every token you send. Today it mostly lets an LLM answer from private data it was never trained on. So the choice depends on your data:\n\n- **Small data, and the bill is fine.** Paste it in. RAG is one more thing to keep working.\n- **Live data, such as records pulled from a company's customer system.** A plain tool call fits better.\n- **Large or private documents.** This is where RAG pays for itself.\n\n**From the chat: can one app use both RAG and tool calling?**\n\nYes. For a single question you pick one, based on what the user asked."},{"h":"02 Seven stations on the Retrieval Bench","a":"02-seven-stations-on-the-retrieval-bench","text":"We did the [Retrieval Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/bench) together. The data is our notes for Lectures 1 to 12, cut into 183 chunks of up to 170 words (the bench calls them passages). Five questions, Q1 to Q5, sit on a scoreboard.\n\n> Sketch: The seven stations of the Retrieval Bench in a row, with what each one did to the scoreboard. Ask: 0 of 5. By number: question 1 passes. Paste it all: refused. By its words: question 2 passes. By its meaning: question 3 passes. Nearest is not right: question 4 fails with a confident answer. Let it search: question 5 passes after a second search.\n>\n> Caption: Each way of searching won one more question. Station 6 is the warning: a close chunk is not always the right one.\n\nStations 1 to 3 replay Lecture 13. With no data, the LLM asked which course we meant. With a lecture number in the question, the server fetched that lecture. Pasting everything was refused: about 30,000 tokens asked for, and a free Groq key takes 8,000 a minute. The last four stations were new:\n\n- **4 By its words.** \"Verifier's Law\" is in two chunks, both from Lecture 1. \"Making things up\" is in none.\n- **5 By its meaning.** The nearest chunk is Lecture 10, on why LLMs hallucinate. No shared word.\n- **6 Nearest is not right.** The closest chunk did not hold the answer. The LLM answered anyway, and was marked wrong.\n- **7 Let it search.** Q5 asks which three UI builders a fix added. The first search found the topic. The LLM searched again for \"UI builder\" and named them: Lovable, AI Studio, Antigravity.\n\nAt station 4 the instructor asked: do zero matching chunks prove the answer is missing? No. Only those exact words are missing.\n\nHow does station 5 search by meaning? A small program turns each chunk, and the question, into a list of 384 numbers. Texts that mean the same get numbers that sit close together. That list is an **embedding**. The server ranks the chunks by closeness and hands the LLM the top 3. How many it hands over is called **top-k**, and here k is 3.\n\nStation 7 costs more: every extra search adds tokens and waiting. So search again only when the first result points at the topic but lacks the detail.\n\n> Choose the simplest way of searching that finds enough evidence.\n\n**From the chat: where does gte-small come from?**\n\ngte-small is the small program that makes the embeddings on the bench. It is its own small embedding model and runs in your browser. Groq does not supply it: Groq serves the LLM."},{"h":"03 A demo is one question","a":"03-a-demo-is-one-question","text":"Your app answers one question correctly in a demo. Is it ready for a company? The instructor put it this way: what could go wrong if 500 employees start using it tomorrow?\n\nThe running example was a helper for a company's human resources (HR) team. It holds six documents: the employee handbook, three policies (annual leave, maternity leave, attendance), the salary structure and the expense policy. An employee asks: \"How many annual leaves am I entitled to?\"\n\nA demo tests one document, one question, one answer. A company brings the rest. The same question comes in many wordings. A policy changes. Some documents are not for everyone. Some questions have no answer in the documents.\n\nSo we set four goals: accuracy, efficiency, cost and reliability. Reliability means the app still behaves when a document is old or the answer is missing.\n\nThe goals pull against each other: send less and you may drop the one condition that mattered. So keep a fixed set of test questions, and check the answers after every change.\n\n> A RAG answer is only as dependable as the source, the choice of chunks, and how the LLM uses them.\n\nWhat makes an answer one you would trust? The chat said: an answer and where it came from. The full list: a direct answer, any condition or exception that matters, and the source. Showing the source (the document, and the page or section) is a **citation**."},{"h":"04 Follow one question through the app","a":"04-follow-one-question-through-the-app","text":"The LLM does not read every file each time. The retrieve step chooses what reaches it. So when an answer is wrong, where do you look? Split one request into its stages:\n\n> Sketch: Six stages in two rows. Top row: 1, the question arrives. 2, the app picks filters from the question. 3, the search returns candidate chunks, marked as the place to look first. Bottom row: 4, the app selects the evidence. 5, the LLM writes the answer. 6, the app shows the answer with its citation.\n>\n> Caption: Each stage can fail in its own way. A smooth answer at stage 6 does not prove stage 3 found the right chunks.\n\nTwo cases from the slides show why stage 3 comes first.\n\nThe search returns 20 chunks for a simple leave question. You read them, and only 3 helped. The other 17 take up tokens and pull the LLM's attention away. Sort what came back into three groups: answers the question, adds a condition that matters, unrelated. Then find out why the search returned the unrelated ones.\n\nAn employee asks, \"When is my salary credited?\" The search returns chunks from the annual leave policy. \"Salary\", \"annual\" and \"leave\" appear together in many documents, so closeness of meaning alone ranked the wrong one high. The mistake happened before the LLM wrote a word.\n\n> Before you change the LLM, read what was retrieved.\n\nFetching more is not fetching better. How do you make the search return fewer, better chunks?"},{"h":"05 Two versions of one policy","a":"05-two-versions-of-one-policy","text":"The company updates its leave policy. Someone uploads the new file. The old file stays in the database. What does the search return now? Both. The app may quote the old rule, or mix the two.\n\nUploading a new file does not retire the old one. It takes four steps. Identify the new version. Mark the previous one inactive. Process the new file. Check that the search uses the version you meant.\n\nKeep two dates for every document: the day it was uploaded and the day it takes effect. A policy uploaded today can start next month.\n\nA citation helps here too. When an answer is wrong, it shows where to look: the source, the chunk the search fetched, or the LLM's reading of it. And if the documents do not hold the answer, the app says it cannot confirm the rule and points to the right team."},{"h":"06 Cut chunks where the meaning ends","a":"06-cut-chunks-where-the-meaning-ends","text":"How big should a chunk be? Take one sentence from a leave policy: \"This applies only after probation.\" Cut the document every so many characters. That sentence can land in a different chunk from the rule it limits. The search finds the rule and never sees the condition.\n\n> Sketch: Left: a leave policy cut by a count of characters. The leave rule is in chunk 1 and the sentence This applies only after probation is alone in chunk 2. Right: the same text cut by meaning. The rule and its condition sit in one chunk.\n>\n> Caption: A chunk should make sense with nothing else beside it.\n\nToo small, and the meaning is incomplete. Too large, and one chunk carries several unrelated topics. So cut where the meaning ends: headings, lists, tables and paragraph breaks show you where. A rule, its condition and its exception stay together.\n\nThe slide gave three sizes to start from:\n\n| Chunk size | Repeated from the chunk before |\n| --- | --- |\n| 200 tokens | 20 tokens |\n| 500 tokens | 50 tokens |\n| 1,000 tokens | 100 tokens |\n\nThe repeated part is the **overlap**. It keeps an idea that sits on a boundary from being cut in half. Too much of it and the same evidence comes back several times.\n\nThese are starting points to test. Keep the documents and the test questions the same and change only the size. Then compare which chunks came back and how good the answers were.\n\n> Chunking is a design choice: keep the meaning whole, keep the search precise."},{"h":"07 Label the document, label the chunk","a":"07-label-the-document-label-the-chunk","text":"Six HR files in one unlabelled pile all look alike to a search. Which one is current? Who may read it? The text of a policy does not say. So you write those things down when you upload the file, as the labels from [Lecture 13](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s06). The usual name for these labels is **metadata**.\n\n> Sketch: Left: a document card with its labels. Name: Annual leave policy 2026. Department: HR. Category: leave. Effective date. Version: 3. Access group. Document ID. Right: a chunk card with its labels. Chunk ID, page number, section, and the document ID. An arrow from the chunk's document ID points back to the document card.\n>\n> Caption: The document ID ties every chunk to its file, so you can cite the page and remove an old version in one go.\n\nNow the labels can narrow the search before it ranks anything. That is a **filter**, the Amazon idea from Lecture 13.\n\n- **By topic.** \"What is the maternity leave policy?\" searches only HR documents in the leave category. \"How do I claim a travel expense?\" goes to finance.\n- **By version.** Only the active version is searched.\n- **By access.** The database checks who is asking before it returns a chunk.\n\nA prompt line such as \"do not reveal confidential information\" protects nothing. Once a restricted chunk has reached the LLM, the boundary is crossed. The database or the app has to enforce access.\n\nTwo cautions. A filter that is too narrow hides the right document. And write each label one way: \"HR\" in one file and \"Human Resources\" in another become two categories.\n\nThe last setting is top-k from the bench. Too few chunks can miss evidence. Too many add noise and tokens. You find your number by reading the chunks that come back for your test questions."},{"h":"08 Count what you pay for","a":"08-count-what-you-pay-for","text":"What are you paying for on every question? Tokens in and tokens out. Four things make up the tokens in: the instruction, the conversation so far, the retrieved chunks and the question. The answer is the tokens out.\n\nSo measure before you tune. Run your test questions once and write down five things: tokens in, chunks retrieved, time to answer, answer right or wrong, citation right or wrong. That is your baseline. Change one setting, run the same questions, and compare.\n\nTwo ways to stop repeating work came up:\n\n- **Prepare a summary ahead of time.** One summary of all the leave documents can answer a general leave question quickly.\n- **Reuse an answer.** When many people ask the same thing, store the answer and serve it again. The stored copy is a **cache**."},{"h":"09 Build it: a car manual you can question","a":"09-build-it-a-car-manual-you-can-question","text":"A car's service manual runs to 154 pages. In an emergency nobody reads 154 pages. The instructor showed a finished app first. Asked what to do about a leak, it answered from the manual and cited its source. No code was written for it. Every step was a prompt in Antigravity, the IDE from [Lecture 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes).\n\n> Sketch: Two rows. Top row, done once: the car manual, a PDF of 154 pages, is cut into chunks with page labels, and the chunks and their embeddings are stored in Supabase with pgvector. Bottom row, on every question: you ask the app, the app searches Supabase for the top chunks, sends them with the question to the LLM on Groq, and shows the answer with its page.\n>\n> Caption: Two jobs: load the document once, then answer every question from the few chunks that match.\n\nYou need Antigravity, a Supabase account, a Groq key and the manual as a PDF. The manual and the build guide are on the LMS, under Week 7, RAG resources. The guide has five prompts. Paste them one at a time into the Antigravity chat, and let each one finish before the next.\n\n1. **Create the project.** A separate client and server. **Prompt 1: copy this**\n    ```\n    Build a web application named \"Porsche Cayenne RAG\".\n    \n    Create exactly two main application folders:\n    - client/ : responsive Porsche-inspired chat interface.\n    - server/ : Express.js backend, retrieval workflow, document processing and service integrations.\n    \n    Use a clean, maintainable structure inside these folders. The client must never call Groq or expose database secrets directly. Keep all API keys and service credentials on the server.\n    \n    The interface must include:\n    - Chat input and conversation history.\n    - Suggested service-question buttons.\n    - Formatted answers with clear step-by-step instructions.\n    - Safety callouts for Danger, Warning and Caution.\n    - Clickable source references showing the manual page and supporting excerpt.\n    - Loading, error and connection-status states.\n    \n    Do not add unrelated features. Do not put implementation instructions, developer notes or placeholder content in the user-facing interface.\n    ```\n2. **Connect the services.** Supabase with pgvector stores the chunks; Groq writes the answers. **Prompt 2: copy this**\n    ```\n    Integrate these services into the server:\n    \n    1. Supabase\n    - Use my Supabase project URL and server-side API key.\n    - Enable the pgvector extension.\n    - Create the database structure required to store manual passages, page number, section, system category, procedure metadata and embeddings.\n    - Add vector similarity search so the server can retrieve the most relevant passages.\n    - Keep credentials in server environment variables only.\n    \n    2. Groq\n    - Connect the Groq API using my server-side API key.\n    - Use the model I select from the models currently available to my Groq account.\n    - Use Groq only for answer generation.\n    \n    3. Embeddings\n    - Use a local embedding model in the server (all-MiniLM-L6-v2 or a compatible replacement).\n    - Store vectors in Supabase pgvector.\n    - Ensure the embedding dimensions in the model, database column and search function match exactly.\n    \n    Add a simple connection check for Supabase, pgvector and Groq. Show service status in the app without exposing keys.\n    ```\n3. **Load the manual automatically.** The app cuts, labels and stores the PDF by itself, once. **Prompt 3: copy this**\n    ```\n    Make the knowledge-base setup automatic.\n    \n    When the application starts, check whether the Porsche Cayenne (9PA) technical manual has already been indexed.\n    \n    If it has not:\n    1. Locate the supplied 154-page PDF in the server's document area.\n    2. Extract the text while preserving page numbers, headings, tables, procedures, torque values, tool references and safety warnings.\n    3. Split the content into meaningful procedure-based passages. Do not separate a warning, measurement condition or torque value from the step it belongs to.\n    4. Add metadata to every passage: page number, section title, vehicle/system category and procedure name where available.\n    5. Generate embeddings using the configured local embedding model.\n    6. Store passages and embeddings in Supabase pgvector.\n    7. Mark the document as indexed so it is not processed again on every restart.\n    \n    If the PDF or a service is unavailable, show a clear status and error message. Do not silently claim that indexing succeeded. If the manual is updated, detect the new version and re-index it safely.\n    \n    I do not want to run a separate manual seed command or maintain a separate scripts folder as part of the normal user workflow.\n    ```\n4. **Build the question and answer flow.** Every answer must come from retrieved passages. **Prompt 4: copy this**\n    ```\n    Implement the complete RAG flow in the Express server:\n    1. Receive the user's question from the client.\n    2. Generate an embedding for the question.\n    3. Search Supabase pgvector for the most relevant manual passages.\n    4. Retrieve a small, configurable set of relevant passages.\n    5. Send the question and retrieved passages to Groq.\n    6. Generate an answer using only the supplied manual evidence.\n    7. Return the answer with source page numbers and supporting excerpts.\n    \n    Use this system instruction:\n    \n    \"You are a technical assistant for the Porsche Cayenne (9PA) workshop service manual. Answer only from the retrieved manual excerpts. Do not guess, infer missing specifications or invent procedures. If the retrieved material does not contain the answer, say that the manual excerpts available to you do not establish the answer.\n    \n    Present procedures as numbered steps. Show tightening torques, fluid quantities, temperatures, units and special tool references exactly as stated in the source. Include the manual page number beside each relevant claim. Put any source safety instruction before the procedure and label it Danger, Warning or Caution as appropriate. If a specification depends on a vehicle variant, state the variant and do not combine values from different variants.\"\n    \n    Return a structured response containing:\n    - Answer.\n    - Safety notices, if present in the source.\n    - Source page numbers.\n    - Supporting source excerpts.\n    - Any relevant tool references and specifications.\n    ```\n5. **Test before you trust it.** Check that each cited page really says it. **Prompt 5: copy this**\n    ```\n    Test the complete application using the following questions. For each answer, verify that the cited page and supporting excerpt actually contain the information. Do not mark a test as passed based only on a plausible-sounding answer.\n    \n    1. \"How do I switch off the air suspension before lifting the vehicle?\"\n    2. \"What is the engine oil capacity and the tightening torque for the drain plugs and filter housing?\"\n    3. \"What is the test condition for checking the ATF level?\"\n    4. \"What is the brake fluid bleeding sequence and pressure?\"\n    5. \"How do I adjust the foot-operated parking brake?\"\n    \n    Also test:\n    - A question whose answer is not present in the manual.\n    - A question using different wording from the manual.\n    - A question that mentions a specific tool number or technical code.\n    - A question where the vehicle variant is important.\n    \n    Fix retrieval, citations or answer-grounding issues before launch. Confirm that the app starts, the PDF is indexed, Supabase vector search works, Groq responds and the client displays answers and source references correctly.\n    \n    Provide the final local run instructions and a short service-status checklist. Do not claim the application is live or connected unless each check has actually passed.\n    ```\n\nAfter each prompt, Antigravity shows its plan and waits. Read enough to guess what each part does, then approve. When it asks to run npm, allow it: npm downloads the ready-made parts the app is built from. A plan takes 5 to 10 minutes, depending on the LLM you picked. When prompt 2 writes the tables as SQL, sign in to Supabase and run that SQL there.\n\nTwo things in the prompts are worth a second look. pgvector is an add-on for Postgres, the database inside Supabase. It lets the database store embeddings and find the closest ones. And the embeddings are made by a small embedding model that runs inside your own server, not by Groq. Groq only writes the answer.\n\nTroubles the room hit:\n\n| Trouble | Fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| Your flow chart looks different | Fine. The plan varies with the LLM. Approve it, or ask for changes. |\n| It stops and waits | It needs another approval from you. |\n| \"This site can't be reached\" on localhost | The app is not running. Check the terminal: `npm run dev` should still be running there. |\n| A step takes very long | Some LLMs are slow at this. Let it finish before the next prompt. |\n\n**Check yourself.** Answer each one aloud before you open it.\n\n**The search returns 20 chunks for a leave question and the answer is right. A friend says the search is fine. What would you check?**\n\nRead the 20. In the slide's case only 3 helped. The other 17 cost tokens and pull the LLM's attention away.\n\n**HR uploads the new leave policy. The next day the app quotes the old rule. What went wrong, and what fixes it?**\n\nThe old file is still in the database, so both versions are searched. Mark the old version inactive and filter on the active one.\n\n**You add \"never reveal salary data\" to the prompt. Is salary data now safe?**\n\nNo. If the search can fetch a salary chunk, it has already reached the LLM. The database or the app must check who is asking before it returns the chunk.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Embedding**: A list of numbers that places a text, so texts that mean the same land close.\n- **Top-k**: How many chunks the search hands to the LLM. On the bench, 3.\n- **Citation**: Where an answer came from: the document, and the page or section.\n- **Overlap**: The few tokens repeated from one chunk into the next.\n- **Metadata**: The labels on a document or a chunk: category, version, dates, who may read it.\n- **Filter**: Using the labels to narrow which chunks are searched at all.\n- **Cache**: A stored answer to a question people ask often.\n- **pgvector**: An add-on for Postgres that stores embeddings and finds the closest ones.\n\nKeep this\n\nOne right answer is a demo. A dependable RAG app cuts chunks by meaning, labels every document and chunk, filters before it searches, and shows its source. When an answer is wrong, read what was retrieved first, then count tokens and compare against a fixed set of questions."},{"h":"10 Your turn: your own documents","a":"10-your-turn-your-own-documents","text":"Finish the car manual app first, so you have seen every step work once. Then run the same steps on documents of your own: a long PDF, your lecture notes, HR policies.\n\nPrompt 5 in section 09 has five test questions for the manual, and four kinds of question to add: one the manual cannot answer, one worded differently from the manual, one with a tool number or code, and one where the car's variant matters. Use the same four kinds on your own documents.\n\nBefore you call it done, write a few questions whose answers you know. Ask them. For each one, check the answer and check that the cited source really says it. That is your fixed set for every change you make later.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-14-notes.md","title":"Lecture Notes: Building RAG apps","meta":"title: Lecture Notes: Building RAG apps\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes.html\nlecture: 14\nabout: Lecture notes · Lecture 14 · 21 November 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2748","sections":[{"h":"Find the passage, one line at a time.","a":"find-the-passage-one-line-at-a-time","text":"We counted the tokens, searched by shared words, made rare words count more, then searched by meaning, and scored every change on ten questions.\n\nIn [Lecture 13](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes) we derived RAG on the whiteboard: retrieve, augment, generate. Today you wrote the retrieve step in Python, one line at a time, in the RAG Lab. We did levels 1 to 4 together. Levels 5 to 9 are yours."},{"h":"01 The pile and the ten questions","a":"01-the-pile-and-the-ten-questions","text":"The data is our own notes, Lectures 1 to 12, cut into 183 passages (the chunks from Lecture 13). Lecture 1 alone is 16 of them. The LLM is GPT-OSS 120B on Groq, and it has never read our notes. So for every question, your code has to find the passage that holds the answer and send it along with the question.\n\n> Sketch: A question, Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law, goes to your search, one line of Python. Your search reads the 183 passages and picks the best 3. Those 3 go to the LLM, GPT-OSS 120B, which writes the answer.\n>\n> Caption: The LLM answers only from what reaches it. The part you write is the search in the middle.\n\nHow were the notes cut? A question from the chat. By paragraph: each notes paragraph was written to make sense on its own, so one paragraph can travel without the rest. Cutting by paragraph or by sentence is a common way to make chunks.\n\nThere are ten questions, A to J, and for each one we know which passage holds the answer. A is the question from Lecture 13: \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" Its answer sits in one of Lecture 1's 16 passages.\n\nEvery level runs the same four steps. You guess. You change one marked line of Python. You see how many of the ten your code finds. Then you answer, aloud, a question that companies have asked in AI engineer interviews."},{"h":"02 Paste all 183, and count","a":"02-paste-all-183-and-count","text":"The first guess:\n\n> \"You paste all 183 passages of our notes with one question. What comes back?\"\n\nThe right answer, a wrong answer, or a refusal? Most of the room said a refusal. Right: Groq turns the request down. To see why, count the tokens. The chat asked: do we need a for loop? Yes.\n\n```\ndef total_tokens(passages):\n    total = 0\n    for p in passages:\n        total = total + p[\"tokens\"]\n    return total\n```\n\nThe notes come to 28,801 tokens. The context window of GPT-OSS 120B is about 128,000 tokens, so they fit. The window is not what stopped it. A free Groq key takes 8,000 tokens a minute, and one question with all 183 passages asks for 28,801.\n\n> Sketch: One bar is the context window, about 128,000 tokens. Our notes, 28,801 tokens, fill a small part of it. A dashed line near the start marks 8,000 tokens a minute, all a free Groq key takes. The notes run well past that line.\n>\n> Caption: The notes fit the window four times over. They do not fit the key.\n\nSo the limit you hit first is how much one question may spend. That is its **token budget**. A paid key raises it, but every question still pays for every token you send.\n\n> You do not reach for RAG only when the window is full. The budget decides.\n\nAsked at Cursor and at Moonshot AI (the company behind the Kimi LLM): \"Context windows hold a million tokens now. Why build RAG?\" Most answers in the chat said cost and speed. True, but anyone can look that up. Say what you hit: \"My notes were 28,801 tokens and fit in the window, but my free key allows 8,000 a minute. So I send the few passages that matter.\" An answer from something you built is one you can show.\n\nSo you send a few passages. Which few?"},{"h":"03 Count the shared words","a":"03-count-the-shared-words","text":"How do you know which passage answers \"Which lecture teaches Verifier's Law?\" Most of the chat said: search for the keywords. Then the next question: \"verifier\" is in many passages, so which one wins? One answer from the chat: the one where the words come up most.\n\nStart simpler and test it. Give each passage a score: how many different words it shares with the question. Send the top 3.\n\n```\ndef score_shared(question, passage):\n    q = set(words(question))           # the words in the question\n    p = set(words(passage[\"text\"]))    # the words in the passage\n    return len(q & p)                  # how many words are in both\n```\n\nIt found 5 of the 10: A, E, F, G and I. It missed B (\"Why is the model making things up?\") and J (\"What does CRUD stand for?\"). Shared words work when people type the words your notes use. Most people do not.\n\nThe ten questions with known answers are your **golden set**: ten golden pairs, the idea from [Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06). Count how often the right passage lands in the top 3, over the whole set: that is **recall at 3**. Here it is 5 of 10, or 50%. The 3 is how many passages you keep, called **top-k**. You pick k to fit your budget.\n\n> Know the right answers first. Then count how often your search finds them.\n\nAsked at Google and at Meta: \"How do you know your retrieval works?\" The strong answers in the chat named the number. \"I keep a small set of questions where I know which passage holds the answer. After every change I count how often that passage lands in the top 3. Counting shared words found 5 of 10. That is where I started.\"\n\nWhy did B fail?"},{"h":"04 Make rare words count more","a":"04-make-rare-words-count-more","text":"Look at B: \"Why is the model making things up?\" It has no rare word in it. \"Model\" turns up all over the notes. So many passages share its words, and none of them is the answer.\n\nShould every shared word count the same? \"lecture\" is in 47 of the 183 passages. \"law\" is in 2. A match on \"law\" tells you far more. So give each shared word a weight that grows as the word gets rarer.\n\n```\ndef score_rare(question, passage):\n    q = set(words(question))\n    p = set(words(passage[\"text\"]))\n    total = 0\n    for w in q & p:\n        total += math.log(N / seen_in(w))   # was: total += 1\n    return total\n```\n\n`seen_in(w)` is how many passages contain the word, and N is 183. \"law\" weighs log(183 / 2), about 4.5. \"lecture\" weighs log(183 / 47), about 1.4. The log keeps the weights small as the pile grows.\n\n> Sketch: Two bars out of 183 passages. The word lecture is in 47 of them, so it weighs about 1.4. The word law is in only 2, so it weighs about 4.5.\n>\n> Caption: The fewer passages a word is in, the more a match on it is worth.\n\nThe guess: 6, 8 or 10 of the 10? It found 6. J moved from place 41 to the top 3. B is still at place 27. This is the core of **BM25** (best match 25), the keyword search behind many search engines, and you wrote it in one line.\n\nAsked at Microsoft, Perplexity and Glean: \"When is keyword search the right choice?\" From the chat: when the question has the exact term. Say it fully: a name, a code, an error message. It needs no extra model, and you can explain every score. It fails when people use other words: \"making things up\" was at place 27.\n\nYou could see why some questions worked and the rest did not, because you know these notes. On data you do not know, you cannot choose how to search it. The golden set comes first.\n\n> Know your data before you choose how to search it.\n\nB, C, D and H share no rare word with their answer. How do you search by what a question means?"},{"h":"05 Teach a machine what words mean","a":"05-teach-a-machine-what-words-mean","text":"The question to the room: how does an LLM understand words? To a machine, \"cat\" and \"dog\" carry no meaning. It works only with numbers. So how can numbers hold meaning? The whiteboard started with houses.\n\nPlot houses by size and price. Houses of about 1,000 square feet bunch together at about the same price. A new 1,000 square foot house in the same city lands in that bunch, so you can guess its price. Two numbers place each house, and houses that are alike land close together.\n\n> Sketch: Left: houses plotted by size and price. Most houses of about 1,000 square feet bunch together, and a new house lands inside the bunch. Right: texts plotted by meaning on axes the machine picks. Making things up lands close to hallucination. Tool call is far away.\n>\n> Caption: Same trick on both sides: numbers place each thing, and things that are alike land close.\n\nDo the same with text. Give each text numbers that place it, so texts with the same meaning land close together. \"Making things up\" lands near \"hallucination\", with no word in common, and far from \"tool call\". Two numbers are not enough to hold meaning, so the machine uses hundreds. The machine also picks what each number measures, and does it better than we could by hand.\n\nThe list of numbers that places a text is its **embedding**. The program that makes it is an **embedding model**. Ranking passages by how close their embeddings sit to the question's is search by meaning.\n\nWhich embedding model do you use?"},{"h":"06 Pick the smallest model that works","a":"06-pick-the-smallest-model-that-works","text":"A model's size is counted in parameters: the numbers it learned in training (see [course chapter 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/)). The question to the room: a 1 billion, a 20 billion and a 200 billion parameter model all find the right passages. Which do you pick? The smallest. It does the same job faster and for less.\n\nHugging Face lists embedding models by task and by downloads. Some are trained on one field: one model on the list has read biomedical papers, so it places medical words well. Claude can write the four lines that call one. Claude cannot tell you which one fits your data. Your golden set can.\n\n1. Start with the smallest model.\n2. Run the questions your keyword search missed (here B, C, D and H).\n3. If it finds their passages, keep it. If not, try the next size up.\n\nWe used gte-small: 33 million parameters, about 70 MB, small enough to run on a laptop or in a browser. GPT-OSS 120B has 120 billion. gte-small turns every passage into 384 numbers.\n\n```\npip install sentence-transformers\n\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"thenlper/gte-small\")\nnumbers = model.encode(\"Why is the model making things up?\", normalize_embeddings=True)\nprint(len(numbers))   # 384\n```\n\nFor B, its three closest passages were Lecture 10's \"Why models hallucinate\" (a closeness of 0.869, where 1 is the most) and two from Lecture 12. The first one holds the answer. You can judge that only because you know the notes.\n\n**Does gte-small send our data to Hugging Face?**\n\nNo. It downloads once, about 70 MB, and runs on your own machine. Your text never leaves it."},{"h":"07 Score by meaning","a":"07-score-by-meaning","text":"The guess for this level was about F, \"What goes wrong when two people save at the same moment?\" By its words it was at place 1. By meaning: still at place 1, in the top 3, or outside it?\n\nFirst write the score. Two texts, two lists of 384 numbers. Multiply them pair by pair, then add up the 384 products.\n\n```\ndef closeness(a, b):\n    total = 0\n    for x, y in zip(a, b):\n        total += x * y\n    return total\n```\n\nThe bigger the total, the closer the two meanings. gte-small scales every list so its overall size is 1 (that is `normalize_embeddings=True`). For lists scaled like that, this sum is the **cosine similarity**: how closely two embeddings point the same way.\n\nIt found 8 of 10. B, C, D and H are found now. But F fell to place 10, outside the top 3, and J (\"What does CRUD stand for?\") fell to place 6. Both had been found by rare words.\n\n> Sketch: The ten questions A to J, three rows. Shared words found A, E, F, G and I: 5 of 10. Rare words found those and J: 6 of 10. Meaning found A, B, C, D, E, G, H and I: 8 of 10, but lost F and J.\n>\n> Caption: Each search found a different set. Coral: the two that meaning lost and words had found.\n\n> Words find the exact term. Meaning finds other words for the same idea. Neither finds everything.\n\n**Why cosine, and not the straight-line distance between the two points?**\n\nA question from the chat. You can use either. gte-small scales every list to size 1, so both put the passages in the same order here. Cosine is the common default, not a law: on some data another measure does better.\n\nTools that search millions of embeddings run this same sum. Do you need one?"},{"h":"08 Do you need a vector database?","a":"08-do-you-need-a-vector-database","text":"Asked at Cohere, and in many design meetings: \"Do you need a vector database?\" A **vector database** is one built to find the closest embeddings among millions, fast. Two answers in the chat said yes. The better answer: it depends, and you cannot say until you know the data and its golden set.\n\nIt is two decisions. First, do you need embeddings at all? Keyword search found 5 of 10 here. If your users type the exact words, that may be enough. Second, where do the numbers live? 183 passages, each 384 numbers at 4 bytes a number, is about 281 KB. A plain file holds that, and sorting it takes no time. That is what the lab does.\n\nAt 100 million passages the same numbers come to about 154 GB. Now you need a database that can find the closest ones without reading every row. Start with the Postgres you already have. Its pgvector add-on stores and searches these numbers, and Supabase (from [Lecture 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/notes)) includes it. Move to a dedicated vector database only when a real limit says so: a file too big, a search too slow.\n\n> The embedding is the numbers. A vector database is only where you keep them.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Token budget**: The most tokens one question may send. A free Groq key takes 8,000 a minute, so one question can never send more.\n- **Golden set**: Golden pairs: questions where you already know which passage holds the answer.\n- **Recall at 3**: How often the right passage lands in the top 3, over the golden set.\n- **Top-k**: How many passages you keep and send. Here k is 3.\n- **BM25**: Keyword search where rare shared words count more than common ones.\n- **Embedding**: A list of numbers that places a text, so texts that mean the same land close.\n- **Embedding model**: The program that turns text into an embedding. Ours is gte-small: 384 numbers.\n- **Cosine similarity**: Multiply two embeddings pair by pair and add up: the bigger, the closer the meaning.\n- **Vector database**: A database built to find the closest embeddings among millions, fast.\n\nKeep this\n\nCount the tokens first: the budget, not the window, decides how much you send. Then find the few passages that matter, by shared words, by rare words or by meaning, and score every change on a golden set. Each search found a different set of the ten (5, 6, then 8), and a file holds the numbers until it gets too big."},{"h":"09 Finish levels 5 to 9","a":"09-finish-levels-5-to-9","text":"Do levels 5 to 9 of the RAG Lab before the office hour on Tuesday 24 November. Level 5 joins the two ranked lists into one. Words found 6 and meaning found 8: will both together find 9, 10, or stay at 8? Write your guess down before you run it.\n\nEvery level adds one function to the same file, rag.py. Download it at the end and run it on your laptop with your own Groq key, the one from the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench).\n\n[Open the RAG Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/lab)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: the office hour on Tuesday 24 November goes through levels 5 to 9."}]},{"path":"raw/lectures/lecture-14-overview.md","title":"Building RAG apps","meta":"title: Building RAG apps\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/index.html\nlecture: 14\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Lecture 14 · Sat 21 Nov 2026 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 269","sections":[{"h":"Building RAG apps","a":"building-rag-apps","text":"The search behind RAG, in Python, one line at a time, on our own notes.\n\nThe lecture in one line\n\nThe token budget, not the window, decides how much you send. Find the few passages that matter by words or by meaning, and score every change on a golden set.\n\n[Read the notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes)\n\n01"},{"h":"The lecture","a":"the-lecture","text":"1. [The pile and the ten questions](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s01)\n2. [Paste all 183, and count](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s02)\n3. [Count the shared words](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s03)\n4. [Make rare words count more](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s04)\n5. [Teach a machine what words mean](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s05)\n6. [Pick the smallest model that works](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s06)\n7. [Score by meaning](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s07)\n8. [Do you need a vector database?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s08)\n9. [Finish levels 5 to 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes#s09)\n\n02"},{"h":"Try it yourself","a":"try-it-yourself","text":"The RAG Lab has nine levels. We did 1 to 4 in the lecture; finish 5 to 9 before the office hour on Tuesday 24 November. Python runs in the page, and every level adds one function to rag.py, which you can download and run on your laptop.\n\n[Open the RAG Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/lab)\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [No-code track notes: from one right answer to a dependable app](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes-no-code)\n- [Office hour, 24 Nov: your RAG app, part by part](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/office-hour)\n- [Notes: the lecture, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/notes)\n- [Lecture 13: find the few chunks that matter](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes)\n- [The Retrieval Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/bench)\n- [Lecture 10: diagnose with a golden pair](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06)\n- [Course lesson 5.1: embeddings, a map of words](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/)\n- Lecture 14 on the map\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: the office hour on Tuesday 24 November goes through levels 5 to 9."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/checklist.md","title":"Lectures 9 to 12: Checklist","meta":"title: Lectures 9 to 12: Checklist\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/checklist/\nfile: c8/module-2/checklist/index.html\nabout: Checklist · Lectures 9 to 12 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 396","sections":[{"h":"Lectures 9 to 12","a":"lectures-9-to-12","text":"Tick what you have done. Finish the rest before Lecture 13.\n\n0 of 23 ready\n\n**Which track are you on?** The list shows your track's lectures and practice sets."},{"h":"01 Lecture 9 · LLMs from first principles 0 of 2","a":"01-lecture-9--llms-from-first-principles-0-of-2","text":"- [Read the Lecture 9 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes)\n- [Read Jason Wei on the Verifier's Law](https://www.jasonwei.net/blog/asymmetry-of-verification-and-verifiers-law)"},{"h":"02 Lecture 10 · Tool calling and MCP 0 of 2","a":"02-lecture-10--tool-calling-and-mcp-0-of-2","text":"- [Read the Lecture 10 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n- [Read the 3 Nov office hour recap](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour)"},{"h":"03 Practice set 1 · Verify your own job 0 of 4","a":"03-practice-set-1--verify-your-own-job-0-of-4","text":"- [Write your North Star](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s01)\n- [Fill the sheet with last week's tasks](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s02)Each row with its verifier and data.\n- [Test one task on the model](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04)Golden pair first, then bare and with context. Two scores.\n- [Share your sheet on the Discord](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s05)"},{"h":"04 Practice set 2 · Write the eval 0 of 3","a":"04-practice-set-2--write-the-eval-0-of-3","text":"- [Work through the Write the eval exercise](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/office-hour/)About 30 minutes. Download your eval sheet at the end.\n- [Re-score your tested task with it](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/#s02)\n- [Share your eval on the Discord](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/#s03)"},{"h":"05 Lecture 11 · Tool calling and MCP, part 2 0 of 3","a":"05-lecture-11--tool-calling-and-mcp-part-2-0-of-3","text":"- [Read the Lecture 11 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)\n- [Finish the five steps on the Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench)Bring your Groq key. Ask, carry it by hand, write the card, switch the server on, move the card.\n- [Read the Tool Bench notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench-notes)"},{"h":"06 Lecture 12 · Building with tool calling and MCP · Code track 0 of 1","a":"06-lecture-12--building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp--code-track-0-of-1","text":"- [Read the Lecture 12 notes](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes)A worked example in Python."},{"h":"07 Practice set 3 · Write the code behind the bench · Code track 0 of 4","a":"07-practice-set-3--write-the-code-behind-the-bench--code-track-0-of-4","text":"- [Part 1: write the tool call in tool_call.py](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set#s02)Done when it answers a lecture question from the sheet, and \"What is an API?\" on its own.\n- [Part 2: put mcp_server.py on the internet](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set#s03)Done when fastmcp call returns the Lecture 12 row from your address.\n- [Do it once more with your own data](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set#s04)\n- [Submit your fork and your address on the LMS](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set#s05)Due before Lecture 13."},{"h":"08 From sand to language models 0 of 4","a":"08-from-sand-to-language-models-0-of-4","text":"- [Chapters 1 to 3: compute, remember, trust](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/01-sand-to-screen/)About 48 minutes.\n- [Chapter 4: What is learning?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/04-what-is-learning/)About 30 minutes.\n- [Chapters 5 and 6: attention, hardware](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/05-attention-and-transformers/)About 32 minutes.\n- [Chapters 7 and 8: guesser to assistant](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/first-principles/07-guesser-to-assistant/)About 37 minutes."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/delegation-framework.md","title":"AI Delegation Framework","meta":"title: AI Delegation Framework\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/delegation-framework/\nfile: c8/delegation-framework/index.html\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1259","sections":[{"h":"AI Delegation Framework","a":"ai-delegation-framework","text":"AI can now do whole tasks step by step; this page helps you decide which of yours to hand over, which to keep, and how to check the work.\n\n> Sketch: Your job, drawn as a bundle of tasks: weekly report, client calls, competitor scan, sign-offs. Each task goes through one question, can you check it? It comes out in one of three places. Automate, amber: a rule checks it, so AI does it alone. Augment, violet: criteria you wrote check it, so AI drafts and you approve. Stays human, green: nothing checks it reliably, so you keep it.\n\n1. List your tasks\n2. Can you check it?\n3. Sort your tasks\n4. Test, then learn from failures"},{"h":"The idea","a":"the-idea","text":"Every job is a bundle of tasks. Whether AI should take one comes down to a single question: **can you check that it was done well?** The answer puts each task in one of three places.\n\n- **Automate.** A simple rule can check it, such as \"the totals match the source sheet\". Once AI proves itself, it does the task alone.\n- **Augment.** No rule checks it, but you can write down what a good result looks like. AI drafts and you approve.\n- **Stays human.** Nothing can check it reliably: a hard conversation, work done by hand, a decision you sign. You keep it.\n\nThe written check is called a **verifier**. The steps below walk you through writing one for each of your own tasks."},{"h":"Step 1: list your tasks","a":"step-1-list-your-tasks","text":"List tasks from your actual job. Each one should pass two tests. **It repeats**: you do it every day, or every other day. **It matters**: automating it would really pay off. Ordering groceries repeats, but it unlocks nothing. Daily competitor research, a weekly report, a queue of support tickets: those are worth a look.\n\nWhat do you do?\n\nType your job title the way you would say it, and press Enter. It is only used to suggest tasks."},{"h":"Your tasks","a":"your-tasks","text":"One line per task, the way you would log it: \"send the weekly sales summary to the regional head\", not \"reporting\". Start with three, up to 12, with how often and how long each time."},{"h":"Step 2: can you check it?","a":"step-2-can-you-check-it","text":"This is the filter that decides everything. For each task, ask: how would I know it was done well? If you can't say, keep it for now: a coaching conversation is hard to score. If you can, write the **verifier**: the checklist a good result has to pass.\n\nA verifier, for example: competitor research for a headphone brand\n\n- Every rival covered\n- Live sources cited\n- What changed is flagged\n- Ends with a plan for today\n\nThat checklist is what lets AI work without you watching. The rule: **if you can't check a task, don't automate it**. AI does best wherever its results can be checked."},{"h":"Write the verifier for each task","a":"write-the-verifier-for-each-task","text":"For an amber task, write the rule a program could run. For a violet one, write up to three criteria that two reviewers would apply the same way. Press Test and Jev checks that each verifier is specific enough to use. A violet task stays with you until its criteria pass."},{"h":"Step 3: sort your tasks","a":"step-3-sort-your-tasks","text":"Each task lands where its check puts it. A rule checks it: **automate**. Criteria you wrote check it: **augment**. Nothing checks it reliably: it **stays human**. Whatever AI takes on adds a **new task** for you: checking its work.\n\nScroll through yours, then change any answer you disagree with.\n\nStays human Augment Automate New task"},{"h":"Your tasks, one by one","a":"your-tasks-one-by-one","text":"Jev's sort is a starting point; you know your job better. Change any colour, and say whether a mistake would be cheap to undo. The map above updates."},{"h":"Step 4: test, then learn from failures","a":"step-4-test-then-learn-from-failures","text":"Give one task to an AI tool and run it ten times. Score every run against your verifier. If your data can't leave your machine, pick a tool that runs locally.\n\nTask\n\nTap a run to score it: pass, fail, or not run yet.\n\nDon't throw the failures away. Correct each one and you have an example of what good looks like: use it to sharpen your instructions and your verifier, then run again."},{"h":"The whole loop","a":"the-whole-loop","text":"Everything above, as a loop you repeat. Each move only matters once the one before it holds; the ticks show how far you got on this page.\n\nColours on this page. Amber: a rule checks it. Violet: criteria you wrote check it. Green: nothing reliable checks it, because it happens live, by hand, under your signature or only shows results much later. Coral: the new task of checking the AI's work."},{"h":"This week","a":"this-week","text":"**Pick one task**One that repeats and matters. **Write its verifier**The checklist a good result has to pass. **Hand it to AI**Ten test runs, scored against the verifier.\n\n> Hand over what you can check. Keep what you can’t."},{"h":"Sources","a":"sources","text":"***** Jev is an AI model from TypeSafe. It reads each task and asks whether it can be checked, and TypeSafe doesn't train on your tasks. Jev is paid, so checking needs a Google login, and each person gets 60 checks a day. Your email stays in your browser's sign-in cookie. We keep only a one-way code of your account, and Jev's answers for 30 days so the same task isn't paid for twice. [Privacy](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/privacy)\n\n**How the sort works, and where the data comes from**\n\n- **Sorting**: Each task goes to [TypeSafe's Jev 1.13](https://docs.typesafe.ai) (pinned to jev-1.13.0) with seven narrow questions: the three-bucket question asked twice with the options in opposite orders, then whether a fixed rule could check it, whether criteria could be written, whether it happens live, by hand or under a signature, whether AI could reach the inputs, and whether a mistake could be undone. The page's code combines the answers; when they disagree or sit near the middle, it asks you instead of guessing. Each verifier you write goes back to Jev to test whether it is a rule, clear criteria, and about the task. On 300 labelled tasks from software engineers, founders, management consultants and IT services staff, the sort matches the label 96% of the time (macro-F1 0.96), keeps 92% of the tasks that should stay with you, and asks you a question on 3%; verifiers you write are judged right 97% of the time (19 Oct 2026). Jev reads literally and can be wrong: override any colour when you know better.\n- **Suggestions**: Task suggestions come from O*NET (below) and a bank of about 1,500 tasks written for this page for Indian and global roles.\n- **O*NET**: Jobs, tasks, importance and frequency ratings from the [O*NET 31.0 Database](https://www.onetcenter.org/database.html) by the U.S. Department of Labor, Employment and Training Administration (USDOL/ETA), used under the [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) license. O*NET® is a trademark of USDOL/ETA. The suggested minutes, and the default answers used when Jev can't be reached, are this page's own estimates from those ratings and the task wording; USDOL/ETA has not approved or endorsed them.\n- **Further reading**: Anthropic’s Economic Index, a running study of how people use AI at work, which also breaks jobs into tasks: [Economic primitives, Jan 2026](https://www.anthropic.com/research/anthropic-economic-index-january-2026-report) · [Learning curves, Mar 2026](https://www.anthropic.com/research/economic-index-march-2026-report) · [Cadences, Jun 2026](https://www.anthropic.com/research/economic-index-june-2026-report) · [open dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Anthropic/EconomicIndex)\n- **Verifier's law**: [Jason Wei, Asymmetry of verification and verifier's law](https://www.jasonwei.net/blog/asymmetry-of-verification-and-verifiers-law). The idea behind step 2: tasks that are easy to check are the ones AI takes over first."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/hackathon-billshield.md","title":"BillShield brief","meta":"title: BillShield brief\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield\nfile: c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield.html\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1089","sections":[{"h":"","a":"","text":"[Hackathon](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/#problems)/Problem 1 of 3"},{"h":"BillShield","a":"billshield","text":"Turn a bill you don’t trust into one message worth sending.\n\nAsk a classmate for a bill they paid without reading: phone, broadband, electricity, a card statement. Go through it line by line together. Somewhere is a charge they can’t explain. A ₹199 pack they never asked for. A late fee on a bill paid on time. **They noticed. They never wrote in, because writing that message is a chore.**\n\n**Teaches** Where code must decide, because a model can’t be trusted to add.\n\nInput\n\nA bill (photo or PDF) and what they were promised\n\nProcess\n\nRead every line, check the maths against the promise\n\nOutput\n\nThe line that doesn’t add up, and one message to send\n\nYou win when**They send the message.**Write your bar before you build, for example: sent with fewer than 20 words changed. If every line checks out, saying so, with the maths, is a real answer.\n\nTest with a classmate\n\nA classmate with a real bill from the last year.\n\nAnd a stranger\n\nA cohort-mate you have never worked with, or anyone outside the cohort. They have never seen your app and get no help from you."},{"h":"Five moves, on this problem","a":"five-moves-on-this-problem","text":"Each one is a lecture from the last four weeks.\n\n**DiscoveryDo it by handGreen by Sun 3 PM**\n\n- Sit with one classmate and their real bill. You are BillShield.\n- Mark every line. Do the maths on paper. Write the message yourself.\n- Watch what they do with it. Do they send it? What do they change?\n- Each bill and promise, with the line and message that solved it, is a golden pair. Collect ten across the cohort.\n\n**Sit with it.** Which line did they care about that you didn’t flag?\n\nUses [Aarav's discovery](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example)[Golden pairs](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s05)[Verifier threshold](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s06)\n\n**User interfaceNeeds firstGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Write what it must do, with no tools named: read a bill photo, take the promised price, show the maths, write the message, let them copy it.\n- Replay your by-hand session. What did they ask right after reading your message? “Is 18% GST right?” is a follow-up, and a follow-up needs memory.\n- That replay picks the interface: form, chat or both. Not your taste.\n\n**Sit with it.** Is every feature on your list traced to something they did or said?\n\nUses [Search, form or chat](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s09)[Two systems, one interface](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s08)\n\n**System designDraw the lineGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Draw the boxes: UI, your server, the LLM, the database. Write whose machine each lives on.\n- The bill photo and the prompt go to the LLM as one package. The key stays on your server.\n- Split what code decides from what AI decides. Circle the no-go. The line is drawn below.\n\n**Sit with it.** If the model did the maths, how would you ever know it was wrong?\n\nUses [High-level design](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07)[Prompt + input = package](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s11)[The key leaves the browser](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s05)\n\n**DataLock the dataDrawn Sun 9 PM, locked Mon 9 PM**\n\n- Write the questions your app must answer next month. Those decide what is worth storing.\n- Every bill is a row, every line on it is a row. History is a row, not a cell.\n- The photo goes to file storage. The row keeps its link.\n\n**Sit with it.** What would you be sued for storing? Mask it before it is saved.\n\nUses [What is worth storing](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s08)[Things, facts, links](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s09)[History is a row](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s11)[Keys and pointers](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s10)\n\n**User testingWatch them use itTue 9 AM, then Tue 9 PM**\n\n- Your classmate uploads a bill you haven’t seen.\n- Your stranger uses it alone. You watch, you don’t help.\n- Write down what they did: did they send it, and how many words did they change?\n- Write the one surprise: where real bills broke your plan.\n\n**Sit with it.** Did they send it, or did they say they would?\n\nUses [End-to-end MVP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/)"},{"h":"Draw the line","a":"draw-the-line","text":"Draw yours on paper. This is where to start, not the answer."},{"h":"Code decides","a":"code-decides","text":"Same input, same answer, every time.\n\n- Totals, taxes and the difference from the promise\n- Charges that repeat across months\n- Dates: billing period, due date, late fees\n- What gets masked before saving\n\nYour callIs a charge wrong, or only unexplained?"},{"h":"AI decides","a":"ai-decides","text":"Judgment. Can differ run to run.\n\n- Reading the photo into lines\n- Each line in plain words\n- The message, in their tone\n- **No-go**AI states an amount code didn’t compute.\n\nThe model will happily add up the bill for you. It will also be confidently wrong by ₹40. **Every number in the message comes from code.**"},{"h":"What is worth storing","a":"what-is-worth-storing","text":"The questions it must answer tomorrow. Draw the tables that answer them.\n\n- Has this charge shown up on my earlier bills?\n- Which messages did I send, and about which line?\n- What was I promised, and when did it change?\n\nThe one link that is your proof`message`→`line_item`\n\nThe message points at the line it disputes, with when it was sent and how many words were changed. Without it you can’t show the tool did anything.\n\n- **Rows for facts, files for bytes.** The bill image is a file. Its row holds the link.\n- **Store the least.** No account numbers, phone numbers or addresses."},{"h":"Watch them use it","a":"watch-them-use-it","text":"What they did, not what they said."},{"h":"Counts","a":"counts","text":"- They sent it to the company\n- Words changed before sending, counted\n- A charge they found that you didn’t"},{"h":"Doesn’t count","a":"doesnt-count","text":"- “This is useful”\n- A sample bill from the internet\n- A message they’d send “later”"},{"h":"Traps","a":"traps","text":"- **A bill summariser.** Explaining the bill isn’t the job. The message is.\n- **The model doing maths.** Every rupee comes from code.\n- **Inventing an error.** “Every line checks out” is an answer."},{"h":"Your filled template","a":"your-filled-template","text":"Copy it into a doc. Fill it as you go. It is what you submit.\n\nBillShield template\n\n```\nBILLSHIELD SUBMISSIONName / track:Live link:Public repo:MOVE 1, BY HANDWhose bill, what kind (masked photo attached):The line I flagged and the maths:The message I wrote / did they send it / what they changed:Golden pairs (link):MOVE 2, NEEDS FIRSTNeed list:The follow-up from the replay, and the interface it picked:MOVE 3, THE LINE[photo of the drawing]Code decides:AI decides:My call on wrong vs unexplained, and its failure mode:MOVE 4, THE DATA[photo of the tables, message -> line link marked]What I chose not to store:MOVE 5, WATCH THEMClassmate: sent? words changed?Stranger: sent? words changed?The surprise:\n```"}]},{"path":"raw/practice/hackathon-concept-check.md","title":"Concept Check brief","meta":"title: Concept Check brief\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check\nfile: c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check.html\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1126","sections":[{"h":"","a":"","text":"[Hackathon](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/#problems)/Problem 3 of 3"},{"h":"Concept Check","a":"concept-check","text":"Find where a learner stops understanding and starts reciting.\n\nAsk a classmate why the API key can’t live in the browser. They start well. By the third sentence they’re saying “for security reasons”. **The words keep coming. The thinking has stopped.** Point at that sentence, ask one question about what breaks, and see if they can work it out.\n\n**Teaches** How to judge a judgment about someone’s mind.\n\nInput\n\nA Module 2 idea, explained in their own words\n\nProcess\n\nFind the recited sentence, ask one follow-up\n\nOutput\n\nThe sentence, the follow-up, and a new case to try\n\nYou win when**They work out a case they hadn’t seen.**Write your bar before you build, for example: failed a fresh case before the follow-up, passed a different one after.\n\nTest with a classmate\n\nA classmate who says they get it.\n\nAnd a stranger\n\nA cohort-mate you have never worked with, or anyone outside the cohort. They have never seen your app and get no help from you."},{"h":"Five moves, on this problem","a":"five-moves-on-this-problem","text":"Each one is a lecture from the last four weeks.\n\n**DiscoveryDo it by handGreen by Sun 3 PM**\n\n- Sit with two classmates who say they get one of the ideas below. You are Concept Check.\n- Ask them to work it out, not define it. Push with why, and what breaks without it.\n- Mark the sentence where it turned into a phrase. Ask one follow-up.\n- Then give a new case they haven’t seen. Each case, with its known answer, is a golden pair. Write ten.\n\n**Ideas to test.** Why does a backend exist at all? · What does one API call carry? · Why can’t the key live in the browser? · Why does the app forget without storage? · Why does the pointer sit on the many side? · Why is history a row, not a cell? · How do you decide where a piece lives?\n\n**Sit with it.** Did both break at the same sentence? That is worth a lot.\n\nUses [Aarav's discovery](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example)[Golden pairs](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s05)[Verifier threshold](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s06)\n\n**User interfaceNeeds firstGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Write what it must do, with no tools named: take an idea, take an explanation, point at one sentence, ask one follow-up, take a second try, give a new case.\n- Replay your by-hand session. When you pointed at the sentence, did they argue, go quiet or try again? Each needs a different screen.\n\n**Sit with it.** Does a learner need a conversation here, or one question and one answer?\n\nUses [Search, form or chat](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s09)[Two systems, one interface](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s08)\n\n**System designDraw the lineGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Draw the boxes: UI, your server, the LLM, the database. Write whose machine each lives on.\n- The explanation and your instructions go to the LLM as one package. The key stays on your server.\n- Split what code decides from what AI decides. Circle the no-go. The line is drawn below.\n\n**Sit with it.** Who decides they understand now? That is the whole project.\n\nUses [High-level design](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07)[Prompt + input = package](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s11)[Strip it to the function](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s06)\n\n**DataLock the dataDrawn Sun 9 PM, locked Mon 9 PM**\n\n- Write the questions your app must answer next week. Those decide what is worth storing.\n- Every attempt is a row. The second try points at the gap it answers.\n- Is the list of ideas a table, or a fixed list you ship? Decide, and say why.\n\n**Sit with it.** Your best question crosses learners. Can your tables answer it without showing anyone’s name?\n\nUses [What is worth storing](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s08)[Things, facts, links](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s09)[Keys and pointers](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s10)[History is a row](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s11)\n\n**User testingWatch them use itTue 9 AM, then Tue 9 PM**\n\n- Your classmate tries an idea you haven’t tested them on.\n- Your stranger uses it alone. You watch, you don’t help.\n- Write down what they did: the fresh case before, and the different fresh case after.\n- Write the one surprise: where real learners broke your plan.\n\n**Sit with it.** Did they work it out, or recite a better phrase?\n\nUses [End-to-end MVP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/)"},{"h":"Draw the line","a":"draw-the-line","text":"Draw yours on paper. This is where to start, not the answer."},{"h":"Code decides","a":"code-decides","text":"Same input, same answer, every time.\n\n- The list of ideas\n- The new cases and their known answers\n- Every attempt, with a time\n- Passed or not, against the answer key\n\nYour callWho judges the second try?"},{"h":"AI decides","a":"ai-decides","text":"Judgment. Can differ run to run.\n\n- Finding the recited sentence\n- Writing the one follow-up\n- **No-go**The model decides on its own that they understand now.\n\nA model asked “do they understand now?” says yes, kindly, to everyone. **The new case has an answer you wrote before the session. That answer is the judge, not the model’s mood.**"},{"h":"What is worth storing","a":"what-is-worth-storing","text":"The questions it must answer tomorrow. Draw the tables that answer them.\n\n- Which idea does this learner keep reciting?\n- Did the second try pass a new case?\n- Which sentence do most learners recite?\n\nThe one link that is your proof`retry`→`gap`\n\nThe second try points at the gap it answers. Without it you can’t show a gap was named and then closed.\n\n- **Consent first.** Recording a classmate thinking out loud needs a yes.\n- **Store the least.** Their explanation, not their name in the shared view."},{"h":"Watch them use it","a":"watch-them-use-it","text":"What they did, not what they said."},{"h":"Counts","a":"counts","text":"- Failed a new case before, passed a different one after\n- The recited sentence, quoted\n- A follow-up that didn’t work, and what you tried next"},{"h":"Doesn’t count","a":"doesnt-count","text":"- “That was helpful”\n- The same phrase, said faster\n- A case they’d already seen"},{"h":"Traps","a":"traps","text":"- **A quiz.** Picking from four options tests recognition. You are testing the opposite.\n- **A soft judge.** The model passes everyone.\n- **Ideas they never met.** Stick to Module 2.\n- **A follow-up that gives the answer.** Ask what breaks. Don’t tell them."},{"h":"Your filled template","a":"your-filled-template","text":"Copy it into a doc. Fill it as you go. It is what you submit.\n\nConcept Check template\n\n```\nCONCEPT CHECK SUBMISSIONName / track:Live link:Public repo:MOVE 1, BY HANDPerson 1 and 2, idea tested, raw record (with consent):The sentence where it became a phrase:Follow-up used / new case before and after:Golden pairs (new cases with answers, link):MOVE 2, NEEDS FIRSTNeed list:What they did when I pointed at the sentence, and the interface it picked:MOVE 3, THE LINE[photo of the drawing]Code decides:AI decides:Who judges the second try, and its failure mode:MOVE 4, THE DATA[photo of the tables, retry -> gap link marked]MOVE 5, WATCH THEMClassmate: case before / follow-up / case after:Stranger: case before / follow-up / case after:The surprise:\n```"}]},{"path":"raw/practice/hackathon-eval-copilot.md","title":"Eval Co-pilot brief","meta":"title: Eval Co-pilot brief\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot\nfile: c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot.html\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1130","sections":[{"h":"","a":"","text":"[Hackathon](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/#problems)/Problem 2 of 3"},{"h":"Eval Co-pilot","a":"eval-co-pilot","text":"Catch the case a friend’s AI feature gets wrong, before they ship it.\n\nEvery classmate just shipped an AI app: paste a workflow, get a plan. Ask one if it is good. They’ll say it works on what they tried. **They picked what they tried.** Take ten real inputs, run every one, and check each answer against what they said good looks like. One fails in a way they didn’t know about.\n\n**Teaches** How to verify the verifier.\n\nInput\n\nTheir live URL, ten golden pairs, their verifier\n\nProcess\n\nSend every input, check every answer\n\nOutput\n\nThe cases that fail and why, next to the last run\n\nYou win when**They change something, re-run, and the failing case passes.**Write your bar before you build, for example: at least one failure they didn’t know about, fixed and re-run within the session.\n\nTest with a classmate\n\nA classmate with a live AI app, the one from Lecture 8 counts.\n\nAnd a stranger\n\nA cohort-mate you have never worked with, or anyone outside the cohort. They have never seen your app and get no help from you."},{"h":"Five moves, on this problem","a":"five-moves-on-this-problem","text":"Each one is a lecture from the last four weeks.\n\n**DiscoveryDo it by handGreen by Sun 3 PM**\n\n- Sit with one classmate and their live app. You are the Eval Co-pilot.\n- With them, write ten golden pairs from real inputs: what their user types, and what a good answer has in it.\n- Run all ten by hand. Mark each answer pass or fail against their verifier.\n- Show them the fail they didn’t expect. Do they change something and run it again?\n\n**Sit with it.** Which fail surprised them, and which did they already know about?\n\nUses [Golden pairs](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s05)[Verifier threshold](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s06)[Aarav's discovery](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example)\n\n**User interfaceNeeds firstGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Write what it must do, with no tools named: hold the pairs, send each one, show pass or fail per check, put two runs side by side.\n- Replay your by-hand session. Their first words after seeing a fail are your next screen. “Why did it fail?” and “Run just that one again” are different products.\n\n**Sit with it.** What did they look at first: the score, or the failed answer?\n\nUses [Search, form or chat](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s09)[Two systems, one interface](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/building-ui-notes#s08)\n\n**System designDraw the lineGreen by Sun 9 PM**\n\n- Your server calls their server. The second interface, pointed at another app.\n- Draw the boxes: your UI, your server, their app, the LLM if you use one as a judge, the database.\n- Split what code checks from what AI judges. Circle the no-go. The line is drawn below.\n- Code track: your server calls their URL with requests.post.No-code track: if your builder can’t call their URL, they paste each answer in. The checks stay the same.\n\n**Sit with it.** Which checks need no model at all?\n\nUses [requests.post](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s13)[Prompt + input = package](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s11)[High-level design](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s07)\n\n**DataLock the dataDrawn Sun 9 PM, locked Mon 9 PM**\n\n- Write the questions your app must answer after the second run. Those decide what is worth storing.\n- Each run is a row. Each answer, for each pair, in each run, is a row.\n- One answer can fail many checks, and one check fails in many answers. That is a third table.\n\n**Sit with it.** If run 1 is overwritten by run 2, can you still prove the fix?\n\nUses [What is worth storing](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s08)[Things, facts, links](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s09)[Keys and pointers](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s10)[History is a row](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s11)\n\n**User testingWatch them use itTue 9 AM, then Tue 9 PM**\n\n- Your classmate brings their live app and their own pairs.\n- Your stranger, a builder you have never helped, runs it alone.\n- Write down what they did: what did they change, and did the case flip on the re-run?\n- Write the one surprise: where real apps broke your plan.\n\n**Sit with it.** Did they change their app, or only nod at the report?\n\nUses [End-to-end MVP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/)"},{"h":"Draw the line","a":"draw-the-line","text":"Draw yours on paper. This is where to start, not the answer."},{"h":"Code decides","a":"code-decides","text":"Same input, same answer, every time.\n\n- Sending each input to their URL\n- A 500 or a timeout is a fail\n- Rule checks: has a number, has a baseline, under 300 words\n- The pass count, and run 1 against run 2\n\nYour callWho judges the checks a rule can’t?"},{"h":"AI decides","a":"ai-decides","text":"Judgment. Can differ run to run.\n\n- Checks a rule can’t read: “names the real bottleneck”\n- Why an answer failed, in one line\n- **No-go**An AI judge nobody checked against pairs you graded by hand.\n\nA judge that says “looks good” to everything is worse than no judge: it manufactures confidence. **Grade three pairs by hand first. If your AI judge disagrees with you on those, it doesn’t judge.**"},{"h":"What is worth storing","a":"what-is-worth-storing","text":"The questions it must answer tomorrow. Draw the tables that answer them.\n\n- Which cases failed in run 1 and passed in run 2?\n- Which check fails most often?\n- What exactly did their app say each time?\n\nThe one link that is your proof`result`→`golden_pair`\n\nEach result points at its golden pair and its run. That is how one case, run twice, shows the fix worked.\n\n- **Their key never reaches you.** Store their public URL, nothing else of theirs.\n- **History is a row.** A new run never overwrites the last one."},{"h":"Watch them use it","a":"watch-them-use-it","text":"What they did, not what they said."},{"h":"Counts","a":"counts","text":"- They changed a prompt, a model or code\n- The re-run, with the case now passing\n- A failure they called new"},{"h":"Doesn’t count","a":"doesnt-count","text":"- “Good tool”\n- A score with no failed answer shown\n- Cases you wrote without them"},{"h":"Traps","a":"traps","text":"- **A leaderboard.** One score per app says nothing about their own inputs.\n- **The rubber stamp.** A judge that passes everything.\n- **Their hand-picked cases.** They picked them because they pass.\n- **An unchecked AI judge.** Calibrate it on pairs you already graded."},{"h":"Your filled template","a":"your-filled-template","text":"Copy it into a doc. Fill it as you go. It is what you submit.\n\nEval Co-pilot template\n\n```\nEVAL CO-PILOT SUBMISSIONName / track:Live link:Public repo:MOVE 1, BY HANDWhose app (URL):Their ten golden pairs (link):My pass / fail sheet:The fail they didn’t know about / what they changed:MOVE 2, NEEDS FIRSTNeed list:Their first words after a fail, and the screen it picked:MOVE 3, THE LINE[photo of the drawing]Code checks:AI judges:My call on the judge, how I checked it, its failure mode:MOVE 4, THE DATA[photo of the tables, result -> golden_pair link marked]MOVE 5, WATCH THEMClassmate: what failed, what they changed, run 1 vs run 2:Stranger: what failed, what they changed, run 1 vs run 2:The surprise:\n```"}]},{"path":"raw/practice/hackathon.md","title":"Hackathon","meta":"title: Hackathon\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/\nfile: c8/module-2/hackathon/index.html\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 541","sections":[{"h":"Build an app in 72 hours, verified by a stranger.","a":"build-an-app-in-72-hours-verified-by-a-stranger","text":"**You pass when a real person uses what you built.**\n\nYour track**CodeNo-code**\n\n```\n\n```"},{"h":"Pick one problem","a":"pick-one-problem","text":"Lock it by Sunday, 9 AM.\n\n**BillShieldTurn a confusing bill into one message worth sending.**\n\n- **Test with**: A classmate with a real bill\n- **You win when**: They send the message\n\n[Read the brief](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield)\n\n**Eval Co-pilotCatch the bug in a friend's AI feature before they ship.**\n\n- **Test with**: A classmate with a live AI app\n- **You win when**: They fix it and re-run\n\n[Read the brief](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot)\n\n**Concept CheckFind where a learner stops understanding and starts reciting.**\n\n- **Test with**: A classmate who says they get it\n- **You win when**: They work out a new case\n\n[Read the brief](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check)\n\nEvery problem also needs **one stranger** to test it. Briefs: [BillShield](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield), [Eval Co-pilot](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot), [Concept Check](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check)"},{"h":"Five moves","a":"five-moves","text":"What you learned in the last four weeks, in order.\n\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \n5."},{"h":"72 hours, six check-ins","a":"72-hours-six-check-ins","text":"Each one shows what Green looks like.\n\nResults on Thu 22 Oct. Submissions are reviewed in the 8:30 PM office hour."},{"h":"How to check in","a":"how-to-check-in","text":"All six are mandatory.\n\n1. One thread, titled with your name.\n2. Each check-in is a reply.\n3. First word: Green or Yellow.\n4. Attach one proof.\n\n**Green**On plan\n\n**Yellow**Behind or blocked. Say why.\n\n**Red**No post. Graded negative.\n\nAn hour late still counts. After that, it's missed. Honest beats silent.\n\nCodeNo-code check-ins / **Your name**\n\nYou\n\n**You**Sun, 9:02 AM\n\nGreen template\n\n```\nGreen\nDoing now: \nDone since last check-in: \nNext: \nProof attached: \n```\n\nproof.jpg"},{"h":"The rule book","a":"the-rule-book","text":"We build alone, ship for real and prove it with real people. These nine rules bind every builder on both tracks.\n\n1. 1.**Time** Work submitted after Tue 20 Oct, 9 PM IST does not count, however good it is.\n2. 2.**One problem** You pick one problem, lock it by Check-in 1 on Sun 9 AM, and do not switch after.\n3. 3.**Solo** You build alone, and your testers give feedback but never write, design or build any part.\n4. 4.**AI tools** You may use any AI tool, because what we grade is what a real person did, and AI cannot fake that.\n5. 5.**Paper first** Every sketch, need list and table starts on paper, and you photograph it and paste it in.\n6. 6.**Needs** Every feature in your app traces back to a need you wrote down in Move 2.\n7. 7.**Deployed** Your app runs at a live link anyone can open, because a screenshot is not an app.\n8. 8.**Real evidence** Every note, screenshot and session is real, and faked evidence fails the whole submission.\n9. 9.**Consent** You ask before you record anyone, and you hide names and account numbers in everything you post.\n\n```\n\n```"},{"h":"Submit by Tue, 9 PM","a":"submit-by-tue-9-pm","text":"- Filled template\n- Public repo\n- Live link\n\nThe form link goes up in your check-in channel on Tuesday.\n\nNo stranger test, no submission."},{"h":"Stuck?","a":"stuck","text":"Ask in **Code Hackathon help****No Code Hackathon help** on Discord. We answer questions on the problem, rules and dates. The build is yours.\n\nStuck 30 minutes? Note it and move on.\n\nYour brief: [BillShield](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/billshield), [Eval Co-pilot](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/eval-copilot), [Concept Check](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/hackathon/concept-check)"}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-01-office-hour.md","title":"Office Hour: Tools change. The foundation stays.","meta":"title: Office Hour: Tools change. The foundation stays.\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/office-hour\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/office-hour.html\nlecture: 1\nabout: Office hour · Tue 22 Sep 2026 · After Lecture 1 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 1295","sections":[{"h":"Tools change. The foundation stays.","a":"tools-change-the-foundation-stays","text":"How office hours run, one topic in depth (world models), the road ahead, and the room's questions, with a first look at the second brain.\n\nMissed the office hour? This is what mattered from it, in about five minutes. It came three days after [Lecture 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes), led by the instructor who takes the no-code track."},{"h":"01 How these office hours run","a":"01-how-these-office-hours-run","text":"The instructor opened by asking who had started [First Proof](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1), the brief from week 0. Several had, and one learner shared their start. Then he laid out the plan for every office hour.\n\n1. **A weekly update.** New releases and research from the week in AI.\n2. **A progress check.** Where you are with the current work.\n3. **One topic in depth.** About half an hour on where a field is heading and what it opens up for you.\n4. **An open floor.** Your questions on the Friday and Saturday lectures.\n\nWhy spend time on research news? Because what is in research today reaches the market in about three to six months. You hear it early, so you can plan around it.\n\nOffice hours are planned for Tuesdays and Thursdays at 8:30. The room asked for more discussion between learners, so the instructor will try sharing the topic a couple of days ahead."},{"h":"02 An LLM predicts the next word","a":"02-an-llm-predicts-the-next-word","text":"This week's topic started from your phone. When you type a message, the keyboard offers three words that could come next. An LLM does the same job at a far larger scale. It has read a huge amount of text, and it predicts the next word.\n\nDone at that scale, next-word prediction looks like intelligence. So where does it stop?\n\nThe instructor held up his phone. You know that if you press on it hard enough, it breaks. An LLM can say so too, because it has read the sentence. But it knows the answer without knowing the cause, so it writes well and still slips on actions in the real world."},{"h":"03 A world model predicts what happens next","a":"03-a-world-model-predicts-what-happens-next","text":"Something that acts in the world needs a different prediction: if I do this, what happens? The slide showed a person, a table and a glass.\n\n> Sketch: Two halves. On the left, an LLM: you type a few words and it offers three candidate next words, asking which word comes next. On the right, a world model with a glass on a table: the hand waits and the glass stays, the hand pushes and the glass slides, the hand tips it and the glass falls. It asks what happens next.\n>\n> Caption: Same habit, a different question: the next word on the left, the next event on the right.\n\nA system that learns this from watching the world carries a small simulation inside it. The simulation says how things behave, and how they change when you act. That is a **world model**.\n\nOne example was Genie, from Google. You type \"a rainy Tokyo street at night\" and walk into it. Underneath, it draws each next video frame from your key presses. Robots and self-driving cars are trained on the same idea.\n\nThen the instructor asked: what if the simulation is wrong? A simulation is not reality. His verdict: world models are early, and they will add to what AI systems can do.\n\n**From the room: \"I haven't got the actual concept of AGI\"**\n\nArtificial general intelligence (AGI) has no single definition. Treat it as a yardstick that every company measures in its own way. Tests (hard exams, coding, using a computer) check how much of a person's work an AI can do. When an AI nears the top of those tests, people start using the word."},{"h":"04 The road ahead on the no-code track","a":"04-the-road-ahead-on-the-no-code-track","text":"Someone asked: \"Will we be using any of these tools in our cohort work?\" Maybe some, the instructor said. But every week brings a new one.\n\n> The curriculum is the foundation: a way of thinking that lets you pick up whatever arrives next.\n\nThen he showed the order of the no-code track.\n\n> Sketch: The no-code track in order, as nine boxes joined by arrows: design, backend on Antigravity, database, MVP, a visual builder, MCP and RAG, a second brain (highlighted), joining the parts up, then production apps with security audits.\n>\n> Caption: The order shown on the slide. The highlighted box comes back in section 05.\n\nYou start with design. Where design alone runs out, you add a backend with Antigravity, then a database, then a first working version (an MVP). The later boxes are names for now, and each gets its own lecture.\n\nThe track is built for people who know a line of work from the inside. What counts is how well you understand the problem. The instructor's own goal for the room: apps that earn real money."},{"h":"05 \"How do you codify that domain knowledge?\"","a":"05-how-do-you-codify-that-domain-knowledge","text":"This question came from the room. A learner building an app for a field they know well wants to direct the AI, not do every step themselves. But the AI does not have what they know. How does that knowledge get in?\n\nLecture 1 met the same wall: the editors' judgment [had no written form](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-01-orientation/notes#s05). The instructor's answer was a preview. You take what you actually understand and write it down as files an LLM can read: plain text files (Markdown files) and skills. The course calls that set of files a **second brain**.\n\n> Sketch: Three boxes joined by arrows. What you know, in your head. Written down as files, your second brain, highlighted. An LLM that reads those files.\n>\n> Caption: A second brain is what you know, written down where an LLM can read it.\n\nAnother learner had already started one: a notes folder with an inbox, a page about themselves and a folder per project. It worked, though their tools did not yet read from it every time.\n\nThe instructor said two things about it:\n\n- **There is a structured way to do it.** The method is how you collect your sources and arrange them. It gets its own lecture, about a month from this session, and stands on the lectures before it.\n- **It keeps changing.** A second brain on day one cannot be compared with the same one after you have collected more and given it structure."},{"h":"06 From the Q&A","a":"06-from-the-qa","text":"- **\"Could you include payment gateways?\"** Yes, briefly. It comes up when the room reaches a limit of Google AI Studio, and it is shown once for everyone.\n- **\"How will we learn how to get users for the things we build?\"** That comes closer to the build. It covers how to pitch and how to set up a sales funnel.\n- **\"Can I come to you with feedback on the lectures?\"** Yes. Use this measure: an office hour takes one hour of your weekday, and it should be worth that hour.\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **World model**: A simulation inside an AI system of how things behave, and how they change when you act.\n- **Second brain**: What you know, written down as files an LLM can read.\n\nKeep this\n\nNew tools arrive every week; the course gives you the foundation to pick them up. An LLM predicts the next word, and a world model predicts what happens next. What only you can add is what you know, written down as files an LLM can read: your second brain."},{"h":"07 Your turn: keep going on First Proof","a":"07-your-turn-keep-going-on-first-proof","text":"Open the brief and do the next step you have not done yet.\n\n[Open First Proof](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-03-office-hour.md","title":"Office Hour: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click","meta":"title: Office Hour: When Software Stops Waiting for a Click\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/office-hour\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/office-hour.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Office hour · Thu 1 Oct 2026 · After Lecture 3 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 1412","sections":[{"h":"When software stops waiting for a click","a":"when-software-stops-waiting-for-a-click","text":"Questions on Stitch and on picking the right LLM, then one idea: what an interface is for once you can say what you want.\n\nMissed the office hour? This is what mattered from it. It stands on [Lecture 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-02-what-is-an-interface/notes), where an interface was the bridge between two systems, and [Lecture 3](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes), where you built one."},{"h":"01 Three questions about Stitch","a":"01-three-questions-about-stitch","text":"The room had tried Stitch, Google's design tool, since the last session. Three questions came back.\n\n- **\"Can I make an iPhone app?\"** Yes. AI Studio writes the app so that it opens in Xcode, Apple's app-building tool, on a Mac. Xcode makes the build file, and the App Store needs a paid Apple developer account. The next session covers it.\n- **\"I am not happy with the designs Stitch gives me.\"** Sketch the screen on paper and photograph it. Upload the photo with style references from Dribbble or Pinterest.\n- **\"Why Stitch and not another tool?\"** Because it is one path: Stitch for the design, AI Studio for the app, Antigravity for the finished product."},{"h":"02 A nail does not need a bulldozer","a":"02-a-nail-does-not-need-a-bulldozer","text":"Someone asked whether the biggest LLM could edit video. It can, the instructor said, but that is \"like hitting a nail with a bulldozer\". A few seconds of footage could eat a month of credits.\n\nThen a harder case from the room. Three years of customer emails, most with a PDF or an Excel file attached. The goal: AI reads them all and learns how customers ask for things. \"A cloth to keep the mouse on\" means a mousepad. The coding tool used up its limit in hours, before it had read one week of mail.\n\n> \"I don't know which model I have to use.\"\n\nThe instructor's answer: the trouble is how the job is split, not which LLM does it. A big LLM charges for every click: open the list, open the email, open the file. So split the job.\n\n| Part of the job | What does it |\n| --- | --- |\n| Open each email, download the file | **Plain automation**: a script that repeats the same clicks (Selenium is one). No LLM, so no credits. |\n| Get the text out of a clear PDF | Software that turns a picture of text into text (optical character recognition, OCR). |\n| Read a PDF that is not clear text | An LLM that reads images. |\n| Sort each request into a category | A small, fast LLM that only picks from a short list. It reads; it does not take actions. |\n\nHow do you judge which LLM a task needs? No fixed rule: you experiment. Ask what kind of thinking the task needs (text, maths, pictures). About 90% of the instructor's own live products still run fine on GPT 3.5, an old, cheap LLM.\n\n> Split the job first. Then give each part the smallest tool that does it.\n\n**From the room: which problems in AI are still open to build on?**\n\nThe labs spend billions to build the intelligence, but the public cannot do much with it raw. Someone has to turn it into things people can use. That is this room: the most value is in applying the intelligence, not in building it."},{"h":"03 One table, booked two ways","a":"03-one-table-booked-two-ways","text":"The session's topic opened with two screens side by side. Both book a restaurant table.\n\n> Sketch: Two ways to book the same table. On the left, you click: search, then filter, then a Book button. On the right, you say it: one sentence, Table for 4 at 8 this evening, and the software does the rest. Both end at the same result, a table booked.\n>\n> Caption: Same result on both sides. Only the right side had nothing to click.\n\nThe instructor asked the room:\n\n> \"What if the computer understood what we wanted? Would we still need an interface?\"\n\nThe chat split between yes and no. The verdict was yes, but for a different job. Say you want to set a budget with a price slider, or see the options ranked, or look at the pictures before anything is booked. Each of those needs a screen. The interface appears where you want to steer."},{"h":"04 Three products for one goal","a":"04-three-products-for-one-goal","text":"The same goal, a table tonight, can be built as three different products. What changes is who does the steps.\n\n> Sketch: Three boxes for one goal. Operate: you choose and you click. Consult: you say what you need, the software does the work and you have the final say. Delegate: you give the instruction and the software does it. An arrow under the boxes runs from you do more, on the left, to it does more, on the right.\n>\n> Caption: One goal, three products. Move right and the software takes more of the steps.\n\nIn the first you choose everything and click: **operate**. In the second the software does the work and you give the final yes: **consult**. In the third you say it and it is done: **delegate**.\n\nWhich one is the future? The instructor's answer: a mix of all three, moving towards the third over time."},{"h":"05 What may it do without asking?","a":"05-what-may-it-do-without-asking","text":"Design used to start from one question: what should happen when the user clicks? The instructor swapped it: what should happen when the user does not have to click?\n\nSo you draw the limits before you build:\n\n1. What can it do without asking?\n2. What needs your confirmation?\n3. What must never happen unless you say so?\n4. What does it do when it is unsure?\n\nThe slide then ran the dinner through six checks.\n\n| The check | For the dinner |\n| --- | --- |\n| What do you want? | Dinner for 4. Any open table will not do. |\n| What is it allowed to do? | It may reserve. It may not pay: a human pays. |\n| Can you follow what it did? | It allowed for traffic and moved the booking to a later slot. |\n| Is the step the right size? | It drafts the email first. It does not send it straight away. |\n| Can you undo it? | Any change it made can be reversed. |\n| Is there a record? | It logs what it did and takes your feedback, so the same mistake is not repeated. |\n\nThe instructor called the fifth check, undo, \"a superpower\"."},{"h":"06 Why good apps still take time","a":"06-why-good-apps-still-take-time","text":"The last question from the room: if AI can build anything, why do the apps we use still take so long to ship updates? Wispr Flow's note-taker, for one, came to Mac first and Windows later.\n\nThe instructor's answer: putting up a building has become quick. The architect's job, deciding what to build, still takes time. Working out what the user wants is the slow part, and AI is poor at it.\n\n> \"AI can do a million things, but that million things is noise.\"\n\nWhat changed for him since these tools arrived? He barely writes code now. He works out the logic and how the parts fit. So the exact wording of code matters less, and the principles matter more: is it efficient, is it reliable, does it do what you meant?\n\n**Words from this lecture**\n\n- **Plain automation**: A script that repeats the same steps the same way each time. No LLM in it.\n- **Operate**: You choose everything and press the buttons yourself.\n- **Consult**: You say what you need, the software does the work, and you give the final yes.\n- **Delegate**: You give the instruction and the software does the whole task.\n\nKeep this\n\nWhen software can act on what you say, the interface does not vanish. It moves to the places where the user wants to steer, confirm or undo. Split every job, and give each part the smallest tool that does it."},{"h":"07 Your turn: your product, no screen","a":"07-your-turn-your-product-no-screen","text":"Take the product you are building. How would it behave with no screen, if it already knew what your user wanted? Answer the four questions in section 05 for it. Then mark where your user would still want to steer. Those are the screens you need.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-03-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: Replay Before You Render","meta":"title: Practice Set: Replay Before You Render\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/practice-set.html\nlecture: 3\nabout: Practice set · Lecture 3 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 792","sections":[{"h":"Replay before you render","a":"replay-before-you-render","text":"One real user. Ten golden pairs. A chat running on your own machine.\n\nFollows Lecture 3, Sat 26 Sep 2026.Post in the code track channel.\n\nThe brief\n\nWrite a spec for one real user with 10 golden pairs and a numeric verifier, replay one pair to derive the interface, then run that interface locally in Gradio and compare it with a vibe-coded version."},{"h":"01 Why this exists","a":"01-why-this-exists","text":"Aarav's status report was a proxy. The skill is not Gradio; it is deriving an interface from an output. In the lecture, 17 people voted for a search box and one follow-up question overturned it ([notes, section 09](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-03-building-ui/notes#s09)). Run the same replay on your own user, where nobody hands you the answer."},{"h":"02 Track A: your own problem (preferred) 0 of 6 ready","a":"02-track-a-your-own-problem-preferred-0-of-6-ready","text":"- Write the specObservation (no solution in it, readable by a stranger), North Star (6 months, a direction not a product), hypothesis with a number, a deadline and a stop rule, one named user with their designation, input, output.\n- Collect 10 golden pairsThe exact text your user would type and the output that would solve their problem. From the user, not from your head. If you are the user, check them with two people like you.\n- Write the verifierBaseline, threshold and the arithmetic, the way Aarav's was worked: 15 minutes to 5, 10 a day, 50 a week against a 1 hour bar. State what a failing output looks like in the same units.\n- Replay one pairTake your best golden output and write the first follow-up your user would ask after reading it. Then pick search, form or chat, with the one-line reason the replay gave you.\n- Build it in Gradio, locallyHello World first. Then the interface you picked, with an echo or placeholder process, and input and output types that match your spec.\n- Vibe code the same interfaceAny tool you like. Then a three-line review: what each route gave you, and what the difference was."},{"h":"03 Track B: Aarav's documents","a":"03-track-b-aaravs-documents","text":"Use the [discovery document](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hmt3C81bok7wzrSx2nn1xEvJleeBbumnyQugXcklETg/edit?tab=t.1k1ewmgjta8l) and the [golden pairs](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qg8zt7yHflGM7ojtnUBSPj2e_I3ACYapztGZQnzJa_c/edit?tab=t.0). Run the same loop with Aarav as the user and pair 1 (the Friday status report, about 3 hours) as your anchor. Two changes stop this from being a copy:\n\n- **Write 3 new pairs yourself** for other repeated tasks Aarav could plausibly have, each with its own verifier.\n- **Replay a different follow-up**, not \"What does backtesting mean?\". If your replay still lands on chat, say why in your own words."},{"h":"04 Rules of the build","a":"04-rules-of-the-build","text":"- **Gradio only.** No deployment yet; the office hour takes your local app to a link.\n- **Predict before you run.** Write down your interface pick before the replay, and the error you expect before your first `python app.py`. Keep both, even when wrong.\n- **Docs before chatbots.** Learn `gr.Interface` and `gr.ChatInterface` from the [Gradio documentation](https://gradio.app/docs). Ask an LLM only after you have read the relevant page.\n- **AI is allowed, blind pasting is not.** You must be able to point at the input, the process and the output in every line of your first-principles version.\n- **Log every error.** Message, cause, fix. Python not found and Gradio not installed count. The office hour reviews this log.\n- **Mask, do not withhold.** Replace proprietary numbers with percentages or X, Y, Z, but share the spec.\n\nThe starting template:\n\n```\nimport gradio as gr\n\ndef respond(message, history):\n    return \"You typed: \" + message\n\ngr.ChatInterface(fn=respond).launch()\n```"},{"h":"05 What to submit 0 of 8 ready","a":"05-what-to-submit-0-of-8-ready","text":"- SpecObservation, North Star, hypothesis with a number and a stop rule, one named user with designation, input, output.\n- Golden datasetAt least 10 input and output pairs, each input written as the user would type it. Track B: 3 new pairs of your own.\n- VerifierBaseline, threshold, the arithmetic, and one example of an output that fails it.\n- Replay noteYour pre-replay pick, the follow-up question, the final interface, and the one-line reason.\n- ScreenshotsHello World and your chosen interface, both running on localhost on your machine.\n- CodeYour app.py, with a comment marking input, process and output.\n- Error logEvery error hit, with cause and fix.\n- Three-line reviewVibe coded versus first principles: what each gave you, and the difference."},{"h":"06 When you are stuck","a":"06-when-you-are-stuck","text":"Post everything in the code track channel. Stuck on installation: post the exact error message and what you already tried. First-time coders: pair up with someone else starting out and compare errors; one person's fix is often another's blocker."},{"h":"07 After you submit","a":"07-after-you-submit","text":"The next office hour reviews your specs and error logs live, then puts your local app on a shareable link.\n\nStart from the output. The interface follows."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-04-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: API as the Second Interface","meta":"title: Practice Set: API as the Second Interface\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/practice-set.html\nlecture: 4\nabout: Practice set · Follows Lecture 4 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1145","sections":[{"h":"Practice Set: API as the Second Interface","a":"practice-set-api-as-the-second-interface","text":"Send real requests to real machines, put a model behind one of them, then design every request your own app needs.\n\nDue before the Lecture 5 practical, Fri 9 Oct.Office hour Thu 8 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.\n\nThe brief\n\nWork through the ten steps of the Request Builder in your browser, keep what each step produces, and finish by writing the API table for your own app."},{"h":"01 What to Submit 0 of 5 ready","a":"01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready","text":"Tick each deliverable when it is ready. Ticks save on this device.\n\n- 1. A 201 from the real Aarav serviceA screenshot of the reply with the diagnosis in it. Mask your key if it shows anywhere. Earned in [step 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s4).\n- 2. The \"break it\" tableFor 400, 401, 404 and 405, plus the 500 from `/aarav/broken`: what you changed, the code you got, and the part it points to. Use the template below. Earned in [step 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s5).\n- 3. A 200 from Groq, twiceThe two answers to the same request, side by side, and one line: which would you trust, and why? Earned in [step 8](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s8).\n- 4. Where your key was, and where it should beOne sentence: where you found your key in DevTools, and which box it should live in instead. Never a screenshot of a key. Earned in [step 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9).\n- 5. Design your APIEvery request your app needs, in the table format in Design your API. At least one request calls a model. Every key marked with the box it lives in. Earned in [step 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s10)."},{"h":"Template for deliverable 2: break it on purpose","a":"template-for-deliverable-2-break-it-on-purpose","text":"| What I changed | Code I got | The part it points to |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Example: deleted the closing brace of the package | 400 | package |\n|   | 401 |   |\n|   | 404 |   |\n|   | 405 |   |\n| Sent anything to `/aarav/broken` | 500 | None of yours: their machine |"},{"h":"02 How It Works","a":"02-how-it-works","text":"1. **Open the Request Builder.** Everything happens in that page. Nothing to install.\n2. **Do the steps in order, 1 to 10.** Each step ends with a checkpoint. Tick it when you have the result; ticks save on this device.\n3. **Keep what the steps produce.** Screenshots and short notes, as listed in What to submit.\n4. **Post it** in the Discord channel before the Lecture 5 practical. Before that, the [office hour on Thu 8 Oct at 8:30 pm IST](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting#s04) checks your progress: steps 1 to 8 done and a draft of your API table.\n\nPlan for about two hours in total. Step 8 needs a free Groq account; create it with Google, no card needed.\n\n[Open the Request Builder](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder)"},{"h":"03 Code Track and No-Code Track","a":"03-code-track-and-no-code-track","text":"**Both tracks: every step, in the browser.** The Request Builder sends real requests for you. You fill in the four parts and read the reply. That is the whole skill, whichever track you are on.\n\n**Code track, optional extra: run step 8 in Python.** If you set up Python in Lecture 3, run the Python version of the Groq request shown in step 8. It becomes the body of your FastAPI route in Lecture 5.\n\n**No-code track: the same four boxes in any HTTP step.** Every no-code tool that calls an API asks for the same things: URL for the address, Method for the action, a header named Authorization for the key, and a JSON body for the package. Each step in the Request Builder shows the mapping. In Lecture 5, Antigravity will ask you for the same parts."},{"h":"04 Deliverable 5: Design Your API","a":"04-deliverable-5-design-your-api","text":"Use your Assignment 1 observation. If your hypothesis is not ready yet, use Aarav's workflow. Start from one question: what does your user do, and which of those actions would your app need another machine for?"},{"h":"The worked example: Aarav's app","a":"the-worked-example-aaravs-app","text":"| CRUD | Method | Address | Package in | Reply | Key |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Create | POST | /workflows | {\"description\": \"...\"} | 201 + workflow id · 400 if the description is missing | None from the UI |\n| Read | GET | /workflows/{id} | none | 200 + the workflow · 404 if no such id | None from the UI |\n| Create | POST | /workflows/{id}/diagnoses | none; the backend sends the workflow to Groq | 201 + diagnosis · 5xx if Groq fails | Groq key, in the backend |\n| Read | GET | /workflows/{id}/diagnoses | none | 200 + past diagnoses | None from the UI |\n\nThree boxes sit behind that table: **UI → backend → Groq**. The UI calls only the backend. The backend holds the Groq key and stores the data, because a page can do neither."},{"h":"Your table","a":"your-table","text":"| CRUD | Method | Address | Package in | Reply, with codes | Key, and the box it lives in |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|   |   |   |   |   |   |\n|   |   |   |   |   |   |\n|   |   |   |   |   |   |\n|   |   |   |   |   |   |"},{"h":"Check your table before you post","a":"check-your-table-before-you-post","text":"- Every action is one of Create, Read, Update, Delete, spelled with the matching method.\n- Addresses are nouns (`/workflows`), not verbs (`/getWorkflow`).\n- At least one request calls a model.\n- No key sits in the UI box.\n- Each reply lists the success code and at least one failure code."},{"h":"05 Rules","a":"05-rules","text":"- **Predict the code before you send.** Write down the code you expect before every request in steps 4 to 6. Keep the prediction even when it is wrong; the wrong ones are where you learn.\n- **Never paste a key in Discord.** Not in a message, not in a screenshot, not in an AI chat. If a key leaks, revoke it and create a new one. See [step 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/playgrounds/request-builder#s9).\n- **Use your own Groq account.** Groq's free limits apply per account, not per key: 30 requests a minute and 1,000 a day on `openai/gpt-oss-120b`. A shared account runs out for everyone at once.\n- **Stuck for more than 20 minutes?** Check [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting) first. Then post in the Discord channel: the exact error and code, what you tried, and the request with the key masked."},{"h":"06 Where to Post","a":"06-where-to-post","text":"Post all five deliverables in one thread in the Discord channel, before the Lecture 5 practical on Fri 9 Oct. The [office hour on Thu 8 Oct](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/troubleshooting#s04) goes through the Groq step and reviews API designs from that thread. Bring a draft; it does not need to be finished.\n\nFour things in, two things out."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-05-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: Building APIs","meta":"title: Practice Set: Building APIs\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/practice-set.html\nlecture: 5\nabout: Practice set · Follows Lecture 5 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1471","sections":[{"h":"Building APIs","a":"building-apis","text":"One chat app. One raw POST request. Proof against your golden pair.\n\nFollows Lecture 5, Fri 9 Oct 2026.Post in the Discord code channel.\n\nThe brief\n\nConnect your Gradio app with a raw `requests.post` to an LLM, carrying your own prompt and your user's real input, and prove the reply is useful by checking it against your golden pair.\n\nYour track"},{"h":"01 What to Submit 0 of 6 ready","a":"01-what-to-submit-0-of-6-ready","text":"- App screenshotThe Gradio chat running locally, with your user's real input and the model's reply visible. Key not visible.\n- Code fileOne Python file (repo link or paste) using `requests.post`, with the address, action, key and package. No SDK import.\n- Raw request screenshot[httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app) showing POST, the full endpoint, and a 200 response, key blurred.\n- Prediction logAt least three requests (two deliberate failures), each with predicted status code, actual status code, and the error message for failures. Copy it from section 04.\n- Verification noteGolden output beside actual output, a one-line verdict, the one prompt change you made, and the reply after the change. Copy it from section 05.\n- The 201 questionTwo sentences: why did the chat completions call return 200 and not 201?"},{"h":"The 201 question","a":"the-201-question","text":"Why did the chat completions call return 200 and not 201?Two sentences, in your own words. A starting point: what a 201 meant on Aarav's service in [Lecture 4](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/notes), and whether Groq kept anything after it replied."},{"h":"02 The Five Steps 0 of 5 ready","a":"02-the-five-steps-0-of-5-ready","text":"**Track B, Aarav as the user.** Input: his weekly workflow. Output: a plan to automate part of it. Starting prompt: *\"Here is the workflow of an analyst working at an IT company in India. Diagnose and generate a plan to automate this work, or a part of the work.\"* Target: the plan drifts into jargon Aarav cannot use.\n\n- 1. Start from the echoRun your Lecture 3 Gradio chat. Confirm it returns your input unchanged: no process yet.\n- 2. Test the machine before the codeIn [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app), send a POST to `https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions` with your key and a body naming `openai/gpt-oss-20b`. Write the status code you expect before you press send. Practise it on this page first.\n- 3. Write your promptOne instruction that encodes what you learned doing Assignment 1 by hand.Start from the lecture's prompt above. Send a prompt plus your user's real input in the browser first and read the reply as your user would.\n- 4. Rip off the cushionIn Python, replace the echo with `requests.post`, using the same address, action, key and package. Return only the content field. No Groq or OpenAI SDK in the final file.\n- 5. VerifyRun your golden input through the app. Compare the reply to your golden output, write a one-line verdict, change the prompt once based on what the comparison showed, and run it again.Find the jargon in the plan. Fix it with a prompt (no jargon, one step at a time, the minimum action needed now) and show the before and after. Use the verification note."},{"h":"Step 1 · Where you start","a":"step-1--where-you-start","text":""},{"h":"Step 4 · The shape of the change","a":"step-4--the-shape-of-the-change","text":"Each blank is one of the four parts, with the values from the raw request. Write it yourself.\n\nYour machine\n\nexport GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your_key_here\n\n$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"gsk_your_key_here\""},{"h":"03 The Raw Request, Live","a":"03-the-raw-request-live","text":"Step 2's request, sent from this page to Groq. Pick your predicted status code to unlock Send; every send goes to the prediction log. Your screenshot still comes from [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app).\n\nPOST · Groq chat completionsFree tier: 30 requests a minute\n\n**Address**\n\nhttps://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions\n\n**Action**\n\nPOST · a Create: each call creates a new reply\n\n**Key**\n\nSent as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Held in this tab's memory only: never saved, gone when you reload. [Get a key](https://console.groq.com/keys).\n\n**Package**\n\nmodel Your prompt · one instruction Your user's real input\n\nThe package on the wire\n\nBreak one part on purpose for this send:\n\nWhat status code do you expect?\n\nPick a prediction first.\n\n—\n\n**The full reply package**\n\n```\n\n```\n\n**What each code means here**\n\n| 200 | Groq understood you and sent a reply. It does not mean the reply helps anyone: that is section 05. |\n| --- | --- |\n| 400 | The package is wrong: broken JSON or a missing field. |\n| 401 | The key is missing, mistyped, or sent without `Bearer`. |\n| 404 | The model named in the package does not exist, or the address is wrong. Read the message: it says which. |\n| 429 | Too many requests this minute on your account. Wait and send again. |\n| 5xx | Their machine, not yours. Try again later. |\n\nStatus code says whose fault; the message says what. Try the fix the error points to before pasting anything into an LLM. More in [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting).\n\n**Got an error?** Search it in [Troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/troubleshooting): the part it points to and the fix."},{"h":"04 Prediction Log 0 of 4 ready","a":"04-prediction-log-0-of-4-ready","text":"Three requests minimum, two of them deliberate failures. Sends from section 03 land here; add the rest by hand.\n\nThree requestsA wrong model nameA missing or wrong keyA prediction on every row\n\n| Request | Predicted | Actual | Error message, for failures |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"},{"h":"05 Verify Against Your Golden Pair","a":"05-verify-against-your-golden-pair","text":"Golden output beside the reply, a one-line verdict, one prompt change, run again.Load Aarav's golden pair below. The target is jargon.\n\nGolden inputWhat your user typed, word for word. Golden outputWhat a useful reply looks like, written by you by hand.\n\nActual output, before the changeThe reply your app returned. Actual output, after the changeThe reply once the prompt was changed.\n\nOne-line verdictDoes the actual output do what the golden output does, for this user? The one prompt changeWhat you changed, and which part of the comparison made you change it.\n\nSaves on this device as you type."},{"h":"06 Rules of the Build","a":"06-rules-of-the-build","text":"- **Predict before you run.** Every request in the prediction log gets its expected status code written down first. Include at least two deliberate failures: a wrong model name and a missing or wrong key. Record what each actually returned.\n- **Read the error before you ask.** Status code says whose fault; the message says what. Try the fix the error points to before pasting anything into an LLM.\n- **Minimum path.** No extra parameters, no chat history handling, no changed interface, no renamed functions. The diff from the echo app should be the process and nothing else.\n- **Keys never in code or screenshots.** Set it in the terminal, never in the file. Blur the key in every image you post. If a key leaks, revoke it and create a new one: [Keys](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-04-the-second-interface/post-lecture/key-safety).\n- **Stay inside the free tier.** Groq allows 30 requests per minute. You will not need more.\n- **LLM help: hints and debugging yes; generating the solution no.** Think for the first minute on your own. Pasting this brief in and copying the code out defeats the exercise; the code is short enough that writing it is the learning."},{"h":"07 Stretch: Any Machine, Four Lines","a":"07-stretch-any-machine-four-lines","text":"- Pick one free API from the [public-apis list](https://github.com/public-apis/public-apis) ([MediaStack](https://mediastack.com) is a fine start). Answer the four questions for it in writing before writing any code.\n- Call it with `requests` and print one field from the JSON response.\n- Optional swap test: point your chat app at another OpenAI-compatible endpoint by changing only the URL, key and model name. Note what else, if anything, had to change.\n\n**API** **Address** **Action** **Key** **Package** **Swap test**"},{"h":"08 Why This Exercise Exists","a":"08-why-this-exercise-exists","text":"A passenger enjoys the comfort of a car. A driver understands its controls and knows what to do when things go wrong.\n\n**Comfort without understanding comes at the cost of control.**\n\nIn software, SDKs, libraries, no-code/vibe-coding tools are like comfortable passenger seats. They make the journey easier by hiding the machinery underneath.\n\nBut hidden machinery doesn't disappear.\n\nWhen something breaks, what you understand determines what you can fix.\n\n**To drive software machines, understand its controls: APIs, endpoints, authentication, requests, and responses.**\n\n**Use abstractions for comfort. Build understanding for control.**\n\nA 200 proves the call worked. Only your verifier proves the reply helps."},{"h":"09 Where to Post and How to Get Unblocked","a":"09-where-to-post-and-how-to-get-unblocked","text":"Post on the C8 Discord code channel; earliest posts are reviewed first. Questions on the channel, not DMs (replies Mon and Tue). Still stuck: next week's office hour.\n\n**Next:** building your own API, and how much protection it actually needs.\n\n**Hold this.** A 200 means the machine understood you. It does not mean you helped anyone. Four lines get you to any machine on the internet; your golden pair tells you whether the trip was worth it for a human.\n\nFour lines to any machine."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-06-office-hour.md","title":"Office Hour: Write the Logic Before the Code","meta":"title: Office Hour: Write the Logic Before the Code\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/office-hour\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/office-hour.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Office hour · Thu 15 Oct 2026 · After Lecture 6 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 0f4c65a\nwords: 1309","sections":[{"h":"Write the logic before the code.","a":"write-the-logic-before-the-code","text":"We wrote a login, a cup of coffee and a shopping cart as plain steps, then took your questions on keys, tables and security.\n\nBy [Lecture 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes) your app could call an API, and in [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes) you decided what it should store. This office hour practised the step before any prompt: writing down what a feature must do, in order."},{"h":"01 What happens when you click Log in?","a":"01-what-happens-when-you-click-log-in","text":"The instructor opened with a login page and one question for the chat:\n\n> \"What happens when someone clicks the login button?\"\n\nThe chat answered in single words: an API call, the server, check the email and password. One answer was closer: check the id and password, and if they do not match, throw an error.\n\nThe instructor's version had four steps, in order:\n\n1. Check the email has the right form: an @ sign, and an ending such as.com.\n2. Send the email and password to be checked.\n3. If they are wrong, show an error.\n4. If they are right, open the dashboard.\n\nNothing here is code. It is the thinking you do before you ask an LLM for code, in either track."},{"h":"02 Black coffee, in three moves","a":"02-black-coffee-in-three-moves","text":"To show the moves, the instructor wrote out something everyone can do: make black coffee.\n\n> Sketch: Three cards. Sequence: boil water, add coffee grounds, pour hot water, wait 3 minutes, serve. Selection: if the water is not boiling, keep heating, else add the coffee grounds. Repetition: stir until the sugar is dissolved.\n>\n> Caption: One cup of coffee, written as the three moves every program is made of.\n\n- **Do this, then that.** Boil water, add coffee grounds, pour, wait 3 minutes, serve. This is **sequence**.\n- **Choose a path.** If the water is not boiling, keep heating. Else, add the grounds. This is **selection**.\n- **Do it again.** Stir until the sugar is dissolved. This is **repetition**.\n\nSteps written this way, in plain English with no programming language, are called **pseudocode**. You met one line of it in [Lecture 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-05-building-apis/notes#s04)."},{"h":"03 One rule, said two ways","a":"03-one-rule-said-two-ways","text":"Take a rule in plain English: if a student's marks are 35 or more, show pass, otherwise fail. As pseudocode it becomes:\n\n```\nif marks >= 35\n    display \"Pass\"\nelse\n    display \"Fail\"\n```\n\nGive those four lines to an LLM and it can write them in any programming language. What the pseudocode adds over the paragraph is precision: nothing is left for the LLM to guess.\n\nThen the chat was asked what happens when you click Add to cart. The answers came as scraps: the stock goes down by one, a message appears, go to the cart page. The instructor sorted any feature into four parts.\n\n> Sketch: Four boxes joined by arrows, for the Add to cart button. Input: price and quantity. Process: price times quantity. Decision, highlighted: does a discount apply? Output: the final amount.\n>\n> Caption: What comes in, what is done with it, what must be decided, what comes out.\n\nOrder matters too. \"Calculate the percentage, get the marks, show the result\" is broken: step 1 needs what step 2 fetches. Get the marks first.\n\nAnd write every \"if not\". What if the cart is empty at checkout? What if a required field is blank? What if the user is not allowed to use this feature?"},{"h":"04 Three levels of asking","a":"04-three-levels-of-asking","text":"The last example: the school office adds a student. The app checks the details, saves the student to the database, and updates the list. The instructor showed three ways to ask an LLM for it.\n\n- **Level 1.** \"Build a student management app.\" Most people start here.\n- **Level 2.** Name the feature and the tools, then list what must be handled. Check the name and email. Save valid records to Supabase. Handle database errors. Update the list only after a successful save.\n- **Level 3.** Write the steps yourself:\n\n```\nget name and email\nif name is empty\n    show name error\nelse if email is invalid\n    show email error\nelse\n    save student to database\n    if save succeeds\n        update student list\n        show success message\n    else\n        show save error\n```\n\nRepetition has its own line. To show the list: \"for each student in the student list, display the student's name\". It stops when the list ends.\n\nBefore you build any feature, answer three questions, then write the answers as steps.\n\n1. What information enters the system?\n2. What decision must it take?\n3. What should happen when it succeeds, and when it fails?\n\nIt works in reverse as well. Handed code someone else wrote, ask an LLM to turn it into pseudocode, read that, and change the steps.\n\n> The LLM can write the code. The steps are yours."},{"h":"05 From the Q&A","a":"05-from-the-qa","text":"- **\"I want a backend so my key lives there. How do I design the address for a chat?\"** The next two lectures build exactly this. In [Lecture 7](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/notes#s07) the function sits behind one address on your server, and the Groq key stays there.\n- **\"How do I decide which tables my own app needs?\"** Go back to the part of [Lecture 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/notes#s09) that confused you and rewatch it. Then paste the transcript into an LLM and keep asking until you can say it in your own words. Databases come back in Lecture 7, on Supabase.\n- **\"Clients ask if an app I built with an LLM is secure. What do I say?\"** The same basic protections apply whether you typed the code or an LLM did. Nothing is impossible to break; you make it harder to break.\n- **How much protection is enough?** Ask how sensitive the data is and what someone gains by getting in. Add barriers until getting in costs more than it is worth, and stop there: you do not bring an elephant to break a needle.\n- **\"If code is written in minutes, why do big companies still take so long to ship?\"** Writing got faster; the rest did not. People still have to read what the LLM wrote, test it, and answer for it. A fix can work and still be wrong for the business, for example if it multiplies the server cost. If you never touch the code, you stay at a prototype. Pseudocode is how you touch it without knowing a programming language.\n- **\"My plan is written. When do I start building?\"** Now. The plan is refined enough; the rest you fix while building. Both tracks are at the same point: an app that talks to an API. Next comes the database on Supabase, then deployment.\n\n**Words from this office hour**\n\n- **Sequence**: Steps done one after another, in a fixed order.\n- **Selection**: A step that picks one path or another: if this, do that, else do the other.\n- **Repetition**: A step done again and again until something is true, or once for each item in a list.\n- **Pseudocode**: The steps of a feature written in plain English, in order, with no programming language.\n\nKeep this\n\nBefore you ask an LLM for code, write the steps in plain English. Say what comes in, what must be decided, and what happens when it works and when it fails. Every feature is sequence, selection and repetition. The more exact your steps, the less the LLM has to guess."},{"h":"06 Your turn: three to write","a":"06-your-turn-three-to-write","text":"The instructor asked everyone for pseudocode for three features. Write each one before you open any tool.\n\n1. A login page.\n2. Add to cart on a shopping site.\n3. Registering a student.\n\nThen do the same for the next feature of your own app, and paste those steps into Antigravity as the prompt.\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-06-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: Databases and Domain Modelling","meta":"title: Practice Set: Databases and Domain Modelling\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/practice-set.html\nlecture: 6\nabout: Practice set · Lecture 6 · Databases and domain modelling\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 604","sections":[{"h":"Draw your app's HLD","a":"draw-your-apps-hld","text":"Every box, where it lives, and the calls between them. Then fill its storage box in the Modelling Lab.\n\nDue before the office hour, Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST.\n\nThe brief\n\nDraw the high-level design of your own app: every box, whose machine it lives on, and the API calls between them. Then decide what goes in its storage box by playing the Modelling Lab, first for Aarav and then for your app. Four things to post, below."},{"h":"01 What to Submit 0 of 4 ready","a":"01-what-to-submit-0-of-4-ready","text":"- 1. Your HLD, storage box filled with your tablesA photo or screenshot. Earned in Draw yours.\n- 2. Aarav's modelScreenshot the board with no dashed pieces. [Lab Level 5](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s5) ticks this when you beat it.\n- 3. The Arena at 6 / 6Screenshot of the briefs with their stars. [Lab Level 6](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s6) ticks this at 6 / 6.\n- 4. Your modelYour picture and \"Copy my blueprint as text\". [Lab Level 7](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab#s7) ticks this when you finish it.\n\nPost all four in the Discord assignments channel before Thu 15 Oct, 8:30 pm IST. Bring your HLD to the office hour. Stuck in the lab? See [troubleshooting](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/troubleshooting)."},{"h":"02 Draw Yours","a":"02-draw-yours","text":"The same picture for your Assignment 1 app. No app yet? Draw Aarav's from memory. Paper or any drawing app.\n\n- **The user:** input in, output out\n- **An icon and a number for each piece:** laptop, browser, UI, server, endpoint, storage, LLM\n- **A dashed zone per machine:** the user's, yours, or someone else's, and why each box lives there\n- **Every arrow is an API call:** its address in the box it reaches, and where the key lives\n- **Storage:** a file on your server. What goes in the file?\n\n> What is worth storing?\n\nAarav's HLD, as drawn in class, for reference:\n\n> Sketch: High-level design of Aarav's app in three zones. Aarav's machine: his laptop (1), Chrome (2) and the UI (3), where he types his workflow in and reads the plan out. Your server (4), with the endpoint POST /diagnose (5) on it, the Groq key, and storage (7): a file, memory.txt, with save and fetch arrows. Groq's machine: a GPU, the endpoint POST /chat/completions (6) and the model gpt-oss-120b, which is 14 files, 65 GB. An API arrow pair joins the UI and your server, and another joins your server and Groq. Underneath: anyone can read and the user can wipe what is on his machine; only you can reach your server; Groq's machine is rented because the model is too big to host.\n>\n> Caption: Numbers name the pieces, so you can say \"5 calls 6\". Dashed zones show whose machine each piece runs on. Arrows are API calls: address, action, key, package."},{"h":"03 Fill the Box in the Modelling Lab","a":"03-fill-the-box-in-the-modelling-lab","text":"The lab splits the question into three: what are the things worth storing? what does each have? how do they connect? Answer them for Aarav in Levels 1 to 5, then for your app in Level 7. Your tables are what go in the file.\n\n[Open the Modelling Lab](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-06-databases-and-domain-modelling/playgrounds/modelling-lab)"},{"h":"04 Rules","a":"04-rules","text":"- Write a line before every reveal in the lab, and keep it even when the reveal disagrees.\n- Questions before tables. A column no question needs is not worth storing.\n- Pointers go on the many side. A list in a cell means a missing table.\n- History is a row, not a cell.\n- Ask an LLM to explain, not to model for you.\n\nDecide what is worth storing before you pick where to store it."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-07-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: Connect Backend to the Database","meta":"title: Practice Set: Connect Backend to the Database\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-07-connect-backend-to-the-database/practice-set.html\nlecture: 7\nabout: Practice set · Lecture 7 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1085","sections":[{"h":"Your function, live on a URL","a":"your-function-live-on-a-url","text":"Steps 01 to 04 of the lecture, done for your own app. The key never leaves your server.\n\nDue before Lecture 8, Sat 17 Oct, 6 pm IST.Post in the Discord code channel.\n\nThe brief\n\nStrip your Assignment 1 app to one function. Put it behind a FastAPI route with Pydantic at the door. Deploy it to Vercel with your key in an environment variable. Point your Gradio app at the live URL. Then write down every error you met on the way. Lecture 8 adds storage to this same URL, so finish before Saturday 6 pm."},{"h":"01 What to Submit 0 of 5 ready","a":"01-what-to-submit-0-of-5-ready","text":"- 1. Your function, in English and in PythonThree lines of English: the input, the output, the process. Then the Python file with that function alone, run locally. Key blurred or absent.\n- 2. A `/docs` screenshotFastAPI's own page for your API, showing a POST route and its input model.\n- 3. Your live URL and a 200The `vercel.app` address, plus an [httpie.io/app](https://httpie.io/app) screenshot of a POST to it returning your output.\n- 4. Gradio calling the live URLA screenshot of your Gradio app with a reply, and the file it runs from. One URL in it, no key anywhere.\n- 5. Your error logEvery error you hit: the status code or message, what it meant, what fixed it. Use the table in section 04. Lecture 8 opens with the common ones.\n\nNo app of your own yet? Do all five for Aarav's diagnoser from [the lecture repository](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-db), then change the workflow to one you actually do each week."},{"h":"02 Before You Start 0 of 5 ready","a":"02-before-you-start-0-of-5-ready","text":"- Python runs locally and you have an IDECursor, VS Code, PyCharm. Whatever you used for Lecture 5.\n- A GitHub account and one public repositoryRepository is the project. Commit is a save. Push sends the saves up. Public, or it cannot be reviewed.\n- A Vercel account, and the CLI logged in`npm i -g vercel`, then `vercel login` with the same account. Hobby plan, free.\n- One LLM desktop app that can see your project folder[Codex](https://openai.com/codex/), [Claude Code](https://claude.com/product/claude-code), [Hermes](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/) or [OpenCode](https://opencode.ai/). One, not four. Let it install Git, the GitHub CLI and the Vercel CLI, and read the commands it runs.\n- Your Groq key set in the terminalNot in a file you will share. Not on a shared screen.\n\nYour machine\n\nexport GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your_key_here\n\n$env:GROQ_API_KEY=\"gsk_your_key_here\""},{"h":"03 The Four Steps 0 of 4 ready","a":"03-the-four-steps-0-of-4-ready","text":"- 1. Witness the leak, then strip to the functionOpen `01-leak/index.html`, click Diagnose, read the 401 in the Network tab. Then write your function in English: input, output, process. Replace the body of `02-function/diagnose.py` with it and run it from the terminal until it answers.\n- 2. Put it behind an APIFill the four dials first, below. Then FastAPI: `app.post` on your address, a Pydantic model for the input, the function under it. Run `uvicorn api.index:app --reload` inside the folder, open `/docs`, and POST to it from httpie.\n- 3. DeployNew terminal, `cd` into the API folder, `vercel`, then `vercel --prod`. Add `GROQ_API_KEY` under Settings, Environment Variables, exact name, then redeploy. POST to the full address, including `/api/…`.\n- 4. Gradio becomes a clientYour Lecture 5 Gradio app posts to the live URL and shows one field of the reply. Delete the key and the Groq address from that file. They are on the server now."},{"h":"Step 2 · Your four dials, before any code","a":"step-2--your-four-dials-before-any-code","text":"| Dial | Aarav's diagnoser | Yours |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| action | POST | POST, for a function that makes something |\n| address | `/api/diagnose` | `/api/` and a verb for your function |\n| package | `{\"workflow\"}` in, `{\"plan\"}` out | Your input field in, your output field out. Text only, for now. |\n| key | none on your API. Groq's key stays on the server. | Same. Your own key comes later in the module. |"},{"h":"Step 3 · Test the live route","a":"step-3--test-the-live-route","text":"http POST https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api/diagnose workflow=\"I edit videos until Sunday night\""},{"h":"Step 4 · Run Gradio against the live server","a":"step-4--run-gradio-against-the-live-server","text":"API_URL=https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api/diagnose python 04-gradio-client/app.py"},{"h":"04 Your Error Log","a":"04-your-error-log","text":"Predict the status before every call. Then write what came back. The lecture's own log, to start you off:\n\n| Call | Predicted | Actual | Meant | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Leak page, Diagnose | 200 | 401 | No key in the request | The key, on the server, not here |\n| `python diagnose.py` | a plan | error | Retired model name | Check the model name on the day |\n| POST to the deploy URL | 200 | 404 | Wrong address, no route at `/` | Add `/api/diagnose` |\n| POST to `/api/diagnose` | 200 | 500 | Server failed, key not on that machine | Logs tab, then the env var |\n| POST after the env var | 200 | 200 | A plan | Share the URL |\n\nStatus code says whose fault. Message says what. Try the fix the error points to before pasting it into an LLM. Still stuck: screenshot, context, your LLM desktop app, then a support ticket."},{"h":"05 Rules","a":"05-rules","text":"- **One function first.** Not the UI, not storage. If it does not run from the terminal, nothing above it will.\n- **Keys live in environment variables.** In the terminal on your laptop, in the dashboard on Vercel. Never in the file, never in a screenshot, never hardcoded to make a 500 go away.\n- **Predict, then run.** Write the status code you expect before each call.\n- **Thin API.** JSON in, JSON out. Convert to sheets or files on the user's device, not on the server.\n- **Mock the LLM if you need to.** Build the deterministic path from your golden pair first. Add the model call last.\n- **LLM help: terminal chores and diagnosis, yes. Writing your function, no.** Read every command it runs.\n- **Public repository.** Commit after each step, push when the step works.\n\nThe key lives on the machine that spends the money."},{"h":"06 Next: Lecture 8","a":"06-next-lecture-8","text":"Bring the live URL and your Modelling Lab tables. The server you deployed learns to remember: a file that dies, then Supabase, then the whole app end to end. [Lecture 8 overview.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-08-end-to-end-mvp/) Then the hackathon, where you run this trace again on a fresh problem."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-09-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set: Verify Your Own Job","meta":"title: Practice Set: Verify Your Own Job\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set.html\nlecture: 9\nabout: Practice set 1 · Lectures 9 and 10 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 815","sections":[{"h":"Verify your own job","a":"verify-your-own-job","text":"One real working week. Every atomic task. A verifier, or the reason there isn't one.\n\nLecture 9, Fri 30 Oct, starts it. Lecture 10, Sat 31 Oct, adds one step. Practice set 2 follows.\n\nThe brief\n\nWrite your North Star. List every atomic task from last week. For each, write its success criteria, whether it moves your North Star, its verifier and verification type, and whether the data exists. [Copy the template](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1QbuRsvhH9LpZIRgYKoDd83rMmXqLPTooGeY_55FzwNY/copy): Aarav's week is the first tab, yours is the second. After Lecture 10, test one task from it on the model. [Practice set 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/) then turns that test into an eval."},{"h":"01 Start with your North Star","a":"01-start-with-your-north-star","text":"One sentence, six months out: **By [month + year], I am [role or identity] doing [specific thing] at [level, income or scale].** Someone outside the cohort should be able to check it on the date. [Week 0 notes, sections 02 and 03.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s02)\n\nIt decides what is worth verifying. An atomic task that does not move your North Star gets one line and nothing more."},{"h":"02 Fill the sheet 0 of 5 ready","a":"02-fill-the-sheet-0-of-5-ready","text":"- Guess firstThe share of your tasks that are verifiable. Don't change it later.\n- List last weekFrom your calendar, sent mail and Slack, not memory. One row per atomic task, with the time spent. Expect 25 to 60 rows.\n- Success criteria, and does it move your North Star?What must be true for it to count as done. If it moves your North Star not at all, stop there.\n- Verifier, verification type, dataThe verifier is a rule (\"totals match finance's export\") or a judge, a signal and a time (\"manager · approves with no rework · same day\"). Verification type: Rule, Judgment or Unverifiable. \"It's subjective\", or an answer that only comes months later, is Unverifiable. Data: how many past input and output pairs exist. Then the bucket: see Rules.\n- Show it to your judgeAsk your manager or client: \"Is this how you check my work?\" Fix what they correct.\n\nThe three shares at the top of the sheet fill themselves.\n\n**Not working right now?** Interview one person in the role you want about one specific day (\"Open your calendar for last Tuesday: what did you produce, and who checked it?\") and fill the sheet for their week."},{"h":"03 Rules","a":"03-rules","text":"- **Your week, from your records.** An LLM listing your role's tasks gives you the average job, not yours.\n- **Bucket on the verifier and the data, not the verification type alone.** Automate when a rule catches mistakes and the data exists. Augment when a judge has to sign off. Human when it is unverifiable.\n- **If a script or formula can produce it, no LLM.**\n- **Company data stays on your machine.** In section 04, paste only what you are allowed to share, or a stand-in with the same shape."},{"h":"04 After Lecture 10: test one task 0 of 2 ready","a":"04-after-lecture-10-test-one-task-0-of-2-ready","text":"Pick one row you bucketed Automate or Augment. Find out what the model gets wrong, and what context it was missing.\n\n- Write its golden pair firstOne real input from that task and the output you would accept, before you run anything. [Lecture 10 notes, section 06](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06).\n- Run it bare, then with context, and score bothGPT-OSS 120B in the [GroqCloud playground](https://console.groq.com/playground): the input alone, then with the data the answer needs pasted above it. Score each out of 10 against your ideal output. Beside the row, write both scores and what was missing. [Missing context, or the habit of never saying no?](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s04)\n\nThe lecture's run: 1 out of 10 bare, 5 with the real tracks pasted in. Still missing: the tools."},{"h":"05 Share it 0 of 3 ready","a":"05-share-it-0-of-3-ready","text":"- Your copy of the sheetEvery atomic task from last week, your North Star and your guess. Share it so anyone with the link can view.\n- The tested rowFrom section 04: two scores and what was missing, in the same sheet.\n- One line: what surprised youWhich task, and why.\n\nOnce you're done, share it on the Discord. We go through them in the office hour.\n\nStuck on a row? Write your best guess and move on; bring it to the office hour. For a second opinion on a bucket, paste your tasks into [the delegation framework](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/delegation-framework/)."},{"h":"06 What happens next","a":"06-what-happens-next","text":"**[Practice set 2: Write the eval](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/).** Your score out of 10 in section 04 was by feel. Practice set 2 turns it into a check anyone could run the same way. Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\n\nThen we build the bridge that gets the missing context to the model with no pasting. That build starts from this sheet, so a sharp verifier and real data matter. \"Help the team\" gets you four weeks of rewriting this sheet. [Verifier first, data second: notes, section 10.](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes#s10)"}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-10-office-hour.md","title":"Office Hour: Your Sheet Is the Data","meta":"title: Office Hour: Your Sheet Is the Data\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour.html\nlecture: 10\nabout: Office hour · Tue 3 Nov 2026 · After Lectures 9 and 10 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1100","sections":[{"h":"Your sheet is the data","a":"your-sheet-is-the-data","text":"Why the Lecture 9 sheet is the same work AI labs now pay experts to do, and why the next step is writing the eval.\n\nMissed the office hour? This is what mattered from it, in ten minutes. It assumes you have started the [practice set](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set): your atomic tasks, each with success criteria, a verifier and a verification type, and one row tested on the model."},{"h":"01 What every lab needs from you","a":"01-what-every-lab-needs-from-you","text":"Every AI lab runs on three things: compute, algorithms and data. The labs buy compute and hire researchers for algorithms. The data that matters now, what experts know about real work, can only come from the people who do that work. That is you, and the sheet you are filling is where it starts.\n\n> Sketch: Three boxes. Compute: GPUs and data centres, which the labs buy. Algorithms: research, done by the labs' researchers. Data: what experts know, which comes from you, highlighted.\n>\n> Caption: The three pillars of an AI lab, from the office-hour whiteboard.\n\nThe labs say the open internet is largely used up (Ilya Sutskever, December 2024: \"we've achieved peak data\"), and the walled-off parts are being licensed (Reddit's deal with OpenAI is one). What is still scarce is **expert judgment on real tasks**: what a good answer is, and where the model's answer goes wrong.\n\nThat work has moved. A few years ago an expert wrote one prompt and one ideal answer. Models can now produce those themselves by searching. Where they still fail is **long tasks**: a lawyer working with a model for days, where it drifts five times along the way. Someone who knows the domain has to say where, and why."},{"h":"02 It is the sheet you are filling","a":"02-it-is-the-sheet-you-are-filling","text":"Expert-work listings on data platforms such as [Mercor](https://work.mercor.com/explore) read like this, for a lawyer: write a problem from a matter you worked on, with the facts and the reference answer; grade a model's draft against it; flag where the model states the law correctly and would still lose. The engineering listing is the same shape: grade model-written code, and flag where it passes review and would fail in production.\n\nPut that next to what you have already done:\n\n| The listing asks for | You did it as | Where you met it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A real problem from your work | An atomic task row | [Practice set, 02](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s02) |\n| What a correct answer must do | Success criteria | [Practice set, 02](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s02) |\n| The reference answer | The golden pair, written before the run | [Lecture 10, 06](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06) |\n| Grade the model's attempt | Your bare and with-context scores | [Practice set, 04](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04) |\n| Say what it got wrong and why | What was missing, beside the row | [Lecture 10, 04](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s04) |\n\nIn Lecture 10 we did exactly this on one question, \"Which track should I choose?\": ideal input, ideal output, the model's output, and the difference naming the cause. Repeat that for one task, 100 or 200 times, and it becomes a **golden dataset** for that task."},{"h":"03 Which rows matter","a":"03-which-rows-matter","text":"- Rule rows: same input, same output. Automate them with a script or a formula. No model needed, and no lab needs your help with them.\n- Judgment rows: this is where the value is. A rule cannot say whether the output is good; an expert can.\n- **Unverifiable** rows: leave them for now. They come back when we build agents.\n\nSo from your sheet, pick one or two Judgment rows that move your North Star. Those are the ones worth turning into data."},{"h":"04 Two ways to scale what you know","a":"04-two-ways-to-scale-what-you-know","text":"Either way, step one is the same: know the one narrow task, and how you would check it.\n\n1. **Build your own.** Fine-tune a small open model on one narrow, verifiable task. [Coinbase reported doing this](https://x.com/brian_armstrong/status/2109412046244913643) for fraud prevention and beating frontier models on that task at lower cost. Note the scope: one task, not \"replace Claude\". It is the bigger build, and you will fine-tune your first model later in this course.\n2. **Partner first.** Turn one or two Judgment tasks into a dataset and work with a lab or a data vendor. You learn the whole cycle, and get paid for it, before you build anything of your own.\n\nWhichever you choose, nobody can do either without the sheet: the task, its success criteria, its verifier, and real examples."},{"h":"05 From the Q&A","a":"05-from-the-qa","text":"- **\"My work is research. How do I verify that?\"** \"Research\" is not one task; it is closer to a North Star. Break it into atomic tasks (estimating the market size, for example) and write a verifier for each. The method is the one from [Assignment 1](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1): observe, hypothesise, test.\n- **\"I'm not a domain expert.\"** Ask why you were hired and not let go: the work that only you do well in your role. That is the expertise. Write those tasks down.\n- **\"Won't the labs stop needing experts?\"** No. The world keeps changing, and a model with no fresh data goes stale the way a genius from 50 years ago would be lost today. The bar for good work keeps rising too.\n- **\"Should I start collecting data now?\"** First talk to whoever would use it, and ask what shape they need. Data nobody asked for is effort with no buyer."},{"h":"06 Next: Practice set 2, write the eval","a":"06-next-practice-set-2-write-the-eval","text":"Look at the score you gave your tested row in Practice set 1, section 04. It was out of 10, and it was by feel. Give the same output to someone else and they might give it a 4 where you gave it a 7.\n\nA lab cannot use a score like that, and neither can you. The grader in that listing is paid to apply a check that two people would apply the same way. Writing that check is Practice set 2: the decision, the hard lines, what a 1 and a 5 look like, and who grades.\n\n[Open Practice set 2](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/)\n\nOn your own, about 45 minutes. Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov. It goes in a new tab of your Practice set 1 sheet.\n\nKeep this\n\nWhat a model still lacks is an expert's judgment on real tasks, written down: the task, what success means, how to check it, and real examples. That is your Lecture 9 sheet. The eval is what makes your judgment usable by anyone else."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-12-practice-set.md","title":"Practice Set 3: Write the Code Behind the Bench","meta":"title: Practice Set 3: Write the Code Behind the Bench\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp/practice-set.html\nlecture: 12\nabout: Practice set 3 · after Lecture 12 · Code track\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 304","sections":[{"h":"Write the code behind the bench","a":"write-the-code-behind-the-bench","text":"Due before Lecture 13, Fri 20 Nov 2026.\n\nThe brief\n\nOn the [Tool Bench](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-11-tool-calling-and-mcp/bench) you pressed buttons. Now write the code behind each one, on the same course sheet, then put the tool on the internet. Every step is in [the c8-tools repo](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#readme)."},{"h":"01 What you write","a":"01-what-you-write","text":"| On the bench | In the repo |   |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| The sheet | `get_lecture(number)` | Given |\n| The model | `ask_model(messages, tools)` | Given |\n| Your server | The bottom of `tool_call.py` | Part 1 |\n| The sheet's server | `mcp_server.py` | Part 2 |\n\n[Set up](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#setup) first: fork the repo, install, add your Groq key."},{"h":"02 Part 1: the tool call","a":"02-part-1-the-tool-call","text":"In `tool_call.py`. Run it after every step.\n\n1. [Ask without a card](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-1-ask-without-a-card)\n2. [Write the card](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-2-write-the-card)\n3. [Run the request](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-3-run-the-request)\n4. [Hand the row back](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-4-hand-the-row-back)\n5. [Let your code carry it](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-5-let-your-code-carry-it)\n6. [Test the card](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-6-test-the-card)\n\nDone when the same file answers a lecture question from the sheet and \"What is an API?\" on its own."},{"h":"03 Part 2: the MCP server","a":"03-part-2-the-mcp-server","text":"In `mcp_server.py`.\n\n1. [Move the function](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-1-move-the-function)\n2. [Write the card into the function](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-2-write-the-card-into-the-function)\n3. [Switch it on](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-3-switch-it-on)\n4. [Put it on the internet](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-4-put-it-on-the-internet)\n5. [Call it from anywhere](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#step-5-call-it-from-anywhere)\n\nDone when `fastmcp call` returns the Lecture 12 row from your address on the internet."},{"h":"04 Now your own data","a":"04-now-your-own-data","text":"[Do it once more](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/c8-tools#now-your-own-data) with your own Google Sheet, a CSV file, a Supabase table, or an API from this course: your function, your card, your five questions."},{"h":"05 Submit it","a":"05-submit-it","text":"- The link to your fork, with the five-question table filled in.\n- Your deployed address.\n\nOnce you're done, submit it on the LMS. We go through them in the office hour."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/lecture-14-office-hour.md","title":"Office Hour: Your RAG app, part by part.","meta":"title: Office Hour: Your RAG app, part by part.\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/office-hour\nfile: c8/module-2/lecture-14-building-rag-apps/office-hour.html\nlecture: 14\nabout: Office hour · Tue 24 Nov 2026 · After Lecture 14 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 51f573a\nwords: 1381","sections":[{"h":"Your RAG app, part by part.","a":"your-rag-app-part-by-part","text":"The room's doubts about the RAG app: what each part does, why build it at all, and why an off-topic question still came back with sources.\n\nMissed the office hour? This is what the room asked, and the answers. In [Lecture 13](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes) you derived RAG. In the 21 November practical you built a RAG app: it answers questions about a Porsche manual and cites the pages."},{"h":"01 How did it cut my manual?","a":"01-how-did-it-cut-my-manual","text":"Someone's app worked and cited the right pages. But in Supabase they could not see how the manual had been split. How did the app cut it into chunks?\n\nThe prompt from the practical asks for cuts by meaning: a chunk ends where a sentence and its context end. To see what your own build did, ask the coding tool that wrote it (Antigravity, for most of the room).\n\nIs there one right chunk size? No. Some manuals need two sections read together. With 10 or 15 documents, page 1 of one may connect to a page deep in another. So load a document you know well, and try sizes until the answers hold up.\n\n> There is no rule of thumb for chunks. Test on a document you know."},{"h":"02 Is RAG right for my research papers?","a":"02-is-rag-right-for-my-research-papers","text":"Someone wants each hard research paper turned into a simpler version: a newsletter, a blog, plain notes. Is RAG the right way?\n\nThe instructor asked back: can ChatGPT or Gemini not do that the moment you upload the file? RAG simplifies nothing. It cuts the text into chunks, indexes them, and hands the right ones to the LLM when a question comes.\n\nA paper is 50 or 100 pages at most. That fits inside the context window, so there is nothing to search for. Finding patterns across a huge pile of papers is a different job.\n\n> Ask first why RAG exists. Then ask whether your case has that problem.\n\nLecture 13 gave the reason: [the data stops fitting, or costs too much to paste](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-13-retrieval-augmented-generation/notes#s04)."},{"h":"03 A method, or a system?","a":"03-a-method-or-a-system","text":"The same learner had a longer aim. Keep every paper, so that later the app can say: this paper matters for the project you are on now.\n\nThe instructor said that fits the coming lecture better. It is about a **second brain**, where you draw on your own past know-how.\n\nThe learner guessed how the two relate: RAG is a method, and a second brain is a system. The instructor did not rule on it. He said a second brain is \"more to do with your memory\", and that you can decide better after the session. Hold the guess until Lecture 15."},{"h":"04 What each part does","a":"04-what-each-part-does","text":"From the room: \"Why does Groq exist?\" And in the practical's chat, some people thought gte-small came from Groq. So here is each part by its job.\n\n- **Groq serves the LLM.** Groq is a company that runs LLMs on its own chips, built for speed. It hosts a few open LLMs, ones anyone may download (GPT-OSS is one). Its free key needs no credit card. It is not Grok, the chatbot on X.\n- **gte-small turns text into numbers.** It is a small program of its own, listed on Hugging Face, and has nothing to do with Groq. It turns each chunk into a list of numbers that places its meaning.\n- **Supabase stores them.** Supabase hosts a Postgres database. **pgvector** is an extension you switch on inside Postgres, not a second database. With it, Postgres can store each list of numbers beside the chunk's text and page number, and find the lists closest to a question's.\n\n> Sketch: Two rows through the same parts. Top row, done once: the manual's chunks go into gte-small, which turns them into numbers, and the numbers are stored in Supabase, which is Postgres with pgvector. Bottom row, done for every question: the question goes into the same gte-small, Supabase finds the closest chunks, and those chunks go with the question to the LLM on Groq, which writes the answer with its sources.\n>\n> Caption: Three parts, three jobs. The same gte-small handles the chunks once and every question after.\n\nWhen you ask a question, the same gte-small turns it into numbers. pgvector finds the closest chunks. The server sends them, with the question, to the LLM on Groq, which answers and cites the pages.\n\nThe build guide for the car manual app names another small embedding model, all-MiniLM-L6-v2. Whichever one your app uses, the job is the same, and it does not come from Groq.\n\nDoes pgvector do the chunking? No. It cuts nothing and converts nothing. It stores and it searches.\n\n**Can I run the LLM without Groq?**\n\nYes. Ollama is a program that runs open LLMs on your own machine. Several open LLMs carry the Apache 2.0 licence, which allows commercial use."},{"h":"05 Why build it at all?","a":"05-why-build-it-at-all","text":"Someone had uploaded their car's manuals to Claude and asked questions there. It felt more interactive. So why build the app?\n\nThe instructor agreed with the facts. RAG came about when context windows were small. Today's LLMs can read a document of about 1,400 to 1,500 pages and cite from it. Not quite as well as RAG, he said, but well enough. The companies that make LLMs are racing to make RAG unnecessary.\n\n\"So this matters when my data is much bigger?\" Yes, and for one more reason. An app needs its own way to store its files and find the right part of them. If that lives inside one company's LLM, your app depends on that LLM. If it is yours, you can swap the LLM and keep everything else.\n\n> The LLM can be swapped. The storing and the finding stay yours."},{"h":"06 Best biryani, with sources","a":"06-best-biryani-with-sources","text":"One learner asked the Porsche app: \"Where is the best biryani?\" It answered, and listed sources from the manual, one about battery coding. Typing only \"biryani\" gave the same sources. Why?\n\n- **The search always hands something back.** It returns a set number of chunks, called **top-k** (the instructor guessed 6 to 8 for this build). It returns the k closest even when none is close, so here they are still car chunks.\n- **Nothing told the LLM to stay on the manual.** The manual is added context, and the LLM underneath still knows about food. The fix is a rule in the instruction, called a **guardrail**: answer only questions about the manual.\n\n> Sketch: The question Where is the best biryani goes into the search. The search always returns its top k chunks, so it returns the closest chunks it has, and none of them is about food. They still show up as sources.\n>\n> Caption: Closest does not mean close. The search has no way to say \"nothing here\".\n\nAnother learner's app found the right answer to a test question but buried it in too much text. Three fixes:\n\n- **Lower top-k.** The answer gets shorter, but you can lose chunks that belong together (tyre pressure monitoring and fuel efficiency, in the instructor's example).\n- **Add one more step.** Ask the LLM to pick the exact answer out of what the search returned.\n- **Sharpen the question first.** Add precision and context to what the user typed before the search runs.\n\nKeep this\n\nGroq serves the LLM, gte-small turns text into numbers, and Supabase stores and searches them with pgvector. A chat app can read one file for you. An app you build keeps its own storing and finding, so you can swap the LLM. The search always returns its closest chunks, so an off-topic question still gets sources until you add a rule."},{"h":"07 Before Lecture 15","a":"07-before-lecture-15","text":"Build the Porsche app and run the test questions shared with the practical. You find a system's limits only by building it.\n\nThen bring the guess from section 03. Lecture 15 starts from the search you built and asks what search still cannot find. A separate office hour will take up how to choose the tools you build with.\n\n[Open Lecture 15](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-15-building-a-second-brain/)\n\nYou can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.Next: Lecture 15, building a second brain."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/practice-set-2-write-the-eval.md","title":"Practice Set 2: Write the Eval","meta":"title: Practice Set 2: Write the Eval\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/\nfile: c8/module-2/practice-set-2-write-the-eval/index.html\nabout: Practice set 2 · After Lectures 9 and 10 and the 3 Nov office hour · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 255","sections":[{"h":"Write the eval","a":"write-the-eval","text":"Turn the score you gave by feel into a check anyone could run the same way.\n\nOn your own, about 45 minutes. Finish it before Lecture 11, Thu 12 Nov.\n\nThe brief\n\nTake the task you tested in [Practice set 1, section 04](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/practice-set#s04) and write its eval: the decision it serves, the inputs, the hard lines, what a 1 and a 5 look like, and who grades. The exercise walks you through it.\n\n[Start the exercise](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/office-hour/)"},{"h":"01 Why","a":"01-why","text":"- **[Lecture 9](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/notes):** a model can take over a task as far as its output can be checked.\n- **[Lecture 10](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/notes#s06):** you tested one task and scored it out of 10, by feel. Someone else could give the same reply a 4 where you gave it a 7.\n- **[Office hour](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-10-tool-calling-and-mcp/office-hour):** a written-down grade is exactly what labs pay experts for, on [Mercor](https://work.mercor.com/explore) and elsewhere.\n\nThe eval is that check, written down. From Lecture 11 on, every build ends with \"did it get better?\", and this is how you will know."},{"h":"02 Do it 0 of 3 ready","a":"02-do-it-0-of-3-ready","text":"- [Work through the exercise](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/lecture-09-llms-from-first-principles/office-hour/)Three parts, about 30 minutes. Write it yourself, not with an LLM.\n- Download your eval sheet\"Download as spreadsheet\" at the end. Fill it in Google Sheets or Excel.\n- Re-score your tested task with itHard lines first, then each quality from 1 to 5, beside your old scores."},{"h":"03 Share it","a":"03-share-it","text":"Once you're done, share it on the Discord. We go through them in the office hour."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/what-to-build-aarav-example.md","title":"Aarav, Discovery to MVP","meta":"title: Aarav, Discovery to MVP\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example\nfile: c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example.html\nabout: What to build · Discovery to MVP · Worked example\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 2020","sections":[{"h":"Aarav, from observation to MVP plan","a":"aarav-from-observation-to-mvp-plan","text":"One full run of the Discovery to MVP coach, condensed. Read what each phase produced, then run it on your own observation.\n\nThe one thing to keep\n\nEvery phase must produce evidence or surface an assumption. If it does neither, it is theatre. Prove the outcome by hand with real people before any software exists: if humans can deliver it, you know what to automate; if they cannot, software would not have saved you."},{"h":"01 How the Coach Works","a":"01-how-the-coach-works","text":"1. **Entry gate.** Do you have a real observed problem, not a solution you like? If not, run the [OPT Coach](https://github.com/Siddhant-Goswami/100x-LLM/blob/main/prompts/OPT_COACH.md) first.\n2. **Discovery, phases 1 to 5.** Observation, subject, evidence audit, causal chain, a hypothesis that can fail.\n3. **Stress test, phase 6.** Rank the riskiest assumptions, a Mom Test interview, three synthetic personas.\n4. **Manual MVP, phase 7.** A 21-day pilot by hand: who, what, price, metrics, success criteria, kill conditions.\n5. **Output.** A discovery document and a manual MVP plan. About 90 to 150 minutes, one question at a time.\n\nThe one rule: no simulated outcomes\n\nThe coach simulates a practice interview and practice personas, because those teach a skill. It never simulates the result of your pilot. A fake \"Cohort 1 report\" is fluent fiction built from your own assumptions. Twenty-one real days teach more than twenty-one simulations.\n\n[Run itThe skill file](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1whXRBIFPSQn8iSIy5JIrVfZEHGmgVGX7/view?usp=sharing) [Watch itAarav's full chat with the coach](https://claude.ai/share/00c923f0-4a34-4892-90dc-714eff1b44b2)"},{"h":"02 The User: Aarav","a":"02-the-user-aarav","text":"26, Pune. B.Tech from a tier-2 college, three years as a business analyst at an IT services company. Not non-technical, not deeply technical. It is 12:38 am and he has spent eleven minutes on one resume bullet that describes work AI already does.\n\nHe has tried everything: ChatGPT, Claude, Cursor, n8n, agent tutorials. He has 11 saved startup ideas, 3 half-finished projects, 19 bookmarked tutorials and a folder called \"AI Projects Final\" with nothing final in it. The loop: **anxiety → tutorial → tool → idea → overthinking → more anxiety.**\n\nHis problem is not laziness or information. It is **high ambition, low clarity**. He does not need another tool or 100 ideas. He needs one real workflow worth solving, and proof he can build for it."},{"h":"03 The Discovery Document","a":"03-the-discovery-document","text":""},{"h":"1 · Observation","a":"1--observation","text":"Early-career professionals in urban India arrive AI-aware and motivated, but fail at **selection, scoping and first proof**. They see AI at the category level (\"sales automation\") and skip defining the user, the repeated task, the bottleneck, the inputs, the outputs and the success metric. Every project starts vague and collapses back into consumption: many saved ideas, zero shipped proof."},{"h":"2 · Subject","a":"2--subject","text":"| Who | 22 to 30, urban India, graduates in early roles: analyst, junior dev, marketer, ops, freelancer. |\n| --- | --- |\n| Behaviour | 3 to 7 AI videos a week, 5 to 15 saves, 1 to 2 new tools. Shipping close to zero. |\n| Money | ₹4L to ₹20L salary. Would pay ₹15,000 to ₹40,000 for clarity, structure and proof, not content. |\n| Feeling | Late, scattered, unconverted. Buys after a rupture: a peer lands an AI role, a manager asks him to build. |\n| Constraints | Cannot quit, cannot spend a year on fundamentals, needs visible progress fast. |\n| Stage | 3 to 9 months in the loop. |\n\n**Smallest win in 60 to 90 days:** one specific workflow, mapped, its bottleneck found, a small AI-assisted version tested on real inputs, presented as a demo and case study.\n\n**Out of scope:** passive learners with no urgency, and validated founders who need scale, not diagnosis."},{"h":"3 · Evidence audit","a":"3--evidence-audit","text":"| Bucket | Evidence | Confidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Direct | Students fail at selection and scoping; capstone quality tracks problem clarity; structure and reminders raise submissions. | High |\n| Adjacent | AI awareness outpaces applied skill (Pluralsight, LinkedIn); workplace adoption is messy (Microsoft Work Trend Index). | Medium-high |\n| Passive | Vocabulary spreads faster than artifacts; 84% use or plan to use AI tools, agent use still a minority. | Medium |\n| Inference | **Load-bearing:** one diagnosed workflow plus one working proof is worth paying for. | Low to medium |"},{"h":"4 · Causal chain","a":"4--causal-chain","text":"1. AI changes fast → pressure to upgrade, urgency without direction.\n2. The internet rewards novelty → option overload.\n3. **No method to diagnose a workflow → he cannot filter options; every idea stays vague. Intervention point.**\n4. Vague idea → no boundary for the build → scope collapse.\n5. Building feels risky → back to tutorials → motion without proof.\n6. No artifact → self-image stays \"learner\".\n7. Any trigger (a peer's demo, a manager's ask) restarts the loop.\n\nOnly link 3 is addressed. Saying which links you will not touch is part of the answer."},{"h":"5 · Hypothesis","a":"5--hypothesis","text":"Falsifiable\n\nWe believe AI-aware early-career professionals with no shipped proof are stuck because they lack a method to turn broad AI possibility into one scoped workflow. If we provide a workflow diagnosis system and a guided build sprint, **at least 60% of active students will map one real workflow, build a working AI-assisted version and present a credible demo within 90 days.**\n\n**What would prove it wrong, and what it does not claim**\n\nWrong if\n\n- Fewer than 40% produce a credible demo in 90 days.\n- Students finish diagnosis but still do not build: execution is the real bottleneck.\n- Students do not see the proof as a career win.\n- Students mainly want jobs or income, not proof.\n- Students cannot find real workflows even with guidance.\n\nDoes not claim\n\nJobs, placements or revenue; that one proof ends the anxiety; that diagnosis removes execution difficulty; that coders, no-coders and founders need the same build help."},{"h":"6 · Stress test scorecard","a":"6--stress-test-scorecard","text":"| Assumption | What the interview and personas said | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Selection and scoping is the main bottleneck | The interviewee stopped at defining the user, not at building. Two of three personas confirmed. | Validated |\n| A rupture event turns a browser into a buyer | The rupture (a peer's LinkedIn post) led to more consumption, not a purchase. | Weakened: needs a visible path right after the rupture |\n| One workflow proof is worth the program | Hunger for completion is real; \"one workflow proof\" sounds thin. | Weakened: package it as a portfolio artifact |"},{"h":"7 · Top risks going into the MVP","a":"7--top-risks-going-into-the-mvp","text":"- Students diagnose and still do not build. Then the product needs a heavier build sprint.\n- The proof does not land as a career win without repackaging.\n- Aarav avoids talking to real users because it feels riskier than another video. If fewer than 6 finish their conversations by Day 5, the thesis is revised."},{"h":"04 The Manual MVP Plan","a":"04-the-manual-mvp-plan","text":"21 days, 10 users, ₹21,000. No software built."},{"h":"Who","a":"who","text":"Past 100xEngineers students and first and second degree connections. One screening question:\n\n\"Tell me about the last AI project you seriously tried to build. What triggered it, what did you do first, where exactly did you stop, and what did you do after?\"\n\nAccept only a specific stalled attempt. Reject \"I've been exploring AI\", validated founders and passive learners. Screen 12 to 14 to land 10."},{"h":"What, day by day","a":"what-day-by-day","text":"| Days | Action | Output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | Paid intake and screening | 10 paid students |\n| 1 to 2 | Diagnosis call, then rank 5 to 7 real workflows on frequency, pain, access, AI fit, proof speed | One workflow, one user |\n| 3 to 5 | Talk to 2 people in that workflow. No pitching, only study the current process | Real evidence |\n| 6 to 7 | Map the workflow, scope the smallest AI version. **Gate:** one user, task, bottleneck, input, output, metric | One-page build brief |\n| 8 to 14 | Build sprint, code or no-code. Works on 3 realistic inputs | Testable prototype |\n| 15 to 16 | Show it to 1 to 2 real users; write a before-and-after proof statement | Proof of value |\n| 17 to 21 | Case study, 3 to 5 minute Loom, review, Demo Day | \"AI Workflow Proof 01\" folder |\n\nMissed the Day 7 gate: a 20-minute rescue call (narrow to a weekly task, take a standard archetype, or step back to observer). Still unscoped by Day 9: out of the sprint."},{"h":"Price","a":"price","text":"₹21,000 upfront. Full refund until Day 2 if no viable workflow is found. No refunds after Day 3. Did every step and still no proof: 7 more days of support, free."},{"h":"Success criteria, fixed before Day 1","a":"success-criteria-fixed-before-day-1","text":"| Measure, at n = 10 | Weak | Mixed | Strong |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Complete a proof by Day 21 | < 5 | 5 to 6 | 7+ |\n| Cross the Day 7 gate and reach a prototype | < 6 | 6 to 7 | 8+ |\n| \"Would you show this to an employer or client?\" Yes | < 5 | 5 to 6 | 8+ |\n| Name proof, not placement, as the reason at intake | < 5 | 5 to 6 | 7+ |\n| Leave Day 6 with a scoped workflow | < 7 | 7 to 8 | 9+ |\n\n**Strong** (3+ strong, none weak): run Cohort 2 at ₹35,000. **Mixed:** fix the phase that broke, rerun with 10. **Weak** (2+ weak): stop and go back to the evidence audit."},{"h":"Kill conditions, checked during the pilot","a":"kill-conditions-checked-during-the-pilot","text":"| Tripwire | When | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Fewer than 7 paid | Before Day 1 | The offer is not compelling. Do not start. |\n| Fewer than 6 log 2 real conversations | Day 5 | The bottleneck is avoiding real users. |\n| Fewer than 6 scoped after rescue | Day 9 | The diagnosis method is weak. |\n| Fewer than 5 submit any prototype | Day 14 | Execution is the bottleneck. Thesis wrong. |\n\nIf one trips, stop and write it down. Do not improvise a rescue; it is the most useful data the pilot produces.\n\n**Cohort 2: is the founder the product?**\n\nTwo to three weeks later, a senior mentor runs the same playbook. The founder trains them, reviews recordings and attends Demo Day silently, but never rescues a student. If completions drop more than 20%, the founder was carrying the outcome: turn what they did into scripts and rubrics, and rerun. Cohort 1 must hand over every template, script and rubric it used.\n\n**What the pilot is explicitly not doing**\n\n- No new software, no automation of delivery, no course, no platform.\n- No students without a recent stalled attempt; no passive learners or validated founders.\n- No promises of jobs, income or revenue.\n- No building before a scoped workflow; no skipping user conversations.\n- No dashboards, logins, databases, agents or full SaaS. No polish.\n- No prototype counts without user feedback; no case study without a working demo.\n- No moving the success criteria once the pilot starts, and no scaling before n = 10 produces evidence.\n\nThe sentence at the end\n\nWhatever the outcome, this pilot will tell us whether 10 genuine Aaravs will pay to go from AI confusion to one real workflow proof in 21 days, or whether the real bottleneck is deeper than workflow diagnosis."},{"h":"05 Now Yours","a":"05-now-yours","text":"1. [Run the coach on your own observation](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1whXRBIFPSQn8iSIy5JIrVfZEHGmgVGX7/view?usp=sharing)\n2. Write your hypothesis that can fail\n3. Run your manual MVP by hand for Assignment 1\n\nAarav's pilot is a founder's plan with 10 paying users. Assignment 1 is smaller: 2 to 5 real people, by hand, in 21 days. Keep the same discipline: criteria and kill conditions written before Day 1."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/what-to-build-assignment-1.md","title":"Assignment 1: First Proof","meta":"title: Assignment 1: First Proof\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1\nfile: c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1.html\nabout: Assignment 1 (mandatory) · Week 0 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 1349","sections":[{"h":"First Proof","a":"first-proof","text":"21 days. One observation from your own life. One link a stranger can use.\n\nFollows Live Lecture 01: Idea to App WorkshopDue Fri 9 Oct 2026\n\nThe brief\n\nFix your North Star, write an observation from your own lived experience, run discovery-to-mvp-coach to a falsifiable hypothesis and a manual MVP plan, publish a tracker for that plan, then deliver the value by hand to 2 to 5 real people and write down the input, process and output you used."},{"h":"01 Why this exists","a":"01-why-this-exists","text":"- **Problem first.** Most products fail because someone built a real solution to a problem nobody had. Investigate the problem, attack your own assumptions, and run a cheap experiment before you build anything.\n- **Aarav was a proxy.** The skill is finding the one workflow in your own experience worth solving, proving part of its value by hand, and only then deciding what to automate. Weeks 1 to 8 teach the automation; they do not make sense without knowing what to automate.\n- **Manual on purpose.** The output is not an app. It is a process you have run once by hand, with a number attached, and a hypothesis you now know to be truer or falser than it was."},{"h":"02 The five steps","a":"02-the-five-steps","text":"1. **Fix the North Star.** Six-month timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing that bridges them. Test: could someone else verify it on the date? Absolute number, or relative to a baseline you have measured. One goal, not four.\n2. **Write the observation.** One real person, one real situation, at the depth of what is on their desk at 12:38 AM, what they do, what breaks, how long it takes. Your lived experience, not a borrowed story, with no solution in it. It points toward your North Star; it is where the North Star's specific thing comes from.\n3. **Run discovery-to-mvp-coach.** Install the [skill file](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1whXRBIFPSQn8iSIy5JIrVfZEHGmgVGX7/view) (Settings, Customise, Skills, upload) and invoke it with `/discovery-to-mvp-coach`. Paste the observation. Budget 60 to 90 minutes. Answer every question yourself and add assumptions the skill missed. Do the Phase 6 simulated interviews properly and keep the 4 to 5 questions it grades you on. Save both outputs: the discovery document and the MVP plan.\n4. **Publish the tracker.** Paste the MVP plan into Lovable (free credits on a new account) or any AI builder and ask for a tracker of every line item in the 21-day pilot. Preview, then Publish. The link must open for a stranger without your help. Note what breaks: it is material for the next two weeks.\n5. **Run the manual MVP and write the process.** Deliver the value by hand to 2 to 5 real people in the first 14 days, asking Mom Test questions: their life, their past, specifics, emotions. Fill the card in section 03 for what you actually did. Update the tracker daily."},{"h":"03 Input, process, output","a":"03-input-process-output","text":"Every piece of software reduces to these three words.\n\n| Field | What to write | Stand-up example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Input | The raw material, its format, where it comes from, how often it arrives. Attach one real sample. | Messy transcript of the weekly Zoom stand-up, one file per call |\n| Output | What the recipient gets, its format, who receives it, and the decision it lets them make. | One-page summary of last week's calls, emailed to the founder, brief enough to act on |\n| Process | Every step you took by hand to turn the input into the output, in order, including judgment calls and how you made them. | Paste transcript into an LLM; prompt for summary; format; send by email; when two updates conflict, check Jira and keep the later one |\n| Time and cost today | Minutes per run before your process, and what an hour of that person costs. | Founder or assistant, about 3 hours a week |\n| Time and cost after | Minutes per run with your manual process, measured on at least two real runs. | 15 minutes to review and sign off |\n| Metric | Revenue increased or cost and time saved. One number, with its unit. | Roughly $300 a week saved per person at $100 an hour |\n| Cannot yet be done by hand | Steps you could not perform manually and why. These are your open questions for weeks 1 to 8. | Pulling from three systems with conflicting values |\n\nDo not automate anything on this card during the 21 days. The card is the input to Week 1, where the module turns it into a system: deterministic parts that do exactly what they are told, probabilistic parts that use an LLM, and parts that stay human."},{"h":"04 Rules of the build","a":"04-rules-of-the-build","text":"- **Manual first.** No agents, no automation, no product. Use an LLM by hand if it helps you do a step, and write down the prompt as part of the process.\n- **Your observation, not a borrowed one.** \"People want to automate workflows so I will build five agents\" is rejected on sight.\n- **The hypothesis must be able to fail.** Subject, problem, cause, intervention, a number, a deadline, and the result that would prove you wrong.\n- **Real people, not only the simulation.** Phase 6 in the skill is practice. It does not count toward the user research conversations.\n- **One cycle at a time.** Around 90% of the room may change idea by Week 2. Run one observation to the end of validation before starting the next. If it dies, that is a result: log it and rerun the skill on the next one.\n- **LLM assistance.** The skill, summaries of The Mom Test, and Lovable are the tools of the exercise. Do not generate the observation with an LLM: it is the one input that is in no training set, and the only part that is yours."},{"h":"05 What to submit","a":"05-what-to-submit","text":"| Deliverable | What it must show |\n| --- | --- |\n| North Star | All four elements in one sentence. An outsider could verify it on the date. |\n| Observation | One real person, one real situation, real numbers, no solution. Aligned to the North Star's specific thing. |\n| Discovery document | From discovery-to-mvp-coach: compressed observation, subject, evidence audit, causal chain, and a hypothesis with a number, a deadline and at least one falsifier. |\n| MVP plan | Who you recruit, what you deliver, what you measure, what you charge (optional). Manual; no software. |\n| Published tracker link | Opens for a stranger without help. Every 21-day line item present. Shows daily updates. |\n| Validation log | 2 to 5 real conversations. For each: who, when, the 4 to 5 questions asked, what you heard, and one contact artefact (calendar invite, message thread or call recording). |\n| Input-Process-Output card | Section 03 filled from a process you ran by hand at least once, with before and after time and the one metric. |\n| Verdict | Two lines: is the hypothesis truer or falser than on day 0, and what you are changing. |\n\nSubmit through the assignment form on the LMS. Share your tracker link early in the assignments channel: the sooner it is shared, the sooner it gets feedback. Stuck on the North Star, the observation, or testing a hypothesis by hand? Open a support ticket."},{"h":"06 After submission","a":"06-after-submission","text":"Week 1 begins with your Input-Process-Output card, not with a hypothetical. Over the next two weeks the tracker gets a database and a backend built from first principles; over Weeks 1 to 8 the process on your card becomes a system of deterministic and probabilistic parts. None of that lands without a process you have already run by hand.\n\n> Cohort zero was 10 people, Rs 21,000 and 21 days with no software, testing one hypothesis with a 60% bar. Nine of ten shipped a credible demo. That was the first proof."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/what-to-build-idea-to-app-notes.md","title":"Workshop Notes: Idea to App","meta":"title: Workshop Notes: Idea to App\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes\nfile: c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes.html\nabout: Post-read notes · Idea to App workshop · Week 0\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 3316","sections":[{"h":"Observation before idea","a":"observation-before-idea","text":"A 21-day journey from discovery to a minimum viable product. What was said, in the order the room heard it. Read once end to end, then come back to sections 03, 09 and 11 while you do Assignment 1.\n\nFri 18 Sep 2026Instructor: Siddhant GoswamiBoth tracks"},{"h":"01 The room: 1,400 years of experience","a":"01-the-room-1400-years-of-experience","text":"> Sketch: Three cards. 66% working professionals, median 8 plus years. Plus 68% from outside tech, 57 industries. Equals about 1,400 years of experience in one room. Your industry is the raw material.\n>\n> Caption: The room, added up. Applied AI is industry agnostic; what you know is the input.\n\n- **The Launchpad numbers.** 66% working professionals, median 8+ years of experience. 68% from outside tech, across 57 industries. About 1,400 years of experience in one room.\n- **Applied AI is industry agnostic.** Everyone in your field says \"I cannot build software\". The first one to codify their judgment and scale it wins.\n- **That experience is the raw material** for the next eight weeks."},{"h":"02 A North Star has four elements","a":"02-a-north-star-has-four-elements","text":"> Sketch: A North Star drawn as a bridge. On the left a person, labelled role or identity: AI real estate consultant. On the right a box, $15,000 a month, labelled level, income or scale. Above, a calendar card: December 2026, the timeline. The bridge between them is the specific thing: automating lead gen, the element most people left out.\n>\n> Caption: Four elements. The specific thing is the bridge from who you are to the number.\n\nTwo North Stars were pulled apart on the whiteboard. Kaushik: by December 2026, a dental tourism company running fully on AI agents except the patient calls, at $500 a month recurring. A C6 student: by December 2026, an AI real estate consultant in the UAE automating lead generation for agencies, at $15,000 a month.\n\n- **Timeline.** A date, six months out. A North Star for this cohort, not for your life.\n- **Role and identity.** What people will call you. \"Leading an AI-powered engineering workflow\" is a task, and begs the question of who leads it.\n- **Level, income or scale.** The quantity you will have on the date. Money, a designation, an appraisal or a measured impact, as long as it is a number.\n- **The specific thing.** The one workflow that bridges the role to the number. For the UAE consultant, automating lead gen. Almost everyone left this out.\n\nThree from chat, reviewed live:\n\n- **Anand.** Timeline and a task, no identity. \"100x productivity\" with no baseline to measure against.\n- **Prateek.** \"Build the top 5 AI use cases at my organisation.\" A constraint (a quarter of a 75-person team using them) in place of an outcome, and a specific thing that was still generic.\n- **Saiteja.** Passed all four: growth marketer or indie maker, marketing the AEO tool he already built, to 1K MRR.\n\n> The North Star defines the goal. It never explains the route."},{"h":"03 The mom test for a North Star","a":"03-the-mom-test-for-a-north-star","text":"> Sketch: The mom test. A person in the middle: mom, on the date. Left card: 1K MRR, an invoice, verifiable. Right card: 100x productivity, 100x of what, a wish. Below: an absolute number, or a baseline you already measured.\n>\n> Caption: Could someone who does not know the jargon check it on the date?\n\n- **Send the sentence to your mother**, or anyone older who does not know the jargon. On the date, can she verify whether it happened?\n- **Saiteja: yes.** 1,000 MRR is a bank statement or an invoice.\n- **Anand: no.** Nobody can check \"100x productivity\", because there is no number for today.\n- **So the scale is absolute**, or relative to a baseline you have already measured and written down. \"100x better\" with no baseline is a wish.\n- **It may evolve, never stay vague.** When you reach it (Saiteja may hit 1K MRR next month), rewrite it."},{"h":"04 The path is a proof, not a plan","a":"04-the-path-is-a-proof-not-a-plan","text":"> Sketch: A dashed line from today to the North Star. A short solid coral step from today: the proof, the first $10 or $20, 0.1% of the value already real. The rest of the line: the plan comes later.\n>\n> Caption: Research works by building evidence. Stage 2 is the first piece of it.\n\n- **No standard path exists.** Build the app, build agents, take another course: everyone has their own answer. Module 2 borrows from research, which works by building evidence.\n- **Stage 2 is a proof:** the smallest demonstration that 0.1% of the value already exists. For Saiteja, the first $10 or $20 his tool earns, not a marketing strategy."},{"h":"05 Week 0, and why the order changed","a":"05-week-0-and-why-the-order-changed","text":"> Sketch: A dashed zone labelled week 0, what to build, holds three cards: discovery, what is worth building; manual MVP, what it looks like by hand; process, what to automate. An arrow down to weeks 1 to 8, how to build.\n>\n> Caption: Three questions before any building. Until C6, the capstones failed here, not at the code.\n\n- **Weeks 1 to 8 teach how to build:** LLMs, making them trustworthy, harnesses, loops, agents, fine-tuning.\n- **Week 0 answers three questions first.** What is worth building (discovery). What the manual version looks like (the MVP). What in it should be automated (the process).\n- **Six cohorts of evidence.** Until C6 the capstones were low impact. Students could build. They did not know what to build.\n- **So no artificial problem solved live while you watch.** You solve yours. No clear North Star yet? Fill the Launchpad form and ask for a review."},{"h":"06 Aarav at 12.38 AM: what an observation looks like","a":"06-aarav-at-1238-am-what-an-observation-looks-like","text":"> Sketch: Aarav's loop, drawn as a clockwise ring: anxiety, tutorial, tool, idea, overthinking, back to anxiety. In the middle: 12.38 AM, high ambition, low clarity. 11 ideas, 19 tutorials, 0 final.\n>\n> Caption: Not laziness, not lack of information. He needs one real workflow worth solving.\n\n*It is 12.38 AM in Pune. Aarav is at his desk with three tabs open: a YouTube video called Top 10 AI Agent Projects to Build in 2026, an n8n automation tutorial, and his own resume. He has been staring at one bullet point for 11 minutes.*\n\n- **Who.** 26, Tier 2 BTech, three years a business analyst at a mid-size IT services company. Neither non-technical nor deeply technical.\n- **What he has tried.** ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Cursor, n8n, every agent tutorial. 11 startup ideas in Notion, 3 finished, 19 tutorials bookmarked, a folder called AI Projects Final with nothing final in it.\n- **At work.** His manager wants \"AI productivity\" because the client asked. Everyone makes a deck. Nobody knows what to build.\n- **The diagnosis.** High ambition, low clarity. Anxiety, tutorial, tool, idea, overthinking, more anxiety. He needs one real workflow worth solving.\n\n- **It came from sitting next to Aarav**, day after day. It is not in any model's training data. What you have lived and nobody else has is the only moat left.\n- **An observation** is a detailed record of a real person, from your own experience, with no solution in it. \"People want to automate workflows, so I will build five agents\" is rejected on day one.\n- **Your North Star tells you where to look.** Saiteja's says: sit with growth marketers and watch what breaks."},{"h":"07 Story to subject to evidence to causal chain","a":"07-story-to-subject-to-evidence-to-causal-chain","text":"> Sketch: Four cards left to right with arrows: story, Aarav at 12.38 AM; subject, who, where, how they behave; evidence, what already confirms it; causal chain, why, step by step, as assumptions.\n>\n> Caption: The four moves the coach makes on your observation.\n\nA story can be wrong. The discovery-to-mvp-coach skill asks questions for 60 to 90 minutes and turns it into something checkable. Four moves, on Aarav's own document:\n\n- **Compress the observation** into a paragraph about a segment: ambitious early-career professionals in urban India, AI-aware and motivated, who fail at selection, scoping and the first proof.\n- **Define the subject.** Where they live, their role, their behaviour: AI content daily in bursts, 5 to 15 posts saved a week, one or two new tools tried a day. Demographic, behavioural, financial, psychological and structural markers.\n- **Audit the evidence.** What already confirms what you saw? Passive consumption turning active across every cohort; MOOCs with very high enrolment and very low completion.\n- **Write the causal chain**, labelled as assumptions. AI changes fast, so urgency without direction. The feed rewards novelty, so overload. No mental model to filter, so every idea feels possible. Stacked: the builder's anxiety."},{"h":"08 A hypothesis must be able to fail","a":"08-a-hypothesis-must-be-able-to-fail","text":"> Sketch: A bar from 0% to 100% of 250 students within 90 days. A coral dashed line at 60%. Left of it, shaded coral: the belief is false. Right of it, shaded green: it holds, for now. A hypothesis names what kills it.\n>\n> Caption: Aarav's hypothesis, with its kill line drawn in.\n\n- **A hypothesis** is your belief about what the problem is, how you would solve it, and how you would know you were wrong. The last clause is the point: maybe the observation is bias.\n- **Aarav's.** Ambitious early-career professionals in urban India who are AI-aware but have no shipped proof are stuck because they lack a method to turn broad AI possibility into one workflow worth solving. With a structured workflow diagnosis and a guided sprint, at least 60% of active students will find one real user, map one repeated workflow, build a working AI-assisted version and present a credible demo within 90 days.\n- **What kills it.** Fewer than 60% of 250. Under 40% with a credible demo. Students finish diagnosis but still do not build. They want placement, not proof (a different buyer). They cannot find real workflows even with guidance (the gap is upstream).\n- **Run the skill** rather than the template alone. Subject, problem, cause, intervention, a number, a deadline, and what kills it."},{"h":"09 Stress test: the Mom Test, simulated","a":"09-stress-test-the-mom-test-simulated","text":"> Sketch: An interview. On the left a person, you; on the right a person, Rohan, 27, simulated. Between them four rules, each crossing out one thing for another: your idea becomes their life, the future becomes the past, generalities become specifics, opinions become emotions.\n>\n> Caption: The Mom Test's four rules, practised on a simulated person before the real ones.\n\n- **Phase 6 of the skill forces the practice** most students skip. It lists every assumption so far (add your own), asks your confidence in each, then plays a person from your segment. In Aarav's run: Rohan Verma, 27, business analyst, an hour of AI content a day. You interview him.\n- **The rules,** from Rob Fitzpatrick's *The Mom Test*. Read the whole book.\n\n- **Talk about their life, not your idea.**\n- **Ask about the past, not the future.**\n- **Listen for specifics, not generalities.**\n- **Dig on emotions, not opinions.**\n\n- **Then it grades you:** strong moves, missed questions, what to listen for. You leave with four or five questions for a real conversation.\n- **Practice, not a replacement.** Real interviews are mandatory: 2 to 5 people minimum."},{"h":"10 Cohort zero: 10 people, Rs 21,000, 21 days","a":"10-cohort-zero-10-people-rs-21000-21-days","text":"> Sketch: Ten circles for cohort zero. Nine are filled green, one is empty. A coral dashed line after the sixth circle marks the bar, 6 of 10. Above: 10 people, Rs 21,000 each, 21 days, no software. Below: 9 of 10 presented a credible demo.\n>\n> Caption: Cohort zero tested the hypothesis by hand before any software existed.\n\n- **Phase 7 is a manual MVP plan:** who you recruit, what you deliver, what you measure, what you charge.\n- **The one on screen was 100xEngineers' own.** Ten people, Rs 21,000 each, 21 days, no software, to test the hypothesis above. The bar was 60%. Nine of ten presented a credible demo. One later raised Rs 1 crore from NVIDIA and Startup India. The 3-month and then 6-month programmes grew from what those ten said.\n- **The counter-example.** Earlier workshops handed out five trending ideas to paste into a builder. It produced a pile of AI slop and value for nobody. Two of the instructor's businesses failed before this one for lack of a standard process. This is that process.\n\n> If you cannot test it by hand, do not build it. Do it by hand once, then decide what to automate."},{"h":"11 Input, process, output","a":"11-input-process-output","text":"> Sketch: Input, process, output. Input: the messy stand-up transcript. Process: paste into an LLM, summarise, format, send. Output: a summary you can decide on, by email. From next week the process splits into a deterministic part, if length is 0 then error 500 every time, and a probabilistic part, the LLM may answer differently each call.\n>\n> Caption: Every piece of software is three words. Do the middle by hand once, then decide what to automate.\n\n- **Every piece of software, and every SOP, is three words.** Write down the inputs, write down the outputs, define the process between them. The manual MVP is where you find yours.\n- **The weekly stand-up.** A 15-person team, too many updates for one founder, so an assistant was hired. **Input:** the messy transcript. **Output:** a summary brief enough to decide on, by email. **Process:** paste into an LLM, summarise, format, send.\n- **No tool needed.** Resist agents. Do the most manual thing that works, once, and write down what you did, including how you judged when two sources disagreed. \"I used my judgment\" is the rabbit hole; how you judge is the question.\n- **From next week it becomes a system.** Some parts **deterministic**: \"if the transcript length is 0, print error 500\" does so every time. Some **probabilistic**: an LLM may answer differently each call. Which is which, and what cannot be automated, is weeks 1 to 8."},{"h":"12 Where is the app? A tracker, not a product","a":"12-where-is-the-app-a-tracker-not-a-product","text":"> Sketch: An MVP plan card with lines of text, an arrow labelled paste into Lovable, publish, to a 21-day tracker card with a grid of 21 squares, the first four ticked. Below: a link anyone can open, update it daily.\n>\n> Caption: No backend, no database yet. The link tracks the manual work; it is not the product.\n\n- **The MVP is manual, so there is nothing to build yet.** The link is a personal dashboard.\n- **Paste your MVP plan into Lovable** (or Google AI Studio, any builder) and ask for a tracker of every line item in the 21-day pilot. Check the preview, click Publish, share the link.\n- **No backend, no database yet.** Whatever breaks is a learning item for the next two weeks.\n- **Update it daily**, which means doing the work daily."},{"h":"13 From the Q&A","a":"13-from-the-qa","text":"> Sketch: Charu's math on the left: 3 hours times $100 is $300 a week. A 15-minute review instead saves about $275 a week per PM. A coral arrow carries that to the right, where there are only two metrics: revenue up, or cost or time down. Charu's report lands on cost or time down.\n>\n> Caption: Charu's Friday report becomes her specific thing once it has a number.\n\n**Charu's Friday report.** North Star: by March 2027, an AI-enabled project manager with up to a 100% increase in total compensation. Timeline, identity and income present; the specific thing missing. Her observation supplied it: every Friday a PM pulls from several systems to build a status report, and when the facts differ she cannot tell which is right, though she answers for what leadership reads.\n\n- **Put a number on it.** 3 hours a week at $100 an hour is $300 a week per PM. Cut to a 15-minute review and that saves $250 to $300 a week per PM, compounding across the team.\n- **The observation must align with the North Star**, and in doing so supplies the specificity it lacked.\n\n> Only two metrics move compensation: revenue increased, or cost and time saved. Pick the one your observation touches and put a number on it.\n\nA student who replaced a $10,000 Slack bill with a configured open-source messenger had a proof his manager could not ignore, and a freelance offer for every other company paying the same bill.\n\n- **Time budget.** 8 to 10 hours a week, about 1 to 1.5 hours a day. No upper limit.\n- **How long validation takes.** The skill, 40 to 90 minutes. Four calls of 15 to 20 minutes each. 3 to 4 hours in all; double it for safety.\n- **Pivoting.** Expected. Around 90% may change their idea by week 2. Run one cycle to the end before starting another.\n- **Several incentives.** Mohit's North Star had four. Keep one, and add the industry: that is where the observation lives.\n- **\"What are you charging\".** A validation device. If the problem is internal to your team, write not applicable and keep the time-saved metric.\n- **Competitors already exist.** Sai's question, why validate when paying customers exist: answered with data in the orientation.\n- **Certifications and portfolios** are proxies. Clarity on your specific thing beats another certificate."},{"h":"14 What to do next","a":"14-what-to-do-next","text":"1. **Rewrite your North Star** with all four elements and run the mom test on it. No North Star yet? Fill the Launchpad form first.\n2. **Write your observation** at the depth of the Pune story: a real person, a real desk, real numbers, no solution.\n3. **Run discovery-to-mvp-coach** (skill file on the LMS; Settings, Customise, Skills, then `/discovery-to-mvp-coach`). 60 to 90 minutes. Keep the discovery document and the MVP plan.\n4. **Publish a 21-day tracker** from the MVP plan in Lovable or any builder.\n5. **Start the manual MVP.** Speak to 2 to 5 real people with the questions the simulation gave you, and write down the input, process and output of what you delivered by hand. Full brief in Assignment 1.\n\n**Words from this workshop**\n\n- **North Star**: A six-month goal with four elements: timeline, role and identity, level or income or scale, and the specific thing that bridges them.\n- **The mom test (North Star)**: Can someone else verify on the date whether it happened? Absolute number, or relative to a measured baseline.\n- **Done Equals**: The artefact that proves the outcome. Here: a tracker link.\n- **Observation**: A detailed record of a real person's problem, from your lived experience, with no solution.\n- **Subject**: Who you are solving for: demographic, behavioural, financial, psychological and structural markers.\n- **Evidence audit**: What already exists that confirms or contradicts what you observed.\n- **Causal chain**: The causes you believe produced the observation, labelled as assumptions.\n- **Hypothesis**: Subject, problem, cause, intervention, a number, a deadline, and the condition that makes it false.\n- **Falsifiable**: Written so a specific result would prove it wrong. Aarav's: under 60% of 250 within 90 days.\n- **The Mom Test (the book)**: Their life, not your idea; the past, not the future; specifics, not generalities; emotions, not opinions.\n- **Manual MVP**: Deliver the value by hand to 2 to 5 real people before any software.\n- **Input, process, output**: The three words all software reduces to. Define the first and last; the middle is what you automate.\n- **Deterministic**: Rule-based. Does exactly what it is told, the same way every time.\n- **Probabilistic**: An LLM. May answer differently on each call.\n\nKeep this\n\nThe idea does not come first. The observation does, from your own lived experience, aimed by a North Star with four elements and written as a hypothesis that can be proven false. Nothing gets automated until you have done it by hand once and written down the input, the process and the output."}]},{"path":"raw/practice/what-to-build.md","title":"Idea to App Workshop","meta":"title: Idea to App Workshop\npage: https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/\nfile: c8/module-2/what-to-build/index.html\nabout: Cohort 8 · Module 2 · Week 0 · Fri 18 Sep 2026 · Both tracks\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nsite_commit: 4fd0f0d\nwords: 172","sections":[{"h":"Idea to App workshop","a":"idea-to-app-workshop","text":"Observation before idea.\n\nThe workshop in one line\n\nThe idea does not come first. The observation does, aimed by a North Star and written as a hypothesis that can fail. Do it by hand once before you automate anything.\n\n01"},{"h":"The workshop","a":"the-workshop","text":"1. [A North Star has four elements](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s02)\n2. [The mom test for a North Star](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s03)\n3. [Aarav at 12.38 AM: an observation](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s06)\n4. [A hypothesis must be able to fail](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s08)\n5. [The Mom Test, simulated](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s09)\n6. [Cohort zero: the manual MVP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s10)\n7. [Input, process, output](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes#s11)\n\n02"},{"h":"Done equals","a":"done-equals","text":"A North Star with all four elements that someone else could verify on the date, and an observation from your own life with no solution in it.\n\n03"},{"h":"When you need it","a":"when-you-need-it","text":"- [Notes: what was said, with the whiteboard](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/idea-to-app-notes)\n- [Worked example: Aarav, Discovery to MVP](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/aarav-example)\n- [Assignment 1: First Proof](https://100x-curriculum.vercel.app/c8/module-2/what-to-build/assignment-1)\n\nIf you cannot test it by hand, do not build it.Next: Lecture 1, Orientation and State of AI 2026, Sat 19 Sep."}]},{"path":"raw/sheet/code-track.md","title":"Curriculum sheet, code track","meta":"title: Curriculum sheet, code track\npage: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Kg6T-dJWaYx_JCPiRk3u0nBuAQcsCVRDPj1NFKP4WzM/\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nwords: 2080","sections":[{"h":"Curriculum sheet: code track","a":"curriculum-sheet-code-track","text":"One section per row of the sheet. The sheet is the authority for dates and titles."},{"h":"Sat 19 Sep 2026 · 1. Orientation and State of AI 2026","a":"sat-19-sep-2026--1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces"},{"h":"Fri 25 Sep 2026 · 2. What is an interface; UI as the first interface","a":"fri-25-sep-2026--2-what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can:\n    - Understand First principles of building AI Systems.\n    - Learn ⁠⁠how to turn your manual MVP's input, process, and output into an AI system\n    - Learn the anatomy of a full stack system: frontend, backend, UI,API, LLM, database.\n    - Break the tracker you published last week, map every component inside it, and find out exactly why it breaks without them.\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Sat 26 Sep 2026 · 3. Building UI: Design to Deploy","a":"sat-26-sep-2026--3-building-ui-design-to-deploy","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Python, Gradio or Streamlit, Hugging Face Spaces\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can:\n    - Build UI using Python.\n    - Learn Gradio and Streamlit.\n    - Deploy your first app on the cloud.\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Fri 02 Oct 2026 · Gandhi Jayanti — Holiday","a":"fri-02-oct-2026--gandhi-jayanti--holiday","text":"- Type: Holiday"},{"h":"Sat 03 Oct 2026 · 4. API as the second interface","a":"sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Explain why the frontend cannot store or protect data on its own, and what an API is for.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Specify an endpoint’s URL, HTTP method, request, and response.\n    - Read JSON responses and diagnose common status codes and authentication errors.\n    - Connect to an LLM API."},{"h":"Fri 09 Oct 2026 · 5. Building APIs and backend","a":"fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Python, FastAPI, Render\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Build a FastAPI backend that the UI calls, and put it live.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Separate frontend responsibilities from backend logic, secrets, and controls.\n    - Implement and validate the core input-processing-output function before exposing an API.\n    - Create and test FastAPI endpoints locally.\n    - Deploy a backend and connect it to a frontend and an LLM service."},{"h":"Sat 10 Oct 2026 · 6. Databases and domain modelling","a":"sat-10-oct-2026--6-databases-and-domain-modelling","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: Paper exercise\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Model their domain as entities and relationships before any table exists.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Distinguish temporary in-memory state from persistent storage.\n    - Identify a product’s entities, attributes, and relationships through domain modeling.\n    - Use primary keys, foreign keys, and relationship types to design a relational schema.\n    - Choose storage based on the data’s structure, keeping large files separate from relational records."},{"h":"Fri 16 Oct 2026 · 7. Connect backend to database","a":"fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: FastAPI, Supabase\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Connect the backend to Supabase, store real data, and show it surviving a refresh.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain when an application needs a database instead of variables or files.\n    - Translate a domain model into tables, data types, primary keys, and foreign keys.\n    - Connect the backend to persistent storage and save linked conversations and messages.\n    - Retrieve the correct conversation history and verify that stored data survives a restart."},{"h":"Sat 17 Oct 2026 · 8. End to end MVP build and deploy","a":"sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Gradio, FastAPI, Supabase, Render\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Deploy the full app and hand a live URL to a real user who completes one task on it.\n- Done equals:\n    - ARC 1 DONE EQUALS: a named user who did not build it completes the task on the deployed link.\n    - Public moment 1: the live URL in a real user's hands.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Turn a validated hypothesis into a core function that solves the user’s problem.\n    - Define output quality through evaluation criteria and codify it.\n    - Use an AI coding agent with reviewed plans, explicit specifications, and decisions.\n    - Assemble and deploy a full-stack MVP, then add authentication, usage limits, and cost per user."},{"h":"Sat 17 Oct 2026 · Hackathon (Sat to Tue)","a":"sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue","text":"- Type: Hackathon\n- Done equals: Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Thu 22 Oct 2026 · Hackathon results announcement","a":"thu-22-oct-2026--hackathon-results-announcement","text":"- Type: Announcement\n- Done equals: Hackathon results announced\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Fri 23 Oct 2026 · Catch-up week — No lecture","a":"fri-23-oct-2026--catch-up-week--no-lecture","text":"- Type: Catch-up\n- Done equals: No lectures Fri 23 and Sat 24 October 2026"},{"h":"Fri 30 Oct 2026 · 9. LLMs from First Principles","a":"fri-30-oct-2026--9-llms-from-first-principles","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Explain what an LLM can and cannot know, and where its knowledge stops.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain learning through goals, attempts, errors, and feedback.\n    - Distinguish self-supervised learning, supervised fine-tuning, and human preference alignment.\n    - Explain next-token prediction and the stages that turn a base language model into an assistant.\n    - Recognize why data quality, verifiable examples, and reliable surrounding systems matter when building with LLMs."},{"h":"Sat 31 Oct 2026 · 10. Tool calling and MCP","a":"sat-31-oct-2026--10-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Explain tool calling as the model asking for an action, not taking it.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Part 1: Tool Calling and MCP\n    - Explain how missing context and model training contribute to hallucinated answers.\n    - Distinguish problems solved with supplied context from those requiring live external data.\n    - Use an LLM to extract structured inputs from natural-language requests.\n    - Design the three-layer tool-calling flow: LLM, backend execution environment, and deterministic tool.\n    - Part 2: The Road to MCP\n    - Decide when a tool is needed, which tool to select, and when clarification is necessary.\n    - Describe tools using names, descriptions, parameter types, and structured schemas.\n    - Separate the model’s tool-call decision from the backend’s execution responsibility.\n    - Explain how MCP standardizes tool discovery and communication, and when a direct integration is sufficient."},{"h":"Fri 06 Nov 2026 · Diwali break — Holiday","a":"fri-06-nov-2026--diwali-break--holiday","text":"- Type: Holiday\n- Done equals: No lectures Fri 6 and Sat 7 November 2026"},{"h":"Fri 13 Nov 2026 · 11. Tool calling and MCP","a":"fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: Tool Bench, Groq\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Write a tool card for the curriculum sheet, mark the tests before the run, and move the card to an MCP server a second app can use.\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Sat 14 Nov 2026 · 12. Building with tool calling and MCP","a":"sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Python, Groq, FastMCP\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Wire one tool into a Python tool-calling loop, expose it over MCP, and write the check that proves it was called with the right inputs.\n- Done equals: ARC 2 DONE EQUALS: a log showing the call, the arguments and the result, plus the verifier written before the run.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Identify where an application needs current information and connect an appropriate external tool.\n    - Write precise tool descriptions and argument schemas to reduce ambiguity.\n    - Implement the loop of model decision, backend execution, tool-result return, and final response.\n    - Test routing between tools and use observed failures to improve prompts and interface contracts."},{"h":"Fri 20 Nov 2026 · 13. Retrieval-augmented generation","a":"fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Explain why the model cannot answer from their documents by default, and what retrieval changes.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain retrieval, augmentation, and generation as distinct stages of an application.\n    - Diagnose context-window constraints and retrieve only information relevant to the query.\n    - Compare indexing, metadata filtering, keyword search, and embedding-based semantic search.\n    - Use representative queries and ideal answers to choose a retrieval strategy and evaluate correctness."},{"h":"Sat 21 Nov 2026 · 14. Building RAG apps","a":"sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Build a RAG flow over their own documents in Python and show one answer that cites its source chunk.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Measure token usage before sending retrieved information to a model.\n    - Implement chunking, metadata indexing, and query-specific filtering.\n    - Reduce unnecessary context through pre-aggregation and reuse repeated results through caching.\n    - Compare token usage before and after optimization while checking that answer quality is preserved.\n    - Diagnose whether a failure comes from missing knowledge, outdated information, scale, or model behavior."},{"h":"Fri 27 Nov 2026 · 15. LLM memory: building a second brain","a":"fri-27-nov-2026--15-llm-memory-building-a-second-brain","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Build a second brain that answers a question about something told to it a week earlier.\n- Done equals: ARC 3 DONE EQUALS: three questions with known answers, scored before and after retrieval.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain memory as persistent state with policies for writing, retrieval, and resolving contradictions.\n    - Identify stale memories, incorrect stored facts, and context pollution.\n    - Decide whether memory is needed based on how long facts remain useful and the cost of repeating them.\n    - Design a reviewable memory system using authoritative sources, organized files, retrieval, and version history."},{"h":"Sat 28 Nov 2026 · 16. Connecting the dots; deriving harness engineering","a":"sat-28-nov-2026--16-connecting-the-dots-deriving-harness-engineering","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 4: Harness\n- Students can: Draw the control graph of their own system (deterministic, model and human nodes) and explain why the arrangement, not the model, decides reliability.\n- Done equals:\n    - ARC 4 DONE EQUALS: the diagram, plus one failure reproduced and one node added to catch it.\n    - Public moment 2: the end to end harness of their own system.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Write a specific, time-bound North Star whose achievement can be independently verified.\n    - Identify relevant information sources and filter incoming material against that goal.\n    - Combine ingestion, APIs, retrieval, memory, and evaluation into a personal second brain.\n    - Distinguish reusable procedural skills from accumulated knowledge and maintain both as the system grows."},{"h":"Fri 04 Dec 2026 · 17. Fine-tuning LLMs","a":"fri-04-dec-2026--17-fine-tuning-llms","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: State when fine-tuning is the wrong answer, and choose between prompt, retrieval and fine-tune for one case.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Choose among prompting, examples, RAG, workflow design, and fine-tuning based on the actual performance gap.\n    - Recognize when fine-tuning is appropriate for repeated behavior, style, or output structure.\n    - Explain pre-training, supervised fine-tuning, and preference alignment, including RLHF and DPO.\n    - Compare full fine-tuning, LoRA, and QLoRA in terms of adaptation, compute, memory, and cost."},{"h":"Sat 05 Dec 2026 · 18. Data preparation techniques","a":"sat-05-dec-2026--18-data-preparation-techniques","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: Prepare a small clean dataset in Python and explain what clean means for it.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Select instruction, dialogue, domain-specific, or reasoning datasets suited to the intended task.\n    - Clean and normalize data, remove duplicates, redact sensitive information, and prevent training–evaluation leakage.\n    - Create consistent instruction-input-output or conversation examples with accurate annotations.\n    - Improve dataset diversity and edge-case coverage, validate quality, and iterate based on model failures."},{"h":"Fri 11 Dec 2026 · 19. Evaluation of LLMs","a":"fri-11-dec-2026--19-evaluation-of-llms","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: Run an eval with a written rubric, and judge the judge: show one case where the LLM grader was wrong.\n- Done equals: ARC 5 DONE EQUALS: an eval sheet with rubric, scores, and the flagged grader error.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Define task-specific quality criteria and assemble representative test prompts, ideal outputs, and edge cases.\n    - Compare public benchmarks, automated metrics, LLM judges, and human evaluation.\n    - Validate automated judgments against human reviews and use clear rubrics or A/B comparisons.\n    - Use evaluation failures to improve prompts, training data, and model behavior continuously."}]},{"path":"raw/sheet/no-code-track.md","title":"Curriculum sheet, no code track","meta":"title: Curriculum sheet, no code track\npage: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Kg6T-dJWaYx_JCPiRk3u0nBuAQcsCVRDPj1NFKP4WzM/\ntaken: 2026-11-27\nwords: 1804","sections":[{"h":"Curriculum sheet: no code track","a":"curriculum-sheet-no-code-track","text":"One section per row of the sheet. The sheet is the authority for dates and titles."},{"h":"Sat 19 Sep 2026 · 1. Orientation and State of AI 2026","a":"sat-19-sep-2026--1-orientation-and-state-of-ai-2026","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces"},{"h":"Fri 25 Sep 2026 · 2. What is an interface; UI as the first interface","a":"fri-25-sep-2026--2-what-is-an-interface-ui-as-the-first-interface","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can:\n    - Understand First principles of building AI Systems.\n    - Learn ⁠⁠how to turn your manual MVP's input, process, and output into an AI system\n    - Learn the anatomy of a full stack system: frontend, backend, UI,API, LLM, database.\n    - Break the tracker you published last week, map every component inside it, and find out exactly why it breaks without them.\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Sat 26 Sep 2026 · 3. PRD to UI design to frontend app","a":"sat-26-sep-2026--3-prd-to-ui-design-to-frontend-app","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Google Stitch, Google AI Studio\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Turn a PRD into a designed UI in Stitch and a running frontend in AI Studio.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Fri 02 Oct 2026 · Gandhi Jayanti — Holiday","a":"fri-02-oct-2026--gandhi-jayanti--holiday","text":"- Type: Holiday"},{"h":"Sat 03 Oct 2026 · 4. API as the second interface","a":"sat-03-oct-2026--4-api-as-the-second-interface","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Explain why the frontend cannot store or protect data on its own, and what an API is for.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Specify an endpoint’s URL, HTTP method, request, and response.\n    - Read JSON responses and diagnose common status codes and authentication errors.\n    - Connect to an LLM API."},{"h":"Fri 09 Oct 2026 · 5. Building APIs and backend","a":"fri-09-oct-2026--5-building-apis-and-backend","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Antigravity\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Move the app into Antigravity, read the agent's plan before approving it, and reject one wrong step.\n- Done equals: THE CLIFF: expect a third of the track to stall here.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Sat 10 Oct 2026 · 6. Databases and domain modelling","a":"sat-10-oct-2026--6-databases-and-domain-modelling","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: Paper exercise\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Model their domain as entities and relationships before any table exists.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Distinguish temporary in-memory state from persistent storage.\n    - Identify a product’s entities, attributes, and relationships through domain modeling.\n    - Use primary keys, foreign keys, and relationship types to design a relational schema.\n    - Choose storage based on the data’s structure, keeping large files separate from relational records."},{"h":"Fri 16 Oct 2026 · 7. Connect backend to database","a":"fri-16-oct-2026--7-connect-backend-to-database","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Antigravity, Supabase\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Connect Supabase, store real data, and show it surviving a refresh.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Sat 17 Oct 2026 · 8. End to end MVP build and deploy","a":"sat-17-oct-2026--8-end-to-end-mvp-build-and-deploy","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: Antigravity, Supabase, Vercel\n- Arc: Arc 1: Interfaces\n- Students can: Deploy to Vercel and hand a live URL to a real user who completes one task on it.\n- Done equals:\n    - ARC 1 DONE EQUALS: a named user who did not build it completes the task on the deployed link.\n    - Public moment 1: the live URL in a real user's hands.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Sat 17 Oct 2026 · Hackathon (Sat to Tue)","a":"sat-17-oct-2026--hackathon-sat-to-tue","text":"- Type: Hackathon\n- Done equals: Ends Tuesday, 20 October 2026, 9:00 PM IST\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Thu 22 Oct 2026 · Hackathon results announcement","a":"thu-22-oct-2026--hackathon-results-announcement","text":"- Type: Announcement\n- Done equals: Hackathon results announced\n- Status: Planned"},{"h":"Fri 23 Oct 2026 · Catch-up week — No lecture","a":"fri-23-oct-2026--catch-up-week--no-lecture","text":"- Type: Catch-up\n- Done equals: No lectures Fri 23 and Sat 24 October 2026"},{"h":"Fri 30 Oct 2026 · 9. LLMs from First Principles","a":"fri-30-oct-2026--9-llms-from-first-principles","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Explain what an LLM can and cannot know, and where its knowledge stops.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain learning through goals, attempts, errors, and feedback.\n    - Distinguish self-supervised learning, supervised fine-tuning, and human preference alignment.\n    - Explain next-token prediction and the stages that turn a base language model into an assistant.\n    - Recognize why data quality, verifiable examples, and reliable surrounding systems matter when building with LLMs."},{"h":"Sat 31 Oct 2026 · 10. Tool calling and MCP","a":"sat-31-oct-2026--10-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Explain tool calling as the model asking for an action, not taking it.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Part 1: Tool Calling and MCP\n    - Explain how missing context and model training contribute to hallucinated answers.\n    - Distinguish problems solved with supplied context from those requiring live external data.\n    - Use an LLM to extract structured inputs from natural-language requests.\n    - Design the three-layer tool-calling flow: LLM, backend execution environment, and deterministic tool.\n    - Part 2: The Road to MCP\n    - Decide when a tool is needed, which tool to select, and when clarification is necessary.\n    - Describe tools using names, descriptions, parameter types, and structured schemas.\n    - Separate the model’s tool-call decision from the backend’s execution responsibility.\n    - Explain how MCP standardizes tool discovery and communication, and when a direct integration is sufficient."},{"h":"Fri 06 Nov 2026 · Diwali break — Holiday","a":"fri-06-nov-2026--diwali-break--holiday","text":"- Type: Holiday\n- Done equals: No lectures Fri 6 and Sat 7 November 2026"},{"h":"Fri 13 Nov 2026 · 11. Tool calling and MCP","a":"fri-13-nov-2026--11-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: Tool Bench, Groq\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Write a tool card for the curriculum sheet, mark the tests before the run, and move the card to an MCP server a second app can use.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Sat 14 Nov 2026 · 12. Building with tool calling and MCP","a":"sat-14-nov-2026--12-building-with-tool-calling-and-mcp","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 2: LLM primitives\n- Students can: Wire one tool through MCP into a working flow, and write the check that proves the tool was called with the right inputs.\n- Done equals: ARC 2 DONE EQUALS: a log showing the call, the arguments and the result, plus the verifier written before the run.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Fri 20 Nov 2026 · 13. Retrieval-augmented generation","a":"fri-20-nov-2026--13-retrieval-augmented-generation","text":"- Type: combined theory\n- Tools: None\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Explain why the model cannot answer from their documents by default, and what retrieval changes.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain retrieval, augmentation, and generation as distinct stages of an application.\n    - Diagnose context-window constraints and retrieve only information relevant to the query.\n    - Compare indexing, metadata filtering, keyword search, and embedding-based semantic search.\n    - Use representative queries and ideal answers to choose a retrieval strategy and evaluate correctness."},{"h":"Sat 21 Nov 2026 · 14. Building RAG apps","a":"sat-21-nov-2026--14-building-rag-apps","text":"- Type: separate practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Build a RAG flow over their own documents and show one answer that cites its source chunk.\n- Status: Done"},{"h":"Fri 27 Nov 2026 · 15. LLM memory: building a second brain","a":"fri-27-nov-2026--15-llm-memory-building-a-second-brain","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 3: Context\n- Students can: Build a second brain that answers a question about something told to it a week earlier.\n- Done equals: ARC 3 DONE EQUALS: three questions with known answers, scored before and after retrieval.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Explain memory as persistent state with policies for writing, retrieval, and resolving contradictions.\n    - Identify stale memories, incorrect stored facts, and context pollution.\n    - Decide whether memory is needed based on how long facts remain useful and the cost of repeating them.\n    - Design a reviewable memory system using authoritative sources, organized files, retrieval, and version history."},{"h":"Sat 28 Nov 2026 · 16. Connecting the dots; deriving harness engineering","a":"sat-28-nov-2026--16-connecting-the-dots-deriving-harness-engineering","text":"- Type: combined practical\n- Tools: [TO NAME]\n- Arc: Arc 4: Harness\n- Students can: Draw the control graph of their own system (deterministic, model and human nodes) and explain why the arrangement, not the model, decides reliability.\n- Done equals:\n    - ARC 4 DONE EQUALS: the diagram, plus one failure reproduced and one node added to catch it.\n    - Public moment 2: the end to end harness of their own system.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Write a specific, time-bound North Star whose achievement can be independently verified.\n    - Identify relevant information sources and filter incoming material against that goal.\n    - Combine ingestion, APIs, retrieval, memory, and evaluation into a personal second brain.\n    - Distinguish reusable procedural skills from accumulated knowledge and maintain both as the system grows."},{"h":"Fri 04 Dec 2026 · 17. Fine-tuning LLMs","a":"fri-04-dec-2026--17-fine-tuning-llms","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: State when fine-tuning is the wrong answer, and choose between prompt, retrieval and fine-tune for one case.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Choose among prompting, examples, RAG, workflow design, and fine-tuning based on the actual performance gap.\n    - Recognize when fine-tuning is appropriate for repeated behavior, style, or output structure.\n    - Explain pre-training, supervised fine-tuning, and preference alignment, including RLHF and DPO.\n    - Compare full fine-tuning, LoRA, and QLoRA in terms of adaptation, compute, memory, and cost."},{"h":"Sat 05 Dec 2026 · 18. Data preparation techniques","a":"sat-05-dec-2026--18-data-preparation-techniques","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: Prepare a small clean dataset and explain what clean means for it.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Select instruction, dialogue, domain-specific, or reasoning datasets suited to the intended task.\n    - Clean and normalize data, remove duplicates, redact sensitive information, and prevent training–evaluation leakage.\n    - Create consistent instruction-input-output or conversation examples with accurate annotations.\n    - Improve dataset diversity and edge-case coverage, validate quality, and iterate based on model failures."},{"h":"Fri 11 Dec 2026 · 19. Evaluation of LLMs","a":"fri-11-dec-2026--19-evaluation-of-llms","text":"- Type: combined theory + practical\n- Tools: TBD\n- Arc: Arc 5: Model Behavior\n- Students can: Run an eval with a written rubric, and judge the judge: show one case where the LLM grader was wrong.\n- Done equals: ARC 5 DONE EQUALS: an eval sheet with rubric, scores, and the flagged grader error.\n- Status: Planned\n- Learning outcomes:\n    - Define task-specific quality criteria and assemble representative test prompts, ideal outputs, and edge cases.\n    - Compare public benchmarks, automated metrics, LLM judges, and human evaluation.\n    - Validate automated judgments against human reviews and use clear rubrics or A/B comparisons.\n    - Use evaluation failures to improve prompts, training data, and model behavior continuously."}]}]};
